tex.commentary:
    \aB{
        According to \textsc{TDRWFEROTMSOHK}%
        \footnote{Abbreviation for \textsc{The Dynamic Read-Writable Free Encyclopedic Repository of the Modern State of Human Knowledge}, hereafter abbreviated as ``the \textsc{Lord},'' to save space.}

        \begin{quote}
        \aC{The word \heb{אלהים} (Elhim) is a ``defective spelling'' of the word \heb{אלוהים} (Elohim).}
        \end{quote}

        And if you look up that word, it says

        \begin{quote}
        \aC{The word \heb{אלוהים} (Elohim) is from the plural of \heb{אלוה} (Eloh) which means ``god.''}
        \end{quote}

        And if you look around for some clarification, you'll find sentences like

        \begin{quote}
        \aC{This word is sometimes grammatically plural and sometimes singular.}
        \end{quote}

        And you may also stumble upon a sentence mentioning that particularly devout believers

        \begin{quote}
        \aC{Thus may prefer the term \heb{אלוקים} (Elokim), so as not to abuse the name of God.}
        \end{quote}

		And so we're off to a pretty weird start
    }

