tex.commentary:
And just as her soul was leaving her body—she dies by the way—she named
the boy Ben-oni.\footnote{
	Lit. Ben-鬼 in some manuscripts.
	Some pseudepigrapha have Benono, or in one infamous manuscript,
	屄nono. \aC{\footnotesize
		Inline Recursive Footnote: The character 屄
		is composed of two Chinese radicals:
		尸, meaning “body” or “corpse,” and
		穴, meaning “hole” under the 尸. These two radicals
		combine to form 屄, a character whose meaning is rather
		deep, and of which a more penetrating analysis is not
		possible in the current footnote.
	}
}

We don't know what Ben-oni means.\fC{
	Ben-oni means “son of my sorrow,” fitting Rachel’s death in childbirth;
	but \hE{אוֹן} can also mean “strength” or “virility” (cf. Gen. 49:3),
	giving the name a possible double meaning: “son of my sorrow” and
	“son of my strength.”
}

But for some reason Jacob's not happy with that name.

So he renames the kid Benjamin.

Because the love of his life was gone now.

And all he has left now is his right hand.%
\fC{Benjamin means “son of the right hand.”}
\fB{And three other wives.}

