tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then Moses and the children of Israel sang this song to
	YHWH. And they said, saying:
}%
\eProtoA{
	Let me sing to YHWH, for He triumphed! Horse and its
	rider He cast in the sea.
}%

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		rider. This refers to those who are riding in the
%		chariots. Cavalry was
%		not used in Egypt in this period.
%	}

tex.english:
\eProtoA{
	My strength and song are Yah, and He became a salvation
	for me. This is my God, and I’ll praise Him, my father’s
	God, and I’ll hail Him.
}

\eProtoA{
	Pharaoh’s chariots and his army He plunged in the sea and
	the choice of his troops drowned in the Red Sea.
}

\eProtoA{
	The deeps covered them. They sank in the depths like a
	stone.
}

\eProtoA{
	Your right hand, YHWH, awesome in power, your right hand,
	YHWH, crushed the foe.
}

\eProtoA{
	And in your triumph’s greatness you threw down your
	adversaries. You let go your fury: it consumed them like
	straw.
}

\eProtoA{
	And by wind from your nostrils water was massed, surf
	piled up like a heap, the deeps congealed in the heart of
	the sea.
}

\eProtoA{
	The enemy said, “I’ll pursue! I’ll catch up! I’ll divide
	spoil! My soul will be sated! I’ll unsheathe my sword! My
	hand will deprive them!”
}

\eProtoA{
	You blew with your wind. Sea covered them. They sank like
	lead in the awesome water.
}

\eProtoA{
	Who is like you among the gods, YHWH! Who is like you:
	awesome in holiness! fearsome with splendors! making
	miracles!
}

\eProtoA{
	You led, in your kindness, the people you redeemed; you
	ushered, in your strength, to your holy abode.
}

\eProtoA{
	Peoples heard—they shuddered. Shaking seized Philistia’s
	residents.
}

\eProtoA{
	Then Edom’s chiefs were terrified. Moab’s chieftains:
	trembling seized them. All Canaan’s residents melted.
}

\eProtoA{
	Terror and fear came over them. At the power of your arm
	they’re silent like stone. ’Til your people passed, YHWH,
	’til the people you created passed.
}

\eProtoA{
	You’ll bring them and plant them in your legacy’s
	mountain, your throne’s platform, that you made, YHWH; a
	holy place, Lord, that your hands reared.
}

\eR{
	Because Pharaoh’s horses with his chariots and with his
	horsemen came in the sea, and YHWH brought back the water
	of the sea over them, and the children of Israel had gone
	on the dry ground through the sea.%
}

\eE{
	And Miriam, the prophetess, Aaron’s sister, took a drum in
	her hand, and all the women went out behind her with drums
	and with dances.
}

\eR{And Moses had Israel travel from the Red Sea, and they
	went out to the wilderness of Shur.}
\eJ{And they went three
	days in the wilderness. And they did not find water.}

\eJ{
	And they came to Marah, and they were not able to drink
	water from Marah because it was bitter. On account of this
	he called its name Marah.
}

\eJ{
	And the people complained at Moses, saying, “What shall we
	drink?”
}

\eJ{And he cried to YHWH, and YHWH showed him a tree, and he
	threw it into the water, and the water sweetened.}
\eE{He set
	law and judgment for them there, and He tested them there.}

\eR{
	And they came to Elim. And twelve springs of water and
	seventy palm trees were there. And they camped there by
	the water.%
}

\eP{
	And all of the congregation of the children of Israel
	complained at Moses and at Aaron in the wilderness.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Here, I’m raining bread from the
	skies for you, and let the people go out and gather the
	daily ration in its day in order that I can test them:
	will they go by my instruction or not.
}

\eJ{
	And it will be that on the sixth day they’ll prepare what
	they bring in, and it will be twice as much as they’ll
	gather on regular days.”
}

\eP{
	And Moses and Aaron said to all the children of Israel,
	“At evening you’ll know that YHWH brought you out from the
	land of Egypt,
}

\eP{
	and in the morning you’ll see YHWH’s glory, because He has
	heard your complaints about YHWH. And what are we, that
	you complain at us?!”
}

\eP{
	And Moses said, “When YHWH gives you meat to eat in the
	evening and bread to the full in the morning, because YHWH
	has heard your complaints that you’re making at Him! And
	what are we? Your complaints are not at us but at YHWH!”
}

\eP{
	And Moses said to Aaron, “Say to all of the congregation
	of the children of Israel, ‘Come close in front of YHWH,
	because He has heard your complaints.’”
}

\eP{
	And it was as Aaron was speaking to all of the
	congregation of the children of Israel: and they turned to
	the wilderness, and here was YHWH’s glory appearing in a
	cloud.
}

\eP{
	“I’ve heard the complaints of the children of Israel.
	Speak to them, saying, ‘Between the two evenings you shall
	eat meat, and in the morning you shall be filled with
	bread, and you will know that I am YHWH, your God.’”
}

\eP{
	And it was in the evening, and quail went up and covered
	the camp. And in the morning there was a layer of dew
	around the camp.
}

\eP{
	And the layer of dew went up, and here, on the face of the
	wilderness: scaly thin, thin as frost on the ground.
}

\eP{
	And the children of Israel saw, and each said to his
	brother, “What is it?”—because they did not know what it
	was. And Moses said to them, “That is the bread that YHWH
	has given you for food.
}

\eP{
	This is the thing that YHWH has commanded: Collect some of
	it, each according to what he eats; you shall take an omer
	per head, the number of your persons, each for whoever is
	in his tent.”
}

\eP{
	And the children of Israel did so. And they collected—the
	one who took the most and the one who took the least.
}

\eP{
	And they measured by the omer, and the one who took the
	most had not exceeded, and the one who took the least had
	not fallen short. Each had collected according to what he
	eats.
}

\eP{
	And Moses said to them, “Let no one leave any of it over
	until morning.”
}

\eP{
	And they did not listen to Moses, and people left some of
	it over until morning, and it yielded worms and stank. And
	Moses was angry at them.
}

\eP{
	And they collected it morning by morning, each by what he
	eats, and when the sun was hot it melted.
}

\eP{
	And it was: on the sixth day they collected double bread,
	two times the omer for each one, and all the chiefs of the
	congregation came and told Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he said to them, “That is what YHWH spoke. Tomorrow is
	a ceasing, a holy Sabbath to YHWH. Bake what you’ll bake,
	and cook what you’ll cook, and leave what is left over for
	keeping until the morning.”
}

\eP{
	And they left it until the morning, as Moses had
	commanded, and it did not stink, and there was not a worm
	in it.
}

\eP{
	And Moses said, “Eat it today, because today is a Sabbath
	to YHWH. Today you won’t find it in the field.
}

\eP{
	Six days you shall collect it. And on the seventh day is a
	Sabbath; there will not be any in it.”
}

\eP{
	And it was: on the seventh day some of the people went out
	to collect, and they did not find any.
}

\eP{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “How long do you refuse to observe
	my commandments and my instructions?
}

\eP{
	See that YHWH has given you the Sabbath. On account of
	this He is giving you two days’ bread on the sixth day.
	Stay, each in his place. Let no man go out from his place
	in the seventh day.”
}

\eP{
	And the house of Israel called its name “manna.” And it
	was like a coriander seed, white, and its taste was like a
	wafer in honey.
}

\eP{
	And Moses said to Aaron, “Take one jar and place an
	omer-ful of manna there and lay it in front of YHWH for
	watching over through your generations,”
}

\eP{
	as YHWH had commanded Moses. And Aaron laid it in front of
	the Testimony for watching over.
}

\eP{And the children of Israel ate the manna forty years until
	they came to settled land.}
\eJ{They ate the manna until they
	came to the edge of the land of Canaan.}

\eR{
	And all of the congregation of the children of Israel
	traveled from the wilderness of Sîn on their travels by
	YHWH’s word, and they camped in Rephidim. And there was no
	water for the people to drink.%
}

\eE{
	And the people quarreled with Moses. And they said, “Give
	us water, and let us drink.” And Moses said to them, “Why
	do you quarrel with me? Why do you test YHWH?!”
}

\eE{
	And the people thirsted for water there, and the people
	complained at Moses and said, “Why is this that you
	brought us up from Egypt: to kill me and my children and
	my cattle with thirst?!”
}

\eE{
	And Moses cried to YHWH, saying, “What shall I do to this
	people? A little more and they’ll stone me!”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Pass in front of the people and
	take some of Israel’s elders with you, and take your staff
	with which you struck the Nile in your hand, and you’ll
	go.
}

\eE{
	Here, I’ll be standing in front of you there on a rock at
	Horeb. And you’ll strike the rock, and water will come out
	of it, and the people will drink.” And Moses did so before
	the eyes of Israel’s elders.
}

\eE{
	And he called the place’s name Massah and Meribah because
	of the quarrel of the children of Israel and because of
	their testing YHWH, saying, “Is YHWH among us or not?”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		he called the place’s name Massah and Meribah. That is,
%		he named it
%		Testing and Quarrel.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And Moses said to Joshua, “Choose men for us and go, fight
	against Amalek. Tomorrow I’ll be standing up on the
	hilltop, and the staff of God in my hand.”
}

\eE{
	And Joshua did as Moses said to him, to fight against
	Amalek; and Moses, Aaron, and Hur went up to the hilltop.
}

\eE{
	And it was: when Moses would lift his hand, then Israel
	would predominate; and when he would rest his hand, then
	Amalek would predominate.
}

\eE{
	And Moses’ hands were heavy. And they took a stone and set
	it under him, and he sat on it, and Aaron and Hur held up
	his hands, one from this side and one from this side, and
	his hands were supported until the sunset.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Write this—a memorial—in a scroll
	and set it in Joshua’s ears, because I shall wipe out the
	memory of Amalek from under the skies!”
}

\eE{
	And Moses built an altar and called its name “YHWH is my
	standard.”
}

\eE{
	And he said, “Because—hand on YH’s throne—YHWH has war
	against Amalek from generation to generation.”
}

\eE{
	And Jethro, priest of Midian, Moses’ father-in-law, heard
	everything that God had done for Moses and for Israel, His
	people, that YHWH had brought Israel out from Egypt.
}

\eE{
	and her two sons, of whom one’s name was Gershom, because
	he said, “I was an alien in a foreign land,”
}

\eE{
	and one’s name was Eliezer, “because my father’s God was
	my help and rescued me from Pharaoh’s sword.”
}

\eE{
	And Jethro, Moses’ father-in-law, and his sons and his
	wife came to Moses, to the wilderness in which he was
	camping, at the Mountain of God.
}

\eE{
	And he said to Moses, “I, your father-in-law, Jethro, have
	come to you, and your wife and her two sons with her.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses went out to his father-in-law, and he bowed, and
	he kissed him, and they asked each other how they were,
	and they came to the tent.
}

\eE{
	And Moses told his father-in-law everything that YHWH had
	done to Pharaoh and to Egypt with regard to Israel, all
	the hardship that had found them on the way, and YHWH
	rescued them.
}

\eE{
	And Jethro rejoiced over all the good that YHWH had done
	for Israel, that He had rescued it from Egypt’s hand.
}

\eE{
	And Jethro said, “Blessed is YHWH, who rescued you from
	Egypt’s hand and from Pharaoh’s hand, who rescued the
	people from under Egypt’s hand:
}

\eE{
	now I know that YHWH is bigger than all the gods, because
	of the thing that they plotted against them.”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		the thing that they plotted against them. Meaning:
%		Egypt’s plot to
%		control the Israelites by enslaving them.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And Jethro, Moses’ father-in-law, took a burnt offering
	and sacrifices to God. And Aaron and all of Israel’s
	elders came to eat bread with Moses’ father-in-law before
	God.
}

\eE{
	And it was the next day, and Moses sat to judge the
	people. And the people stood by Moses from the morning to
	the evening.
}

\eE{
	And Moses said to his father-in-law, “Because the people
	come to me to inquire of God.
}

\eE{
	When they have a matter, it comes to me, and I judge
	between each one and his companion, and I make known God’s
	laws and His instructions.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses’ father-in-law said to him, “The thing that
	you’re doing isn’t good.
}

\eE{
	You’ll be worn out, both you and this people who are with
	you, because the thing is too heavy for you. You won’t be
	able to do it by yourself.
}

\eE{
	Now listen to my voice, and I’ll advise you, and may God
	be with you. You be for the people toward God, and you
	will bring the matters to God.
}

\eE{
	And you’ll enlighten them with the laws and the
	instructions, and you’ll make known to them the way in
	which they’ll go and the thing that they’ll do.
}

\eE{
	And you will envision, out of all the people, worthy men,
	who fear God, men of truth, who hate bribery, and you’ll
	set chiefs of thousands, chiefs of hundreds, chiefs of
	fifties, and chiefs of tens over them.
}

\eE{
	And they’ll judge the people at all times. And it will be:
	they’ll bring every matter that is big to you, and they
	will judge every matter that is small. And make it lighter
	on you, and they’ll bear it with you.
}

\eE{
	If you’ll do this thing, and YHWH will command you, then
	you’ll be able to stand, and also this entire people will
	come to its place in peace.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses listened to his father-in-law’s voice and did
	everything that he had said.
}

\eE{
	And Moses chose worthy men out of all of Israel and made
	them heads over the people: chiefs of thousands, chiefs of
	hundreds, chiefs of fifties, and chiefs of tens.
}

\eE{
	And they judged the people at all times. They would bring
	the matter that was hard to Moses, and they would judge
	every matter that was small.
}

\eE{
	And Moses let his father-in-law go, and he went to his
	land.
}

\eP{
	In the third month after the exodus of the children of
	Israel from the land of Egypt, on this day, they came to
	the wilderness of Sinai.
}

\eR{And they traveled from Rephidim and came to the wilderness
	of Sinai and camped in the wilderness.}
\eE{And Israel camped
	there opposite the mountain.}

\eE{
	And Moses had gone up to God. And YHWH called to him from
	the mountain, saying, “This is what you shall say to the
	house of Jacob and tell to the children of Israel:
}

\eE{
	‘You’ve seen what I did to Egypt, and I carried you on
	eagles’ wings and brought you to me.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		on eagles’ wings. The eagle is the highest-flying bird,
%		so there is no
%		threat from above. Eagles fly with their young on their
%		backs.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And now, if you’ll listen to my voice and observe my
	covenant, then you’ll be a treasure to me out of all the
	peoples, because all the earth is mine.
}

\eE{
	And you’ll be a kingdom of priests and a holy nation to
	me.’ These are the words that you shall speak to the
	children of Israel.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses came and called the people’s elders and set
	before them all these words that YHWH had commanded him.
}

\eE{
	And all the people responded together, and they said,
	“We’ll do everything that YHWH has spoken.” And Moses
	brought back the people’s words to YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Go to the people and consecrate
	them today and tomorrow; and they shall wash their clothes
}

\eJ{
	and be ready for the third day, because on the third day
	YHWH will come down on Mount Sinai before the eyes of all
	the people.
}

\eJ{
	And you shall limit the people all around, saying, ‘Watch
	yourselves about going up in the mountain and touching its
	edge. Anyone who touches the mountain shall be put to
	death.
}

\eJ{
	A hand shall not touch him, but he shall be stoned or
	shot. Whether animal or man, he will not live.’ At the
	blowing of the horn they shall go up the mountain.”
}

\eJ{
	And Moses went down from the mountain to the people. And
	he consecrated the people, and they washed their clothes.
}

\eJ{
	And he said to the people, “Be ready for three days. Don’t
	come close to a woman.”
}

\eJ{And it was on the third day, when it was morning,}
\eE{and it
	was: thunders and lightning and a heavy cloud on the
	mountain, and a sound of a horn, very strong. And the
	entire people that was in the camp trembled.}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		a heavy cloud. The word “heavy” recurs, but this time it
%		refers to
%		something divine.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And Moses brought out the people toward God from the camp,
	and they stood up at the bottom of the mountain.
}

\eJ{
	And Mount Sinai was all smoke because YHWH came down on it
	in fire, and its smoke went up like the smoke of a
	furnace, and the whole mountain trembled greatly.
}

\eE{
	And the sound of the horn was getting much stronger. Moses
	would speak, and God would answer him in a voice.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH came down on Mount Sinai, at the top of the
	mountain, and YHWH called to Moses at the top of the
	mountain, and Moses went up.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Go down. Warn the people in case
	they break through to YHWH, to see, and many of them fall.
}

\eJ{
	And also let the priests who approach YHWH consecrate
	themselves, or else YHWH will break out against them.”
}

\eJ{
	And Moses said to YHWH, “The people is not able to go up
	to Mount Sinai, because you warned us, saying, ‘Limit the
	mountain and consecrate it.’”
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH said to him, “Go. Go down. Then you’ll come up,
	you and Aaron with you. And let the priests and the people
	not break through to come up to YHWH, or else He’ll break
	out against them.”
}

\eOther{
	I am YHWH, your God, who brought you out from the land of
	Egypt, from a house of slaves.
}

\eOther{
	You shall not make a statue or any form that is in the
	skies above or that is in the earth below or that is in
	the water below the earth.
}

\eOther{
	You shall not bow to them, and you shall not serve them.
	Because I, YHWH, your God, am a jealous God, counting
	parents’ crime on children, on the third generation, and
	on the fourth generation of those who hate me,
}

\eOther{
	but practicing kindness to thousands for those who love me
	and for those who observe my commandments.
}

\eOther{
	You shall not bring up the name of YHWH, your God, for a
	falsehood, because YHWH will not make one innocent who
	will bring up His name for a falsehood.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		the Sabbath. On the significance of the Sabbath, see the
%		comments on Gen
%		2:2 and 2:3.
%	}

tex.english:
\eOther{
	and the seventh day is a Sabbath to YHWH, your God. You
	shall not do any work: you and your son and your daughter,
	your servant and your maid and your animal and your alien
	who is in your gates.
}

\eR{
	Because for six days YHWH made the skies and the earth,
	the sea, and everything that is in them, and He rested on
	the seventh day. On account of this, YHWH blessed the
	Sabbath day and made it holy.%
}

\eOther{
	Honor your father and your mother, so that your days will
	be extended on the land that YHWH, your God, is giving
	you.
}

\eOther{
	You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You
	shall not steal. You shall not testify against your
	neighbor as a lying witness.
}

\eOther{
	You shall not covet your neighbor’s house. You shall not
	covet your neighbor’s wife or his servant or his maid or
	his ox or his ass or anything that your neighbor has.
}

\eE{
	And all the people were seeing the thunders and the
	flashes and the sound of the horn and the mountain
	smoking. And the people saw, and they moved and stood at a
	distance,
}

\eE{
	and they said to Moses, “You speak with us so we may
	listen, but let God not speak with us or else we’ll die.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses said to the people, “Don’t be afraid, because
	God is coming for the purpose of testing you and for the
	purpose that his fear will be on your faces so that you
	won’t sin.”
}

\eE{
	And the people stood at a distance, and Moses went over to
	the nimbus where God was.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “You shall say this to the
	children of Israel: You have seen that I have spoken with
	you from the skies.
}

\eE{
	You shall not make gods of silver with me, and you shall
	not make gods of gold for yourselves.
}

\eE{
	And if you’ll make an altar of stones for me, you shall
	not make them cut. When you have elevated your sword over
	it, then you have desecrated it.
}

\eE{
	And you shall not go up by stairs on my altar, so that
	your nudity will not be exposed over it.”
}

\eE{
	“And these are the judgments that you shall set before
	them:
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		these are the judgments that you shall set. These three
%		chapters, Exodus
%		21–23, are a corpus of law known as the Covenant Code.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	“When you will buy a Hebrew slave, he shall work six
	years, and in the seventh he shall go out liberated for
	free.
}

\eE{
	If he will come by himself, he shall go out by himself. If
	he is a woman’s husband, then his wife shall go out with
	him.
}

\eE{
	If his master will give him a wife, and she will give
	birth to sons or daughters for him, the wife and her
	children will be her master’s, and he shall go out by
	himself.
}

\eE{
	And if the slave will say: ‘I love my master, my wife, and
	my children; I won’t go out liberated!’
}

\eE{
	then his master shall bring him over to God and bring him
	over to the door or to the doorpost, and his master shall
	pierce his ear with an awl, and he shall serve him
	forever.
}

\eE{
	“And if a man will sell his daughter as a maid, she shall
	not go out as the slaves go out.
}

\eE{
	If she is bad in the eyes of her master who has designated
	her for himself, then he shall let her be redeemed. He
	shall not dominate so as to sell her to a foreign people
	in his betrayal of her.
}

\eE{
	And if he will designate her for his son, he shall treat
	her according to the manner of daughters.
}

\eE{
	If he will take another for himself, he shall not subtract
	from her food, her apparel, and her hygiene.
}

\eE{
	And if he will not do these three for her, then she shall
	go out free. There is no money.
}

\eE{
	“One who strikes a man, and he dies, he shall be put to
	death.
}

\eE{
	And one who did not scheme, but God conveyed it to his
	hand, I shall set a place for you, that he shall flee
	there.
}

\eE{
	But if a man will plot against his neighbor, to kill him
	with treachery, you shall take him from my altar to die.
}

\eE{
	“And one who strikes his father and his mother shall be
	put to death.
}

\eE{
	“And one who steals a man and has sold him, or he was
	found in his hand, will be put to death.
}

\eE{
	“And one who curses his father and his mother shall be put
	to death.
}

\eE{
	“And if people will quarrel, and a man strikes his
	neighbor with a stone or with a fist, and he does not die,
	and he falls to bed,
}

\eE{
	if he will get up and walk himself outside on his staff,
	then the one who struck will be innocent. Only he shall
	compensate for his staying home and shall have him healed.
}

\eE{
	“And if a man will strike his slave or his maid with a
	rod, and he dies by his hand, he shall be avenged.
}

\eE{
	Just: if he will stand for a day or two days, he shall not
	be avenged, because he is his money.
}

\eE{
	And if there will be an injury, then you shall give a life
	for a life,
}

\eE{
	an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth, a hand for a hand,
	a foot for a foot,
}

\eE{
	“And if a man will strike his slave’s eye or his maid’s
	eye and destroy it, he shall let him go, liberated, for
	his eye.
}

\eE{
	And if he will knock out his slave’s tooth or his maid’s
	tooth, he shall let him go liberated for his tooth.
}

\eE{
	“And if an ox will gore a man or a woman and they die, the
	ox shall be stoned, and its meat shall not be eaten—and
	the ox’s owner is innocent.
}

\eE{
	And if it was a goring ox from the day before yesterday,
	and it had been so testified to its owner, and he did not
	watch it, and it killed a man or a woman, the ox will be
	stoned, and its owner will be put to death as well.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		from the day before yesterday. Meaning: this ox had
%		gored before.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	If a ransom will be set on him, then he shall give
	everything that will be set on him for the redemption of
	his life.
}

\eE{
	Whether it will gore a son or it will gore a daughter,
	according to this judgment shall be done to it.
}

\eE{
	If the ox will gore a slave or a maid, he shall pay thirty
	silver shekels to his owner, and the ox shall be stoned.
}

\eE{
	“And if a man will open a pit, or if a man will dig a pit,
	and he will not cover it, and an ox or an ass will fall
	there,
}

\eE{
	the owner of the pit shall pay; he shall pay back money to
	its owner, and the dead one will be his.
}

\eE{
	And if a man’s ox will strike his neighbor’s ox, and it
	dies, then they shall sell the live ox and split the money
	for it, and they shall split the dead one as well.
}

\eE{
	Or: if it was known that it was a goring ox from the day
	before yesterday, and its owner did not watch it, he shall
	pay an ox for the ox, and the dead one shall be his.
}

\eE{
	“If a man will steal an ox or a sheep and slaughter it or
	sell it, he shall pay five oxen for the ox and four sheep
	for the sheep.
}

\eE{
	“If the thief will be found while breaking in and will
	be struck, and he dies, there is no blood for him.
}

\eE{
	If the sun has risen on him there is blood for him. He shall pay. If
	he does not have it, then he shall be sold for his theft.
}

\eE{
	If the theft will be found in his hand—from ox to ass to
	sheep—alive, he shall pay two.
}

\eE{
	“If a man will have a field or a vineyard grazed, and he
	will let his beast go and graze in another person’s field,
	he shall pay the best of his field and the best of his
	vineyard.
}

\eE{
	“If fire will break out and find thorns and stacked grain
	or standing grain or a field is consumed, the one who set
	the blaze shall pay.
}

\eE{
	“If a man will give his neighbor money or items to watch,
	and it will be stolen from the man’s house, if the thief
	will be found he shall pay two.
}

\eE{
	If the thief will not be found, then the owner of the
	house shall be brought near to God: that he has not put
	out his hand into his neighbor’s property.
}

\eE{
	“If a man will give an ass or an ox or a sheep or any
	animal to his neighbor to watch, and it dies or is injured
	or is seized—no one seeing—
}

\eE{
	an oath of YHWH shall be between the two of them: that he
	has not put out his hand into his neighbor’s property; and
	its owner shall take it, and he shall not pay.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		he shall not pay. The person who was watching the animal
%		does not pay
%		anything to the animal’s owner.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	If it will be torn, he shall bring it in witness; he shall
	not pay for the torn one.
}

\eE{
	And if a man will ask it of his neighbor, and it is
	injured or dies, its owner not being with it, he shall
	pay.
}

\eE{
	If its owner is with it, he shall not pay. If it was
	hired, he has its hire coming to him.
}

\eE{
	“And if a man will deceive a virgin who is not betrothed,
	and he lies with her, he shall espouse her by a
	bride-price to him as a wife.
}

\eE{
	If her father will refuse to give her to him, he shall
	weigh out money corresponding to the bride-price of
	virgins.
}

\eE{
	“One who sacrifices to gods shall be completely
	destroyed—except to YHWH alone.
}

\eE{
	“And you shall not persecute an alien, and you shall not
	oppress him, because you were aliens in the land of Egypt.
}

\eE{
	If you will degrade them, when they will cry out to me
	I’ll hear their cry,
}

\eE{
	and my anger will flare, and I’ll kill you with a sword,
	and your wives will be widows and your children orphans!
}

\eE{
	“If you will lend money to my people, to the poor who is
	with you, you shall not be like a creditor to him; you
	shall not impose interest on him.
}

\eE{
	If you take your neighbor’s clothing as security, you
	shall give it back to him by the sunset,
}

\eE{
	because it is his only apparel; it is his clothing for his
	skin. In what will he sleep? And it will be, when he will
	cry out to me, that I shall listen, because I am gracious.
}

\eE{
	“You shall not blaspheme God, and you shall not curse a
	chieftain among your people.
}

\eE{
	“You shall not delay your fulfillment and your flowing.
	“You shall give me the firstborn of your sons.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		fulfillment and your flowing. Everyone is unsure of what
%		this means.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	“You shall do this to your ox and to your sheep: Seven
	days it will be with its mother. On the eighth day you
	shall give it to me.
}

\eE{
	“And you shall be holy people to me. “And you shall not
	eat meat in the field that is torn. You shall throw it to
	the dog.
}

\eE{
	“You shall not bring up a false report. Do not join
	your hand with a wicked person to be a malevolent witness.
}

\eE{
	You shall not be following many to do bad. And you shall
	not testify about a dispute to bend following many, to
	bend it.
}

\eE{
	“If you will happen upon your enemy’s ox or his ass
	straying, you shall bring it back to him.
}

\eE{
	If you will see the ass of someone who hates you sagging
	under its burden, and you would hold back from helping
	him: you shall help with him.
}

\eE{
	“You shall not bend the judgment of your poor in his
	dispute.
}

\eE{
	“You shall keep far from a word of a lie, and do not kill
	an innocent and a virtuous person, because I shall not
	vindicate a wicked person.
}

\eE{
	“And you shall not take a bribe, because bribery will
	blind those who can see and will undermine the words of
	the virtuous.
}

\eE{
	“And you shall not oppress an alien—since you know the
	alien’s soul, because you were aliens in the land of
	Egypt.
}

\eE{
	“And six years you shall sow your land and gather its
	produce;
}

\eE{
	and the seventh: you shall let it lie fallow and leave it,
	and your people’s indigent will eat, and what they leave
	the animal of the field will eat. You shall do this to
	your vineyard, to your olives.
}

\eE{
	“Six days you shall do the things you do, and on the
	seventh day you shall cease, so that your ox and your ass
	will rest, and your maid’s son and the alien will be
	refreshed.
}

\eE{
	“And you shall be watchful in everything that I have said
	to you. And you shall not commemorate the name of other
	gods. Let it not be heard on your mouth.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		empty. Meaning: empty-handed. One must go to the
%		designated place and
%		bring a sacrifice.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	“And the Festival of Harvest, the first fruits of what you
	do, of what you will sow in the field. “And the Festival
	of Gathering, at the end of the year, when you gather what
	you have done from the field.
}

\eE{
	“Three times in the year every male of yours shall appear
	in front of the Lord YHWH.
}

\eE{
	“You shall not offer the blood of my sacrifice on leavened
	bread. And the fat of my festival shall not remain until
	morning.
}

\eE{
	“You shall bring the first of the firstfruits of your land
	to the house of YHWH, your God. “You shall not cook a kid
	in its mother’s milk.
}

\eE{
	“Here, I’m sending an angel ahead of you to watch over you
	on the way and to bring you to the place that I’ve
	prepared.
}

\eE{
	Be watchful in front of him and listen to his voice. Don’t
	rebel against him, because he will not bear your offense,
	because my name is within him.
}

\eE{
	But if you will listen to his voice and do everything that
	I speak, then I’ll be an enemy to your enemies and an
	opponent to your opponents.
}

\eE{
	When my angel will go ahead of you and will bring you to
	the Amorite and the Hittite and the Perizzite and the
	Canaanite, the Hivite, and the Jebusite, and I obliterate
	them,
}

\eE{
	you shall not bow to their gods, and you shall not serve
	them, and you shall not do like the things they do; but
	you shall tear them down and shatter their pillars.
}

\eE{
	And you will serve YHWH, your God, and He will bless your
	bread and your water, and I shall turn sickness away from
	within you.
}

\eE{
	There won’t be a bereaved woman and an infertile woman in
	your land. I shall fulfill the number of your days.
}

\eE{
	I shall have my terror go ahead of you, and I shall throw
	all the people against whom you come into tumult, and I
	shall give all your enemies to you by the back.
}

\eE{
	And I shall send the hornet ahead of you, and it will
	drive out the Hivite, the Canaanite, and the Hittite from
	in front of you.
}

\eE{
	I won’t drive them out from in front of you in one year,
	or else the land will be a devastation, and the animal of
	the field will be many at you.
}

\eE{
	Little by little I shall drive them out from in front of
	you, until you will be fruitful, and you will have a
	legacy of the land.
}

\eE{
	And I shall set your border from the Red Sea to the sea of
	the Philistines and from the wilderness to the river,
	because I shall put the residents of the land in your
	hand, and you’ll drive them out from in front of you.
}

\eE{
	You shall not make a covenant with them and with their
	gods.
}

\eE{
	They shall not live in your land, in case they will make
	you sin against me when you’ll serve their gods, because
	it will be a trap for you.”
}

\eE{
	And He said to Moses, “Come up to YHWH: you and Aaron,
	Nadab and Abihu, and seventy of Israel’s elders, and bow
	from a distance.
}

\eE{
	And Moses will come over alone to YHWH, and they shall not
	come over, and the people shall not come up with him.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses came and told the people all of YHWH’s words and
	all the judgments. And all the people answered, one voice,
	and they said, “We’ll do all the things that YHWH has
	spoken.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses wrote all of YHWH’s words. And he got up early
	in the morning and built an altar below the mountain and
	twelve pillars for twelve tribes of Israel.
}

\eE{
	And he sent young men of the children of Israel, and they
	made burnt offerings, and they made peace-offering
	sacrifices to YHWH: bulls.
}

\eE{
	And Moses took half of the blood and set it in basins and
	threw half of the blood on the altar.
}

\eE{
	And he took the scroll of the covenant and read in the
	people’s ears. And they said, “We’ll do everything that
	YHWH has spoken, and we’ll listen.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses took the blood and threw it on the people, and
	he said, “Here is the blood of the covenant that YHWH has
	made with you regarding all these things.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses and Aaron, Nadab and Abihu, and seventy of
	Israel’s elders went up.
}

\eE{
	And they saw the God of Israel. And below His feet it was like a
	structure of sapphire brick and like the essence of the skies for
	clarity.
}

\eE{
	And He did not put out His hand to the chiefs of the
	children of Israel. And they envisioned God. And they ate
	and drank.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Come up to me, to the mountain,
	and be there, and I’ll give you stone tablets and the
	instruction and the commandment that I’ve written to
	instruct them.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses and Joshua, his attendant, got up, and Moses
	went up to the Mountain of God.
}

\eE{
	And he said to the elders, “Sit for us here until we’ll
	come back to you. And here, Aaron and Hur are with you.
	Let whoever has any matters go over to them.”
}

\eE{And Moses went up to the mountain.}
\eP{And the cloud covered
	the mountain.}

\eP{
	And YHWH’s glory settled on Mount Sinai, and the cloud
	covered it six days. And He called to Moses on the seventh
	day from inside the cloud.
}

\eP{
	And the appearance of YHWH’s glory was like a consuming
	fire in the mountaintop before the eyes of the children of
	Israel.
}

tex.original:
\hP{וַיָּבֹא מֹשֶׁה בְּתוֹךְ הֶעָנָן}
\hR{וַיַּעַל אֶל הָהָר}
\hJ{וַיְהִי מֹשֶׁה בָּהָר אַרְבָּעִים יוֹם וְאַרְבָּעִים לָיְלָה׃}

tex.english:
\eP{And Moses came inside the cloud}
\eR{and went up into the mountain.}
\eJ{And Moses was in the mountain forty days and forty nights.}

\eP{
	“Speak to the children of Israel that they shall take a
	donation for me. You shall take my donation from every man
	whose heart will move him.
}

\eP{
	And this is the donation that you shall take from them:
	gold and silver and bronze
}

\eP{
	and rams’ skins dyed red and leather skins and acacia
	wood,
}

\eP{
	oil for lighting, spices for the anointing oil and for the
	incense fragrances,
}

\eP{
	onyx stones and stones to be set for the ephod and for the
	breastplate.
}

\eP{
	And they shall make me a holy place, and I shall tent
	among them.
}

\eP{
	According to everything that I show you: the design of the
	Tabernacle and the design of all its equipment. And you
	shall do so.
}

\eP{
	“And they shall make an ark of acacia wood, its length two
	and a half cubits and its width a cubit and a half and its
	height a cubit and a half.
}

\eP{
	And you shall plate it with pure gold. You shall plate it
	inside and outside. And you shall make a border of gold
	all around on it.
}

\eP{
	And you shall cast four rings of gold for it and place
	them on its four bases: and two rings on its one side and
	two rings on its second side.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make poles of acacia wood and plate them
	with gold,
}

\eP{
	and bring the poles through the rings on the ark’s sides,
	in order to carry the ark with them.
}

\eP{
	The poles shall be in the ark’s rings; they shall not
	depart from it.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place in the ark the Testimony that I shall
	give you.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make an atonement dais of pure gold, its
	length two and a half cubits and its width a cubit and a
	half.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make two cherubs of gold—you shall make them
	of hammered work—at the two ends of the atonement dais.
}

\eP{
	And make one cherub at this end and one cherub at that
	end. You shall make the cherubs from the atonement dais on
	its two sides.
}

\eP{
	And the two cherubs will be spreading wings above,
	covering over the atonement dais with their wings, and
	their faces each toward its brother: the cherubs’ faces
	shall be toward the atonement dais.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place the atonement dais on the ark, from
	above, and you shall place in the ark the Testimony that I
	shall give you.
}

\eP{
	And I shall meet with you there and speak with you from
	above the atonement dais, from between the two cherubs
	that are on the Ark of the Testimony, everything that I
	shall command you to the children of Israel.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		meet. The term denotes meeting at an appointed place or
%		at an appointed
%		time. Thus the Tabernacle itself is known as the Tent of
%		Meeting.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	“And you shall make a table of acacia wood, its length two
	cubits and its width a cubit and its height a cubit and a
	half.
}

\eP{
	And you shall plate it with pure gold and make a border of
	gold all around for it
}

\eP{
	and make a rim of a handbreadth for it all around and make
	a border of gold for its rim all around.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make four rings of gold for it and put the
	rings on the four corners that its four legs have.
}

\eP{
	The rings shall be in juxtaposition to the rim as housings
	for the poles in order to carry the table.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make the poles of acacia wood and plate them
	with gold, and the table will be carried with them.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make its dishes and its pans and its jars
	and its bowls with which libations will be poured. You
	shall make them of pure gold.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place show bread on the table in front of me
	always.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make a menorah of pure gold. The menorah
	shall be made of hammered work—its shaft and its branch,
	its cups, its ornaments, and its flowers shall be part of
	it—
}

\eP{
	and six branches coming out from its sides, three of the
	menorah’s branches from its one side and three of the
	menorah’s branches from its second side,
}

\eP{
	three almond-shaped cups in the one branch, with ornament
	and flower, and three almond-shaped cups in the other
	branch, with ornament and flower. So it is for the six
	branches that come out from the menorah,
}

\eP{
	and four almond-shaped cups within the menorah, with its
	ornaments and its flowers,
}

\eP{
	and an ornament under the two branches from it, and an
	ornament under the two branches from it, and an ornament
	under the two branches from it, for the six branches that
	come out from the menorah.
}

\eP{
	Their ornaments and their branches shall be part of it,
	all of it one hammered work of pure gold.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make its lamps seven, and it will hold up
	its lamps and light the area in front of it.
}

\eP{
	He shall make it—all of these items—of a talent of pure
	gold.
}

\eP{
	And see and make them by their design that you are shown
	in the mountain.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make the Tabernacle with ten curtains of
	woven linen and blue and purple and scarlet, with
	cherubs—you shall make them designer’s work.
}

\eP{
	The length of one curtain shall be twenty-eight in cubits,
	and the width of one curtain four in cubits: one size to
	all the curtains.
}

\eP{
	Five curtains will be connected, each to its sister-piece,
	and five curtains connected, each to its sister-piece.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make loops of blue on the side of the one
	curtain at the end of the connected group, and you shall
	do so in the side of the end curtain in the second
	connected group.
}

\eP{
	You shall make fifty loops in the end of the curtain that
	is in the second connected group, with the loops parallel:
	each to its sister-piece.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make fifty clasps of gold and connect the
	curtains, each to its sister-piece, with the clasps. And
	the Tabernacle will be one.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make curtains of goats’ hair for a tent
	over the Tabernacle. You shall make them eleven curtains.
}

\eP{
	The length of one curtain shall be thirty in cubits, and
	the width of one curtain four in cubits: one size for
	eleven curtains.
}

\eP{
	And you shall connect five of the curtains by themselves
	and six of the curtains by themselves. And you shall
	double-fold the sixth curtain opposite the front of the
	tent.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make fifty loops on the side of the one end
	curtain in the connected group and fifty loops on the side
	of the curtain of the second connected group.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make fifty clasps of bronze and bring the
	clasps into the loops and connect the tent. And it will be
	one.
}

\eP{
	And the hanging one that is left over among the tent’s
	curtains: you shall hang half of the leftover curtain on
	the back parts of the Tabernacle.
}

\eP{
	And the cubit on this side and the cubit on that side in
	the leftover in the length of the tent’s curtains shall be
	hung on the sides of the Tabernacle, on this side and on
	that side, to cover it.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make a covering for the tent of rams’ skins
	dyed red and a covering of leather skins above.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make the frames for the Tabernacle of
	acacia wood, standing.
}

\eP{
	The frame’s length shall be ten cubits, and the width of
	one frame a cubit and a half cubit.
}

\eP{
	One frame has two projections, each aligned with its
	sister-piece: so you shall make for all the Tabernacle’s
	frames.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make the frames for the Tabernacle: twenty
	frames for the south side—to the south.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make forty bases of silver under the twenty
	frames: two bases under one frame for its two projections
	and two bases under each other frame for its two
	projections.
}

\eP{
	And for the Tabernacle’s second side, for the north side:
	twenty frames
}

\eP{
	and their forty bases of silver, two bases under one frame
	and two bases under each other frame.
}

\eP{
	And for the rear of the Tabernacle, to the west, you shall
	make six frames.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make two frames for the Tabernacle’s corners
	in the rear,
}

\eP{
	and they shall be doubles from below, and together they
	shall be integrated on its top to one ring. It shall be so
	for the two of them; they will be for the two corners.
}

\eP{
	And they shall be eight frames and their bases of silver,
	sixteen bases, two bases under one frame and two bases
	under each other frame.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make bars of acacia wood: five for the
	frames of one side of the Tabernacle
}

\eP{
	and five bars for the frames of the second side of the
	Tabernacle and five bars for the frames of the side of the
	Tabernacle at the rear, to the west,
}

\eP{
	and the middle bar through the frames extending from end
	to end.
}

\eP{
	And you shall plate the frames with gold, and you shall
	make their rings gold, housings for the bars, and you
	shall plate the bars with gold.
}

\eP{
	And you shall set up the Tabernacle according to the model
	of it that you were shown in the mountain.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make a pavilion of blue and purple and
	scarlet and woven linen; he shall make them designer’s
	work with cherubs.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place it on four acacia columns plated with
	gold, their hooks of gold, on four bases of silver.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place the pavilion under the clasps. And you
	shall bring the Ark of the Testimony there, inside the
	pavilion, and the pavilion will distinguish for you
	between the Holy and the Holy of Holies.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place the atonement dais on the Ark of the
	Testimony in the Holy of Holies.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place the table outside the pavilion, and
	the menorah opposite the table on the south side of the
	Tabernacle, and you shall place the table on the north
	side.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make a cover for the entrance of the tent:
	blue and purple and scarlet and woven linen—embroiderer’s
	work.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make five acacia columns for the cover and
	plate them with gold, their hooks of gold, and cast five
	bases of bronze for them.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make the altar of acacia wood. Five
	cubits long and five cubits wide, the altar shall be
	square—and its height three cubits.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make its horns on its four corners. Its
	horns shall be part of it. And you shall plate it with
	bronze.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make its pots to remove ashes from it, and
	its shovels and its basins and its forks and its
	fire-holders. You shall make all of its equipment bronze.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make a grate for it, a network of bronze,
	and on the net you shall make four rings of bronze on its
	four ends.
}

\eP{
	And you shall place it under the altar’s ledge below so
	that the net will be up to the middle of the altar.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make poles for the altar, poles of acacia
	wood, and plate them with bronze.
}

\eP{
	And its poles shall be brought through rings so that the
	poles shall be on the altar’s two sides when carrying it.
}

\eP{
	You shall make it hollow of boards, as it was shown to you
	in the mountain. They shall do it so.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make the Tabernacle’s courtyard: for the
	south side—to the south, hangings for the courtyard of
	woven linen, one side having a hundred in cubits,
}

\eP{
	and its columns twenty, and their bases twenty of bronze,
	and the columns’ hooks and their rods of silver.
}

\eP{
	And so for the north side: in length, hangings of a
	hundred long, and its columns twenty, and their bases
	twenty of bronze, and the columns’ hooks and their bands
	of silver.
}

\eP{
	And the courtyard’s width for the west side: hangings of
	fifty cubits, their columns ten and their bases ten.
}

\eP{
	And the courtyard’s width for the east side—to the
	east—fifty cubits.
}

\eP{
	And the panel shall have fifteen cubits of hangings, their
	columns three and their bases three;
}

\eP{
	and the second panel shall have fifteen of hangings, their
	columns three and their bases three.
}

\eP{
	And the courtyard’s gate shall have a cover of twenty
	cubits: blue and purple and scarlet and woven linen,
	embroiderer’s work, their columns four and their bases
	four.
}

\eP{
	All of the courtyard’s columns all around shall be banded
	with silver, their hooks of silver and their bases bronze.
}

\eP{
	The courtyard’s length a hundred in cubits, and width
	consistently fifty, and height five cubits of woven linen,
	and their bases of bronze.
}

\eP{
	For all of the Tabernacle’s equipment in all of the
	service of it, and all of its pegs and all of the
	courtyard’s pegs: bronze.
}

\eP{
	COMMAND “And you: you shall command the children of Israel
	that they shall bring clear pressed olive oil to you for
	the light, to keep up a lamp always.
}

\eP{
	In the Tent of Meeting, outside the pavilion that is over
	the Testimony, Aaron and his sons shall arrange it from
	evening until morning in front of YHWH, an eternal law
	through their generations from the children of Israel.
}

\eP{
	“And you, bring Aaron, your brother, forward to you,
	and his sons with him, from among the children of Israel,
	for him to function as a priest for me: Aaron, Nadab and
	Abihu, Eleazar and Ithamar, Aaron’s sons.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make holy clothes for Aaron, your brother,
	for glory and for beauty
}

\eP{
	And you shall speak to all the wise of heart whom I have
	filled with a spirit of wisdom, that they shall make
	Aaron’s clothes, to sanctify him for him to function as a
	priest for me.
}

\eP{
	And they shall take the gold and the blue and the purple
	and the scarlet and the linen.
}

\eP{
	“And they shall make the ephod: gold, blue, and purple,
	scarlet, and woven linen, designer’s work.
}

\eP{
	It shall have two connected shoulder-pieces at its two
	ends, and it will be connected.
}

\eP{
	And the ephod’s designed belt that is on it shall be like
	its work and be a part of it: gold, blue, and purple, and
	scarlet and woven linen.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take two onyx stones and inscribe the names
	of the children of Israel on them:
}

\eP{
	six of their names on one stone and the names of the
	remaining six on the second stone, according to their
	birth orders.
}

\eP{
	By a stone engraver’s work you shall inscribe the two
	stones with signet inscriptions according to the names of
	the children of Israel. You shall make them surrounded by
	settings of gold.
}

\eP{
	And you shall set the two stones on the ephod’s
	shoulder-pieces, commemorative stones for the children of
	Israel, and Aaron shall carry their names in front of YHWH
	on his two shoulders as a commemoration.
}

\eP{
	and two chains of pure gold. You shall make them twisted,
	rope work, and put the rope chains on the settings.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make a breastplate of judgment, designer’s
	work. You shall make it like the work of the ephod. You
	shall make it gold, blue, and purple and scarlet and woven
	linen.
}

\eP{
	It shall be square when doubled, its length a span and its
	width a span.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put a mounting of stones in it: four rows of stone. A
	row of a carnelian, a topaz, and an emerald: the one row.
}

\eP{
	And the fourth row: a beryl and an onyx and a jasper. They
	shall be set in gold in their mountings.
}

\eP{
	And the stones shall be with the names of the children of
	Israel, twelve with their names; they shall be signet
	engravings, each with its name, for twelve tribes.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make twisted chains, rope work, of pure
	gold, on the breastplate.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make two rings of gold on the breastplate
	and put the two rings on the two ends of the breastplate
}

\eP{
	and put the two ropes of gold on the two rings at the ends
	of the breastplate.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put the two ends of the two ropes on the two
	settings, and you shall put them on the ephod’s
	shoulder-pieces opposite the front of it.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make two rings of gold and set them on the
	breastplate’s two ends on its side that is adjacent to the
	ephod, inward.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make two rings of gold and put them on the
	ephod’s two shoulder-pieces below, opposite the front of
	it, by its connection, above the ephod’s designed belt.
}

\eP{
	And they shall attach the breastplate from its rings to
	the ephod’s rings with a blue string so it will be on the
	ephod’s designed belt and the breastplate will not be
	detached from the ephod.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall carry the names of the children of Israel
	in the breastplate of judgment on his heart when he comes
	into the Holy, as a commemorative in front of YHWH always.
}

\eP{
	And its head-opening shall be within it. It shall have a
	binding for its opening all around, weaver’s work. It
	shall be like the opening of a coat of mail for him; it
	will not be torn.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make on its skirts pomegranates of blue and
	purple and scarlet, on its skirts all around, and bells of
	gold within them all around:
}

\eP{
	a golden bell and a pomegranate, a golden bell and a
	pomegranate, on the robe’s skirts all around.
}

\eP{
	And it shall be on Aaron for ministering: and the sound of
	it will be heard when he comes to the Holy in front of
	YHWH and when he goes out, so he will not die.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make a plate of pure gold and make signet
	inscriptions on it: ‘Holiness to YHWH.’
}

\eP{
	And you shall set it on a blue string, and it shall be on
	the headdress. It shall be opposite the front of the
	headdress.
}

\eP{
	And it shall be on Aaron’s forehead, and Aaron shall bear the crime
	of the holy things that the children of Israel will sanctify, for
	all their holy gifts. And it shall be on his forehead always for
	acceptance for them in front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And you shall weave the coat in a pattern of linen and
	make a headdress of linen. And you shall make a sash,
	embroiderer’s work,
}

\eP{
	and you shall make coats for Aaron’s sons, and you shall
	make sashes for them, and you shall make hats for them—for
	glory and for beauty.
}

\eP{
	And you shall dress them—Aaron, your brother, and his sons
	with him—and you shall anoint them and fill their hand and
	sanctify them, and they shall function as priests for me.
}

\eP{
	And make shorts of linen for them, to cover naked flesh;
	they shall be from hips to thighs.
}

\eP{
	“And this is the thing that you shall do to them to
	sanctify them to function as priests for me: Take one bull
	of the cattle and two unblemished rams
}

\eP{
	and unleavened bread and unleavened cakes mixed with oil
	and unleavened wafers with oil poured on them—you shall
	make them of fine flour of wheat.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put them on one basket and bring them
	forward in the basket—and the bull and the two rams.
}

\eP{
	And you shall bring Aaron and his sons forward to the Tent
	of Meeting and wash them with water.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the clothes and dress Aaron with the
	coat and with the ephod’s robe and with the ephod and with
	the breastplate, and you shall put on the ephod for him
	with the ephod’s designed belt.
}

\eP{
	And you shall set the headdress on his head and put the
	crown of Holiness on the headdress.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		crown of Holiness. It is clarified later (Exod 39:30;
%		Lev 8:9) that this
%		is the golden plate that has the words “Holiness to
%		YHWH” inscribed on
%		it, as described in Exod 28:36. Like a king (2 Sam 1:10;
%		2 Kings 11:12),
%		the high priest wears a crown.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And you shall take the anointing oil and pour it on his
	head and anoint him.
}

\eP{
	And you shall bring his sons forward, and you shall dress
	them in coats
}

\eP{
	and you shall belt them with a sash, Aaron and his sons,
	and put hats on for them. And they shall have priesthood
	as an eternal law. And you shall fill Aaron’s hand and his
	sons’ hand.
}

\eP{
	And you shall bring the bull forward in front of the Tent
	of Meeting, and Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands
	on the bull’s head.
}

\eP{
	And you shall slaughter the bull in front of YHWH at the
	entrance of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take some of the bull’s blood and put it on
	the horns of the altar with your finger, and you shall
	spill all of the blood at the base of the altar.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take all of the fat that covers the inside
	and the appendage on the liver and the two kidneys and the
	fat that is on them, and you shall burn them into smoke at
	the altar.
}

\eP{
	And you shall burn the meat of the bull and its skin and
	its dung in fire outside the camp. It is a sin offering.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the one ram, and Aaron and his sons
	shall lay their hands on the ram’s head.
}

\eP{
	And you shall slaughter the ram and take its blood and
	fling it on the altar all around.
}

\eP{
	And you shall cut up the ram into its parts, and you shall
	wash its inside and its legs and put them on its parts and
	on its head.
}

\eP{
	And you shall burn all of the ram to smoke at the altar.
	It is a burnt offering to YHWH. It is a pleasant smell, an
	offering by fire to YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the second ram, and Aaron and his sons
	shall lay their hands on the ram’s head.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the fat from the ram and the fat tail
	and the fat that covers the inside and the appendage of
	the liver and the two kidneys and the fat that is on them
	and the right thigh—because it is a ram of ordination—
}

\eP{
	and one loaf of bread and one cake of bread with oil and
	one wafer from the basket of unleavened bread that is in
	front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put it all on Aaron’s hands and on his sons’
	hands and elevate them as an elevation offering in front
	of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take them from their hand and burn them to
	smoke at the altar in addition to the burnt offering as a
	pleasant smell in front of YHWH. It is an offering by fire
	to YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the breast from the ram of ordination
	that is Aaron’s and elevate it as an elevation offering in
	front of YHWH, and it shall be a portion for you.
}

\eP{
	And you shall sanctify the breast that is an elevation
	offering and the thigh that is a donation, that is
	elevated and that is donated, from the ram of ordination,
	from Aaron’s and from his sons’.
}

\eP{
	And the holy clothes that are Aaron’s shall be his sons’
	after him, to be anointed in them and to fill their hand
	in them.
}

\eP{
	The priest in his place from among his sons, who will come
	to the Tent of Meeting to minister in the Holy, shall wear
	them for seven days.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the ram of ordination and cook its meat
	in a holy place,
}

\eP{
	and Aaron and his sons shall eat the meat of the ram and
	the bread that is in the basket at the entrance of the
	Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And those who have acquired atonement by them shall eat
	them, to fill their hand, to make them holy; and an
	outsider shall not eat, because they are holy.
}

\eP{
	And if some of the meat of the ordination or some of the
	bread will be left until the morning, then you shall burn
	what is left in fire. It shall not be eaten, because it is
	holy.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall do thus to Aaron and to his sons, according
	to all that I have commanded you. You shall fill their
	hand for seven days.
}

\eP{
	And you shall do a bull sin offering per day for
	atonement, and you shall make a sin offering on the altar
	when you make atonement for it, and you shall anoint it to
	make it holy.
}

\eP{
	Seven days you shall make atonement on the altar and make
	it holy, and the altar shall be holy of holies. Anyone who
	touches the altar will be holy.
}

\eP{
	“And this is what you shall do on the altar: year-old
	lambs, two per day, always.
}

\eP{
	You shall do the one sheep in the morning and do the
	second sheep between the two evenings.
}

\eP{
	And a tenth of a measure of fine flour mixed with a fourth
	of a hin of pressed oil and a libation of a fourth of a
	hin of wine for the first lamb.
}

\eP{
	And you shall do the second lamb between the two evenings.
	You shall do it like the morning grain offering and like
	its libation for a pleasant smell, an offering by fire to
	YHWH,
}

\eP{
	a continual burnt offering through your generations at the
	entrance of the Tent of Meeting in front of YHWH, where I
	shall meet with you to speak to you there.
}

\eP{
	And I shall meet there with the children of Israel, and it
	will be made holy through my glory.
}

\eP{
	And I shall make the Tent of Meeting and the altar holy,
	and I shall make Aaron and his sons holy to function as
	priests for me.
}

\eP{
	And I shall tent among the children of Israel, and I shall
	be God to them.
}

\eP{
	And they will know that I am YHWH, their God, who brought them
	out from the land of Egypt for me to tent among them. I am YHWH,
	their God.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make an altar for burning incense. You
	shall make it of acacia wood.
}

\eP{
	Its length a cubit and its width a cubit, it shall be
	square, and its height two cubits, its horns a part of it.
}

\eP{
	And you shall plate it with pure gold, its roof and its
	walls all around and its horns. And you shall make a
	border of gold for it all around.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make two rings of gold for it below its rim
	on its two sides; you shall make it on its two sidewalls,
	and it will be for housings for poles with which to carry
	it.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make the poles of acacia wood and plate them
	with gold.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put it in front of the pavilion that is over
	the Ark of the Testimony, in front of the atonement dais
	that is over the Testimony, where I shall meet with you.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall burn incense of fragrances on it. Morning
	by morning, when he attends to the lamps, he shall burn
	it,
}

\eP{
	and when Aaron puts up the lamps between the two evenings
	he shall burn it: a continual incense in front of YHWH
	through your generations.
}

\eP{
	You shall not offer unfitting incense or a burnt offering
	or a grain offering on it, and you shall not pour a
	libation on it.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall make atonement on its horns once per year;
	he shall make atonement on it with the blood of the sin
	offering of atonement once per year through your
	generations. It is holy of holies to YHWH.”
}

\eP{
	“When you add up the heads of the children of Israel by
	their counts, each of them shall give a ransom for his
	life to YHWH when counting them, so there will not be a
	plague among them when counting them.
}

\eP{
	Everyone who passes through the counts shall give this:
	half of a shekel, by the shekel of the Holy (the shekel is
	twenty gerah); half of a shekel as a donation to YHWH.
}

\eP{
	Everyone who passes through the counts, from twenty years
	old and up, shall give YHWH’s donation.
}

\eP{
	The rich one shall not multiply, and the poor one shall
	not diminish from the half of a shekel, to give YHWH’s
	donation, to make atonement for your lives.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall make a basin of bronze and its stand of
	bronze for washing, and you shall put it between the Tent
	of Meeting and the altar and put water there.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron and his sons will wash their hands and their
	feet from it.
}

\eP{
	When they come to the Tent of Meeting they shall wash with
	water, and they will not die—or when they come near to the
	altar to minister, to burn an offering by fire to YHWH to
	smoke.
}

\eP{
	And they shall wash their hands and their feet, and they
	will not die. And it will be an eternal law for them—for
	him and for his seed through their generations.”
}

\eP{
	“And you, take spices, choice: flowing myrrh, five
	hundred; and aromatic cinnamon, half as much, two hundred
	fifty; and aromatic reed, two hundred fifty;
}

\eP{
	and cassia, five hundred—by the shekel of the Holy—and
	olive oil, a hin.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make it oil of holy anointing, an ointment
	mixture, the work of an ointment-maker. It shall be oil of
	holy anointing.
}

\eP{
	And you shall anoint the Tent of Meeting and the Ark of
	the Testimony with it,
}

\eP{
	and the table and all its equipment and the menorah and
	its equipment and the incense altar
}

\eP{
	and the altar of burnt offering and all its equipment and
	the basin and its stand.
}

\eP{
	And you shall make them holy, and they shall be holy of
	holies. Anyone who touches them will be holy.
}

\eP{
	And you shall anoint Aaron and his sons and make them holy
	to function as priests for me.
}

\eP{
	And you shall speak to the children of Israel, saying,
	‘This shall be oil of holy anointing for me through your
	generations.
}

\eP{
	It shall not be poured on human flesh, and you shall not
	make anything else like it in its quantities. It is a holy
	thing. It shall be a holy thing to you.
}

\eP{
	Anyone who will make a compound like it and who will give
	some of it to an outsider: he will be cut off from his
	people.’”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		cut off from his people. The meaning of this phrase is
%		not known for
%		certain. See the comment on Gen 17:14.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Take fragrances—stacte and onycha
	and galbanum—fragrances and clear frankincense; it shall
	be part for part.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		part for part. Meaning: there are to be equal portions
%		of each of the
%		ingredients in the compound.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And you shall make it ointment incense, the work of an
	ointment-maker: salted, pure, holy.
}

\eP{
	And you shall pound some of it, making it thin, and put
	some of it in front of the Testimony in the Tent of
	Meeting where I shall meet with you. It shall be holy of
	holies to you.
}

\eP{
	And the incense that you will make: you shall not make for
	yourselves in its quantities. It shall be a holy thing to
	you for YHWH.
}

\eP{
	Anyone who will make anything like it to make a fragrance
	with it will be cut off from his people.”
}

\eP{
	“See, I’ve called by name Bezalel, son of Uri, son of Hur,
	of the tribe of Judah.
}

\eP{
	And I’ve filled him with the spirit of God in wisdom and
	in understanding and in knowledge and in every kind of
	work,
}

\eP{
	to form conceptions to make in gold and in silver and in
	bronze
}

\eP{
	and in cutting stone for setting and in cutting wood—for
	making things in every kind of work.
}

\eP{
	And I: here, I’ve put Oholiab, son of Ahisamach, of the
	tribe of Dan, with him. And in the heart of every
	wise-hearted person I’ve put wisdom. And they’ll make
	everything that I’ve commanded you:
}

\eP{
	the Tent of Meeting and the Ark of the Testimony and the
	atonement dais that is on it and all the equipment of the
	tent
}

\eP{
	and the table and its equipment and the pure menorah and
	all its equipment and the incense altar
}

\eP{
	and the burnt offering altar and all its equipment and the
	basin and its stand
}

\eP{
	and the fabric clothes and the holy clothes for Aaron, the
	priest, and his sons’ clothes for functioning as priests
}

\eP{
	and the anointing oil and the incense of fragrances for
	the Holy. According to everything that I’ve commanded you,
	they shall do.”
}

\eP{
	“And you, speak to the children of Israel, saying, ‘Just:
	you shall observe my Sabbaths, because it is a sign
	between me and you through your generations: to know that
	I, YHWH, make you holy.
}

\eP{
	And you shall observe the Sabbath, because it is a holy thing to
	you. One who desecrates it shall be put to death. Because anyone who
	does work in it: that person will be cut off from among his people.
}

\eP{
	Six days work shall be done, and in the seventh day is a
	Sabbath, a ceasing, a holy thing to YHWH. Anyone who does
	work in the Sabbath day shall be put to death.
}

\eP{
	And the children of Israel shall observe the Sabbath, to
	make the Sabbath through their generations, an eternal
	covenant.
}

\eP{
	Between me and the children of Israel it is a sign
	forever, because for six days YHWH made the skies and the
	earth, and in the seventh day He ceased and was
	refreshed.’”
}

\eP{
	And when He finished speaking with him in Mount Sinai, He
	gave the two tablets of the Testimony to Moses, tablets of
	stone, written by the finger of God.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		two tablets of the Testimony. There are two tablets of
%		testimony/witness, and there must always be at least two
%		witnesses to
%		give testimony in law. And Moses names two witnesses,
%		heaven and earth,
%		concerning the people’s future at the end of the Torah
%		(Deut 31:28;
%		32:1).
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And Aaron said to them, “Take off the gold rings that are
	in your wives’, your sons’, and your daughters’ ears, and
	bring them to me.”
}

\eE{
	And all the people took off the gold rings that were in
	their ears and brought them to Aaron.
}

\eE{
	And he took them from their hand and fashioned it with a
	stylus and made it a molten calf. And they said, “These
	are your gods, Israel, who brought you up from the land of
	Egypt!”
}

\eE{
	And Aaron saw, and he built an altar in front of it, and
	Aaron called, and he said, “A festival to YHWH tomorrow!”
}

\eE{
	And they got up early the next day, and they made burnt
	offerings and brought over peace offerings, and the people
	sat down to eat and drink, and they got up to fool around.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		fool around. Some of the occurrences of this word
%		involve sex (Gen 26:8;
%		39:14,17), and some do not (Gen 19:14; 21:9; Judg
%		16:25). It is not
%		justified, therefore, to assume that sexual play is
%		implied here.
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And YHWH spoke to Moses: “Go. Go down. Because your
	people, whom you brought up from the land of Egypt, has
	corrupted.
}

\eE{
	They’ve turned quickly from the way that I commanded them.
	They’ve made themselves a molten calf, and they’ve bowed
	to it and sacrificed to it and said, ‘These are your gods,
	Israel, who brought you up from the land of Egypt!’”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “I’ve seen this people; and, here,
	it’s a hard-necked people.
}

\eE{
	And now, leave off from me, and my anger will flare at
	them, and I’ll finish them, and I’ll make you into a big
	nation!”
}

\eE{
	And Moses conciliated in front of YHWH, his God, and said,
	“Why, YHWH, should your anger flare at your people whom
	you brought out from the land of Egypt with big power and
	with a strong hand?
}

\eE{
	Why should Egypt say, saying, ‘He brought them out for
	bad, to kill them in the mountains, and to finish them
	from on the face of the earth’? Turn back from your
	flaring anger, and relent about the bad to your people.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH relented about the bad that He had spoken, to do
	to His people.
}

\eE{
	And Moses turned and went down from the mountain. And the
	two tablets of witness were in his hand, tablets written
	from their two sides: from this side and from this side
	they were written.
}

\eE{
	And the tablets: they were God’s doing. And the writing:
	it was God’s writing, inscribed on the tablets.
}

\eE{
	And Joshua heard the sound of the people in its shouting,
	and he said to Moses, “A sound of war is in the camp.”
}

\eE{
	And he said, “It’s not a sound of singing of victory, and
	it’s not a sound of singing of defeat. It’s just the sound
	of singing I hear!”
}

\eE{
	And it was when he came close to the camp, and he saw the
	calf and dancing: and Moses’ anger flared, and he threw
	the tablets from his hands and shattered them below the
	mountain.
}

\eE{
	And he took the calf that they had made, and he burned it
	in fire and ground it until it was thin, and he scattered
	it on the face of the water, and he made the children of
	Israel drink!
}

\eE{
	And Moses said to Aaron, “What did this people do to you,
	that you’ve brought a big sin on it?!”
}

\eE{
	And Aaron said, “Let my lord’s anger not flare. You know
	the people, that it’s in a bad state.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		in a bad state. In the sense of “in for trouble,” “up to
%		no good,” or
%		“in a sorry state.”
%	}

tex.english:
\eE{
	And they said to me, ‘Make gods for us who will go in
	front of us, because this Moses, the man who brought us up
	from the land of Egypt: we don’t know what has become of
	him!’
}

\eE{
	And I said to them, ‘Whoever has gold: take it off.’ And
	they gave it to me, and I threw it into the fire, and this
	calf came out!”
}

\eE{
	And Moses saw the people, that it was turned loose,
	because Aaron had turned it loose—to denigration among
	their adversaries!
}

\eE{
	And Moses stood in the gate of the camp and said, “Whoever
	is for YHWH: to me!” And all the children of Levi were
	gathered to him.
}

\eE{
	And the children of Levi did according to Moses’ word, and
	about three thousand men fell from the people in that day.
}

\eE{
	And Moses said, “Fill your hand to YHWH today—because each
	man was at his son and at his brother—and to put a
	blessing on you today.”
}

\eE{
	And it was on the next day, and Moses said to the people,
	“You’ve committed a big sin. And now I’ll go up to YHWH.
	Perhaps I may make atonement for your sin.”
}

\eE{
	And Moses went back to YHWH and said, “Please, this people
	has committed a big sin and made gods of gold for
	themselves.
}

\eE{
	And now, if you will bear their sin—and if not, wipe me
	out from your scroll that you’ve written.”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “The one who has sinned against
	me, I’ll wipe him out from my scroll.
}

\eE{
	And now, go. Lead the people to where I spoke to you.
	Here, my angel will go ahead of you. And, in the day that
	I take account, I’ll account their sin on them.”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH struck the people because they had made the calf,
	which Aaron had made.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH spoke to Moses: “Go. Go up from here, you and
	the people whom you brought up from the land of Egypt, to
	the land that I swore to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob,
	saying, ‘I’ll give it to your seed.’
}

\eE{
	And I’ll send an angel ahead of you—and I’ll drive out the
	Canaanite, the Amorite, and the Hittite and the Perizzite
	and the Hivite and the Jebusite—
}

\eE{
	to a land flowing with milk and honey, because I won’t go
	up among you, because you’re a hard-necked people, or else
	I’ll finish you on the way.”
}

\eE{
	And the people heard this bad thing, and they mourned, and
	not a man put his jewelry on him.
}

\eE{
	And the children of Israel divested their jewelry from
	Mount Horeb.
}

\eE{
	And it would be, when Moses would go out to the Tent, all
	the people would get up, and they would stand up, each one
	at the entrance of his tent, and they would look after
	Moses until he came to the Tent.
}

\eE{
	And it would be, when Moses came to the Tent, the column
	of cloud would come down, and it would stand at the
	entrance of the Tent, and He would speak with Moses.
}

\eE{
	And all the people would see the column of cloud standing
	at the entrance of the Tent, and all the people would get
	up and bow, each at the entrance of his tent.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH would speak to Moses face-to-face, the way a man
	speaks to his fellow man. And he would come back to the
	camp. And his attendant, Joshua, son of Nun, a young man,
	would not depart from inside the Tent.
}

\eE{
	And Moses said to YHWH, “See, you say to me, ‘Bring this
	people up,’ and you haven’t made known to me whom you will
	send with me. And you’ve said, ‘I’ve known you by name,’
	and also ‘You’ve found favor in my eyes.’
}

\eE{
	And now, if I’ve found favor in your eyes, make your way
	known to me, so I may know you, so that I’ll find favor in
	your eyes. And see that this nation is your people.”
}

\eE{
	And he said to Him, “If your face isn’t going, don’t take
	us up from here.
}

\eE{
	And by what then will it be known that I’ve found favor in
	your eyes, I and your people? Is it not by your going with
	us? And we’ll be distinguished, I and your people, from
	every people that is on the face of the earth.”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “I’ll do this thing that you’ve
	spoken as well, because you’ve found favor in my eyes, and
	I’ve known you by name.”
}

\eE{
	And He said, “I shall have all my good pass in front of
	you, and I shall invoke the name YHWH in front of you. And
	I shall show grace to whomever I shall show grace, and I
	shall show mercy to whomever I shall show mercy.”
}

\eE{
	And He said, “You won’t be able to see my face, because a
	human will not see me and live.”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said, “Here is a place with me, and you’ll stand
	up on a rock;
}

\eE{
	and it will be, when my glory passes, that I’ll set you in
	a cleft of the rock, and I’ll cover my hand over you until
	I’ve passed,
}

\eE{
	and I’ll turn my hand away, and you’ll see my back, but my
	face won’t be seen.”
}

\eJ{And YHWH said to Moses, “Carve two tablets of stones}
\eRJE{like the first ones, and I’ll write on the tablets the
	words that were on the first tablets, which you shattered.}

\eJ{
	And be ready for the morning, and you shall go up in the
	morning to Mount Sinai and present yourself to me there on
	the top of the mountain.
}

\eJ{
	And no man shall go up with you, and also let no man be
	seen in all of the mountain. Also let the flock and the
	oxen not feed opposite that mountain.”
}

tex.original:
\hJE{וַיִּפְסֹל שְׁנֵי לֻחֹת אֲבָנִים}
\hRJE{כָּרִאשֹׁנִים}
\hJE{וַיַּשְׁכֵּם מֹשֶׁה בַבֹּקֶר וַיַּעַל אֶל הַר סִינַי כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה יְהֹוָה אֹתוֹ וַיִּקַּח בְּיָדוֹ שְׁנֵי לֻחֹת אֲבָנִים׃}

tex.english:
\eJE{And he carved two tablets of stones}
\eRJE{like the first ones,}
\eJE{and Moses got up early in the morning and went up to Mount
	Sinai as YHWH had commanded him, and he took in his hand
	two tablets of stones.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH came down in a cloud and stood with him there,
	and he invoked the name YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH passed in front of him and called, YHWH, YHWH,
	merciful and gracious God, slow to anger and abounding in
	kindness and faithfulness,
}

\eJ{
	keeping kindness for thousands, bearing crime and offense and sin;
	though not making one innocent: reckoning fathers’ crime on children
	and on children’s children, on third generations and on fourth
	generations.
}

\eJ{
	and he said, “If I’ve found favor in your eyes, my Lord,
	may my Lord go among us, because it is a stiff-necked
	people, and forgive our crime and our sin, and make us
	your legacy.”
}

\eJ{
	Watch yourself regarding what I command you today. Here,
	I’m driving out from before you the Amorite and the
	Canaanite and the Hittite and the Perizzite and the Hivite
	and the Jebusite.
}

\eJ{
	Be watchful of yourself that you don’t make a covenant
	with the resident of the land onto which you’re coming,
	that he doesn’t become a trap among you.
}

\eJ{
	But you shall demolish their altars and shatter their
	pillars and cut down their Asherahs.
}

\eJ{
	For you shall not bow to another god—because YHWH: His
	name is Jealous, He is a jealous God—
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		a jealous God. See the comment on Exod 20:5.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	that you not make a covenant with the resident of the
	land, and they will prostitute themselves after their gods
	and sacrifice to their gods, and he will call to you, and
	you will eat from his sacrifice.
}

\eJ{
	And you will take some of his daughters for your sons, and
	his daughters will prostitute themselves after their gods
	and cause your sons to prostitute themselves after their
	gods.
}

\eJ{
	You shall observe the Festival of Unleavened Bread. Seven
	days you shall eat unleavened bread, which I commanded
	you, at the appointed time, the month of Abib; because in
	the month of Abib you went out of Egypt.
}

\eJ{
	Every first birth of a womb is mine, and all your animals
	that have a male first birth, ox or sheep.
}

\eJ{
	And you shall redeem an ass’s first birth with a sheep,
	and if you do not redeem it then you shall break its neck.
	You shall redeem every firstborn of your sons. And none
	shall appear before me empty-handed.
}

\eJ{
	Six days you shall work, and in the seventh day you shall
	cease. In plowing time and in harvest, you shall cease.
}

\eJ{
	And you shall make a Festival of Weeks, of the firstfruits
	of the wheat harvest, and the Festival of Gathering at the
	end of the year.
}

\eJ{
	Three times in the year every one of your males shall
	appear before the Lord YHWH, God of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	For I shall dispossess nations before you and widen your
	border, and no man will covet your land while you are
	going up to appear before YHWH, your God, three times in
	the year.
}

\eJ{
	You shall not offer the blood of my sacrifice on leavened
	bread. And the sacrifice of the Festival of Passover shall
	not remain until the morning.
}

\eJ{
	You shall bring the first of the firstfruits of your land
	to the house of YHWH, your God. You shall not cook a kid
	in its mother’s milk.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Write these words for yourself,
	because I’ve made a covenant with you and with Israel
	based on these words.”
}

\eJ{
	And he was there with YHWH forty days and forty nights. He
	did not eat bread, and he did not drink water. And he
	wrote on the tablets the words of the covenant, the Ten
	Commandments.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron and all the children of Israel saw Moses; and,
	here, the skin of his face was transformed, and they were
	afraid of going over to him.
}

\eP{
	And Moses called to them. And Aaron and all the chiefs in
	the congregation came back to him, and he spoke to them.
}

\eP{
	And after that all the children of Israel went over. And
	he commanded them everything that YHWH had spoken with him
	in Mount Sinai.
}

\eP{
	And Moses finished speaking with them, and he put a veil
	on his face.
}

\eP{
	And when Moses would come in front of YHWH to speak with
	Him, he would turn away the veil until he would go out;
	and he would go out and speak to the children of Israel
	what he had been commanded.
}

\eP{
	And the children of Israel would see Moses’ face, that the
	skin of Moses’ face was transformed, and Moses would put
	back the veil on his face until he would come to speak
	with Him.
}

\eP{
	And Moses assembled all of the congregation of the
	children of Israel and said to them, “These are the things
	that YHWH commanded, to do them:
}

\eP{
	Six days work shall be done, and in the seventh day you
	shall have a holy thing, a Sabbath, a ceasing to YHWH.
	Anyone who does work in shall be put to death.
}

\eP{
	You shall not burn a fire in all of your homes on the
	Sabbath day.”
}

\eP{
	And Moses said to all of the congregation of the children
	of Israel, saying, “This is the thing that YHWH commanded,
	saying,
}

\eP{
	‘Take a donation for YHWH from among you. Everyone whose
	heart is moved shall bring it, YHWH’s donation: gold and
	silver and bronze
}

\eP{
	and oil for lighting and spices for the anointing oil and
	for the incense fragrances
}

\eP{
	and onyx stones and stones to be set for the ephod and for
	the breastplate.
}

\eP{
	And let everyone wise of heart among you come and make
	everything that YHWH has commanded:
}

\eP{
	the Tabernacle, its tent and its cover and its clasps and
	its frames, its bars, its columns and its bases,
}

\eP{
	the ark and its poles, the atonement dais and the covering
	pavilion,
}

\eP{
	the table and its poles and all of its equipment and the
	show bread
}

\eP{
	and the menorah for lighting and its equipment and its
	lamps and the oil for lighting
}

\eP{
	and the incense altar and its poles and the anointing oil
	and the incense fragrances and the entrance cover for the
	Tabernacle’s entrance,
}

\eP{
	the altar of burnt offering and the bronze grate that it
	has, its poles and all of its equipment, the basin and its
	stand,
}

\eP{
	the courtyard’s hangings, its columns and its bases and
	the cover of the courtyard’s gate,
}

\eP{
	the Tabernacle’s pegs and the courtyard’s pegs and their
	cords,
}

\eP{
	the fabric clothes for ministering in the Holy, the holy
	clothes for Aaron the priest, and his sons’ clothes for
	functioning as priests’.”
}

\eP{
	And all of the congregation of the children of Israel went
	out from in front of Moses.
}

\eP{
	And everyone whose heart inspired him came, and everyone
	whose spirit moved him brought a contribution for YHWH for
	the work of the Tent of Meeting and for all of its
	construction and for the holy clothes.
}

\eP{
	And the men came together with the women. All whose hearts
	moved them had brought brooches and earrings and rings and
	ornaments, every kind of gold item. And every man who
	brought an elevation offering of gold to YHWH
}

\eP{
	and every man with whom was found blue and purple and
	scarlet and linen and goats’ hair and rams’ skins dyed red
	and leather skins had brought them.
}

\eP{
	Everyone making a donation of silver and bronze had
	brought YHWH’s donation. And everyone with whom was found
	acacia wood for all the work of the construction had
	brought it.
}

\eP{
	And every woman who was wise of heart with her hands had
	spun, and they brought yarn, the blue and the purple, the
	scarlet and the linen.
}

\eP{
	And all the women whose hearts inspired them with wisdom
	had spun the goats’ hair.
}

\eP{
	And the chieftains had brought onyx stones and stones to
	be set for the ephod and for the breastplate
}

\eP{
	and the spice and the oil for lighting and for the
	anointing oil and for the incense fragrances.
}

\eP{
	Every man and woman whose heart moved them to bring for
	all the work that YHWH had commanded to do by the hand of
	Moses: the children of Israel brought a contribution for
	YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And Moses said to the children of Israel, “See, YHWH has
	called Bezalel, son of Uri, son of Hur, of the tribe of
	Judah by name,
}

\eP{
	and He has filled him with the spirit of God in wisdom, in
	understanding and in knowledge and in every kind of work
}

\eP{
	and to form conceptions to make in gold and in silver and
	in bronze
}

\eP{
	and in cutting stone for setting and in cutting wood, for
	making every kind of conceived work.
}

\eP{
	And He has put it in his heart to instruct: he and
	Oholiab, son of Ahisamach, of the tribe of Dan.
}

\eP{
	And Bezalel and Oholiab and every man who is wise of
	heart, in whom YHWH has put wisdom and understanding to
	know how to do all the work of the service of the Holy
	shall do it, for everything that YHWH has commanded.”
}

\eP{
	And Moses called Bezalel and Oholiab and every man who was
	wise of heart, in whose heart YHWH had put wisdom,
	everyone whose heart inspired him to come forward for the
	work, to do it.
}

\eP{
	And they took from in front of Moses all of the donation
	that the children of Israel had brought for the work of
	the construction of the Holy, to do it. And they had
	brought him more contribution, morning by morning.
}

\eP{
	And all the wise persons who were doing all the work of
	the Holy came, each from his kind of work that they were
	doing,
}

\eP{
	and they said to Moses, saying, “The people are bringing
	more than enough for the construction, for the work that
	YHWH has commanded, to do it!”
}

\eP{
	And Moses commanded, and they passed an announcement
	through the camp saying, “Man and woman: let them not do
	any more work for the donation for the Holy.” And the
	people were held back from bringing.
}

\eP{
	And the work had been enough for them, for all the work,
	to do it and more.
}

\eP{
	And all those who were wise of heart among those who were
	doing the work made the Tabernacle with ten curtains of
	woven linen and blue and purple and scarlet, with cherubs.
	They made them designer’s work.
}

\eP{
	The length of one curtain was twenty-eight in cubits, and
	the width of one curtain four in cubits: one size to all
	the curtains.
}

\eP{
	And he connected five of the curtains, one to one; and he
	connected five curtains, one to one.
}

\eP{
	And he made loops of blue on the side of one curtain at
	the end of the connected group. He did so in the side of
	the end curtain in the second connected group.
}

\eP{
	He made fifty loops in the one curtain and made fifty
	loops in the end of the curtain that was in the second
	connected group with the loops parallel: one to one.
}

\eP{
	And he made fifty clasps of gold and connected the
	curtains, one to one, with the clasps. And the Tabernacle
	was one.
}

\eP{
	And he made curtains of goats’ hair for the tent over the
	Tabernacle. He made them eleven curtains.
}

\eP{
	The length of one curtain was thirty in cubits, and the
	width of one curtain was four cubits: one size for eleven
	curtains.
}

\eP{
	And he connected five of the curtains by themselves and
	six of the curtains by themselves.
}

\eP{
	And he made fifty loops on the side of the end curtain in
	the connected group and made fifty loops on the side of
	the curtain of the second connected group.
}

\eP{
	And he made fifty bronze clasps to connect the tent, to be
	one.
}

\eP{
	And he made a covering for the tent of rams’ skins dyed
	red and a covering of leather skins above.
}

\eP{
	And he made the frames for the Tabernacle of acacia wood,
	standing.
}

\eP{
	The frame’s length was ten cubits, and the width of one
	frame was a cubit and a half cubit.
}

\eP{
	One frame had two projections, aligned one to one: so he
	made for all of the Tabernacle’s frames.
}

\eP{
	And he made the frames for the Tabernacle: twenty frames
	for the south side—to the south.
}

\eP{
	And he made forty bases of silver under the twenty frames:
	two bases under one frame for its two projections and two
	bases under each other frame for its two projections.
}

\eP{
	And for the Tabernacle’s second side, for the north side,
	he made twenty frames
}

\eP{
	and their forty bases of silver, two bases under one frame
	and two bases under each other frame.
}

\eP{
	And for the rear of the Tabernacle, to the west, he made
	six frames.
}

\eP{
	And he made two frames for the Tabernacle’s corners in the
	rear,
}

\eP{
	and they were doubles from below, and together they were
	integrated on its top to one ring. He made it so for the
	two of them, for the two corners.
}

\eP{
	And they were eight frames and their bases of silver,
	sixteen bases, two bases and two bases under each one
	frame.
}

\eP{
	And he made bars of acacia wood: five for the frames of
	one side of the Tabernacle
}

\eP{
	and five bars for the frames of the second side of the
	Tabernacle and five bars for the frames of the side of the
	Tabernacle at the rear, to the west.
}

\eP{
	And he made the middle bar to extend through the frames
	from end to end.
}

\eP{
	And he plated the frames with gold, and he made their
	rings gold, housings for the bars, and he plated the bars
	with gold.
}

\eP{
	And he made the pavilion of blue and purple and scarlet
	and woven linen; he made it designer’s work with cherubs.
}

\eP{
	And he made four acacia columns for it and plated them
	with gold, their hooks of gold, and he cast four bases of
	silver for them.
}

\eP{
	And he made a cover for the entrance of the tent: blue and
	purple and scarlet and woven linen—embroiderer’s work—
}

\eP{
	and its five columns and their hooks, and he plated their
	tops and their bands with gold, and their five bases were
	bronze.
}

\eP{
	And Bezalel made the ark of acacia wood, its length two
	and a half cubits and its width a cubit and a half and its
	height a cubit and a half.
}

\eP{
	And he plated it with pure gold inside and outside. And he
	made a border of gold all around for it.
}

\eP{
	And he cast four rings of gold for it on its four bottoms:
	and two rings on its one side and two rings on its second
	side.
}

\eP{
	and brought the poles through the rings on the ark’s
	sides, in order to carry the ark.
}

\eP{
	And he made an atonement dais of pure gold, its length two
	and a half cubits and its width a cubit and a half.
}

\eP{
	And he made two cherubs of gold—he made them of hammered
	work—at the two ends of the atonement dais,
}

\eP{
	one cherub at this end and one cherub at that end. He made
	the cherubs from the atonement dais at its two sides.
}

\eP{
	And the two cherubs were spreading wings above, covering
	over the atonement dais with their wings, and their faces
	each toward its brother: the cherubs’ faces were toward
	the atonement dais.
}

\eP{
	And he made the table of acacia wood, its length two
	cubits and its width a cubit and its height a cubit and a
	half.
}

\eP{
	And he plated it with pure gold and made a border of gold
	all around for it
}

\eP{
	and made a rim of a handbreadth for it all around and made
	a border of gold for its rim all around.
}

\eP{
	And he cast four rings of gold for it and put the rings on
	the four corners that its four legs had.
}

\eP{
	The rings were in juxtaposition to the rim, housings for
	the poles in order to carry the table.
}

\eP{
	And he made the poles of acacia wood and plated them with
	gold in order to carry the table.
}

\eP{
	And he made the items that were on the table—its dishes
	and its pans and its bowls and its jars with which
	libations would be poured—of pure gold.
}

\eP{
	And he made the menorah of pure gold. He made the menorah
	of hammered work—its shaft and its branch, its cups, its
	ornaments, and its flowers were part of it—
}

\eP{
	and six branches coming out from its sides, three of the
	menorah’s branches from its one side and three of the
	menorah’s branches from its second side,
}

\eP{
	three almond-shaped cups in the one branch, with ornament
	and flower, and three almond-shaped cups in the other
	branch, with ornament and flower. So it was for the six
	branches that came out from the menorah,
}

\eP{
	and four almond-shaped cups within the menorah, with its
	ornaments and its flowers,
}

\eP{
	and an ornament under the two branches from it, and an
	ornament under the two branches from it, and an ornament
	under the two branches from it, for the six branches that
	came out from it.
}

\eP{
	Their ornaments and their branches were part of it, all of
	it one hammered work of pure gold.
}

\eP{
	And he made its lamps, seven, and its tongs and its
	fire-holders of pure gold.
}

\eP{
	He made it and all of its equipment out of a talent of
	pure gold.
}

\eP{
	And he made the incense altar of acacia wood: its length a
	cubit and its width a cubit, square, and its height two
	cubits; its horns were a part of it.
}

\eP{
	And he plated it with pure gold, its roof and its walls
	all around and its horns. And he made a border of gold for
	it all around.
}

\eP{
	And he made two rings of gold for it below its rim on its
	two sides, on its two sidewalls, for housings for poles
	with which to carry it.
}

\eP{
	And he made the poles of acacia wood and plated them with
	gold.
}

\eP{
	And he made the holy anointing oil and the pure incense
	fragrances, the work of an ointment-maker.
}

\eP{
	And he made the altar of burnt offering of acacia wood,
	its length five cubits and its width five cubits, square,
	and its height three cubits.
}

\eP{
	And he made its horns on its four corners. Its horns were
	part of it. And he plated it with bronze.
}

\eP{
	And he made all the altar’s equipment: the pots, the
	shovels and the basins, the forks and the fire-holders. He
	made all of its equipment bronze.
}

\eP{
	And he made a grate for the altar, a network of bronze,
	under its ledge up to the middle of it.
}

\eP{
	And he cast four rings in the bronze grate’s four ends:
	housings for the poles.
}

\eP{
	And he made the poles of acacia wood and plated them with
	bronze
}

\eP{
	and brought the poles through the rings on the altar’s
	sides in order to carry it with them. He made it hollow of
	boards.
}

\eP{
	And he made the basin of bronze and its stand of bronze
	with the mirrors of the women who served at the entrance
	of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And he made the courtyard: for the south side—to the
	south, the courtyard’s hangings of woven linen, a hundred
	in cubits,
}

\eP{
	their columns twenty, and their bases twenty of bronze,
	and the columns’ hooks and their rods of silver.
}

\eP{
	And for the north side: a hundred in cubits, their columns
	twenty, and their bases twenty of bronze, and the columns’
	hooks and their bands of silver.
}

\eP{
	And for the west side: hangings of fifty in cubits, their
	columns ten, and their bases ten, the columns’ hooks and
	their bands of silver.
}

\eP{
	And the courtyard’s width for the east side—to the
	east—fifty cubits.
}

\eP{
	Fifteen cubits of hangings to the panel, their columns
	three and their bases three;
}

\eP{
	and the second panel—on this side and that side of the
	courtyard’s gate—hangings of fifteen cubits, their columns
	three and their bases three.
}

\eP{
	All of the courtyard’s hangings, all around, were woven
	linen.
}

\eP{
	And the column’s bases were bronze, the columns’ hooks and
	their bands were silver, and the plating of their tops was
	silver, and they were banded with silver: all of the
	courtyard’s columns.
}

\eP{
	And the cover of the courtyard’s gate was embroiderer’s
	work, blue and purple and scarlet and woven linen, and
	twenty cubits in length, and five cubits in height in the
	width, in juxtaposition to the courtyard’s hangings.
}

\eP{
	And their columns were four and their bases four of
	bronze, their hooks silver, and the plating of their tops
	and their bands silver.
}

\eP{
	And all of the pegs of the Tabernacle and the courtyard
	all around were bronze.
}

\eP{
	These are the accounts of the Tabernacle, the
	Tabernacle of the Testimony, that were made by Moses’
	word, the work of the Levites by the hand of Ithamar, son
	of Aaron, the priest.
}

