tex.english:
\eP{
	And Bezalel, son of Uri, son of Hur, of the tribe of
	Judah, made everything that YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And with him was Oholiab, son of Ahisamach, of the tribe
	of Dan, an engraver and designer and embroiderer in blue
	and in purple and in scarlet and in linen.
}

\eP{
	All the gold that was used for the work, in all the work
	of the Holy: and the gold of the elevation offering was
	twenty-nine talents and seven hundred thirty shekels by
	the shekel of the Holy.
}

\eP{
	And the silver of the congregation’s accounts was a
	hundred talents and a thousand seven hundred seventy-five
	shekels by the shekel of the Holy:
}

\eP{
	a beqa per head, half of a shekel by the shekel of the
	Holy, for everyone who passed through the counts, from
	twenty years old and up, for six hundred thousand and
	three thousand five hundred fifty.
}

\eP{
	And the hundred talents of silver were for casting the
	bases of the Holy and the bases of the pavilion, a hundred
	bases to the hundred talents, a talent per base.
}

\eP{
	And he made the thousand seven hundred seventy-five into
	hooks for the columns, and he plated their tops and banded
	them.
}

\eP{
	And the bronze of the elevation offering was seventy
	talents and two thousand four hundred shekels.
}

\eP{
	And with it he made the bases of the entrance of the Tent
	of Meeting and the bronze altar and the bronze network
	that it had and all of the altar’s equipment
}

\eP{
	and the bases of the courtyard all around and the bases of
	the courtyard’s gate and all of the Tabernacle’s pegs and
	all of the courtyard’s pegs all around.
}

\eP{
	And from the blue and the purple and the scarlet they
	made fabric clothes for ministering in the Holy, and they
	made the holy clothes that were Aaron’s, as YHWH had
	commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he made the ephod: gold, blue, and purple and scarlet
	and woven linen.
}

\eP{
	And they hammered out foil sheets of the gold, and he cut
	threads to use among the blue and among the purple and
	among the scarlet and among the linen—designer’s work.
}

\eP{
	They made shoulder-pieces for it, connected: on its two
	ends it was connected.
}

\eP{
	And its ephod designed belt that was on it was a part of
	it, like its work, gold, blue, and purple and scarlet and
	woven linen, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And they made the onyx stones surrounded by settings of
	gold, inscribed with signet inscriptions with the names of
	the children of Israel.
}

\eP{
	And he set them on the ephod’s shoulder-pieces,
	commemorative stones for the children of Israel, as YHWH
	had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he made the breastplate, designer’s work, like the
	work of the ephod: gold, blue, and purple and scarlet and
	woven linen.
}

\eP{
	It was square. They made the breastplate doubled, its
	length a span and its width a span, doubled.
}

\eP{
	And they mounted four rows of stones in it: a row of a
	carnelian, a topaz, and an emerald: the one row;
}

\eP{
	and the fourth row: a beryl, an onyx, and a jasper—set in
	gold in their mountings.
}

\eP{
	And the stones: they were with the names of the children
	of Israel, twelve with their names, signet engravings,
	each with its name, for twelve tribes.
}

\eP{
	And they made twisted chains, rope work, of pure gold, on
	the breastplate.
}

\eP{
	And they made two settings of gold and two rings of gold
	and put the two rings on the two ends of the breastplate
}

\eP{
	and put the two ropes of gold on the two rings at the ends
	of the breastplate.
}

\eP{
	And they put the two ends of the two ropes on the two
	settings and put them on the ephod’s shoulder-pieces
	opposite the front of it.
}

\eP{
	And they made two rings of gold and put them on the
	breastplate’s two ends on its side that is adjacent to the
	ephod, inward.
}

\eP{
	And they made two rings of gold and put them on the
	ephod’s two shoulder-pieces below, opposite the front of
	it, by its connection, above the ephod’s designed belt.
}

\eP{
	And they attached the breastplate from its rings to the
	ephod’s rings with a blue string so it would be on the
	ephod’s designed belt and the breastplate would not be
	detached from the ephod, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he made the robe of the ephod, weaver’s work, all of
	blue,
}

\eP{
	and the robe’s opening within it like the opening of a
	coat of mail, a binding for its opening all around so it
	would not be torn.
}

\eP{
	And they made on the robe’s skirts pomegranates of blue
	and purple and scarlet, woven.
}

\eP{
	And they made bells of pure gold and put the bells among
	the pomegranates on the robe’s skirts all around among the
	pomegranates:
}

\eP{
	bell and pomegranate, bell and pomegranate, on the robe’s
	skirts all around, for ministering, as YHWH had commanded
	Moses.
}

\eP{
	And they made the coats of linen, weaver’s work, for Aaron
	and for his sons,
}

\eP{
	and the headdress of linen and the beautiful hats of linen
	and the shorts of linen, woven linen,
}

\eP{
	and the sash of woven linen and blue and purple and
	scarlet, embroiderer’s work, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And they made the plate of the crown of Holiness of pure
	gold, and they wrote on it a text of signet inscriptions:
	“Holiness to YHWH.”
}

\eP{
	And they put a blue string on it for putting it on the
	headdress above, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And all of the construction of the Tabernacle of the Tent
	of Meeting was finished. And the children of Israel did
	according to everything that YHWH had commanded Moses.
	They did so.
}

\eP{
	And they brought the Tabernacle to Moses: the tent and all
	of its equipment, its clasps, its frames, its bars, and
	its columns and its bases,
}

\eP{
	and the covering of rams’ skins dyed red and the covering
	of leather skins, and the covering pavilion,
}

\eP{
	the Ark of the Testimony and its poles and the atonement
	dais,
}

\eP{
	the pure menorah, its lamps, lamps for the row, and all of
	its equipment, and the oil for lighting,
}

\eP{
	and the golden altar and the anointing oil and the incense
	of fragrances and the cover of the entrance of the Tent,
}

\eP{
	the bronze altar and the bronze grate that it had, its
	poles and all of its equipment, the basin and its stand,
}

\eP{
	the courtyard’s hangings, its columns and its bases and
	the cover for the courtyard’s gate, its cords and its
	pegs, and all the equipment for the service of the
	Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting,
}

\eP{
	the fabric clothes for ministering in the Holy, the holy
	clothes for Aaron, the priest, and his sons’ clothes for
	functioning as priests.
}

\eP{
	According to everything that YHWH had commanded Moses: so
	the children of Israel did all of the construction.
}

\eP{
	And Moses saw all of the work; and, here, they had done it
	as YHWH had commanded. They did so. And Moses blessed
	them.
}

\eP{
	“On the day of the first month, on the first of the month,
	you shall set up the Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And you shall set the Ark of the Testimony there and have
	the pavilion cover over the ark.
}

\eP{
	And you shall bring the table and make its arrangement,
	and bring the menorah and put up its lamps.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put the golden altar for incense in front of
	the Ark of the Testimony, and you shall set the cover of
	the entrance of the Tabernacle.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put the altar of burnt offering in front of
	the entrance of the Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And you shall put the basin between the Tent of Meeting
	and the altar and put water there.
}

\eP{
	And you shall set the courtyard all around and put on the
	cover of the courtyard’s gate.
}

\eP{
	And you shall take the anointing oil and anoint the
	Tabernacle and everything that is in it, and you shall
	make it and all of its equipment holy, and it will be
	holiness.
}

\eP{
	And you shall anoint the altar of burnt offering and all
	of its equipment, and you shall make the altar holy: and
	the altar will be holy of holies.
}

\eP{
	And you shall anoint the basin and its stand and make it
	holy.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall bring Aaron and his sons forward to the
	entrance of the Tent of Meeting and wash them with water.
}

\eP{
	And you shall dress Aaron with the holy clothes and anoint
	him and make him holy, and he shall function as a priest
	for me.
}

\eP{
	And you shall bring his sons forward and dress them with
	coats
}

\eP{
	and anoint them as you anointed their father, and they
	shall function as priests for me. And it will be for their
	anointing to be theirs as an eternal priesthood through
	their generations.”
}

\eP{
	And Moses did it. According to everything that YHWH
	commanded him, he did so.
}

\eP{
	And it was: in the first month, in the second year, on the
	first of the month, the Tabernacle was set up.
}

\eP{
	And Moses set up the Tabernacle and put on its bases and
	set its frames and put on its bars and set up its columns.
}

\eP{
	And he spread the Tent over the Tabernacle and set the
	Tent’s covering on it above, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he took the Testimony and put it into the ark, and he
	set the poles on the ark, and he put the atonement dais on
	the ark above.
}

\eP{
	And he brought the ark into the Tabernacle and set the
	covering pavilion and covered over the Ark of the
	Testimony, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he put the table in the Tent of Meeting on the
	northward side of the Tabernacle, outside of the pavilion.
}

\eP{
	And he made the arrangement of bread on it in front of
	YHWH, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he set the menorah in the Tent of Meeting opposite the
	table, on the southward side of the Tabernacle,
}

\eP{
	and he put up the lamps in front of YHWH, as YHWH had
	commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he set the golden altar in the Tent of Meeting in
	front of the pavilion,
}

\eP{
	and he burned the incense of fragrances on it, as YHWH had
	commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he set the altar of burnt offering at the entrance of
	Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting, and he offered up the
	burnt offering and the grain offering on it, as YHWH had
	commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he set the basin between the Tent of Meeting and the
	altar, and he put water for washing there.
}

\eP{
	And Moses and Aaron and his sons washed their hands and
	their feet from it.
}

\eP{
	When they came to the Tent of Meeting and when they came
	forward to the altar they would wash, as YHWH had
	commanded Moses.
}

\eP{
	And he set up the courtyard all around the Tabernacle and
	the altar, and he put on the cover of the courtyard’s
	gate. And Moses finished the work.
}

\eP{
	And the cloud covered the Tent of Meeting, and YHWH’s
	glory filled the Tabernacle.
}

\eP{
	And Moses was not able to come into the Tent of Meeting,
	because the cloud had settled on it and YHWH’s glory
	filled the Tabernacle.
}

\eP{
	And when the cloud was lifted from on the Tabernacle, the
	children of Israel would travel—in all their travels—
}

\eP{
	and if the cloud would not be lifted, then they would not
	travel until the day that it would be lifted.
}

	\eP{
		Because YHWH’s cloud was on the Tabernacle by day, and
		fire would be in it at night, before the eyes of all the
		house of Israel in all their travels.
    }

tex.commentary:
\eX{
\sloppy

% The answer-key footnotes transcribe the English readings in
% include/jhwh-and-möse-01.png through include/jhwh-and-möse-10.png.
Here we have the first ever fragment of the \textit{JHWH and Mösen} Apocryphon yet excavated.

Sadly for the curious layperson, the screenplay is a bit heavy of mouth and tongue.\footnote{That is, a foreign film.}

Namely, when the original Nameless text was originally discovered, we believed it to have been composed entirely in an odd dialect of German. The plot seemed to be a story in which YHWH is looking for Moses, and cannot find him, as can be seen here:

\ApocryphonYHWHDialogue{
Möse. Wo sie Möse? Wer hat Möse? Wer bin? Ich bin! Jahwe. Jah, wir. Wir war, Jah [wirr, warr]. Mein \heb{גר} man ist sehr \heb{שער}, sehr \heb{אדם}, sehr männ, sehr wirr. Est vir verus. Wir werus. Yah [wirr]. Wo ist Mösen? Wo bist du? Aha, Mösen! Warum rennst du?... Lauf nicht. Wen? Die Mösen!? Warum nicht? Wir esse Mösen. Wer bin? Mein Gott. Du Göttin, dich. Gib mir das Möse holen. Komm her, Baby. Sie, mein wenig? Sie mein ekliges Ding. Ich liebe Dich. Dich und Möse. Wo sie ich? Dieses Buch. Gutes Buch. Wen? Eure? Seir seir seir gut. Wen Möse die? Nicht lassen. War? Ich werde bund tun. Komm mit dich babe. Du komm bald? Ich komm bald. Dei Möse ist mein, babe. Dei Möse ist mein für immer. Für immer und immer Moses. Für immer und immer. Nur du und ich.
}

Since ``losing track of someone'' is a common theme in YHWH related stories (history or narrative) of all forms, the assumption that we'd understood the text went unchallenged for some time.

However, a breakthrough came recently when an anonymous individual calling himself ``Adam Ben Ben Ben Möses'' (sic?) made an anonymous post on a forum of Nameless Studies on the The Dynamic Read-Writable Free Encyclopedic Repository of the Modern State of Human Knowledge.

No claim is made as to the complete accuracy of the translation, but we believe it is roughly accurate in most details.

Whatever imperfections might remain, the text does appear to read as two completely different scenes in two different languages: a criterion now understood \mdash{} thanks to recent advances in Nameless Studies \mdash{} to be a requirement for all authentic texts written by ``the hand of god,'' or by the authentic sect of its nameless authors.

The biblically knowledgeable reader will likely recognize a similarity to Exodus 4's notoriously difficult ``Lodging Place'' scene, specifically where the text below reads ``Die Mösen'' and later in the same passage, ``Wen Möses die?''

The scholarly community is just coming to grips with the possibility that all ancient texts previously attributed to a god or gods appear to share the ``dual reading'' property.

This suggests that the above mentioned section of Exodus 4 may have simply been a result of the deity (or the author (or both)) making a series of crude plays on words, in multiple languages and language families, and that the original text may have been not far from the fragment below.

Just a final note: the text that follows is not appropriate for children, so please do make sure to put the kids to bed before reading any further. Thank you for reading, and we hope you find the text to be clarifying of whatever relationship you may have with the Lord, or perhaps clarifying of any of the topics discussed directly in the body of the text... if you're into that sort of thing. We now proceed with the text.

% English reading transcribed from include/jhwh-and-möse-full-transcript.png.
\ApocryphonYHWHDialogue{
\eRJE{Moses}. Where is \eRJE{Moses}? Who has \eRJE{Moses}? Who am I? I am! Yahweh. Yah, we. We were, Yah [confused, chaos]. My foreign\footnote{Lit. My Ger man. From Heb. \heb{גר}, meaning foreign.} man is very hairy, very human, very male, very chaotic. He is a true man. We are true. Yah [confused]. Where are \eRJE{Mos}es? Where are you? Aha, \eRJE{Mos}es! Why are you running?... Don't run. Who? The \eRJE{Mos}es!? Why not? We eat \eRJE{Mos}es. Who am I? My God. You goddess, you. Let me get that \eRJE{Mos}es. Come here, baby. You, my little one? You, my nasty thing. I love you. You and \eRJE{Moses}. Where am I? This book. Good book. Whose? Yours? So, so, so good. Whose \eRJE{Moses} is it? Don't let go. What? I'm going to do it. Come with me, babe. You coming soon? I'm coming soon. Your \eRJE{Moses} is mine, babe. Your \eRJE{Moses} is mine forever. Forever and ever, Moses. Forever and ever. Just you and me.
}%
\footnote{\aC{Translation, transliteration, and re-punctuation provided courtesy of our new bleeding-edge and always-up-to-date Machine Learning Algorithm sponsored by Art of the State® designed expressly for the task of Culturally Egalitarian Omni-Lingual Deacronymification™ (or: C.E.O-L.D.™).}}%
