Featuring: JHWH and The Ten Things You Müsen Do... The Ten Kommand Möse!\fC{The surviving publicity material supplies rather more working titles than the upcoming film can reasonably use. A few representative proposals, with their accompanying English renderings, are \textit{Titel: Schadei nist Gott} (``Title: Shaddai is God''), \textit{Titel: Schade ist Gott} (``Title: God Is a Pity''), and \textit{Titel: Schaden ist Gott} (``Title: Harm is God''). The running joke is a divine name, Shaddai, coming uncomfortably close to \textit{Schade} (``a pity'') and \textit{Schaden} (``harm''): a small revision to the title can turn a profession of faith into a complaint. These are the publicity department's proposed readings; the reader need not accept its German to appreciate its theological difficulties. Its final proposal, \textit{Gott's Tit ist El Schadden}, arrives in English as ``God's title is El Shaddai.'' The English has discreetly restored the missing letters in ``title'' before anyone can ask about the deity's anatomy.

The ten girls fare little better in the cast list. Their collective billing appears as \textit{Die Zehn Kommende Mädchen!}, rendered ``The Ten Girls Who Are Coming!'', and as \textit{The 10 Kommend Mösen}, rendered ``The 10 Coming \redacted{Pussies}''. No individual character names survive in these notices. The advertised ``Ten Kommand Möse'' thus lets the Ten Commandments take the stage as ten women: \textit{Möse} supplies the vulgar anatomical sense, while ``coming'' leaves their entrance open to interpretation. The publicity department assures us that all ten will come.}

Post Script. We apologize on behalf of the scholarly community for whatever effect this might have had on the reader's relationship with the Lord.

