	And if the bright spot will have stayed the same in its
	place—it has not spread in the skin—and it has become dim,
	it is the swelling of the burn, and the priest shall
	identify him as pure, because it is the scar of the burn.
}

\eP{
	“And when a man or woman will have an affliction in the
	head or beard,
}

\eP{
	And when the priest will see the affliction of the scab,
	and, here, its appearance is not deeper than the skin, and
	there is not black hair in it, then the priest shall shut
	in the affliction of the scab seven days.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall see the affliction on the seventh
	day. And, here, the scab has not spread, and there is not
	yellow hair in it, and the scab’s appearance is not deeper
	than the skin.
}

\eP{
	Then he shall shave himself, and he shall not shave the
	scab. And the priest shall shut in the one with the scab
	seven days a second time.
}

\eP{
	But if the scab has spread in the skin, after his
	purification,
}

\eP{
	and the priest will see him, and, here, the scab has
	spread in the skin, the priest shall not inspect for the
	yellow hair. He is impure.
}

\eP{
	And if, in his eyes, the scab has stayed the same, and
	black hair has developed in it, the scab has been healed.
	He is pure. And the priest shall identify him as pure.
}

\eP{
	“And when a man or woman will have bright spots in the
	skin of their flesh, white bright spots,
}

\eP{
	and the priest will see, and, here, bright spots in the
	skin of their flesh are dim white, it is a tetter. It has
	developed in the skin. He is pure.
}

\eP{
	“And when a man’s head will become hairless, he is bald.
	He is pure.
}

\eP{
	And if his head will become hairless from the corner of
	his face, he has a bald forehead. He is pure.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		the corner of his face. The hair around the forehead.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And when there will be in the bald head or in the bald
	forehead a reddish white affliction, it is leprosy
	developing in his bald head or in his bald forehead.
}

\eP{
	And the priest will see it, and, here, the affliction’s
	swelling is reddish white in his bald head or in his bald
	forehead, like the appearance of leprosy of skin of flesh,
}

\eP{
	he is a leprous man. He is impure. The priest shall
	identify him as impure. His affliction is in his head.
}

\eP{
	And the leper in whom is the affliction: his clothes shall
	be torn, and the hair of his head shall be loosed, and he
	shall cover over his mustache. And he shall call, ‘Impure!
	Impure!’
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		he shall call, ‘Impure! Impure!’ It has been said that the reason for
%		this is so that people will pray for the afflicted person. The apparent
%		reason, however, is to warn people away, to avoid the spread of
%		impurity.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	All the days that the affliction is in him he will be
	impure. He is impure. He shall live separate. His home is
	outside of the camp.
}

\eP{
	“And when clothing will have an affliction of leprosy in
	it—in clothing of wool or in clothing of linen,
}

\eP{
	either in the warp or in the woof of the linen or of the
	wool, or in leather or in anything made of leather:
}

\eP{
	if the affliction will be greenish or reddish in the
	clothing or in the leather or in the warp or in the woof
	or in any item of leather, it is an affliction of
	leprosy—and it will be shown to the priest,
}

\eP{
	and the priest will see the affliction, and he shall shut
	in the thing with the affliction seven days.
}

\eP{
	And he shall burn the clothing or the warp or the woof in
	the wool or in the linen or any item of leather in which
	the affliction will be, because it is malignant leprosy.
	It shall be burned in fire.
}

\eP{
	And if the priest will look, and, here, the affliction has
	not spread in the clothing or in the warp or in the woof
	or any item of leather,
}

\eP{
	then the priest shall command, and they shall wash the
	thing in which the affliction is, and he shall shut it in
	seven days a second time.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		in its “bald head” or in its “forehead.” Meaning: on the clothing’s
%		inside or its outside.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	If the priest will see, and, here, the affliction has
	become dim after it was washed, then he shall rip it from
	the clothing or from the leather or from the warp or from
	the woof.
}

\eP{
	And if it will appear again in the clothing or in the warp
	or in the woof or in any item of leather, it is
	developing. You shall burn it, the thing in which the
	affliction is, in fire.
}

\eP{
	And the clothing or the warp or the woof or any item of
	leather that you will wash: if the affliction will turn
	away from them, then it shall be washed a second time, and
	it will be pure.
}

\eP{
	“This is the instruction for the affliction of leprosy of
	clothing of wool or linen or warp or woof or any item of
	leather, to identify it as pure or to identify it as
	impure.”
}

\eP{
	“This shall be the instruction for the leper in the day of
	his purification: And he shall be brought to the priest,
}

\eP{
	and the priest shall go outside the camp, and the priest
	shall see, and if, here, the affliction of leprosy has
	been healed from the leper,
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		the priest shall see. Again, the priest is not a physician. The priest
%		has identified the condition (in the preceding chapter) and now sees
%		that the condition is healed. So what follows is not a ritual of
%		healing. The person is already healed. It is a ritual of purification.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	then the priest shall command, and one shall take two
	live, pure birds and cedarwood and scarlet and hyssop for
	the one being purified.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall command, and he shall slaughter one
	of the birds in a clay container over living water.
}

\eP{
	The live bird: he shall take it and the cedarwood and the
	scarlet and the hyssop, and he shall dip them and the live
	bird in the slaughtered bird’s blood over the living
	water.
}

\eP{
	And he shall sprinkle it on the one being purified from
	the leprosy seven times. And he shall identify him as
	pure. And he shall let the live bird go at the open field.
}

\eP{
	And the one being purified shall wash his clothes and
	shave all his hair and wash in water, and he will be pure,
	and after that he shall come to the camp. And he shall
	live outside his tent seven days.
}

\eP{
	And it will be, on the seventh day: he shall shave all his
	hair—he shall shave his head and his beard and his
	eyebrows and all his hair—and he shall wash his clothes
	and wash his flesh in water, and he will be pure.
}

\eP{
	And on the eighth day he shall take two unblemished
	he-lambs and one unblemished ewe-lamb in its first year
	and three-tenths of a measure of fine flour as a grain
	offering mixed with oil and one log of oil.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		log. A liquid measure (thought to be about one-half liter).
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And the priest who is purifying shall have the man being
	purified and them stand in front of YHWH at the entrance
	of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall take one of the lambs and bring it
	forward as a guilt offering and the log of oil, and he
	shall elevate them, an elevation offering in front of
	YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And he shall slaughter the lamb in the place where he
	slaughters the sin offering and the burnt offering, in the
	place of the Holy, because, like the sin offering, the
	guilt offering is the priest’s. It is the holy of holies.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall take some of the blood of the guilt
	offering, and the priest shall put it on the right earlobe
	of the one being purified and on the thumb of his right
	hand and on the big toe of his right foot.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall take some of the log of oil and pour
	it on the priest’s left hand.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall dip his right finger in some of the
	oil that is on his left hand and sprinkle some of the oil
	with his finger seven times in front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall put some of the remaining oil that is
	on his hand on the right earlobe of the one being purified
	and on the thumb of his right hand and on the big toe of
	his right foot, over the blood of the guilt offering.
}

\eP{
	And he shall put what is left of the oil that is on the
	priest’s hand on the head of the one being purified. And
	the priest shall make atonement over him in front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall make the sin offering and make
	atonement over the one being purified from his impurity,
	and after that he shall slaughter the burnt offering.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall make the burnt offering and the grain
	offering at the altar. And the priest shall make atonement
	over him, and he will be pure.
}

\eP{
	and two turtledoves or two pigeons, whichever his hand can
	attain, and one shall be a sin offering and one a burnt
	offering.
}

\eP{
	And he shall bring them on the eighth day of his
	purification to the priest, to the entrance of the Tent of
	Meeting, in front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall take the lamb of the guilt offering
	and the log of oil, and the priest shall elevate them, an
	elevation offering in front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall pour some of the oil on the priest’s
	left hand,
}

\eP{
	and the priest shall sprinkle some of the oil that is on
	his left hand with his right finger seven times in front
	of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And what is left of the oil that is on the priest’s hand
	he shall put on the head of the one being purified to make
	atonement over him in front of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	And he shall make one of the turtledoves or the pigeons,
	from those which his hand can attain—
}

\eP{
	whichever his hand will attain—one a sin offering and one
	a burnt offering, with the grain offering, and the priest
	shall make atonement over the one being purified in front
	of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	“This is the instruction for one who has an affliction of
	leprosy in him, whose hand cannot attain enough for his
	purification.”
}

\eP{
	“When you will come to the land of Canaan, which I am
	giving to you as a possession, and I shall put an
	affliction of leprosy in a house at the land of your
	possession,
}

\eP{
	and the one whose house it is will come and tell the
	priest, saying, ‘It appears like an affliction to me in
	the house,’
}

\eP{
	then the priest shall command, and they shall clear the
	house before the priest will come to see the affliction,
	so everything that is in the house will not become impure.
	And, after that, the priest shall come to see the house.
}

\eP{
	And he shall see the affliction, and, here, the affliction
	in the walls of the house is greenish or reddish streaks,
	and their appearance is deeper than the wall;
}

\eP{
	then the priest shall come out from the house, to the
	entrance of the house, and he shall shut the house up
	seven days.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall go back on the seventh day and see,
	and, here, the affliction has spread in the walls of the
	house;
}

\eP{
	then the priest shall command, and they shall extract the
	stones in which is the affliction, and they shall throw
	them outside the city, to an impure place.
}

\eP{
	And he shall have the house scraped inside, all around,
	and they shall spill the dust that they have scraped
	outside the city, to an impure place.
}

\eP{
	And they shall take other stones and bring them in place
	of the stones, and he shall take other dust and coat the
	house.
}

\eP{
	And if the affliction will come back and develop in the
	house after he has extracted the stones and after scraping
	the house and after being coated,
}

\eP{
	then the priest shall come and see, and, here, the
	affliction has spread in the house, it is malignant
	leprosy in the house. It is impure.
}

\eP{
	And he shall demolish the house: its stones and its wood
	and all the dust of the house; and he shall take it
	outside the city, to an impure place.
}

\eP{
	And one who comes into the house all the days that it is
	shut up will be impure until evening,
}

\eP{
	and one who lies in the house shall wash his clothes, and
	one who eats in the house shall wash his clothes.
}

\eP{
	And if the priest will come and see, and, here, the
	affliction has not spread in the house after coating the
	house, then the priest shall identify the house as pure,
	because the affliction has been healed.
}

\eP{
	And, to decontaminate the house, he shall take two birds
	and cedarwood and scarlet and hyssop,
}

\eP{
	and he shall slaughter one of the birds in a clay
	container over living water.
}

\eP{
	And he shall take the cedarwood and the hyssop and the
	scarlet and the live bird and dip them in the blood of the
	slaughtered bird and in the living water, and he shall
	sprinkle it at the house seven times.
}

\eP{
	And he shall decontaminate the house with the bird’s blood
	and with the living water and with the live bird and with
	the cedarwood and with the hyssop and with the scarlet.
}

\eP{
	And he shall let the live bird go outside the city at the
	open field. And he shall make atonement over the house,
	and it will be pure.
}

\eP{
	“This is the instruction for every affliction of leprosy
	and for a scab
}

\eP{
	to instruct on the day of the impure and on the day of the
	pure. This is the instruction for leprosy.”
}

\eP{
	“Speak to the children of Israel, and you shall say to
	them: When any man will have an emission from his flesh,
	his emission: it is impure.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		an emission from his flesh. A spontaneous discharge from the penis.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And this shall be his impurity when he has an emission:
	his flesh leaks with his emission, or his flesh is
	obstructed from his emission. It is his impurity.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		this shall be his impurity. That is, the following shall be the nature
%		of his impure condition.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Any bed on which the one with the emission will lie will
	be impure, and any item on which he will sit will be
	impure.
}

\eP{
	And a man who will touch his bed shall wash his clothes
	and shall wash in water and will be impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And one who sits on an item on which the one with the
	emission has sat shall wash his clothes and shall wash in
	water and will be impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And one who touches the flesh of the one with the emission
	shall wash his clothes and shall wash in water and will be
	impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And if the one with the emission will spit on one who is
	pure, then he shall wash his clothes and shall wash in
	water and will be impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And any conveyance on which the one with the emission will
	ride will be impure.
}

\eP{
	And anyone who touches anything that has been under him
	will be impure until evening. And one who carries them
	shall wash his clothes and shall wash in water and will be
	impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And anyone whom the one with the emission will touch when
	he has not rinsed his hands in water shall wash his
	clothes and shall wash in water and will be impure until
	evening.
}

\eP{
	And a clay container that the one with the emission will
	touch shall be broken, and any wood container shall be
	rinsed in water.
}

\eP{
	And when the one with the emission will be purified from
	his emission, he shall count seven days for his
	purification and shall wash his clothes and shall wash his
	flesh in living water and will be pure.
}

\eP{
	And on the eighth day he shall take two turtledoves or two
	pigeons and shall come in front of YHWH, to the entrance
	of the Tent of Meeting, and shall give them to the priest.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall do them: one a sin offering and one a
	burnt offering, and the priest shall make atonement over
	him in front of YHWH from his emission.
}

\eP{
	“And when a man’s intercourse seed will come out from him:
	he shall wash all his flesh in water and will be impure
	until evening.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		intercourse seed will come out from him. This refers to a spontaneous
%		flow of semen, as in a nocturnal emission.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And any clothing and any leather on which there will be
	intercourse seed: it shall be washed with water and will
	be impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And a woman with whom a man will lie—an intercourse of
	seed—they shall wash with water and will be impure until
	evening.
}

\eP{
	“And when a woman will have an emission—her emission will
	be blood in her flesh—she shall be in her impurity seven
	days. And anyone who touches her will be impure until
	evening.
}

\eP{
	And anything on which she will lie during her impurity
	will be impure, and anything on which she will sit will be
	impure.
}

\eP{
	And anyone who touches her bed shall wash his clothes and
	shall wash in water and will be impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And anyone who touches any item on which she will sit
	shall wash his clothes and shall wash in water and will be
	impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And if he is on the bed or on the item on which she sits
	while he is touching it, he will be impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	And if a man will lie with her, and her impurity will be
	on him, then he will be impure seven days. And any bed on
	which he will lie will be impure.
}

\eP{
	Any bed on which she will lie all the days of her emission
	will be like a bed of her impurity for her, and any item
	on which she will sit will be impure like the impurity of
	her menstruation.
}

\eP{
	And anyone who touches them will be impure. And he shall
	wash his clothes and shall wash with water and will be
	impure until evening.
}

\eP{
	“And if she has become purified from her emission, then
	she shall count seven days, and after that she will be
	pure.
}

\eP{
	And on the eighth day she shall take two turtledoves or
	two pigeons, and she shall bring them to the priest at the
	entrance of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And the priest shall make one a sin offering and one a
	burnt offering, and the priest shall make atonement over
	her in front of YHWH from her impure emission.
}

\eP{
	“And you shall alert the children of Israel regarding
	their impurity so they will not die through their impurity
	when they defile my Tabernacle that is among them.
}

\eP{
	This is the instruction for one who has an emission and
	for one from whom seed of intercourse will come out, so as
	to become impure by it,
}

\eP{
	and one who is in her menstrual impurity and one who has
	an emission—for a male and for a female—and for a man who
	will lie with a woman who is impure.”
}

\eP{
	And YHWH spoke to Moses after the death of Aaron’s two
	sons—when they came forward in front of YHWH and died.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		inside the pavilion. The Hebrew is mibbêt lapprket. The use of the term
%		bêt (house, building) may be further evidence that the prket is a
%		four-sided structure, not a curtain. See the comments on Tabernacle
%		architecture at Exodus 26.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Aaron shall come to the Holy with this: with a bull of the
	cattle for a sin offering and a ram for a burnt offering.
}

\eP{
	And he shall take from the congregation of the children of
	Israel two goats for a sin offering and one ram for a
	burnt offering.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall bring forward the bull of the sin offering
	that is his and shall make atonement for himself and for
	his house.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		his house. Meaning: his family, his household.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And he shall take the two goats and stand them in front of
	YHWH, at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall cast lots on the two goats, one lot for
	YHWH and one lot for Azazel.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall bring forward the goat on which the lot
	for YHWH arose, and he shall make it a sin offering.
}

\eP{
	And the goat on which the lot for Azazel arose shall be
	stood alive in front of YHWH to make atonement over it, to
	let it go to Azazel, into the wilderness.
}

\eP{
	And Aaron shall bring forward the bull of the sin offering
	that is his and shall make atonement for him and for his
	house, and he shall slaughter the bull of the sin offering
	that he has.
}

\eP{
	And he shall take a fire-holder-full of coals of fire from
	on the altar, from in front of YHWH, and handfuls of fine
	incense of fragrances, and he shall bring it inside the
	pavilion
}

\eP{
	and put the incense on the fire in front of YHWH so that
	the cloud of the incense will cover the atonement dais
	that is over the Testimony, and he will not die.
}

\eP{
	And he shall take some of the bull’s blood and sprinkle
	with his finger on the front of the atonement dais
	eastward, and he shall sprinkle some of the blood with his
	finger in front of the atonement dais seven times.
}

\eP{
	And no human shall be in the Tent of Meeting when he comes
	to make atonement in the Holy until he comes out. And he
	shall make atonement for him and for his house and for all
	the community of Israel.
}

\eP{
	“And he shall go out to the altar that is in front of YHWH
	and make atonement over it, and he shall take some of the
	bull’s blood and some of the goat’s blood and put it on
	the altar’s horns all around.
}

\eP{
	And he shall sprinkle some of the blood on it with his
	finger seven times and purify it and make it holy from the
	impurities of the children of Israel.
}

\eP{
	“And when he will finish making atonement for the Holy and
	the Tent of Meeting and the altar, then he shall bring
	forward the live goat.
}

\eP{
	And the goat will carry all their crimes on it to an
	inaccessible land. And he shall let the goat go in the
	wilderness.
}

\eP{
	“And Aaron shall come to the Tent of Meeting and take off
	the linen clothes that he wore when he came to the Holy,
	and he shall leave them there.
}

\eP{
	And he shall wash his flesh with water in a holy place and
	put on his clothes, and he shall go out and make his burnt
	offering and the people’s burnt offering and make
	atonement for him and for the people.
}

\eP{
	And he shall burn the fat of the sin offering into smoke
	at the altar.
}

\eP{
	And the one who let the goat go to Azazel shall wash his
	clothes and wash his flesh with water, and after that he
	shall come to the camp.
}

\eP{
	And he shall take the bull of the sin offering and the
	goat of the sin offering, whose blood was brought to make
	atonement in the Holy, outside the camp; and they shall
	burn their skin and their meat and their dung in fire.
}

\eP{
	And the one who burns them shall wash his clothes and wash
	his flesh with water, and after that he shall come to the
	camp.
}

\eP{
	“And it shall be an eternal law for you: in the seventh
	month, on the tenth of the month, you shall degrade
	yourselves, and you shall not do any work: the citizen and
	the alien who resides among you.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		degrade yourselves. The verb here is the same word that is used to
%		describe what the Egyptians did to the Israelites (Exod 1:11–12):
%		degradation, affliction. It has been understood to mean that one does
%		not eat or drink or bathe or wear fine shoes on the Day of Atonement;
%		rather, one humbles oneself.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Because on this day he shall make atonement over you, to
	purify you. You will be pure from all your sins in front
	of YHWH.
}

\eP{
	It is a Sabbath, a ceasing, to you. And you shall degrade
	yourselves—an eternal law.
}

\eP{
	And the priest whom one will anoint and who will fill his
	hand to function as a priest in his father’s place shall
	make atonement. And he shall wear the linen clothes, the
	holy clothes.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		fill his hand to function as a priest. See comment on Exod 29:29.
%	}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And he shall make atonement for the holy place of the
	Holy, and he shall make atonement for the Tent of Meeting
	and for the altar, and he shall make atonement over the
	priests and over all the people of the community.
