Semi Historical Note:

The first two sons died in a story written by E, as polemic against Jeroboam soon after 922 BC.

Then a bit later when the sons of Aaron\fC{The term here appears to denote the Aaronid priesthood.} decides they're not fans of JE

The sons of Aaron\fC{
	The term here appears to denote the biological sons, in the narrow literal sense,
	of the (fictional or non-fictional) Aaron.
} gain two new members, when E's fictitious two are copied by a son of Aaron\fC{
	The term here appears to denote the biological sons, in the broad sense of ``sons, grandsons, or later descendants,''
	of the (fictional or non-fictional) Aaron.
}
we'll call P.

Unsurprisingly, the fictional sons didn't leave a footprint in the geneological records.

Now add a quick resumptive repetition\fB{
	Or just standard P writing, in which repetitions are frequent enough that they're always around
	when you need to use one for resumption.
} to get us back to where we were in verse 1 so we can start the story.

So YHWH says to Moses,

