tex.commentary:
Don't do that for you always, he's your god, it's offensive to YHWH, he hates
that, don't do the things he hates for their gods, because they would also do
human sacrifice and burn their sons and daughters to their gods!%
\fB{The bible often accuses the Canaanites of human sacrifice, but there is no evidence in the archaeological record of this practice ever occuring among the native Canaanites—even at the more well excavated sites such as Ugarit. The one people in the ancient Near East whom our sources explicitly report practicing human sacrifice are, ironically, Israel themselves. The well documented evidence of human sacrifice to the god Baal Hammon comes much later from outside the ancient near east, at Punic sites in North Africa, particularly Carthage.}
\fC{Kings explicitly attributes child sacrifice to two kings of Judah: Ahaz (2 Kgs 16:3) and Manasseh (21:6); it also says Israelites generally sacrificed their sons and daughters (17:17) and records Josiah suppressing the practice at Topheth (23:10). Mesha, king of Moab, sacrifices a royal son during a war (3:27); the text calls him Mesha’s own heir, though this is likely a mistake of Mesha's own son for an Edomite prince from the opposing army, as Mesha had just attacked the king of Edom and Amos 2:1 independently accuses Moab of burning the bones of an Edomite king.}
\fA{End of chapter!}

