	remission for YHWH has been called.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		demand. This is the same root that is understood in its noun form to
%		mean taskmasters in the story of the enslavement in Egypt (Exod 3:7;
%		5:10). As a verb it means to require, demand, or exact something from
%		someone.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	You may demand it of a foreigner, but your hand shall
	remit whatever of yours is with your brother.
}

\eDtn{
	Nonetheless, there won’t be an indigent one among you,
	because YHWH will bless you in the land that YHWH, your
	God, is giving you as a legacy, to take possession of it—
}

\eDtn{
	only if you’ll listen to the voice of YHWH, your God, to
	be watchful to do all of this commandment that I command
	you today.
}

\eDtn{
	When YHWH, your God, will have blessed you as He spoke to
	you, then you’ll lend to many nations, but you won’t
	borrow; and you’ll dominate many nations, but they won’t
	dominate you.
}

\eDtn{
	But you shall open your hand to him and shall lend to him,
	enough for his shortage that he has.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall give to him, and your heart shall not be bad
	when you’re giving to him, because on account of this
	thing YHWH, your God, will bless you in everything you do
	and in everything your hand has taken on.
}

\eDtn{
	Because there won’t stop being an indigent in the land. On
	account of this I command you, saying: you shall open your
	hand to your brother, to your poor, and to your indigent
	in your land.
}

\eDtn{
	“When your Hebrew brother or sister will be sold to you,
	then he shall work for you six years, and in the seventh
	year you shall let him go liberated from you.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		let him go. This is the same term that was used for Pharaoh’s release of
%		the Israelite slaves: “Let my people go.” The Israelite should do no
%		less than Pharaoh! The Israelite should let his slave go, without the
%		coercion that Pharaoh needed, and after no more than six years.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And when you let him go liberated from you, you shall not
	let him go empty-handed.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall provide him from your flock and from your
	threshing floor and from your winepress; as YHWH, your
	God, has blessed you, you shall give to him.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall remember that you were a slave in the land
	of Egypt, and YHWH, your God, redeemed you. On account of
	this, I command you this thing today.
}

\eDtn{
	“And it will be, if he’ll say to you, ‘I won’t go out from
	you’ because he loves you and your house, because it is
	good for him with you,
}

\eDtn{
	then you shall take an awl and put it in his ear and in
	the door, and he shall be a slave forever to you. And you
	shall also do this to your maid.
}

\eDtn{
	It shall not be hard in your eyes when you let him go
	liberated from you, because he served you six years at
	twice the value of an employee, and YHWH, your God, will
	bless you in everything that you’ll do.
}

\eDtn{
	“Every male firstborn that will be born in your herd and
	in your flock you shall consecrate to YHWH, your God. You
	shall not work with your ox’s firstborn, and you shall not
	shear your sheep’s firstborn.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall eat it in front of YHWH, your God, year by year
	in the place that YHWH will choose—you and your household.
}

\eDtn{
	And if there will be an injury in it—crippled or blind,
	any bad injury—you shall not sacrifice it to YHWH, your
	God.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall eat it within your gates—the impure and the pure
	together, like a gazelle and like a deer.
}

\eDtn{
	Only: you shall not eat its blood. You shall spill it like
	water on the ground.
}

\eDtn{
	“Observe the month of Abib, and you shall make Passover
	for YHWH, your God, because in the month of Abib YHWH,
	your God, brought you out from Egypt at night.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		the month of Abib. See the comment on Exod 13:4.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And you shall make a Passover sacrifice to YHWH, your God,
	of the flock and herd, in the place that YHWH will choose
	to tent His name there.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall not have leaven appear within all your
	borders for seven days, and none of the meat that you will
	sacrifice in the evening on the first day shall remain
	until the morning.
}

\eDtn{
	You may not make the Passover sacrifice within one of your
	gates that YHWH, your God, is giving you.
}

\eDtn{
	But, rather, to the place that YHWH, your God, will choose
	to tent His name: there you shall make the Passover
	sacrifice in the evening at sunset, the time when you went
	out from Egypt.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall cook and eat it in the place that YHWH, your
	God, will choose; and you shall turn in the morning and go
	to your tents.
}

\eDtn{
	Six days you shall eat unleavened bread, and on the
	seventh day shall be a convocation to YHWH, your God. You
	shall not do work.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall count seven weeks. From when the sickle begins
	to be in the standing grain you shall begin to count seven
	weeks.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall make a Festival of Weeks for YHWH, your God,
	the full amount of your hand’s contribution that you can
	give insofar as YHWH, your God, will bless you.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall remember that you were a slave in Egypt, and
	you shall be watchful and do these laws.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall make a Festival of Booths seven days, when you
	gather from your threshing floor and from your winepress.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall rejoice on your festival—you and your son
	and your daughter and your servant and your maid and the
	Levite and the alien and the orphan and the widow who are
	within your gates.
}

\eDtn{
	Seven days you shall celebrate for YHWH, your God, in the
	place that YHWH will choose, because YHWH, your God, will
	bless you in all your produce and all your hands’ work,
	and you shall just be happy.
}

\eDtn{
	each according to what his hand can give, according to the
	blessing of YHWH, your God, that He has given you.
}

\eDtn{
	JUDGES “You shall put judges and officers in all your
	gates that YHWH, your God, is giving you, for your tribes,
	and they shall judge the people: judgment with justice.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall not bend judgment, you shall not recognize a
	face, and you shall not take a bribe, because bribery will
	blind the eyes of the wise and undermine the words of the
	virtuous.
}

\eDtn{
	Justice, justice you shall pursue, so that you’ll live,
	and you’ll take possession of the land that YHWH, your
	God, is giving you.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not plant an Asherah of any wood near the altar
	of YHWH, your God, that you will make.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Asherah. The word is used for the goddess of that name or for a tree or
%		wooden pole that was associated with the worship of Asherah (or possibly
%		other deities as well).
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And you shall not set up a pillar, which YHWH, your God,
	hates.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not sacrifice to YHWH, your God, a bull or a
	sheep in which will be any injury, any bad thing, because
	that is an offensive thing of YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtn{
	“If there will be found among you, in one of your gates
	that YHWH, your God, is giving you, a man or woman who
	will do what is bad in the eyes of YHWH, your God, to
	violate His covenant,
}

\eDtn{
	and will go and serve other gods and bow to them and to
	the sun or to the moon or to any of the array of the
	skies, which I did not command,
}

\eDtn{
	and it will be told to you and you will hear it, and you
	will inquire well, and, here, it is true, the thing is
	right, this offensive thing has been done in Israel,
}

\eDtn{
	then you shall bring that man or that woman who did this
	bad thing out to your gates, the man or the woman, and you
	shall stone them with stones so they die.
}

\eDtn{
	“On the word of two witnesses or three witnesses shall the
	one who is to die be put to death. One shall not be put to
	death on the word of one witness.
}

\eDtn{
	The witnesses’ hand shall be on him first to put him to
	death, and all the people’s hand after that. So you shall
	burn away what is bad from among you.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall come to the Levite priests and to the judge
	who will be in those days, and you shall inquire, and they
	will tell you the matter of judgment.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall do according to the word on the matter that
	they will tell you from that place that YHWH will choose,
	and you shall be watchful to do according to everything
	that they will instruct you.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall do it according to the word of the instruction
	that they will give you and according to the judgment that
	they will say to you. You shall not turn from the thing
	that they will tell you, right or left.
}

\eDtn{
	And the man who will act presumptuously, not listening to
	the priest who is standing to serve YHWH, your God, there,
	or to the judge: that man shall die. So you shall burn
	away what is bad from Israel.
}

\eDtn{
	And all the people will listen and fear and won’t act
	presumptuously anymore.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll come to the land that YHWH, your God, is
	giving you, and you’ll take possession of it and live in
	it, and you’ll say, ‘Let me set a king over me like all
	the nations that are around me,’
}

\eDtn{
	you shall set a king over you whom YHWH, your God, will
	choose! You shall set a king from among your brothers over
	you. You may not put a foreign man, who is not your
	brother, over you.
}

\eDtn{
	Only, he shall not get himself many horses, and he shall
	not bring the people back to Egypt in order to get many
	horses, when YHWH has said to you, ‘You shall not go back
	this way ever again.’
}

\eDtn{
	And he shall not get himself many wives, so his heart will
	not turn away. And he shall not get himself very much
	silver and gold.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, when he sits on his kingdom’s throne, that
	he shall write himself a copy of this instruction on a
	scroll from in front of the Levite priests.
}

\eDtn{
	And it shall be with him, and he shall read it all the
	days of his life, so that he will learn to fear YHWH, his
	God, to observe all the words of this instruction and
	these laws, to do them,
}

\eDtn{
	so his heart will not be elevated above his brothers, and
	so he will not turn from the commandment, right or left,
	and so he will extend days over his kingdom, he and his
	sons, within Israel.
}

\eDtn{
	“The Levite priests, all the tribe of Levi, shall not
	have a portion and legacy with Israel. They shall eat
	YHWH’s offerings by fire and His legacy.
}

\eDtn{
	But he shall not have a legacy among his brothers. YHWH:
	He is his legacy, as He spoke to him.
}

\eDtn{
	And this shall be the rule for the priests from the
	people, from those who make a sacrifice, whether an ox or
	a sheep: he shall give the shoulder, the cheeks, and the
	stomach.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall give him the first of your grain, your wine, and
	your oil, and the first shearing of your sheep.
}

\eDtn{
	Because YHWH, your God, has chosen him from all your
	tribes to stand to serve in YHWH’s name, he and his sons,
	for all time.
}

\eDtn{
	“And when a Levite will come from one of your gates, from
	all of Israel, where he lives, then he shall come as much
	as his soul desires to the place that YHWH will choose
}

\eDtn{
	and serve in the name of YHWH, his God, like all of his
	Levite brothers who are standing there in front of YHWH.
}

\eDtn{
	They shall eat, portion for portion, aside from his sales
	of patrimony.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		portion for portion. Any Levite who comes to serve at the central place
%		of worship is supposed to receive the same share of the priestly food as
%		every other Levite. And this share is not affected by whether he has any
%		other income from family property, i.e., “from his sales of patrimony.”
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	“When you come to the land that YHWH, your God, is giving
	you, you shall not learn to do like the offensive things
	of those nations.
}

\eDtn{
	There shall not be found among you someone who passes his
	son or his daughter through fire, one who practices
	enchantment, a soothsayer or a diviner or a sorcerer
}

\eDtn{
	or one who casts spells or who asks of a ghost or of a
	spirit of an acquaintance or inquires of the dead,
}

\eDtn{
	because everyone who does these is an offensive thing of
	YHWH, and because of these offensive things YHWH, your
	God, is dispossessing them from in front of you.
}

\eDtn{
	because these nations whom you are dispossessing listen to
	soothsayers and enchanters, but you: YHWH, your God, has
	not permitted such for you.
}

\eDtn{
	YHWH, your God, will raise up for you a prophet from among
	you, from your brothers, like me. You shall listen to him—
}

\eDtn{
	in accordance with everything that you asked from YHWH,
	your God, at Horeb in the day of the assembly, saying,
	‘Let me not continue to hear the voice of YHWH, my God,
	and let me not see this big fire anymore, so I won’t die!’
}

\eDtn{
	“And YHWH said to me, ‘They’ve been good in what they’ve
	spoken.
}

\eDtn{
	I’ll raise up a prophet for them from among their
	brothers, like you, and I’ll put my words in his mouth,
	and he’ll speak to them everything that I’ll command him.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be: the man who won’t listen to my words that
	he’ll speak in my name, I shall require it from him!
}

\eDtn{
	Just: the prophet who will presume to speak a thing in my
	name that I didn’t command him to speak, and who will
	speak in the name of other gods—that prophet shall die.’
}

\eDtn{
	“And if you’ll say in your heart, ‘How shall we know the
	thing, that YHWH didn’t speak it?!’—
}

\eDtn{
	when the prophet will speak in YHWH’s name, and the thing
	won’t be and won’t come to pass: that is the thing that
	YHWH did not say. The prophet spoke it presumptuously. You
	shall not be fearful of him.
}

\eDtn{
	“When YHWH, your God, will cut off the nations whose
	land YHWH, your God, is giving you, and you’ll dispossess
	them and live in their cities and in their houses,
}

\eDtn{
	you shall distinguish three cities within your land that
	YHWH, your God, is giving you to take possession of it.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall prepare the way and divide the border of your
	land that YHWH, your God, will give you as a legacy into
	three sections, and it will be for any murderer to flee
	there.
}

\eDtn{
	And this is the case of the manslayer who will flee there
	and live: one who will strike his neighbor without
	knowing, when he did not hate him from the day before
	yesterday;
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		from the day before yesterday. Meaning: he had no prior malice toward
%		the person whom he killed.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	on account of this I command you, saying, ‘You shall
	distinguish three cities.’
}

\eDtn{
	And if YHWH, your God, will widen your border as He swore
	to your fathers and will give you all the land that He
	spoke to give to your fathers,
}

\eDtn{
	when you’ll observe all of this commandment to do it, that
	which I command you today, to love YHWH, your God, and to
	go in His ways every day, then you shall add three more
	cities to these three.
}

\eDtn{
	So innocent blood will not be spilled within your land
	that YHWH, your God, is giving you as a legacy, and blood
	would be on you.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		blood would be on you. Meaning: you would incur guilt for spilled blood.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	“But if there will be a man who hates his neighbor and
	will lie in wait for him and get up against him and strike
	him mortally, and he dies, and he will flee to one of
	these cities,
}

\eDtn{
	then his city’s elders shall send and take him from there
	and put him in the hand of the avenger of blood, so he
	will die.
}

\eDtn{
	Your eye shall not pity him. And you shall burn away the
	innocent blood from Israel, and it will be well with you.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not move your neighbor’s landmark that the
	first ones set, in your legacy that you will have in the
	land that YHWH, your God, is giving you to take possession
	of it.
}

\eDtn{
	“One witness shall not get up against a man for any crime
	or for any sin, in any sin that one will commit. On the
	word of two witnesses or on the word of three witnesses a
	case shall stand up.
}

\eDtn{
	If a malicious witness will get up against a man to
	testify a misrepresentation against him,
}

\eDtn{
	and the two people who have the dispute shall stand in
	front of YHWH, in front of the priests and the judges who
	will be in those days,
}

\eDtn{
	and the judges will inquire well, and, here, the witness
	is a lying witness, he testified a lie against his
	brother,
}

\eDtn{
	then you shall do to him as he schemed to do to his
	brother. So you shall burn away what is bad from among
	you.
}

\eDtn{
	And those who remain will listen and fear and won’t
	continue to do anything like this bad thing anymore among
	you.
}

\eDtn{
	And your eye shall not pity: life for life, eye for eye,
	tooth for tooth, hand for hand, foot for foot.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		life for life, eye for eye, tooth for tooth. See the comments on Exod
%		21:23 and Lev 24:20.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	“When you’ll go out to war against your enemies, and
	you’ll see horses and chariots, a people more numerous
	than you, you shall not fear them, because YHWH, your God,
	is with you, who brought you up from the land of Egypt.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, when you approach the war, that the priest
	shall go over and speak to the people.
}

\eDtn{
	And he shall say to them, ‘Listen, Israel, you’re
	approaching war against your enemies today. Let your heart
	not be weak. Don’t be afraid and don’t panic and don’t be
	scared in front of them,
}

\eDtn{
	because YHWH, your God, is the one going with you to fight
	for you with your enemies, to save you.’
}

\eDtn{
	“And the officers shall speak to the people, saying, ‘Who
	is a man who has built a new house and has not dedicated
	it? Let him go, and let him go back to his house, in case
	he would die in the war and another man would dedicate it.
}

\eDtn{
	And who is a man who has planted a vineyard and has not
	desanctified it? Let him go, and let him go back to his
	house, in case he would die in the war and another man
	would desanctify it.
}

\eDtn{
	And who is a man who has betrothed a woman and has not
	taken her? Let him go, and let him go back to his house,
	in case he would die in the war and another man would take
	her.’
}

\eDtn{
	And the officers shall continue to speak to the people and
	shall say, ‘Who is a man who is afraid and weakhearted?
	Let him go, and go back to his house, so he won’t melt his
	brothers’ heart like his heart.’
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, when the officers finish speaking to the
	people, that they shall appoint officers of the armies at
	the head of the people.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll approach a city to fight against it, then you
	shall call to it for peace.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, if it will answer you with peace and open
	up to you, then it will be that all the people who are
	found in it shall become yours for a work-company and
	shall serve you.
}

\eDtn{
	And if it will not make peace with you, but it will make
	war with you, then you shall besiege it.
}

\eDtn{
	And YHWH, your God, will put it in your hand, and you
	shall strike all its males by the sword.
}

\eDtn{
	Only, you shall take as spoil the women and the infants
	and the animals and everything that will be in the city,
	all its spoil, and you shall eat your enemies’ spoil that
	YHWH, your God, has given you.
}

\eDtn{
	That is what you shall do to all the cities that are very
	distant from you, those that are not from these nations’
	cities.
}

\eDtn{
	Only from these peoples’ cities that YHWH, your God, is
	giving you as a legacy shall you not let any soul live.
}

\eDtn{
	But you shall completely destroy them—the Hittite and the
	Amorite, the Canaanite and the Perizzite, the Hivite and
	the Jebusite—as YHWH, your God, has commanded you,
}

\eDtn{
	so they won’t teach you to do things like all their
	offensive things that they did for their gods, and you’ll
	sin to YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtn{
	Only a tree that you’ll know that it isn’t a tree for
	eating: that one you may destroy and cut down so you may
	build a siege-work against the city that is making war
	with you until its fall.
}

\eDtn{
	“If a corpse will be found in the land that YHWH, your
	God, is giving you to take possession of it, fallen in the
	field, unknown who struck him,
}

\eDtn{
	then your elders and your judges shall go out and measure
	to the cities that are around the corpse.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, the closest city to the corpse: that
	city’s elders shall take a heifer that has not been worked
	with, that has not pulled in a yoke.
}

\eDtn{
	And that city’s elders shall take the heifer down to a
	strongly flowing wadi that would not be worked with and
	would not be seeded, and they shall break the heifer’s
	neck there in the wadi.
}

\eDtn{
	And the priests, sons of Levi, shall go over, because
	YHWH, your God, chose them to serve Him and to bless in
	YHWH’s name, and every dispute and every injury shall be
	by their word.
}

\eDtn{
	And all of that city’s elders, who are close to the
	corpse, shall wash their hands over the heifer whose neck
	was broken in the wadi.
}

\eDtn{
	And they shall testify, and they shall say, ‘Our hands did
	not spill this blood, and our eyes did not see it.
}

\eDtn{
	Grant atonement for your people Israel, whom you redeemed,
	YHWH, and don’t impute innocent blood among your people
	Israel.’ And for them the blood will be atoned for.
}

\eDtn{
	So you shall burn away the innocent blood from among you
	when you will do what is right in YHWH’s eyes.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll go out to war against your
	enemies, and YHWH, your God, will put him in your hand,
	and you’ll take prisoners from him,
}

\eDtn{
	and you’ll see among the prisoners a woman with a
	beautiful figure, and you’ll be attracted to her and take
	her for yourself as a wife,
}

\eDtn{
	then you shall bring her into your house, and she shall
	shave her head and do her nails
}

\eDtn{
	and take away her prisoner’s garment from on her. And she
	shall live in your house and shall mourn her father and
	her mother a month of days. And after that you may come to
	her and marry her, and she shall become your wife.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, if you don’t desire her then you shall let
	her go on her own, and you shall not sell her for money.
	You shall not get profit through her, because you degraded
	her.
}

\eDtn{
	“When a man will have two wives, one loved and one hated,
	and they’ll give birth to children for him, the loved and
	the hated, and the hated will have the firstborn son,
}

\eDtn{
	then it will be, on the day that he gives what he has as a
	legacy to his children, he shall not be able to give a son
	of the loved one the birthright before the firstborn son
	of the hated one.
}

\eDtn{
	But he shall recognize the firstborn son of the hated one,
	to give him a double portion of all that he has, because
	he is the beginning of his might. The legal due of the
	firstborn is his.
}

\eDtn{
	“When a man will have a stubborn and rebellious son, not
	listening to his father’s voice and his mother’s voice,
	and they will discipline him, but he will not listen to
	them,
}

\eDtn{
	then his father and his mother shall take hold of him and
	bring him out to his city’s elders and to the gate of his
	place.
}

\eDtn{
	And they shall say to his city’s elders, ‘This son of ours
	is stubborn and rebellious, he doesn’t listen to our
	voice, a glutton, and a drunk.’
}

\eDtn{
	And all his city’s people shall batter him with stones so
	he dies. So you shall burn away what is bad from among
	you. And all Israel will listen and fear.
}

\eDtn{
	“And if there will be a sin bringing a sentence of death
	on a man, and he will be put to death, and you will hang
	him on a tree,
}

\eDtn{
	you shall not leave his corpse on the tree, but you shall
	bury him on that day, because a hanged person is an
	offense to God, and you shall not make impure your land
	that YHWH, your God, is giving you as a legacy.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not see your brother’s ox or his sheep
	driven off, and you hide yourself from them. You shall
	bring them back to your brother.
}

\eDtn{
	And if your brother is not close to you, and you don’t
	know him, then you shall gather it into your house, and it
	shall be with you until your brother inquires about it,
	and you shall give it back to him.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall do that with his ass, and you shall do that
	with his garment, and you shall do that with any lost
	thing of your brother’s that will be lost by him and you
	find it. You may not hide yourself.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall not see your brother’s ass or his ox falling in
	the road, and you hide yourself from them. You shall lift
	it up with him.
}

\eDtn{
	“There shall not be a man’s item on a woman, and a man
	shall not wear a woman’s garment, because everyone who
	does these is an offensive thing of YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtn{
	“When a bird’s nest will happen to be in front of you on
	the road in any tree or on the ground—chicks or eggs—and
	the mother is sitting over the chicks or over the eggs,
	you shall not take the mother along with the children.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall let the mother go, and you may take the children
	for you, so that it will be good for you, and you will
	extend days.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll build a new house, you shall make a railing
	for your roof, so you won’t set blood in your house when
	someone will fall from it.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not seed your vineyard with two kinds, or else
	the whole of the seed that you’ll sow and the vineyard’s
	produce will become holy.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall make braided threads on the four corners of
	your apparel with which you cover yourself.
}

\eDtn{
	“When a man will take a wife and come to her and then hate
	her,
}

\eDtn{
	and he’ll assert words of abuse toward her and bring out a
	bad name on her and say, ‘I took this woman, and I came
	close to her, and I didn’t find signs of virginity for
	her,’
}

\eDtn{
	then the young woman’s father and her mother shall take
	and bring out the signs of the young woman’s virginity to
	the city’s elders at the gate.
}

\eDtn{
	“And the young woman’s father shall say to the elders, ‘I
	gave my daughter to this man for a wife, and he hated her.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		garment. It is either the clothing she wore on the wedding night or the
%		cloth or sheet beneath the couple that night, on which a bloodstain
%		would be evidence of her virginity.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	“And that city’s elders shall take the man and discipline
	him.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		discipline him. It is unclear if this means a verbal criticism, a
%		flogging, or some other form of chastisement.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And they shall fine him a hundred weights of silver and
	give it to the young woman’s father because he brought out
	a bad name on a virgin of Israel. And she shall be his for
	a wife: he shall not be able to let her go, all his days.
}

\eDtn{
	“But if this thing was true—signs of virginity for the
	young woman were not found—
}

\eDtn{
	“If a man will be found lying with a woman who is a
	husband’s wife, then the two of them shall die: the man
	who lay with the woman, and the woman. So you shall burn
	away what is bad from Israel.
}

\eDtn{
	“If it will be that a virgin young woman will be betrothed
	to a man, and a man will find her in the city and lie with
	her,
}

\eDtn{
	But if the man will find the betrothed young woman in the
	field, and the man will take hold of her and lie with her,
	then only the man who lay with her shall die,
}

\eDtn{
	but you shall not do a thing to the young woman. The young
	woman does not have a sin deserving death, because, just
	as a man would get up against his neighbor and murder him:
	this case is like that;
}

\eDtn{
	because he found her in the field, the betrothed young
	woman cried out, and there was no one to save her.
}

\eDtn{
	“If a man will find a virgin young woman who is not
	betrothed, and he’ll grasp her and lie with her, and
	they’ll be found,
}

\eDtn{
	then the man who lay with her shall give the young woman’s
	father fifty weights of silver, and she shall be his for a
	wife. Because he degraded her, he shall not be able to let
	her go, all his days.
}

\eDtn{
	“A man shall not take his father’s wife, so he will not
	expose his father’s hem.
}

\eDtn{
	One who is wounded by crushing or whose organ is cut off
	shall not come into YHWH’s community.
}

\eDtn{
	A bastard shall not come into YHWH’s community; even in
	the tenth generation one shall not come into YHWH’s
	community.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		bastard. This has not been understood to mean someone born out of
%		wedlock, but rather someone born from one of the forbidden sexual
%		relationships.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	An Ammonite and a Moabite shall not come into YHWH’s
	community; even in the tenth generation they shall not
	come into YHWH’s community, forever,
}

\eDtn{
	on account of the fact that they did not meet you with
	bread and with water on the way when you came out from
	Egypt, and the fact that it hired Balaam son of Beor from
	Pethor of Aram Naharaim against you to curse you.
}

\eDtn{
	But YHWH, your God, was not willing to listen to Balaam,
	and YHWH, your God, turned the curse into a blessing for
	you because YHWH, your God, loved you.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall not seek their well-being or their good, all
	your days, forever.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not abhor an Edomite, because he is your
	brother. You shall not abhor an Egyptian, because you were
	an alien in his land.
}

\eDtn{
	Third-generation children who will be born to them may
	come into YHWH’s community.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll go out encamped against your enemies, you
	shall be watchful against any bad thing.
}

\eDtn{
	If there will be among you a man who will not be pure by a
	night occurrence, then he shall go outside the camp. He
	shall not come inside the camp.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		night occurrence. As in Lev 15:16—“when a man’s intercourse seed will
%		come out from him”—this refers to a flow of semen.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And it shall be: toward evening he shall wash in water,
	and when the sun sets he shall come inside the camp.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall have a location outside the camp, and you
	shall go out there, outside;
}

\eDtn{
	and you shall have a spade among your equipment, and it
	shall be, when you sit outside, that you shall dig with
	it, and you shall go back and cover what comes out of you.
}

\eDtn{
	Because YHWH, your God, is going within your camp, to
	rescue you and to put your enemies in front of you, so
	your camp shall be holy, so He won’t see an exposure of
	something in you and turn back from you.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not turn over to his master a slave who will
	seek deliverance with you from his master.
}

\eDtn{
	He shall live with you, among you, in the place that he
	will choose in one of your gates, where it is good for
	him. You shall not persecute him.
}

\eDtn{
	“There shall not be a sacred prostitute from the daughters
	of Israel, and there shall not be a sacred prostitute from
	the sons of Israel.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall not bring the price of a prostitute or the cost
	of a dog to the house of YHWH, your God, for any vow,
	because the two of them are both an offensive thing of
	YHWH.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not require interest for your brother: interest
	of money, interest of food, interest of anything that one
	might charge.
}

\eDtn{
	For a foreigner you may require it, but for your brother
	you shall not require it, so that YHWH, your God, will
	bless you in everything your hand has taken on, on the
	land to which you’re coming to take possession of it.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll make a vow to YHWH, your God, you shall not
	delay to fulfill it, because YHWH, your God, will require
	it from you, and it will be a sin in you.
}

\eDtn{
	But if you desist from vowing, that will not be a sin in
	you.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall watch what comes out of your lips and do as you
	vowed to YHWH, your God, the contribution that you spoke
	with your mouth.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll come into your neighbor’s vineyard, then you
	may eat grapes as you wish, your fill; but you shall not
	put any into your container.
}

\eDtn{
	When you’ll come into your neighbor’s standing grain, then
	you may pluck ears with your hand; but you shall not lift
	a sickle at your neighbor’s grain.
}

\eDtn{
	and she will go out from his house and go and become
	another man’s,
}

\eDtn{
	and the latter man will hate her and write a document of
	cutting-off for her and put it in her hand and let her go
	from his house, or if the latter man who took her to him
	for a wife will die:
}

\eDtn{
	“When a man will take a new wife, he shall not go out in
	the army and not go along with it for any matter. He shall
	be free at his house for one year and shall make his wife
	whom he has taken happy.
}

\eDtn{
	One shall not take a mill or an upper millstone as
	security, because he is taking one’s life as security.
}

\eDtn{
	“When a man will be found stealing a person from among his
	brothers, from the children of Israel, so he will get
	profit through him and sell him, then that thief shall
	die. So you shall burn away what is bad from among you.
}

\eDtn{
	“Be watchful with the plague of leprosy, to be very
	watchful and to do according to everything that the Levite
	priests will instruct you. You shall be watchful to do
	according to what I commanded them.
}

\eDtn{
	Remember what YHWH, your God, did to Miriam on the way
	when you were coming out from Egypt.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll make a loan of anything to your neighbor, you
	shall not come into his house to get his pledge.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall stand outside, and the man to whom you’re
	lending shall bring the pledge outside to you.
}

\eDtn{
	And if he is a poor man, you shall not lie down with his
	pledge.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall give back the pledge to him as the sun sets, and
	he’ll lie down with his clothing, and he’ll bless you, and
	you’ll have virtue in front of YHWH, your God.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		you’ll have virtue. See the comment on Deut 6:25.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	“You shall not exploit a poor or an indigent employee,
	from your brothers or from your aliens who are in your
	land, in your gates.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall give his pay in his day, and the sun shall not
	set on it—because he is poor, and he maintains his life by
	it—so he won’t call against you to YHWH, and it will be a
	sin in you.
}

\eDtn{
	“Fathers shall not be put to death for sons, and sons
	shall not be put to death for fathers. They shall each be
	put to death through his own sin.
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not bend judgment of an alien or an orphan, and
	you shall not take a widow’s clothing as security.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall remember that you were a slave in Egypt, and
	YHWH, your God, redeemed you from there. On account of
	this I command you to do this thing.
}

\eDtn{
	When you’ll beat your olive trees, you shall not do a
	bough afterward. It shall be the alien’s and the orphan’s
	and the widow’s.
}

\eDtn{
	When you’ll cut off grapes of your vineyard, you shall not
	glean afterward. It shall be the alien’s and the orphan’s
	and the widow’s.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall remember that you were a slave in the land
	of Egypt. On account of this I command you to do this
	thing.
}

\eDtn{
	“When there will be a dispute between people, and they
	will go over to judgment, and they will judge them, then
	they shall find in favor of the one who is in the right
	and find against the one who is in the wrong.
}

\eDtn{
	And it will be, if the one who is in the wrong is to be
	struck, that the judge shall have him laid down and have
	him struck in front of him, according to his wrongdoing in
	number.
}

\eDtn{
	They shall strike him forty times. He shall not add, in
	case he would add onto these to strike him a great amount,
	and your brother would be treated as inconsequential
	before your eyes.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		brother-in-law. This is known as the law of levirate marriage, from the
%		Latin levir, meaning brother-in-law.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And the firstborn to whom she will give birth shall be
	signified by the name of his brother who died, so his name
	will not be wiped out from Israel.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		by the name of his brother who died. The child is not called “son of”
%		followed by the name of his biological father, but rather “son of”
%		followed by the name of the deceased brother of his biological father.
%		In this way the deceased brother’s name is preserved after his death.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	And his city’s elders shall call him and speak to him, and
	if he’ll stand and say, ‘I don’t desire to take her,’
}

\eDtn{
	And his name shall be called in Israel ‘the house of the
	one whose shoe was taken off.’
}

\eDtn{
	“If people will fight together, a man and his brother, and
	the wife of one will come close to rescue her husband from
	the hand of the one striking him, and she’ll put out her
	hand and take hold of his private parts,
}

\eDtn{
	“You shall not have in your bag multiple stones, a big and
	a small.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		multiple stones, a big and a small. Meaning: one should not have larger
%		and smaller weights so as to weigh things dishonestly.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtn{
	You shall not have in your house multiple ephah measures,
	a big and a small.
}

\eDtn{
	You shall have a whole and honest stone; you shall have a
	whole and honest ephah measure, so your days will be
	extended on the land that YHWH, your God, is giving you.
}

\eDtn{
	Because everyone who does these is an offensive thing of
	YHWH, everyone who does injustice.
}

\eDtn{
	“Remember what Amalek did to you on the way when you came
	out from Egypt,
}

\eDtn{
	how he fell upon you on the way and cut off all the weak
	ones at your rear, when you were exhausted and tired, and
	he didn’t fear God.
}

\eDtn{
	So it shall be, when YHWH, your God, will give you rest
	from all your enemies all around in the land that YHWH,
	your God, is giving you as a legacy to take possession of
	it, you shall wipe out the memory of Amalek from under the
	skies. You shall not forget.
}

\eDtn{
	“And it shall be, when you’ll come to
	the land that YHWH, your God, is giving you as a legacy,
	and you’ll take possession of it and live in it,
}

\eDtn{
	that you shall take from the first of all the land’s fruit
	that you’ll bring in from your land that YHWH, your God,
	is giving you, and set it in a basket and go to the place
	that YHWH, your God, will choose to tent His name there.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall come to the priest who will be in those days
	and say to him: I declare today to YHWH, your God, that
	I’ve come to the land that YHWH swore to our fathers to
	give to us.
}

\eDtn{
	And the priest shall take the basket from your hand and
	set it down in front of the altar of YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall answer and say in front of YHWH, your God:
	My father was a perishing Aramean, so he went down to
	Egypt and resided there with few persons and became a big,
	powerful, and numerous nation there.
}

\eDtn{
	And the Egyptians were bad to us and degraded us and
	imposed hard work on us.
}

\eDtn{
	And we cried out to YHWH, our fathers’ God, and YHWH
	listened to our voice and saw our degradation and our
	trouble and our oppression.
}

\eDtn{
	And YHWH brought us out from Egypt with a strong hand and
	an outstretched arm and with great fear and with signs and
	with wonders.
}

\eDtn{
	And He brought us to this place and gave us this land, a
	land flowing with milk and honey.
}

\eDtn{
	And now, here, I’ve brought the first of the fruit of the
	land that you’ve given me, YHWH. And you shall set it down
	in front of YHWH, your God, and bow in front of YHWH, your
	God.
}

\eDtn{
	And you shall rejoice in all the good that YHWH, your God,
	has given to you and to your house, you and the Levite and
	the alien who is among you.
}

\eDtn{
	“When you’ll finish doing all the tithe of your produce in
	the third year, the year of the tithe, and you’ll give it
	to the Levite, to the alien, to the orphan, and to the
	widow, and they’ll eat in your gates and be full,
}

\eDtn{
	Gaze from your holy abode, from the skies, and bless your
	people, Israel, and the land that you’ve given us as you
	swore to our fathers, a land flowing with milk and honey.
}

\eDtrA{
	“This day YHWH, your God, commands you to do these laws
	and judgments. And you shall be watchful and do them with
	all your heart and all your soul.
}

\eDtrA{
	You have proclaimed YHWH today to be God to you, and to go
	in His ways and to observe His laws and His commandments
	and His judgments and to listen to His voice.
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH proclaimed you today to be a treasured people to
	Him as He spoke to you—and to observe all His
	commandments—
}

\eDtrA{
	and to set you high above all the nations that He has made
	in praise and name and beauty, and for you to be a holy
	people to YHWH, your God, as He spoke.”
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses and Israel’s elders commanded the people,
	saying, “Observe all the commandment that I command you
	today.
}

\eDtrA{
	And it shall be, in the day that you’ll cross the Jordan
	to the land that YHWH, your God, is giving you, that you
	shall set up big stones and cover them with plaster.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you shall write on them all the words of this
	instruction when you cross so that you’ll come to the land
	that YHWH, your God, is giving you, a land flowing with
	milk and honey, as YHWH, your fathers’ God, spoke to you.
}

\eDtrA{
	And it shall be, when you cross the Jordan, that you shall
	set up these stones that I command you today in Mount Ebal
	and cover them with plaster.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you shall build an altar to YHWH, your God, there, an
	altar of stones. You shall not lift iron over them.
}

\eDtrA{
	You shall build the altar of YHWH, your God, of whole
	stones, and you shall offer burnt offerings on it to YHWH,
	your God.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		whole stones. Unhewn stones, not cut or shaped with tools.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrA{
	And you shall sacrifice peace offerings and eat there and
	rejoice in front of YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you shall write on the stones all the words of this
	instruction very clearly.”
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses and the Levite priests spoke to all Israel,
	saying, “Be silent, and listen, Israel: this day you have
	become a people to YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you shall listen to the voice of YHWH, your God, and
	do His commandments and His laws that I command you
	today.”
}

\eDtrA{
	“These shall stand to bless the people on Mount Gerizim
	when you cross the Jordan: Simeon and Levi and Judah and
	Issachar and Joseph and Benjamin.
}

\eDtrA{
	And these shall stand for the curse on Mount Ebal: Reuben,
	Gad, and Asher and Zebulun, Dan, and Naphtali.
}

\eDtrA{
	And the Levites shall answer, and they shall say in a loud
	voice to every man of Israel:
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be the man who will make a statue or molten thing,
	an offensive thing of YHWH, a stone engraver’s work, and
	set it up in secret.’ And all the people shall answer, and
	they shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who disrespects one’s father and his
	mother.’ And all the people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who moves his neighbor’s landmark.’ And all
	the people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who misleads a blind person on the way.’
	And all the people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who bends the judgment of an alien, an
	orphan, or a widow.’ And all the people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who lies with his father’s wife, because he
	has exposed his father’s hem.’ And all the people shall
	say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who lies with any animal.’ And all the
	people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who lies with his sister —his father’s
	daughter or his mother’s daughter.’ And all the people
	shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who lies with his mother-in-law.’ And all
	the people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who strikes his neighbor in secret.’ And
	all the people shall say: ‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who takes a bribe to strike a
	person—innocent blood.’ And all the people shall say:
	‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	‘Cursed be one who will not uphold the words of this
	instruction, to do them.’ And all the people shall say:
	‘Amen.’
}

\eDtrA{
	“And it will be, if you’ll listen to the voice of YHWH,
	your God, to be watchful to do all His commandments that I
	command you today, that YHWH, your God, will set you high
	above all the nations of the earth.
}

\eDtrA{
	And all these blessings will come on you and catch up with
	you when you’ll listen to the voice of YHWH, your God:
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll be blessed in the city, and you’ll be blessed in
	the field.
}

\eDtrA{
	The fruit of your womb and the fruit of your land and the
	fruit of your animals, your cattle’s offspring and your
	flock’s young, will be blessed.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll be blessed when you come in, and you’ll be blessed
	when you go out.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will make your enemies who come up against you
	stricken in front of you. By one road they’ll come out at
	you, and by seven roads they’ll flee in front of you.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will command the blessing for you in your storehouses
	and in everything your hand takes on and will bless you in
	the land that YHWH, your God, is giving you.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will establish you for him as a holy people as He
	swore to you if you’ll keep the commandments of YHWH, your
	God, and go in His ways.
}

\eDtrA{
	And all the peoples of the earth will see that YHWH’s name
	is called on you, and they’ll be afraid of you.
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH will give you a surplus of good in the fruit of
	your womb and in the fruit of your animals and in the
	fruit of your land, on the land that YHWH swore to your
	fathers to give you.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will open His good treasure, the skies, to you, to
	give your lands showers at their time and to bless all
	your hand’s work. And you’ll lend to many nations, and you
	won’t borrow;
}

\eDtrA{
	and YHWH will put you at the head and not at the tail; and
	you’ll only be above, and you won’t be below—if you’ll
	listen to the commandments of YHWH, your God, that I
	command you today, to observe and to do,
}

\eDtrA{
	and you won’t turn from all the things that I command you
	today, right or left, to go after other gods, to serve
	them.
}

\eDtrA{
	“And it will be, if you won’t listen to the voice of YHWH,
	your God, to be watchful to do all His commandments and
	His laws that I command you today, that all these curses
	will come on you and catch up with you:
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll be cursed in the city, and you’ll be cursed in the
	field.
}

\eDtrA{
	The fruit of your womb and the fruit of your land, your
	cattle’s offspring and your flock’s young, will be cursed.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll be cursed when you come in, and you’ll be cursed
	when you go out.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will send curse and tumult and annoyance at you in
	everything your hand takes on that you’ll do, until you’re
	destroyed and until you perish quickly because of your bad
	practices, in that you left me.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will make an epidemic cling to you until He finishes
	you from the land to which you’re coming to take
	possession of it.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will strike you with consumption and with fever and
	with inflammation and with burning and with the sword and
	with blight and with mildew. And they’ll pursue you until
	you perish.
}

\eDtrA{
	And your skies that are over your head will be bronze, and
	the land that is under you iron.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will make your land’s showers powder and dust; it
	will fall on you from the skies until you’re destroyed.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will make you stricken in front of your enemies. By
	one road you’ll go out at him, and by seven roads you’ll
	flee in front of him. And you’ll be a horrifying thing to
	all the earth’s kingdoms.
}

\eDtrA{
	And your carcass will become food for every bird of the
	skies and for the animals of the earth, with no one making
	them afraid.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will strike you with the boils of Egypt and with
	hemorrhoids and with scabs and with itches, from which you
	won’t be able to be healed.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will strike you with madness and with blindness and
	with amazement of heart.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you’ll be feeling around at noon the way the blind
	would feel around, in the dark. And you won’t make your
	ways successful, but you’ll just be exploited and robbed
	every day, and there will be no one to save you.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll betroth a woman, and another man will ravish her.
	You’ll build a house, and you won’t live in it. You’ll
	plant a vineyard, and you won’t desanctify it.
}

\eDtrA{
	Your ox slaughtered before your eyes—and you won’t eat any
	of it. Your ass stolen from in front of you—and it won’t
	come back to you. Your sheep given to your enemies—and
	you’ll have no one to save you.
}

\eDtrA{
	Your sons and your daughters given to another people—while
	your eyes are looking for them all day and giving out, and
	there’s no God at your hand.
}

\eDtrA{
	A people whom you haven’t known will eat your land’s fruit
	and all the product of your exhaustion, and you’ll only be
	exploited and crushed every day.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you’ll be driven mad from the sight before your eyes
	that you’ll see.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will strike you with bad boils on the knees and on
	the thighs from which you won’t be able to be healed, from
	your foot to the top of your head.
}

\eDtrB{
	YHWH will drive you and your king whom you’ll set up over
	you to a nation whom you haven’t known, you and your
	fathers, and you’ll serve other gods, wood and stone,
	there.
}

\eDtrB{
	And you’ll become an astonishment, a proverb, and an
	expression among all the peoples to which YHWH will drive
	you.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll take out much seed to the field, but you’ll gather
	little, because locusts will finish it off.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll plant vineyards and work them, but you won’t drink
	wine or gather grapes, because worms will eat it.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll have olives within all your border, but you won’t
	anoint with oil, because your olive will drop off.
}

\eDtrA{
	You’ll give birth to sons and daughters, but you won’t
	have them, because they’ll go into captivity.
}

\eDtrA{
	Crickets will take possession of all your trees and your
	land’s fruit.
}

\eDtrA{
	The alien who is among you will go up above you higher and
	higher, and you will go down lower and lower.
}

\eDtrA{
	He will lend to you, but you won’t lend to him. He will
	become a head, and you will become a tail.
}

\eDtrA{
	“And all these curses will come over you and pursue you
	and catch up with you until you’re destroyed because you
	didn’t listen to the voice of YHWH, your God, to observe
	His commandments and His laws that He commanded you.
}

\eDtrA{
	And they’ll be a sign and a wonder in you and in your seed
	forever.
}

\eDtrA{
	Because you didn’t serve YHWH, your God, with joy and with
	good feeling from the abundance of everything,
}

\eDtrA{
	so you’ll serve your enemies whom YHWH will send at you in
	hunger and in thirst and in nakedness and in lack of
	everything. And He’ll put an iron yoke on your neck until
	He has destroyed you.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will fetch a nation from far, from the end of the
	earth, over you the way an eagle soars, a nation whose
	language you won’t understand,
}

\eDtrA{
	a fierce-faced nation who won’t be partial to the old and
	won’t show grace to the young—
}

\eDtrA{
	and it will eat the fruit of your animals and the fruit of
	your land until you are destroyed—who won’t leave you
	grain, wine, and oil, your cattle’s offspring and your
	flock’s young until it has made you perish.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you’ll eat the fruit of your womb, the flesh of your
	sons and your daughters whom YHWH, your God, has given
	you, in the siege and in the constraint that your enemy
	will put on you.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		you’ll eat … the flesh of your sons. This horrid curse comes true four
%		centuries later during the Aramean siege of Samaria (2 Kings 6:25–29).
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrA{
	The tenderest man among you and the very delicate: his eye
	will look with evil intent at his brother and the wife of
	his bosom and the rest of his children that he’ll have
	left
}

\eDtrA{
	from giving to one of them any of the flesh of his
	children that he’ll eat, because nothing will be left to
	him in the siege and in the constraint that your enemy
	will put on you in all your gates.
}

\eDtrA{
	If you won’t be watchful to do the words of this
	instruction that are written in this scroll, to fear this
	honored and awesome name: YHWH, your God,
}

\eDtrA{
	then YHWH will make your plagues and your seed’s plagues
	astonishing, great and enduring plagues, and great and
	enduring illnesses.
}

\eDtrA{
	And He’ll bring back among you every disease of Egypt,
	which you were dreading, and they’ll cling to you.
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH will bring over you every illness and every plague
	that is not written in this scroll of instruction, as
	well, until you are destroyed.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you’ll be left with few persons when you had been like
	the stars of the skies for multitude, because you didn’t
	listen to the voice of YHWH, your God.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH will scatter you among all the peoples from one
	end of the earth to the other end of the earth. And you’ll
	serve other gods, whom you haven’t known, you and your
	fathers, there, wood and stone.
}

\eDtrB{
	And among those nations you won’t have a respite, and
	there won’t be a resting place for your foot, and YHWH
	will give you there a trembling heart and a failing of
	eyes and a fainting of soul.
}

\eDtrB{
	And your life will be hanging opposite you, and you’ll
	fear night and day, and you won’t trust in your life.
}

\eDtrB{
	In the morning you’ll say, ‘Who would make it evening,’
	and in the evening you’ll say, ‘Who would make it
	morning,’ because of your heart’s fear that you’ll have
	and because of the sight before your eyes that you’ll see.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH will bring you back to Egypt in boats, by the way
	that I said to you: ‘You won’t go on to see it anymore.’
	And you’ll sell yourselves there to your enemies as slaves
	and as maids, and none will buy!”
}

\eDtrA{
	These are the words of the covenant that YHWH commanded
	Moses to make with the children of Israel in the land of
	Moab, aside from the covenant that He made with them at
	Horeb.
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses called all of Israel and said to them,
	“You’ve seen everything that YHWH did before your eyes in
	the land of Egypt to Pharaoh and to all his servants and
	to all his land,
}

\eDtrA{
	the great tests that your eyes saw, those great signs and
	wonders.
}

\eDtrA{
	But YHWH did not give you a heart to know and eyes to see
	and ears to hear until this day.
}

\eDtrA{
	As I led you forty years in the wilderness, your clothing
	did not become worn on you, and your shoe did not become
	worn on your foot,
}

\eDtrA{
	you didn’t eat bread, and you didn’t drink wine and beer,
	so you would know that ‘I am YHWH your God.’
}

\eDtrA{
	And you came to this place. (And Sihon, king of Heshbon,
	and Og, king of Bashan, came out at us for war, and we
	struck them.
}

\eDtrA{
	And we took their land and gave it as a legacy to the
	Reubenite and to the Gadite and to half of the Manassite
	tribe.)
}

\eDtrA{
	And you shall observe the words of this covenant and do
	them so that you’ll understand all that you’ll do.
}

\eDtrA{
	“You’re standing today, all of you, in front of
	YHWH, your God—your heads, your tribes, your elders, and
	your officers, every man of Israel,
}

\eDtrA{
	your infants, your women, and your alien who is in your
	camps, from one who cuts your wood to one who draws your
	water—
}

\eDtrA{
	for you to enter into the covenant of YHWH, your God, and
	into His oath, which YHWH, your God, is making with you
	today,
}

\eDtrA{
	in order to establish you today for Him as a people, and
	He will be a God to you, as He spoke to you and as He
	swore to your fathers: to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob.
}

\eDtrA{
	And I am not making this covenant and this oath with you
	alone,
}

\eDtrA{
	but with the one who is here standing with us today in
	front of YHWH, our God, and with the one who isn’t here
	with us today.
}

\eDtrA{
	Because you know that we lived in the land of Egypt and
	that we passed among the nations that you passed.
}

\eDtrA{
	And you’ve seen their disgraces and their idols, wood and
	stone, silver and gold, that were with them.
}

\eDtrA{
	In case there will be among you a man or woman or family
	or tribe whose heart is turning today from YHWH, our God,
	to go to serve those nations’ gods; in case there is among
	you a root bearing poison and wormwood,
}

\eDtrA{
	and it will be when he hears the words of this oath that
	he’ll feel himself blessed in his heart, saying, ‘I’ll
	have peace, though I’ll go on in my heart’s obstinacy,’ so
	as to annihilate the wet with the dry:
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH will separate him from all of Israel’s tribes for
	bad, according to all the curses of the covenant that is
	written in this scroll of instruction.
}

\eDtrB{
	“And a later generation will say—your children who will
	come up after you, and the foreigner who will come from a
	far land, when they’ll see that land’s plagues and its
	illnesses that YHWH put in it,
}

\eDtrB{
	and all the nations will say, ‘For what did YHWH do
	something like this to this land? What is this big flaring
	of anger?’
}

\eDtrB{
	“And they’ll say, ‘For the fact that they left the
	covenant of YHWH, their fathers’ God, which He made with
	them when He brought them out from the land of Egypt.
}

\eDtrB{
	And they went and served other gods and bowed to them,
	gods whom they hadn’t known and He hadn’t allocated to
	them.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH’s anger flared at that land, to bring over it
	every curse that was written in this scroll.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH plucked them from their land in anger and in fury
	and in great rage, and He threw them into another land, as
	it is this day.’
}

\eDtrA{
	“The hidden things belong to YHWH, our God, and the
	revealed things belong to us and to our children forever,
	to do all the words of this instruction.
}

\eDtrB{
	And it will be, when all these things, the blessing and
	the curse that I’ve put in front of you, will come upon
	you, and you’ll store it in your heart among the nations
	to which YHWH, your God, has driven you,
}

\eDtrB{
	and you’ll come back to YHWH, your God, and listen to His
	voice, according to everything that I command you today,
	you and your children, with all your heart and with all
	your soul,
}

\eDtrB{
	that YHWH, your God, will bring back your captivity and be
	merciful to you. And He’ll come back and gather you from
	all the peoples to which YHWH, your God, has scattered
	you.
}

\eDtrB{
	If you’ll be driven to the end of the skies, YHWH, your
	God, will gather you from there, and He’ll take you from
	there.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH, your God, will bring you to the land that your
	fathers possessed, and you’ll take possession of it, and
	He’ll be good to you and multiply you more than your
	fathers.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH, your God, will circumcise your heart and your
	seed’s heart so as to love YHWH, your God, with all your
	heart and with all your soul so that you’ll live.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH, your God, will put all these curses on your
	enemies and on those who hate you who have pursued you.
}

\eDtrB{
	And you’ll come back and listen to YHWH’s voice and do all
	His commandments that I command you today.
}

\eDtrB{
	when you’ll listen to the voice of YHWH, your God, to
	observe His commandments and His laws, written in this
	scroll of instruction, when you’ll come back to YHWH, your
	God, with all your heart and with all your soul.
}

\eDtrA{
	“Because this commandment that I command you today: it’s
	not too wondrous for you, and it’s not too far.
}

\eDtrA{
	It’s not in the skies, that one would say, ‘Who will go up
	for us to the skies and get it for us and enable us to
	hear it so we’ll do it?’
}

\eDtrA{
	And it’s not across the sea, that one would say, ‘Who will
	cross for us, across the sea, and get it for us and enable
	us to hear it so we’ll do it?’
}

\eDtrA{
	But the thing is very close to you, in your mouth, and in
	your heart, to do it.
}

\eDtrB{
	“See: I’ve put in front of you today life and good, and
	death and bad,
}

\eDtrB{
	But if your heart will turn away, and you won’t listen,
	and you’ll be driven so that you’ll bow to other gods and
	serve them,
}

\eDtrB{
	I’ve told you today that you’ll perish. You won’t extend
	days on the land to which you’re crossing the Jordan to
	come to take possession of it.
}

\eDtrB{
	I call the skies and the earth to witness regarding you
	today: I’ve put life and death in front of you, blessing
	and curse. And you shall choose life, so you’ll live, you
	and your seed,
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses went and spoke these things to
	all Israel.
}

\eDtrA{
	And he said to them, “I’m a hundred twenty years old
	today. I’m not able to go out and come in anymore. And
	YHWH said to me, ‘You shall not cross this Jordan.’
}

\eDtrA{
	YHWH, your God: He is crossing in front of you. He’ll
	destroy these nations in front of you, and you’ll
	dispossess them. Joshua: he is crossing in front of you,
	as YHWH has spoken.
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH will do to them as He did to Sihon and to Og, the
	kings of the Amorites, and to their land, that He
	destroyed them.
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH will put them in front of you, and you shall do
	to them according to all of the commandment that I’ve
	commanded you.
}

\eDtrA{
	Be strong and be bold. Don’t be afraid and don’t be scared
	in front of them, because YHWH, your God: He is the one
	going with you. He won’t let you down and won’t leave
	you.”
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH: He is the one who is going in front of you. He
	will be with you. He won’t let you down and won’t leave
	you. You shall not fear, and you shall not be dismayed.”
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses wrote this instruction and gave it to the
	priests, sons of Levi, who were carrying the ark of YHWH’s
	covenant and to all of Israel’s elders.
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses commanded them, saying, “At the end of seven
	years, at the appointed time of the year of the remission,
	on the Festival of Booths,
}

\eDtrA{
	when all Israel comes to appear before YHWH, your God, in
	the place that He will choose, you shall read this
	instruction in front of all Israel in their ears.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		your alien. Five times in the Torah, laws about aliens in Israel say
%		“your alien” (compare “your poor” in the Covenant Code; Exod
%		23:6)—including in the Decalogue in both Exodus and Deuteronomy.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrA{
	And their children who have not known will listen and
	learn to fear YHWH, your God, all the days that you’re
	living on the land to which you’re crossing the Jordan to
	take possession of it.”
}

\eE{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Here, your days to die have come
	close. Call Joshua, and stand up in the Tent of Meeting,
	and I’ll command him.” And Moses and Joshua went and stood
	up in the Tent of Meeting.
}

\eE{
	And YHWH appeared in the tent in a column of cloud, and
	the column of cloud stood at the entrance of the tent.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH said to Moses, “Here, when you’re lying with your
	fathers, this people will get up and whore after foreign
	gods of the land into which it is coming, and it will
	leave me and break my covenant that I’ve made with it.
}

\eDtrB{
	But I: I’ll hide my face on that day over all the bad that
	it has done, because it turned to other gods.
}

\eDtrB{
	So now write this song and teach it to the children of
	Israel. Set it in their mouths, so this song will become a
	witness for me among the children of Israel.
}

\eDtrB{
	When I’ll bring it to the land that I swore to its
	fathers, flowing with milk and honey, and it will eat and
	be full and get fat and turn to other gods, and they will
	serve them and reject me, and it will break my covenant,
}

\eDtrB{
	And Moses wrote this song on that day, and he taught it to
	the children of Israel.
}

\eE{
	And He commanded Joshua, son of Nun, and said, “Be strong
	and bold, because you will bring the children of Israel to
	the land that I swore to them, and I shall be with you.”
}

\eDtrA{
	And it was when Moses finished writing the words of this
	instruction on a scroll to their end,
}

\eDtrA{
	and Moses commanded the Levites, who carried the ark of
	the covenant of YHWH, saying,
}

\eDtrA{
	“Take this scroll of instruction and set it at the side of
	the ark of the covenant of YHWH, your God, and it will be
	there for you as a witness.
}

\eDtrA{
	Because I know your rebellion and your hard neck. Here,
	while I’m still alive with you today, you’ve been
	rebelling at YHWH, so how much more after my death!
}

\eDtrB{
	Assemble all the elders of your tribes and your officers
	to me so I may speak these things in their ears and call
	the skies and the earth to witness regarding them,
}

\eDtrB{
	And Moses spoke in the ears of all the community of Israel
	the words of this song to their end:
}

\eDtrB{
	Listen, skies, so I may speak and let the earth
	hear what my mouth says.
}

\eDtrB{
	Let my teaching come down like showers; let my saying
	emerge like dew, like raindrops on plants and like
	rainfalls on herbs.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		plants. This word, too, occurs among the first words of the Torah and
%		does not occur again until its appearance here. In Genesis, God causes
%		the earth to produce plants by His word (Gen 1:11–12). Now Moses turns
%		this image and sings of his words dropping like rain on the plants.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrB{
	The Rock: His work is unblemished, for all His ways are
	judgment. A God of trust, and without injustice, He’s
	virtuous and right.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		The Rock. The first letter of the word is oversized in the Torah,
%		possibly to distinguish it from other uses in the song. The song plays
%		on this word, using it seven more times with several different meanings.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrB{
	It corrupted at Him—not His children, their flaw— a
	crooked and twisted generation.
}

\eDtrB{
	Is it to YHWH that you repay like this?! Foolish people
	and unwise! Isn’t He your father, who created you, He who
	made you and reared you?
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Is it to YHWH … ?! The first letter (the Hebrew interrogative h, which
%		makes the line a question) is oversized in many manuscripts. This seems
%		to underscore the question, to make it incredible: “You’d repay God this
%		way?!”
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrB{
	Remember the days of old. Grasp the years through
	generations. Ask your father, and he’ll tell you, your
	elders, and they’ll say to you:
}

tex.original:
\hDtrB{בְּהַנְחֵל}
\hDtrB{\hlB{עֶלְיוֹן}}
\hDtrB{גּוֹיִם בְּהַפְרִידוֹ בְּנֵי אָדָם יַצֵּב גְּבֻלֹת עַמִּים לְמִסְפַּר בְּנֵי אֱלֹהִים׃}

tex.english:
\eDtrB{
	When \hlB{the Highest} gave nations legacies, when He dispersed
	humankind, He set the peoples’ borders to the number of
	the children of the gods.
}

\eDtrB{
	For YHWH’s portion is His people. Jacob is the share of
	His legacy.
}

\eDtrB{
	He found it in a wilderness land and in a formless place,
	a howling desert. He surrounded it. He attended to it. He
	guarded it, like the pupil of his eye.
}

\eDtrB{
	As an eagle stirs its nest, hovers over its young, spreads
	its wings, takes it, lifts it on its pinion,
}

\eDtrB{
	He had it ride over earth’s high places and fed it the
	field’s bounties and had it suck honey from a rock and oil
	from a flint rock
}

\eDtrB{
	and curds of cattle and milk of the flock and fat of lambs
	and Bashan rams and he-goats with fat of innards of wheat
	—and from grape’s blood you drank wine.
}

\eDtrB{
	And Jeshurun got fat and kicked —you got fat, you got
	wide, you got stuffed!— and it left God who made it and
	took its saving rock for granted.
}

\eDtrB{
	They made Him jealous with outsiders. With offensive
	things they made him angry.
}

\eDtrB{
	They sacrificed to demons, a non-god, gods they hadn’t
	known; new ones, they came of late; your fathers hadn’t
	been acquainted with them.
}

\eDtrB{
	The rock that fathered you, you ignored, and you forgot
	God who bore you.
}

\eDtrB{
	And YHWH saw and rejected from His sons’ and His
	daughters’ angering.
}

\eDtrB{
	And He said, “Let me hide my face from them; I’ll see what
	their future will be. For they’re a generation of
	overthrows, children with no trust in them.
}

\eDtrB{
	They made me jealous with no-god. They made me angry with
	their nothings. And I: I’ll make them jealous with
	no-people. I’ll make them angry with a foolish nation.
}

\eDtrB{
	For fire has ignited in my anger and burned to Sheol at
	bottom and consumed land and its crop and set the
	mountains’ foundations ablaze.
}

\eDtrB{
	I’ll mass bad things over them. I’ll exhaust my arrows on
	them,
}

\eDtrB{
	sapped by hunger and devoured by flame. And bitter
	destruction and animals’ teeth I’ll let loose at them with
	venom of serpents of the dust.
}

\eDtrB{
	Outside: a sword will bereave, and inside: terror, both
	young man and virgin, suckling with aged man.
}

\eDtrB{
	I’d say, ‘I’ll erase them. I’ll make their memory cease
	from mankind,’
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		erase them … make their memory cease. To capture the full irony and
%		horror of these words, one must recall that this is what is supposed to
%		happen to Amalek, the people who symbolize Israel’s worst enemies: “wipe
%		out the memory of Amalek” (Deut 25:19).
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrB{
	if I didn’t fear the enemy’s anger, in case their foes
	would misread, in case they’d say, ‘Our hand was high, and
	it wasn’t YHWH who did all this!’
}

\eDtrB{
	For they’re a nation void of counsel, and there’s no
	understanding in them.
}

\eDtrB{
	If they were wise they’d comprehend this; they’d grasp
	their future.
}

\eDtrB{
	How could one pursue a thousand and two chase ten thousand
	if not that their rock had sold them, that YHWH had turned
	them over?”
}

\eDtrB{
	For their rock is not like our rock, though our enemies
	are the judges.
}

\eDtrB{
	For their vine is from Sodom’s vine and from Gomorrah’s
	fields. Their grapes are poison grapes. They have bitter
	clusters.
}

\eDtrB{
	Vengeance and recompense are mine, for the time their foot
	will slip; for the day of their ordeal is close and comes
	fast: things prepared for them.”
}

\eDtrB{
	For YHWH will judge His people and regret about His
	servants when He’ll see that their strength is gone and
	there’s none: held back or left alone.
}

\eDtrB{
	And He’ll say, “Where are their gods, the rock in whom
	they sought refuge,
}

\eDtrB{
	who would eat the fat of their sacrifices, would drink the
	wine of their libations? Let them get up and help you! Let
	that be a shelter over you.
}

\eDtrB{
	See now that I, I am He, and there is no god with me. I
	cause death and give life. I’ve pierced, and I’ll heal.
	And there’s no deliverer from my hand,
}

\eDtrB{
	when I raise my hand to the skies, and I say, ‘As I live
	forever,
}

\eDtrB{
	if I whet the lightning of my sword, and my hand takes
	hold of judgment, I’ll give back vengeance to my foes and
	pay back those who hate me.
}

\eDtrB{
	I’ll make my arrows drunk with blood, and my sword will
	eat flesh from the blood of the slain and captured, from
	the head of loose hair of the enemy.’”
}

\eDtrB{
	Nations: cheer His people! For He’ll requite His servants’
	blood and give back vengeance to His foes and make
	atonement for His land, His people.
}

\eDtrB{
	And Moses came and spoke all the words of this song in the
	people’s ears, he and Hoshea son of Nun.
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses finished speaking all these things to all of
	Israel.
}

\eDtrA{
	And he said to them, “Pay attention to all the things that
	I testify regarding you today, that you’ll command them to
	your children, to observe and to do all the words of this
	instruction.
}

\eDtrA{
	Because it’s not an empty thing for you, because it’s your
	life. And through this thing you’ll extend days on the
	land to which you’re crossing the Jordan to take
	possession of it.”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		it’s your life. Again we are reminded here at the end of the Torah that
%		these things are a path back to the tree of life that was lost at the
%		Torah’s beginning. See the comment on Deut 30:19.
%	}

tex.english:
\eR{
	“Go up to this mountain of Abarim, Mount Nebo, which is in
	the land of Moab, which is facing Jericho, and see the
	land of Canaan, which I’m giving to the children of Israel
	for a possession.%
}

\eR{
	And die in the mountain to which you’re going up, and be
	gathered to your people, as Aaron, your brother, died in
	Mount Hor and was gathered to his people,%
}

\eR{
	because you made a breach with me among the children of
	Israel at the waters of Meribah of Kadesh at the
	wilderness of Zin, because you didn’t make me holy among
	the children of Israel.%
}

\eR{
	Because you’ll see the land from opposite, but you shall
	not come there, to the land that I’m giving to the
	children of Israel.%
}

\eProto{
	And this is the blessing with which Moses, the man of God,
	blessed the children of Israel before his death.
}

\eProto{
	And he said: YHWH came from Sinai and rose from Seir for
	them. He shone from Mount Paran and came from ten
	thousands of the holy, slopes at His right, for them.
}

\eProto{
	Also loving peoples, all holy ones are in your hand. And
	they knelt at your feet; they bore your words.
}

\eProto{
	Moses commanded us instruction, a possession, community of
	Jacob.
}

\eProto{
	And He was king in Jeshurun when the people’s heads were
	gathered: Israel’s tribes together.
}

\eProto{
	Let Reuben live and not die, but his men will be few in
	number.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		few in number. The territory of Reuben was the most vulnerable of any
%		tribe: located east of the Dead Sea, cut off from the tribes that were
%		west of the Jordan. It virtually ceased to exist by the tenth century
%		B.C.E.
%	}

tex.english:
\eProto{
	And this for Judah—and he said: Hear, YHWH, Judah’s voice,
	and bring him to his people. With his hands he strove for
	himself, and you’ll be a help from its foes.
}

\eProto{
	And for Levi he said: Your Tummim and your Urim are your
	faithful man’s, whom you tested at Massah, disputed with
	him over Meribah’s water.
}

\eProto{
	Who said of his father and his mother: “I haven’t seen
	him,” and didn’t recognize his brothers and didn’t know
	his children, for they observed what you said and kept
	your covenant.
}

\eProto{
	They’ll teach your judgments to Jacob and your instruction
	to Israel. They’ll set incense at your nose, entirely
	burnt on your altar.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		entirely burnt on your altar. The term for “entirely burnt”
%		(klîl) is used in connection with the offering that the priests
%		make on the day of the high priest’s anointing (Lev 6:12–16). It thus
%		especially symbolizes the priestly status of Levi.
%	}

tex.english:
\eProto{
	Bless, YHWH, his wealth and accept his hands’ work. Pierce
	his adversaries’ hips, and those who hate him, so they
	won’t get up.
}

\eProto{
	For Benjamin he said: Beloved of YHWH, he’ll dwell in
	security by Him. He shelters over him all day as he dwells
	between his shoulders.
}

\eProto{
	And for Joseph he said: Blessed of YHWH is his land, from
	the skies’ abundance, from dew, and from the deep,
	crouching below,
}

\eProto{
	and from the abundance of the sun’s produce and from the
	abundance of the moon’s output
}

\eProto{
	and from the top of the ancient mountains and from the
	abundance of the hills of antiquity
}

\eProto{
	and from the abundance of earth and what fills it and the
	favor of the one who dwelt in the bush. May it be on
	Joseph’s head, on the top of the head of the one separate
	from his brothers.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		May it be. Following Cross and Freedman, I have read the Hebrew
%		on the likelihood that it conflated two readings: “May it come”
%		() and “May it be” (). The latter is preferable on the
%		basis of comparison with the parallel blessing of Joseph in Gen 49:26.
%	}

tex.english:
\eProto{
	His firstborn bull: it has majesty. And its horns are a
	wild ox’s horns. It will gore peoples with them, together,
	the ends of the earth. And they’re Ephraim’s ten
	thousands, And they’re Manasseh’s thousands.
}

\eProto{
	And for Zebulun he said: Rejoice, Zebulun, when you go
	out, and Issachar in your tents.
}

\eProto{
	They’ll call peoples to the mountain. There they’ll offer
	the sacrifices of virtue. For they’ll suck the seas’
	bounty and the sand’s hidden treasures.
}

\eProto{
	And for Gad he said: Blessed is one who enlarges Gad. Like
	a feline, abiding and tearing an arm and the top of a
	head,
}

\eProto{
	so he saw the foremost for himself, for a ruler’s share
	was kept there. And the heads of the people came. And he
	did YHWH’s justice and His laws with Israel.
}

\eProto{
	And for Dan he said: Dan is a lion’s whelp that leapt from
	Bashan.
}

\eProto{
	And for Naphtali he said: Naphtali is full of favor and
	filled with YHWH’s blessing. He’ll possess west and south.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		west and south. But Naphtali is located in the north and east of Israel.
%		So this verse is understood to refer to the west and south of the Sea of
%		Galilee (known also as Sea of Tiberias, or Sea of Kinneret).
%	}

tex.english:
\eProto{
	And for Asher he said: Blessed out of the sons is Asher.
	Let him be favored by his brothers and dipping his foot in
	oil.
}

\eProto{
	Your lock is iron and bronze, and your strength is as much
	as your days.
}

\eProto{
	There’s none like the God of Jeshurun, riding skies to
	help you and clouds in His majesty.
}

\eProto{
	The ancient God is a refuge; and below: the arms of the
	eternal. And He drove out an enemy before you and said,
	“Destroy!”
}

\eProto{
	And Israel will dwell secure, Jacob dwells alone. To a
	land of grain and wine; and its skies drop dew.
}

\eProto{
	Happy are you, Israel! Who is like you, a people saved by
	YHWH, your strong shield who is your majestic sword?! And
	your enemies will fawn to you. And you: you’ll step on
	their high places.
}

\eDtrA{
	And Moses went up from the plains of Moab to Mount
	Nebo, the top of Pisgah, which is facing Jericho. And YHWH
	showed him all of the land, Gilead to Dan,
}

\eDtrA{
	and all of Naphtali and the land of Ephraim and Manasseh
	and all the land of Judah to the far sea
}

\eDtrA{
	and the Negeb and the plain, the valley of Jericho, city
	of the palms, to Zoar.
}

\eDtrA{
	And YHWH said to him, “This is the land that I swore to
	Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, saying, ‘I’ll give it to
	your seed.’ I’ve caused you to see it with your eyes, but
	you won’t pass there.”
}

\eJ{
	And Moses, YHWH’s servant, died there in the land of Moab
	by YHWH’s mouth,
}

\eJ{
	and He buried him in the valley in the land of Moab
	opposite Beth Peor. And no man knows his burial place to
	this day.
}

\eJ{
	And Moses was a hundred twenty years old at his death. His
	eye was not dim, and his vitality had not fled.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		a hundred twenty years old. Moses gets the maximum that anyone can live
%		according to YHWH’s decree in Genesis: “My spirit won’t stay in
%		humankind forever, since they’re also flesh; and their days shall be a
%		hundred twenty years” (Gen 6:3).
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrB{
	And the children of Israel mourned Moses in the plains of
	Moab thirty days. And the days of weeping, the mourning of
	Moses, ended.
}

\eDtrB{
	And Joshua son of Nun was full of the spirit of wisdom
	because Moses had laid his hands on him, and the children
	of Israel listened to him. And they did as YHWH commanded
	Moses.
}

\eDtrA{
	And a prophet did not rise again in Israel like Moses,
	whom YHWH knew face-to-face,
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		face-to-face. This does not appear to be meant literally. See the
%		comments on Exod 33:11 and Deut 5:4.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrA{
	with all the signs and the wonders that YHWH sent him to
	do in the land of Egypt to Pharaoh and to all his servants
	and to all his land,
}

\eDtrA{
	and with all the strong hand and with all the great fear
	that Moses made before the eyes of all Israel.
}

\eJ{
	After the death of Moses the servant of YHWH, YHWH said to Joshua
	son of Nun, Moses’ attendant:
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 1:1. Moses' attendant. The Hebrew calls Joshua Moses' m'sharet, the one
%		who serves or attends him. At the end of the book Joshua will be called YHWH's
%		servant. The title is not just promotion. It is the whole plot in two words.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“My servant Moses is dead. Prepare to cross the Jordan, together
	with all this people, into the land that I am giving to the
	Israelites.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Every spot on which your foot treads I give to you, as I promised
	Moses.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Your territory shall extend from the wilderness and the Lebanon to
	the Great River, the River Euphrates [on the east]—the whole
	Hittite country—and up to the Mediterranean Sea on the west.%
}

\eDtrH{
	No one shall be able to resist you as long as you live. As I was
	with Moses, so I will be with you; I will not fail you or forsake
	you.%
}

\eJ{
	“Be strong and resolute, for you shall apportion to this people
	the land that I swore to their fathers to assign to them.
}

\eDtrH{
	But you must be very strong and resolute to observe faithfully all
	the Teaching that My servant Moses enjoined upon you. Do not
	deviate from it to the right or to the left, that you may be
	successful wherever you go.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Let not this Book of the Teaching cease from your lips, but recite
	it day and night, so that you may observe faithfully all that is
	written in it. Only then will you prosper in your undertakings and
	only then will you be successful.%
}

\eJ{
	“I charge you: Be strong and resolute; do not be terrified or
	dismayed, for YHWH your God is with you wherever you go.”
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua thereupon gave orders to the officials of the people:%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	עִבְרוּ בְּקֶרֶב הַמַּחֲנֶה וְצַוּוּ אֶת־הָעָם לֵאמֹר הָכִינוּ לָכֶם צֵידָה כִּי בְּעוֹד שְׁלֹשֶׁת יָמִים אַתֶּם עֹבְרִים אֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן הַזֶּה לָבוֹא לָרֶשֶׁת אֶת־הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר
	יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם נֹתֵן לָכֶם לְרִשְׁתָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“Go through the camp and charge the people thus: Get provisions
	ready, for in three days’ time you are to cross the Jordan, in
	order to enter and possess the land that YHWH your God is giving
	you as a possession.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then Joshua said to the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the
	half-tribe of Manasseh,%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Remember what Moses the servant of YHWH enjoined upon you, when
	he said, ‘YHWH your God is granting you a haven; He has assigned
	this territory to you’:%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	נְשֵׁיכֶם טַפְּכֶם וּמִקְנֵיכֶם יֵשְׁבוּ בָּאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לָכֶם מֹשֶׁה בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן וְאַתֶּם תַּעַבְרוּ חֲמֻשִׁים לִפְנֵי אֲחֵיכֶם כֹּל גִּבּוֹרֵי הַחַיִל וַעֲזַרְתֶּם
	אוֹתָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Let your wives, children, and livestock remain in the land that
	Moses assigned to you on this side of the Jordan; but every one of
	your fighting men shall go across armed in the van of your
	kinsmen. And you shall assist them%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	עַד אֲשֶׁר־יָנִיחַ יְהֹוָה לַאֲחֵיכֶם כָּכֶם וְיָרְשׁוּ גַם־הֵמָּה אֶת־הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם נֹתֵן לָהֶם וְשַׁבְתֶּם לְאֶרֶץ יְרֻשַּׁתְכֶם וִירִשְׁתֶּם אוֹתָהּ אֲשֶׁר
	נָתַן לָכֶם מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד יְהֹוָה בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְרַח הַשָּׁמֶשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	They answered Joshua, “We will do everything you have commanded us
	and we will go wherever you send us.%
}

\eDtrH{
	We will obey you just as we obeyed Moses; let but YHWH your God be
	with you as He was with Moses!%
}

\eDtrH{
	Any man who flouts your commands and does not obey every order you
	give him shall be put to death. Only be strong and resolute!”%
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יְהוֹשֻׁעַ־בִּן־נוּן מִן־הַשִּׁטִּים שְׁנַיִם־אֲנָשִׁים מְרַגְּלִים חֶרֶשׁ לֵאמֹר לְכוּ רְאוּ אֶת־הָאָרֶץ וְאֶת־יְרִיחוֹ וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ בֵּית־אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה
	וּשְׁמָהּ רָחָב וַיִּשְׁכְּבוּ־שָׁמָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joshua son of Nun secretly sent two spies from Shittim, saying,
	“Go, reconnoiter the region of Jericho.” So they set out, and they
	came to the house of a harlot named Rahab and lodged there.
}

\eJ{
	The king of Jericho was told, “Some men have come here tonight,
	Israelites, to spy out the country.”
}

\eJ{
	The king of Jericho thereupon sent orders to Rahab: “Produce the
	men who came to you and entered your house, for they have come to
	spy out the whole country.”
}

\eJ{
	The woman, however, had taken the two men and hidden them. “It is
	true,” she said, “the men did come to me, but I didn’t know where
	they were from.
}

\eJ{
	And at dark, when the gate was about to be closed, the men left;
	and I don’t know where the men went. Quick, go after them, for you
	can overtake them.”—
}

\eJ{
	Now she had taken them up to the roof and hidden them under some
	stalks of flax which she had lying on the roof.—
}

\eJ{
	So the men pursued them in the direction of the Jordan down to the
	fords; and no sooner had the pursuers gone out than the gate was
	shut behind them.
}

\eJ{
	The spies had not yet gone to sleep when she came up to them on
	the roof.
}

\eJ{
	She said to the men, “I know that YHWH has given the country to
	you, because dread of you has fallen upon us, and all the
	inhabitants of the land are quaking before you.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	כִּי שָׁמַעְנוּ אֵת אֲשֶׁר־הוֹבִישׁ יְהֹוָה אֶת־מֵי יַם־סוּף מִפְּנֵיכֶם בְּצֵאתְכֶם מִמִּצְרָיִם וַאֲשֶׁר עֲשִׂיתֶם לִשְׁנֵי מַלְכֵי הָאֱמֹרִי אֲשֶׁר בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן לְסִיחֹן
	וּלְעוֹג אֲשֶׁר הֶחֱרַמְתֶּם אוֹתָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	For we have heard how YHWH dried up the waters of the Sea of Reeds
	for you when you left Egypt, and what you did to Sihon and Og, the
	two Amorite kings across the Jordan, whom you doomed.
}

\eJ{
	When we heard about it, we lost heart, and no man had any more
	spirit left because of you; for YHWH your God is the only God in
	heaven above and on earth below.
}

\eJ{
	Now, since I have shown loyalty to you, swear to me by YHWH that
	you in turn will show loyalty to my family. Provide me with a
	reliable sign
}

\eJ{
	that you will spare the lives of my father and mother, my brothers
	and sisters, and all who belong to them, and save us from death.”
}

\eJ{
	The men answered her, “Our persons are pledged for yours, even to
	death! If you do not disclose this mission of ours, we will show
	you true loyalty when YHWH gives us the land.”
}

\eJ{
	She let them down by a rope through the window—for her dwelling
	was at the outer side of the city wall and she lived in the actual
	wall.
}

\eJ{
	She said to them, “Make for the hills, so that the pursuers may
	not come upon you. Stay there in hiding three days, until the
	pursuers return; then go your way.”
}

\eJ{
	But the men warned her, “We will be released from this oath which
	you have made us take
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	הִנֵּה אֲנַחְנוּ בָאִים בָּאָרֶץ אֶת־תִּקְוַת חוּט הַשָּׁנִי הַזֶּה תִּקְשְׁרִי בַּחַלּוֹן אֲשֶׁר הוֹרַדְתֵּנוּ בוֹ וְאֶת־אָבִיךְ וְאֶת־אִמֵּךְ וְאֶת־אַחַיִךְ וְאֵת כּל־בֵּית
	אָבִיךְ תַּאַסְפִי אֵלַיִךְ הַבָּיְתָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	[unless,] when we invade the country, you tie this length of
	crimson cord to the window through which you let us down. Bring
	your father, your mother, your brothers, and all your family
	together in your house;
}

\eJ{
	and if anyone ventures outside the doors of your house, his blood
	will be on his head, and we shall be clear. But if a hand is laid
	on anyone who remains in the house with you, his blood shall be on
	our heads.
}

\eJ{
	And if you disclose this mission of ours, we shall likewise be
	released from the oath which you made us take.”
}

\eJ{
	She replied, “Let it be as you say.” She sent them on their way,
	and they left; and she tied the crimson cord to the window.
}

\eJ{
	They went straight to the hills and stayed there three days, until
	the pursuers turned back. And so the pursuers, searching all along
	the road, did not find them.
}

\eJ{
	Then the two men came down again from the hills and crossed over.
	They came to Joshua son of Nun and reported to him all that had
	happened to them.
}

\eJ{
	They said to Joshua, “YHWH has delivered the whole land into our
	power; in fact, all the inhabitants of the land are quaking before
	us.”
}

\eJ{
	Early next morning, Joshua and all the Israelites set out from
	Shittim and marched to the Jordan. They did not cross immediately,
	but spent the night there.
}

\eJ{
	and charged the people as follows: “When you see the Ark of the
	Covenant of YHWH your God being borne by the levitical priests,
	you shall move forward. Follow it—
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	אַךְ רָחוֹק יִהְיֶה בֵּינֵיכֶם וּבֵנָיו כְּאַלְפַּיִם אַמָּה בַּמִּדָּה אַל־תִּקְרְבוּ אֵלָיו לְמַעַן אֲשֶׁר־תֵּדְעוּ אֶת־הַדֶּרֶךְ אֲשֶׁר תֵּלְכוּ־בָהּ כִּי לֹא עֲבַרְתֶּם
	בַּדֶּרֶךְ מִתְּמוֹל שִׁלְשׁוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	but keep a distance of some two thousand cubits from it, never
	coming any closer to it—so that you may know by what route to
	march, since it is a road you have not traveled before.”
}

\eJ{
	And Joshua said to the people, “Purify yourselves, for tomorrow
	YHWH will perform wonders in your midst.”
}

\eJ{
	Then Joshua ordered the priests, “Take up the Ark of the Covenant
	and advance to the head of the people.” And they took up the Ark
	of the Covenant and marched at the head of the people.
}

\eJ{
	YHWH said to Joshua, “This day, for the first time, I will exalt
	you in the sight of all Israel, so that they shall know that I
	will be with you as I was with Moses.
}

\eJ{
	For your part, command the priests who carry the Ark of the
	Covenant as follows: When you reach the edge of the waters of the
	Jordan, make a halt in the Jordan.”
}

\eJ{
	And Joshua said to the Israelites, “Come closer and listen to the
	words of YHWH your God.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בְּזֹאת תֵּדְעוּן כִּי אֵל חַי בְּקִרְבְּכֶם וְהוֹרֵשׁ יוֹרִישׁ מִפְּנֵיכֶם אֶת־הַכְּנַעֲנִי וְאֶת־הַחִתִּי וְאֶת־הַחִוִּי וְאֶת־הַפְּרִזִּי
	וְאֶת־הַגִּרְגָּשִׁי וְהָאֱמֹרִי וְהַיְבוּסִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	By this,” Joshua continued, “you shall know that a living God is
	among you, and that He will dispossess for you the Canaanites,
	Hittites, Hivites, Perizzites, Girgashites, Amorites, and
	Jebusites:
}

\eJ{
	the Ark of the Covenant of the Sovereign of all the earth is
	advancing before you into the Jordan.
}

\eJ{
	Now select twelve men from the tribes of Israel, one man from each
	tribe.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהָיָה כְּנוֹחַ כַּפּוֹת רַגְלֵי הַכֹּהֲנִים נֹשְׂאֵי אֲרוֹן יְהֹוָה אֲדוֹן כּל־הָאָרֶץ בְּמֵי הַיַּרְדֵּן מֵי הַיַּרְדֵּן יִכָּרֵתוּן הַמַּיִם הַיֹּרְדִים מִלְמָעְלָה וְיַעַמְדוּ
	נֵד אֶחָד׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When the feet of the priests bearing the Ark of YHWH, the
	Sovereign of all the earth, come to rest in the waters of the
	Jordan, the waters of the Jordan—the water coming from
	upstream—will be cut off and will stand in a single heap.”
}

\eJ{
	When the people set out from their encampment to cross the Jordan,
	the priests bearing the Ark of the Covenant were at the head of
	the people.
}

\eJ{
	Now the Jordan keeps flowing over its entire bed throughout the
	harvest season. But as soon as the bearers of the Ark reached the
	Jordan, and the feet of the priests bearing the Ark dipped into
	the water at its edge,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַמְדוּ הַמַּיִם הַיֹּרְדִים מִלְמַעְלָה קָמוּ נֵד־אֶחָד הַרְחֵק מְאֹד (באדם) [מֵאָדָם] הָעִיר אֲשֶׁר מִצַּד צָרְתָן וְהַיֹּרְדִים עַל יָם הָעֲרָבָה יָם־הַמֶּלַח תַּמּוּ
	נִכְרָתוּ וְהָעָם עָבְרוּ נֶגֶד יְרִיחוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	the waters coming down from upstream piled up in a single heap a
	great way off, at Adam, the town next to Zarethan; and those
	flowing away downstream to the Sea of the Arabah (the Dead Sea)
	ran out completely. So the people crossed near Jericho.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַמְדוּ הַכֹּהֲנִים נֹשְׂאֵי הָאָרוֹן בְּרִית־יְהֹוָה בֶּחָרָבָה בְּתוֹךְ הַיַּרְדֵּן הָכֵן וְכל־יִשְׂרָאֵל עֹבְרִים בֶּחָרָבָה עַד אֲשֶׁר־תַּמּוּ כּל־הַגּוֹי לַעֲבֹר
	אֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The priests who bore the Ark of YHWH’s Covenant stood on dry land
	exactly in the middle of the Jordan, while all Israel crossed over
	on dry land, until the entire nation had finished crossing the
	Jordan.
}

\eX{
	When the entire nation had finished crossing the Jordan, YHWH said
	to Joshua,
}

\eX{
	“Select twelve men from among the people, one from each tribe,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְצַוּוּ אוֹתָם לֵאמֹר שְׂאוּ־לָכֶם מִזֶּה מִתּוֹךְ הַיַּרְדֵּן מִמַּצַּב רַגְלֵי הַכֹּהֲנִים הָכִין שְׁתֵּים־עֶשְׂרֵה אֲבָנִים וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם אוֹתָם עִמָּכֶם וְהִנַּחְתֶּם אוֹתָם
	בַּמָּלוֹן אֲשֶׁר־תָּלִינוּ בוֹ הַלָּיְלָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	and instruct them as follows: Pick up twelve stones from the spot
	exactly in the middle of the Jordan, where the priests’ feet are
	standing; take them along with you and deposit them in the place
	where you will spend the night.”
}

\eX{
	Joshua summoned the twelve men whom he had designated among the
	Israelites, one from each tribe;
}

\eX{
	and Joshua said to them, “Walk up to the Ark of YHWH your God, in
	the middle of the Jordan, and each of you lift a stone onto his
	shoulder—corresponding to the number of the tribes of Israel.
}

\eX{
	This shall serve as a symbol among you: in time to come, when your
	children ask, ‘What is the meaning of these stones for you?’
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַאֲמַרְתֶּם לָהֶם אֲשֶׁר נִכְרְתוּ מֵימֵי הַיַּרְדֵּן מִפְּנֵי אֲרוֹן בְּרִית־יְהֹוָה בְּעבְרוֹ בַּיַּרְדֵּן נִכְרְתוּ מֵי הַיַּרְדֵּן וְהָיוּ הָאֲבָנִים הָאֵלֶּה לְזִכָּרוֹן
	לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל עַד־עוֹלָם׃
}

\hX{
	וַיַּעֲשׂוּ־כֵן בְּנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיִּשְׂאוּ שְׁתֵּי־עֶשְׂרֵה אֲבָנִים מִתּוֹךְ הַיַּרְדֵּן כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ לְמִסְפַּר
	שִׁבְטֵי בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיַּעֲבִרוּם עִמָּם אֶל־הַמָּלוֹן וַיַּנִּחוּם שָׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Joshua also set up twelve stones in the middle of the Jordan, at
	the spot where the feet of the priests bearing the Ark of the
	Covenant had stood; and they have remained there to this day.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהַכֹּהֲנִים נֹשְׂאֵי הָאָרוֹן עֹמְדִים בְּתוֹךְ הַיַּרְדֵּן עַד תֹּם כּל־הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה יְהֹוָה אֶת־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ לְדַבֵּר אֶל־הָעָם כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה מֹשֶׁה
	אֶת־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיְמַהֲרוּ הָעָם וַיַּעֲבֹרוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And when all the people finished crossing, the Ark of YHWH and the
	priests advanced to the head of the people.
}

\eX{
	The Reubenites, the Gadites, and the half-tribe of Manasseh went
	across armed in the van of the Israelites, as Moses had charged
	them.
}

\eX{
	About forty thousand shock troops went across, at the instance of
	YHWH, to the steppes of Jericho for battle.
}

\eJ{
	On that day YHWH exalted Joshua in the sight of all Israel, so
	that they revered him all his days as they had revered Moses.
}

\eX{
	“Command the priests who bear the Ark of the Pact to come up out
	of the Jordan.”
}

\eX{
	So Joshua commanded the priests, “Come up out of the Jordan.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי (בעלות) [כַּעֲלוֹת] הַכֹּהֲנִים נֹשְׂאֵי אֲרוֹן בְּרִית־יְהֹוָה מִתּוֹךְ הַיַּרְדֵּן נִתְּקוּ כַּפּוֹת רַגְלֵי הַכֹּהֲנִים אֶל הֶחָרָבָה וַיָּשֻׁבוּ מֵי־הַיַּרְדֵּן
	לִמְקוֹמָם וַיֵּלְכוּ כִתְמוֹל־שִׁלְשׁוֹם עַל־כּל־גְּדוֹתָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	As soon as the priests who bore the Ark of YHWH’s Covenant came up
	out of the Jordan, and the feet of the priests stepped onto the
	dry ground, the waters of the Jordan resumed their course, flowing
	over its entire bed as before.
}

\eX{
	The people came up from the Jordan on the tenth day of the first
	month, and encamped at Gilgal on the eastern border of Jericho.
}

\eX{
	And Joshua set up in Gilgal the twelve stones they had taken from
	the Jordan.
}

\eX{
	He charged the Israelites as follows: “In time to come, when your
	children ask their fathers, ‘What is the meaning of those stones?’
}

\eX{
	tell your children: ‘Here the Israelites crossed the Jordan on dry
	land.’
}

\eX{
	For YHWH your God dried up the waters of the Jordan before you
	until you crossed, just as YHWH your God did to the Sea of Reeds,
	which He dried up before us until we crossed.
}

\eX{
	Thus all the peoples of the earth shall know how mighty is the
	hand of YHWH, and you shall fear YHWH your God always.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כִשְׁמֹעַ כּל־מַלְכֵי הָאֱמֹרִי אֲשֶׁר בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן יָמָּה וְכל־מַלְכֵי הַכְּנַעֲנִי אֲשֶׁר עַל־הַיָּם אֵת אֲשֶׁר־הוֹבִישׁ יְהֹוָה אֶת־מֵי הַיַּרְדֵּן מִפְּנֵי
	בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל עַד־[עבְרָם] (עברנו) וַיִּמַּס לְבָבָם וְלֹא־הָיָה בָם עוֹד רוּחַ מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	At that time YHWH said to Joshua, “Make flint knives and proceed
	with a second circumcision of the Israelites.”
}

\eJ{
	So Joshua had flint knives made, and the Israelites were
	circumcised at Gibeath-haaraloth.
}

\eJ{
	This is the reason why Joshua had the circumcision performed: All
	the people who had come out of Egypt, all the males of military
	age, had died during the desert wanderings after leaving Egypt.
}

\eJ{
	Now, whereas all the people who came out of Egypt had been
	circumcised, none of the people born after the exodus, during the
	desert wanderings, had been circumcised.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	כִּי אַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה הָלְכוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּמִּדְבָּר עַד־תֹּם כּל־הַגּוֹי אַנְשֵׁי הַמִּלְחָמָה הַיֹּצְאִים מִמִּצְרַיִם אֲשֶׁר לֹא־שָׁמְעוּ בְּקוֹל יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר
	נִשְׁבַּע יְהֹוָה לָהֶם לְבִלְתִּי הַרְאוֹתָם אֶת־הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע יְהֹוָה לַאֲבוֹתָם לָתֶת לָנוּ אֶרֶץ זָבַת חָלָב וּדְבָשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But He had raised up their sons in their stead; and it was these
	that Joshua circumcised, for they were uncircumcised, not having
	been circumcised on the way.
}

\eJ{
	After the circumcising of the whole nation was completed, they
	remained where they were, in the camp, until they recovered.
}

\eJ{
	And YHWH said to Joshua, “Today I have rolled away from you the
	disgrace of Egypt.” So that place was called Gilgal, as it still
	is.
}

\eX{
	Encamped at Gilgal, in the steppes of Jericho, the Israelites
	offered the passover sacrifice on the fourteenth day of the month,
	toward evening.
}

\eX{
	On the day after the passover offering, on that very day, they ate
	of the produce of the country, unleavened bread and parched grain.
}

\eX{
	On that same day, when they ate of the produce of the land, the
	manna ceased. The Israelites got no more manna; that year they ate
	of the yield of the land of Canaan.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי בִּהְיוֹת יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּירִיחוֹ וַיִּשָּׂא עֵינָיו וַיַּרְא וְהִנֵּה־אִישׁ עֹמֵד לְנֶגְדּוֹ וְחַרְבּוֹ שְׁלוּפָה בְּיָדוֹ וַיֵּלֶךְ יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֵלָיו וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ הֲלָנוּ
	אַתָּה אִם־לְצָרֵינוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Once, when Joshua was near Jericho, he looked up and saw a man
	standing before him, drawn sword in hand. Joshua went up to him
	and asked him, “Are you one of us or of our enemies?”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 5:13. a man standing opposite him. The scene deliberately sounds like
%		Moses at the bush and Balaam on the road: a human sees a figure with a drawn sword
%		and then finds out that the question is not whether God is on his side. The
%		question is whether he is standing on holy ground.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He replied, “No, I am captain of YHWH’s host. Now I have come!”
	Joshua threw himself face down to the ground and, prostrating
	himself, said to him, “What does my lord command his servant?”
}

\eJ{
	The captain of YHWH’s host answered Joshua, “Remove your sandals
	from your feet, for the place where you stand is holy.” And Joshua
	did so.
}

\eJ{
	Now Jericho was shut up tight because of the Israelites; no one
	could leave or enter.
}

\eJ{
	YHWH said to Joshua, “See, I will deliver Jericho and her king
	[and her] warriors into your hands.
}

\eJ{
	Let all your troops march around the city and complete one circuit
	of the city. Do this six days,
}

\eJ{
	with seven priests carrying seven ram’s horns preceding the Ark.
	On the seventh day, march around the city seven times, with the
	priests blowing the horns.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהָיָה בִּמְשֹׁךְ בְּקֶרֶן הַיּוֹבֵל (בשמעכם) [כְּשׁמְעֲכֶם] אֶת־קוֹל הַשּׁוֹפָר יָרִיעוּ כל־הָעָם תְּרוּעָה גְדוֹלָה וְנָפְלָה חוֹמַת הָעִיר תַּחְתֶּיהָ וְעָלוּ הָעָם אִישׁ
	נֶגְדּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And when a long blast is sounded on the horn—as soon as you hear
	that sound of the horn—all the people shall give a mighty shout.
	Thereupon the city wall will collapse, and the people shall
	advance, every man straight ahead.”
}

\eJ{
	Joshua son of Nun summoned the priests and said to them, “Take up
	the Ark of the Covenant, and let seven priests carrying seven
	ram’s horns precede the Ark of YHWH.”
}

\eJ{
	And he instructed the people, “Go forward, march around the city,
	with the vanguard marching in front of the Ark of YHWH.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כֶּאֱמֹר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶל־הָעָם וְשִׁבְעָה הַכֹּהֲנִים נֹשְׂאִים שִׁבְעָה שׁוֹפְרוֹת הַיּוֹבְלִים לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה עָבְרוּ וְתָקְעוּ בַּשּׁוֹפָרוֹת וַאֲרוֹן בְּרִית יְהֹוָה
	הֹלֵךְ אַחֲרֵיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When Joshua had instructed the people, the seven priests carrying
	seven ram’s horns advanced before YHWH, blowing their horns; and
	the Ark of YHWH’s Covenant followed them.
}

\eJ{
	The vanguard marched in front of the priests who were blowing the
	horns, and the rear guard marched behind the Ark, with the horns
	sounding all the time.
}

\eJ{
	But Joshua’s orders to the rest of the people were, “Do not shout,
	do not let your voices be heard, and do not let a sound issue from
	your lips until the moment that I command you, ‘Shout!’ Then you
	shall shout.”
}

\eJ{
	So he had the Ark of YHWH go around the city and complete one
	circuit; then they returned to camp and spent the night in camp.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua rose early the next day; and the priests took up the Ark of
	YHWH,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְשִׁבְעָה הַכֹּהֲנִים נֹשְׂאִים שִׁבְעָה שׁוֹפְרוֹת הַיֹּבְלִים לִפְנֵי אֲרוֹן יְהֹוָה הֹלְכִים הָלוֹךְ וְתָקְעוּ בַּשּׁוֹפָרוֹת וְהֶחָלוּץ הֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיהֶם וְהַמְאַסֵּף הֹלֵךְ
	אַחֲרֵי אֲרוֹן יְהֹוָה (הולך) [הָלוֹךְ] וְתָקוֹעַ בַּשּׁוֹפָרוֹת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	while the seven priests bearing the seven ram’s horns marched in
	front of the Ark of YHWH, blowing the horns as they marched. The
	vanguard marched in front of them, and the rear guard marched
	behind the Ark of YHWH, with the horns sounding all the time.
}

\eJ{
	And so they marched around the city once on the second day and
	returned to the camp. They did this six days.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי וַיַּשְׁכִּמוּ כַּעֲלוֹת הַשַּׁחַר וַיָּסֹבּוּ אֶת־הָעִיר כַּמִּשְׁפָּט הַזֶּה שֶׁבַע פְּעָמִים רַק בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא סָבְבוּ אֶת־הָעִיר שֶׁבַע
	פְּעָמִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	On the seventh day, they rose at daybreak and marched around the
	city, in the same manner, seven times; that was the only day that
	they marched around the city seven times.
}

\eJ{
	On the seventh round, as the priests blew the horns, Joshua
	commanded the people, “Shout! For YHWH has given you the city.
}

\eJ{
	The city and everything in it are to be proscribed for YHWH; only
	Rahab the harlot is to be spared, and all who are with her in the
	house, because she hid the messengers we sent.
}

\eJ{
	But you must beware of that which is proscribed, or else you will
	be proscribed: if you take anything from that which is proscribed,
	you will cause the camp of Israel to be proscribed; you will bring
	calamity upon it.
}

\eJ{
	All the silver and gold and objects of copper and iron are
	consecrated to YHWH; they must go into the treasury of YHWH.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּרַע הָעָם וַיִּתְקְעוּ בַּשֹּׁפָרוֹת וַיְהִי כִשְׁמֹעַ הָעָם אֶת־קוֹל הַשּׁוֹפָר וַיָּרִיעוּ הָעָם תְּרוּעָה גְדוֹלָה וַתִּפֹּל הַחוֹמָה תַּחְתֶּיהָ וַיַּעַל הָעָם הָעִירָה
	אִישׁ נֶגְדּוֹ וַיִּלְכְּדוּ אֶת־הָעִיר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So the people shouted when the horns were sounded. When the people
	heard the sound of the horns, the people raised a mighty shout and
	the wall collapsed. The people rushed into the city, every man
	straight in front of him, and they captured the city.
}

\eJ{
	They exterminated everything in the city with the sword: man and
	woman, young and old, ox and sheep and ass.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְלִשְׁנַיִם הָאֲנָשִׁים הַמְרַגְּלִים אֶת־הָאָרֶץ אָמַר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בֹּאוּ בֵּית־הָאִשָּׁה הַזּוֹנָה וְהוֹצִיאוּ מִשָּׁם אֶת־הָאִשָּׁה וְאֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר־לָהּ כַּאֲשֶׁר
	נִשְׁבַּעְתֶּם לָהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But Joshua bade the two men who had spied out the land, “Go into
	the harlot’s house and bring out the woman and all that belong to
	her, as you swore to her.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ הַנְּעָרִים הַמְרַגְּלִים וַיֹּצִיאוּ אֶת־רָחָב וְאֶת־אָבִיהָ וְאֶת־אִמָּהּ וְאֶת־אַחֶיהָ וְאֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר־לָהּ וְאֵת כּל־מִשְׁפְּחוֹתֶיהָ הוֹצִיאוּ וַיַּנִּיחוּם מִחוּץ
	לְמַחֲנֵה יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So the young spies went in and brought out Rahab, her father and
	her mother, her brothers and all that belonged to her—they brought
	out her whole family and left them outside the camp of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	They burned down the city and everything in it. But the silver and
	gold and the objects of copper and iron were deposited in the
	treasury of the House of YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְאֶת־רָחָב הַזּוֹנָה וְאֶת־בֵּית אָבִיהָ וְאֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר־לָהּ הֶחֱיָה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַתֵּשֶׁב בְּקֶרֶב יִשְׂרָאֵל עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה כִּי הֶחְבִּיאָה אֶת־הַמַּלְאָכִים אֲשֶׁר־שָׁלַח
	יְהוֹשֻׁעַ לְרַגֵּל אֶת־יְרִיחוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Only Rahab the harlot and her father’s family were spared by
	Joshua, along with all that belonged to her, and she dwelt among
	the Israelites—as is still the case. For she had hidden the
	messengers that Joshua sent to spy out Jericho.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּשְׁבַּע יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר אָרוּר הָאִישׁ לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר יָקוּם וּבָנָה אֶת־הָעִיר הַזֹּאת אֶת־יְרִיחוֹ בִּבְכֹרוֹ יְיַסְּדֶנָּה וּבִצְעִירוֹ יַצִּיב
	דְּלָתֶיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	At that time Joshua pronounced this oath: “Cursed of YHWH be the
	man who shall undertake to fortify this city of Jericho: he shall
	lay its foundations at the cost of his first-born, and set up its
	gates at the cost of his youngest.”
}

\eJ{
	YHWH was with Joshua, and his fame spread throughout the land.
}

\eJ{
	The Israelites, however, violated the proscription: Achan son of
	Carmi son of Zabdi son of Zerah, of the tribe of Judah, took of
	that which was proscribed, and YHWH was incensed with the
	Israelites.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֲנָשִׁים מִירִיחוֹ הָעַי אֲשֶׁר עִם־בֵּית אָוֶן מִקֶּדֶם לְבֵית־אֵל וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם לֵאמֹר עֲלוּ וְרַגְּלוּ אֶת־הָאָרֶץ וַיַּעֲלוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים
	וַיְרַגְּלוּ אֶת־הָעָי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joshua sent men from Jericho to Ai, which lies close to
	Beth-aven—east of Bethel—with orders to go up and spy out the
	country. So the men went up and spied out Ai.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּשֻׁבוּ אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו אַל־יַעַל כּל־הָעָם כְּאַלְפַּיִם אִישׁ אוֹ כִּשְׁלֹשֶׁת אֲלָפִים אִישׁ יַעֲלוּ וְיַכּוּ אֶת־הָעָי אַל־תְּיַגַּע־שָׁמָּה אֶת־כּל־הָעָם
	כִּי מְעַט הֵמָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	They returned to Joshua and reported to him, “Not all the troops
	need go up. Let two or three thousand men go and attack Ai; do not
	trouble all the troops to go up there, for [the people] there are
	few.”
}

\eJ{
	So about three thousand of the troops marched up there; but they
	were routed by the men of Ai.
}

\eJ{
	The men of Ai killed about thirty-six of them, pursuing them
	outside the gate as far as Shebarim, and cutting them down along
	the descent. And the heart of the troops sank in utter dismay.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua thereupon rent his clothes. He and the elders of Israel lay
	until evening with their faces to the ground in front of the Ark
	of YHWH; and they strewed earth on their heads.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֲהָהּ אֲדֹנָי יֱהֹוִה לָמָה הֵעֲבַרְתָּ הַעֲבִיר אֶת־הָעָם הַזֶּה אֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן לָתֵת אֹתָנוּ בְּיַד הָאֱמֹרִי לְהַאֲבִידֵנוּ וְלוּ הוֹאַלְנוּ וַנֵּשֶׁב
	בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	“Ah, Lord GOD!” cried Joshua. “Why did You lead this people across
	the Jordan only to deliver us into the hands of the Amorites, to
	be destroyed by them? If only we had been content to remain on the
	other side of the Jordan!
}

\eJ{
	O Lord, what can I say after Israel has turned tail before its
	enemies?
}

\eJ{
	When the Canaanites and all the inhabitants of the land hear of
	this, they will turn upon us and wipe out our very name from the
	earth. And what will You do about Your great name?”
}

\eJ{
	But YHWH answered Joshua: “Arise! Why do you lie prostrate?
}

\eJ{
	Israel has sinned! They have broken the covenant by which I bound
	them. They have taken of the proscribed and put it in their
	vessels; they have stolen; they have broken faith!
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְלֹא יֻכְלוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָקוּם לִפְנֵי אֹיְבֵיהֶם עֹרֶף יִפְנוּ לִפְנֵי אֹיְבֵיהֶם כִּי הָיוּ לְחֵרֶם לֹא אוֹסִיף לִהְיוֹת עִמָּכֶם אִם־לֹא תַשְׁמִידוּ הַחֵרֶם
	מִקִּרְבְּכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Therefore, the Israelites will not be able to hold their ground
	against their enemies; they will have to turn tail before their
	enemies, for they have become proscribed. I will not be with you
	any more unless you root out from among you what is proscribed.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	קֻם קַדֵּשׁ אֶת־הָעָם וְאָמַרְתָּ הִתְקַדְּשׁוּ לְמָחָר כִּי כֹה אָמַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל חֵרֶם בְּקִרְבְּךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא תוּכַל לָקוּם לִפְנֵי אֹיְבֶיךָ עַד־הֲסִירְכֶם
	הַחֵרֶם מִקִּרְבְּכֶם׃
}

\hJ{
	וְנִקְרַבְתֶּם בַּבֹּקֶר לְשִׁבְטֵיכֶם וְהָיָה הַשֵּׁבֶט אֲשֶׁר־יִלְכְּדֶנּוּ יְהֹוָה יִקְרַב לַמִּשְׁפָּחוֹת וְהַמִּשְׁפָּחָה אֲשֶׁר־יִלְכְּדֶנָּה יְהֹוָה תִּקְרַב לַבָּתִּים
	וְהַבַּיִת אֲשֶׁר יִלְכְּדֶנּוּ יְהֹוָה יִקְרַב לַגְּבָרִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Tomorrow morning you shall present yourselves by tribes. Whichever
	tribe YHWH indicates shall come forward by clans; the clan that
	YHWH indicates shall come forward by ancestral houses, and the
	ancestral house that YHWH indicates shall come forward man by man.
}

\eJ{
	Then he who is indicated for proscription, and all that is his,
	shall be put to the fire, because he broke the Covenant of YHWH
	and because he committed an outrage in Israel.”
}

\eJ{
	Early next morning, Joshua had Israel come forward by tribes; and
	the tribe of Judah was indicated.
}

\eJ{
	He then had the clans of Judah come forward, and the clan of Zerah
	was indicated. Then he had the clan of Zerah come forward by
	ancestral houses, and Zabdi was indicated.
}

\eJ{
	Finally he had his ancestral house come forward man by man, and
	Achan son of Carmi, son of Zabdi, son of Zerah, of the tribe of
	Judah, was indicated.
}

\eJ{
	Then Joshua said to Achan, “My son, pay honor to YHWH, the God of
	Israel, and make confession to Him. Tell me what you have done; do
	not hold anything back from me.”
}

\eJ{
	Achan answered Joshua, “It is true, I have sinned against YHWH,
	the God of Israel. This is what I did:
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	(ואראה) [וָאֵרֶא] בַשָּׁלָל אַדֶּרֶת שִׁנְעָר אַחַת טוֹבָה וּמָאתַיִם שְׁקָלִים כֶּסֶף וּלְשׁוֹן זָהָב אֶחָד חֲמִשִּׁים שְׁקָלִים מִשְׁקָלוֹ וָאֶחְמְדֵם וָאֶקָּחֵם וְהִנָּם
	טְמוּנִים בָּאָרֶץ בְּתוֹךְ הָאהֳלִי וְהַכֶּסֶף תַּחְתֶּיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	I saw among the spoil a fine Shinar mantle, two hundred shekels of
	silver, and a wedge of gold weighing fifty shekels, and I coveted
	them and took them. They are buried in the ground in my tent, with
	the silver under it.”
}

\eJ{
	Joshua sent messengers, who hurried to the tent; and there it was,
	buried in his tent, with the silver underneath.
}

\eJ{
	They took them from the tent and brought them to Joshua and all
	the Israelites, and displayed them before YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּקַּח יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶת־עָכָן בֶּן־זֶרַח וְאֶת־הַכֶּסֶף וְאֶת־הָאַדֶּרֶת וְאֶת־לְשׁוֹן הַזָּהָב וְאֶת־בָּנָיו וְאֶת־בְּנֹתָיו וְאֶת־שׁוֹרוֹ וְאֶת־חֲמֹרוֹ וְאֶת־צֹאנוֹ וְאֶת־אהֳלוֹ
	וְאֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר־לוֹ וְכל־יִשְׂרָאֵל עִמּוֹ וַיַּעֲלוּ אֹתָם עֵמֶק עָכוֹר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then Joshua, and all Israel with him, took Achan son of Zerah—and
	the silver, the mantle, and the wedge of gold—his sons and
	daughters, and his ox, his ass, and his flock, and his tent, and
	all his belongings, and brought them up to the Valley of Achor.
}

\eJ{
	And Joshua said, “What calamity you have brought upon us! YHWH
	will bring calamity upon you this day.” And all Israel pelted him
	with stones. They put them to the fire and stoned them.
}

\eJ{
	They raised a huge mound of stones over him, which is still there.
	Then the anger of YHWH subsided. That is why that place was named
	the Valley of Achor—as is still the case.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אַל־תִּירָא וְאַל־תֵּחָת קַח עִמְּךָ אֵת כּל־עַם הַמִּלְחָמָה וְקוּם עֲלֵה הָעָי רְאֵה נָתַתִּי בְיָדְךָ אֶת־מֶלֶךְ הָעַי וְאֶת־עַמּוֹ וְאֶת־עִירוֹ
	וְאֶת־אַרְצוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	YHWH said to Joshua, “Do not be frightened or dismayed. Take all
	the fighting troops with you, go and march against Ai. See, I will
	deliver the king of Ai, his people, his city, and his land into
	your hands.
}

\eJ{
	You shall treat Ai and her king as you treated Jericho and her
	king; however, you may take the spoil and the cattle as booty for
	yourselves. Now set an ambush against the city behind it.”
}

\eJ{
	So Joshua and all the fighting troops prepared for the march on
	Ai. Joshua chose thirty thousand men, valiant warriors, and sent
	them ahead by night.
}

\eJ{
	He instructed them as follows: “Mind, you are to lie in ambush
	behind the city; don’t stay too far from the city, and all of you
	be on the alert.
}

\eJ{
	I and all the troops with me will approach the city; and when they
	come out against us, as they did the first time, we will flee from
	them.
}

\eJ{
	They will come rushing after us until we have drawn them away from
	the city. They will think, ‘They are fleeing from us the same as
	last time’; but while we are fleeing before them,
}

\eJ{
	you will dash out from your ambush and seize the city, and YHWH
	your God will deliver it into your hands.
}

\eJ{
	And when you take the city, set it on fire. Do as YHWH has
	commanded. Mind, I have given you your orders.”
}

\eJ{
	Joshua then sent them off, and they proceeded to the ambush; they
	took up a position between Ai and Bethel—west of Ai—while Joshua
	spent the night with the rest of the troops.
}

\eJ{
	Early in the morning, Joshua mustered the troops; then he and the
	elders of Israel marched upon Ai at the head of the troops.
}

\eJ{
	All the fighting force that was with him advanced near the city
	and encamped to the north of Ai, with a hollow between them and
	Ai.—
}

\eJ{
	He selected about five thousand men and stationed them as an
	ambush between Bethel and Ai, west of the city.
}

\eJ{
	Thus the main body of the army was disposed on the north of the
	city, but the far end of it was on the west. (This was after
	Joshua had spent the night in the valley.)—
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כִּרְאוֹת מֶלֶךְ־הָעַי וַיְמַהֲרוּ וַיַּשְׁכִּימוּ וַיֵּצְאוּ אַנְשֵׁי־הָעִיר לִקְרַאת־יִשְׂרָאֵל לַמִּלְחָמָה הוּא וְכל־עַמּוֹ לַמּוֹעֵד לִפְנֵי הָעֲרָבָה וְהוּא לֹא יָדַע
	כִּי־אֹרֵב לוֹ מֵאַחֲרֵי הָעִיר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joshua and all Israel fled in the direction of the wilderness, as
	though routed by them.
}

\eJ{
	All the troops in the city gathered to pursue them; pursuing
	Joshua, they were drawn out of the city.
}

\eJ{
	Not a man was left in Ai or in Bethel who did not go out after
	Israel; they left the city open while they pursued Israel.
}

\eJ{
	YHWH then said to Joshua, “Hold out the javelin in your hand
	toward Ai, for I will deliver it into your hands.” So Joshua held
	out the javelin in his hand toward the city.
}

\eJ{
	As soon as he held out his hand, the ambush came rushing out of
	their station. They entered the city and captured it; and they
	swiftly set fire to the city.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּפְנוּ אַנְשֵׁי הָעַי אַחֲרֵיהֶם וַיִּרְאוּ וְהִנֵּה עָלָה עֲשַׁן הָעִיר הַשָּׁמַיְמָה וְלֹא־הָיָה בָהֶם יָדַיִם לָנוּס הֵנָּה וָהֵנָּה וְהָעָם הַנָּס הַמִּדְבָּר נֶהְפַּךְ
	אֶל־הָרוֹדֵף׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The men of Ai looked back and saw the smoke of the city rising to
	the sky; they had no room for flight in any direction. The people
	who had been fleeing to the wilderness now became the pursuers.
}

\eJ{
	For when Joshua and all Israel saw that the ambush had captured
	the city, and that smoke was rising from the city, they turned
	around and attacked the men of Ai.
}

\eJ{
	Now the other [Israelites] were coming out of the city against
	them, so that they were between two bodies of Israelites, one on
	each side of them. They were slaughtered, so that no one escaped
	or got away.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כְּכַלּוֹת יִשְׂרָאֵל לַהֲרֹג אֶת־כּל־יֹשְׁבֵי הָעַי בַּשָּׂדֶה בַּמִּדְבָּר אֲשֶׁר רְדָפוּם בּוֹ וַיִּפְּלוּ כֻלָּם לְפִי־חֶרֶב עַד־תֻּמָּם וַיָּשֻׁבוּ כל־יִשְׂרָאֵל הָעַי
	וַיַּכּוּ אֹתָהּ לְפִי־חָרֶב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When Israel had killed all the inhabitants of Ai who had pursued
	them into the open wilderness, and all of them, to the last man,
	had fallen by the sword, all the Israelites turned back to Ai and
	put it to the sword.
}

\eJ{
	The total of those who fell that day, men and women, the entire
	population of Ai, came to twelve thousand.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua did not draw back the hand with which he held out his
	javelin until all the inhabitants of Ai had been exterminated.
}

\eJ{
	However, the Israelites took the cattle and the spoil of the city
	as their booty, in accordance with the instructions that YHWH had
	given to Joshua.
}

\eJ{
	Then Joshua burned down Ai, and turned it into a mound of ruins
	for all time, a desolation to this day.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְאֶת־מֶלֶךְ הָעַי תָּלָה עַל־הָעֵץ עַד־עֵת הָעָרֶב וּכְבוֹא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ צִוָּה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיֹּרִידוּ אֶת־נִבְלָתוֹ מִן־הָעֵץ וַיַּשְׁלִיכוּ אוֹתָהּ אֶל־פֶּתַח שַׁעַר הָעִיר
	וַיָּקִימוּ עָלָיו גַּל־אֲבָנִים גָּדוֹל עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And the king of Ai was impaled on a stake until the evening. At
	sunset, Joshua had the corpse taken down from the stake and it was
	left lying at the entrance to the city gate. They raised a great
	heap of stones over it, which is there to this day.
}

\eDtrH{
	At that time Joshua built an altar to YHWH, the God of Israel, on
	Mount Ebal,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד־יְהֹוָה אֶת־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּכָּתוּב בְּסֵפֶר תּוֹרַת מֹשֶׁה מִזְבַּח אֲבָנִים שְׁלֵמוֹת אֲשֶׁר לֹא־הֵנִיף עֲלֵיהֶן בַּרְזֶל וַיַּעֲלוּ עָלָיו
	עֹלוֹת לַיהֹוָה וַיִּזְבְּחוּ שְׁלָמִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	as Moses, the servant of YHWH, had commanded the Israelites—as is
	written in the Book of the Teaching of Moses—an altar of unhewn
	stone upon which no iron had been wielded. They offered on it
	burnt offerings to YHWH, and brought sacrifices of well-being.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And there, on the stones, he inscribed a copy of the Teaching that
	Moses had written for the Israelites.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְכל־יִשְׂרָאֵל וּזְקֵנָיו וְשֹׁטְרִים וְשֹׁפְטָיו עֹמְדִים מִזֶּה וּמִזֶּה לָאָרוֹן נֶגֶד הַכֹּהֲנִים הַלְוִיִּם נֹשְׂאֵי אֲרוֹן בְּרִית־יְהֹוָה כַּגֵּר כָּאֶזְרָח חֶצְיוֹ
	אֶל־מוּל הַר־גְּרִזִים וְהַחֶצְיוֹ אֶל־מוּל הַר־עֵיבָל כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד־יְהֹוָה לְבָרֵךְ אֶת־הָעָם יִשְׂרָאֵל בָּרִאשֹׁנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	After that, he read all the words of the Teaching, the blessing
	and the curse, just as is written in the Book of the Teaching.%
}

\eDtrH{
	There was not a word of all that Moses had commanded that Joshua
	failed to read in the presence of the entire assembly of Israel,
	including the women and children and the strangers who accompanied
	them.%
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כִשְׁמֹעַ כּל־הַמְּלָכִים אֲשֶׁר בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן בָּהָר וּבַשְּׁפֵלָה וּבְכֹל חוֹף הַיָּם הַגָּדוֹל אֶל־מוּל הַלְּבָנוֹן הַחִתִּי וְהָאֱמֹרִי הַכְּנַעֲנִי הַפְּרִזִּי
	הַחִוִּי וְהַיְבוּסִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When all the kings west of the Jordan—in the hill country, in the
	Shephelah, and along the entire coast of the Mediterranean Sea up
	to the vicinity of Lebanon, the [land of the] Hittites, Amorites,
	Canaanites, Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites—learned of this,
}

\eJ{
	they gathered with one accord to fight against Joshua and Israel.
}

\eJ{
	But when the inhabitants of Gibeon learned how Joshua had treated
	Jericho and Ai,
}

\eJ{
	they for their part resorted to cunning. They set out in disguise:
	they took worn-out sacks for their asses, and worn-out waterskins
	that were cracked and patched;
}

\eJ{
	they had worn-out, patched sandals on their feet, and threadbare
	clothes on their bodies; and all the bread they took as provision
	was dry and crumbly.
}

\eJ{
	And so they went to Joshua in the camp at Gilgal and said to him
	and to the men of Israel, “We come from a distant land; we propose
	that you make a pact with us.”
}

\eJ{
	The men of Israel replied to the Hivites, “But perhaps you live
	among us; how then can we make a pact with you?”
}

\eJ{
	They said to Joshua, “We will be your subjects.” But Joshua asked
	them, “Who are you and where do you come from?”
}

\eJ{
	They replied, “Your servants have come from a very distant
	country, because of the fame of YHWH your God. For we heard the
	report of Him: of all that He did in Egypt,
}

\eJ{
	and of all that He did to the two Amorite kings on the other side
	of the Jordan, King Sihon of Heshbon and King Og of Bashan who
	lived in Ashtaroth.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלֵינוּ זְקֵינֵינוּ וְכל־יֹשְׁבֵי אַרְצֵנוּ לֵאמֹר קְחוּ בְיֶדְכֶם צֵידָה לַדֶּרֶךְ וּלְכוּ לִקְרָאתָם וַאֲמַרְתֶּם אֲלֵיהֶם עַבְדֵיכֶם אֲנַחְנוּ וְעַתָּה
	כִּרְתוּ־לָנוּ בְרִית׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So our elders and all the inhabitants of our country instructed us
	as follows, ‘Take along provisions for a trip, and go to them and
	say: We will be your subjects; come make a pact with us.’
}

\eJ{
	This bread of ours, which we took from our houses as provision,
	was still hot when we set out to come to you; and see how dry and
	crumbly it has become.
}

\eJ{
	These wineskins were new when we filled them, and see how they
	have cracked. These clothes and sandals of ours are worn out from
	the very long journey.”
}

\eJ{
	The men took [their word] because of their provisions, and did not
	inquire of YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua established friendship with them; he made a pact with them
	to spare their lives, and the chieftains of the community gave
	them their oath.
}

\eJ{
	But when three days had passed after they made this pact with
	them, they learned that they were neighbors, living among them.
}

\eX{
	So the Israelites set out, and on the third day they came to their
	towns; these towns were Gibeon, Chephirah, Beeroth, and
	Kiriath-jearim.
}

\eX{
	But the Israelites did not attack them, since the chieftains of
	the community had sworn to them by YHWH, the God of Israel. The
	whole community muttered against the chieftains,
}

\eX{
	but all the chieftains answered the whole community, “We swore to
	them by YHWH, the God of Israel; therefore we cannot touch them.
}

\eX{
	This is what we will do to them: We will spare their lives, so
	that there may be no wrath against us because of the oath that we
	swore to them.”
}

\eX{
	And the chieftains declared concerning them, “They shall live!”
	And they became hewers of wood and drawers of water for the whole
	community, as the chieftains had decreed concerning them.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua summoned them and spoke to them thus: “Why did you deceive
	us and tell us you lived very far from us, when in fact you live
	among us?
}

\eJ{
	Therefore, be accursed! Never shall your descendants cease to be
	slaves, hewers of wood and drawers of water for the House of my
	God.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲנוּ אֶת־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיֹּאמְרוּ כִּי הֻגֵּד הֻגַּד לַעֲבָדֶיךָ אֵת אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֶת־מֹשֶׁה עַבְדּוֹ לָתֵת לָכֶם אֶת־כּל־הָאָרֶץ וּלְהַשְׁמִיד
	אֶת־כּל־יֹשְׁבֵי הָאָרֶץ מִפְּנֵיכֶם וַנִּירָא מְאֹד לְנַפְשֹׁתֵינוּ מִפְּנֵיכֶם וַנַּעֲשֵׂה אֶת־הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And now we are at your mercy; do with us what you consider right
	and proper.”
}

\eJ{
	And he did so; he saved them from being killed by the Israelites.
}

\eX{
	That day Joshua made them hewers of wood and drawers of water—as
	they still are—for the community and for the altar of YHWH, in the
	place that He would choose.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כִשְׁמֹעַ אֲדֹנִי־צֶדֶק מֶלֶךְ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם כִּי־לָכַד יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶת־הָעַי וַיַּחֲרִימָהּ כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לִירִיחוֹ וּלְמַלְכָּהּ כֵּן־עָשָׂה לָעַי וּלְמַלְכָּהּ וְכִי
	הִשְׁלִימוּ יֹשְׁבֵי גִבְעוֹן אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיִּהְיוּ בְּקִרְבָּם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When King Adoni-zedek of Jerusalem learned that Joshua had
	captured Ai and proscribed it, treating Ai and its king as he had
	treated Jericho and its king, and that, moreover, the people of
	Gibeon had come to terms with Israel and remained among them,
}

\eJ{
	he was very frightened. For Gibeon was a large city, like one of
	the royal cities—in fact, larger than Ai—and all its men were
	warriors.
}

\eJ{
	So King Adoni-zedek of Jerusalem sent this message to King Hoham
	of Hebron, King Piram of Jarmuth, King Japhia of Lachish, and King
	Debir of Eglon:
}

\eJ{
	“Come up and help me defeat Gibeon; for it has come to terms with
	Joshua and the Israelites.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּאָסְפוּ וַיַּעֲלוּ חֲמֵשֶׁת מַלְכֵי הָאֱמֹרִי מֶלֶךְ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם מֶלֶךְ־חֶבְרוֹן מֶלֶךְ־יַרְמוּת מֶלֶךְ־לָכִישׁ מֶלֶךְ־עֶגְלוֹן הֵם וְכל־מַחֲנֵיהֶם וַיַּחֲנוּ עַל־גִּבְעוֹן
	וַיִּלָּחֲמוּ עָלֶיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The five Amorite kings—the king of Jerusalem, the king of Hebron,
	the king of Jarmuth, the king of Lachish, and the king of Eglon,
	with all their armies—joined forces and marched on Gibeon, and
	encamped against it and attacked it.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלְחוּ אַנְשֵׁי גִבְעוֹן אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶל־הַמַּחֲנֶה הַגִּלְגָּלָה לֵאמֹר אַל־תֶּרֶף יָדֶיךָ מֵעֲבָדֶיךָ עֲלֵה אֵלֵינוּ מְהֵרָה וְהוֹשִׁיעָה לָּנוּ וְעזְרֵנוּ כִּי
	נִקְבְּצוּ אֵלֵינוּ כּל־מַלְכֵי הָאֱמֹרִי יֹשְׁבֵי הָהָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The people of Gibeon thereupon sent this message to Joshua in the
	camp at Gilgal: “Do not fail your servants; come up quickly and
	aid us and deliver us, for all the Amorite kings of the hill
	country have gathered against us.”
}

\eJ{
	So Joshua marched up from Gilgal with his whole fighting force,
	all the trained warriors.
}

\eJ{
	YHWH said to Joshua, “Do not be afraid of them, for I will deliver
	them into your hands; not one of them shall withstand you.”
}

\eJ{
	Joshua took them by surprise, marching all night from Gilgal.
}

\eJ{
	YHWH threw them into a panic before Israel: [Joshua] inflicted a
	crushing defeat on them at Gibeon, pursued them in the direction
	of the Beth-horon ascent, and harried them all the way to Azekah
	and Makkedah.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי בְּנֻסָם מִפְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל הֵם בְּמוֹרַד בֵּית־חוֹרֹן וַיהֹוָה הִשְׁלִיךְ עֲלֵיהֶם אֲבָנִים גְּדֹלוֹת מִן־הַשָּׁמַיִם עַד־עֲזֵקָה וַיָּמֻתוּ רַבִּים אֲשֶׁר־מֵתוּ
	בְּאַבְנֵי הַבָּרָד מֵאֲשֶׁר הָרְגוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בֶּחָרֶב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	While they were fleeing before Israel down the descent from
	Beth-horon, YHWH hurled huge stones on them from the sky, all the
	way to Azekah, and they perished; more perished from the
	hailstones than were killed by the Israelite weapons.
}

\eJ{
	On that occasion, when YHWH routed the Amorites before the
	Israelites, Joshua addressed YHWH; he said in the presence of the
	Israelites: “Stand still, O sun, at Gibeon, O moon, in the Valley
	of Aijalon!”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 10:12. Sun, be still. The line is poetry inside prose, and the book says
%		so by sending the reader to the Book of Jashar. The Bible has just stopped the
%		sun, and it still pauses to cite its source. Even miracles have footnotes.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And the sun stood still And the moon halted, While a nation
	wreaked judgment on its foes —as is written in the Book of Jashar.
	Thus the sun halted in midheaven, and did not press on to set, for
	a whole day;
}

\eJ{
	for YHWH fought for Israel. Neither before nor since has there
	ever been such a day, when YHWH acted on words spoken by a man.
}

\eJ{
	Then Joshua together with all Israel returned to the camp at
	Gilgal.
}

\eJ{
	Meanwhile, those five kings fled and hid in a cave at Makkedah.
}

\eJ{
	When it was reported to Joshua that the five kings had been found
	hiding in a cave at Makkedah,
}

\eJ{
	Joshua ordered, “Roll large stones up against the mouth of the
	cave, and post men over it to keep guard over them.
}

\eJ{
	But as for the rest of you, don’t stop, but press on the heels of
	your enemies and harass them from the rear. Don’t let them reach
	their towns, for YHWH your God has delivered them into your
	hands.”
}

\eJ{
	When Joshua and the Israelites had finished dealing them a deadly
	blow, they were wiped out, except for some fugitives who escaped
	into the fortified towns.
}

\eJ{
	The whole army returned in safety to Joshua in the camp at
	Makkedah; no one so much as snarled at the Israelites.
}

\eJ{
	And now Joshua ordered, “Open the mouth of the cave, and bring
	those five kings out of the cave to me.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲשׂוּ כֵן וַיֹּצִיאוּ אֵלָיו אֶת־חֲמֵשֶׁת הַמְּלָכִים הָאֵלֶּה מִן־הַמְּעָרָה אֵת מֶלֶךְ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם אֶת־מֶלֶךְ חֶבְרוֹן אֶת־מֶלֶךְ יַרְמוּת אֶת־מֶלֶךְ לָכִישׁ אֶת־מֶלֶךְ
	עֶגְלוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	This was done. Those five kings—the king of Jerusalem, the king of
	Hebron, the king of Jarmuth, the king of Lachish, and the king of
	Eglon—were brought out to him from the cave.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כְּהוֹצִיאָם אֶת־הַמְּלָכִים הָאֵלֶּה אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיִּקְרָא יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶל־כּל־אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־קְצִינֵי אַנְשֵׁי הַמִּלְחָמָה הֶהָלְכוּא אִתּוֹ קִרְבוּ
	שִׂימוּ אֶת־רַגְלֵיכֶם עַל־צַוְּארֵי הַמְּלָכִים הָאֵלֶּה וַיִּקְרְבוּ וַיָּשִׂימוּ אֶת־רַגְלֵיהֶם עַל־צַוְּארֵיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joshua said to them, “Do not be frightened or dismayed; be firm
	and resolute. For this is what YHWH is going to do to all the
	enemies with whom you are at war.”
}

\eJ{
	After that, Joshua had them put to death and impaled on five
	stakes, and they remained impaled on the stakes until evening.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי לְעֵת בּוֹא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ צִוָּה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַיֹּרִידוּם מֵעַל הָעֵצִים וַיַּשְׁלִכֻם אֶל־הַמְּעָרָה אֲשֶׁר נֶחְבְּאוּ־שָׁם וַיָּשִׂמוּ אֲבָנִים גְּדֹלוֹת עַל־פִּי הַמְּעָרָה
	עַד־עֶצֶם הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	At sunset Joshua ordered them taken down from the poles and thrown
	into the cave in which they had hidden. Large stones were placed
	over the mouth of the cave, [and there they are] to this very day.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְאֶת־מַקֵּדָה לָכַד יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא וַיַּכֶּהָ לְפִי־חֶרֶב וְאֶת־מַלְכָּהּ הֶחֱרִם אוֹתָם וְאֶת־כּל־הַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּהּ לֹא הִשְׁאִיר שָׂרִיד וַיַּעַשׂ לְמֶלֶךְ
	מַקֵּדָה כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְמֶלֶךְ יְרִיחוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	At that time Joshua captured Makkedah and put it and its king to
	the sword, proscribing it and every person in it and leaving none
	that escaped. And he treated the king of Makkedah as he had
	treated the king of Jericho.
}

\eJ{
	From Makkedah, Joshua proceeded with all Israel to Libnah, and he
	attacked it.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּתֵּן יְהֹוָה גַּם־אוֹתָהּ בְּיַד יִשְׂרָאֵל וְאֶת־מַלְכָּהּ וַיַּכֶּהָ לְפִי־חֶרֶב וְאֶת־כּל־הַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּהּ לֹא־הִשְׁאִיר בָּהּ שָׂרִיד וַיַּעַשׂ לְמַלְכָּהּ כַּאֲשֶׁר
	עָשָׂה לְמֶלֶךְ יְרִיחוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	YHWH delivered it and its king into the hands of Israel; they put
	it and all the people in it to the sword, letting none escape. And
	he treated its king as he had treated the king of Jericho.
}

\eJ{
	From Libnah, Joshua proceeded with all Israel to Lachish; he
	encamped against it and attacked it.
}

\eJ{
	YHWH delivered Lachish into the hands of Israel. They captured it
	on the second day and put it and all the people in it to the
	sword, just as they had done to Libnah.
}

\eJ{
	At that time King Horam of Gezer marched to the help of Lachish;
	but Joshua defeated him and his army, letting none of them escape.
}

\eJ{
	From Lachish, Joshua proceeded with all Israel to Eglon; they
	encamped against it and attacked it.
}

\eJ{
	They captured it on the same day and put it to the sword,
	proscribing all the people that were in it, as they had done to
	Lachish.
}

\eJ{
	From Eglon, Joshua marched with all Israel to Hebron and attacked
	it.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּלְכְּדוּהָ וַיַּכּוּהָ־לְפִי־חֶרֶב וְאֶת־מַלְכָּהּ וְאֶת־כּל־עָרֶיהָ וְאֶת־כּל־הַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּהּ לֹא־הִשְׁאִיר שָׂרִיד כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה לְעֶגְלוֹן וַיַּחֲרֵם אוֹתָהּ
	וְאֶת־כּל־הַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	They captured it and put it, its king, and all its towns, and all
	the people that were in it, to the sword. He let none escape,
	proscribing it and all the people in it, just as he had done in
	the case of Eglon.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua and all Israel with him then turned back to Debir and
	attacked it.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּלְכְּדָהּ וְאֶת־מַלְכָּהּ וְאֶת־כּל־עָרֶיהָ וַיַּכּוּם לְפִי־חֶרֶב וַיַּחֲרִימוּ אֶת־כּל־נֶפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּהּ לֹא הִשְׁאִיר שָׂרִיד כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְחֶבְרוֹן כֵּן־עָשָׂה
	לִדְבִרָה וּלְמַלְכָּהּ וְכַאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְלִבְנָה וּלְמַלְכָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He captured it and its king and all its towns. They put them to
	the sword and proscribed all the people in it. They let none
	escape; just as they had done to Hebron, and as they had done to
	Libnah and its king, so they did to Debir and its king.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּכֶּה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶת־כּל־הָאָרֶץ הָהָר וְהַנֶּגֶב וְהַשְּׁפֵלָה וְהָאֲשֵׁדוֹת וְאֵת כּל־מַלְכֵיהֶם לֹא הִשְׁאִיר שָׂרִיד וְאֵת כּל־הַנְּשָׁמָה הֶחֱרִים כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה יְהֹוָה
	אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Thus Joshua conquered the whole country: the hill country, the
	Negeb, the Shephelah, and the slopes, with all their kings; he let
	none escape, but proscribed everything that breathed—as YHWH, the
	God of Israel, had commanded.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua conquered them from Kadesh-barnea to Gaza, all the land of
	Goshen, and up to Gibeon.
}

\eJ{
	All those kings and their lands were conquered by Joshua at a
	single stroke, for YHWH, the God of Israel, fought for Israel.
}

\eJ{
	Then Joshua, with all Israel, returned to the camp at Gilgal.
}

\eJ{
	When the news reached King Jabin of Hazor, he sent messages to
	King Jobab of Madon, to the king of Shimron, to the king of
	Achshaph,
}

\eJ{
	and to the other kings in the north—in the hill country, in the
	Arabah south of Chinnereth, in the lowlands, and in the district
	of Dor on the west;
}

\eJ{
	to the Canaanites in the east and in the west; to the Amorites,
	Hittites, Perizzites, and Jebusites in the hill country; and to
	the Hivites at the foot of Hermon, in the land of Mizpah.
}

\eJ{
	They took the field with all their armies—an enormous host, as
	numerous as the sands on the seashore—and a vast multitude of
	horses and chariots.
}

\eJ{
	All these kings joined forces; they came and encamped together at
	the Waters of Merom to give battle to Israel.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אַל־תִּירָא מִפְּנֵיהֶם כִּי־מָחָר כָּעֵת הַזֹּאת אָנֹכִי נֹתֵן אֶת־כֻּלָּם חֲלָלִים לִפְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־סוּסֵיהֶם תְּעַקֵּר
	וְאֶת־מַרְכְּבֹתֵיהֶם תִּשְׂרֹף בָּאֵשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But YHWH said to Joshua, “Do not be afraid of them; tomorrow at
	this time I will have them all lying slain before Israel. You
	shall hamstring their horses and burn their chariots.”
}

\eJ{
	So Joshua, with all his fighting men, came upon them suddenly at
	the Waters of Merom, and pounced upon them.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּתְּנֵם יְהֹוָה בְּיַד־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיַּכּוּם וַיִּרְדְּפוּם עַד־צִידוֹן רַבָּה וְעַד מִשְׂרְפוֹת מַיִם וְעַד־בִּקְעַת מִצְפֶּה מִזְרָחָה וַיַּכֻּם עַד־בִּלְתִּי הִשְׁאִיר־לָהֶם
	שָׂרִיד׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	YHWH delivered them into the hands of Israel, and they defeated
	them and pursued them all the way to Great Sidon and
	Misrephothmaim, and all the way to the Valley of Mizpeh on the
	east; they crushed them, letting none escape.
}

\eJ{
	And Joshua dealt with them as YHWH had ordered him; he hamstrung
	their horses and burned their chariots.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua then turned back and captured Hazor and put her king to the
	sword.—Hazor was formerly the head of all those kingdoms.—
}

\eJ{
	They proscribed and put to the sword every person in it. Not a
	soul survived, and Hazor itself was burned down.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua captured all those royal cities and their kings. He put
	them to the sword; he proscribed them in accordance with the
	charge of Moses, the servant of YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	However, all those towns that are still standing on their mounds
	were not burned down by Israel; it was Hazor alone that Joshua
	burned down.
}

\eJ{
	The Israelites kept all the spoil and cattle of the rest of those
	cities as booty. But they cut down their populations with the
	sword until they exterminated them; they did not spare a soul.
}

\eJ{
	Just as YHWH had commanded His servant Moses, so Moses had charged
	Joshua, and so Joshua did; he left nothing undone of all that YHWH
	had commanded Moses.
}

\eJ{
	Joshua conquered the whole of this region: the hill country [of
	Judah], the Negeb, the whole land of Goshen, the Shephelah, the
	Arabah, and the hill country and coastal plain of Israel—
}

\eJ{
	[everything] from Mount Halak, which ascends to Seir, all the way
	to Baal-gad in the Valley of the Lebanon at the foot of Mount
	Hermon; and he captured all the kings there and executed them.
}

\eJ{
	Apart from the Hivites who dwelt in Gibeon, not a single city made
	terms with the Israelites; all were taken in battle.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	כִּי מֵאֵת יְהֹוָה הָיְתָה לְחַזֵּק אֶת־לִבָּם לִקְרַאת הַמִּלְחָמָה אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל לְמַעַן הַחֲרִימָם לְבִלְתִּי הֱיוֹת־לָהֶם תְּחִנָּה כִּי לְמַעַן הַשְׁמִידָם כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה
	יְהֹוָה אֶת־מֹשֶׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	For it was YHWH’s doing to stiffen their hearts to give battle to
	Israel, in order that they might be proscribed without quarter and
	wiped out, as YHWH had commanded Moses.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹא יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בָּעֵת הַהִיא וַיַּכְרֵת אֶת־הָעֲנָקִים מִן־הָהָר מִן־חֶבְרוֹן מִן־דְּבִר מִן־עֲנָב וּמִכֹּל הַר יְהוּדָה וּמִכֹּל הַר יִשְׂרָאֵל עִם־עָרֵיהֶם הֶחֱרִימָם
	יְהוֹשֻׁעַ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	At that time, Joshua went and wiped out the Anakites from the hill
	country, from Hebron, Debir, and Anab, from the entire hill
	country of Judah, and from the entire hill country of Israel;
	Joshua proscribed them and their towns.
}

\eJ{
	No Anakites remained in the land of the Israelites; but some
	remained in Gaza, Gath, and Ashdod.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּקַּח יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶת־כּל־הָאָרֶץ כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה וַיִּתְּנָהּ יְהוֹשֻׁעַ לְנַחֲלָה לְיִשְׂרָאֵל כְּמַחְלְקֹתָם לְשִׁבְטֵיהֶם וְהָאָרֶץ שָׁקְטָה
	מִמִּלְחָמָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Thus Joshua conquered the whole country, just as YHWH had promised
	Moses; and Joshua assigned it to Israel to share according to
	their tribal divisions. And the land had rest from war.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאֵלֶּה מַלְכֵי הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הִכּוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיִּרְשׁוּ אֶת־אַרְצָם בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְרְחָה הַשָּׁמֶשׁ מִנַּחַל אַרְנוֹן עַד־הַר חֶרְמוֹן וְכל־הָעֲרָבָה
	מִזְרָחָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	The following are the local kings whom the Israelites defeated and
	whose territories they took possession of: East of the Jordan,
	from the Wadi Arnon to Mount Hermon, including the eastern half of
	the Arabah:%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	סִיחוֹן מֶלֶךְ הָאֱמֹרִי הַיּוֹשֵׁב בְּחֶשְׁבּוֹן מֹשֵׁל מֵעֲרוֹעֵר אֲשֶׁר עַל־שְׂפַת־נַחַל אַרְנוֹן וְתוֹךְ הַנַּחַל וַחֲצִי הַגִּלְעָד וְעַד יַבֹּק הַנַּחַל גְּבוּל בְּנֵי
	עַמּוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	King Sihon of the Amorites, who resided in Heshbon and ruled over
	part of Gilead—from Aroer on the bank of the Wadi Arnon and the
	wadi proper up to the Wadi Jabbok [and] the border of the
	Ammonites—%
}

\eDtrH{
	and over the eastern Arabah up to the Sea of Chinnereth and,
	southward by way of Beth-jeshimoth at the foot of the slopes of
	Pisgah on the east, down to the Sea of the Arabah, that is, the
	Dead Sea.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Also the territory of King Og of Bashan—one of the last of the
	Rephaim—who resided in Ashtaroth and in Edrei%
}

\eDtrH{
	and ruled over Mount Hermon, Salcah, and all of Bashan up to the
	border of the Geshurites and the Maacathites, as also over part of
	Gilead [down to] the border of King Sihon of Heshbon.%
}

\eDtrH{
	These were vanquished by Moses, the servant of YHWH, and the
	Israelites; and Moses, the servant of YHWH, assigned that
	territory as a possession to the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the
	half-tribe of Manasseh.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאֵלֶּה מַלְכֵי הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן יָמָּה מִבַּעַל גָּד בְּבִקְעַת הַלְּבָנוֹן וְעַד־הָהָר הֶחָלָק הָעֹלֶה שֵׂעִירָה
	וַיִּתְּנָהּ יְהוֹשֻׁעַ לְשִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל יְרֻשָּׁה כְּמַחְלְקֹתָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	in the hill country, in the lowlands, in the Arabah, in the
	slopes, in the wilderness, and in the Negeb—[in the land of] the
	Hittites, the Amorites, the Canaanites, the Perizzites, the
	Hivites, and the Jebusites.%
}

\eDtrH{
	They were: the king of Jericho1 the king of Ai, near Bethel,1%
}

\eDtrH{
	the king of Dor in the district of Dor1 the king of Goiim in
	Gilgal1%
}

\eJ{
	Joshua was now old, advanced in years. YHWH said to him, “You have
	grown old, you are advanced in years; and very much of the land
	still remains to be taken possession of.
}

\eJ{
	This is the territory that remains: all the districts of the
	Philistines and all [those of] the Geshurites,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	מִן־הַשִּׁיחוֹר אֲשֶׁר עַל־פְּנֵי מִצְרַיִם וְעַד גְּבוּל עֶקְרוֹן צָפוֹנָה לַכְּנַעֲנִי תֵּחָשֵׁב חֲמֵשֶׁת סַרְנֵי פְלִשְׁתִּים הָעַזָּתִי וְהָאַשְׁדּוֹדִי הָאֶשְׁקְלוֹנִי
	הַגִּתִּי וְהָעֶקְרוֹנִי וְהָעַוִּים׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	on the south; further, all the Canaanite country from Mearah of
	the Sidonians to Aphek at the Amorite border
}

\eJ{
	and the land of the Gebalites, with the whole [Valley of the]
	Lebanon, from Baal-gad at the foot of Mount Hermon to Lebo-hamath
	on the east,
}

\eJ{
	Therefore, divide this territory into hereditary portions for the
	nine tribes and the half-tribe of Manasseh.”
}

\eJ{
	Now the Reubenites and the Gadites, along with the other
	half-tribe, had already received the shares which Moses assigned
	to them on the east side of the Jordan—as assigned to them by
	Moses the servant of YHWH:
}

\eJ{
	from Aroer on the edge of the Wadi Arnon and the town in the
	middle of the wadi, the entire Tableland [from] Medeba to Dibon,
}

\eJ{
	embracing all the towns of King Sihon of the Amorites, who had
	reigned in Heshbon, up to the border of the Ammonites;
}

\eJ{
	further, Gilead, the territories of the Geshurites and the
	Maacathites, and all of Mount Hermon, and the whole of Bashan up
	to Salcah—
}

\eJ{
	the entire kingdom of Og, who had reigned over Bashan at Ashtaroth
	and at Edrei. (He was the last of the remaining Rephaim.) These
	were defeated and dispossessed by Moses;
}

\eJ{
	but the Israelites failed to dispossess the Geshurites and the
	Maacathites, and Geshur and Maacath remain among Israel to this
	day.
}

\eP{
	No hereditary portion, however, was assigned to the tribe of Levi,
	their portion being the fire offerings of YHWH, the God of Israel,
	as He spoke concerning them.
}

\eP{
	And so Moses assigned [the following] to the tribe of the
	Reubenites, for their various clans,
}

\eP{
	and it became theirs: The territory from Aroer, on the edge of the
	Wadi Arnon and the town in the middle of the wadi, up to Medeba—
	the entire Tableland—
}

\eP{
	Heshbon and all its towns in the Tableland: Dibon, Bamoth-baal,
	Beth-baal-meon,
}

\eP{
	Kiriathaim, Sibmah, and Zereth-shahar in the hill of the valley,
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְכֹל עָרֵי הַמִּישֹׁר וְכל־מַמְלְכוּת סִיחוֹן מֶלֶךְ הָאֱמֹרִי אֲשֶׁר מָלַךְ בְּחֶשְׁבּוֹן אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה מֹשֶׁה אֹתוֹ וְאֶת־נְשִׂיאֵי מִדְיָן אֶת־אֱוִי וְאֶת־רֶקֶם וְאֶת־צוּר
	וְאֶת־חוּר וְאֶת־רֶבַע נְסִיכֵי סִיחוֹן יֹשְׁבֵי הָאָרֶץ׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	all the towns of the Tableland and the entire kingdom of Sihon,
	the king of the Amorites, who had reigned in Heshbon. (For Moses
	defeated him and the Midianite chiefs Evi, Rekem, Zur, Hur, and
	Reba, who had dwelt in the land as princes of Sihon.
}

\eP{
	Together with the others that they slew, the Israelites put Balaam
	son of Beor, the augur, to the sword.)
}

\eP{
	The boundary of the Reubenites was the edge of the Jordan. That
	was the portion of the Reubenites for their various clans—those
	towns with their villages.
}

\eP{
	To the tribe of Gad, for the various Gadite clans, Moses assigned
	[the following],
}

\eP{
	and it became their territory: Jazer, all the towns of Gilead,
	part of the country of the Ammonites up to Aroer, which is close
	to Rabbah,
}

\eP{
	and from Heshbon to Ramath-mizpeh and Betonim, and from Mahanaim
	to the border of Lidbir;
}

\eP{
	and in the Valley, Beth-haram, Beth-nimrah, Succoth, and
	Zaphon—the rest of the kingdom of Sihon, the king of Heshbon—down
	to the edge of the Jordan and up to the tip of the Sea of
	Chinnereth on the east side of the Jordan.
}

\eP{
	That was the portion of the Gadites, for their various clans—those
	towns with their villages.
}

\eP{
	And to the half-tribe of Manasseh Moses assigned [the following],
	so that it went to the half-tribe of Manasseh, for its various
	clans,
}

\eP{
	and became their territory: Mahanaim, all of Bashan, the entire
	kingdom of Og, king of Bashan, and all of Havvoth-jair in Bashan,
	sixty towns;
}

\eP{
	and part of Gilead, and Ashtaroth and Edrei, the royal cities of
	Og in Bashan, were assigned to the descendants of Machir son of
	Manasseh—to a part of the descendants of Machir—for their various
	clans.
}

\eP{
	Those, then, were the portions that Moses assigned in the steppes
	of Moab, on the east side of the Jordan.
}

\eP{
	But no portion was assigned by Moses to the tribe of Levi; YHWH,
	the God of Israel, is their portion, as He spoke concerning them.
}

\eP{
	And these are the allotments of the Israelites in the land of
	Canaan, that were apportioned to them by the priest Eleazar, by
	Joshua son of Nun, and by the heads of the ancestral houses of the
	Israelite tribes,
}

\eP{
	the portions that fell to them by lot, as YHWH had commanded
	through Moses for the nine and a half tribes.
}

\eP{
	For the portion of the other two and a half tribes had been
	assigned to them by Moses on the other side of the Jordan. He had
	not assigned any portion among them to the Levites;
}

\eP{
	for whereas the descendants of Joseph constituted two tribes,
	Manasseh and Ephraim, the Levites were assigned no share in the
	land, but only some towns to live in, with the pastures for their
	livestock and cattle.
}

\eP{
	Just as YHWH had commanded Moses, so the Israelites did when they
	apportioned the land.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּגְּשׁוּ בְנֵי־יְהוּדָה אֶל־יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בַּגִּלְגָּל וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו כָּלֵב בֶּן־יְפֻנֶּה הַקְּנִזִּי אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ אֶת־הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר־דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה
	אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים עַל אֹדוֹתַי וְעַל אֹדוֹתֶיךָ בְּקָדֵשׁ בַּרְנֵעַ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The Judites approached Joshua at Gilgal, and Caleb son of
	Jephunneh the Kenizzite said to him: “You know what instructions
	YHWH gave at Kadesh-barnea to Moses, the man of God, concerning
	you and me.
}

\eX{
	I was forty years old when Moses the servant of YHWH sent me from
	Kadesh-barnea to spy out the land, and I gave him a forthright
	report.
}

\eX{
	While my companions who went up with me took the heart out of the
	people, I was loyal to YHWH my God.
}

\eX{
	On that day, Moses promised on oath, ‘The land on which your foot
	trod shall be a portion for you and your descendants forever,
	because you were loyal to YHWH my God.’
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְעַתָּה הִנֵּה הֶחֱיָה יְהֹוָה אוֹתִי כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֵּר זֶה אַרְבָּעִים וְחָמֵשׁ שָׁנָה מֵאָז דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה אֶת־הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה אֲשֶׁר־הָלַךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּמִּדְבָּר
	וְעַתָּה הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי הַיּוֹם בֶּן־חָמֵשׁ וּשְׁמֹנִים שָׁנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Now YHWH has preserved me, as He promised. It is forty-five years
	since YHWH made this promise to Moses, when Israel was journeying
	through the wilderness; and here I am today, eighty-five years
	old.
}

\eX{
	I am still as strong today as on the day that Moses sent me; my
	strength is the same now as it was then, for battle and for
	activity.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְעַתָּה תְּנָה־לִּי אֶת־הָהָר הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר־דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא כִּי אַתָּה־שָׁמַעְתָּ בַיּוֹם הַהוּא כִּי־עֲנָקִים שָׁם וְעָרִים גְּדֹלוֹת בְּצֻרוֹת אוּלַי יְהֹוָה
	אוֹתִי וְהוֹרַשְׁתִּים כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So assign to me this hill country as YHWH promised on that day.
	Though you too heard on that day that Anakites are there and great
	fortified cities, if only YHWH is with me, I will dispossess them,
	as YHWH promised.”
}

\eX{
	So Joshua blessed Caleb son of Jephunneh and assigned Hebron to
	him as his portion.
}

\eX{
	Thus Hebron became the portion of Caleb son of Jephunneh the
	Kenizzite, as it still is, because he was loyal to YHWH, the God
	of Israel.—
}

\eX{
	The name of Hebron was formerly Kiriath-arba: [Arba] was the great
	man among the Anakites. And the land had rest from war.
}

\eP{
	The portion that fell by lot to the various clans of the tribe of
	Judah lay farthest south, down to the border of Edom, which is the
	Wilderness of Zin.
}

\eP{
	Their southern boundary began from the tip of the Dead Sea, from
	the tongue that projects southward.
}

\eP{
	It proceeded to the south of the Ascent of Akrabbim, passed on to
	Zin, ascended to the south of Kadesh-barnea, passed on to Hezron,
	ascended to Addar, and made a turn to Karka.
}

\eP{
	From there it passed on to Azmon and proceeded to the Wadi of
	Egypt; and the boundary ran on to the Sea. That shall be your
	southern boundary.
}

\eP{
	The boundary on the east was the Dead Sea up to the mouth of the
	Jordan. On the northern side, the boundary began at the tongue of
	the Sea at the mouth of the Jordan.
}

\eP{
	The boundary ascended to Beth-hoglah and passed north of
	Beth-arabah; then the boundary ascended to the Stone of Bohan son
	of Reuben.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְעָלָה הַגְּבוּל דְּבִרָה מֵעֵמֶק עָכוֹר וְצָפוֹנָה פֹּנֶה אֶל־הַגִּלְגָּל אֲשֶׁר־נֹכַח לְמַעֲלֵה אֲדֻמִּים אֲשֶׁר מִנֶּגֶב לַנָּחַל וְעָבַר הַגְּבוּל אֶל־מֵי־עֵין שֶׁמֶשׁ וְהָיוּ
	תֹצְאֹתָיו אֶל־עֵין רֹגֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The boundary ascended from the Valley of Achor to Debir and turned
	north to Gilgal, facing the Ascent of Adummim which is south of
	the wadi; from there the boundary continued to the waters of
	En-shemesh and ran on to En-rogel.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְעָלָה הַגְּבוּל גֵּי בֶן־הִנֹּם אֶל־כֶּתֶף הַיְבוּסִי מִנֶּגֶב הִיא יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם וְעָלָה הַגְּבוּל אֶל־רֹאשׁ הָהָר אֲשֶׁר עַל־פְּנֵי גֵי־הִנֹּם יָמָּה אֲשֶׁר בִּקְצֵה
	עֵמֶק־רְפָאִים צָפוֹנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	From that hilltop the boundary curved to the fountain of the
	Waters of Nephtoah and ran on to the towns of Mount Ephron; then
	the boundary curved to Baalah—that is, Kiriath-jearim.
}

\eP{
	From Baalah the boundary turned westward to Mount Seir, passed
	north of the slope of Mount Jearim—that is, Chesalon—descended to
	Beth-shemesh, and passed on to Timnah.
}

\eP{
	The boundary then proceeded to the northern flank of Ekron; the
	boundary curved to Shikkeron, passed on to Mount Baalah, and
	proceeded to Jabneel; and the boundary ran on to the Sea.
}

\eP{
	And the western boundary was the edge of the Mediterranean Sea.
	Those were the boundaries of the various clans of the Judites on
	all sides.
}

\eP{
	In accordance with YHWH’s command to Joshua, Caleb son of
	Jephunneh was given a portion among the Judites, namely,
	Kiriath-arba—that is, Hebron. ([Arba] was the father of Anak.)
}

\eP{
	Caleb dislodged from there the three Anakites: Sheshai, Ahiman,
	and Talmai, descendants of Anak.
}

\eP{
	From there he marched against the inhabitants of Debir—the name of
	Debir was formerly Kiriath-sepher—
}

\eP{
	and Caleb announced, “I will give my daughter Achsah in marriage
	to the man who attacks and captures Kiriath-sepher.”
}

\eP{
	His kinsman Othniel the Kenizzite captured it; and Caleb gave him
	his daughter Achsah in marriage.
}

\eP{
	When she came [to him], she induced him to ask her father for some
	property. She dismounted from her donkey, and Caleb asked her,
	“What is the matter?”
}

\eP{
	She replied, “Give me a present; for you have given me away as
	Negeb-land; so give me springs of water.” And he gave her Upper
	and Lower Gulloth.
}

\eP{
	This was the portion of the tribe of the Judites by their clans:
}

\eP{
	The towns at the far end of the tribe of Judah, near the border of
	Edom, in the Negeb, were: Kabzeel, Eder, Jagur,
}

\eP{
	Lebaoth, Shilhim, Ain and Rimmon. Total: 29 towns, with their
	villages.
}

\eP{
	Shaaraim, Adithaim, Gederah, and Gederothaim—14 towns, with their
	villages.
}

\eP{
	Gederoth, Beth-dagon, Naamah, and Makkedah: 16 towns, with their
	villages.
}

\eP{
	Keilah, Achzib, and Mareshah: 9 towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	From Ekron westward, all the towns in the vicinity of Ashdod, with
	their villages—
}

\eP{
	Ashdod, its dependencies and its villages—Gaza, its dependencies
	and its villages, all the way to the Wadi of Egypt and the edge of
	the Mediterranean Sea.
}

\eP{
	Humtah, Kiriath-arba—that is, Hebron—and Zior: 9 towns, with their
	villages.
}

\eP{
	Maarath, Beth-anoth, and Eltekon: 6 towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	Kiriath-baal—that is, Kiriath-jearim—and Rabbah: 2 towns, with
	their villages.
}

\eP{
	Nibshan, Ir-melah, and En-gedi: 6 towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	But the Judites could not dispossess the Jebusites, the
	inhabitants of Jerusalem; so the Judites dwell with the Jebusites
	in Jerusalem to this day.
}

\eP{
	The portion that fell by lot to the Josephites ran from the Jordan
	at Jericho—from the waters of Jericho east of the wilderness. From
	Jericho it ascended through the hill country to Bethel.
}

\eP{
	From Bethel it ran to Luz and passed on to the territory of the
	Archites at Ataroth,
}

\eP{
	descended westward to the territory of the Japhletites as far as
	the border of Lower Beth-horon and Gezer, and ran on to the Sea.
}

\eP{
	Thus the Josephites—that is, Manasseh and Ephraim—received their
	portion.
}

\eP{
	The territory of the Ephraimites, by their clans, was as follows:
	The boundary of their portion ran from Atroth-addar on the east to
	Upper Beth-horon,
}

\eP{
	and the boundary ran on to the Sea. And on the north, the boundary
	proceeded from Michmethath to the east of Taanath-shiloh and
	passed beyond it up to the east of Janoah;
}

\eP{
	from Janoah it descended to Ataroth and Naarath, touched on
	Jericho, and ran on to the Jordan.
}

\eP{
	Westward, the boundary proceeded from Tappuah to the Wadi Kanah
	and ran on to the Sea. This was the portion of the tribe of the
	Ephraimites, by their clans,
}

\eP{
	together with the towns marked off for the Ephraimites within the
	territory of the Manassites—all those towns with their villages.
}

\eP{
	However, they failed to dispossess the Canaanites who dwelt in
	Gezer; so the Canaanites remained in the midst of Ephraim, as is
	still the case. But they had to perform forced labor.
}

\eP{
	And this is the portion that fell by lot to the tribe of
	Manasseh—for he was Joseph’s first-born. Since Machir, the
	first-born of Manasseh and the father of Gilead, was a valiant
	warrior, Gilead and Bashan were assigned to him.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיְהִי לִבְנֵי מְנַשֶּׁה הַנּוֹתָרִים לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם לִבְנֵי אֲבִיעֶזֶר וְלִבְנֵי־חֵלֶק וְלִבְנֵי אַשְׂרִיאֵל וְלִבְנֵי־שֶׁכֶם וְלִבְנֵי־חֵפֶר וְלִבְנֵי שְׁמִידָע אֵלֶּה בְּנֵי
	מְנַשֶּׁה בֶן־יוֹסֵף הַזְּכָרִים לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And now assignments were made to the remaining Manassites, by
	their clans: the descendants of Abiezer, Helek, Asriel, Shechem,
	Hepher, and Shemida. Those were the male descendants of Manasseh
	son of Joseph, by their clans.
}

\eP{
	Now Zelophehad son of Hepher son of Gilead son of Machir son of
	Manasseh had no sons, but only daughters. The names of his
	daughters were Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, and Tirzah.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַתִּקְרַבְנָה לִפְנֵי אֶלְעָזָר הַכֹּהֵן וְלִפְנֵי יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן־נוּן וְלִפְנֵי הַנְּשִׂיאִים לֵאמֹר יְהֹוָה צִוָּה אֶת־מֹשֶׁה לָתֶת־לָנוּ נַחֲלָה בְּתוֹךְ אַחֵינוּ וַיִּתֵּן
	לָהֶם אֶל־פִּי יְהֹוָה נַחֲלָה בְּתוֹךְ אֲחֵי אֲבִיהֶן׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Ten districts fell to Manasseh, apart from the lands of Gilead and
	Bashan, which are across the Jordan.
}

\eP{
	Manasseh’s daughters inherited a portion in these together with
	his sons, while the land of Gilead was assigned to the rest of
	Manasseh’s descendants.
}

\eP{
	The boundary of Manasseh ran from Asher to Michmethath, which lies
	near Shechem. The boundary continued to the right, toward the
	inhabitants of En-tappuah.—
}

\eP{
	The region of Tappuah belonged to Manasseh; but Tappuah, on the
	border of Manasseh, belonged to the Ephraimites.—
}

\eP{
	Then the boundary descended to the Wadi Kanah. Those towns to the
	south of the wadi belonged to Ephraim as an enclave among the
	towns of Manasseh. The boundary of Manasseh lay north of the wadi
	and ran on to the Sea.
}

\eP{
	What lay to the south belonged to Ephraim, and what lay to the
	north belonged to Manasseh, with the Sea as its boundary. [This
	territory] was contiguous with Asher on the north and with
	Issachar on the east.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיְהִי לִמְנַשֶּׁה בְּיִשָּׂשכָר וּבְאָשֵׁר בֵּית־שְׁאָן וּבְנוֹתֶיהָ וְיִבְלְעָם וּבְנוֹתֶיהָ וְאֶת־יֹשְׁבֵי דֹאר וּבְנוֹתֶיהָ וְיֹשְׁבֵי עֵין־דֹּר וּבְנוֹתֶיהָ וְיֹשְׁבֵי
	תַעְנַךְ וּבְנֹתֶיהָ וְיֹשְׁבֵי מְגִדּוֹ וּבְנוֹתֶיהָ שְׁלֹשֶׁת הַנָּפֶת׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The Manassites could not dispossess [the inhabitants of] these
	towns, and the Canaanites stubbornly remained in this region.
}

\eP{
	When the Israelites became stronger, they imposed tribute on the
	Canaanites; but they did not dispossess them.
}

\eP{
	The Josephites complained to Joshua, saying, “Why have you
	assigned as our portion a single allotment and a single district,
	seeing that we are a numerous people whom YHWH has blessed so
	greatly?”
}

\eP{
	“If you are a numerous people,” Joshua answered them, “go up to
	the forest country and clear an area for yourselves there, in the
	territory of the Perizzites and the Rephaim, seeing that you are
	cramped in the hill country of Ephraim.”
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ בְּנֵי יוֹסֵף לֹא־יִמָּצֵא לָנוּ הָהָר וְרֶכֶב בַּרְזֶל בְּכל־הַכְּנַעֲנִי הַיֹּשֵׁב בְּאֶרֶץ־הָעֵמֶק לַאֲשֶׁר בְּבֵית־שְׁאָן וּבְנוֹתֶיהָ וְלַאֲשֶׁר בְּעֵמֶק
	יִזְרְעֶאל׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	“The hill country is not enough for us,” the Josephites replied,
	“and all the Canaanites who live in the valley area have iron
	chariots, both those in Beth-shean and its dependencies and those
	in the Valley of Jezreel.”
}

\eP{
	But Joshua declared to the House of Joseph, to Ephraim and
	Manasseh, “You are indeed a numerous people, possessed of great
	strength; you shall not have one allotment only.
}

\eP{
	The hill country shall be yours as well; true, it is forest land,
	but you will clear it and possess it to its farthest limits. And
	you shall also dispossess the Canaanites, even though they have
	iron chariots and even though they are strong.”
}

\eP{
	The whole community of the Israelite people assembled at Shiloh,
	and set up the Tent of Meeting there. The land was now under their
	control;
}

\eP{
	but there remained seven tribes of the Israelites which had not
	yet received their portions.
}

\eP{
	So Joshua said to the Israelites, “How long will you be slack
	about going and taking possession of the land which YHWH, the God
	of your fathers, has assigned to you?
}

\eP{
	Appoint three men of each tribe; I will send them out to go
	through the country and write down a description of it for
	purposes of apportionment, and then come back to me.
}

\eP{
	They shall divide it into seven parts—Judah shall remain by its
	territory in the south, and the house of Joseph shall remain by
	its territory in the north.—
}

\eP{
	When you have written down the description of the land in seven
	parts, bring it here to me. Then I will cast lots for you here
	before YHWH our God.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	כִּי אֵין־חֵלֶק לַלְוִיִּם בְּקִרְבְּכֶם כִּי־כְהֻנַּת יְהֹוָה נַחֲלָתוֹ וְגָד וּרְאוּבֵן וַחֲצִי שֵׁבֶט הַמְנַשֶּׁה לָקְחוּ נַחֲלָתָם מֵעֵבֶר לַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְרָחָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַן
	לָהֶם מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	For the Levites have no share among you, since the priesthood of
	YHWH is their portion; and Gad and Reuben and the half-tribe of
	Manasseh have received the portions which were assigned to them by
	Moses the servant of YHWH, on the eastern side of the Jordan.”
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיָּקֻמוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים וַיֵּלֵכוּ וַיְצַו יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶת־הַהֹלְכִים לִכְתֹּב אֶת־הָאָרֶץ לֵאמֹר לְכוּ וְהִתְהַלְּכוּ בָאָרֶץ וְכִתְבוּ אוֹתָהּ וְשׁוּבוּ אֵלַי וּפֹה אַשְׁלִיךְ
	לָכֶם גּוֹרָל לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה בְּשִׁלֹה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The men set out on their journeys. Joshua ordered the men who were
	leaving to write down a description of the land—“Go, traverse the
	country and write down a description of it. Then return to me, and
	I will cast lots for you here at Shiloh before YHWH.”
}

\eP{
	So the men went and traversed the land; they described it in a
	document, town by town, in seven parts, and they returned to
	Joshua in the camp at Shiloh.
}

\eP{
	Joshua cast lots for them at Shiloh before YHWH, and there Joshua
	apportioned the land among the Israelites according to their
	divisions.
}

\eP{
	The lot of the tribe of the Benjaminites, by their clans, came out
	first. The territory which fell to their lot lay between the
	Judites and the Josephites.
}

\eP{
	The boundary on their northern rim began at the Jordan; the
	boundary ascended to the northern flank of Jericho, ascended
	westward into the hill country and ran on to the Wilderness of
	Beth-aven.
}

\eP{
	From there the boundary passed on southward to Luz, to the flank
	of Luz—that is, Bethel; then the boundary descended to
	Atroth-addar [and] to the hill south of Lower Beth-horon.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְתָאַר הַגְּבוּל וְנָסַב לִפְאַת־יָם נֶגְבָּה מִן־הָהָר אֲשֶׁר עַל־פְּנֵי בֵית־חֹרוֹן נֶגְבָּה (והיה) [וְהָיוּ] תֹצְאֹתָיו אֶל־קִרְיַת־בַּעַל הִיא קִרְיַת יְעָרִים עִיר בְּנֵי
	יְהוּדָה זֹאת פְּאַת־יָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The boundary now turned and curved onto the western rim; and the
	boundary ran southward from the hill on the south side of
	Beth-horon till it ended at Kiriath-baal—that is, Kiriath-jearim—a
	town of the Judites. That was the western rim.
}

\eP{
	The southern rim: From the outskirts of Kiriath-jearim, the
	boundary passed westward and ran on to the fountain of the Waters
	of Nephtoah.
}

\eP{
	Then the boundary descended to the foot of the hill by the Valley
	of Ben-hinnom at the northern end of the Valley of Rephaim; then
	it ran down the Valley of Hinnom along the southern flank of the
	Jebusites to En-rogel.
}

\eP{
	Curving northward, it ran on to En-shemesh and ran on to Geliloth,
	facing the Ascent of Adummim, and descended to the Stone of Bohan
	son of Reuben.
}

\eP{
	It continued northward to the edge of the Arabah and descended
	into the Arabah.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְעָבַר הַגְּבוּל אֶל־כֶּתֶף בֵּית־חגְלָה צָפוֹנָה (והיה) [וְהָיוּ ] (תצאותיו) [תֹּצְאוֹת] הַגְּבוּל אֶל־לְשׁוֹן יָם־הַמֶּלַח צָפוֹנָה אֶל־קְצֵה הַיַּרְדֵּן נֶגְבָּה זֶה גְּבוּל
	נֶגֶב׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The boundary passed on to the northern flank of Beth-hoglah, and
	the boundary ended at the northern tongue of the Dead Sea, at the
	southern end of the Jordan. That was the southern boundary.
}

\eP{
	On their eastern rim, finally, the Jordan was their boundary. That
	was the portion of the Benjaminites, by their clans, according to
	its boundaries on all sides.
}

\eP{
	And the towns of the tribe of the Benjaminites, by its clans,
	were: Jericho, Beth-hoglah, Emek-keziz,
}

\eP{
	Chephar-ammonah, Ophni, and Geba—12 towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	Zela, Eleph, and Jebus—that is, Jerusalem—Gibeath [and] Kiriath:
	14 towns, with their villages. That was the portion of the
	Benjaminites, by their clans.
}

\eP{
	The second lot fell to Simeon. The portion of the tribe of the
	Simeonites, by their clans, lay inside the portion of the Judites.
}

\eP{
	Ain, Rimmon, Ether, and Ashan: 4 towns, with their villages—
}

\eP{
	together with all the villages in the vicinity of those towns,
	down to Baalath-beer [and] Ramath-negeb. That was the portion of
	the tribe of the Simeonites, by their clans.
}

\eP{
	The portion of the Simeonites was part of the territory of the
	Judites; since the share of the Judites was larger than they
	needed, the Simeonites received a portion inside their portion.
}

\eP{
	The third lot emerged for the Zebulunites, by their clans. The
	boundary of their portion: Starting at Sarid,
}

\eP{
	their boundary ascended westward to Maralah, touching Dabbesheth
	and touching the wadi alongside Jokneam.
}

\eP{
	And it also ran from Sarid along the eastern side, where the sun
	rises, past the territory of Chisloth-tabor and on to Daberath and
	ascended to Japhia.
}

\eP{
	From there it ran [back] to the east, toward the sunrise, to
	Gath-hepher, to Eth-kazin, and on to Rimmon, where it curved to
	Neah.
}

\eP{
	Then it turned—that is, the boundary on the north—to Hannathon.
	Its extreme limits were the Valley of Iphtah-el,
}

\eP{
	Kattath, Nahalal, Shimron, Idalah, and Bethlehem: 12 towns, with
	their villages.
}

\eP{
	That was the portion of the Zebulunites by their clans—those
	towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	The fourth lot fell to Issachar, the Issacharites by their clans.
}

\eP{
	The boundary touched Tabor, Shahazimah, and Beth-shemesh; and
	their boundary ran to the Jordan: 16 towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	That was the portion of the tribe of the Issacharites, by their
	clans—the towns with their villages.
}

\eP{
	The fifth lot fell to the tribe of the Asherites, by their clans.
}

\eP{
	Allammelech, Amad, and Mishal; and it touched Carmel on the west,
	and Shihor-libnath.
}

\eP{
	It also ran along the east side to Beth-dagon, and touched Zebulun
	and the Valley of Iphtah-el to the north, [as also] Beth-emek and
	Neiel; then it ran to Cabul on the north,
}

\eP{
	The boundary turned to Ramah and on to the fortified city of Tyre;
	then the boundary turned to Hosah and it ran on westward to
	Mehebel, Achzib,
}

\eP{
	That was the portion of the tribe of the Asherites, by their
	clans—those towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	The sixth lot fell to the Naphtalites, the Naphtalites by their
	clans.
}

\eP{
	Their boundary ran from Heleph, Elon-bezaanannim, Adami-nekeb, and
	Jabneel to Lakkum, and it ended at the Jordan.
}

\eP{
	The boundary then turned westward to Aznoth-tabor and ran from
	there to Hukok. It touched Zebulun on the south, and it touched
	Asher on the west, and Judah at the Jordan on the east.
}

\eP{
	Its fortified towns were Ziddim, Zer, Hammath, Rakkath,
	Chinnereth,
}

\eP{
	Iron, Migdal-el, Horem, Beth-anath, and Beth-shemesh: 19 towns,
	with their villages.
}

\eP{
	That was the portion of the tribe of the Naphtalites, by their
	clans—the towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	The seventh lot fell to the tribe of the Danites, by their clans.
}

\eP{
	Their allotted territory comprised: Zorah, Eshtaol, Ir-shemesh,
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיֵּצֵא גְבוּל־בְּנֵי־דָן מֵהֶם וַיַּעֲלוּ בְנֵי־דָן וַיִּלָּחֲמוּ עִם־לֶשֶׁם וַיִּלְכְּדוּ אוֹתָהּ וַיַּכּוּ אוֹתָהּ לְפִי־חֶרֶב וַיִּרְשׁוּ אוֹתָהּ וַיֵּשְׁבוּ בָהּ וַיִּקְרְאוּ
	לְלֶשֶׁם דָּן כְּשֵׁם דָּן אֲבִיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	But the territory of the Danites slipped from their grasp. So the
	Danites migrated and made war on Leshem. They captured it and put
	it to the sword; they took possession of it and settled in it. And
	they changed the name of Leshem to Dan, after their ancestor Dan.
}

\eP{
	That was the portion of the tribe of the Danites, by their
	clans—those towns, with their villages.
}

\eP{
	When they had finished allotting the land by its boundaries, the
	Israelites gave a portion in their midst to Joshua son of Nun.
}

\eP{
	At the command of YHWH they gave him the town that he asked for,
	Timnath-serah in the hill country of Ephraim; he fortified the
	town and settled in it.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	אֵלֶּה הַנְּחָלֹת אֲשֶׁר נִחֲלוּ אֶלְעָזָר הַכֹּהֵן וִיהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן־נוּן וְרָאשֵׁי הָאָבוֹת לְמַטּוֹת בְּנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּגוֹרָל בְּשִׁלֹה לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה פֶּתַח אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד
	וַיְכַלּוּ מֵחַלֵּק אֶת־הָאָרֶץ׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	These are the portions assigned by lot to the tribes of Israel by
	the priest Eleazar, Joshua son of Nun, and the heads of the
	ancestral houses, before YHWH at Shiloh, at the entrance of the
	Tent of Meeting. When they had finished dividing the land,
}

\eP{
	“Speak to the Israelites: Designate the cities of refuge—about
	which I commanded you through Moses—
}

\eP{
	to which a manslayer who kills a person by mistake,
	unintentionally, may flee. They shall serve you as a refuge from
	the blood avenger.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְנָס אֶל־אַחַת מֵהֶעָרִים הָאֵלֶּה וְעָמַד פֶּתַח שַׁעַר הָעִיר וְדִבֶּר בְּאזְנֵי זִקְנֵי־הָעִיר הַהִיא אֶת־דְּבָרָיו וְאָסְפוּ אֹתוֹ הָעִירָה אֲלֵיהֶם וְנָתְנוּ־לוֹ מָקוֹם
	וְיָשַׁב עִמָּם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	He shall flee to one of those cities, present himself at the
	entrance to the city gate, and plead his case before the elders of
	that city; and they shall admit him into the city and give him a
	place in which to live among them.
}

\eP{
	Should the blood avenger pursue him, they shall not hand the
	manslayer over to him, since he killed the other person without
	intent and had not been his enemy in the past.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְיָשַׁב בָּעִיר הַהִיא עַד־עמְדוֹ לִפְנֵי הָעֵדָה לַמִּשְׁפָּט עַד־מוֹת הַכֹּהֵן הַגָּדוֹל אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה בַּיָּמִים הָהֵם אָז יָשׁוּב הָרוֹצֵחַ וּבָא אֶל־עִירוֹ וְאֶל־בֵּיתוֹ
	אֶל־הָעִיר אֲשֶׁר־נָס מִשָּׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	He shall live in that city until he can stand trial before the
	assembly, [and remain there] until the death of the high priest
	who is in office at that time. Thereafter, the manslayer may go
	back to his home in his own town, to the town from which he fled.”
}

\eP{
	So they set aside Kedesh in the hill country of Naphtali in
	Galilee, Shechem in the hill country of Ephraim, and
	Kiriath-arba—that is, Hebron—in the hill country of Judah.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וּמֵעֵבֶר לְיַרְדֵּן יְרִיחוֹ מִזְרָחָה נָתְנוּ אֶת־בֶּצֶר בַּמִּדְבָּר בַּמִּישֹׁר מִמַּטֵּה רְאוּבֵן וְאֶת־רָאמֹת בַּגִּלְעָד מִמַּטֵּה־גָד וְאֶת־[גּוֹלָן] (גלון) בַּבָּשָׁן
	מִמַּטֵּה מְנַשֶּׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	And across the Jordan, east of Jericho, they assigned Bezer in the
	wilderness, in the Tableland, from the tribe of Reuben; Ramoth in
	Gilead from the tribe of Gad; and Golan in Bashan from the tribe
	of Manasseh.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	אֵלֶּה הָיוּ עָרֵי הַמּוּעָדָה לְכֹל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְלַגֵּר הַגָּר בְּתוֹכָם לָנוּס שָׁמָּה כּל־מַכֵּה־נֶפֶשׁ בִּשְׁגָגָה וְלֹא יָמוּת בְּיַד גֹּאֵל הַדָּם עַד־עמְדוֹ לִפְנֵי
	הָעֵדָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Those were the towns designated for all the Israelites and for
	aliens residing among them, to which anyone who killed a person
	unintentionally might flee, and not die by the hand of the blood
	avenger before standing trial by the assembly.
}

\eP{
	The heads of the ancestral houses of the Levites approached the
	priest Eleazar, Joshua son of Nun, and the heads of the ancestral
	houses of the Israelite tribes,
}

\eP{
	and spoke to them at Shiloh in the land of Canaan, as follows:
	“YHWH commanded through Moses that we be given towns to live in,
	along with their pastures for our livestock.”
}

\eP{
	So the Israelites, in accordance with YHWH’s command, assigned to
	the Levites, out of their own portions, the following towns with
	their pastures:
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיֵּצֵא הַגּוֹרָל לְמִשְׁפְּחֹת הַקְּהָתִי וַיְהִי לִבְנֵי אַהֲרֹן הַכֹּהֵן מִן־הַלְוִיִּם מִמַּטֵּה יְהוּדָה וּמִמַּטֵּה הַשִּׁמְעֹנִי וּמִמַּטֵּה בִנְיָמִן בַּגּוֹרָל עָרִים
	שְׁלֹשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The [first] lot among the Levites fell to the Kohathite clans. To
	the descendants of the priest Aaron, there fell by lot 13 towns
	from the tribe of Judah, the tribe of Simeon, and the tribe of
	Benjamin;
}

\eP{
	and to the remaining Kohathites [there fell] by lot 10 towns from
	the clans of the tribe of Ephraim, the tribe of Dan, and the
	half-tribe of Manasseh.
}

\eP{
	To the Gershonites [there fell] by lot 13 towns from the clans of
	the tribe of Issachar, the tribe of Asher, the tribe of Naphtali,
	and the half-tribe of Manasseh in Bashan.
}

\eP{
	[And] to the Merarites, by their clans—12 towns from the tribe of
	Reuben, the tribe of Gad, and the tribe of Zebulun.
}

\eP{
	The Israelites assigned those towns with their pastures by lot to
	the Levites—as YHWH had commanded through Moses.
}

\eP{
	From the tribe of the Judites and the tribe of the Simeonites were
	assigned the following towns, which will be listed by name;
}

\eP{
	they went to the descendants of Aaron among the Kohathite clans of
	the Levites, for the first lot had fallen to them.
}

\eP{
	To them were assigned in the hill country of Judah
	Kiriath-arba—that is, Hebron—together with the pastures around it.
	[Arba was] the father of the Anokites.
}

\eP{
	They gave the fields and the villages of the town to Caleb son of
	Jephunneh as his holding.
}

\eP{
	But to the descendants of Aaron the priest they assigned
	Hebron—the city of refuge for manslayers—together with its
	pastures, Libnah with its pastures,
}

\eP{
	Ain with its pastures, Juttah with its pastures, and Beth-shemesh
	with its pastures—9 towns from those two tribes.
}

\eP{
	And from the tribe of Benjamin: Gibeon with its pastures, Geba
	with its pastures,
}

\eP{
	Anathoth with its pastures, and Almon with its pastures—4 towns.
}

\eP{
	All the towns of the descendants of the priest Aaron, 13 towns
	with their pastures.
}

\eP{
	As for the other clans of the Kohathites, the remaining Levites
	descended from Kohath, the towns in their lot were: From the tribe
	of Ephraim
}

\eP{
	they were given, in the hill country of Ephraim, Shechem—the city
	of refuge for manslayers—with its pastures, Gezer with its
	pastures,
}

\eP{
	Kibzaim with its pastures, and Beth-horon with its pastures—4
	towns.
}

\eP{
	From the tribe of Dan, Elteke with its pastures, Gibbethon with
	its pastures,
}

\eP{
	Aijalon with its pastures, and Gath-rimmon with its pastures—4
	towns.
}

\eP{
	And from the half-tribe of Manasseh, Taanach with its pastures,
	and Gath-rimmon with its pastures—2 towns.
}

\eP{
	All the towns for the remaining clans of the Kohathites came to
	10, with their pastures.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְלִבְנֵי גֵרְשׁוֹן מִמִּשְׁפְּחֹת הַלְוִיִּם מֵחֲצִי מַטֵּה מְנַשֶּׁה אֶת־עִיר מִקְלַט הָרֹצֵחַ אֶת־[גּוֹלָן] (גלון) בַּבָּשָׁן וְאֶת־מִגְרָשֶׁהָ וְאֶת־בְּעֶשְׁתְּרָה
	וְאֶת־מִגְרָשֶׁהָ עָרִים שְׁתָּיִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	To the Gershonites of the levitical clans: From the half-tribe of
	Manasseh, Golan in Bashan—the city of refuge for manslayers—with
	its pastures, and Beeshterah with its pastures—2 towns.
}

\eP{
	From the tribe of Issachar: Kishion with its pastures, Dobrath
	with its pastures,
}

\eP{
	Jarmuth with its pastures, and En-gannim with its pastures—4
	towns.
}

\eP{
	From the tribe of Asher: Mishal with its pastures, Abdon with its
	pastures,
}

\eP{
	Helkath with its pastures, and Rehob with its pastures—4 towns.
}

\eP{
	From the tribe of Naphtali, Kedesh in Galilee—the city of refuge
	for manslayers—with its pastures, Hammoth-dor with its pastures,
	and Kartan with its pastures—3 towns.
}

\eP{
	All the towns of the Gershonites, by their clans, came to 13
	towns, with their pastures.
}

\eP{
	To the remaining Levites, the clans of the Merarites: From the
	tribe of Zebulun, Jokneam with its pastures, Kartah with its
	pastures,
}

\eP{
	Dimnah with its pastures, and Nahalal with its pastures—4 towns.
}

\eP{
	And out of the tribe of Reuben, Bezer with her pasture lands,
	and Jahazah with her pasture lands,
}

\eP{
	Kedemoth with her pasture lands, and Mephaath with her pasture
	lands; four cities.
}

\eP{
	From the tribe of Gad, Ramoth in Gilead—the city of refuge for
	manslayers—with its pastures, Mahanaim with its pastures,
}

\eP{
	Heshbon with its pastures, and Jazer with its pastures—4 towns in
	all.
}

\eP{
	All the towns which went by lot to the Merarites, by their
	clans—the rest of the levitical clans—came to 12 towns.
}

\eP{
	All the towns of the Levites within the holdings of the Israelites
	came to 48 towns, with their pastures.
}

\eP{
	Thus those towns were assigned, every town with its surrounding
	pasture; and so it was with all those towns.
}

\eP{
	YHWH gave to Israel the whole country which He had sworn to their
	fathers that He would assign to them; they took possession of it
	and settled in it.
}

\eP{
	YHWH gave them rest on all sides, just as He had promised to their
	fathers on oath. Not one man of all their enemies withstood them;
	YHWH delivered all their enemies into their hands.
}

\eP{
	Not one of the good things which YHWH had promised to the House of
	Israel was lacking. Everything was fulfilled.
}

\eP{
	Then Joshua summoned the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the
	half-tribe of Manasseh,
}

\eP{
	and said to them, “You have observed all that Moses the servant of
	YHWH commanded you, and have obeyed me in everything that I
	commanded you.
}

\eP{
	You have not forsaken your kinsmen through the long years down to
	this day, but have faithfully observed the Instruction of YHWH
	your God.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְעַתָּה הֵנִיחַ יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם לַאֲחֵיכֶם כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר לָהֶם וְעַתָּה פְּנוּ וּלְכוּ לָכֶם לְאהֳלֵיכֶם אֶל־אֶרֶץ אֲחֻזַּתְכֶם אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לָכֶם מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד יְהֹוָה
	בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Now YHWH your God has given your kinsmen rest, as He promised
	them. Therefore turn and go to your homes, to the land of your
	holdings beyond the Jordan that Moses the servant of YHWH assigned
	to you.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	רַק שִׁמְרוּ מְאֹד לַעֲשׂוֹת אֶת־הַמִּצְוָה וְאֶת־הַתּוֹרָה אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה אֶתְכֶם מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד־יְהֹוָה לְאַהֲבָה אֶת־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם וְלָלֶכֶת בְּכל־דְּרָכָיו וְלִשְׁמֹר
	מִצְוֺתָיו וּלְדבְקָה־בוֹ וּלְעבְדוֹ בְּכל־לְבַבְכֶם וּבְכל־נַפְשְׁכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Then Joshua blessed them and dismissed them, and they went to
	their homes.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְלַחֲצִי שֵׁבֶט הַמְנַשֶּׁה נָתַן מֹשֶׁה בַּבָּשָׁן וּלְחֶצְיוֹ נָתַן יְהוֹשֻׁעַ עִם־אֲחֵיהֶם (מעבר) [בְּעֵבֶר] הַיַּרְדֵּן יָמָּה וְגַם כִּי־שִׁלְּחָם יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶל־אהֳלֵיהֶם
	וַיְבָרְכֵם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	To the one half-tribe of Manasseh Moses had assigned territory in
	Bashan, and to the other Joshua assigned [territory] on the west
	side of the Jordan, with their kinsmen. Furthermore, when Joshua
	sent them off to their homes, he blessed them
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם לֵאמֹר בִּנְכָסִים רַבִּים שׁוּבוּ אֶל־אהֳלֵיכֶם וּבְמִקְנֶה רַב־מְאֹד בְּכֶסֶף וּבְזָהָב וּבִנְחֹשֶׁת וּבְבַרְזֶל וּבִשְׂלָמוֹת הַרְבֵּה מְאֹד חִלְקוּ
	שְׁלַל־אֹיְבֵיכֶם עִם־אֲחֵיכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	and said to them, “Return to your homes with great wealth—with
	very much livestock, with silver and gold, with copper and iron,
	and with a great quantity of clothing. Share the spoil of your
	enemies with your kinsmen.”
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיָּשֻׁבוּ וַיֵּלְכוּ בְּנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וּבְנֵי־גָד וַחֲצִי שֵׁבֶט הַמְנַשֶּׁה מֵאֵת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִשִּׁלֹה אֲשֶׁר בְּאֶרֶץ־כְּנָעַן לָלֶכֶת אֶל־אֶרֶץ הַגִּלְעָד אֶל־אֶרֶץ
	אֲחֻזָּתָם אֲשֶׁר נֹאחֲזוּ־בָהּ עַל־פִּי יְהֹוָה בְּיַד־מֹשֶׁה׃
}

\hP{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל־גְּלִילוֹת הַיַּרְדֵּן אֲשֶׁר בְּאֶרֶץ כְּנָעַן וַיִּבְנוּ בְנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וּבְנֵי־גָד וַחֲצִי שֵׁבֶט הַמְנַשֶּׁה שָׁם מִזְבֵּחַ עַל־הַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְבֵּחַ גָּדוֹל
	לְמַרְאֶה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	When they came to the region of the Jordan in the land of Canaan,
	the Reubenites and the Gadites and the half-tribe of Manasseh
	built an altar there by the Jordan, a great conspicuous altar.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיִּשְׁמְעוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל לֵאמֹר הִנֵּה בָנוּ בְנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וּבְנֵי־גָד וַחֲצִי שֵׁבֶט הַמְנַשֶּׁה אֶת־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ אֶל־מוּל אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן אֶל־גְּלִילוֹת הַיַּרְדֵּן
	אֶל־עֵבֶר בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	A report reached the Israelites: “The Reubenites, the Gadites, and
	the half-tribe of Manasseh have built an altar opposite the land
	of Canaan, in the region of the Jordan, across from the
	Israelites.”
}

\eP{
	When the Israelites heard this, the whole community of the
	Israelites assembled at Shiloh to make war on them.
}

\eP{
	But [first] the Israelites sent the priest Phinehas son of Eleazar
	to the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the half-tribe of Manasseh in
	the land of Gilead,
}

\eP{
	accompanied by ten chieftains, one chieftain from each ancestral
	house of each of the tribes of Israel; they were every one of them
	heads of ancestral houses of the contingents of Israel.
}

\eP{
	When they came to the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the half-tribe
	of Manasseh in the land of Gilead, they spoke to them as follows:
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	כֹּה אָמְרוּ כֹּל עֲדַת יְהֹוָה מָה־הַמַּעַל הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר מְעַלְתֶּם בֵּאלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָשׁוּב הַיּוֹם מֵאַחֲרֵי יְהֹוָה בִּבְנוֹתְכֶם לָכֶם מִזְבֵּחַ לִמְרדְכֶם הַיּוֹם
	בַּיהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	“Thus said the whole community of YHWH: What is this treachery
	that you have committed this day against the God of Israel,
	turning away from YHWH, building yourselves an altar and rebelling
	this day against YHWH!
}

\eP{
	Is the sin of Peor, which brought a plague upon the community of
	YHWH, such a small thing to us? We have not cleansed ourselves
	from it to this very day;
}

\eP{
	and now you would turn away from YHWH! If you rebel against YHWH
	today, tomorrow He will be angry with the whole community of
	Israel.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וְאַךְ אִם־טְמֵאָה אֶרֶץ אֲחֻזַּתְכֶם עִבְרוּ לָכֶם אֶל־אֶרֶץ אֲחֻזַּת יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר שָׁכַן־שָׁם מִשְׁכַּן יְהֹוָה וְהֵאָחֲזוּ בְּתוֹכֵנוּ וּבַיהֹוָה אַל־תִּמְרֹדוּ וְאֹתָנוּ
	אַל־תִּמְרֹדוּ בִּבְנֹתְכֶם לָכֶם מִזְבֵּחַ מִבַּלְעֲדֵי מִזְבַּח יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	When Achan son of Zerah violated the proscription, anger struck
	the whole community of Israel; he was not the only one who
	perished for that sin.”
}

\eP{
	The Reubenites, the Gadites, and the half-tribe of Manasseh
	replied to the heads of the contingents of Israel: They said,
}

\eP{
	“God, YHWH God! God, YHWH God! He knows, and Israel too shall
	know! If we acted in rebellion or in treachery against YHWH, do
	not vindicate us this day!
}

\eP{
	If we built an altar to turn away from YHWH, if it was to offer
	burnt offerings or meal offerings upon it, or to present
	sacrifices of well-being upon it, may YHWH Himself demand [a
	reckoning].
}

\eP{
	We did this thing only out of our concern that, in time to come,
	your children might say to our children, ‘What have you to do with
	YHWH, the God of Israel?
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וּגְבוּל נָתַן־יְהֹוָה בֵּינֵנוּ וּבֵינֵיכֶם בְּנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וּבְנֵי־גָד אֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן אֵין־לָכֶם חֵלֶק בַּיהֹוָה וְהִשְׁבִּיתוּ בְנֵיכֶם אֶת־בָּנֵינוּ לְבִלְתִּי יְרֹא
	אֶת־יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	YHWH has made the Jordan a boundary between you and us, O
	Reubenites and Gadites; you have no share in YHWH!’ Thus your
	children might prevent our children from worshiping YHWH.
}

\eP{
	So we decided to provide [a witness] for ourselves by building an
	altar—not for burnt offerings or [other] sacrifices,
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	כִּי עֵד הוּא בֵּינֵינוּ וּבֵינֵיכֶם וּבֵין דֹּרוֹתֵינוּ אַחֲרֵינוּ לַעֲבֹד אֶת־עֲבֹדַת יְהֹוָה לְפָנָיו בְּעֹלוֹתֵינוּ וּבִזְבָחֵינוּ וּבִשְׁלָמֵינוּ וְלֹא־יֹאמְרוּ בְנֵיכֶם מָחָר
	לְבָנֵינוּ אֵין־לָכֶם חֵלֶק בַּיהֹוָה׃
}

\hP{
	וַנֹּאמֶר וְהָיָה כִּי־יֹאמְרוּ אֵלֵינוּ וְאֶל־דֹּרֹתֵינוּ מָחָר וְאָמַרְנוּ רְאוּ אֶת־תַּבְנִית מִזְבַּח יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר־עָשׂוּ אֲבוֹתֵינוּ לֹא לְעוֹלָה וְלֹא לְזֶבַח כִּי־עֵד הוּא
	בֵּינֵינוּ וּבֵינֵיכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	We reasoned: should they speak thus to us and to our children in
	time to come, we would reply, ‘See the replica of YHWH’s altar,
	which our fathers made—not for burnt offerings or sacrifices, but
	as a witness between you and us.’
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	חָלִילָה לָּנוּ מִמֶּנּוּ לִמְרֹד בַּיהֹוָה וְלָשׁוּב הַיּוֹם מֵאַחֲרֵי יְהֹוָה לִבְנוֹת מִזְבֵּחַ לְעֹלָה לְמִנְחָה וּלְזָבַח מִלְּבַד מִזְבַּח יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ אֲשֶׁר לִפְנֵי
	מִשְׁכָּנוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Far be it from us to rebel against YHWH, or to turn away this day
	from YHWH and build an altar for burnt offerings, meal offerings,
	and sacrifices other than the altar of YHWH our God which stands
	before His Tabernacle.”
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיִּשְׁמַע פִּינְחָס הַכֹּהֵן וּנְשִׂיאֵי הָעֵדָה וְרָאשֵׁי אַלְפֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר אִתּוֹ אֶת־הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבְּרוּ בְּנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וּבְנֵי־גָד וּבְנֵי מְנַשֶּׁה
	וַיִּיטַב בְּעֵינֵיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	When the priest Phinehas and the chieftains of the community—the
	heads of the contingents of Israel—who were with him heard the
	explanation given by the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the
	Manassites, they approved.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיֹּאמֶר פִּינְחָס בֶּן־אֶלְעָזָר הַכֹּהֵן אֶל־בְּנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וְאֶל־בְּנֵי־גָד וְאֶל־בְּנֵי מְנַשֶּׁה הַיּוֹם יָדַעְנוּ כִּי־בְתוֹכֵנוּ יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר לֹא־מְעַלְתֶּם בַּיהֹוָה
	הַמַּעַל הַזֶּה אָז הִצַּלְתֶּם אֶת־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִיַּד יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The priest Phinehas son of Eleazar said to the Reubenites, the
	Gadites, and the Manassites, “Now we know that YHWH is in our
	midst, since you have not committed such treachery against YHWH.
	You have indeed saved the Israelites from punishment by YHWH.”
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיָּשׁב פִּינְחָס בֶּן־אֶלְעָזָר הַכֹּהֵן וְהַנְּשִׂיאִים מֵאֵת בְּנֵי־רְאוּבֵן וּמֵאֵת בְּנֵי־גָד מֵאֶרֶץ הַגִּלְעָד אֶל־אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן אֶל־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיָּשִׁבוּ אוֹתָם
	דָּבָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	Then the priest Phinehas son of Eleazar and the chieftains
	returned from the Reubenites and the Gadites in the land of Gilead
	to the Israelites in the land of Canaan, and gave them their
	report.
}

tex.original:
\hP{
	וַיִּיטַב הַדָּבָר בְּעֵינֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיְבָרְכוּ אֱלֹהִים בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְלֹא אָמְרוּ לַעֲלוֹת עֲלֵיהֶם לַצָּבָא לְשַׁחֵת אֶת־הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר בְּנֵי־רְאוּבֵן
	וּבְנֵי־גָד יֹשְׁבִים בָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eP{
	The Israelites were pleased, and the Israelites praised God; and
	they spoke no more of going to war against them, to ravage the
	land in which the Reubenites and Gadites dwelt.
}

\eP{
	The Reubenites and the Gadites named the altar [“Witness”],
	meaning, “It is a witness between us and them that YHWH is [our]
	God.”
}

\eDtrH{
	Much later, after YHWH had given Israel rest from all the enemies
	around them, and when Joshua was old and well advanced in years,%
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua summoned all Israel, their elders and commanders, their
	magistrates and officials, and said to them: “I have grown old and
	am advanced in years.%
}

\eDtrH{
	You have seen all that YHWH your God has done to all those nations
	on your account, for it was YHWH your God who fought for you.%
}

\eDtrH{
	See, I have allotted to you, by your tribes, [the territory of]
	these nations that still remain, and that of all the nations that
	I have destroyed, from the Jordan to the Mediterranean Sea in the
	west.%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH your God Himself will thrust them out on your account and
	drive them out to make way for you, and you shall possess their
	land as YHWH your God promised you.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“But be most resolute to observe faithfully all that is written in
	the Book of the Teaching of Moses, without ever deviating from it
	to the right or to the left,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and without intermingling with these nations that are left among
	you. Do not utter the names of their gods or swear by them; do not
	serve them or bow down to them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But hold fast to YHWH your God as you have done to this day.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“YHWH has driven out great, powerful nations on your account, and
	not a man has withstood you to this day.%
}

\eDtrH{
	A single man of you would put a thousand to flight, for YHWH your
	God Himself has been fighting for you, as He promised you.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For your own sakes, therefore, be most mindful to love YHWH your
	God.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For should you turn away and attach yourselves to the remnant of
	those nations—to those that are left among you—and intermarry with
	them, you joining them and they joining you,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	יָדוֹעַ תֵּדְעוּ כִּי לֹא יוֹסִיף יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם לְהוֹרִישׁ אֶת־הַגּוֹיִם הָאֵלֶּה מִלִּפְנֵיכֶם וְהָיוּ לָכֶם לְפַח וּלְמוֹקֵשׁ וּלְשֹׁטֵט בְּצִדֵּיכֶם וְלִצְנִנִים
	בְּעֵינֵיכֶם עַד־אֲבדְכֶם מֵעַל הָאֲדָמָה הַטּוֹבָה הַזֹּאת אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לָכֶם יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	know for certain that YHWH your God will not continue to drive
	these nations out before you; they shall become a snare and a trap
	for you, a scourge to your sides and thorns in your eyes, until
	you perish from this good land that YHWH your God has given you.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהִנֵּה אָנֹכִי הוֹלֵךְ הַיּוֹם בְּדֶרֶךְ כּל־הָאָרֶץ וִידַעְתֶּם בְּכל־לְבַבְכֶם וּבְכל־נַפְשְׁכֶם כִּי לֹא־נָפַל דָּבָר אֶחָד מִכֹּל הַדְּבָרִים הַטּוֹבִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר
	יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם עֲלֵיכֶם הַכֹּל בָּאוּ לָכֶם לֹא־נָפַל מִמֶּנּוּ דָּבָר אֶחָד׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“I am now going the way of all the earth. Acknowledge with all
	your heart and soul that not one of the good things that YHWH your
	God promised you has failed to happen; they have all come true for
	you, not a single one has failed.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהָיָה כַּאֲשֶׁר־בָּא עֲלֵיכֶם כּל־הַדָּבָר הַטּוֹב אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם אֲלֵיכֶם כֵּן יָבִיא יְהֹוָה עֲלֵיכֶם אֵת כּל־הַדָּבָר הָרָע עַד־הַשְׁמִידוֹ אוֹתְכֶם מֵעַל
	הָאֲדָמָה הַטּוֹבָה הַזֹּאת אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לָכֶם יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	But just as every good thing that YHWH your God promised you has
	been fulfilled for you, so YHWH can bring upon you every evil
	thing until He has wiped you off this good land that YHWH your God
	has given you.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	בְּעבְרְכֶם אֶת־בְּרִית יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיכֶם אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה אֶתְכֶם וַהֲלַכְתֶּם וַעֲבַדְתֶּם אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים וְהִשְׁתַּחֲוִיתֶם לָהֶם וְחָרָה אַף־יְהֹוָה בָּכֶם וַאֲבַדְתֶּם
	מְהֵרָה מֵעַל הָאָרֶץ הַטּוֹבָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לָכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	If you break the covenant that YHWH your God enjoined upon you,
	and go and serve other gods and bow down to them, then YHWH’s
	anger will burn against you, and you shall quickly perish from the
	good land that He has given you.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua assembled all the tribes of Israel at Shechem. He summoned
	Israel’s elders and commanders, magistrates and officers; and they
	presented themselves before God.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶל־כּל־הָעָם כֹּה־אָמַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּעֵבֶר הַנָּהָר יָשְׁבוּ אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם מֵעוֹלָם תֶּרַח אֲבִי אַבְרָהָם וַאֲבִי נָחוֹר וַיַּעַבְדוּ אֱלֹהִים
	אֲחֵרִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Then Joshua said to all the people, “Thus said YHWH, the God of
	Israel: In olden times, your forefathers—Terah, father of Abraham
	and father of Nahor—lived beyond the Euphrates and worshiped other
	gods.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But I took your father Abraham from beyond the Euphrates and led
	him through the whole land of Canaan and multiplied his offspring.
	I gave him Isaac,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and to Isaac I gave Jacob and Esau. I gave Esau the hill country
	of Seir as his possession, while Jacob and his children went down
	to Egypt.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Then I sent Moses and Aaron, and I plagued Egypt with [the
	wonders] that I wrought in their midst, after which I freed you—%
}

\eDtrH{
	I freed your fathers—from Egypt, and you came to the Sea. But the
	Egyptians pursued your fathers to the Sea of Reeds with chariots
	and horsemen.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיִּצְעֲקוּ אֶל־יְהֹוָה וַיָּשֶׂם מַאֲפֵל בֵּינֵיכֶם וּבֵין הַמִּצְרִים וַיָּבֵא עָלָיו אֶת־הַיָּם וַיְכַסֵּהוּ וַתִּרְאֶינָה עֵינֵיכֶם אֵת אֲשֶׁר־עָשִׂיתִי בְּמִצְרָיִם
	וַתֵּשְׁבוּ בַמִּדְבָּר יָמִים רַבִּים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	They cried out to YHWH, and He put darkness between you and the
	Egyptians; then He brought the Sea upon them, and it covered them.
	Your own eyes saw what I did to the Egyptians. “After you had
	lived a long time in the wilderness,%
}

\eDtrH{
	I brought you to the land of the Amorites who lived beyond the
	Jordan. They gave battle to you, but I delivered them into your
	hands; I annihilated them for you, and you took possession of
	their land.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Thereupon Balak son of Zippor, the king of Moab, made ready to
	attack Israel. He sent for Balaam son of Beor to curse you,%
}

\eDtrH{
	but I refused to listen to Balaam; he had to bless you, and thus I
	saved you from him.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַתַּעַבְרוּ אֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן וַתָּבֹאוּ אֶל־יְרִיחוֹ וַיִּלָּחֲמוּ בָכֶם בַּעֲלֵי־יְרִיחוֹ הָאֱמֹרִי וְהַפְּרִזִּי וְהַכְּנַעֲנִי וְהַחִתִּי וְהַגִּרְגָּשִׁי הַחִוִּי וְהַיְבוּסִי
	וָאֶתֵּן אוֹתָם בְּיֶדְכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“Then you crossed the Jordan and you came to Jericho. The citizens
	of Jericho and the Amorites, Perizzites, Canaanites, Hittites,
	Girgashites, Hivites, and Jebusites fought you, but I delivered
	them into your hands.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I sent a plague ahead of you, and it drove them out before
	you—[just like] the two Amorite kings—not by your sword or by your
	bow.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I have given you a land for which you did not labor and towns
	which you did not build, and you have settled in them; you are
	enjoying vineyards and olive groves which you did not plant.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Now, therefore, revere YHWH and serve Him with undivided loyalty;
	put away the gods that your forefathers served beyond the
	Euphrates and in Egypt, and serve YHWH.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאִם רַע בְּעֵינֵיכֶם לַעֲבֹד אֶת־יְהֹוָה בַּחֲרוּ לָכֶם הַיּוֹם אֶת־מִי תַעֲבֹדוּן אִם אֶת־אֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר־עָבְדוּ אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם אֲשֶׁר (בעבר) [מֵעֵבֶר] הַנָּהָר וְאִם אֶת־אֱלֹהֵי
	הָאֱמֹרִי אֲשֶׁר אַתֶּם יֹשְׁבִים בְּאַרְצָם וְאָנֹכִי וּבֵיתִי נַעֲבֹד אֶת־יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Or, if you are loath to serve YHWH, choose this day which ones you
	are going to serve—the gods that your forefathers served beyond
	the Euphrates, or those of the Amorites in whose land you are
	settled; but I and my household will serve YHWH.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	In reply, the people declared, “Far be it from us to forsake YHWH
	and serve other gods!%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	כִּי יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ הוּא הַמַּעֲלֶה אֹתָנוּ וְאֶת־אֲבוֹתֵינוּ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם מִבֵּית עֲבָדִים וַאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְעֵינֵינוּ אֶת־הָאֹתוֹת הַגְּדֹלוֹת הָאֵלֶּה וַיִּשְׁמְרֵנוּ
	בְּכל־הַדֶּרֶךְ אֲשֶׁר הָלַכְנוּ בָהּ וּבְכֹל הָעַמִּים אֲשֶׁר עָבַרְנוּ בְּקִרְבָּם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And then YHWH drove out before us all the peoples—the
	Amorites—that inhabited the country. We too will serve YHWH, for
	He is our God.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua, however, said to the people, “You will not be able to
	serve YHWH, for He is a holy God. He is a jealous God; He will not
	forgive your transgressions and your sins.%
}

\eDtrH{
	If you forsake YHWH and serve alien gods, He will turn and deal
	harshly with you and make an end of you, after having been
	gracious to you.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	But the people replied to Joshua, “No, we will serve YHWH!”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Thereupon Joshua said to the people, “You are witnesses against
	yourselves that you have by your own act chosen to serve YHWH.”
	“Yes, we are!” they responded.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Then put away the alien gods that you have among you and direct
	your hearts to YHWH, the God of Israel.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	And the people declared to Joshua, “We will serve none but YHWH
	our God, and we will obey none but Him.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	On that day at Shechem, Joshua made a covenant for the people and
	he made a fixed rule for them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua recorded all this in a book of divine instruction. He took
	a great stone and set it up at the foot of the oak in the sacred
	precinct of YHWH;%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ אֶל־כּל־הָעָם הִנֵּה הָאֶבֶן הַזֹּאת תִּהְיֶה־בָּנוּ לְעֵדָה כִּי־הִיא שָׁמְעָה אֵת כּל־אִמְרֵי יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר עִמָּנוּ וְהָיְתָה בָכֶם לְעֵדָה
	פֶּן־תְּכַחֲשׁוּן בֵּאלֹהֵיכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	and Joshua said to all the people, “See, this very stone shall be
	a witness against us, for it heard all the words that YHWH spoke
	to us; it shall be a witness against you, lest you break faith
	with your God.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua then dismissed the people to their allotted portions.%
}

\eDtrH{
	After these events, Joshua son of Nun, the servant of YHWH, died
	at the age of one hundred and ten years.%
}

\eDtrH{
	They buried him on his own property, at Timnath-serah in the hill
	country of Ephraim, north of Mount Gaash.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעֲבֹד יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־יְהֹוָה כֹּל יְמֵי יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וְכֹל יְמֵי הַזְּקֵנִים אֲשֶׁר הֶאֱרִיכוּ יָמִים אַחֲרֵי יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וַאֲשֶׁר יָדְעוּ אֵת כּל־מַעֲשֵׂה יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה
	לְיִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Israel served YHWH during the lifetime of Joshua and the lifetime
	of the elders who lived on after Joshua, and who had experienced
	all the deeds that YHWH had wrought for Israel.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאֶת־עַצְמוֹת יוֹסֵף אֲשֶׁר־הֶעֱלוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל מִמִּצְרַיִם קָבְרוּ בִשְׁכֶם בְּחֶלְקַת הַשָּׂדֶה אֲשֶׁר קָנָה יַעֲקֹב מֵאֵת בְּנֵי־חֲמוֹר אֲבִי־שְׁכֶם בְּמֵאָה קְשִׂיטָה
	וַיִּהְיוּ לִבְנֵי־יוֹסֵף לְנַחֲלָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Eleazar son of Aaron also died, and they buried him on the hill of
	his son Phinehas, which had been assigned to him in the hill
	country of Ephraim.%
}

\eDtrH{
	After the death of Joshua, the Israelites inquired of YHWH, “Which
	of us shall be the first to go up against the Canaanites and
	attack them?”%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH replied, “Let [the tribe of] Judah go up. I now deliver the
	land into their hands.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Judah then said to their brother-tribe Simeon, “Come up with us to
	our allotted territory and let us attack the Canaanites, and then
	we will go with you to your allotted territory.” So Simeon joined
	them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When Judah advanced, YHWH delivered the Canaanites and the
	Perizzites into their hands, and they defeated ten thousand of
	them at Bezek.%
}

\eDtrH{
	At Bezek, they encountered Adoni-bezek, engaged him in battle, and
	defeated the Canaanites and the Perizzites.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Adoni-bezek fled, but they pursued him and captured him; and they
	cut off his thumbs and his big toes.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֲדֹנִי־בֶזֶק שִׁבְעִים מְלָכִים בְּהֹנוֹת יְדֵיהֶם וְרַגְלֵיהֶם מְקֻצָּצִים הָיוּ מְלַקְּטִים תַּחַת שֻׁלְחָנִי כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי כֵּן שִׁלַּם־לִי אֱלֹהִים
	וַיְבִיאֻהוּ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וַיָּמת שָׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And Adoni-bezek said, “Seventy kings, with thumbs and big toes cut
	off, used to pick up scraps under my table; as I have done, so God
	has requited me.” They brought him to Jerusalem and he died there.%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 1:7. Adoni-bezek counts seventy kings with thumbs and big toes cut off.
%		The Hebrew puts the body parts first, as if the missing thumbs and toes are what
%		he sees before he sees his own theology.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	The Judites attacked Jerusalem and captured it; they put it to the
	sword and set the city on fire.%
}

\eDtrH{
	After that the Judites went down to attack the Canaanites who
	inhabited the hill country, the Negeb, and the Shephelah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Judites marched against the Canaanites who dwelt in Hebron,
	and they defeated Sheshai, Ahiman, and Talmai. (The name of Hebron
	was formerly Kiriath-arba.)%
}

\eDtrH{
	From there they marched against the inhabitants of Debir (the name
	of Debir was formerly Kiriath-sepher).%
}

\eDtrH{
	And Caleb announced, “I will give my daughter Achsah in marriage
	to the man who attacks and captures Kiriath-sepher.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	His younger kinsman, Othniel the Kenizzite, captured it; and Caleb
	gave him his daughter Achsah in marriage.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When she came [to him], she induced him to ask her father for some
	property. She dismounted from her donkey, and Caleb asked her,
	“What is the matter?”%
}

\eDtrH{
	She replied, “Give me a present, for you have given me away as
	Negeb-land; give me springs of water.” And Caleb gave her Upper
	and Lower Gulloth.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The descendants of the Kenite, the father-in-law of Moses, went up
	with the Judites from the City of Palms to the wilderness of
	Judah; and they went and settled among the people in the Negeb of
	Arad.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And Judah with its brother-tribe Simeon went on and defeated the
	Canaanites who dwelt in Zephath. They proscribed it, and so the
	town was named Hormah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And Judah captured Gaza and its territory, Ashkelon and its
	territory, and Ekron and its territory.%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH was with Judah, so that they took possession of the hill
	country; but they were not able to dispossess the inhabitants of
	the plain, for they had iron chariots.%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 1:19. The sentence lets the failure stand in plain sight: YHWH was with
%		Judah, but iron chariots were still iron chariots. Judges is already getting its
%		theology dirty.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	They gave Hebron to Caleb, as Moses had promised; and he drove the
	three Anakites out of there.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Benjaminites did not dispossess the Jebusite inhabitants of
	Jerusalem; so the Jebusites have dwelt with the Benjaminites in
	Jerusalem to this day.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The House of Joseph, for their part, advanced against Bethel, and
	YHWH was with them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	While the House of Joseph were scouting at Bethel (the name of the
	town was formerly Luz),%
}

\eDtrH{
	their patrols saw a man leaving the town. They said to him, “Just
	show us how to get into the town, and we will treat you kindly.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	He showed them how to get into the town; they put the town to the
	sword, but they let the man and all his relatives go free.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The man went to the Hittite country. He founded a city and named
	it Luz, and that has been its name to this day.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְלֹא־הוֹרִישׁ מְנַשֶּׁה אֶת־בֵּית־שְׁאָן וְאֶת־בְּנוֹתֶיהָ וְאֶת־תַּעְנַךְ וְאֶת־בְּנֹתֶיהָ וְאֶת־[יוֹשְׁבֵי] (יושב) דוֹר וְאֶת־בְּנוֹתֶיהָ וְאֶת־יוֹשְׁבֵי יִבְלְעָם
	וְאֶת־בְּנֹתֶיהָ וְאֶת־יוֹשְׁבֵי מְגִדּוֹ וְאֶת־בְּנוֹתֶיהָ וַיּוֹאֶל הַכְּנַעֲנִי לָשֶׁבֶת בָּאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And when Israel gained the upper hand, they subjected the
	Canaanites to forced labor; but they did not dispossess them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Nor did Ephraim dispossess the Canaanites who inhabited Gezer; so
	the Canaanites dwelt in their midst at Gezer.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Zebulun did not dispossess the inhabitants of Kitron or the
	inhabitants of Nahalol; so the Canaanites dwelt in their midst,
	but they were subjected to forced labor.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Asher did not dispossess the inhabitants of Acco or the
	inhabitants of Sidon, Ahlab, Achzib, Helbah, Aphik, and Rehob.%
}

\eDtrH{
	So the Asherites dwelt in the midst of the Canaanites, the
	inhabitants of the land, for they did not dispossess them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Naphtali did not dispossess the inhabitants of Beth-shemesh or the
	inhabitants of Beth-anath. But they settled in the midst of the
	Canaanite inhabitants of the land, and the inhabitants of
	Beth-shemesh and Beth-anath had to perform forced labor for them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Amorites pressed the Danites into the hill country; they would
	not let them come down to the plain.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Amorites also persisted in dwelling in Har-heres, in Aijalon,
	and in Shaalbim. But the hand of the House of Joseph bore heavily
	on them and they had to perform forced labor.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The territory of the Amorites extended from the Ascent of
	Akrabbim—from Sela—onward.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַל מַלְאַךְ־יְהֹוָה מִן־הַגִּלְגָּל אֶל־הַבֹּכִיםוַיֹּאמֶר אַעֲלֶה אֶתְכֶם מִמִּצְרַיִם וָאָבִיא אֶתְכֶם אֶל־הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי לַאֲבֹתֵיכֶם וָאֹמַר לֹא־אָפֵר
	בְּרִיתִי אִתְּכֶם לְעוֹלָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	An angel of YHWH came up from Gilgal to Bochim and said, “I
	brought you up from Egypt and I took you into the land which I had
	promised on oath to your fathers. And I said, ‘I will never break
	My covenant with you.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And you, for your part, must make no covenant with the inhabitants
	of this land; you must tear down their altars.’ But you have not
	obeyed Me—look what you have done!%
}

\eDtrH{
	Therefore, I have resolved not to drive them out before you; they
	shall become your oppressors, and their gods shall be a snare to
	you.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	As the angel of YHWH spoke these words to all the Israelites, the
	people broke into weeping.%
}

\eDtrH{
	So they named that place Bochim, and they offered sacrifices there
	to YHWH.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When Joshua dismissed the people, the Israelites went to their
	allotted territories and took possession of the land.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַבְדוּ הָעָם אֶת־יְהֹוָה כֹּל יְמֵי יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וְכֹל יְמֵי הַזְּקֵנִים אֲשֶׁר הֶאֱרִיכוּ יָמִים אַחֲרֵי יְהוֹשׁוּעַ אֲשֶׁר רָאוּ אֵת כּל־מַעֲשֵׂה יְהֹוָה הַגָּדוֹל אֲשֶׁר
	עָשָׂה לְיִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	The people served YHWH during the lifetime of Joshua and the
	lifetime of the older people who lived on after Joshua and who had
	witnessed all the marvelous deeds that YHWH had wrought for
	Israel.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Joshua son of Nun, the servant of YHWH, died at the age of one
	hundred and ten years,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and was buried on his own property, at Timnath-heres in the hill
	country of Ephraim, north of Mount Gaash.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And all that generation were likewise gathered to their fathers.
	Another generation arose after them, which had not experienced
	[the deliverance of] YHWH or the deeds that He had wrought for
	Israel.%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 2:10. A generation that did not know YHWH is not ignorance as lack of
%		data. In this book, not knowing becomes not serving, and then serving the wrong
%		gods.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And the Israelites did what was offensive to YHWH. They worshiped
	the Baalim%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַזְבוּ אֶת־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי אֲבוֹתָם הַמּוֹצִיא אוֹתָם מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם וַיֵּלְכוּ אַחֲרֵי אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים מֵאֱלֹהֵי הָעַמִּים אֲשֶׁר סְבִיבוֹתֵיהֶם וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ לָהֶם
	וַיַּכְעִסוּ אֶת־יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	and forsook YHWH, the God of their fathers, who had brought them
	out of the land of Egypt. They followed other gods, from among the
	gods of the peoples around them, and bowed down to them; they
	provoked YHWH.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then YHWH was incensed at Israel, and He handed them over to foes
	who plundered them. He surrendered them to their enemies on all
	sides, and they could no longer hold their own against their
	enemies.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In all their campaigns, the hand of YHWH was against them to their
	undoing, as YHWH had declared and as YHWH had sworn to them; and
	they were in great distress.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then YHWH raised up chieftains who delivered them from those who
	plundered them.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְגַם אֶל־שֹׁפְטֵיהֶם לֹא שָׁמֵעוּ כִּי זָנוּ אַחֲרֵי אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ לָהֶם סָרוּ מַהֵר מִן־הַדֶּרֶךְ אֲשֶׁר הָלְכוּ אֲבוֹתָם לִשְׁמֹעַ מִצְוֺת־יְהֹוָה
	לֹא־עָשׂוּ כֵן׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	But they did not heed their chieftains either; they went astray
	after other gods and bowed down to them. They were quick to turn
	aside from the way their fathers had followed in obedience to the
	commandments of YHWH; they did not do right.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְכִי־הֵקִים יְהֹוָה לָהֶם שֹׁפְטִים וְהָיָה יְהֹוָה עִם־הַשֹּׁפֵט וְהוֹשִׁיעָם מִיַּד אֹיְבֵיהֶם כֹּל יְמֵי הַשּׁוֹפֵט כִּי־יִנָּחֵם יְהֹוָה מִנַּאֲקָתָם מִפְּנֵי לֹחֲצֵיהֶם
	וְדֹחֲקֵיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	When YHWH raised up chieftains for them, YHWH would be with the
	chieftain and would save them from their enemies during the
	chieftain’s lifetime; for YHWH would be moved to pity by their
	moanings because of those who oppressed and crushed them.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהָיָה בְּמוֹת הַשּׁוֹפֵט יָשֻׁבוּ וְהִשְׁחִיתוּ מֵאֲבוֹתָם לָלֶכֶת אַחֲרֵי אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים לְעבְדָם וּלְהִשְׁתַּחֲוֺת לָהֶם לֹא הִפִּילוּ מִמַּעַלְלֵיהֶם וּמִדַּרְכָּם
	הַקָּשָׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	But when the chieftain died, they would again act basely, even
	more than the preceding generation —following other gods,
	worshiping them, and bowing down to them; they omitted none of
	their practices and stubborn ways.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then YHWH became incensed against Israel, and He said, “Since that
	nation has transgressed the covenant that I enjoined upon their
	fathers and has not obeyed Me,%
}

\eDtrH{
	I for My part will no longer drive out before them any of the
	nations that Joshua left when he died.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	For it was in order to test Israel by them—[to see] whether or not
	they would faithfully walk in the ways of YHWH, as their fathers
	had done—%
}

\eDtrH{
	that YHWH had left those nations, instead of driving them out at
	once, and had not delivered them into the hands of Joshua.%
}

\eDtrH{
	These are the nations that YHWH left so that He might test by them
	all the Israelites who had not known any of the wars of Canaan,%
}

\eDtrH{
	so that succeeding generations of Israelites might be made to
	experience war—but only those who had not known the former wars:%
}

\eDtrH{
	the five principalities of the Philistines and all the Canaanites,
	Sidonians, and Hivites who inhabited the hill country of the
	Lebanon from Mount Baal-hermon to Lebo-hamath.%
}

\eDtrH{
	These served as a means of testing Israel, to learn whether they
	would obey the commandments which YHWH had enjoined upon their
	fathers through Moses.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Israelites settled among the Canaanites, Hittites, Amorites,
	Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites;%
}

\eDtrH{
	they took their daughters to wife and gave their own daughters to
	their sons, and they worshiped their gods.%
}

\eX{
	The Israelites did what was offensive to YHWH; they ignored YHWH
	their God and worshiped the Baalim and the Asheroth.
}

\eX{
	YHWH became incensed at Israel and surrendered them to King
	Cushan-rishathaim of Aram-naharaim; and the Israelites were
	subject to Cushan-rishathaim for eight years.
}

\eX{
	The Israelites cried out to YHWH, and YHWH raised a champion for
	the Israelites to deliver them: Othniel the Kenizzite, a younger
	kinsman of Caleb.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתְּהִי עָלָיו רוּחַ־יְהֹוָה וַיִּשְׁפֹּט אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֵּצֵא לַמִּלְחָמָה וַיִּתֵּן יְהֹוָה בְּיָדוֹ אֶת־כּוּשַׁן רִשְׁעָתַיִם מֶלֶךְ אֲרָם וַתָּעז יָדוֹ עַל כּוּשַׁן
	רִשְׁעָתָיִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The spirit of YHWH descended upon him and he became Israel’s
	chieftain. He went out to war, and YHWH delivered King
	Cushan-rishathaim of Aram into his hands. He prevailed over
	Cushan-rishathaim,
}

\eX{
	and the land had peace for forty years. When Othniel the Kenizzite
	died,
}

\eX{
	the Israelites again did what was offensive to YHWH. And because
	they did what was offensive to YHWH, YHWH let King Eglon of Moab
	prevail over Israel.
}

\eX{
	[Eglon] brought the Ammonites and the Amalekites together under
	his command, and went and defeated Israel and occupied the City of
	Palms.
}

\eX{
	The Israelites were subject to King Eglon of Moab for eighteen
	years.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּזְעֲקוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל־יְהֹוָה וַיָּקֶם יְהֹוָה לָהֶם מוֹשִׁיעַ אֶת־אֵהוּד בֶּן־גֵּרָא בֶּן־הַיְמִינִי אִישׁ אִטֵּר יַד־יְמִינוֹ וַיִּשְׁלְחוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּיָדוֹ
	מִנְחָה לְעֶגְלוֹן מֶלֶךְ מוֹאָב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the Israelites cried out to YHWH, and YHWH raised up a
	champion for them: the Benjaminite Ehud son of Gera, a left-handed
	man. It happened that the Israelites sent tribute to King Eglon of
	Moab through him.
}

\eX{
	So Ehud made for himself a two-edged dagger, a gomed in length,
	which he girded on his right side under his cloak.
}

\eX{
	He presented the tribute to King Eglon of Moab. Now Eglon was a
	very stout man.
}

\eX{
	When [Ehud] had finished presenting the tribute, he dismissed the
	people who had conveyed the tribute.
}

\eX{
	But he himself returned from Pesilim, near Gilgal, and said, “Your
	Majesty, I have a secret message for you.” [Eglon] thereupon
	commanded, “Silence!” So all those in attendance left his
	presence;
}

\eX{
	and when Ehud approached him, he was sitting alone in his cool
	upper chamber. Ehud said, “I have a message for you from God”;
	whereupon he rose from his seat.
}

\eX{
	Reaching with his left hand, Ehud drew the dagger from his right
	side and drove it into [Eglon’s] belly.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 3:21. Ehud's left hand, the locked upper room, the fat closing over the
%		blade: the Hebrew tells the assassination with almost indecent physical calm.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The fat closed over the blade and the hilt went in after the
	blade—for he did not pull the dagger out of his belly—and the
	filth came out.
}

\eX{
	Stepping out into the vestibule, Ehud shut the doors of the upper
	chamber on him and locked them.
}

\eX{
	After he left, the courtiers returned. When they saw that the
	doors of the upper chamber were locked, they thought, “He must be
	relieving himself in the cool chamber.”
}

\eX{
	They waited a long time; and when he did not open the doors of the
	chamber, they took the key and opened them—and there their master
	was lying dead on the floor!
}

\eX{
	But Ehud had made good his escape while they delayed; he had
	passed Pesilim and escaped to Seirah.
}

\eX{
	When he got there, he had the ram’s horn sounded through the hill
	country of Ephraim, and all the Israelites descended with him from
	the hill country; and he took the lead.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵהֶם רִדְפוּ אַחֲרַי כִּי־נָתַן יְהֹוָה אֶת־אֹיְבֵיכֶם אֶת־מוֹאָב בְּיֶדְכֶם וַיֵּרְדוּ אַחֲרָיו וַיִּלְכְּדוּ אֶת־מַעְבְּרוֹת הַיַּרְדֵּן לְמוֹאָב וְלֹא־נָתְנוּ אִישׁ
	לַעֲבֹר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“Follow me closely,” he said, “for YHWH has delivered your
	enemies, the Moabites, into your hands.” They followed him down
	and seized the fords of the Jordan against the Moabites; they let
	no one cross.
}

\eX{
	On that occasion they slew about 10,000 Moabites; they were all
	robust and brave men, yet not one of them escaped.
}

\eX{
	On that day, Moab submitted to Israel; and the land was tranquil
	for eighty years.
}

\eX{
	After him came Shamgar son of Anath, who slew six hundred
	Philistines with an oxgoad. He too was a champion of Israel.
}

\eX{
	The Israelites again did what was offensive to YHWH—Ehud now being
	dead.
}

\eX{
	And YHWH surrendered them to King Jabin of Canaan, who reigned in
	Hazor. His army commander was Sisera, whose base was
	Harosheth-goiim.
}

\eX{
	The Israelites cried out to YHWH; for he had nine hundred iron
	chariots, and he had oppressed Israel ruthlessly for twenty years.
}

\eX{
	Deborah, wife of Lappidoth, was a prophetess; she led Israel at
	that time.
}

\eX{
	She used to sit under the Palm of Deborah, between Ramah and
	Bethel in the hill country of Ephraim, and the Israelites would
	come to her for decisions.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתִּשְׁלַח וַתִּקְרָא לְבָרָק בֶּן־אֲבִינֹעַם מִקֶּדֶשׁ נַפְתָּלִי וַתֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו הֲלֹא צִוָּה יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל לֵךְ וּמָשַׁכְתָּ בְּהַר תָּבוֹר וְלָקַחְתָּ עִמְּךָ
	עֲשֶׂרֶת אֲלָפִים אִישׁ מִבְּנֵי נַפְתָּלִי וּמִבְּנֵי זְבֻלוּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	She summoned Barak son of Abinoam, of Kedesh in Naphtali, and said
	to him, “YHWH, the God of Israel, has commanded: Go, march up to
	Mount Tabor, and take with you ten thousand men of Naphtali and
	Zebulun.
}

\eX{
	And I will draw Sisera, Jabin’s army commander, with his chariots
	and his troops, toward you up to the Wadi Kishon; and I will
	deliver him into your hands.”
}

\eX{
	But Barak said to her, “If you will go with me, I will go; if not,
	I will not go.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתֹּאמֶר הָלֹךְ אֵלֵךְ עִמָּךְ אֶפֶס כִּי לֹא תִהְיֶה תִּפְאַרְתְּךָ עַל־הַדֶּרֶךְ אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה הוֹלֵךְ כִּי בְיַד־אִשָּׁה יִמְכֹּר יְהֹוָה אֶת־סִיסְרָא וַתָּקם דְּבוֹרָה
	וַתֵּלֶךְ עִם־בָּרָק קֶדְשָׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“Very well, I will go with you,” she answered. “However, there
	will be no glory for you in the course you are taking, for then
	YHWH will deliver Sisera into the hands of a woman.” So Deborah
	went with Barak to Kedesh.
}

\eX{
	Barak then mustered Zebulun and Naphtali at Kedesh; ten thousand
	men marched up after him; and Deborah also went up with him.
}

\eX{
	Now Heber the Kenite had separated from the other Kenites,
	descendants of Hobab, father-in-law of Moses, and had pitched his
	tent at Elon-bezaanannim, which is near Kedesh.
}

\eX{
	Sisera was informed that Barak son of Abinoam had gone up to Mount
	Tabor.
}

\eX{
	So Sisera ordered all his chariots—nine hundred iron chariots—and
	all the troops he had to move from Harosheth-goiim to the Wadi
	Kishon.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתֹּאמֶר דְּבֹרָה אֶל־בָּרָק קוּם כִּי זֶה הַיּוֹם אֲשֶׁר נָתַן יְהֹוָה אֶת־סִיסְרָא בְּיָדֶךָ הֲלֹא יְהֹוָה יָצָא לְפָנֶיךָ וַיֵּרֶד בָּרָק מֵהַר תָּבוֹר וַעֲשֶׂרֶת אֲלָפִים
	אִישׁ אַחֲרָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then Deborah said to Barak, “Up! This is the day on which YHWH
	will deliver Sisera into your hands: YHWH is marching before you.”
	Barak charged down Mount Tabor, followed by the ten thousand men,
}

\eX{
	and YHWH threw Sisera and all his chariots and army into a panic
	before the onslaught of Barak. Sisera leaped from his chariot and
	fled on foot
}

\eX{
	as Barak pursued the chariots and the soldiers as far as
	Harosheth-goiim. All of Sisera’s soldiers fell by the sword; not a
	man was left.
}

\eX{
	Sisera, meanwhile, had fled on foot to the tent of Jael, wife of
	Heber the Kenite; for there was friendship between King Jabin of
	Hazor and the family of Heber the Kenite.
}

\eX{
	Jael came out to greet Sisera and said to him, “Come in, my lord,
	come in here, do not be afraid.” So he entered her tent, and she
	covered him with a blanket.
}

\eX{
	He said to her, “Please let me have some water; I am thirsty.” She
	opened a skin of milk and gave him some to drink; and she covered
	him again.
}

\eX{
	He said to her, “Stand at the entrance of the tent. If anybody
	comes and asks you if there is anybody here, say ‘No.’”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתִּקַּח יָעֵל אֵשֶׁת־חֶבֶר אֶת־יְתַד הָאֹהֶל וַתָּשֶׂם אֶת־הַמַּקֶּבֶת בְּיָדָהּ וַתָּבוֹא אֵלָיו בַּלָּאט וַתִּתְקַע אֶת־הַיָּתֵד בְּרַקָּתוֹ וַתִּצְנַח בָּאָרֶץ וְהוּא־נִרְדָּם
	וַיָּעַף וַיָּמֹת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then Jael wife of Heber took a tent pin and grasped the mallet.
	When he was fast asleep from exhaustion, she approached him
	stealthily and drove the pin through his temple till it went down
	to the ground. Thus he died.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 4:21. Jael uses a tent peg and a hammer, household tools in the hand of a
%		woman. Sisera comes in looking for cover and gets the roof of the tent, personally
%		delivered.
%	}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהִנֵּה בָרָק רֹדֵף אֶת־סִיסְרָא וַתֵּצֵא יָעֵל לִקְרָאתוֹ וַתֹּאמֶר לוֹ לֵךְ וְאַרְאֶךָּ אֶת־הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־אַתָּה מְבַקֵּשׁ וַיָּבֹא אֵלֶיהָ וְהִנֵּה סִיסְרָא נֹפֵל מֵת
	וְהַיָּתֵד בְּרַקָּתוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Now Barak appeared in pursuit of Sisera. Jael went out to greet
	him and said, “Come, I will show you the man you are looking for.”
	He went inside with her, and there Sisera was lying dead, with the
	pin in his temple.
}

\eX{
	On that day God subdued King Jabin of Canaan before the
	Israelites.
}

\eX{
	The hand of the Israelites bore harder and harder on King Jabin of
	Canaan, until they destroyed King Jabin of Canaan.
}

\eProto{
	When locks go untrimmed in Israel, When people dedicate
	themselves— Bless YHWH!
}

\eProto{
	Hear, O kings! Give ear, O potentates! I will sing, will sing to
	YHWH, Will hymn YHWH, the God of Israel.
}

\eProto{
	O LORD, when You came forth from Seir, Advanced from the country
	of Edom, The earth trembled; The heavens dripped, Yea, the clouds
	dripped water,
}

\eProto{
	The mountains quaked— Before YHWH, Him of Sinai, Before YHWH, God
	of Israel.
}

\eProto{
	In the days of Shamgar son of Anath, In the days of Jael, caravans
	ceased, And wayfarers went By roundabout paths.
}

\eProto{
	Deliverance ceased, Ceased in Israel, Till you arose, O Deborah,
	Arose, O mother, in Israel!
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 5:7. The song says the villages ceased until Deborah arose. The poetry
%		makes the rescue begin in grammar before it begins in battle.
%	}

tex.english:
\eProto{
	When they chose new gods, Was there a fighter then in the gates?
	No shield or spear was seen Among forty thousand in Israel!
}

\eProto{
	My heart is with Israel’s leaders, With the dedicated of the
	people— Bless YHWH!
}

\eProto{
	You riders on tawny she-asses, You who sit on saddle rugs, And you
	wayfarers, declare it!
}

\eProto{
	Louder than the sound of archers, There among the watering places
	Let them chant the gracious acts of YHWH, His gracious deliverance
	of Israel. Then did the people of YHWH March down to the gates!
}

\eProto{
	Awake, awake, O Deborah! Awake, awake, strike up the chant! Arise,
	O Barak; Take your captives, O son of Abinoam!
}

\eProto{
	Then was the remnant made victor over the mighty, YHWH’s people
	won my victory over the warriors.
}

\eProto{
	From Ephraim came they whose roots are in Amalek; After you, your
	kin Benjamin; From Machir came down leaders, From Zebulun such as
	hold the marshal’s staff.
}

\eProto{
	And Issachar’s chiefs were with Deborah; As Barak, so was
	Issachar— Rushing after him into the valley. Among the clans of
	Reuben Were great decisions of heart.
}

\eProto{
	Why then did you stay among the sheepfolds And listen as they pipe
	for the flocks? Among the clans of Reuben Were great searchings of
	heart!
}

\eProto{
	Gilead tarried beyond the Jordan; And Dan—why did he linger by the
	ships? Asher remained at the seacoast And tarried at his landings.
}

\eProto{
	Zebulun is a people that mocked at death, Naphtali—on the open
	heights.
}

\eProto{
	Then the kings came, they fought: The kings of Canaan fought At
	Taanach, by Megiddo’s waters— They got no spoil of silver.
}

\eProto{
	The stars fought from heaven, From their courses they fought
	against Sisera.
}

\eProto{
	The torrent Kishon swept them away, The raging torrent, the
	torrent Kishon. March on, my soul, with courage!
}

\eProto{
	Then the horses’ hoofs pounded As headlong galloped the steeds.
}

\eProto{
	“Curse Meroz!” said the angel of YHWH. “Bitterly curse its
	inhabitants, Because they came not to the aid of YHWH, To the aid
	of YHWH among the warriors.”
}

\eProto{
	Most blessed of women be Jael, Wife of Heber the Kenite, Most
	blessed of women in tents.
}

\eProto{
	He asked for water, she offered milk; In a princely bowl she
	brought him curds.
}

\eProto{
	Her [left] hand reached for the tent pin, Her right for the
	workmen’s hammer. She struck Sisera, crushed his head, Smashed and
	pierced his temple.
}

\eProto{
	At her feet he sank, lay outstretched, At her feet he sank, lay
	still; Where he sank, there he lay—destroyed.
}

\eProto{
	Through the window peered Sisera’s mother, Behind the lattice she
	whined: “Why is his chariot so long in coming? Why so late the
	clatter of his wheels?”
}

\eProto{
	The wisest of her ladies give answer; She, too, replies to
	herself:
}

\eProto{
	“They must be dividing the spoil they have found: A damsel or two
	for each man, Spoil of dyed cloths for Sisera, Spoil of
	embroidered cloths, A couple of embroidered cloths Round every
	neck as spoil.”
}

\eProto{
	So may all Your enemies perish, O LORD! But may His friends be as
	the sun rising in might! And the land was tranquil forty years.
}

\eX{
	Then the Israelites did what was offensive to YHWH, and YHWH
	delivered them into the hands of the Midianites for seven years.
}

\eX{
	The hand of the Midianites prevailed over Israel; and because of
	Midian, the Israelites provided themselves with refuges in the
	caves and strongholds of the mountains.
}

\eX{
	After the Israelites had done their sowing, Midian, Amalek, and
	the Kedemites would come up and raid them;
}

\eX{
	they would attack them, destroy the produce of the land all the
	way to Gaza, and leave no means of sustenance in Israel, not a
	sheep or an ox or an ass.
}

\eX{
	For they would come up with their livestock and their tents,
	swarming as thick as locusts; they and their camels were
	innumerable. Thus they would invade the land and ravage it.
}

\eX{
	Israel was reduced to utter misery by the Midianites, and the
	Israelites cried out to YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יְהֹוָה אִישׁ נָבִיא אֶל־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמֶר לָהֶם כֹּה־אָמַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אָנֹכִי הֶעֱלֵיתִי אֶתְכֶם מִמִּצְרַיִם וָאֹצִיא אֶתְכֶם מִבֵּית
	עֲבָדִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	YHWH sent a prophet to the Israelites who said to them, “Thus said
	YHWH, the God of Israel: I brought you up out of Egypt and freed
	you from the house of bondage.
}

\eX{
	I rescued you from the Egyptians and from all your oppressors; I
	drove them out before you, and gave you their land.
}

\eX{
	And I said to you, ‘I YHWH am your God. You must not worship the
	gods of the Amorites in whose land you dwell.’ But you did not
	obey Me.”
}

\eX{
	An angel of YHWH came and sat under the terebinth at Ophrah, which
	belonged to Joash the Abiezrite. His son Gideon was then beating
	out wheat inside a winepress in order to keep it safe from the
	Midianites.
}

\eX{
	The angel of YHWH appeared to him and said to him, “YHWH is with
	you, valiant warrior!”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 6:12. The angel calls Gideon a strong man of force while Gideon is hiding
%		wheat in a winepress. Divine address has a sense of humor, or a long fuse.
%	}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו גִּדְעוֹן בִּי אֲדֹנִי וְיֵשׁ יְהֹוָה עִמָּנוּ וְלָמָּה מְצָאַתְנוּ כּל־זֹאת וְאַיֵּה כל־נִפְלְאֹתָיו אֲשֶׁר סִפְּרוּ־לָנוּ אֲבוֹתֵינוּ לֵאמֹר הֲלֹא מִמִּצְרַיִם
	הֶעֱלָנוּ יְהֹוָה וְעַתָּה נְטָשָׁנוּ יְהֹוָה וַיִּתְּנֵנוּ בְּכַף־מִדְיָן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	YHWH turned to him and said, “Go in this strength of yours and
	deliver Israel from the Midianites. I herewith make you My
	messenger.”
}

\eX{
	He said to Him, “Please, my lord, how can I deliver Israel? Why,
	my clan is the humblest in Manasseh, and I am the youngest in my
	father’s household.”
}

\eX{
	YHWH replied, “I will be with you, and you shall defeat Midian to
	a man.”
}

\eX{
	And he said to Him, “If I have gained Your favor, give me a sign
	that it is You who are speaking to me:
}

\eX{
	do not leave this place until I come back to You and bring out my
	offering and place it before You.” And He answered, “I will stay
	until you return.”
}

\eX{
	So Gideon went in and prepared a kid, and [baked] unleavened bread
	from an ephah of flour. He put the meat in a basket and poured the
	broth into a pot, and he brought them out to Him under the
	terebinth. As he presented them,
}

\eX{
	the angel of God said to him, “Take the meat and the unleavened
	bread, put them on yonder rock, and spill out the broth.” He did
	so.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח מַלְאַךְ יְהֹוָה אֶת־קְצֵה הַמִּשְׁעֶנֶת אֲשֶׁר בְּיָדוֹ וַיִּגַּע בַּבָּשָׂר וּבַמַּצּוֹת וַתַּעַל הָאֵשׁ מִן־הַצּוּר וַתֹּאכַל אֶת־הַבָּשָׂר וְאֶת־הַמַּצּוֹת
	וּמַלְאַךְ יְהֹוָה הָלַךְ מֵעֵינָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The angel of YHWH held out the staff that he carried, and touched
	the meat and the unleavened bread with its tip. A fire sprang up
	from the rock and consumed the meat and the unleavened bread. And
	the angel of YHWH vanished from his sight.
}

\eX{
	Then Gideon realized that it was an angel of YHWH; and Gideon
	said, “Alas, O Lord GOD! For I have seen an angel of YHWH face to
	face.”
}

\eX{
	But YHWH said to him, “All is well; have no fear, you shall not
	die.”
}

\eX{
	So Gideon built there an altar to YHWH and called it
	Adonai-shalom. To this day it stands in Ophrah of the Abiezrites.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי בַּלַּיְלָה הַהוּא וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ יְהֹוָה קַח אֶת־פַּר־הַשּׁוֹר אֲשֶׁר לְאָבִיךָ וּפַר הַשֵּׁנִי שֶׁבַע שָׁנִים וְהָרַסְתָּ אֶת־מִזְבַּח הַבַּעַל אֲשֶׁר לְאָבִיךָ
	וְאֶת־הָאֲשֵׁרָה אֲשֶׁר־עָלָיו תִּכְרֹת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	That night YHWH said to him: “Take the young bull belonging to
	your father and another bull seven years old; pull down the altar
	of Baal which belongs to your father, and cut down the sacred post
	which is beside it.
}

\eX{
	Then build an altar to YHWH your God, on the level ground on top
	of this stronghold. Take the other bull and offer it as a burnt
	offering, using the wood of the sacred post that you have cut
	down.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח גִּדְעוֹן עֲשָׂרָה אֲנָשִׁים מֵעֲבָדָיו וַיַּעַשׂ כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר אֵלָיו יְהֹוָה וַיְהִי כַּאֲשֶׁר יָרֵא אֶת־בֵּית אָבִיו וְאֶת־אַנְשֵׁי הָעִיר מֵעֲשׂוֹת יוֹמָם
	וַיַּעַשׂ לָיְלָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So Gideon took ten of his servants and did as YHWH had told him;
	but as he was afraid to do it by day, on account of his father’s
	household and the townspeople, he did it by night.
}

\eX{
	Early the next morning, the townspeople found that the altar of
	Baal had been torn down and the sacred post beside it had been cut
	down, and that the second bull had been offered on the newly built
	altar.
}

\eX{
	They said to one another, “Who did this thing?” Upon inquiry and
	investigation, they were told, “Gideon son of Joash did this
	thing!”
}

\eX{
	The townspeople said to Joash, “Bring out your son, for he must
	die: he has torn down the altar of Baal and cut down the sacred
	post beside it!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יוֹאָשׁ לְכֹל אֲשֶׁר־עָמְדוּ עָלָיו הַאַתֶּם תְּרִיבוּן לַבַּעַל אִם־אַתֶּם תּוֹשִׁיעוּן אוֹתוֹ אֲשֶׁר יָרִיב לוֹ יוּמַת עַד־הַבֹּקֶר אִם־אֱלֹהִים הוּא יָרֶב לוֹ כִּי
	נָתַץ אֶת־מִזְבְּחוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But Joash said to all who had risen against him, “Do you have to
	contend for Baal? Do you have to vindicate him? Whoever fights his
	battles shall be dead by morning! If he is a god, let him fight
	his own battles, since it is his altar that has been torn down!”
}

\eX{
	That day they named him Jerubbaal, meaning “Let Baal contend with
	him, since he tore down his altar.”
}

\eX{
	All Midian, Amalek, and the Kedemites joined forces; they crossed
	over and encamped in the Valley of Jezreel.
}

\eX{
	The spirit of YHWH enveloped Gideon; he sounded the horn, and the
	Abiezrites rallied behind him.
}

\eX{
	And he sent messengers throughout Manasseh, and they too rallied
	behind him. He then sent messengers through Asher, Zebulun, and
	Naphtali, and they came up to meet the Manassites.
}

\eX{
	And Gideon said to God, “If You really intend to deliver Israel
	through me as You have said—
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי מַצִּיג אֶת־גִּזַּת הַצֶּמֶר בַּגֹּרֶן אִם טַל יִהְיֶה עַל־הַגִּזָּה לְבַדָּהּ וְעַל־כּל־הָאָרֶץ חֹרֶב וְיָדַעְתִּי כִּי־תוֹשִׁיעַ בְּיָדִי אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר
	דִּבַּרְתָּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	here I place a fleece of wool on the threshing floor. If dew falls
	only on the fleece and all the ground remains dry, I shall know
	that You will deliver Israel through me, as You have said.”
}

\eX{
	And that is what happened. Early the next day, he squeezed the
	fleece and wrung out the dew from the fleece, a bowlful of water.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר גִּדְעוֹן אֶל־הָאֱלֹהִים אַל־יִחַר אַפְּךָ בִּי וַאֲדַבְּרָה אַךְ הַפָּעַם אֲנַסֶּה נָּא־רַק־הַפַּעַם בַּגִּזָּה יְהִי־נָא חֹרֶב אֶל־הַגִּזָּה לְבַדָּהּ וְעַל־כּל־הָאָרֶץ
	יִהְיֶה־טָּל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then Gideon said to God, “Do not be angry with me if I speak just
	once more. Let me make just one more test with the fleece: let the
	fleece alone be dry, while there is dew all over the ground.”
}

\eX{
	God did so that night: only the fleece was dry, while there was
	dew all over the ground.
}

\eX{
	Early next day, Jerubbaal—that is, Gideon—and all the troops with
	him encamped above En-harod, while the camp of Midian was in the
	plain to the north of him, at Gibeath-moreh.
}

\eX{
	YHWH said to Gideon, “You have too many troops with you for Me to
	deliver Midian into their hands; Israel might claim for themselves
	the glory due to Me, thinking, ‘Our own hand has brought us
	victory.’
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 7:2. YHWH says the army is too many, and the book means it. Victory here
%		must not be something Israel can invoice to itself.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Therefore, announce to the men, ‘Let anybody who is timid and
	fearful turn back, as a bird flies from Mount Gilead.’” Thereupon,
	22,000 of the troops turned back and 10,000 remained.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֶל־גִּדְעוֹן עוֹד הָעָם רָב הוֹרֵד אוֹתָם אֶל־הַמַּיִם וְאֶצְרְפֶנּוּ לְךָ שָׁם וְהָיָה אֲשֶׁר אֹמַר אֵלֶיךָ זֶה יֵלֵךְ אִתָּךְ הוּא יֵלֵךְ אִתָּךְ וְכֹל
	אֲשֶׁר־אֹמַר אֵלֶיךָ זֶה לֹא־יֵלֵךְ עִמָּךְ הוּא לֹא יֵלֵךְ׃
}

\hX{
	וַיּוֹרֶד אֶת־הָעָם אֶל־הַמָּיִם וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֶל־גִּדְעוֹן כֹּל אֲשֶׁר־יָלֹק בִּלְשׁוֹנוֹ מִן־הַמַּיִם כַּאֲשֶׁר יָלֹק הַכֶּלֶב תַּצִּיג אוֹתוֹ לְבָד וְכֹל אֲשֶׁר־יִכְרַע
	עַל־בִּרְכָּיו לִשְׁתּוֹת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So he took the troops down to the water. Then YHWH said to Gideon,
	“Set apart all those who lap up the water with their tongues like
	dogs from all those who get down on their knees to drink.”
}

\eX{
	Now those who “lapped” the water into their mouths by hand
	numbered three hundred; all the rest of the troops got down on
	their knees to drink.
}

\eX{
	Then YHWH said to Gideon, “I will deliver you and I will put
	Midian into your hands through the three hundred ‘lappers’; let
	the rest of the troops go home.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְחוּ אֶת־צֵדָה הָעָם בְּיָדָם וְאֵת שׁוֹפְרֹתֵיהֶם וְאֵת כּל־אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל שִׁלַּח אִישׁ לְאֹהָלָיו וּבִשְׁלֹשׁ־מֵאוֹת הָאִישׁ הֶחֱזִיק וּמַחֲנֵה מִדְיָן הָיָה לוֹ
	מִתַּחַת בָּעֵמֶק׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So [the lappers] took the provisions and horns that the other men
	had with them, and he sent the rest of the men of Israel back to
	their homes, retaining only the three hundred men. The Midianite
	camp was below him, in the plain.
}

\eX{
	That night YHWH said to him, “Come, attack the camp, for I have
	delivered it into your hands.
}

\eX{
	And if you are afraid to attack, first go down to the camp with
	your attendant Purah
}

\eX{
	and listen to what they say; after that you will have the courage
	to attack the camp.” So he went down with his attendant Purah to
	the outposts of the warriors who were in the camp.—
}

\eX{
	Now Midian, Amalek, and all the Kedemites were spread over the
	plain, as thick as locusts; and their camels were countless, as
	numerous as the sands on the seashore.—
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹא גִדְעוֹן וְהִנֵּה־אִישׁ מְסַפֵּר לְרֵעֵהוּ חֲלוֹם וַיֹּאמֶר הִנֵּה חֲלוֹם חָלַמְתִּי וְהִנֵּה צְלִיל לֶחֶם שְׂעֹרִים מִתְהַפֵּךְ בְּמַחֲנֵה מִדְיָן וַיָּבֹא עַד־הָאֹהֶל
	וַיַּכֵּהוּ וַיִּפֹּל וַיַּהַפְכֵהוּ לְמַעְלָה וְנָפַל הָאֹהֶל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	To this the other responded, “That can only mean the sword of the
	Israelite Gideon son of Joash. God is delivering Midian and the
	entire camp into his hands.”
}

\eX{
	When Gideon heard the dream told and interpreted, he bowed low.
	Returning to the camp of Israel, he shouted, “Come on! YHWH has
	delivered the Midianite camp into your hands!”
}

\eX{
	He divided the three hundred men into three columns and equipped
	every man with a ram’s horn and an empty jar, with a torch in each
	jar.
}

\eX{
	“Watch me,” he said, “and do the same. When I get to the outposts
	of the camp, do exactly as I do.
}

\eX{
	When I and all those with me blow our horns, you too, all around
	the camp, will blow your horns and shout, ‘For YHWH and for
	Gideon!’”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹא גִדְעוֹן וּמֵאָה־אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־אִתּוֹ בִּקְצֵה הַמַּחֲנֶה רֹאשׁ הָאַשְׁמֹרֶת הַתִּיכוֹנָה אַךְ הָקֵם הֵקִימוּ אֶת־הַשֹּׁמְרִים וַיִּתְקְעוּ בַּשּׁוֹפָרוֹת וְנָפוֹץ
	הַכַּדִּים אֲשֶׁר בְּיָדָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Gideon and the hundred men with him arrived at the outposts of the
	camp, at the beginning of the middle watch, just after the
	sentries were posted. They sounded the horns and smashed the jars
	that they had with them,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּתְקְעוּ שְׁלֹשֶׁת הָרָאשִׁים בַּשּׁוֹפָרוֹת וַיִּשְׁבְּרוּ הַכַּדִּים וַיַּחֲזִיקוּ בְיַד־שְׂמֹאולָם בַּלַּפִּדִים וּבְיַד־יְמִינָם הַשּׁוֹפָרוֹת לִתְקוֹעַ וַיִּקְרְאוּ חֶרֶב
	לַיהֹוָה וּלְגִדְעוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	and the three columns blew their horns and broke their jars.
	Holding the torches in their left hands and the horns for blowing
	in their right hands, they shouted, “A sword for YHWH and for
	Gideon!”
}

\eX{
	They remained standing where they were, surrounding the camp; but
	the entire camp ran about yelling, and took to flight.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּתְקְעוּ שְׁלֹשׁ־מֵאוֹת הַשּׁוֹפָרוֹת וַיָּשֶׂם יְהֹוָה אֵת חֶרֶב אִישׁ בְּרֵעֵהוּ וּבְכל־הַמַּחֲנֶה וַיָּנס הַמַּחֲנֶה עַד־בֵּית הַשִּׁטָּה צְרֵרָתָה עַד שְׂפַת־אָבֵל מְחוֹלָה
	עַל־טַבָּת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	For when the three hundred horns were sounded, YHWH turned every
	man’s sword against his fellow, throughout the camp, and the
	entire host fled as far as Beth-shittah and on to Zererah—as far
	as the outskirts of Abel-meholah near Tabbath.
}

\eX{
	And now the men of Israel from Naphtali and Asher and from all of
	Manasseh rallied for the pursuit of the Midianites.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּמַלְאָכִים שָׁלַח גִּדְעוֹן בְּכל־הַר אֶפְרַיִם לֵאמֹר רְדוּ לִקְרַאת מִדְיָן וְלִכְדוּ לָהֶם אֶת־הַמַּיִם עַד בֵּית בָּרָה וְאֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן וַיִּצָּעֵק כּל־אִישׁ אֶפְרַיִם
	וַיִּלְכְּדוּ אֶת־הַמַּיִם עַד בֵּית בָּרָה וְאֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן׃
}

\hX{
	וַיִּלְכְּדוּ שְׁנֵי־שָׂרֵי מִדְיָן אֶת־עֹרֵב וְאֶת־זְאֵב וַיַּהַרְגוּ אֶת־עוֹרֵב בְּצוּר־עוֹרֵב וְאֶת־זְאֵב הָרְגוּ בְיֶקֶב־זְאֵב וַיִּרְדְּפוּ אֶל־מִדְיָן וְרֹאשׁ־עֹרֵב וּזְאֵב
	הֵבִיאוּ אֶל־גִּדְעוֹן מֵעֵבֶר לַיַּרְדֵּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They pursued the Midianites and captured Midian’s two generals,
	Oreb and Zeeb. They killed Oreb at the Rock of Oreb and they
	killed Zeeb at the Winepress of Zeeb; and they brought the heads
	of Oreb and Zeeb from the other side of the Jordan to Gideon.
}

\eX{
	And the men of Ephraim said to him, “Why did you do that to us—not
	calling us when you went to fight the Midianites?” And they
	rebuked him severely.
}

\eX{
	But he answered them, “After all, what have I accomplished
	compared to you? Why, Ephraim’s gleanings are better than
	Abiezer’s vintage!
}

\eX{
	God has delivered the Midianite generals Oreb and Zeeb into your
	hands, and what was I able to do compared to you?” And when he
	spoke in this fashion, their anger against him abated.
}

\eX{
	Gideon came to the Jordan and crossed it. The three hundred men
	with him were famished, but still in pursuit.
}

\eX{
	He said to the people of Succoth, “Please give some loaves of
	bread to the men who are following me, for they are famished, and
	I am pursuing Zebah and Zalmunna, the kings of Midian.”
}

\eX{
	But the officials of Succoth replied, “Are Zebah and Zalmunna
	already in your hands, that we should give bread to your troops?”
}

\eX{
	“I swear,” declared Gideon, “when YHWH delivers Zebah and Zalmunna
	into my hands, I’ll thresh your bodies upon desert thorns and
	briers!”
}

\eX{
	From there he went up to Penuel and made the same request of them;
	but the people of Penuel gave him the same reply as the people of
	Succoth.
}

\eX{
	So he also threatened the people of Penuel: “When I come back
	safe, I’ll tear down this tower!”
}

\eX{
	Now Zebah and Zalmunna were at Karkor with their army of about
	15,000; these were all that remained of the entire host of the
	Kedemites, for the slain numbered 120,000 fighting men.
}

\eX{
	Gideon marched up the road of the tent dwellers, up to east of
	Nobah and Jogbehah, and routed the camp, which was off guard.
}

\eX{
	Zebah and Zalmunna took to flight, with Gideon in pursuit. He
	captured Zebah and Zalmunna, the two kings of Midian, and threw
	the whole army into panic.
}

\eX{
	On his way back from the battle at the Ascent of Heres, Gideon son
	of Joash
}

\eX{
	captured a boy from among the people of Succoth and interrogated
	him. The latter drew up for him a list of the officials and elders
	of Succoth, seventy-seven in number.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹא אֶל־אַנְשֵׁי סֻכּוֹת וַיֹּאמֶר הִנֵּה זֶבַח וְצַלְמֻנָּע אֲשֶׁר חֵרַפְתֶּם אוֹתִי לֵאמֹר הֲכַף זֶבַח וְצַלְמֻנָּע עַתָּה בְּיָדֶךָ כִּי נִתֵּן לַאֲנָשֶׁיךָ הַיְּעֵפִים
	לָחֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then he came to the people of Succoth and said, “Here are Zebah
	and Zalmunna, about whom you mocked me, saying, ‘Are Zebah and
	Zalmunna already in your hands, that we should give your famished
	men bread?’”
}

\eX{
	And he took the elders of the city and, [bringing] desert thorns
	and briers, he punished the people of Succoth with them.
}

\eX{
	As for Penuel, he tore down its tower and killed the townspeople.
}

\eX{
	Then he asked Zebah and Zalmunna, “Those men you killed at Tabor,
	what were they like?” “They looked just like you,” they replied,
	“like sons of a king.”
}

\eX{
	“They were my brothers,” he declared, “the sons of my mother. As
	YHWH lives, if you had spared them, I would not kill you.”
}

\eX{
	And he commanded his oldest son Jether, “Go kill them!” But the
	boy did not draw his sword, for he was timid, being still a boy.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר זֶבַח וְצַלְמֻנָּע קוּם אַתָּה וּפְגַע־בָּנוּ כִּי כָאִישׁ גְּבוּרָתוֹ וַיָּקם גִּדְעוֹן וַיַּהֲרֹג אֶת־זֶבַח וְאֶת־צַלְמֻנָּע וַיִּקַּח אֶת־הַשַּׂהֲרֹנִים אֲשֶׁר
	בְּצַוְּארֵי גְמַלֵּיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then Zebah and Zalmunna said, “Come, you slay us; for strength
	comes with manhood.” So Gideon went over and killed Zebah and
	Zalmunna, and he took the crescents that were on the necks of
	their camels.
}

\eX{
	Then the men of Israel said to Gideon, “Rule over us—you, your
	son, and your grandson as well; for you have saved us from the
	Midianites.”
}

\eX{
	But Gideon replied, “I will not rule over you myself, nor shall my
	son rule over you; YHWH alone shall rule over you.”
}

\eX{
	And Gideon said to them, “I have a request to make of you: Each of
	you give me the earring he received as booty.” (They had golden
	earrings, for they were Ishmaelites.)
}

\eX{
	“Certainly!” they replied. And they spread out a cloth, and
	everyone threw onto it the earring he had received as booty.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי מִשְׁקַל נִזְמֵי הַזָּהָב אֲשֶׁר שָׁאָל אֶלֶף וּשְׁבַע־מֵאוֹת זָהָב לְבַד מִן־הַשַּׂהֲרֹנִים וְהַנְּטִיפוֹת וּבִגְדֵי הָאַרְגָּמָן שֶׁעַל מַלְכֵי מִדְיָן וּלְבַד
	מִן־הָעֲנָקוֹת אֲשֶׁר בְּצַוְּארֵי גְמַלֵּיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The weight of the golden earrings that he had requested came to
	1,700 shekels of gold; this was in addition to the crescents and
	the pendants and the purple robes worn by the kings of Midian and
	in addition to the collars on the necks of their camels.
}

\eX{
	Gideon made an ephod of this gold and set it up in his own town of
	Ophrah. There all Israel went astray after it, and it became a
	snare to Gideon and his household.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 8:27. The ephod becomes a snare after Gideon refuses kingship. Judges
%		often lets the wrong thing happen right after the right sentence.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Thus Midian submitted to the Israelites and did not raise its head
	again; and the land was tranquil for forty years in Gideon’s time.
}

\eJ{
	Gideon had seventy sons of his own issue, for he had many wives.
}

\eJ{
	A son was also born to him by his concubine in Shechem, and he
	named him Abimelech.
}

\eJ{
	Gideon son of Joash died at a ripe old age, and was buried in the
	tomb of his father Joash at Ophrah of the Abiezrites.
}

\eX{
	After Gideon died, the Israelites again went astray after the
	Baalim, and they adopted Baal-berith as a god.
}

\eX{
	The Israelites gave no thought to YHWH their God, who saved them
	from all the enemies around them.
}

\eX{
	Nor did they show loyalty to the house of Jerubbaal-Gideon in
	return for all the good that he had done for Israel.
}

\eJ{
	Abimelech son of Jerubbaal went to his mother’s brothers in
	Shechem and spoke to them and to the whole clan of his mother’s
	family. He said,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	דַּבְּרוּ־נָא בְּאזְנֵי כל־בַּעֲלֵי שְׁכֶם מַה־טּוֹב לָכֶם הַמְשֹׁל בָּכֶם שִׁבְעִים אִישׁ כֹּל בְּנֵי יְרֻבַּעַל אִם־מְשֹׁל בָּכֶם אִישׁ אֶחָד וּזְכַרְתֶּם כִּי־עַצְמְכֶם
	וּבְשַׂרְכֶם אָנִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	“Put this question to all the citizens of Shechem: Which is better
	for you, to be ruled by seventy men—by all the sons of
	Jerubbaal—or to be ruled by one man? And remember, I am your own
	flesh and blood.”
}

\eJ{
	His mother’s brothers said all this in his behalf to all the
	citizens of Shechem, and they were won over to Abimelech; for they
	thought, “He is our kinsman.”
}

\eJ{
	They gave him seventy shekels from the temple of Baal-berith; and
	with this Abimelech hired some worthless and reckless fellows, and
	they followed him.
}

\eJ{
	Then he went to his father’s house in Ophrah and killed his
	brothers, the sons of Jerubbaal, seventy men on one stone. Only
	Jotham, the youngest son of Jerubbaal, survived, because he went
	into hiding.
}

\eJ{
	All the citizens of Shechem and all Beth-millo convened, and they
	proclaimed Abimelech king at the terebinth of the pillar at
	Shechem.
}

\eJ{
	When Jotham was informed, he went and stood on top of Mount
	Gerizim and called out to them in a loud voice. “Citizens of
	Shechem!” he cried, “Listen to me, that God may listen to you.
}

\eJ{
	“Once the trees went to anoint a king over themselves. They said
	to the olive tree, ‘Reign over us.’
}

tex.commentary:
Qere Ketiv.

%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 9:8. Jotham's trees are not ornamental. They are a political theory with
%		bark on it.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But the olive tree replied, ‘Have I, through whom God and men are
	honored, stopped yielding my rich oil, that I should go and wave
	above the trees?’
}

\eJ{
	So the trees said to the fig tree, ‘You come and reign over us.’
}

\eJ{
	But the fig tree replied, ‘Have I stopped yielding my sweetness,
	my delicious fruit, that I should go and wave above the trees?’
}

\eJ{
	So the trees said to the vine, ‘You come and reign over us.’
}

\eJ{
	But the vine replied, ‘Have I stopped yielding my new wine, which
	gladdens God and men, that I should go and wave above the trees?’
}

\eJ{
	Then all the trees said to the thornbush, ‘You come and reign over
	us.’
}

\eJ{
	And the thornbush said to the trees, ‘If you are acting honorably
	in anointing me king over you, come and take shelter in my shade;
	but if not, may fire issue from the thornbush and consume the
	cedars of Lebanon!’
}

\eJ{
	“Now then, if you acted honorably and loyally in making Abimelech
	king, if you have done right by Jerubbaal and his house and have
	requited him according to his deserts—
}

\eX{
	considering that my father fought for you and saved you from the
	Midianites at the risk of his life,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְאַתֶּם קַמְתֶּם עַל־בֵּית אָבִי הַיּוֹם וַתַּהַרְגוּ אֶת־בָּנָיו שִׁבְעִים אִישׁ עַל־אֶבֶן אֶחָת וַתַּמְלִיכוּ אֶת־אֲבִימֶלֶךְ בֶּן־אֲמָתוֹ עַל־בַּעֲלֵי שְׁכֶם כִּי אֲחִיכֶם
	הוּא׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	and now you have turned on my father’s household, killed his sons,
	seventy men on one stone, and set up Abimelech, the son of his
	handmaid, as king over the citizens of Shechem just because he is
	your kinsman—
}

\eJ{
	if, I say, you have this day acted honorably and loyally toward
	Jerubbaal and his house, have joy in Abimelech and may he likewise
	have joy in you.
}

\eJ{
	But if not, may fire issue from Abimelech and consume the citizens
	of Shechem and Beth-millo, and may fire issue from the citizens of
	Shechem and Beth-millo and consume Abimelech!”
}

\eJ{
	With that, Jotham fled. He ran to Beer and stayed there, because
	of his brother Abimelech.
}

\eJ{
	Then God sent a spirit of discord between Abimelech and the
	citizens of Shechem, and the citizens of Shechem broke faith with
	Abimelech—
}

\eJ{
	to the end that the crime committed against the seventy sons of
	Jerubbaal might be avenged, and their blood recoil upon their
	brother Abimelech, who had slain them, and upon the citizens of
	Shechem, who had abetted him in the slaying of his brothers.
}

\eJ{
	The citizens of Shechem planted ambuscades against him on the
	hilltops; and they robbed whoever passed by them on the road. Word
	of this reached Abimelech.
}

\eJ{
	Then Gaal son of Ebed and his companions came passing through
	Shechem, and the citizens of Shechem gave him their confidence.
}

\eJ{
	They went out into the fields, gathered and trod out the vintage
	of their vineyards, and made a festival. They entered the temple
	of their god, and as they ate and drank they reviled Abimelech.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר גַּעַל בֶּן־עֶבֶד מִי־אֲבִימֶלֶךְ וּמִי־שְׁכֶם כִּי נַעַבְדֶנּוּ הֲלֹא בֶן־יְרֻבַּעַל וּזְבֻל פְּקִידוֹ עִבְדוּ אֶת־אַנְשֵׁי חֲמוֹר אֲבִי שְׁכֶם וּמַדּוּעַ נַעַבְדֶנּוּ
	אֲנָחְנוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Gaal son of Ebed said, “Who is Abimelech and who are [we]
	Shechemites, that we should serve him? This same son of Jerubbaal
	and his lieutenant Zebul once served the men of Hamor, the father
	of Shechem; so why should we serve him?
}

\eJ{
	Oh, if only this people were under my command, I would get rid of
	Abimelech! One would challenge Abimelech, ‘Fill up your ranks and
	come out here!’”
}

\eJ{
	When Zebul, the governor of the city, heard the words of Gaal son
	of Ebed, he was furious.
}

\eJ{
	He sent messages to Abimelech at Tormah to say, “Gaal son of Ebed
	and his companions have come to Shechem and they are inciting the
	city against you.
}

\eJ{
	Therefore, set out at night with the forces you have with you and
	conceal yourself in the fields.
}

\eJ{
	Early next morning, as the sun rises, advance on the city. He and
	his men will thereupon come out against you, and you will do to
	him whatever you find possible.”
}

\eJ{
	Abimelech and all the men with him set out at night and disposed
	themselves against Shechem in four hiding places.
}

\eJ{
	When Gaal son of Ebed came out and stood at the entrance to the
	city gate, Abimelech and the army with him emerged from
	concealment.
}

\eJ{
	Gaal saw the army and said to Zebul, “That’s an army marching down
	from the hilltops!” But Zebul said to him, “The shadows of the
	hills look to you like men.”
}

\eJ{
	Gaal spoke up again, “Look, an army is marching down from
	Tabbur-erez, and another column is coming from the direction of
	Elon-meonenim.”
}

\eJ{
	“Well,” replied Zebul, “where is your boast, ‘Who is Abimelech
	that we should serve him’? There is the army you sneered at; now
	go out and fight it!”
}

\eJ{
	So Gaal went out at the head of the citizens of Shechem and gave
	battle to Abimelech.
}

\eJ{
	But he had to flee before him, and Abimelech pursued him, and many
	fell slain, all the way to the entrance of the gate.
}

\eJ{
	Then Abimelech stayed in Arumah, while Zebul expelled Gaal and his
	companions and kept them out of Shechem.
}

\eJ{
	The next day, when people went out into the fields, Abimelech was
	informed.
}

\eJ{
	Taking the army, he divided it into three columns and lay in
	ambush in the fields; and when he saw the people coming out of the
	city, he pounced upon them and struck them down.
}

\eJ{
	While Abimelech and the column that followed him dashed ahead and
	took up a position at the entrance of the city gate, the other two
	columns rushed upon all that were in the open and struck them
	down.
}

\eJ{
	Abimelech fought against the city all that day. He captured the
	city and massacred the people in it; he razed the town and sowed
	it with salt.
}

\eJ{
	When all the citizens of the Tower of Shechem learned of this,
	they went into the tunnel of the temple of El-berith.
}

\eJ{
	When Abimelech was informed that all the citizens of the Tower of
	Shechem had gathered [there],
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַל אֲבִימֶלֶךְ הַר־צַלְמוֹן הוּא וְכל־הָעָם אֲשֶׁר־אִתּוֹ וַיִּקַּח אֲבִימֶלֶךְ אֶת־הַקַּרְדֻּמּוֹת בְּיָדוֹ וַיִּכְרֹת שׂוֹכַת עֵצִים וַיִּשָּׂאֶהָ וַיָּשֶׂם עַל־שִׁכְמוֹ
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־הָעָם אֲשֶׁר־עִמּוֹ מָה רְאִיתֶם עָשִׂיתִי מַהֲרוּ עֲשׂוּ כָמוֹנִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Abimelech and all the troops he had with him went up on Mount
	Zalmon. Taking an ax in his hand, Abimelech lopped off a tree limb
	and lifted it onto his shoulder. Then he said to the troops that
	accompanied him, “What you saw me do—quick, do the same!”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּכְרְתוּ גַם־כּל־הָעָם אִישׁ שׂוֹכֹה וַיֵּלְכוּ אַחֲרֵי אֲבִימֶלֶךְ וַיָּשִׂימוּ עַל־הַצְּרִיחַ וַיַּצִּיתוּ עֲלֵיהֶם אֶת־הַצְּרִיחַ בָּאֵשׁ וַיָּמֻתוּ גַּם כּל־אַנְשֵׁי
	מִגְדַּל־שְׁכֶם כְּאֶלֶף אִישׁ וְאִשָּׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So each of the troops also lopped off a bough; then they marched
	behind Abimelech and laid them against the tunnel, and set fire to
	the tunnel over their heads. Thus all the people of the Tower of
	Shechem also perished, about a thousand men and women.
}

\eJ{
	Abimelech proceeded to Thebez; he encamped at Thebez and occupied
	it.
}

\eJ{
	Within the town was a fortified tower; and all the citizens of the
	town, men and women, took refuge there. They shut themselves in,
	and went up on the roof of the tower.
}

\eJ{
	Abimelech pressed forward to the tower and attacked it. He
	approached the door of the tower to set it on fire.
}

\eJ{
	But a woman dropped an upper millstone on Abimelech’s head and
	cracked his skull.
}

\eJ{
	He immediately cried out to his attendant, his arms-bearer, “Draw
	your dagger and finish me off, that they may not say of me, ‘A
	woman killed him!’” So his attendant stabbed him, and he died.
}

\eJ{
	When the men of Israel saw that Abimelech was dead, everyone went
	home.
}

\eJ{
	Thus God repaid Abimelech for the evil he had done to his father
	by slaying his seventy brothers;
}

\eJ{
	and God likewise repaid the men of Shechem for all their
	wickedness. And so the curse of Jotham son of Jerubbaal was
	fulfilled upon them.
}

\eX{
	After Abimelech, Tola son of Puah son of Dodo, a man of Issachar,
	arose to deliver Israel. He lived at Shamir in the hill country of
	Ephraim.
}

\eX{
	He led Israel for twenty-three years; then he died and was buried
	at Shamir.
}

\eX{
	After him arose Jair the Gileadite, and he led Israel for
	twenty-two years.
}

\eX{
	He had thirty sons, who rode on thirty burros and owned thirty
	boroughs in the region of Gilead; these are called Havvoth-jair to
	this day.)
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּסִיפוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לַעֲשׂוֹת הָרַע בְּעֵינֵי יְהֹוָה וַיַּעַבְדוּ אֶת־הַבְּעָלִים וְאֶת־הָעַשְׁתָּרוֹת וְאֶת־אֱלֹהֵי אֲרָם וְאֶת־אֱלֹהֵי צִידוֹן וְאֵת אֱלֹהֵי מוֹאָב
	וְאֵת אֱלֹהֵי בְנֵי־עַמּוֹן וְאֵת אֱלֹהֵי פְלִשְׁתִּים וַיַּעַזְבוּ אֶת־יְהֹוָה וְלֹא עֲבָדוּהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	The Israelites again did what was offensive to YHWH. They served
	the Baalim and the Ashtaroth, and the gods of Aram, the gods of
	Sidon, the gods of Moab, the gods of the Ammonites, and the gods
	of the Philistines; they forsook YHWH and did not serve Him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And YHWH, incensed with Israel, surrendered them to the
	Philistines and to the Ammonites.%
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּרְעֲצוּ וַיְרֹצְצוּ אֶת־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּשָּׁנָה הַהִיא שְׁמֹנֶה עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה אֶת־כּל־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן בְּאֶרֶץ הָאֱמֹרִי אֲשֶׁר
	בַּגִּלְעָד׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	That year they battered and shattered the Israelites—for eighteen
	years—all the Israelites beyond the Jordan, in [what had been] the
	land of the Amorites in Gilead.
}

\eJ{
	The Ammonites also crossed the Jordan to make war on Judah,
	Benjamin, and the House of Ephraim. Israel was in great distress.
}

\eDtrH{
	Then the Israelites cried out to YHWH, “We stand guilty before
	You, for we have forsaken our God and served the Baalim.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	But YHWH said to the Israelites, “[I have rescued you] from the
	Egyptians, from the Amorites, from the Ammonites, and from the
	Philistines.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Sidonians, Amalek, and Maon also oppressed you; and when you
	cried out to Me, I saved you from them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Yet you have forsaken Me and have served other gods. No, I will
	not deliver you again.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Go cry to the gods you have chosen; let them deliver you in your
	time of distress!”%
}

\eDtrH{
	But the Israelites implored YHWH: “We stand guilty. Do to us as
	You see fit; only save us this day!”%
}

\eDtrH{
	They removed the alien gods from among them and served YHWH; and
	He could not bear the miseries of Israel.%
}

\eJ{
	The Ammonites mustered and they encamped in Gilead; and the
	Israelites massed and they encamped at Mizpah.
}

\eJ{
	The troops—the officers of Gilead—said to one another, “Let the
	man who is the first to fight the Ammonites be chieftain over all
	the inhabitants of Gilead.”
}

\eJ{
	Jephthah the Gileadite was an able warrior, who was the son of a
	prostitute. Jephthah’s father was Gilead;
}

\eJ{
	but Gilead also had sons by his wife, and when the wife’s sons
	grew up, they drove Jephthah out. They said to him, “You shall
	have no share in our father’s property, for you are the son of an
	outsider.”
}

\eJ{
	So Jephthah fled from his brothers and settled in the Tob country.
	Men of low character gathered about Jephthah and went out raiding
	with him.
}

\eJ{
	And when the Ammonites attacked Israel, the elders of Gilead went
	to bring Jephthah back from the Tob country.
}

\eJ{
	They said to Jephthah, “Come be our chief, so that we can fight
	the Ammonites.”
}

\eJ{
	Jephthah replied to the elders of Gilead, “You are the very people
	who rejected me and drove me out of my father’s house. How can you
	come to me now when you are in trouble?”
}

\eJ{
	The elders of Gilead said to Jephthah, “Honestly, we have now
	turned back to you. If you come with us and fight the Ammonites,
	you shall be our commander over all the inhabitants of Gilead.”
}

\eJ{
	Jephthah said to the elders of Gilead, “[Very well,] if you bring
	me back to fight the Ammonites and YHWH delivers them to me, I am
	to be your commander.”
}

\eJ{
	And the elders of Gilead answered Jephthah, “YHWH Himself shall be
	witness between us: we will do just as you have said.”
}

\eJ{
	Jephthah went with the elders of Gilead, and the people made him
	their commander and chief. And Jephthah repeated all these terms
	before YHWH at Mizpah.
}

\eJ{
	Jephthah then sent messengers to the king of the Ammonites,
	saying, “What have you against me that you have come to make war
	on my country?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר מֶלֶךְ בְּנֵי־עַמּוֹן אֶל־מַלְאֲכֵי יִפְתָּח כִּי־לָקַח יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־אַרְצִי בַּעֲלוֹתוֹ מִמִּצְרַיִם מֵאַרְנוֹן וְעַד־הַיַּבֹּק וְעַד־הַיַּרְדֵּן וְעַתָּה הָשִׁיבָה
	אֶתְהֶן בְּשָׁלוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The king of the Ammonites replied to Jephthah’s messengers, “When
	Israel came from Egypt, they seized the land which is mine, from
	the Arnon to the Jabbok as far as the Jordan. Now, then, restore
	it peaceably.”
}

\eJ{
	Jephthah again sent messengers to the king of the Ammonites.
}

\eJ{
	He said to him, “Thus said Jephthah: Israel did not seize the land
	of Moab or the land of the Ammonites.
}

\eJ{
	When they left Egypt, Israel traveled through the wilderness to
	the Sea of Reeds and went on to Kadesh.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יִשְׂרָאֵל מַלְאָכִים אֶל־מֶלֶךְ אֱדוֹם לֵאמֹר אֶעְבְּרָה־נָּא בְאַרְצֶךָ וְלֹא שָׁמַע מֶלֶךְ אֱדוֹם וְגַם אֶל־מֶלֶךְ מוֹאָב שָׁלַח וְלֹא אָבָה וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל
	בְּקָדֵשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Israel then sent messengers to the king of Edom, saying, ‘Allow us
	to cross your country.’ But the king of Edom would not consent.
	They also sent a mission to the king of Moab, and he refused. So
	Israel, after staying at Kadesh,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ בַּמִּדְבָּר וַיָּסב אֶת־אֶרֶץ אֱדוֹם וְאֶת־אֶרֶץ מוֹאָב וַיָּבֹא מִמִּזְרַח־שֶׁמֶשׁ לְאֶרֶץ מוֹאָב וַיַּחֲנוּן בְּעֵבֶר אַרְנוֹן וְלֹא־בָאוּ בִּגְבוּל מוֹאָב כִּי
	אַרְנוֹן גְּבוּל מוֹאָב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	traveled on through the wilderness, skirting the land of Edom and
	the land of Moab. They kept to the east of the land of Moab until
	they encamped on the other side of the Arnon; and, since Moab ends
	at the Arnon, they never entered Moabite territory.
}

\eJ{
	“Then Israel sent messengers to Sihon king of the Amorites, the
	king of Heshbon. Israel said to him, ‘Allow us to cross through
	your country to our homeland.’
}

\eJ{
	But Sihon would not trust Israel to pass through his territory.
	Sihon mustered all his troops, and they encamped at Jahaz; he
	engaged Israel in battle.
}

\eJ{
	But YHWH, the God of Israel, delivered Sihon and all his troops
	into Israel’s hands, and they defeated them; and Israel took
	possession of all the land of the Amorites, the inhabitants of
	that land.
}

\eJ{
	Thus they possessed all the territory of the Amorites from the
	Arnon to the Jabbok and from the wilderness to the Jordan.
}

\eJ{
	“Now, then, YHWH, the God of Israel, dispossessed the Amorites
	before His people Israel; and should you possess their land?
}

\eJ{
	Do you not hold what Chemosh your god gives you to possess? So we
	will hold on to everything that YHWH our God has given us to
	possess.
}

\eJ{
	“Besides, are you any better than Balak son of Zippor, king of
	Moab? Did he start a quarrel with Israel or go to war with them?
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	בְּשֶׁבֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּחֶשְׁבּוֹן וּבִבְנוֹתֶיהָ וּבְעַרְעוֹר וּבִבְנוֹתֶיהָ וּבְכל־הֶעָרִים אֲשֶׁר עַל־יְדֵי אַרְנוֹן שְׁלֹשׁ מֵאוֹת שָׁנָה וּמַדּוּעַ לֹא־הִצַּלְתֶּם בָּעֵת
	הַהִיא׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	“While Israel has been inhabiting Heshbon and its dependencies,
	and Aroer and its dependencies, and all the towns along the Arnon
	for three hundred years, why have you not tried to recover them
	all this time?
}

\eJ{
	I have done you no wrong; yet you are doing me harm and making war
	on me. May YHWH, who judges, decide today between the Israelites
	and the Ammonites!”
}

\eJ{
	But the king of the Ammonites paid no heed to the message that
	Jephthah sent him.
}

\eJ{
	Then the spirit of YHWH came upon Jephthah. He marched through
	Gilead and Manasseh, passing Mizpeh of Gilead; and from Mizpeh of
	Gilead he crossed over [to] the Ammonites.
}

\eJ{
	And Jephthah made the following vow to YHWH: “If you deliver the
	Ammonites into my hands,
}

\eJ{
	then whatever comes out of the door of my house to meet me on my
	safe return from the Ammonites shall be YHWH’s and shall be
	offered by me as a burnt offering.”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 11:31. Jephthah's vow is worded with terrible openness: whoever comes
%		out. The tragedy is already inside the grammar.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Jephthah crossed over to the Ammonites and attacked them, and YHWH
	delivered them into his hands.
}

\eJ{
	He utterly routed them—from Aroer as far as Minnith, twenty
	towns—all the way to Abel-cheramim. So the Ammonites submitted to
	the Israelites.
}

\eJ{
	When Jephthah arrived at his home in Mizpah, there was his
	daughter coming out to meet him, with timbrel and dance! She was
	an only child; he had no other son or daughter.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כִרְאוֹתוֹ אוֹתָהּ וַיִּקְרַע אֶת־בְּגָדָיו וַיֹּאמֶר אֲהָהּ בִּתִּי הַכְרֵעַ הִכְרַעְתִּנִי וְאַתְּ הָיִית בְּעֹכְרָי וְאָנֹכִי פָּצִיתִי פִי אֶל־יְהֹוָה וְלֹא אוּכַל
	לָשׁוּב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	On seeing her, he rent his clothes and said, “Alas, daughter! You
	have brought me low; you have become my troubler! For I have
	uttered a vow to YHWH and I cannot retract.”
}

\eJ{
	“Father,” she said, “you have uttered a vow to YHWH; do to me as
	you have vowed, seeing that YHWH has vindicated you against your
	enemies, the Ammonites.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתֹּאמֶר אֶל־אָבִיהָ יֵעָשֶׂה לִּי הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה הַרְפֵּה מִמֶּנִּי שְׁנַיִם חֳדָשִׁים וְאֵלְכָה וְיָרַדְתִּי עַל־הֶהָרִים וְאֶבְכֶּה עַל־בְּתוּלַי אָנֹכִי (ורעיתי)
	[וְרֵעוֹתָי]׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	She further said to her father, “Let this be done for me: let me
	be for two months, and I will go with my companions and lament
	upon the hills and there bewail my maidenhood.”
}

\eJ{
	“Go,” he replied. He let her go for two months, and she and her
	companions went and bewailed her maidenhood upon the hills.
}

\eJ{
	After two months’ time, she returned to her father, and he did to
	her as he had vowed. She had never known a man. So it became a
	custom in Israel
}

\eJ{
	for the maidens of Israel to go every year, for four days in the
	year, and chant dirges for the daughter of Jephthah the Gileadite.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּצָּעֵק אִישׁ אֶפְרַיִם וַיַּעֲבֹר צָפוֹנָה וַיֹּאמְרוּ לְיִפְתָּח מַדּוּעַ עָבַרְתָּ לְהִלָּחֵם בִּבְנֵי־עַמּוֹן וְלָנוּ לֹא קָרָאתָ לָלֶכֶת עִמָּךְ בֵּיתְךָ נִשְׂרֹף עָלֶיךָ
	בָּאֵשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The men of Ephraim mustered and crossed [the Jordan] to Zaphon.
	They said to Jephthah, “Why did you march to fight the Ammonites
	without calling us to go with you? We’ll burn your house down over
	you!”
}

\eX{
	Jephthah answered them, “I and my people were in a bitter conflict
	with the Ammonites; and I summoned you, but you did not save me
	from them.
}

\eX{
	When I saw that you were no saviors, I risked my life and advanced
	against the Ammonites; and YHWH delivered them into my hands. Why
	have you come here now to fight against me?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְבֹּץ יִפְתָּח אֶת־כּל־אַנְשֵׁי גִלְעָד וַיִּלָּחֶם אֶת־אֶפְרָיִם וַיַּכּוּ אַנְשֵׁי גִלְעָד אֶת־אֶפְרַיִם כִּי אָמְרוּ פְּלִיטֵי אֶפְרַיִם אַתֶּם גִּלְעָד בְּתוֹךְ אֶפְרַיִם
	בְּתוֹךְ מְנַשֶּׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And Jephthah gathered all the men of Gilead and fought the
	Ephraimites. The men of Gilead defeated the Ephraimites; for they
	had said, “You Gileadites are nothing but fugitives from
	Ephraim—being in Manasseh is like being in Ephraim.”
}

\eX{
	The Gileadites held the fords of the Jordan against the
	Ephraimites. And when any fugitive from Ephraim said, “Let me
	cross,” the men of Gilead would ask him, “Are you an Ephraimite?”;
	if he said “No,”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ לוֹ אֱמר־נָא שִׁבֹּלֶת וַיֹּאמֶר סִבֹּלֶת וְלֹא יָכִין לְדַבֵּר כֵּן וַיֹּאחֲזוּ אוֹתוֹ וַיִּשְׁחָטוּהוּ אֶל־מַעְבְּרוֹת הַיַּרְדֵּן וַיִּפֹּל בָּעֵת הַהִיא מֵאֶפְרַיִם
	אַרְבָּעִים וּשְׁנַיִם אָלֶף׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	they would say to him, “Then say shibboleth”; but he would say
	“sibboleth,” not being able to pronounce it correctly. Thereupon
	they would seize him and slay him by the fords of the Jordan.
	Forty-two thousand Ephraimites fell at that time.
}

\eX{
	Jephthah led Israel six years. Then Jephthah the Gileadite died
	and he was buried in one of the towns of Gilead.
}

\eX{
	He had thirty sons, and he married off thirty daughters outside
	the clan and brought in thirty girls from outside the clan for his
	sons. He led Israel seven years.
}

\eX{
	After him, Elon the Zebulunite led Israel; he led Israel for ten
	years.
}

\eX{
	Then Elon the Zebulunite died and was buried in Aijalon, in the
	territory of Zebulun.
}

\eX{
	He had forty sons and thirty grandsons, who rode on seventy
	jackasses. He led Israel for eight years.
}

\eX{
	Then Abdon son of Hillel the Pirathonite died. He was buried in
	Pirathon, in the territory of Ephraim, on the hill of the
	Amalekites.
}

\eX{
	The Israelites again did what was offensive to YHWH, and YHWH
	delivered them into the hands of the Philistines for forty years.
}

\eJ{
	There was a certain man from Zorah, of the stock of Dan, whose
	name was Manoah. His wife was barren and had borne no children.
}

\eJ{
	An angel of YHWH appeared to the woman and said to her, “You are
	barren and have borne no children; but you shall conceive and bear
	a son.
}

\eJ{
	Now be careful not to drink wine or other intoxicant, or to eat
	anything unclean.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	כִּי הִנָּךְ הָרָה וְיֹלַדְתְּ בֵּן וּמוֹרָה לֹא־יַעֲלֶה עַל־רֹאשׁוֹ כִּי־נְזִיר אֱלֹהִים יִהְיֶה הַנַּעַר מִן־הַבָּטֶן וְהוּא יָחֵל לְהוֹשִׁיעַ אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל מִיַּד
	פְּלִשְׁתִּים׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	For you are going to conceive and bear a son; let no razor touch
	his head, for the boy is to be a nazirite to God from the womb on.
	He shall be the first to deliver Israel from the Philistines.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתָּבֹא הָאִשָּׁה וַתֹּאמֶר לְאִישָׁהּ לֵאמֹר אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים בָּא אֵלַי וּמַרְאֵהוּ כְּמַרְאֵה מַלְאַךְ הָאֱלֹהִים נוֹרָא מְאֹד וְלֹא שְׁאִלְתִּיהוּ אֵי־מִזֶּה הוּא וְאֶת־שְׁמוֹ
	לֹא־הִגִּיד לִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The woman went and told her husband, “A man of God came to me; he
	looked like an angel of God, very frightening. I did not ask him
	where he was from, nor did he tell me his name.
}

\eJ{
	He said to me, ‘You are going to conceive and bear a son. Drink no
	wine or other intoxicant, and eat nothing unclean, for the boy is
	to be a nazirite to God from the womb to the day of his death!’”
}

\eJ{
	Manoah pleaded with YHWH. “Oh, my Lord!” he said, “please let the
	man of God that You sent come to us again, and let him instruct us
	how to act with the child that is to be born.”
}

\eJ{
	God heeded Manoah’s plea, and the angel of God came to the woman
	again. She was sitting in the field and her husband Manoah was not
	with her.
}

\eJ{
	The woman ran in haste to tell her husband. She said to him, “The
	man who came to me before has just appeared to me.”
}

\eJ{
	Manoah promptly followed his wife. He came to the man and asked
	him: “Are you the man who spoke to my wife?” “Yes,” he answered.
}

\eJ{
	Then Manoah said, “May your words soon come true! What rules shall
	be observed for the boy?”
}

\eJ{
	The angel of YHWH said to Manoah, “The woman must abstain from all
	the things against which I warned her.
}

\eJ{
	She must not eat anything that comes from the grapevine, or drink
	wine or other intoxicant, or eat anything unclean. She must
	observe all that I commanded her.”
}

\eJ{
	Manoah said to the angel of YHWH, “Let us detain you and prepare a
	kid for you.”
}

\eJ{
	But the angel of YHWH said to Manoah, “If you detain me, I shall
	not eat your food; and if you present a burnt offering, offer it
	to YHWH.”—For Manoah did not know that he was an angel of YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	So Manoah said to the angel of YHWH, “What is your name? We should
	like to honor you when your words come true.”
}

\eJ{
	The angel said to him, “You must not ask for my name; it is
	unknowable!”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 13:18. The messenger says his name is wonderful, or beyond knowing.
%		Manoah asks for a label; the scene gives him mystery with fire under it.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Manoah took the kid and the meal offering and offered them up on
	the rock to YHWH; and a marvelous thing happened while Manoah and
	his wife looked on.
}

\eJ{
	As the flames leaped up from the altar toward the sky, the angel
	of YHWH ascended in the flames of the altar, while Manoah and his
	wife looked on; and they flung themselves on their faces to the
	ground.—
}

\eJ{
	The angel of YHWH never appeared again to Manoah and his
	wife.—Manoah then realized that it had been an angel of YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	And Manoah said to his wife, “We shall surely die, for we have
	seen a divine being.”
}

\eJ{
	But his wife said to him, “Had YHWH meant to take our lives, He
	would not have accepted a burnt offering and meal offering from
	us, nor let us see all these things; and He would not have made
	such an announcement to us.”
}

\eJ{
	The woman bore a son, and she named him Samson. The boy grew up,
	and YHWH blessed him.
}

\eJ{
	The spirit of YHWH first moved him in the encampment of Dan,
	between Zorah and Eshtaol.
}

\eJ{
	Once Samson went down to Timnah; and while in Timnah, he noticed a
	girl among the Philistine women.
}

\eJ{
	On his return, he told his father and mother, “I noticed one of
	the Philistine women in Timnah; please get her for me as a wife.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ הַאֵין בִּבְנוֹת אַחֶיךָ וּבְכל־עַמִּי אִשָּׁה כִּי־אַתָּה הוֹלֵךְ לָקַחַת אִשָּׁה מִפְּלִשְׁתִּים הָעֲרֵלִים וַיֹּאמֶר שִׁמְשׁוֹן אֶל־אָבִיו אוֹתָהּ
	קַח־לִי כִּי־הִיא יָשְׁרָה בְעֵינָי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	His father and mother did not realize that this was YHWH’s doing:
	He was seeking a pretext against the Philistines, for the
	Philistines were ruling over Israel at that time.
}

\eJ{
	So Samson and his father and mother went down to Timnah. When he
	came to the vineyards of Timnah [for the first time], a full-grown
	lion came roaring at him.
}

\eJ{
	The spirit of YHWH gripped him, and he tore him asunder with his
	bare hands as one might tear a kid asunder; but he did not tell
	his father and mother what he had done.
}

\eJ{
	Then he went down and spoke to the woman, and she pleased Samson.
}

\eJ{
	Returning the following year to marry her, he turned aside to look
	at the remains of the lion; and in the lion’s skeleton he found a
	swarm of bees, and honey.
}

\eJ{
	He scooped it into his palms and ate it as he went along. When he
	rejoined his father and mother, he gave them some and they ate it;
	but he did not tell them that he had scooped the honey out of a
	lion’s skeleton.
}

\eJ{
	So his father came down to the woman, and Samson made a feast
	there, as young men used to do.
}

\eJ{
	When they saw him, they designated thirty companions to be with
	him.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לָהֶם שִׁמְשׁוֹן אָחוּדָה־נָּא לָכֶם חִידָה אִם־הַגֵּד תַּגִּידוּ אוֹתָהּ לִי שִׁבְעַת יְמֵי הַמִּשְׁתֶּה וּמְצָאתֶם וְנָתַתִּי לָכֶם שְׁלֹשִׁים סְדִינִים וּשְׁלֹשִׁים
	חֲלִפֹת בְּגָדִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then Samson said to them, “Let me propound a riddle to you. If you
	can give me the right answer during the seven days of the feast, I
	shall give you thirty linen tunics and thirty sets of clothing;
}

\eJ{
	but if you are not able to tell it to me, you must give me thirty
	linen tunics and thirty sets of clothing.” And they said to him,
	“Ask your riddle and we will listen.”
}

\eJ{
	So he said to them: “Out of the eater came something to eat, Out
	of the strong came something sweet.” For three days they could not
	answer the riddle.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 14:14. Samson's riddle is clever, unfair, and born from a carcass he
%		should not have touched. This is Judges in miniature: wit with flies on it.
%	}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי וַיֹּאמְרוּ לְאֵשֶׁת־שִׁמְשׁוֹן פַּתִּי אֶת־אִישֵׁךְ וְיַגֶּד־לָנוּ אֶת־הַחִידָה פֶּן־נִשְׂרֹף אוֹתָךְ וְאֶת־בֵּית אָבִיךְ בָּאֵשׁ הַלְירְשֵׁנוּ
	קְרָאתֶם לָנוּ הֲלֹא׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	On the seventh day, they said to Samson’s wife, “Coax your husband
	to provide us with the answer to the riddle; else we shall put you
	and your father’s household to the fire; have you invited us here
	in order to impoverish us?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתֵּבְךְּ אֵשֶׁת שִׁמְשׁוֹן עָלָיו וַתֹּאמֶר רַק־שְׂנֵאתַנִי וְלֹא אֲהַבְתָּנִי הַחִידָה חַדְתָּ לִבְנֵי עַמִּי וְלִי לֹא הִגַּדְתָּה וַיֹּאמֶר לָהּ הִנֵּה לְאָבִי וּלְאִמִּי לֹא
	הִגַּדְתִּי וְלָךְ אַגִּיד׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then Samson’s wife harassed him with tears, and she said, “You
	really hate me, you don’t love me. You asked my countrymen a
	riddle, and you didn’t tell me the answer.” He replied, “I haven’t
	even told my father and mother; shall I tell you?”
}

\eJ{
	During the rest of the seven days of the feast she continued to
	harass him with her tears, and on the seventh day he told her,
	because she nagged him so. And she explained the riddle to her
	countrymen.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ לוֹ אַנְשֵׁי הָעִיר בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי בְּטֶרֶם יָבֹא הַחַרְסָה מַה־מָּתוֹק מִדְּבַשׁ וּמֶה עַז מֵאֲרִי וַיֹּאמֶר לָהֶם לוּלֵא חֲרַשְׁתֶּם בְּעֶגְלָתִי לֹא מְצָאתֶם
	חִידָתִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	On the seventh day, before the sunset, the townsmen said to him:
	“What is sweeter than honey, And what is stronger than a lion?” He
	responded: “Had you not plowed with my heifer, You would not have
	guessed my riddle!”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתִּצְלַח עָלָיו רוּחַ יְהֹוָה וַיֵּרֶד אַשְׁקְלוֹן וַיַּךְ מֵהֶם שְׁלֹשִׁים אִישׁ וַיִּקַּח אֶת־חֲלִיצוֹתָם וַיִּתֵּן הַחֲלִיפוֹת לְמַגִּידֵי הַחִידָה וַיִּחַר אַפּוֹ וַיַּעַל
	בֵּית אָבִיהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The spirit of YHWH gripped him. He went down to Ashkelon and
	killed thirty of its men. He stripped them and gave the sets of
	clothing to those who had answered the riddle. And he left in a
	rage for his father’s house.
}

\eJ{
	Samson’s wife then married one of those who had been his wedding
	companions.
}

\eJ{
	Some time later, in the season of the wheat harvest, Samson came
	to visit his wife, bringing a kid as a gift. He said, “Let me go
	into the chamber to my wife.” But her father would not let him go
	in.
}

\eJ{
	“I was sure,” said her father, “that you had taken a dislike to
	her, so I gave her to your wedding companion. But her younger
	sister is more beautiful than she; let her become your wife
	instead.”
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon Samson declared, “Now the Philistines can have no claim
	against me for the harm I shall do them.”
}

\eJ{
	Samson went and caught three hundred foxes. He took torches and,
	turning [the foxes] tail to tail, he placed a torch between each
	pair of tails.
}

\eJ{
	He lit the torches and turned [the foxes] loose among the standing
	grain of the Philistines, setting fire to stacked grain, standing
	grain, vineyards, [and] olive trees.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ פְלִשְׁתִּים מִי עָשָׂה זֹאת וַיֹּאמְרוּ שִׁמְשׁוֹן חֲתַן הַתִּמְנִי כִּי לָקַח אֶת־אִשְׁתּוֹ וַיִּתְּנָהּ לְמֵרֵעֵהוּ וַיַּעֲלוּ פְלִשְׁתִּים וַיִּשְׂרְפוּ אוֹתָהּ
	וְאֶת־אָבִיהָ בָּאֵשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The Philistines asked, “Who did this?” And they were told, “It was
	Samson, the son-in-law of the Timnite, who took Samson’s wife and
	gave her to his wedding companion.” Thereupon the Philistines came
	up and put her and her father to the fire.
}

\eJ{
	Samson said to them, “If that is how you act, I will not rest
	until I have taken revenge on you.”
}

\eJ{
	He gave them a sound and thorough thrashing. Then he went down and
	stayed in the cave of the rock of Etam.
}

\eJ{
	The Philistines came up, pitched camp in Judah and spread out over
	Lehi.
}

\eJ{
	The men of Judah asked, “Why have you come up against us?” They
	answered, “We have come to take Samson prisoner, and to do to him
	as he did to us.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּרְדוּ שְׁלֹשֶׁת אֲלָפִים אִישׁ מִיהוּדָה אֶל־סְעִיף סֶלַע עֵיטָם וַיֹּאמְרוּ לְשִׁמְשׁוֹן הֲלֹא יָדַעְתָּ כִּי־מֹשְׁלִים בָּנוּ פְּלִשְׁתִּים וּמַה־זֹּאת עָשִׂיתָ לָּנוּ
	וַיֹּאמֶר לָהֶם כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ לִי כֵּן עָשִׂיתִי לָהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Thereupon three thousand men of Judah went down to the cave of the
	rock of Etam, and they said to Samson, “You knew that the
	Philistines rule over us; why have you done this to us?” He
	replied, “As they did to me, so I did to them.”
}

\eJ{
	“We have come down,” they told him, “to take you prisoner and to
	hand you over to the Philistines.” “But swear to me,” said Samson
	to them, “that you yourselves will not attack me.”
}

\eJ{
	“We won’t,” they replied. “We will only take you prisoner and hand
	you over to them; we will not slay you.” So they bound him with
	two new ropes and brought him up from the rock.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	הוּא־בָא עַד־לֶחִי וּפְלִשְׁתִּים הֵרִיעוּ לִקְרָאתוֹ וַתִּצְלַח עָלָיו רוּחַ יְהֹוָה וַתִּהְיֶינָה הָעֲבֹתִים אֲשֶׁר עַל־זְרוֹעוֹתָיו כַּפִּשְׁתִּים אֲשֶׁר בָּעֲרוּ בָאֵשׁ
	וַיִּמַּסּוּ אֱסוּרָיו מֵעַל יָדָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When he reached Lehi, the Philistines came shouting to meet him.
	Thereupon the spirit of YHWH gripped him, and the ropes on his
	arms became like flax that catches fire; the bonds melted off his
	hands.
}

\eJ{
	He came upon a fresh jawbone of an ass and he picked it up; and
	with it he killed a thousand men.
}

\eJ{
	Then Samson said: “With the jaw of an ass, Mass upon mass! With
	the jaw of an ass I have slain a thousand men.”
}

\eJ{
	As he finished speaking, he threw the jawbone away; hence that
	place was called Ramath-lehi.
}

\eJ{
	He was very thirsty and he called to YHWH, “You Yourself have
	granted this great victory through Your servant; and must I now
	die of thirst and fall into the hands of the uncircumcised?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּבְקַע אֱלֹהִים אֶת־הַמַּכְתֵּשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בַּלֶּחִי וַיֵּצְאוּ מִמֶּנּוּ מַיִם וַיֵּשְׁתְּ וַתָּשׁב רוּחוֹ וַיֶּחִי עַל־כֵּן קָרָא שְׁמָהּ עֵין הַקּוֹרֵא אֲשֶׁר בַּלֶּחִי עַד
	הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So God split open the hollow which is at Lehi, and the water
	gushed out of it; he drank, regained his strength, and revived.
	That is why it is called to this day “En-hakkore of Lehi.”
}

\eX{
	He led Israel in the days of the Philistines for twenty years.
}

\eJ{
	Once Samson went to Gaza; there he met a whore and slept with her.
}

\eJ{
	The Gazites [learned] that Samson had come there, so they gathered
	and lay in ambush for him in the town gate the whole night; and
	all night long they kept whispering to each other, “When daylight
	comes, we’ll kill him.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁכַּב שִׁמְשׁוֹן עַד־חֲצִי הַלַּיְלָה וַיָּקם בַּחֲצִי הַלַּיְלָה וַיֶּאֱחֹז בְּדַלְתוֹת שַׁעַר־הָעִיר וּבִשְׁתֵּי הַמְּזֻזוֹת וַיִּסָּעֵם עִם־הַבְּרִיחַ וַיָּשֶׂם
	עַל־כְּתֵפָיו וַיַּעֲלֵם אֶל־רֹאשׁ הָהָר אֲשֶׁר עַל־פְּנֵי חֶבְרוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	After that, he fell in love with a woman in the Wadi Sorek, named
	Delilah.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲלוּ אֵלֶיהָ סַרְנֵי פְלִשְׁתִּים וַיֹּאמְרוּ לָהּ פַּתִּי אוֹתוֹ וּרְאִי בַּמֶּה כֹּחוֹ גָדוֹל וּבַמֶּה נוּכַל לוֹ וַאֲסַרְנוּהוּ לְעַנּוֹתוֹ וַאֲנַחְנוּ נִתַּן־לָךְ אִישׁ
	אֶלֶף וּמֵאָה כָּסֶף׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The lords of the Philistines went up to her and said, “Coax him
	and find out what makes him so strong, and how we can overpower
	him, tie him up, and make him helpless; and we’ll each give you
	eleven hundred shekels of silver.”
}

\eJ{
	So Delilah said to Samson, “Tell me, what makes you so strong? And
	how could you be tied up and made helpless?”
}

\eJ{
	Samson replied, “If I were to be tied with seven fresh tendons
	that had not been dried, I should become as weak as an ordinary
	man.”
}

\eJ{
	So the lords of the Philistines brought up to her seven fresh
	tendons that had not been dried. She bound him with them,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהָאֹרֵב יֹשֵׁב לָהּ בַּחֶדֶר וַתֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו פְּלִשְׁתִּים עָלֶיךָ שִׁמְשׁוֹן וַיְנַתֵּק אֶת־הַיְתָרִים כַּאֲשֶׁר יִנָּתֵק פְּתִיל־הַנְּעֹרֶת בַּהֲרִיחוֹ אֵשׁ וְלֹא נוֹדַע
	כֹּחוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	while an ambush was waiting in her room. Then she called out to
	him, “Samson, the Philistines are upon you!” Whereat he pulled the
	tendons apart, as a strand of tow comes apart at the touch of
	fire. So the secret of his strength remained unknown.
}

\eJ{
	Then Delilah said to Samson, “Oh, you deceived me; you lied to me!
	Do tell me now how you could be tied up.”
}

\eJ{
	He said, “If I were to be bound with new ropes that had never been
	used, I would become as weak as an ordinary man.”
}

\eJ{
	So Delilah took new ropes and bound him with them, while an ambush
	was waiting in a room. And she cried, “Samson, the Philistines are
	upon you!” But he tore them off his arms like a thread.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתֹּאמֶר דְּלִילָה אֶל־שִׁמְשׁוֹן עַד־הֵנָּה הֵתַלְתָּ בִּי וַתְּדַבֵּר אֵלַי כְּזָבִים הַגִּידָה לִּי בַּמֶּה תֵּאָסֵר וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלֶיהָ אִם־תַּאַרְגִי אֶת־שֶׁבַע מַחְלְפוֹת
	רֹאשִׁי עִם־הַמַּסָּכֶת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then Delilah said to Samson, “You have been deceiving me all
	along; you have been lying to me! Tell me, how could you be tied
	up?” He answered her, “If you weave seven locks of my head into
	the web.”
}

\eJ{
	And she pinned it with a peg and cried to him, “Samson, the
	Philistines are upon you!” Awaking from his sleep, he pulled out
	the peg, the loom, and the web.
}

\eJ{
	Then she said to him, “How can you say you love me, when you don’t
	confide in me? This makes three times that you’ve deceived me and
	haven’t told me what makes you so strong.”
}

\eJ{
	Finally, after she had nagged him and pressed him constantly, he
	was wearied to death
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּגֶּד־לָהּ אֶת־כּל־לִבּוֹ וַיֹּאמֶר לָהּ מוֹרָה לֹא־עָלָה עַל־רֹאשִׁי כִּי־נְזִיר אֱלֹהִים אֲנִי מִבֶּטֶן אִמִּי אִם־גֻּלַּחְתִּי וְסָר מִמֶּנִּי כֹחִי וְחָלִיתִי וְהָיִיתִי
	כְּכל־הָאָדָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	and he confided everything to her. He said to her, “No razor has
	ever touched my head, for I have been a nazirite to God since I
	was in my mother’s womb. If my hair were cut, my strength would
	leave me and I should become as weak as an ordinary man.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתֵּרֶא דְלִילָה כִּי־הִגִּיד לָהּ אֶת־כּל־לִבּוֹ וַתִּשְׁלַח וַתִּקְרָא לְסַרְנֵי פְלִשְׁתִּים לֵאמֹר עֲלוּ הַפַּעַם כִּי־הִגִּיד (לה) [לִי] אֶת־כּל־לִבּוֹ וְעָלוּ אֵלֶיהָ
	סַרְנֵי פְלִשְׁתִּים וַיַּעֲלוּ הַכֶּסֶף בְּיָדָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Sensing that he had confided everything to her, Delilah sent for
	the lords of the Philistines, with this message: “Come up once
	more, for he has confided everything to me.” And the lords of the
	Philistines came up and brought the money with them.
}

\eJ{
	She lulled him to sleep on her lap. Then she called in a man, and
	she had him cut off the seven locks of his head; thus she weakened
	him and made him helpless: his strength slipped away from him.
}

\eJ{
	She cried, “Samson, the Philistines are upon you!” And he awoke
	from his sleep, thinking he would break loose and shake himself
	free as he had the other times. For he did not know that YHWH had
	departed from him.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 16:20. He did not know that YHWH had turned away from him. The sentence
%		is quieter than the haircut, and worse.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The Philistines seized him and gouged out his eyes. They brought
	him down to Gaza and shackled him in bronze fetters, and he became
	a mill slave in the prison.
}

\eJ{
	Now the lords of the Philistines gathered to offer a great
	sacrifice to their god Dagon and to make merry. They chanted, “Our
	god has delivered into our hands Our enemy Samson.”
}

\eJ{
	When the people saw him, they sang praises to their god, chanting,
	“Our god has delivered into our hands The enemy who devastated our
	land, And who slew so many of us.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי (כי טוב) [כְּטוֹב] לִבָּם וַיֹּאמְרוּ קִרְאוּ לְשִׁמְשׁוֹן וִישַׂחֶק־לָנוּ וַיִּקְרְאוּ לְשִׁמְשׁוֹן מִבֵּית (האסירים) [הָאֲסוּרִים] וַיְצַחֵק לִפְנֵיהֶם וַיַּעֲמִידוּ
	אוֹתוֹ בֵּין הָעַמּוּדִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	As their spirits rose, they said, “Call Samson here and let him
	dance for us.” Samson was fetched from the prison, and he danced
	for them. Then they put him between the pillars.
}

\eJ{
	And Samson said to the boy who was leading him by the hand, “Let
	go of me and let me feel the pillars that the temple rests upon,
	that I may lean on them.”
}

\eJ{
	Now the temple was full of men and women; all the lords of the
	Philistines were there, and there were some three thousand men and
	women on the roof watching Samson dance.
}

\eJ{
	Then Samson called to YHWH, “O Lord GOD! Please remember me, and
	give me strength just this once, O God, to take revenge of the
	Philistines, if only for one of my two eyes.”
}

\eJ{
	He embraced the two middle pillars that the temple rested upon,
	one with his right arm and one with his left, and leaned against
	them;
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר שִׁמְשׁוֹן תָּמוֹת נַפְשִׁי עִם־פְּלִשְׁתִּים וַיֵּט בְּכֹחַ וַיִּפֹּל הַבַּיִת עַל־הַסְּרָנִים וְעַל־כּל־הָעָם אֲשֶׁר־בּוֹ וַיִּהְיוּ הַמֵּתִים אֲשֶׁר הֵמִית בְּמוֹתוֹ
	רַבִּים מֵאֲשֶׁר הֵמִית בְּחַיָּיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Samson cried, “Let me die with the Philistines!” and he pulled
	with all his might. The temple came crashing down on the lords and
	on all the people in it. Those who were slain by him as he died
	outnumbered those who had been slain by him when he lived.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּרְדוּ אֶחָיו וְכל־בֵּית אָבִיהוּ וַיִּשְׂאוּ אֹתוֹ וַיַּעֲלוּ וַיִּקְבְּרוּ אוֹתוֹ בֵּין צרְעָה וּבֵין אֶשְׁתָּאֹל בְּקֶבֶר מָנוֹחַ אָבִיו וְהוּא שָׁפַט אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל
	עֶשְׂרִים שָׁנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	His brothers and all his father’s household came down and carried
	him up and buried him in the tomb of his father Manoah, between
	Zorah and Eshtaol. He had led Israel for twenty years.
}

\eJ{
	There was a man in the hill country of Ephraim whose name was
	Micah.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לְאִמּוֹ אֶלֶף וּמֵאָה הַכֶּסֶף אֲשֶׁר לֻקַּח־לָךְ (ואתי) [וְאַתְּ] אָלִית וְגַם אָמַרְתְּ בְּאזְנַי הִנֵּה־הַכֶּסֶף אִתִּי אֲנִי לְקַחְתִּיו וַתֹּאמֶר אִמּוֹ בָּרוּךְ
	בְּנִי לַיהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He said to his mother, “The eleven hundred shekels of silver that
	were taken from you, so that you uttered an imprecation which you
	repeated in my hearing—I have that silver; I took it.” “Blessed of
	YHWH be my son,” said his mother.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּשֶׁב אֶת־אֶלֶף־וּמֵאָה הַכֶּסֶף לְאִמּוֹ וַתֹּאמֶר אִמּוֹ הַקְדֵּשׁ הִקְדַּשְׁתִּי אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף לַיהֹוָה מִיָּדִי לִבְנִי לַעֲשׂוֹת פֶּסֶל וּמַסֵּכָה וְעַתָּה אֲשִׁיבֶנּוּ
	לָךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He returned the eleven hundred shekels of silver to his mother;
	but his mother said, “I herewith consecrate the silver to YHWH,
	transferring it to my son to make a sculptured image and a molten
	image. I now return it to you.”
}

\eJ{
	So when he gave the silver back to his mother, his mother took two
	hundred shekels of silver and gave it to a smith. He made of it a
	sculptured image and a molten image, which were kept in the house
	of Micah.
}

\eJ{
	Now the man Micah had a house of God; he had made an ephod and
	teraphim and he had inducted one of his sons to be his priest.
}

\eJ{
	In those days there was no king in Israel; every man did as he
	pleased.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 17:6. No king in Israel is not a neutral date-stamp. It is the book's
%		refrain for moral weather.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	There was a young man from Bethlehem of Judah, from the clan seat
	of Judah; he was a Levite and had resided there as a sojourner.
}

\eJ{
	This man had left the town of Bethlehem of Judah to take up
	residence wherever he could find a place. On his way, he came to
	the house of Micah in the hill country of Ephraim.
}

\eJ{
	“Where do you come from?” Micah asked him. He replied, “I am a
	Levite from Bethlehem of Judah, and I am traveling to take up
	residence wherever I can find a place.”
}

\eJ{
	“Stay with me,” Micah said to him, “and be a father and a priest
	to me, and I will pay you ten shekels of silver a year, an
	allowance of clothing, and your food.” The Levite went.
}

\eJ{
	The Levite agreed to stay with the man, and the youth became like
	one of his own sons.
}

\eJ{
	Micah inducted the Levite, and the young man became his priest and
	remained in Micah’s shrine.
}

\eJ{
	“Now I know,” Micah told himself, “that YHWH will prosper me,
	since the Levite has become my priest.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	בַּיָּמִים הָהֵם אֵין מֶלֶךְ בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל וּבַיָּמִים הָהֵם שֵׁבֶט הַדָּנִי מְבַקֶּשׁ־לוֹ נַחֲלָה לָשֶׁבֶת כִּי לֹא־נָפְלָה לּוֹ עַד־הַיּוֹם הַהוּא בְּתוֹךְ־שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל
	בְּנַחֲלָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	In those days there was no king in Israel, and in those days the
	tribe of Dan was seeking a territory in which to settle; for to
	that day no territory had fallen to their lot among the tribes of
	Israel.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלְחוּ בְנֵי־דָן מִמִּשְׁפַּחְתָּם חֲמִשָּׁה אֲנָשִׁים מִקְצוֹתָם אֲנָשִׁים בְּנֵי־חַיִל מִצּרְעָה וּמֵאֶשְׁתָּאֹל לְרַגֵּל אֶת־הָאָרֶץ וּלְחקְרָהּ וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֲלֵהֶם לְכוּ
	חִקְרוּ אֶת־הָאָרֶץ וַיָּבֹאוּ הַר־אֶפְרַיִם עַד־בֵּית מִיכָה וַיָּלִינוּ שָׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	While in the vicinity of Micah’s house, they recognized the speech
	of the young Levite, so they went over and asked him, “Who brought
	you to these parts? What are you doing in this place? What is your
	business here?”
}

\eJ{
	He replied, “Thus and thus Micah did for me—he hired me and I
	became his priest.”
}

\eJ{
	They said to him, “Please, inquire of God; we would like to know
	if the mission on which we are going will be successful.”
}

\eJ{
	“Go in peace,” the priest said to them, “YHWH views with favor the
	mission you are going on.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּלְכוּ חֲמֵשֶׁת הָאֲנָשִׁים וַיָּבֹאוּ לָיְשָׁה וַיִּרְאוּ אֶת־הָעָם אֲשֶׁר־בְּקִרְבָּהּ יוֹשֶׁבֶת־לָבֶטַח כְּמִשְׁפַּט צִדֹנִים שֹׁקֵט וּבֹטֵחַ וְאֵין־מַכְלִים דָּבָר בָּאָרֶץ
	יוֹרֵשׁ עֶצֶר וּרְחוֹקִים הֵמָּה מִצִּידֹנִים וְדָבָר אֵין־לָהֶם עִם־אָדָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When [the men] came back to their kinsmen at Zorah and Eshtaol,
	their kinsmen asked them, “How did you fare?”
}

\eJ{
	They replied, “Let us go at once and attack them! For we found
	that the land was very good, and you are sitting idle! Don’t
	delay; go and invade the land and take possession of it,
}

\eJ{
	for God has delivered it into your hand. When you come, you will
	come to an unsuspecting people; and the land is spacious and
	nothing on earth is lacking there.”
}

\eJ{
	They departed from there, from the clan seat of the Danites, from
	Zorah and Eshtaol, six hundred strong, girt with weapons of war.
}

\eJ{
	They went up and encamped at Kiriath-jearim in Judah. That is why
	that place is called “the Camp of Dan” to this day; it lies west
	of Kiriath-jearim.
}

\eJ{
	From there they passed on to the hill country of Ephraim and
	arrived at the house of Micah.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲנוּ חֲמֵשֶׁת הָאֲנָשִׁים הַהֹלְכִים לְרַגֵּל אֶת־הָאָרֶץ לַיִשׁ וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֶל־אֲחֵיהֶם הַיְדַעְתֶּם כִּי יֵשׁ בַּבָּתִּים הָאֵלֶּה אֵפוֹד וּתְרָפִים וּפֶסֶל וּמַסֵּכָה
	וְעַתָּה דְּעוּ מַה־תַּעֲשׂוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Here the five men who had gone to spy out the Laish region
	remarked to their kinsmen, “Do you know, there is an ephod in
	these houses, and teraphim, and a sculptured image and a molten
	image? Now you know what you have to do.”
}

\eJ{
	So they turned off there and entered the home of the young Levite
	at Micah’s house and greeted him.
}

\eJ{
	The six hundred Danite men, girt with their weapons of war, stood
	at the entrance of the gate,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲלוּ חֲמֵשֶׁת הָאֲנָשִׁים הַהֹלְכִים לְרַגֵּל אֶת־הָאָרֶץ בָּאוּ שָׁמָּה לָקְחוּ אֶת־הַפֶּסֶל וְאֶת־הָאֵפוֹד וְאֶת־הַתְּרָפִים וְאֶת־הַמַּסֵּכָה וְהַכֹּהֵן נִצָּב פֶּתַח
	הַשַּׁעַר וְשֵׁשׁ־מֵאוֹת הָאִישׁ הֶחָגוּר כְּלֵי הַמִּלְחָמָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	while the five men who had gone to spy out the land went inside
	and took the sculptured image, the ephod, the teraphim, and the
	molten image. The priest was standing at the entrance of the gate,
	and the six hundred men girt with their weapons of war,
}

\eJ{
	while those men entered Micah’s house and took the sculptured
	image, the molten image, the ephod, and the household gods. The
	priest said to them, “What are you doing?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ לוֹ הַחֲרֵשׁ שִׂים־יָדְךָ עַל־פִּיךָ וְלֵךְ עִמָּנוּ וֶהְיֵה־לָנוּ לְאָב וּלְכֹהֵן הֲטוֹב הֱיוֹתְךָ כֹהֵן לְבֵית אִישׁ אֶחָד אוֹ הֱיוֹתְךָ כֹהֵן לְשֵׁבֶט
	וּלְמִשְׁפָּחָה בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But they said to him, “Be quiet; put your hand on your mouth! Come
	with us and be our father and priest. Would you rather be priest
	to one man’s household or be priest to a tribe and clan in
	Israel?”
}

\eJ{
	The priest was delighted. He took the ephod, the household gods,
	and the sculptured image, and he joined the people.
}

\eJ{
	They set out again, placing the children, the cattle, and their
	household goods in front.
}

\eJ{
	They had already gone some distance from Micah’s house, when the
	men in the houses near Micah’s mustered and caught up with the
	Danites.
}

\eJ{
	They called out to the Danites, who turned around and said to
	Micah, “What’s the matter? Why have you mustered?”
}

\eJ{
	He said, “You have taken my priest and the gods that I made, and
	walked off! What do I have left? How can you ask, ‘What’s the
	matter’?”
}

\eJ{
	But the Danites replied, “Don’t do any shouting at us, or some
	desperate men might attack you, and you and your family would lose
	your lives.”
}

\eJ{
	So Micah, realizing that they were stronger than he, turned back
	and went home; and the Danites went on their way,
}

\eJ{
	taking the things Micah had made and the priest he had acquired.
	They proceeded to Laish, a people tranquil and unsuspecting, and
	they put them to the sword and burned down the town.
}

\eJ{
	There was none to come to the rescue, for it was distant from
	Sidon and they had no dealings with anyone; it lay in the valley
	of Beth-rehob. They rebuilt the town and settled there,
}

\eJ{
	and they named the town Dan, after their ancestor Dan who was
	Israel’s son. Originally, however, the name of the town was Laish.
}

\eJ{
	The Danites set up the sculptured image for themselves; and
	Jonathan son of Gershom son of Manasseh, and his descendants,
	served as priests to the Danite tribe until the land went into
	exile.
}

\eJ{
	They maintained the sculptured image that Micah had made
	throughout the time that the House of God stood at Shiloh.
}

\eJ{
	In those days, when there was no king in Israel, a Levite residing
	at the other end of the hill country of Ephraim took to himself a
	concubine from Bethlehem in Judah.
}

\eJ{
	Once his concubine deserted him, leaving him for her father’s
	house in Bethlehem in Judah; and she stayed there a full four
	months.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּקם אִישָׁהּ וַיֵּלֶךְ אַחֲרֶיהָ לְדַבֵּר עַל־לִבָּהּ (להשיבו) [לַהֲשִׁיבָהּ] וְנַעֲרוֹ עִמּוֹ וְצֶמֶד חֲמֹרִים וַתְּבִיאֵהוּ בֵּית אָבִיהָ וַיִּרְאֵהוּ אֲבִי הַנַּעֲרָה
	וַיִּשְׂמַח לִקְרָאתוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then her husband set out, with an attendant and a pair of donkeys,
	and went after her to woo her and to win her back. She admitted
	him into her father’s house; and when the girl’s father saw him,
	he received him warmly.
}

\eJ{
	His father-in-law, the girl’s father, pressed him, and he stayed
	with him three days; they ate and drank and lodged there.
}

\eJ{
	Early in the morning of the fourth day, he started to leave; but
	the girl’s father said to his son-in-law, “Eat something to give
	you strength, then you can leave.”
}

\eJ{
	So the two of them sat down and they feasted together. Then the
	girl’s father said to the man, “Won’t you stay overnight and enjoy
	yourself?”
}

\eJ{
	The man started to leave, but his father-in-law kept urging him
	until he turned back and spent the night there.
}

\eJ{
	Early in the morning of the fifth day, he was about to leave, when
	the girl’s father said, “Come, have a bite.” The two of them ate,
	dawdling until past noon.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּקם הָאִישׁ לָלֶכֶת הוּא וּפִילַגְשׁוֹ וְנַעֲרוֹ וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ חֹתְנוֹ אֲבִי הַנַּעֲרָה הִנֵּה נָא רָפָה הַיּוֹם לַעֲרוֹב לִינוּ־נָא הִנֵּה חֲנוֹת הַיּוֹם לִין פֹּה וְיִיטַב
	לְבָבֶךָ וְהִשְׁכַּמְתֶּם מָחָר לְדַרְכְּכֶם וְהָלַכְתָּ לְאֹהָלֶךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But the man refused to stay for the night. He set out and traveled
	as far as the vicinity of Jebus—that is, Jerusalem; he had with
	him a pair of laden donkeys, and his concubine was with him.
}

\eJ{
	Since they were close to Jebus, and the day was very far spent,
	the attendant said to his master, “Let us turn aside to this town
	of the Jebusites and spend the night in it.”
}

\eJ{
	But his master said to him, “We will not turn aside to a town of
	aliens who are not of Israel, but will continue to Gibeah.
}

\eJ{
	Come,” he said to his attendant, “let us approach one of those
	places and spend the night either in Gibeah or in Ramah.”
}

\eJ{
	So they traveled on, and the sun set when they were near Gibeah of
	Benjamin.
}

\eJ{
	They turned off there and went in to spend the night in Gibeah. He
	went and sat down in the town square, but nobody took them indoors
	to spend the night.
}

\eJ{
	In the evening, an old man came along from his property outside
	the town. (This man hailed from the hill country of Ephraim and
	resided at Gibeah, where the townspeople were Benjaminites.)
}

\eJ{
	He happened to see the wayfarer in the town square. “Where,” the
	old man inquired, “are you going to, and where do you come from?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו עֹבְרִים אֲנַחְנוּ מִבֵּית־לֶחֶם יְהוּדָה עַד־יַרְכְּתֵי הַר־אֶפְרַיִם מִשָּׁם אָנֹכִי וָאֵלֵךְ עַד־בֵּית לֶחֶם יְהוּדָה וְאֶת־בֵּית יְהֹוָה אֲנִי הֹלֵךְ וְאֵין
	אִישׁ מְאַסֵּף אוֹתִי הַבָּיְתָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He replied, “We are traveling from Bethlehem in Judah to the other
	end of the hill country of Ephraim. That is where I live. I made a
	journey to Bethlehem of Judah, and now I am on my way to the House
	of YHWH, and nobody has taken me indoors.
}

\eJ{
	We have both bruised straw and feed for our donkeys, and bread and
	wine for me and your handmaid, and for the attendant with your
	servants. We lack nothing.”
}

\eJ{
	“Rest easy,” said the old man. “Let me take care of all your
	needs. Do not on any account spend the night in the square.”
}

\eJ{
	And he took him into his house. He mixed fodder for the donkeys;
	then they bathed their feet and ate and drank.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	הֵמָּה מֵיטִיבִים אֶת־לִבָּם וְהִנֵּה אַנְשֵׁי הָעִיר אַנְשֵׁי בְנֵי־בְלִיַּעַל נָסַבּוּ אֶת־הַבַּיִת מִתְדַּפְּקִים עַל־הַדָּלֶת וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֶל־הָאִישׁ בַּעַל הַבַּיִת הַזָּקֵן
	לֵאמֹר הוֹצֵא אֶת־הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּא אֶל־בֵּיתְךָ וְנֵדָעֶנּוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The owner of the house went out and said to them, “Please, my
	friends, do not commit such a wrong. Since this man has entered my
	house, do not perpetrate this outrage.
}

\eJ{
	Look, here is my virgin daughter, and his concubine. Let me bring
	them out to you. Have your pleasure of them, do what you like with
	them; but don’t do that outrageous thing to this man.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְלֹא־אָבוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים לִשְׁמֹעַ לוֹ וַיַּחֲזֵק הָאִישׁ בְּפִילַגְשׁוֹ וַיֹּצֵא אֲלֵיהֶם הַחוּץ וַיֵּדְעוּ אוֹתָהּ וַיִּתְעַלְּלוּ־בָהּ כּל־הַלַּיְלָה עַד־הַבֹּקֶר וַיְשַׁלְּחוּהָ
	(בעלות) [כַּעֲלוֹת] הַשָּׁחַר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But the men would not listen to him, so the man seized his
	concubine and pushed her out to them. They raped her and abused
	her all night long until morning; and they let her go when dawn
	broke.
}

\eJ{
	Toward morning the woman came back; and as it was growing light,
	she collapsed at the entrance of the man’s house where her husband
	was.
}

\eJ{
	When her husband arose in the morning, he opened the doors of the
	house and went out to continue his journey; and there was the
	woman, his concubine, lying at the entrance of the house, with her
	hands on the threshold.
}

\eJ{
	“Get up,” he said to her, “let us go.” But there was no reply. So
	the man placed her on the donkey and set out for home.
}

\eJ{
	When he came home, he picked up a knife, and took hold of his
	concubine and cut her up limb by limb into twelve parts. He sent
	them throughout the territory of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	And everyone who saw it cried out, “Never has such a thing
	happened or been seen from the day the Israelites came out of the
	land of Egypt to this day! Put your mind to this; take counsel and
	decide.”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 19:30. The body is cut into twelve pieces and sent through Israel. The
%		text has stopped persuading and has begun mailing evidence.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Thereupon all the Israelites—from Dan to Beer-sheba and [from] the
	land of Gilead—marched forth, and the community assembled to a man
	before YHWH at Mizpah.
}

\eJ{
	All the leaders of the people [and] all the tribes of Israel
	presented themselves in the assembly of God’s people, 400,000
	fighting men on foot.—
}

\eJ{
	The Benjaminites heard that the Israelites had come up to
	Mizpah.—The Israelites said, “Tell us, how did this evil thing
	happen?”
}

\eJ{
	And the Levite, the husband of the murdered woman, replied, “My
	concubine and I came to Gibeah of Benjamin to spend the night.
}

\eJ{
	The citizens of Gibeah set out to harm me. They gathered against
	me around the house in the night; they meant to kill me, and they
	ravished my concubine until she died.
}

\eJ{
	So I took hold of my concubine and I cut her in pieces and sent
	them through every part of Israel’s territory. For an outrageous
	act of depravity had been committed in Israel.
}

\eJ{
	Now you are all Israelites; produce a plan of action here and
	now!”
}

\eJ{
	Then all the people rose, as one man, and declared, “We will not
	go back to our homes, we will not enter our houses!
}

\eJ{
	But this is what we will do to Gibeah: [we will wage war] against
	it according to lot.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְלָקַחְנוּ עֲשָׂרָה אֲנָשִׁים לַמֵּאָה לְכֹל שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וּמֵאָה לָאֶלֶף וְאֶלֶף לָרְבָבָה לָקַחַת צֵדָה לָעָם לַעֲשׂוֹת לְבוֹאָם לְגֶבַע בִּנְיָמִן כְּכל־הַנְּבָלָה
	אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	We will take from all the tribes of Israel ten men to the hundred,
	a hundred to the thousand, and a thousand to the ten thousand to
	supply provisions for the troops—to prepare for their going to
	Geba in Benjamin for all the outrage it has committed in Israel.”
}

\eJ{
	So all the men of Israel, united as one man, massed against the
	town.
}

\eJ{
	And the tribes of Israel sent men through the whole tribe of
	Benjamin, saying, “What is this evil thing that has happened among
	you?
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְעַתָּה תְּנוּ אֶת־הָאֲנָשִׁים בְּנֵי־בְלִיַּעַל אֲשֶׁר בַּגִּבְעָה וּנְמִיתֵם וּנְבַעֲרָה רָעָה מִיִּשְׂרָאֵל וְלֹא אָבוּ [בְּנֵי] בִנְיָמִן לִשְׁמֹעַ בְּקוֹל אֲחֵיהֶם
	בְּנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Come, hand over those scoundrels in Gibeah so that we may put them
	to death and stamp out the evil from Israel.” But the Benjaminites
	would not yield to the demand of their fellow Israelites.
}

\eJ{
	So the Benjaminites gathered from their towns to Gibeah in order
	to take the field against the Israelites.
}

\eJ{
	On that day the Benjaminites mustered from the towns 26,000
	fighting men, mustered apart from the inhabitants of Gibeah; 700
	picked men
}

\eJ{
	of all this force—700 picked men—were left-handed. Every one of
	them could sling a stone at a hair and not miss.
}

\eJ{
	The men of Israel other than Benjamin mustered 400,000 fighting
	men, warriors to a man.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּקֻמוּ וַיַּעֲלוּ בֵית־אֵל וַיִּשְׁאֲלוּ בֵאלֹהִים וַיֹּאמְרוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִי יַעֲלֶה־לָּנוּ בַתְּחִלָּה לַמִּלְחָמָה עִם־בְּנֵי בִנְיָמִן וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה יְהוּדָה
	בַתְּחִלָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	They proceeded to Bethel and inquired of God; the Israelites
	asked, “Who of us shall advance first to fight the Benjaminites?”
	And YHWH replied, “Judah first.”
}

\eJ{
	So the Israelites arose in the morning and encamped against
	Gibeah.
}

\eJ{
	The men of Israel took the field against the Benjaminites; the men
	of Israel drew up in battle order against them at Gibeah.
}

\eJ{
	But the Benjaminites issued from Gibeah, and that day they struck
	down 22,000 men of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	Now the army—the men of Israel—rallied and again drew up in battle
	order at the same place as they had on the first day.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲלוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיִּבְכּוּ לִפְנֵי־יְהֹוָה עַד־הָעֶרֶב וַיִּשְׁאֲלוּ בַיהֹוָה לֵאמֹר הַאוֹסִיף לָגֶשֶׁת לַמִּלְחָמָה עִם־בְּנֵי בִנְיָמִן אָחִי וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה עֲלוּ
	אֵלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	For the Israelites had gone up and wept before YHWH until evening.
	They had inquired of YHWH, “Shall we again join battle with our
	kinsmen the Benjaminites?” And YHWH had replied, “March against
	them.”
}

\eJ{
	The Israelites advanced against the Benjaminites on the second
	day.
}

\eJ{
	But the Benjaminites came out from Gibeah against them on the
	second day and struck down 18,000 more of the Israelites, all of
	them fighting men.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲלוּ כל־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְכל־הָעָם וַיָּבֹאוּ בֵית־אֵל וַיִּבְכּוּ וַיֵּשְׁבוּ שָׁם לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה וַיָּצוּמוּ בַיּוֹם־הַהוּא עַד־הָעָרֶב וַיַּעֲלוּ עֹלוֹת וּשְׁלָמִים
	לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then all the Israelites, all the army, went up and came to Bethel
	and they sat there, weeping before YHWH. They fasted that day
	until evening, and presented burnt offerings and offerings of
	well-being to YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	The Israelites inquired of YHWH (for the Ark of God’s Covenant was
	there in those days,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וּפִינְחָס בֶּן־אֶלְעָזָר בֶּן־אַהֲרֹן עֹמֵד לְפָנָיו בַּיָּמִים הָהֵם לֵאמֹר הַאוֹסִף עוֹד לָצֵאת לַמִּלְחָמָה עִם־בְּנֵי־בִנְיָמִן אָחִי אִם־אֶחְדָּל וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה עֲלוּ כִּי
	מָחָר אֶתְּנֶנּוּ בְיָדֶךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	and Phinehas son of Eleazar son of Aaron the priest ministered
	before Him in those days), “Shall we again take the field against
	our kinsmen the Benjaminites, or shall we not?” YHWH answered, “Go
	up, for tomorrow I will deliver them into your hands.”
}

\eJ{
	And on the third day, the Israelites went up against the
	Benjaminites, as before, and engaged them in battle at Gibeah.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּצְאוּ בְנֵי־בִנְיָמִן לִקְרַאת הָעָם הנְתְּקוּ מִן־הָעִיר וַיָּחֵלּוּ לְהַכּוֹת מֵהָעָם חֲלָלִים כְּפַעַם בְּפַעַם בַּמְסִלּוֹת אֲשֶׁר אַחַת עֹלָה בֵית־אֵל וְאַחַת גִּבְעָתָה
	בַּשָּׂדֶה כִּשְׁלֹשִׁים אִישׁ בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The Benjaminites dashed out to meet the army and were drawn away
	from the town onto the roads, of which one runs to Bethel and the
	other to Gibeah. As before, they started out by striking some of
	the men dead in the open field, about 30 men of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	The Benjaminites thought, “They are being routed before us as
	previously.” But the Israelites had planned: “We will take to
	flight and draw them away from the town to the roads.”
}

\eJ{
	And while the main body of the Israelites had moved away from
	their positions and had drawn up in battle order at Baal-tamar,
	the Israelite ambush was rushing out from its position at
	Maareh-geba.
}

\eJ{
	Thus 10,000 picked men of all Israel came to a point south of
	Gibeah, and the battle was furious. Before they realized that
	disaster was approaching,
}

\eJ{
	YHWH routed the Benjaminites before Israel. That day the
	Israelites slew 25,100 men of Benjamin, all of them fighting men.
}

\eJ{
	Then the Benjaminites realized that they were routed. Now the
	Israelites had yielded ground to the Benjaminites, for they relied
	on the ambush which they had laid against Gibeah.
}

\eJ{
	One ambush quickly deployed against Gibeah, and the other ambush
	advanced and put the whole town to the sword.
}

\eJ{
	A time had been agreed upon by the Israelite men with those in
	ambush: When a huge column of smoke was sent up from the town,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּהֲפֹךְ אִישׁ־יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּמִּלְחָמָה וּבִנְיָמִן הֵחֵל לְהַכּוֹת חֲלָלִים בְּאִישׁ־יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּשְׁלֹשִׁים אִישׁ כִּי אָמְרוּ אַךְ נִגּוֹף נִגָּף הוּא לְפָנֵינוּ
	כַּמִּלְחָמָה הָרִאשֹׁנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	the Israelite men were to turn about in battle. Benjamin had begun
	by striking dead about 30 Israelite men, and they thought, “They
	are being routed before us as in the previous fighting.”
}

\eJ{
	But when the column, the pillar of smoke, began to rise from the
	city, the Benjaminites looked behind them, and there was the whole
	town going up in smoke to the sky!
}

\eJ{
	And now the Israelites turned about, and the men of Benjamin were
	thrown into panic, for they realized that disaster had overtaken
	them.
}

\eJ{
	They retreated before the men of Israel along the road to the
	wilderness, where the fighting caught up with them; meanwhile
	those from the towns were massacring them in it.
}

\eJ{
	They encircled the Benjaminites, pursued them, and trod them down
	[from] Menuhah to a point opposite Gibeah on the east.
}

\eJ{
	That day 18,000 men of Benjamin fell, all of them brave men.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּפְנוּ וַיָּנֻסוּ הַמִּדְבָּרָה אֶל־סֶלַע הָרִמּוֹן וַיְעֹלְלֻהוּ בַּמְסִלּוֹת חֲמֵשֶׁת אֲלָפִים אִישׁ וַיַּדְבִּיקוּ אַחֲרָיו עַד־גִּדְעֹם וַיַּכּוּ מִמֶּנּוּ אַלְפַּיִם
	אִישׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	They turned and fled to the wilderness, to the Rock of Rimmon; but
	[the Israelites] picked off another 5,000 on the roads and,
	continuing in hot pursuit of them up to Gidom, they slew 2,000
	more.
}

\eJ{
	Thus the total number of Benjaminites who fell that day came to
	25,000 fighting men, all of them brave.
}

\eJ{
	But 600 men turned and fled to the wilderness, to the Rock of
	Rimmon; they remained at the Rock of Rimmon four months.
}

\eJ{
	The men of Israel, meanwhile, turned back to the rest of the
	Benjaminites and put them to the sword—towns, people,
	cattle—everything that remained. Finally, they set fire to all the
	towns that were left.
}

\eJ{
	Now the men of Israel had taken an oath at Mizpah: “None of us
	will give his daughter in marriage to a Benjaminite.”
}

\eJ{
	The people came to Bethel and sat there before God until evening.
	They wailed and wept bitterly,
}

\eJ{
	and they said, “O LORD God of Israel, why has this happened in
	Israel, that one tribe must now be missing from Israel?”
}

\eJ{
	Early the next day, the people built an altar there, and they
	brought burnt offerings and offerings of well-being.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִי אֲשֶׁר לֹא־עָלָה בַקָּהָל מִכּל־שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל־יְהֹוָה כִּי הַשְּׁבוּעָה הַגְּדוֹלָה הָיְתָה לַאֲשֶׁר לֹא־עָלָה אֶל־יְהֹוָה הַמִּצְפָּה
	לֵאמֹר מוֹת יוּמָת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The Israelites asked, “Is there anyone from all the tribes of
	Israel who failed to come up to the assembly before YHWH?” For a
	solemn oath had been taken concerning anyone who did not go up to
	YHWH at Mizpah: “He shall be put to death.”
}

\eJ{
	The Israelites now relented toward their kinsmen the Benjaminites,
	and they said, “This day one tribe has been cut off from Israel!
}

\eJ{
	What can we do to provide wives for those who are left, seeing
	that we have sworn by YHWH not to give any of our daughters to
	them in marriage?”
}

\eJ{
	They inquired, “Is there anyone from the tribes of Israel who did
	not go up to YHWH at Mizpah?” Now no one from Jabesh-gilead had
	come to the camp, to the assembly.
}

\eJ{
	For, when the roll of the troops was taken, not one of the
	inhabitants of Jabesh-gilead was present.
}

\eJ{
	So the assemblage dispatched 12,000 of the warriors, instructing
	them as follows: “Go and put the inhabitants of Jabesh-gilead to
	the sword, women and children included.
}

\eJ{
	This is what you are to do: Proscribe every man, and every woman
	who has known a man carnally.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּמְצְאוּ מִיּוֹשְׁבֵי יָבֵישׁ גִּלְעָד אַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת נַעֲרָה בְתוּלָה אֲשֶׁר לֹא־יָדְעָה אִישׁ לְמִשְׁכַּב זָכָר וַיָּבִאוּ אוֹתָם אֶל־הַמַּחֲנֶה שִׁלֹה אֲשֶׁר בְּאֶרֶץ
	כְּנָעַן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	They found among the inhabitants of Jabesh-gilead 400 maidens who
	had not known a man carnally; and they brought them to the camp at
	Shiloh, which is in the land of Canaan.
}

\eJ{
	Then the whole community sent word to the Benjaminites who were at
	the Rock of Rimmon, and offered them terms of peace.
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon the Benjaminites returned, and they gave them the girls
	who had been spared from the women of Jabesh-gilead. But there
	were not enough of them.
}

\eJ{
	Now the people had relented toward Benjamin, for YHWH had made a
	breach in the tribes of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	So the elders of the community asked, “What can we do about wives
	for those who are left, since the women of Benjamin have been
	killed off?”
}

\eJ{
	For they said, “There must be a saving remnant for Benjamin, that
	a tribe may not be blotted out of Israel;
}

\eJ{
	yet we cannot give them any of our daughters as wives,” since the
	Israelites had taken an oath: “Cursed be anyone who gives a wife
	to Benjamin!”
}

\eJ{
	They said, “The annual feast of YHWH is now being held at Shiloh.”
	(It lies north of Bethel, east of the highway that runs from
	Bethel to Shechem, and south of Lebonah.)
}

\eJ{
	So they instructed the Benjaminites as follows: “Go and lie in
	wait in the vineyards.
}

\eJ{
	As soon as you see the girls of Shiloh coming out to join in the
	dances, come out from the vineyards; let each of you seize a wife
	from among the girls of Shiloh, and be off for the land of
	Benjamin.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהָיָה כִּי־יָבֹאוּ אֲבוֹתָם אוֹ אֲחֵיהֶם (לרוב) [לָרִיב ] אֵלֵינוּ וְאָמַרְנוּ אֲלֵיהֶם חנּוּנוּ אוֹתָם כִּי לֹא לָקַחְנוּ אִישׁ אִשְׁתּוֹ בַּמִּלְחָמָה כִּי לֹא אַתֶּם נְתַתֶּם
	לָהֶם כָּעֵת תֶּאְשָׁמוּ׃
}

\hJ{
	וַיַּעֲשׂוּ־כֵן בְּנֵי בִנְיָמִן וַיִּשְׂאוּ נָשִׁים לְמִסְפָּרָם מִן־הַמְּחֹלְלוֹת אֲשֶׁר גָּזָלוּ וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּשׁוּבוּ אֶל־נַחֲלָתָם וַיִּבְנוּ אֶת־הֶעָרִים וַיֵּשְׁבוּ
	בָּהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The Benjaminites did so. They took to wife, from the dancers whom
	they carried off, as many as they themselves numbered. Then they
	went back to their own territory, and rebuilt their towns and
	settled in them.
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon the Israelites dispersed, each to his own tribe and
	clan; everyone departed for his own territory.
}

\eJ{
	In those days there was no king in Israel; everyone did as he
	pleased.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 21:25. The book ends with everyone doing what is straight in his eyes.
%		That is not freedom. It is the last line of the autopsy.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	There was a man from Ramathaim of the Zuphites, in the hill
	country of Ephraim, whose name was Elkanah son of Jeroham son of
	Elihu son of Tohu son of Zuph, an Ephraimite.
}

tex.commentary:
\aA{
	Ok so there's this guy named Elkanah.
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He had two wives, one named Hannah and the other Peninnah;
	Peninnah had children, but Hannah was childless.
}

tex.commentary:
\aA{
	He's got two wives.

	Sort of a Rachel and Leah situation.
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	This man used to go up from his town every year to worship and to
	offer sacrifice to YHWH of Hosts at Shiloh.—Hophni and Phinehas,
	the two sons of Eli, were priests of YHWH there.
}

\eJ{
	One such day, Elkanah offered a sacrifice. He used to give
	portions to his wife Peninnah and to all her sons and daughters;
}

\eJ{
	but to Hannah he would give one portion only—though Hannah was his
	favorite—for YHWH had closed her womb.
}

tex.commentary:
\aA{
	But he's nicer to his favorite wifey.

	Bad idea Elkanah.
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Moreover, her rival, to make her miserable, would taunt her that
	YHWH had closed her womb.
}

tex.commentary:
\aA{
	And fertile wifey is mean to Hannah.

	Sort of a Sarah and Hagar situation, half reversed.
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	This happened year after year: Every time she went up to the House
	of YHWH, the other would taunt her, so that she wept and would not
	eat.
}

tex.commentary:
\aA{
	Mean wife is mean and Hannah's sad.
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Her husband Elkanah said to her, “Hannah, why are you crying and
	why aren’t you eating? Why are you so sad? Am I not more devoted
	to you than ten sons?”
}

tex.commentary:
\aA{
	So husband's like ``Hannah, aren't I better than ten
	sons?''

	And she's like no dumbass.
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
