\aA{
	Most mainstream archaeologists and historians estimate
	the population of Jerusalem was roughly 1,500 to 2,000
	people.
}

\aA{
	The estimated population of Israel overall during the
	Davidic period ranges drastically between 40,000 to
	300,000 people according to archaeological estimates,
	compared to several million people implied by biblical
	texts.
}

\aA{
	Taking the geometric mean of 40k and 300k, we get an
	estimate of about 100,000 people, halfway between the
	two estimates on a logarithmic scale.
}

\aA{
	Now, the shekel is a unit of weight, with the unit of
	currency being the amount of silver with that weight. A
	shekel is about 11-14 grams of silver.
}

\aA{
	A common back-of-the-envelope estimate is that an
	ordinary person in the Iron Age produced goods and
	services worth roughly 10–20 shekels per year (most of
	it consumed directly rather than sold).
