}

\eJ{
	When Uriah’s wife heard that her husband Uriah was dead, she
	lamented over her husband.
}

\eJ{
	After the period of mourning was over, David sent and had her
	brought into his palace; she became his wife and she bore him a
	son. But YHWH was displeased with what David had done,
}

\eJ{
	and YHWH sent Nathan to David. He came to him and said, “There
	were two men in the same city, one rich and one poor.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְלָרָשׁ אֵין־כֹּל כִּי אִם־כִּבְשָׂה אַחַת קְטַנָּה אֲשֶׁר קָנָה וַיְחַיֶּהָ וַתִּגְדַּל עִמּוֹ וְעִם־בָּנָיו יַחְדָּו מִפִּתּוֹ תֹאכַל וּמִכֹּסוֹ תִשְׁתֶּה וּבְחֵיקוֹ תִשְׁכָּב
	וַתְּהִי־לוֹ כְּבַת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	but the poor man had only one little ewe lamb that he had bought.
	He tended it and it grew up together with him and his children: it
	used to share his morsel of bread, drink from his cup, and nestle
	in his bosom; it was like a daughter to him.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹא הֵלֶךְ לְאִישׁ הֶעָשִׁיר וַיַּחְמֹל לָקַחַת מִצֹּאנוֹ וּמִבְּקָרוֹ לַעֲשׂוֹת לָאֹרֵחַ הַבָּא־לוֹ וַיִּקַּח אֶת־כִּבְשַׂת הָאִישׁ הָרָאשׁ וַיַּעֲשֶׂהָ לָאִישׁ הַבָּא
	אֵלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	One day, a traveler came to the rich man, but he was loath to take
	anything from his own flocks or herds to prepare a meal for the
	guest who had come to him; so he took the poor man’s lamb and
	prepared it for the man who had come to him.”
}

\eJ{
	David flew into a rage against the man, and said to Nathan, “As
	YHWH lives, the man who did this deserves to die!
}

\eJ{
	He shall pay for the lamb four times over, because he did such a
	thing and showed no pity.”
}

\eJ{
	And Nathan said to David, “That man is you! Thus said YHWH, the
	God of Israel: ‘It was I who anointed you king over Israel and it
	was I who rescued you from the hand of Saul.
}

\eJ{
	I gave you your master’s house and possession of your master’s
	wives; and I gave you the House of Israel and Judah; and if that
	were not enough, I would give you twice as much more.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	מַדּוּעַ בָּזִיתָ אֶת־דְּבַר יְהֹוָה לַעֲשׂוֹת הָרַע (בעינו) [בְּעֵינַי] אֵת אוּרִיָּה הַחִתִּי הִכִּיתָ בַחֶרֶב וְאֶת־אִשְׁתּוֹ לָקַחְתָּ לְּךָ לְאִשָּׁה וְאֹתוֹ הָרַגְתָּ
	בְּחֶרֶב בְּנֵי עַמּוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Why then have you flouted the command of YHWH and done what
	displeases Him? You have put Uriah the Hittite to the sword; you
	took his wife and made her your wife and had him killed by the
	sword of the Ammonites.
}

\eJ{
	Therefore the sword shall never depart from your House—because you
	spurned Me by taking the wife of Uriah the Hittite and making her
	your wife.’
}

\eJ{
	Thus said YHWH: ‘I will make a calamity rise against you from
	within your own house; I will take your wives and give them to
	another man before your very eyes and he shall sleep with your
	wives under this very sun.
}

\eJ{
	You acted in secret, but I will make this happen in the sight of
	all Israel and in broad daylight.’”
}

\eJ{
	David said to Nathan, “I stand guilty before YHWH!” And Nathan
	replied to David, “YHWH has remitted your sin; you shall not die.
}

\eJ{
	However, since you have spurned the enemies of YHWH by this deed,
	even the child about to be born to you shall die.”
}

\eJ{
	Nathan went home, and YHWH afflicted the child that Uriah’s wife
	had borne to David, and it became critically ill.
}

\eJ{
	David entreated God for the boy; David fasted, and he went in and
	spent the night lying on the ground.
}

\eJ{
	The senior servants of his household tried to induce him to get up
	from the ground; but he refused, nor would he partake of food with
	them.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי וַיָּמת הַיָּלֶד וַיִּרְאוּ עַבְדֵי דָוִד לְהַגִּיד לוֹ כִּי־מֵת הַיֶּלֶד כִּי אָמְרוּ הִנֵּה בִהְיוֹת הַיֶּלֶד חַי דִּבַּרְנוּ אֵלָיו וְלֹא־שָׁמַע
	בְּקוֹלֵנוּ וְאֵיךְ נֹאמַר אֵלָיו מֵת הַיֶּלֶד וְעָשָׂה רָעָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When David saw his servants talking in whispers, David understood
	that the child was dead; David asked his servants, “Is the child
	dead?” “Yes,” they replied.
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon David rose from the ground; he bathed and anointed
	himself, and he changed his clothes. He went into the House of
	YHWH and prostrated himself. Then he went home and asked for food,
	which they set before him, and he ate.
}

\eJ{
	His courtiers asked him, “Why have you acted in this manner? While
	the child was alive, you fasted and wept; but now that the child
	is dead, you rise and take food!”
}

\eJ{
	He replied, “While the child was still alive, I fasted and wept
	because I thought: ‘Who knows? YHWH may have pity on me, and the
	child may live.’
}

\eJ{
	But now that he is dead, why should I fast? Can I bring him back
	again? I shall go to him, but he will never come back to me.”
}

\eJ{
	David consoled his wife Bathsheba; he went to her and lay with
	her. She bore a son and she named him Solomon. YHWH favored him,
}

\eJ{
	and He sent a message through the prophet Nathan; and he was named
	Jedidiah at the instance of YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	Joab sent messengers to David and said, “I have attacked Rabbah
	and I have already captured the water city.
}

\eJ{
	Now muster the rest of the troops and besiege the city and capture
	it; otherwise I will capture the city myself, and my name will be
	connected with it.”
}

\eJ{
	David mustered all the troops and marched on Rabbah, and he
	attacked it and captured it.
}

\eJ{
	The crown was taken from the head of their king and it was placed
	on David’s head—it weighed a talent of gold, and [on it] were
	precious stones. He also carried off a vast amount of booty from
	the city.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְאֶת־הָעָם אֲשֶׁר־בָּהּ הוֹצִיא וַיָּשֶׂם בַּמְּגֵרָה וּבַחֲרִצֵי הַבַּרְזֶל וּבְמַגְזְרֹת הַבַּרְזֶל וְהֶעֱבִיר אוֹתָם (במלכן) [בַּמַּלְבֵּן] וְכֵן יַעֲשֶׂה לְכֹל עָרֵי
	בְנֵי־עַמּוֹן וַיָּשׁב דָּוִד וְכל־הָעָם יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He led out the people who lived there and set them to work with
	saws, iron threshing boards, and iron axes, or assigned them to
	brickmaking; David did this to all the towns of Ammon. Then David
	and all the troops returned to Jerusalem.
}

\eJ{
	This happened sometime afterward: Absalom son of David had a
	beautiful sister named Tamar, and Amnon son of David became
	infatuated with her.
}

\eJ{
	Amnon was so distraught because of his [half-]sister Tamar that he
	became sick; for she was a virgin, and it seemed impossible to
	Amnon to do anything to her.
}

\eJ{
	Amnon had a friend named Jonadab, the son of David’s brother
	Shimah; Jonadab was a very clever man.
}

\eJ{
	He asked him, “Why are you so dejected, O prince, morning after
	morning? Tell me!” Amnon replied, “I am in love with Tamar, the
	sister of my brother Absalom!”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ יְהוֹנָדָב שְׁכַב עַל־מִשְׁכָּבְךָ וְהִתְחָל וּבָא אָבִיךָ לִרְאוֹתֶךָ וְאָמַרְתָּ אֵלָיו תָּבֹא נָא תָמָר אֲחוֹתִי וְתַבְרֵנִי לֶחֶם וְעָשְׂתָה לְעֵינַי
	אֶת־הַבִּרְיָה לְמַעַן אֲשֶׁר אֶרְאֶה וְאָכַלְתִּי מִיָּדָהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Amnon lay down and pretended to be sick. The king came to see him,
	and Amnon said to the king, “Let my sister Tamar come and prepare
	a couple of cakes in front of me, and let her bring them to me.”
}

\eJ{
	David sent a message to Tamar in the palace, “Please go to the
	house of your brother Amnon and prepare some food for him.”
}

\eJ{
	Tamar went to the house of her brother Amnon, who was in bed. She
	took dough and kneaded it into cakes in front of him, and cooked
	the cakes.
}

\eJ{
	She took the pan and set out [the cakes], but Amnon refused to eat
	and ordered everyone to withdraw. After everyone had withdrawn,
}

\eJ{
	Amnon said to Tamar, “Bring the food inside and feed me.” Tamar
	took the cakes she had made and brought them to her brother
	inside.
}

\eJ{
	But when she served them to him, he caught hold of her and said to
	her, “Come lie with me, sister.”
}

\eJ{
	But she said to him, “Don’t, brother. Don’t force me. Such things
	are not done in Israel! Don’t do such a vile thing!
}

\eJ{
	Where will I carry my shame? And you, you will be like any of the
	scoundrels in Israel! Please, speak to the king; he will not
	refuse me to you.”
}

\eJ{
	But he would not listen to her; he overpowered her and lay with
	her by force.
}

\eJ{
	Then Amnon felt a very great loathing for her; indeed, his
	loathing for her was greater than the passion he had felt for her.
	And Amnon said to her, “Get out!”
}

\eJ{
	She pleaded with him, “Please don’t commit this wrong; to send me
	away would be even worse than the first wrong you committed
	against me.” But he would not listen to her.
}

\eJ{
	He summoned his young attendant and said, “Get that woman out of
	my presence, and bar the door behind her.”—
}

\eJ{
	She was wearing an ornamented tunic, for maiden princesses were
	customarily dressed in such garments. —His attendant took her
	outside and barred the door after her.
}

\eJ{
	Tamar put dust on her head and rent the ornamented tunic she was
	wearing; she put her hands on her head, and walked away, screaming
	loudly as she went.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלֶיהָ אַבְשָׁלוֹם אָחִיהָ הַאֲמִינוֹן אָחִיךְ הָיָה עִמָּךְ וְעַתָּה אֲחוֹתִי הַחֲרִישִׁי אָחִיךְ הוּא אַל־תָּשִׁיתִי אֶת־לִבֵּךְ לַדָּבָר הַזֶּה וַתֵּשֶׁב תָּמָר
	וְשֹׁמֵמָה בֵּית אַבְשָׁלוֹם אָחִיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Her brother Absalom said to her, “Was it your brother Amnon who
	did this to you? For the present, sister, keep quiet about it; he
	is your brother. Don’t brood over the matter.” And Tamar remained
	in her brother Absalom’s house, forlorn.
}

\eJ{
	When King David heard about all this, he was greatly upset.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom didn’t utter a word to Amnon, good or bad; but Absalom
	hated Amnon because he had violated his sister Tamar.
}

\eJ{
	Two years later, when Absalom was having his flocks sheared at
	Baal-hazor near Ephraim, Absalom invited all the king’s sons.
}

\eJ{
	And Absalom came to the king and said, “Your servant is having his
	flocks sheared. Would Your Majesty and your retinue accompany your
	servant?”
}

\eJ{
	But the king answered Absalom, “No, my son. We must not all come,
	or we’ll be a burden to you.” He urged him, but he would not go,
	and he said good-bye to him.
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon Absalom said, “In that case, let my brother Amnon come
	with us,” to which the king replied, “He shall not go with you.”
}

\eJ{
	But Absalom urged him, and he sent with him Amnon and all the
	other princes.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְצַו אַבְשָׁלוֹם אֶת־נְעָרָיו לֵאמֹר רְאוּ נָא כְּטוֹב לֵב־אַמְנוֹן בַּיַּיִן וְאָמַרְתִּי אֲלֵיכֶם הַכּוּ אֶת־אַמְנוֹן וַהֲמִתֶּם אֹתוֹ אַל־תִּירָאוּ הֲלוֹא כִּי אָנֹכִי
	צִוִּיתִי אֶתְכֶם חִזְקוּ וִהְיוּ לִבְנֵי־חָיִל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Now Absalom gave his attendants these orders: “Watch, and when
	Amnon is merry with wine and I tell you to strike down Amnon, kill
	him! Don’t be afraid, for it is I who give you the order. Act with
	determination, like brave men!”
}

\eJ{
	Absalom’s attendants did to Amnon as Absalom had ordered;
	whereupon all the other princes mounted their mules and fled.
}

\eJ{
	They were still on the road when a rumor reached David that
	Absalom had killed all the princes, and that not one of them had
	survived.
}

tex.commentary:
The rumors reach David before the people do, and by then the
rumor is that Absalom killed \emph{everybody!}

All the brothers and half brothers.

tex.english:
\eJ{
	At this, David rent his garment and lay down on the ground, and
	all his courtiers stood by with their clothes rent.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַן יוֹנָדָב בֶּן־שִׁמְעָה אֲחִי־דָוִד וַיֹּאמֶר אַל־יֹאמַר אֲדֹנִי אֵת כּל־הַנְּעָרִים בְּנֵי־הַמֶּלֶךְ הֵמִיתוּ כִּי־אַמְנוֹן לְבַדּוֹ מֵת כִּי־עַל־פִּי אַבְשָׁלוֹם הָיְתָה
	שׂוּמָה מִיּוֹם עַנֹּתוֹ אֵת תָּמָר אֲחֹתוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But Jonadab, the son of David’s brother Shimah, said, “My lord
	must not think that all the young princes have been killed. Only
	Amnon is dead; for this has been decided by Absalom ever since his
	sister Tamar was violated.
}

\eJ{
	So my lord the king must not think for a moment that all the
	princes are dead; Amnon alone is dead.”
}

\eJ{
	Meanwhile Absalom had fled. The watchman on duty looked up and saw
	a large crowd coming from the road to his rear, from the side of
	the hill.
}

\eJ{
	Jonadab said to the king, “See, the princes have come! It is just
	as your servant said.”
}

\eJ{
	As he finished speaking, the princes came in and broke into
	weeping; and David and all his courtiers wept bitterly, too.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom had fled, and he came to Talmai son of Ammihud, king of
	Geshur. And [King David] mourned over his son a long time.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom, who had fled to Geshur, remained there three years.
}

\eJ{
	And King David was pining away for Absalom, for [the king] had
	gotten over Amnon’s death.
}

\eJ{
	Joab son of Zeruiah could see that the king’s mind was on Absalom;
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יוֹאָב תְּקוֹעָה וַיִּקַּח מִשָּׁם אִשָּׁה חֲכָמָה וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלֶיהָ הִתְאַבְּלִי־נָא וְלִבְשִׁי־נָא בִגְדֵי־אֵבֶל וְאַל־תָּסוּכִי שֶׁמֶן וְהָיִית כְּאִשָּׁה זֶה יָמִים
	רַבִּים מִתְאַבֶּלֶת עַל־מֵת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	so Joab sent to Tekoa and brought a clever woman from there. He
	said to her, “Pretend you are in mourning; put on mourning clothes
	and don’t anoint yourself with oil; and act like a woman who has
	grieved a long time over a departed one.
}

\eJ{
	Go to the king and say to him thus and thus.” And Joab told her
	what to say.
}

\eJ{
	The woman of Tekoa came to the king, flung herself face down to
	the ground, and prostrated herself. She cried out, “Help, O king!”
}

\eJ{
	The king asked her, “What troubles you?” And she answered, “Alas,
	I am a widow, my husband is dead.
}

tex.commentary:
He asks what the problem is.

She says her husband is dead.

\aB{Naturally, she could use some good help from the king.}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Your maidservant had two sons. The two of them came to blows out
	in the fields where there was no one to stop them, and one of them
	struck the other and killed him.
}

tex.commentary:
\aB{She says she had two sons who came to blows out in the field
where no one could stop them and there was a little death.}

% \fC{Lit. "\chineseA{停} (tīng) [sic]." The tone is incorrect,
% and the [sic] is present in the original text.}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהִנֵּה קָמָה כל־הַמִּשְׁפָּחָה עַל־שִׁפְחָתֶךָ וַיֹּאמְרוּ תְּנִי אֶת־מַכֵּה אָחִיו וּנְמִתֵהוּ בְּנֶפֶשׁ אָחִיו אֲשֶׁר הָרָג וְנַשְׁמִידָה גַּם אֶת־הַיּוֹרֵשׁ וְכִבּוּ
	אֶת־גַּחַלְתִּי אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁאָרָה לְבִלְתִּי (שום) [שִׂים־]לְאִישִׁי שֵׁם וּשְׁאֵרִית עַל־פְּנֵי הָאֲדָמָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The king said to the woman, “Go home. I will issue an order in
	your behalf.”
}

\eJ{
	And the woman of Tekoa said to the king, “My lord king, may the
	guilt be on me and on my ancestral house; Your Majesty and his
	throne are guiltless.”
}

\eJ{
	The king said, “If anyone says anything more to you, have him
	brought to me, and he will never trouble you again.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתֹּאמֶר יִזְכּר־נָא הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ (מהרבית) [מֵהַרְבַּת] גֹּאֵל הַדָּם לְשַׁחֵת וְלֹא יַשְׁמִידוּ אֶת־בְּנִי וַיֹּאמֶר חַי־יְהֹוָה אִם־יִפֹּל מִשַּׂעֲרַת בְּנֵךְ
	אָרְצָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	She replied, “Let Your Majesty be mindful of YHWH your God and
	restrain the blood avenger bent on destruction, so that my son may
	not be killed.” And he said, “As YHWH lives, not a hair of your
	son shall fall to the ground.”
}

\eJ{
	Then the woman said, “Please let your maidservant say another word
	to my lord the king.” “Speak on,” said the king.
}

tex.commentary:
She's like "One more thing."

He's listening.

% \fC{Lit. \chineseA{聽} in the original.}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And the woman said, “Why then have you planned the like against
	God’s people? In making this pronouncement, Your Majesty condemns
	himself in that Your Majesty does not bring back his own banished
	one.
}

\eJ{
	We must all die; we are like water that is poured out on the
	ground and cannot be gathered up. God will not take away the life
	of one who makes plans so that no one may be kept banished.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְעַתָּה אֲשֶׁר־בָּאתִי לְדַבֵּר אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲדֹנִי אֶת־הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה כִּי יֵרְאֻנִי הָעָם וַתֹּאמֶר שִׁפְחָתְךָ אֲדַבְּרָה־נָּא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אוּלַי יַעֲשֶׂה הַמֶּלֶךְ
	אֶת־דְּבַר אֲמָתוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And the reason I have come to say these things to the king, my
	lord, is that the people have frightened me. Your maidservant
	thought I would speak to Your Majesty; perhaps Your Majesty would
	act on his handmaid’s plea.
}

\eJ{
	For Your Majesty would surely agree to deliver his handmaid from
	the hands of anyone [who would seek to] cut off both me and my son
	from the heritage of God.
}

\eJ{
	Your maidservant thought, ‘Let the word of my lord the king
	provide comfort; for my lord the king is like an angel of God,
	understanding everything, good and bad.’ May YHWH your God be with
	you.”
}

\eJ{
	In reply, the king said to the woman, “Do not withhold from me
	anything I ask you!” The woman answered, “Let my lord the king
	speak.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר הַמֶּלֶךְ הֲיַד יוֹאָב אִתָּךְ בְּכל־זֹאת וַתַּעַן הָאִשָּׁה וַתֹּאמֶר חֵי־נַפְשְׁךָ אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ אִם־אִשׁ לְהֵמִין וּלְהַשְׂמִיל מִכֹּל אֲשֶׁר־דִּבֶּר אֲדֹנִי
	הַמֶּלֶךְ כִּי־עַבְדְּךָ יוֹאָב הוּא צִוָּנִי וְהוּא שָׂם בְּפִי שִׁפְחָתְךָ אֵת כּל־הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	It was to conceal the real purpose of the matter that your servant
	Joab did this thing. My lord is as wise as an angel of God, and he
	knows all that goes on in the land.”
}

\eJ{
	Then the king said to Joab, “I will do this thing. Go and bring
	back my boy Absalom.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּפֹּל יוֹאָב אֶל־פָּנָיו אַרְצָה וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ וַיְבָרֶךְ אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֹּאמֶר יוֹאָב הַיּוֹם יָדַע עַבְדְּךָ כִּי־מָצָאתִי חֵן בְּעֵינֶיךָ אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה
	הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־דְּבַר (עבדו) [עַבְדֶּךָ]׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joab flung himself face down on the ground and prostrated himself.
	Joab blessed the king and said, “Today your servant knows that he
	has found favor with you, my lord king, for Your Majesty has
	granted his servant’s request.”
}

\eJ{
	And Joab went at once to Geshur and brought Absalom to Jerusalem.
}

\eJ{
	But the king said, “Let him go directly to his house and not
	present himself to me.” So Absalom went directly to his house and
	did not present himself to the king.
}

\eJ{
	No one in all Israel was so admired for his beauty as Absalom;
	from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head he was without
	blemish.
}

\eJ{
	When he cut his hair—he had to have it cut every year, for it grew
	too heavy for him—the hair of his head weighed two hundred shekels
	by the royal weight.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom had three sons and a daughter whose name was Tamar; she
	was a beautiful woman.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom lived in Jerusalem two years without appearing before the
	king.
}

\eJ{
	Then Absalom sent for Joab, in order to send him to the king; but
	Joab would not come to him. He sent for him a second time, but he
	would not come.
}

\eJ{
	So [Absalom] said to his servants, “Look, Joab’s field is next to
	mine, and he has barley there. Go and set it on fire.” And
	Absalom’s servants set the field on fire.
}

\eJ{
	Joab came at once to Absalom’s house and said to him, “Why did
	your servants set fire to my field?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אַבְשָׁלוֹם אֶל־יוֹאָב הִנֵּה שָׁלַחְתִּי אֵלֶיךָ לֵאמֹר בֹּא הֵנָּה וְאֶשְׁלְחָה אֹתְךָ אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ לֵאמֹר לָמָּה בָּאתִי מִגְּשׁוּר טוֹב לִי עֹד אֲנִי־שָׁם וְעַתָּה
	אֶרְאֶה פְּנֵי הַמֶּלֶךְ וְאִם־יֶשׁ־בִּי עָוֺן וֶהֱמִתָנִי׃
}

\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹא יוֹאָב אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיַּגֶּד־לוֹ וַיִּקְרָא אֶל־אַבְשָׁלוֹם וַיָּבֹא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ לוֹ עַל־אַפָּיו אַרְצָה לִפְנֵי הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיִּשַּׁק הַמֶּלֶךְ
	לְאַבְשָׁלוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joab went to the king and reported to him; whereupon he summoned
	Absalom. He came to the king and flung himself face down to the
	ground before the king. And the king kissed Absalom.
}

\eJ{
	Sometime afterward, Absalom provided himself with a chariot,
	horses, and fifty outrunners.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהִשְׁכִּים אַבְשָׁלוֹם וְעָמַד עַל־יַד דֶּרֶךְ הַשָּׁעַר וַיְהִי כּל־הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־יִהְיֶה־לּוֹ־רִיב לָבוֹא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ לַמִּשְׁפָּט וַיִּקְרָא אַבְשָׁלוֹם אֵלָיו וַיֹּאמֶר
	אֵי־מִזֶּה עִיר אַתָּה וַיֹּאמֶר מֵאַחַד שִׁבְטֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל עַבְדֶּךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Absalom would say to him, “It is clear that your claim is right
	and just, but there is no one assigned to you by the king to hear
	it.”
}

\eJ{
	And Absalom went on, “If only I were appointed judge in the land
	and everyone with a legal dispute came before me, I would see that
	he got his rights.”
}

\eJ{
	And if a man approached to bow to him, [Absalom] would extend his
	hand and take hold of him and kiss him.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom did this to every Israelite who came to the king for
	judgment. Thus Absalom won away the hearts of the men of Israel.
}

\eJ{
	After a period of forty years had gone by, Absalom said to the
	king, “Let me go to Hebron and fulfill a vow that I made to YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	For your servant made a vow when I lived in Geshur of Aram: If
	YHWH ever brings me back to Jerusalem, I will worship YHWH.”
}

\eJ{
	The king said to him, “Go in peace”; and so he set out for Hebron.
}

\eJ{
	But Absalom sent agents to all the tribes of Israel to say, “When
	you hear the blast of the horn, announce that Absalom has become
	king in Hebron.”
}

\eJ{
	Two hundred men of Jerusalem accompanied Absalom; they were
	invited and went in good faith, suspecting nothing.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom also sent [to fetch] Ahithophel the Gilonite, David’s
	counselor, from his town, Giloh, when the sacrifices were to be
	offered. The conspiracy gained strength, and the people supported
	Absalom in increasing numbers.
}

\eJ{
	Someone came and told David, “The loyalty of the men of Israel has
	veered toward Absalom.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר דָּוִד לְכל־עֲבָדָיו אֲשֶׁר־אִתּוֹ בִירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם קוּמוּ וְנִבְרָחָה כִּי לֹא־תִהְיֶה־לָּנוּ פְלֵיטָה מִפְּנֵי אַבְשָׁלֹם מַהֲרוּ לָלֶכֶת פֶּן־יְמַהֵר וְהִשִּׂגָנוּ
	וְהִדִּיחַ עָלֵינוּ אֶת־הָרָעָה וְהִכָּה הָעִיר לְפִי־חָרֶב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Whereupon David said to all the courtiers who were with him in
	Jerusalem, “Let us flee at once, or none of us will escape from
	Absalom. We must get away quickly, or he will soon overtake us and
	bring down disaster upon us and put the city to the sword.”
}

\eJ{
	The king’s courtiers said to the king, “Whatever our lord the king
	decides, your servants are ready.”
}

\eJ{
	So the king left, followed by his entire household, except for ten
	concubines whom the king left to mind the palace.
}

\eJ{
	The king left, followed by all the people, and they stopped at the
	last house.
}

\eJ{
	All his followers marched past him, including all the Cherethites
	and all the Pelethites; and all the Gittites, six hundred men who
	had accompanied him from Gath, also marched by the king.
}

\eJ{
	And the king said to Ittai the Gittite, “Why should you too go
	with us? Go back and stay with the [new] king, for you are a
	foreigner and you are also an exile from your country.
}

\eJ{
	You came only yesterday; should I make you wander about with us
	today, when I myself must go wherever I can? Go back, and take
	your kinsmen with you, [in] true faithfulness.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַן אִתַּי אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֹּאמַר חַי־יְהֹוָה וְחֵי אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ כִּי (אם) בִּמְקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה־שָּׁם אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ אִם־לְמָוֶת אִם־לְחַיִּים כִּי־שָׁם יִהְיֶה
	עַבְדֶּךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Ittai replied to the king, “As YHWH lives and as my lord the king
	lives, wherever my lord the king may be, there your servant will
	be, whether for death or for life!”
}

\eJ{
	And David said to Ittai, “Then march by.” And Ittai the Gittite
	and all his men and all the children who were with him marched by.
}

\eJ{
	The whole countryside wept aloud as the troops marched by. The
	king crossed the Kidron Valley, and all the troops crossed by the
	road to the wilderness.
}

\eJ{
	Then Zadok appeared, with all the Levites carrying the Ark of the
	Covenant of God; and they set down the Ark of God until all the
	people had finished marching out of the city. Abiathar also came
	up.
}

tex.commentary:
Then Zadok appears.\fA{That's not why everyone's crying.}

With all the Levites.\fA{That's not why everyone's crying either.}

And the box that kills you if you touch it\fA{That's not why everyone's
crying either I promise.} and sometimes it gives you more everyday things,
like \eR{b}anal problems.\fA{That's not why everyone's crying either it's
just \eR{b}anal it's a normal thing now.}

Abiathar was there too.\fA{Poor bastard has no idea what's coming.}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But the king said to Zadok, “Take the Ark of God back to the city.
	If I find favor with YHWH, He will bring me back and let me see it
	and its abode.
}

\eJ{
	And if He should say, ‘I do not want you,’ I am ready; let Him do
	with me as He pleases.”
}

tex.commentary:
And if David doesn't win, well... \aB{No way, Yahweh would never do that to
a guy like him.}

Regardless, David says if he loses then YHWH can have his way with him.

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And the king said to the priest Zadok, “Do you understand? You
	return to the safety of the city with your two sons, your own son
	Ahimaaz and Abiathar’s son Jonathan.
}

\eJ{
	Look, I shall linger in the steppes of the wilderness until word
	comes from you to inform me.”
}

\eJ{
	Zadok and Abiathar brought the Ark of God back to Jerusalem, and
	they stayed there.
}

\eJ{
	David meanwhile went up the slope of the [Mount of] Olives,
	weeping as he went; his head was covered and he walked barefoot.
	And all the people who were with him covered their heads and wept
	as they went up.
}

\eJ{
	David [was] told that Ahithophel was among the conspirators with
	Absalom, and he prayed, “Please, O LORD, frustrate Ahithophel’s
	counsel!”
}

\eJ{
	When David reached the top, where people would prostrate
	themselves to God, Hushai the Archite was there to meet him, with
	his robe torn and with earth on his head.
}

\eJ{
	David said to him, “If you march on with me, you will be a burden
	to me.
}

\eJ{
	But if you go back to the city and say to Absalom, ‘I will be your
	servant, O king; I was your father’s servant formerly, and now I
	will be yours,’ then you can nullify Ahithophel’s counsel for me.
}

\eJ{
	You will have the priests Zadok and Abiathar there, and you can
	report everything that you hear in the king’s palace to the
	priests Zadok and Abiathar.
}

\eJ{
	Also, their two sons are there with them, Zadok’s son Ahimaaz and
	Abiathar’s son Jonathan; and through them you can report to me
	everything you hear.”
}

\eJ{
	And so Hushai, the friend of David, reached the city as Absalom
	was entering Jerusalem.
}

\eJ{
	David had passed a little beyond the summit when Ziba the servant
	of Mephibosheth came toward him with a pair of saddled asses
	carrying two hundred loaves of bread, one hundred cakes of raisin,
	one hundred cakes of figs, and a jar of wine.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶל־צִיבָא מָה־אֵלֶּה לָּךְ וַיֹּאמֶר צִיבָא הַחֲמוֹרִים לְבֵית־הַמֶּלֶךְ לִרְכֹּב (ולהלחם) [וְהַלֶּחֶם] וְהַקַּיִץ לֶאֱכוֹל הַנְּעָרִים וְהַיַּיִן לִשְׁתּוֹת
	הַיָּעֵף בַּמִּדְבָּר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The king asked Ziba, “What are you doing with these?” Ziba
	answered, “The asses are for Your Majesty’s family to ride on, the
	bread and figs are for the attendants to eat, and the wine is to
	be drunk by any who are exhausted in the wilderness.”
}

\eJ{
	“And where is your master’s son?” the king asked. “He is staying
	in Jerusalem,” Ziba replied to the king, “for he thinks that the
	House of Israel will now give him back the throne of his
	grandfather.”
}

\eJ{
	The king said to Ziba, “Then all that belongs to Mephibosheth is
	now yours!” And Ziba replied, “I bow low. Your Majesty is most
	gracious to me.”
}

\eJ{
	As King David was approaching Bahurim, a member of Saul’s clan—a
	man named Shimei son of Gera—came out from there, hurling insults
	as he came.
}

\eJ{
	He threw stones at David and all King David’s courtiers, while all
	the troops and all the warriors were at his right and his left.
}

\eJ{
	And these are the insults that Shimei hurled: “Get out, get out,
	you criminal, you villain!
}

tex.commentary:
Here's some of the stuff he yelled:

\begin{itemize}
\item Get out of here.
\item You criminal.
\item You villain.
\end{itemize}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	הֵשִׁיב עָלֶיךָ יְהֹוָה כֹּל דְּמֵי בֵית־שָׁאוּל אֲשֶׁר מָלַכְתָּ תַּחְתָּו וַיִּתֵּן יְהֹוָה אֶת־הַמְּלוּכָה בְּיַד אַבְשָׁלוֹם בְּנֶךָ וְהִנְּךָ בְּרָעָתֶךָ כִּי אִישׁ דָּמִים
	אָתָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	YHWH is paying you back for all your crimes against the family of
	Saul, whose throne you seized. YHWH is handing over the throne to
	your son Absalom; you are in trouble because you are a criminal!”
}

\eJ{
	Abishai son of Zeruiah said to the king, “Why let that dead dog
	abuse my lord the king? Let me go over and cut off his head!”
}

\eJ{
	But the king said, “What has this to do with you, you sons of
	Zeruiah? He is abusing [me] only because YHWH told him to abuse
	David; and who is to say, ‘Why did You do that?’”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר דָּוִד אֶל־אֲבִישַׁי וְאֶל־כּל־עֲבָדָיו הִנֵּה בְנִי אֲשֶׁר־יָצָא מִמֵּעַי מְבַקֵּשׁ אֶת־נַפְשִׁי וְאַף כִּי־עַתָּה בֶּן־הַיְמִינִי הַנִּחוּ לוֹ וִיקַלֵּל כִּי אָמַר־לוֹ
	יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	David said further to Abishai and all the courtiers, “If my son,
	my own issue, seeks to kill me, how much more the Benjaminite! Let
	him go on hurling abuse, for YHWH has told him to.
}

\eJ{
	Perhaps YHWH will look upon my punishment and recompense me for
	the abuse [Shimei] has uttered today.”
}

\eJ{
	David and his men continued on their way, while Shimei walked
	alongside on the slope of the hill, insulting him as he walked,
	and throwing stones at him and flinging dirt.
}

\eJ{
	The king and all who accompanied him arrived exhausted, and he
	rested there.
}

\eJ{
	Meanwhile Absalom and all the people, the men of Israel, arrived
	in Jerusalem, together with Ahithophel.
}

\eJ{
	When Hushai the Archite, David’s friend, came before Absalom,
	Hushai said to Absalom, “Long live the king! Long live the king!”
}

\eJ{
	But Absalom said to Hushai, “Is this your loyalty to your friend?
	Why didn’t you go with your friend?”
}

\eJ{
	“Not at all!” Hushai replied. “I am for the one whom YHWH and this
	people and all the men of Israel have chosen, and I will stay with
	him.
}

\eJ{
	Furthermore, whom should I serve, if not David’s son? As I was in
	your father’s service, so I will be in yours.”
}

\eJ{
	Absalom then said to Ahithophel, “What do you advise us to do?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֲחִיתֹפֶל אֶל־אַבְשָׁלֹם בּוֹא אֶל־פִּלַגְשֵׁי אָבִיךָ אֲשֶׁר הִנִּיחַ לִשְׁמוֹר הַבָּיִת וְשָׁמַע כּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי־נִבְאַשְׁתָּ אֶת־אָבִיךָ וְחָזְקוּ יְדֵי
	כּל־אֲשֶׁר אִתָּךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And Ahithophel said to Absalom, “Have intercourse with your
	father’s concubines, whom he left to mind the palace; and when all
	Israel hears that you have dared the wrath of your father, all who
	support you will be encouraged.”
}

\eJ{
	So they pitched a tent for Absalom on the roof, and Absalom lay
	with his father’s concubines with the full knowledge of all
	Israel.—
}

\eJ{
	In those days, the advice which Ahithophel gave was accepted like
	an oracle sought from God; that is how all the advice of
	Ahithophel was esteemed both by David and by Absalom.
}

\eJ{
	And Ahithophel said to Absalom, “Let me pick twelve thousand men
	and set out tonight in pursuit of David.
}

\eJ{
	I will come upon him when he is weary and disheartened, and I will
	throw him into a panic; and when all the troops with him flee, I
	will kill the king alone.
}

\eJ{
	And I will bring back all the people to you; when all have come
	back [except] the man you are after, all the people will be at
	peace.”
}

tex.commentary:
Absalom agrees.

The senate's on board too.\fC{From Latin \textit{senatus}, from
\textit{senex}, “old man” or “old person”—literally, a council of elders.}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But Absalom said, “Summon Hushai the Archite as well, so we can
	hear what he too has to say.”
}

\eJ{
	Hushai came to Absalom, and Absalom said to him, “This is what
	Ahithophel has advised. Shall we follow his advice? If not, what
	do you say?”
}

\eJ{
	Hushai said to Absalom, “This time the advice that Ahithophel has
	given is not good.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר חוּשַׁי אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ אֶת־אָבִיךָ וְאֶת־אֲנָשָׁיו כִּי גִבֹּרִים הֵמָּה וּמָרֵי נֶפֶשׁ הֵמָּה כְּדֹב שַׁכּוּל בַּשָּׂדֶה וְאָבִיךָ אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה וְלֹא יָלִין
	אֶת־הָעָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	You know,” Hushai continued, “that your father and his men are
	courageous fighters, and they are as desperate as a bear in the
	wild robbed of her whelps. Your father is an experienced soldier,
	and he will not spend the night with the troops;
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	הִנֵּה עַתָּה הוּא־נֶחְבָּא בְּאַחַת הַפְּחָתִים אוֹ בְּאַחַד הַמְּקוֹמֹת וְהָיָה כִּנְפֹל בָּהֶם בַּתְּחִלָּה וְשָׁמַע הַשֹּׁמֵעַ וְאָמַר הָיְתָה מַגֵּפָה בָּעָם אֲשֶׁר אַחֲרֵי
	אַבְשָׁלֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	even now he must be hiding in one of the pits or in some other
	place. And if any of them fall at the first attack, whoever hears
	of it will say, ‘A disaster has struck the troops that follow
	Absalom’;
}

\eJ{
	and even if he is a brave man with the heart of a lion, he will be
	shaken—for all Israel knows that your father and the soldiers with
	him are courageous fighters.
}

\eJ{
	So I advise that all Israel from Dan to Beer-sheba—as numerous as
	the sands of the sea—be called up to join you, and that you
	yourself march into battle.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וּבָאנוּ אֵלָיו (באחת) [בְּאַחַד] הַמְּקוֹמֹת אֲשֶׁר נִמְצָא שָׁם וְנַחְנוּ עָלָיו כַּאֲשֶׁר יִפֹּל הַטַּל עַל־הָאֲדָמָה וְלֹא־נוֹתַר בּוֹ וּבְכל־הָאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר־אִתּוֹ
	גַּם־אֶחָד׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When we come upon him in whatever place he may be, we’ll descend
	on him [as thick] as dew falling on the ground; and no one will
	survive, neither he nor any of the men with him.
}

\eJ{
	And if he withdraws into a city, all Israel will bring ropes to
	that city and drag its stones as far as the riverbed, until not
	even a pebble of it is left.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אַבְשָׁלוֹם וְכל־אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל טוֹבָה עֲצַת חוּשַׁי הָאַרְכִּי מֵעֲצַת אֲחִיתֹפֶלוַיהֹוָה צִוָּה לְהָפֵר אֶת־עֲצַת אֲחִיתֹפֶל הַטּוֹבָה לְבַעֲבוּר הָבִיא יְהֹוָה
	אֶל־אַבְשָׁלוֹם אֶת־הָרָעָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Absalom and all Israel agreed that the advice of Hushai the
	Archite was better than that of Ahithophel.—YHWH had decreed that
	Ahithophel’s sound advice be nullified, in order that YHWH might
	bring ruin upon Absalom.
}

\eJ{
	Then Hushai told the priests Zadok and Abiathar, “This is what
	Ahithophel advised Absalom and the elders of Israel; this is what
	I advised.
}

\eJ{
	Now send at once and tell David, ‘Do not spend the night at the
	fords of the wilderness, but cross over at once; otherwise the
	king and all the troops with him will be annihilated.’”
}

\eJ{
	Jonathan and Ahimaaz were staying at En-rogel, and a slave girl
	would go and bring them word and they in turn would go and inform
	King David. For they themselves dared not be seen entering the
	city.
}

\eJ{
	But a boy saw them and informed Absalom. They left at once and
	came to the house of a man in Bahurim who had a well in his
	courtyard. They got down into it,
}

\eJ{
	and the wife took a cloth, spread it over the mouth of the well,
	and scattered groats on top of it, so that nothing would be
	noticed.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ עַבְדֵי אַבְשָׁלוֹם אֶל־הָאִשָּׁה הַבַּיְתָה וַיֹּאמְרוּ אַיֵּה אֲחִימַעַץ וִיהוֹנָתָן וַתֹּאמֶר לָהֶם הָאִשָּׁה עָבְרוּ מִיכַל הַמָּיִם וַיְבַקְשׁוּ וְלֹא מָצָאוּ
	וַיָּשֻׁבוּ יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	When Absalom’s servants came to the woman at the house and asked
	where Ahimaaz and Jonathan were, the woman told them that they had
	crossed a bit beyond the water. They searched, but found nothing;
	and they returned to Jerusalem.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי לֶכְתָּם וַיַּעֲלוּ מֵהַבְּאֵר וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיַּגִּדוּ לַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֶל־דָּוִד קוּמוּ וְעִבְרוּ מְהֵרָה אֶת־הַמַּיִם כִּי־כָכָה יָעַץ עֲלֵיכֶם
	אֲחִיתֹפֶל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	After they were gone, [Ahimaaz and Jonathan] came up from the well
	and went and informed King David. They said to David, “Go and
	cross the water quickly, for Ahithophel has advised thus and thus
	concerning you.”
}

\eJ{
	David and all the troops with him promptly crossed the Jordan, and
	by daybreak not one was left who had not crossed the Jordan.
}

\eJ{
	When Ahithophel saw that his advice had not been followed, he
	saddled his ass and went home to his native town. He set his
	affairs in order, and then he hanged himself. He was buried in his
	ancestral tomb.
}

\eJ{
	David had reached Mahanaim when Absalom and all the men of Israel
	with him crossed the Jordan.
}

\eJ{
	Absalom had appointed Amasa army commander in place of Joab; Amasa
	was the son of a man named Ithra the Israelite, who had married
	Abigal, daughter of Nahash and sister of Joab’s mother Zeruiah.
}

\eJ{
	The Israelites and Absalom encamped in the district of Gilead.
}

\eJ{
	When David reached Mahanaim, Shobi son of Nahash from
	Rabbath-ammon, Machir son of Ammiel from Lo-debar, and Barzillai
	the Gileadite from Rogelim
}

tex.commentary:
When David reached that place near the Jabbok, Captor son of
Snake\fB{Somebody else is son of Snake already. What's Snake been up to?}
and two other people who also have genealogies and hometowns

tex.english:
\eJ{
	presented couches, basins, and earthenware; also wheat, barley,
	flour, parched grain, beans, lentils, parched grain,
}

\eJ{
	honey, curds, a flock, and cheese from the herd for David and the
	troops with him to eat. For they knew that the troops must have
	grown hungry, faint, and thirsty in the wilderness.
}

tex.commentary:
and more food

\aB{Was one of those foods "a flock"?}

\hphantom{and more food} for David and the troops with him to eat.

\aB{Reason: \emph{(drumroll)} They were hungry.}

End of chapter.

tex.english:
\eJ{
	David mustered the troops who were with him and set over them
	captains of thousands and captains of hundreds.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְשַׁלַּח דָּוִד אֶת־הָעָם הַשְּׁלִשִׁית בְּיַד־יוֹאָב וְהַשְּׁלִשִׁית בְּיַד אֲבִישַׁי בֶּן־צְרוּיָה אֲחִי יוֹאָב וְהַשְּׁלִשִׁת בְּיַד אִתַּי הַגִּתִּיוַיֹּאמֶר הַמֶּלֶךְ
	אֶל־הָעָם יָצֹא אֵצֵא גַּם־אֲנִי עִמָּכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	David sent out the troops, one-third under the command of Joab,
	one-third under the command of Joab’s brother Abishai son of
	Zeruiah, and one-third under the command of Ittai the Gittite. And
	David said to the troops, “I myself will march out with you.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר הָעָם לֹא תֵצֵא כִּי אִם־נֹס נָנוּס לֹא־יָשִׂימוּ אֵלֵינוּ לֵב וְאִם־יָמֻתוּ חֶצְיֵנוּ לֹא־יָשִׂימוּ אֵלֵינוּ לֵב כִּי־עַתָּה כָמֹנוּ עֲשָׂרָה אֲלָפִים וְעַתָּה טוֹב
	כִּי־תִהְיֶה־לָּנוּ מֵעִיר (לעזיר) [לַעְזוֹר]׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And the king said to them, “I will do whatever you think best.” So
	the king stood beside the gate as all the troops marched out by
	their hundreds and thousands.
}

\eJ{
	The king gave orders to Joab, Abishai, and Ittai: “Deal gently
	with my boy Absalom, for my sake.” All the troops heard the king
	give the order about Absalom to all the officers.
}

\eJ{
	The troops marched out into the open to confront the Israelites,
	and the battle was fought in the forest of Ephraim.
}

\eJ{
	The Israelite troops were routed by David’s followers, and a great
	slaughter took place there that day—twenty thousand men.
}

\eJ{
	The battle spread out over that whole region, and the forest
	devoured more troops that day than the sword.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּקָּרֵא אַבְשָׁלוֹם לִפְנֵי עַבְדֵי דָוִד וְאַבְשָׁלוֹם רֹכֵב עַל־הַפֶּרֶד וַיָּבֹא הַפֶּרֶד תַּחַת שׂוֹבֶךְ הָאֵלָה הַגְּדוֹלָה וַיֶּחֱזַק רֹאשׁוֹ בָאֵלָה וַיֻּתַּן בֵּין
	הַשָּׁמַיִם וּבֵין הָאָרֶץ וְהַפֶּרֶד אֲשֶׁר־תַּחְתָּיו עָבָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Absalom encountered some of David’s followers. Absalom was riding
	on a mule, and as the mule passed under the tangled branches of a
	great terebinth, his hair got caught in the terebinth; he was held
	between heaven and earth as the mule under him kept going.
}

\eJ{
	One of the men saw it and told Joab, “I have just seen Absalom
	hanging from a terebinth.”
}

\eJ{
	Joab said to the man who told him, “You saw it! Why didn’t you
	kill him then and there? I would have owed you ten shekels of
	silver and a belt.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר הָאִישׁ אֶל־יוֹאָב (ולא) [וְלוּ] אָנֹכִי שֹׁקֵל עַל־כַּפַּי אֶלֶף כֶּסֶף לֹא־אֶשְׁלַח יָדִי אֶל־בֶּן־הַמֶּלֶךְ כִּי בְאזְנֵינוּ צִוָּה הַמֶּלֶךְ אֹתְךָ וְאֶת־אֲבִישַׁי
	וְאֶת־אִתַּי לֵאמֹר שִׁמְרוּ־מִי בַּנַּעַר בְּאַבְשָׁלוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But the man answered Joab, “Even if I had a thousand shekels of
	silver in my hands, I would not raise a hand against the king’s
	son. For the king charged you and Abishai and Ittai in our
	hearing, ‘Watch over my boy Absalom, for my sake.’
}

\eJ{
	If I betrayed myself—and nothing is hidden from the king—you would
	have stood aloof.”
}

\eJ{
	Joab replied, “Then I will not wait for you.” He took three darts
	in his hand and drove them into Absalom’s chest. [Absalom] was
	still alive in the thick growth of the terebinth,
}

\eJ{
	when ten of Joab’s young arms-bearers closed in and struck at
	Absalom until he died.
}

\eJ{
	Then Joab sounded the horn, and the troops gave up their pursuit
	of the Israelites; for Joab held the troops in check.
}

\eJ{
	They took Absalom and flung him into a large pit in the forest,
	and they piled up a very great heap of stones over it. Then all
	the Israelites fled to their homes.—
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְאַבְשָׁלֹם לָקַח וַיַּצֶּב־לוֹ בְחַיָּו אֶת־מַצֶּבֶת אֲשֶׁר בְּעֵמֶק־הַמֶּלֶךְ כִּי אָמַר אֵין־לִי בֵן בַּעֲבוּר הַזְכִּיר שְׁמִי וַיִּקְרָא לַמַּצֶּבֶת עַל־שְׁמוֹ וַיִּקָּרֵא
	לָהּ יַד אַבְשָׁלוֹם עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Now Absalom, in his lifetime, had taken the pillar which is in the
	Valley of the King and set it up for himself; for he said, “I have
	no son to keep my name alive.” He had named the pillar after
	himself, and it has been called Absalom’s Monument to this day.
}

\eJ{
	Ahimaaz son of Zadok said, “Let me run and report to the king that
	YHWH has vindicated him against his enemies.”
}

\eJ{
	But Joab said to him, “You shall not be the one to bring tidings
	today. You may bring tidings some other day, but you’ll not bring
	any today; for the king’s son is dead!”
}

\eJ{
	And Joab said to a Cushite, “Go tell the king what you have seen.”
	The Cushite bowed to Joab and ran off.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּסֶף עוֹד אֲחִימַעַץ בֶּן־צָדוֹק וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־יוֹאָב וִיהִי מָה אָרֻצָה־נָּא גַם־אָנִי אַחֲרֵי הַכּוּשִׁי וַיֹּאמֶר יוֹאָב לָמָּה־זֶּה אַתָּה רָץ בְּנִי וּלְכָה
	אֵין־בְּשׂוֹרָה מֹצֵאת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But Ahimaaz son of Zadok again said to Joab, “No matter what, let
	me run, too, behind the Cushite.” Joab asked, “Why should you run,
	my boy, when you have no news worth telling?”
}

\eJ{
	“I am going to run anyway.” “Then run,” he said. So Ahimaaz ran by
	way of the Plain, and he passed the Cushite.
}

\eJ{
	David was sitting between the two gates. The watchman on the roof
	of the gate walked over to the city wall. He looked up and saw a
	man running alone.
}

\eJ{
	The watchman called down and told the king; and the king said, “If
	he is alone, he has news to report.” As he was coming nearer,
}

\eJ{
	the watchman saw another man running; and he called out to the
	gatekeeper, “There is another man running alone.” And the king
	said, “That one, too, brings news.”
}

\eJ{
	The watchman said, “I can see that the first one runs like Ahimaaz
	son of Zadok”; to which the king replied, “He is a good man, and
	he comes with good news.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּקְרָא אֲחִימַעַץ וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ שָׁלוֹם וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ לַמֶּלֶךְ לְאַפָּיו אָרְצָהוַיֹּאמֶר בָּרוּךְ יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר סִגַּר אֶת־הָאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר־נָשְׂאוּ
	אֶת־יָדָם בַּאדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Ahimaaz called out and said to the king, “All is well!” He bowed
	low with his face to the ground and said, “Praised be YHWH your
	God, who has delivered up the men who raised their hand against my
	lord the king.”
}

\eJ{
	The king asked, “Is my boy Absalom safe?” And Ahimaaz answered, “I
	saw a large crowd when Your Majesty’s servant Joab was sending
	your servant off, but I don’t know what it was about.”
}

\eJ{
	The king said, “Step aside and stand over there”; he stepped aside
	and waited.
}

\eJ{
	Just then the Cushite came up; and the Cushite said, “Let my lord
	the king be informed that YHWH has vindicated you today against
	all who rebelled against you!”
}

\eJ{
	The king asked the Cushite, “Is my boy Absalom safe?” And the
	Cushite replied, “May the enemies of my lord the king and all who
	rose against you to do you harm fare like that young man!”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּרְגַּז הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיַּעַל עַל־עֲלִיַּת הַשַּׁעַר וַיֵּבְךְּ וְכֹה אָמַר בְּלֶכְתּוֹ בְּנִי אַבְשָׁלוֹם בְּנִי בְנִי אַבְשָׁלוֹם מִי־יִתֵּן מוּתִי אֲנִי תַחְתֶּיךָ אַבְשָׁלוֹם
	בְּנִי בְנִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The king was shaken. He went up to the upper chamber of the
	gateway and wept, moaning these words as he went, “My son Absalom!
	O my son, my son Absalom! If only I had died instead of you! O
	Absalom, my son, my son!”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 18:33. David's cry repeats 'my son' until
%		kingship is gone and only
%		fatherhood is left making noise.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joab was told that the king was weeping and mourning over Absalom.
}

\eJ{
	And the victory that day was turned into mourning for all the
	troops, for that day the troops heard that the king was grieving
	over his son.
}

\eJ{
	The troops stole into town that day like troops ashamed after
	running away in battle.
}

\eJ{
	The king covered his face and the king kept crying aloud, “O my
	son Absalom! O Absalom, my son, my son!”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹא יוֹאָב אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ הַבָּיִת וַיֹּאמֶר הֹבַשְׁתָּ הַיּוֹם אֶת־פְּנֵי כל־עֲבָדֶיךָ הַמְמַלְּטִים אֶת־נַפְשְׁךָ הַיּוֹם וְאֵת נֶפֶשׁ בָּנֶיךָ וּבְנֹתֶיךָ וְנֶפֶשׁ נָשֶׁיךָ
	וְנֶפֶשׁ פִּלַגְשֶׁיךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Joab came to the king in his quarters and said, “Today you have
	humiliated all your followers, who this day saved your life, and
	the lives of your sons and daughters, and the lives of your wives
	and concubines,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	לְאַהֲבָה אֶת־שֹׂנְאֶיךָ וְלִשְׂנֹא אֶת־אֹהֲבֶיךָ כִּי הִגַּדְתָּ הַיּוֹם כִּי אֵין לְךָ שָׂרִים וַעֲבָדִים כִּי יָדַעְתִּי הַיּוֹם כִּי (לא) [לוּ] אַבְשָׁלוֹם חַי וְכֻלָּנוּ
	הַיּוֹם מֵתִים כִּי־אָז יָשָׁר בְּעֵינֶיךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	by showing love for those who hate you and hate for those who love
	you. For you have made clear today that the officers and men mean
	nothing to you. I am sure that if Absalom were alive today and the
	rest of us dead, you would have preferred it.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְעַתָּה קוּם צֵא וְדַבֵּר עַל־לֵב עֲבָדֶיךָכִּי בַיהֹוָה נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי כִּי־אֵינְךָ יוֹצֵא אִם־יָלִין אִישׁ אִתְּךָ הַלַּיְלָה וְרָעָה לְךָ זֹאת מִכּל־הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר־בָּאָה
	עָלֶיךָ מִנְּעֻרֶיךָ עַד־עָתָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So the king arose and sat down in the gateway; and when all the
	troops were told that the king was sitting in the gateway, all the
	troops presented themselves to the king. Now the Israelites had
	fled to their homes.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיְהִי כל־הָעָם נָדוֹן בְּכל־שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֵאמֹר הַמֶּלֶךְ הִצִּילָנוּ מִכַּף אֹיְבֵינוּ וְהוּא מִלְּטָנוּ מִכַּף פְּלִשְׁתִּים וְעַתָּה בָּרַח מִן־הָאָרֶץ מֵעַל
	אַבְשָׁלוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	All the people throughout the tribes of Israel were arguing: Some
	said, “The king saved us from the hands of our enemies, and he
	delivered us from the hands of the Philistines; and just now he
	had to flee the country because of Absalom.
}

\eJ{
	But Absalom, whom we anointed over us, has died in battle; why
	then do you sit idle instead of escorting the king back?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד שָׁלַח אֶל־צָדוֹק וְאֶל־אֶבְיָתָר הַכֹּהֲנִים לֵאמֹר דַּבְּרוּ אֶל־זִקְנֵי יְהוּדָה לֵאמֹר לָמָּה תִהְיוּ אַחֲרֹנִים לְהָשִׁיב אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶל־בֵּיתוֹ וּדְבַר
	כּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל בָּא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶל־בֵּיתוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The talk of all Israel reached the king in his quarters. So King
	David sent this message to the priests Zadok and Abiathar: “Speak
	to the elders of Judah and say, ‘Why should you be the last to
	bring the king back to his palace?
}

\eJ{
	You are my kinsmen, my own flesh and blood! Why should you be the
	last to escort the king back?’
}

\eJ{
	And to Amasa say this, ‘You are my own flesh and blood. May God do
	thus and more to me if you do not become my army commander
	permanently in place of Joab!’”
}

\eJ{
	So [Amasa] swayed the hearts of all the Judites as one man; and
	they sent a message to the king: “Come back with all your
	followers.”
}

\eJ{
	The king started back and arrived at the Jordan; and the Judites
	went to Gilgal to meet the king and to conduct the king across the
	Jordan.
}

\eJ{
	Shimei son of Gera, the Benjaminite from Bahurim, hurried down
	with the Judites to meet King David,
}

\eJ{
	accompanied by a thousand Benjaminites. And Ziba, the servant of
	the House of Saul, together with his fifteen sons and twenty
	slaves, rushed down to the Jordan ahead of the king
}

\eJ{
	while the crossing was being made, to escort the king’s family
	over, and to do whatever he wished. Shimei son of Gera flung
	himself before the king as he was about to cross the Jordan.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אַל־יַחֲשָׁב־לִי אֲדֹנִי עָוֺן וְאַל־תִּזְכֹּר אֵת אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱוָה עַבְדְּךָ בַּיּוֹם אֲשֶׁר־יָצָא אֲדֹנִי־הַמֶּלֶךְ מִירוּשָׁלָ͏ִם לָשׂוּם הַמֶּלֶךְ
	אֶל־לִבּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	He said to the king, “Let not my lord hold me guilty, and do not
	remember the wrong your servant committed on the day my lord the
	king left Jerusalem; let Your Majesty give it no thought.
}

\eJ{
	For your servant knows that he has sinned; so here I have come
	down today, the first of all the House of Joseph, to meet my lord
	the king.”
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon Abishai son of Zeruiah spoke up, “Shouldn’t Shimei be
	put to death for that—insulting YHWH’s anointed?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר דָּוִד מַה־לִּי וְלָכֶם בְּנֵי צְרוּיָה כִּי־תִהְיוּ־לִי הַיּוֹם לְשָׂטָן הַיּוֹם יוּמַת אִישׁ בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי הֲלוֹא יָדַעְתִּי כִּי הַיּוֹם אֲנִי־מֶלֶךְ
	עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But David said, “What has this to do with you, you sons of
	Zeruiah, that you should cross me today? Should a single Israelite
	be put to death today? Don’t I know that today I am again king
	over Israel?”
}

\eJ{
	Then the king said to Shimei, “You shall not die”; and the king
	gave him his oath.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וּמְפִבֹשֶׁת בֶּן־שָׁאוּל יָרַד לִקְרַאת הַמֶּלֶךְ וְלֹא־עָשָׂה רַגְלָיו וְלֹא־עָשָׂה שְׂפָמוֹ וְאֶת־בְּגָדָיו לֹא כִבֵּס לְמִן־הַיּוֹם לֶכֶת הַמֶּלֶךְ עַד־הַיּוֹם אֲשֶׁר־בָּא
	בְשָׁלוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Mephibosheth, the grandson of Saul, also came down to meet the
	king. He had not pared his toenails, or trimmed his mustache, or
	washed his clothes from the day that the king left until the day
	he returned safe.
}

\eJ{
	When he came [from] Jerusalem to meet the king, the king asked
	him, “Why didn’t you come with me, Mephibosheth?”
}

\eJ{
	He replied, “My lord the king, my own servant deceived me. Your
	servant planned to saddle his ass and ride on it and go with Your
	Majesty—for your servant is lame.
}

\eJ{
	[Ziba] has slandered your servant to my lord the king. But my lord
	the king is like an angel of YHWH; do as you see fit.
}

\eJ{
	For all the members of my father’s family deserved only death from
	my lord the king; yet you set your servant among those who ate at
	your table. What right have I to appeal further to Your Majesty?”
}

\eJ{
	The king said to him, “You need not speak further. I decree that
	you and Ziba shall divide the property.”
}

\eJ{
	And Mephibosheth said to the king, “Let him take it all, as long
	as my lord the king has come home safe.”
}

\eJ{
	Barzillai the Gileadite had come down from Rogelin and passed on
	to the Jordan with the king, to see him off at the Jordan.
}

\eJ{
	Barzillai was very old, eighty years of age; and he had provided
	the king with food during his stay at Mahanaim, for he was a very
	wealthy man.
}

\eJ{
	The king said to Barzillai, “Cross over with me, and I will
	provide for you in Jerusalem at my side.”
}

\eJ{
	But Barzillai said to the king, “How many years are left to me
	that I should go up with Your Majesty to Jerusalem?
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	בֶּן־שְׁמֹנִים שָׁנָה אָנֹכִי הַיּוֹם הַאֵדַע בֵּין־טוֹב לְרָע אִם־יִטְעַם עַבְדְּךָ אֶת־אֲשֶׁר אֹכַל וְאֶת־אֲשֶׁר אֶשְׁתֶּה אִם־אֶשְׁמַע עוֹד בְּקוֹל שָׁרִים וְשָׁרוֹת וְלָמָּה
	יִהְיֶה עַבְדְּךָ עוֹד לְמַשָּׂא אֶל־אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	I am now eighty years old. Can I tell the difference between good
	and bad? Can your servant taste what he eats and drinks? Can I
	still listen to the singing of men and women? Why then should your
	servant continue to be a burden to my lord the king?
}

\eJ{
	Your servant could barely cross the Jordan with your Majesty! Why
	should Your Majesty reward me so generously?
}

\eJ{
	Let your servant go back, and let me die in my own town, near the
	graves of my father and mother. But here is your servant Chimham;
	let him cross with my lord the king, and do for him as you see
	fit.”
}

\eJ{
	And the king said, “Chimham shall cross with me, and I will do for
	him as you see fit; and anything you want me to do, I will do for
	you.”
}

\eJ{
	All the troops crossed the Jordan; and when the king was ready to
	cross, the king kissed Barzillai and bade him farewell; and
	[Barzillai] returned to his home.
}

\eJ{
	The king passed on to Gilgal, with Chimham accompanying him; and
	all the Judite soldiers and part of the Israelite army escorted
	the king across.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהִנֵּה כּל־אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל בָּאִים אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ מַדּוּעַ גְּנָבוּךָ אַחֵינוּ אִישׁ יְהוּדָה וַיַּעֲבִרוּ אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ וְאֶת־בֵּיתוֹ אֶת־הַיַּרְדֵּן
	וְכל־אַנְשֵׁי דָוִד עִמּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then all the men of Israel came to the king and said to the king,
	“Why did our kinsmen, the men of Judah, steal you away and escort
	the king and his family across the Jordan, along with all David’s
	men?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַן כּל־אִישׁ יְהוּדָה עַל־אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי־קָרוֹב הַמֶּלֶךְ אֵלַי וְלָמָּה זֶּה חָרָה לְךָ עַל־הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה הֶאָכוֹל אָכַלְנוּ מִן־הַמֶּלֶךְ אִם־נִשֵּׂאת נִשָּׂא
	לָנוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	All the men of Judah replied to the men of Israel, “Because the
	king is our relative! Why should this upset you? Have we consumed
	anything that belongs to the king? Has he given us any gifts?”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַן אִישׁ־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־אִישׁ יְהוּדָה וַיֹּאמֶר עֶשֶׂר־יָדוֹת לִי בַמֶּלֶךְ וְגַם־בְּדָוִד אֲנִי מִמְּךָ וּמַדּוּעַ הֱקִלֹּתַנִי וְלֹא־הָיָה דְבָרִי רִאשׁוֹן לִי לְהָשִׁיב
	אֶת־מַלְכִּי וַיִּקֶשׁ דְּבַר־אִישׁ יְהוּדָה מִדְּבַר אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

\hJ{
	וְשָׁם נִקְרָא אִישׁ בְּלִיַּעַל וּשְׁמוֹ שֶׁבַע בֶּן־בִּכְרִי אִישׁ יְמִינִי וַיִּתְקַע בַּשֹּׁפָר וַיֹּאמֶר אֵין־לָנוּ חֵלֶק בְּדָוִד וְלֹא נַחֲלָה־לָנוּ בְּבֶן־יִשַׁי אִישׁ
	לְאֹהָלָיו יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	A scoundrel named Sheba son of Bichri, a Benjaminite, happened to
	be there. He sounded the horn and proclaimed: “We have no portion
	in David, No share in Jesse’s son! Every man to his tent, O
	Israel!”
}

\eJ{
	All the men of Israel left David and followed Sheba son of Bichri;
	but the men of Judah accompanied their king from the Jordan to
	Jerusalem.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹא דָוִד אֶל־בֵּיתוֹ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וַיִּקַּח הַמֶּלֶךְ אֵת עֶשֶׂר־נָשִׁים פִּלַגְשִׁים אֲשֶׁר הִנִּיחַ לִשְׁמֹר הַבַּיִת וַיִּתְּנֵם בֵּית־מִשְׁמֶרֶת וַיְכַלְכְּלֵם
	וַאֲלֵיהֶם לֹא־בָא וַתִּהְיֶינָה צְרֻרוֹת עַד־יוֹם מֻתָן אַלְמְנוּת חַיּוּת׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The king said to Amasa, “Call up the men of Judah to my standard,
	and report here three days from now.”
}

\eJ{
	Amasa went to call up Judah, but he took longer than the time set
	for him.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֹּאמֶר דָּוִד אֶל־אֲבִישַׁי עַתָּה יֵרַע לָנוּ שֶׁבַע בֶּן־בִּכְרִי מִן־אַבְשָׁלוֹם אַתָּה קַח אֶת־עַבְדֵי אֲדֹנֶיךָ וּרְדֹף אַחֲרָיו פֶּן־מָצָא לוֹ עָרִים בְּצֻרוֹת וְהִצִּיל
	עֵינֵנוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	And David said to Abishai, “Now Sheba son of Bichri will cause us
	more trouble than Absalom. So take your lord’s servants and pursue
	him, before he finds fortified towns and eludes us.”
}

\eJ{
	Joab’s men, the Cherethites and Pelethites, and all the warriors,
	marched out behind him. They left Jerusalem in pursuit of Sheba
	son of Bichri.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	הֵם עִם־הָאֶבֶן הַגְּדוֹלָה אֲשֶׁר בְּגִבְעוֹן וַעֲמָשָׂא בָּא לִפְנֵיהֶם וְיוֹאָב חָגוּר מִדּוֹ לְבֻשׁוֹ וְעָלָו חֲגוֹר חֶרֶב מְצֻמֶּדֶת עַל־מתְנָיו בְּתַעְרָהּ וְהוּא יָצָא
	וַתִּפֹּל׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	They were near the great stone in Gibeon when Amasa appeared
	before them. Joab was wearing his military dress, with his sword
	girded over it and fastened around his waist in its sheath; and,
	as he stepped forward, it fell out.
}

\eJ{
	Joab said to Amasa, “How are you, brother?” and with his right
	hand Joab took hold of Amasa’s beard as if to kiss him.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַעֲמָשָׂא לֹא־נִשְׁמַר בַּחֶרֶב אֲשֶׁר בְּיַד־יוֹאָב וַיַּכֵּהוּ בָהּ אֶל־הַחֹמֶשׁ וַיִּשְׁפֹּךְ מֵעָיו אַרְצָה וְלֹא־שָׁנָה לוֹ וַיָּמֹת וְיוֹאָב וַאֲבִישַׁי אָחִיו רָדַף אַחֲרֵי
	שֶׁבַע בֶּן־בִּכְרִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	while one of Joab’s henchmen stood by the corpse and called out,
	“Whoever favors Joab, and whoever is on David’s side, follow
	Joab!”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַעֲמָשָׂא מִתְגֹּלֵל בַּדָּם בְּתוֹךְ הַמְסִלָּה וַיַּרְא הָאִישׁ כִּי־עָמַד כּל־הָעָם וַיַּסֵּב אֶת־עֲמָשָׂא מִן־הַמְסִלָּה הַשָּׂדֶה וַיַּשְׁלֵךְ עָלָיו בֶּגֶד כַּאֲשֶׁר רָאָה
	כּל־הַבָּא עָלָיו וְעָמָד׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Amasa lay in the middle of the road, drenched in his blood; and
	the man saw that everyone stopped. And when he saw that all the
	people were stopping, he dragged Amasa from the road into the
	field and covered him with a garment.
}

\eJ{
	Once he was removed from the road, everybody continued to follow
	Joab in pursuit of Sheba son of Bichri.
}

\eJ{
	[Sheba] had passed through all the tribes of Israel up to Abel of
	Beth-maacah; and all the Beerites assembled and followed him
	inside.
}

\eJ{
	[Joab’s men] came and besieged him in Abel of Beth-maacah; they
	threw up a siegemound against the city and it stood against the
	rampart. All the troops with Joab were engaged in battering the
	wall,
}

\eJ{
	when a clever woman shouted from the city, “Listen! Listen! Tell
	Joab to come over here so I can talk to him.”
}

\eJ{
	He approached her, and the woman asked, “Are you Joab?” “Yes,” he
	answered; and she said to him, “Listen to what your handmaid has
	to say.” “I’m listening,” he replied.
}

\eJ{
	And she continued, “In olden times people used to say, ‘Let them
	inquire of Abel,’ and that was the end of the matter.
}

\eJ{
	I am one of those who seek the welfare of the faithful in Israel.
	But you seek to bring death upon a mother city in Israel! Why
	should you destroy YHWH’s possession?”
}

\eJ{
	Joab replied, “Far be it, far be it from me to destroy or to ruin!
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	לֹא־כֵן הַדָּבָר כִּי אִישׁ מֵהַר אֶפְרַיִם שֶׁבַע בֶּן־בִּכְרִי שְׁמוֹ נָשָׂא יָדוֹ בַּמֶּלֶךְ בְּדָוִד תְּנוּ־אֹתוֹ לְבַדּוֹ וְאֵלְכָה מֵעַל הָעִיר וַתֹּאמֶר הָאִשָּׁה אֶל־יוֹאָב
	הִנֵּה רֹאשׁוֹ מֻשְׁלָךְ אֵלֶיךָ בְּעַד הַחוֹמָה׃
}

\hJ{
	וַתָּבוֹא הָאִשָּׁה אֶל־כּל־הָעָם בְּחכְמָתָהּ וַיִּכְרְתוּ אֶת־רֹאשׁ שֶׁבַע בֶּן־בִּכְרִי וַיַּשְׁלִכוּ אֶל־יוֹאָב וַיִּתְקַע בַּשֹּׁפָר וַיָּפֻצוּ מֵעַל־הָעִיר אִישׁ לְאֹהָלָיו
	וְיוֹאָב שָׁב יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The woman came to all the people with her clever plan; and they
	cut off the head of Sheba son of Bichri and threw it down to Joab.
	He then sounded the horn; all the men dispersed to their homes,
	and Joab returned to the king in Jerusalem.
}

\eJ{
	Joab was commander of the whole army [of] Israel; Benaiah son of
	Jehoiada was commander of the Cherethites and the Pelethites;
}

\eX{
	Adoram was in charge of forced labor; Jehoshaphat son of Ahilud
	was recorder;
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי רָעָב בִּימֵי דָוִד שָׁלֹשׁ שָׁנִים שָׁנָה אַחֲרֵי שָׁנָה וַיְבַקֵּשׁ דָּוִד אֶת־פְּנֵי יְהֹוָהוַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֶל־שָׁאוּל וְאֶל־בֵּית הַדָּמִים עַל אֲשֶׁר־הֵמִית
	אֶת־הַגִּבְעֹנִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	There was a famine during the reign of David, year after year for
	three years. David inquired of YHWH, and YHWH replied, “It is
	because of the bloodguilt of Saul and [his] house, for he put some
	Gibeonites to death.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְרָא הַמֶּלֶךְ לַגִּבְעֹנִים וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם וְהַגִּבְעֹנִים לֹא מִבְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל הֵמָּה כִּי אִם־מִיֶּתֶר הָאֱמֹרִי וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל נִשְׁבְּעוּ לָהֶם וַיְבַקֵּשׁ
	שָׁאוּל לְהַכֹּתָם בְּקַנֹּאתוֹ לִבְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל וִיהוּדָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	David asked the Gibeonites, “What shall I do for you? How shall I
	make expiation, so that you may bless YHWH’s own people?”
}

\eX{
	The Gibeonites answered him, “We have no claim for silver or gold
	against Saul and his household; and we have no claim on the life
	of any other man in Israel.” And [David] responded, “Whatever you
	say I will do for you.”
}

\eX{
	Thereupon they said to the king, “The man who massacred us and
	planned to exterminate us, so that we should not survive in all
	the territory of Israel—
}

\eX{
	let seven of his male issue be handed over to us, and we will
	impale them before YHWH in Gibeah of Saul, the chosen of YHWH.”
	And the king replied, “I will do so.”
}

\eX{
	The king spared Mephibosheth son of Jonathan son of Saul, because
	of the oath before YHWH between the two, between David and
	Jonathan son of Saul.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־שְׁנֵי בְּנֵי רִצְפָּה בַת־אַיָּה אֲשֶׁר יָלְדָה לְשָׁאוּל אֶת־אַרְמֹנִי וְאֶת־מְפִבֹשֶׁת וְאֶת־חֲמֵשֶׁת בְּנֵי מִיכַל בַּת־שָׁאוּל אֲשֶׁר יָלְדָה
	לְעַדְרִיאֵל בֶּן־בַּרְזִלַּי הַמְּחֹלָתִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Instead, the king took Armoni and Mephibosheth, the two sons that
	Rizpah daughter of Aiah bore to Saul, and the five sons that Merab
	daughter of Saul bore to Adriel son of Barzillai the Meholathite,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּתְּנֵם בְּיַד הַגִּבְעֹנִים וַיֹּקִיעֻם בָּהָר לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה וַיִּפְּלוּ (שבעתים) [שְׁבַעְתָּם] יָחַד (והם) [וְהֵמָּה] הֻמְתוּ בִּימֵי קָצִיר בָּרִאשֹׁנִים (תחלת)
	[בִּתְחִלַּת] קְצִיר שְׂעֹרִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	and he handed them over to the Gibeonites. They impaled them on
	the mountain before YHWH; all seven of them perished at the same
	time. They were put to death in the first days of the harvest, the
	beginning of the barley harvest.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתִּקַּח רִצְפָּה בַת־אַיָּה אֶת־הַשַּׂק וַתַּטֵּהוּ לָהּ אֶל־הַצּוּר מִתְּחִלַּת קָצִיר עַד נִתַּךְ־מַיִם עֲלֵיהֶם מִן־הַשָּׁמָיִם וְלֹא־נָתְנָה עוֹף הַשָּׁמַיִם לָנוּחַ עֲלֵיהֶם
	יוֹמָם וְאֶת־חַיַּת הַשָּׂדֶה לָיְלָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	David was told what Saul’s concubine Rizpah daughter of Aiah had
	done.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ דָּוִד וַיִּקַּח אֶת־עַצְמוֹת שָׁאוּל וְאֶת־עַצְמוֹת יְהוֹנָתָן בְּנוֹ מֵאֵת בַּעֲלֵי יָבֵישׁ גִּלְעָד אֲשֶׁר גָּנְבוּ אֹתָם מֵרְחֹב בֵּית־שַׁן אֲשֶׁר (תלום שם הפלשתים)
	[תְּלָאוּם שָׁמָּה פְּלִשְׁתִּים] בְּיוֹם הַכּוֹת פְּלִשְׁתִּים אֶת־שָׁאוּל בַּגִּלְבֹּעַ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And David went and took the bones of Saul and of his son Jonathan
	from the citizens of Jabesh-gilead, who had made off with them
	from the public square of Beth-shan, where the Philistines had
	hung them up on the day the Philistines killed Saul at Gilboa.
}

\eX{
	He brought up the bones of Saul and of his son Jonathan from
	there; and he gathered the bones of those who had been impaled.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְבְּרוּ אֶת־עַצְמוֹת־שָׁאוּל וִיהוֹנָתָן־בְּנוֹ בְּאֶרֶץ בִּנְיָמִן בְּצֵלָע בְּקֶבֶר קִישׁ אָבִיו וַיַּעֲשׂוּ כֹּל אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֵּעָתֵר אֱלֹהִים לָאָרֶץ
	אַחֲרֵי־כֵן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And they buried the bones of Saul and of his son Jonathan in Zela,
	in the territory of Benjamin, in the tomb of his father Kish. And
	when all that the king had commanded was done, God responded to
	the plea of the land thereafter.
}

\eX{
	Again war broke out between the Philistines and Israel, and David
	and the men with him went down and fought the Philistines; David
	grew weary,
}

\eX{
	and Ishbi-benob tried to kill David.—He was a descendant of the
	Raphah; his bronze spear weighed three hundred shekels and he wore
	new armor.—
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעֲזר־לוֹ אֲבִישַׁי בֶּן־צְרוּיָה וַיַּךְ אֶת־הַפְּלִשְׁתִּי וַיְמִתֵהוּ אָז נִשְׁבְּעוּ אַנְשֵׁי־דָוִד לוֹ לֵאמֹר לֹא־תֵצֵא עוֹד אִתָּנוּ לַמִּלְחָמָה וְלֹא תְכַבֶּה אֶת־נֵר
	יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But Abishai son of Zeruiah came to his aid; he attacked the
	Philistine and killed him. It was then that David’s men declared
	to him on oath, “You shall not go with us into battle any more,
	lest you extinguish the lamp of Israel!”
}

\eX{
	After this, fighting broke out again with the Philistines, at Gob;
	that was when Sibbecai the Hushathite killed Saph, a descendant of
	the Raphah.
}

\eX{
	Again there was fighting with the Philistines at Gob; and Elhanan
	son of Jaare-oregim the Bethlehemite killed Goliath the Gittite,
	whose spear had a shaft like a weaver’s bar.
}

\eX{
	Once again there was fighting, at Gath. There was a giant of a
	man, who had six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot,
	twenty-four in all; he too was descended from the Raphah.
}

\eX{
	When he taunted Israel, Jonathan, the son of David’s brother
	Shimei, killed him.
}

\eX{
	Those four were descended from the Raphah in Gath, and they fell
	by the hands of David and his men.
}

\eDtrH{
	David addressed the words of this song to YHWH, after YHWH had
	saved him from the hands of all his enemies and from the hands of
	Saul.%
}

\eDtrH{
	O God, the rock wherein I take shelter: My shield, my mighty
	champion, my fortress and refuge! My savior, You who rescue me
	from violence!%
}

\eDtrH{
	All praise! I called on YHWH, And I was delivered from my enemies.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For the breakers of Death encompassed me, The torrents of Belial
	terrified me;%
}

\eDtrH{
	The snares of Sheol encircled me, The coils of Death engulfed me.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In my anguish I called on YHWH, Cried out to my God; In His Abode
	He heard my voice, My cry entered His ears.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then the earth rocked and quaked, The foundations of heaven shook—
	Rocked by His indignation.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Smoke went up from His nostrils, From His mouth came devouring
	fire; Live coals blazed forth from Him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He bent the sky and came down, Thick cloud beneath His feet.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He mounted a cherub and flew; He was seen on the wings of the
	wind.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He made pavilions of darkness about Him, Dripping clouds, huge
	thunderheads;%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH thundered forth from heaven, The Most High sent forth His
	voice;%
}

\eDtrH{
	He let loose bolts, and scattered them; Lightning, and put them to
	rout.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The bed of the sea was exposed, The foundations of the world were
	laid bare By the mighty roaring of YHWH, At the blast of the
	breath of His nostrils.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He reached down from on high, He took me, Drew me out of the
	mighty waters;%
}

\eDtrH{
	He rescued me from my enemy so strong, From foes too mighty for
	me.%
}

\eDtrH{
	They attacked me on my day of calamity, But YHWH was my stay.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He brought me out to freedom, He rescued me because He was pleased
	with me.%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH rewarded me according to my merit, He requited the cleanness
	of my hands.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For I have kept the ways of YHWH And have not been guilty before
	my God;%
}

\eDtrH{
	I am mindful of all His rules And have not departed from His laws.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I have been blameless before Him, And have guarded myself against
	sinning—%
}

\eDtrH{
	And YHWH has requited my merit, According to my purity in His
	sight.%
}

\eDtrH{
	With the loyal You deal loyally; With the blameless hero,
	blamelessly.%
}

\eDtrH{
	With the pure You act in purity, And with the perverse You are
	wily.%
}

\eDtrH{
	To humble folk You give victory, And You look with scorn on the
	haughty.%
}

\eDtrH{
	With You, I can rush a barrier, With my God, I can scale a wall.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The way of God is perfect, The word of YHWH is pure. He is a
	shield to all who take refuge in Him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Who made my legs like a deer’s, And set me firm on the heights;%
}

\eDtrH{
	Who trained my hands for battle, So that my arms can bend a bow of
	bronze!%
}

\eDtrH{
	You have granted me the shield of Your protection And Your
	providence has made me great.%
}

\eDtrH{
	You have let me stride on freely, And my feet have not slipped.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I pursued my enemies and wiped them out, I did not turn back till
	I destroyed them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I destroyed them, I struck them down; They rose no more, they lay
	at my feet.%
}

\eDtrH{
	You have girt me with strength for battle, Brought low my foes
	before me,%
}

\eDtrH{
	Made my enemies turn tail before me, My foes—and I wiped them out.%
}

\eDtrH{
	They looked, but there was none to deliver; To YHWH, but He
	answered them not.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I pounded them like dust of the earth, Stamped, crushed them like
	dirt of the streets.%
}

\eDtrH{
	You have rescued me from the strife of peoples, Kept me to be a
	ruler of nations; Peoples I knew not must serve me.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Aliens have cringed before me, Paid me homage at the mere report
	of me.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Aliens have lost courage And come trembling out of their
	fastnesses.%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH lives! Blessed is my rock! Exalted be God, the rock Who gives
	me victory;%
}

\eDtrH{
	The God who has vindicated me And made peoples subject to me,%
}

\eDtrH{
	Rescued me from my enemies, Raised me clear of my foes, Saved me
	from lawless men!%
}

\eDtrH{
	For this I sing Your praise among the nations And hymn Your name:%
}

\eDtrH{
	Tower of victory to His king, Who deals graciously with His
	anointed, With David and his offspring evermore.%
}

\eDtrH{
	These are the last words of David: The utterance of David son of
	Jesse, The utterance of the man set on high, The anointed of the
	God of Jacob, The favorite of the songs of Israel:%
}

\eDtrH{
	The spirit of YHWH has spoken through me, His message is on my
	tongue;%
}

\eDtrH{
	The God of Israel has spoken, The Rock of Israel said concerning
	me: “He who rules men justly, He who rules in awe of God%
}

\eDtrH{
	Is like the light of morning at sunrise, A morning without clouds—
	Through sunshine and rain [Bringing] vegetation out of the earth.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Is not my House established before God? For He has granted me an
	eternal pact, Drawn up in full and secured. Will He not cause all
	my success And [my] every desire to blossom?%
}

\eDtrH{
	But the wicked shall all Be raked aside like thorns; For no one
	will take them in his hand.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Whoever touches them Must arm himself with iron And the shaft of a
	spear; And they must be burned up on the spot.%
}

\eX{
	These are the names of David’s warriors: Josheb-basshebeth, a
	Tahchemonite, the chief officer—he is Adino the Eznite; [he
	wielded his spear] against eight hundred and slew them on one
	occasion.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאַחֲרָו אֶלְעָזָר בֶּן־[דֹּדוֹ] (דדי) בֶּן־אֲחֹחִי בִּשְׁלֹשָׁה (גברים) [הַגִּבֹּרִים] עִם־דָּוִד בְּחָרְפָם בַּפְּלִשְׁתִּים נֶאֶסְפוּ־שָׁם לַמִּלְחָמָה וַיַּעֲלוּ אִישׁ
	יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Next to him was Eleazar son of Dodo son of Ahohi. He was one of
	the three warriors with David when they defied the Philistines
	gathered there for battle. The Israelite soldiers retreated,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	הוּא קָם וַיַּךְ בַּפְּלִשְׁתִּים עַד כִּי־יָגְעָה יָדוֹ וַתִּדְבַּק יָדוֹ אֶל־הַחֶרֶב וַיַּעַשׂ יְהֹוָה תְּשׁוּעָה גְדוֹלָה בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא וְהָעָם יָשֻׁבוּ אַחֲרָיו
	אַךְ־לְפַשֵּׁט׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	but he held his ground. He struck down Philistines until his arm
	grew tired and his hand stuck to his sword; and YHWH wrought a
	great victory that day. Then the troops came back to him—but only
	to strip [the slain].
}

\eX{
	Next to him was Shammah son of Age the Ararite. The Philistines
	had gathered in force where there was a plot of ground full of
	lentils; and the troops fled from the Philistines.
}

\eX{
	But [Shammah] took his stand in the middle of the plot and
	defended it, and he routed the Philistines. Thus YHWH wrought a
	great victory.
}

\eX{
	Once, during the harvest, three of the thirty chiefs went down to
	David at the cave of Adullam, while a force of Philistines was
	encamped in the Valley of Rephaim.
}

\eX{
	David was then in the stronghold, and a Philistine garrison was
	then at Bethlehem.
}

\eX{
	David felt a craving and said, “If only I could get a drink of
	water from the cistern which is by the gate of Bethlehem!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּבְקְעוּ שְׁלֹשֶׁת הַגִּבֹּרִים בְּמַחֲנֵה פְלִשְׁתִּים וַיִּשְׁאֲבוּ־מַיִם מִבֹּאר בֵּית־לֶחֶם אֲשֶׁר בַּשַּׁעַר וַיִּשְׂאוּ וַיָּבִאוּ אֶל־דָּוִד וְלֹא אָבָה לִשְׁתּוֹתָם
	וַיַּסֵּךְ אֹתָם לַיהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So the three warriors got through the Philistine camp and drew
	water from the cistern which is by the gate of Bethlehem, and they
	carried it back. But when they brought it to David he would not
	drink it, and he poured it out as a libation to YHWH.
}

\eX{
	For he said, “YHWH forbid that I should do this! Can [I drink] the
	blood of the men who went at the risk of their lives?” So he would
	not drink it. Such were the exploits of the three warriors.
}

\eX{
	Abishai, the brother of Joab son of Zeruiah, was head of another
	three. He once wielded his spear against three hundred and slew
	them.
}

\eX{
	He won a name among the three; since he was the most highly
	regarded among the three, he became their leader. However, he did
	not attain to the three.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּבְנָיָהוּ בֶן־יְהוֹיָדָע בֶּן־אִישׁ־[חַיִל] (חי) רַב־פְּעָלִים מִקַּבְצְאֵל הוּא הִכָּה אֵת שְׁנֵי אֲרִאֵל מוֹאָב וְהוּא יָרַד וְהִכָּה אֶת־[הָאֲרִי] (האריה) בְּתוֹךְ הַבֹּאר
	בְּיוֹם הַשָּׁלֶג׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Benaiah son of Jehoiada, from Kabzeel, was a brave soldier who
	performed great deeds. He killed the two [sons] of Ariel of Moab.
	Once, on a snowy day, he went down into a pit and killed a lion.
}

\eX{
	He also killed an Egyptian, a huge man. The Egyptian had a spear
	in his hand, yet [Benaiah] went down against him with a club,
	wrenched the spear out of the Egyptian’s hand, and killed him with
	his own spear.
}

\eX{
	Such were the exploits of Benaiah son of Jehoiada; and he won a
	name among the three warriors.
}

\eX{
	He was highly regarded among the thirty, but he did not attain to
	the three. David put him in charge of his bodyguard.
}

\eX{
	Among the thirty were Asahel, the brother of Joab; Elhanan son of
	Dodo [from] Bethlehem,
}

\eX{
	Heleb son of Baanah the Netophathite, Ittai son of Ribai from
	Gibeah of the Benjaminites,
}

\eX{
	Eliphelet son of Ahasbai son of the Maacathite, Eliam son of
	Ahithophel the Gilonite,
}

\eX{
	Zelek the Ammonite, Naharai the Beerothite—the arms-bearer of Joab
	son of Zeruiah—
}

\eX{
	The anger of YHWH again flared up against Israel; and He incited
	David against them, saying, “Go and number Israel and Judah.”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 24:1. The anger of YHWH moves David, but the
%		cost falls on Israel. The
%		theology is not smoothed out; it is left with sharp
%		edges.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The king said to Joab, his army commander, “Make the rounds of all
	the tribes of Israel, from Dan to Beer-sheba, and take a census of
	the people, so that I may know the size of the population.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יוֹאָב אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וְיוֹסֵף יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֶל־הָעָם כָּהֵם וְכָהֵם מֵאָה פְעָמִים וְעֵינֵי אֲדֹנִי־הַמֶּלֶךְ רֹאוֹת וַאדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ לָמָּה חָפֵץ בַּדָּבָר
	הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Joab answered the king, “May YHWH your God increase the number of
	the people a hundredfold, while your own eyes see it! But why
	should my lord king want this?”
}

\eX{
	However, the king’s command to Joab and to the officers of the
	army remained firm; and Joab and the officers of the army set out,
	at the instance of the king, to take a census of the people of
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	They crossed the Jordan and encamped at Aroer, on the right side
	of the town, which is in the middle of the wadi of Gad, and [went
	on] to Jazer.
}

\eX{
	They continued to Gilead and to the region of Tahtim-hodshi, and
	they came to Dan-jaan and around to Sidon.
}

\eX{
	They went onto the fortress of Tyre and all the towns of the
	Hivites and Canaanites, and finished at Beer-sheba in southern
	Judah.
}

\eX{
	They traversed the whole country, and then they came back to
	Jerusalem at the end of nine months and twenty days.
}

\eX{
	Joab reported to the king the number of the people that had been
	recorded: in Israel there were 800,000 soldiers ready to draw the
	sword, and the men of Judah numbered 500,000.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּךְ לֵב־דָּוִד אֹתוֹ אַחֲרֵי־כֵן סָפַר אֶת־הָעָםוַיֹּאמֶר דָּוִד אֶל־יְהֹוָה חָטָאתִי מְאֹד אֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי וְעַתָּה יְהֹוָה הַעֲבֶר־נָא אֶת־עֲוֺן עַבְדְּךָ כִּי נִסְכַּלְתִּי
	מְאֹד׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But afterward David reproached himself for having numbered the
	people. And David said to YHWH, “I have sinned grievously in what
	I have done. Please, O LORD, remit the guilt of Your servant, for
	I have acted foolishly.”
}

\eX{
	When David rose in the morning, the word of YHWH had come to the
	prophet Gad, David’s seer:
}

\eX{
	“Go and tell David, ‘Thus said YHWH: I hold three things over you;
	choose one of them, and I will bring it upon you.’”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹא־גָד אֶל־דָּוִד וַיַּגֶּד־לוֹ וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ הֲתָבוֹא לְךָ שֶׁבַע־שָׁנִים רָעָב בְּאַרְצֶךָ אִם־שְׁלֹשָׁה חֳדָשִׁים נֻסְךָ לִפְנֵי־צָרֶיךָ וְהוּא רֹדְפֶךָ וְאִם־הֱיוֹת
	שְׁלֹשֶׁת יָמִים דֶּבֶר בְּאַרְצֶךָ עַתָּה דַּע וּרְאֵה מָה־אָשִׁיב שֹׁלְחִי דָּבָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	David said to Gad, “I am in great distress. Let us fall into the
	hands of YHWH, for His compassion is great; and let me not fall
	into the hands of men.”
}

\eX{
	YHWH sent a pestilence upon Israel from morning until the set
	time; and 70,000 of the people died, from Dan to Beer-sheba.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יָדוֹ הַמַּלְאָךְ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם לְשַׁחֲתָהּ וַיִּנָּחֶם יְהֹוָה אֶל־הָרָעָה וַיֹּאמֶר לַמַּלְאָךְ הַמַּשְׁחִית בָּעָם רַב עַתָּה הֶרֶף יָדֶךָ וּמַלְאַךְ יְהֹוָה הָיָה
	עִם־גֹּרֶן (האורנה) [הָאֲרַוְנָה] הַיְבֻסִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But when the angel extended his hand against Jerusalem to destroy
	it, YHWH renounced further punishment and said to the angel who
	was destroying the people, “Enough! Stay your hand!” The angel of
	YHWH was then by the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר דָּוִד אֶל־יְהֹוָה בִּרְאֹתוֹ אֶת־הַמַּלְאָךְ הַמַּכֶּה בָעָם וַיֹּאמֶר הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי חָטָאתִי וְאָנֹכִי הֶעֱוֵיתִי וְאֵלֶּה הַצֹּאן מֶה עָשׂוּ תְּהִי נָא יָדְךָ בִּי
	וּבְבֵית אָבִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When David saw the angel who was striking down the people, he said
	to YHWH, “I alone am guilty, I alone have done wrong; but these
	poor sheep, what have they done? Let Your hand fall upon me and my
	father’s house!”
}

\eX{
	Gad came to David the same day and said to him, “Go and set up an
	altar to YHWH on the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite.”
}

\eX{
	David went up, following Gad’s instructions, as YHWH had
	commanded.
}

\eX{
	Araunah looked out and saw the king and his courtiers approaching
	him. So Araunah went out and bowed low to the king, with his face
	to the ground.
}

\eX{
	And Araunah asked, “Why has my lord the king come to his servant?”
	David replied, “To buy the threshing floor from you, that I may
	build an altar to YHWH and that the plague against the people may
	be checked.”
}

\eX{
	And Araunah said to David, “Let my lord the king take it and offer
	up whatever he sees fit. Here are oxen for a burnt offering, and
	the threshing boards and the gear of the oxen for wood.
}

\eX{
	All this, O king, Araunah gives to Your Majesty. And may YHWH your
	God,” Araunah added, “respond to you with favor!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶל־אֲרַוְנָה לֹא כִּי־קָנוֹ אֶקְנֶה מֵאוֹתְךָ בִּמְחִיר וְלֹא אַעֲלֶה לַיהֹוָה אֱלֹהַי עֹלוֹת חִנָּם וַיִּקֶן דָּוִד אֶת־הַגֹּרֶן וְאֶת־הַבָּקָר בְּכֶסֶף
	שְׁקָלִים חֲמִשִּׁים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But the king replied to Araunah, “No, I will buy them from you at
	a price. I cannot sacrifice to YHWH my God burnt offerings that
	have cost me nothing.” So David bought the threshing floor and the
	oxen for fifty shekels of silver.
}

\eX{
	And David built there an altar to YHWH and sacrificed burnt
	offerings and offerings of well-being. YHWH responded to the plea
	for the land, and the plague against Israel was checked.
}

\eJ{
	King David was now old, advanced in years; and though they covered
	him with bedclothes, he never felt warm.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 1:1. Coming in days is the Hebrew old-age idiom. The body of David, so
%		hot in Samuel, is now a cold political fact.
%	}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	His courtiers said to him, “Let a young virgin be sought for my
	lord the king, to wait upon Your Majesty and be his attendant; and
	let her lie in your bosom, and my lord the king will be warm.”
}

\eJ{
	So they looked for a beautiful girl throughout the territory of
	Israel. They found Abishag the Shunammite and brought her to the
	king.
}

\eJ{
	The girl was exceedingly beautiful. She became the king’s
	attendant and waited upon him; but the king was not intimate with
	her.
}

\eJ{
	Now Adonijah son of Haggith went about boasting, “I will be king!”
	He provided himself with chariots and horses, and an escort of
	fifty outrunners.
}

\eJ{
	His father had never scolded him: “Why did you do that?” He was
	the one born after Absalom and, like him, was very handsome.
}

\eJ{
	He conferred with Joab son of Zeruiah and with the priest
	Abiathar, and they supported Adonijah;
}

\eJ{
	but the priest Zadok, Benaiah son of Jehoiada, the prophet Nathan,
	Shimei and Rei, and David’s own fighting men did not side with
	Adonijah.
}

\eJ{
	Adonijah made a sacrificial feast of sheep, oxen, and fatlings at
	the Zoheleth stone which is near En-rogel; he invited all his
	brother princes and all the king’s courtiers of the tribe of
	Judah;
}

\eJ{
	but he did not invite the prophet Nathan, or Benaiah, or the
	fighting men, or his brother Solomon.
}

\eJ{
	Then Nathan said to Bathsheba, Solomon’s mother, “You must have
	heard that Adonijah son of Haggith has assumed the kingship
	without the knowledge of our lord David.
}

\eJ{
	Now take my advice, so that you may save your life and the life of
	your son Solomon.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	לְכִי וּבֹאִי אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד וְאָמַרְתְּ אֵלָיו הֲלֹא־אַתָּה אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ נִשְׁבַּעְתָּ לַאֲמָתְךָ לֵאמֹר כִּי־שְׁלֹמֹה בְנֵךְ יִמְלֹךְ אַחֲרַי וְהוּא יֵשֵׁב
	עַל־כִּסְאִי וּמַדּוּעַ מָלַךְ אֲדֹנִיָּהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Go immediately to King David and say to him, ‘Did not you, O lord
	king, swear to your maidservant: “Your son Solomon shall succeed
	me as king, and he shall sit upon my throne”? Then why has
	Adonijah become king?’
}

\eJ{
	While you are still there talking with the king, I will come in
	after you and confirm your words.”
}

\eJ{
	So Bathsheba went to the king in his chamber.—The king was very
	old, and Abishag the Shunammite was waiting on the king.—
}

\eJ{
	Bathsheba bowed low in homage to the king; and the king asked,
	“What troubles you?”
}

\eJ{
	She answered him, “My lord, you yourself swore to your maidservant
	by YHWH your God: ‘Your son Solomon shall succeed me as king, and
	he shall sit upon my throne.’
}

\eJ{
	Yet now Adonijah has become king, and you, my lord the king, know
	nothing about it.
}

\eJ{
	He has prepared a sacrificial feast of a great many oxen,
	fatlings, and sheep, and he has invited all the king’s sons and
	Abiathar the priest and Joab commander of the army; but he has not
	invited your servant Solomon.
}

\eJ{
	And so the eyes of all Israel are upon you, O lord king, to tell
	them who shall succeed my lord the king on the throne.
}

\eJ{
	Otherwise, when my lord the king lies down with his fathers, my
	son Solomon and I will be regarded as traitors.”
}

\eJ{
	She was still talking to the king when the prophet Nathan arrived.
}

\eJ{
	They announced to the king, “The prophet Nathan is here,” and he
	entered the king’s presence. Bowing low to the king with his face
	to the ground,
}

\eJ{
	Nathan said, “O lord king, you must have said, ‘Adonijah shall
	succeed me as king and he shall sit upon my throne.’
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	כִּי יָרַד הַיּוֹם וַיִּזְבַּח שׁוֹר וּמְרִיא־וְצֹאן לָרֹב וַיִּקְרָא לְכל־בְּנֵי הַמֶּלֶךְ וּלְשָׂרֵי הַצָּבָא וּלְאֶבְיָתָר הַכֹּהֵן וְהִנָּם אֹכְלִים וְשֹׁתִים לְפָנָיו
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ יְחִי הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲדֹנִיָּהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	But he did not invite me your servant, or the priest Zadok, or
	Benaiah son of Jehoiada, or your servant Solomon.
}

\eJ{
	Can this decision have come from my lord the king, without your
	telling your servant who is to succeed to the throne of my lord
	the king?”
}

\eJ{
	King David’s response was: “Summon Bathsheba!” She entered the
	king’s presence and stood before the king.
}

\eJ{
	And the king took an oath, saying, “As YHWH lives, who has rescued
	me from every trouble:
}

\eJ{
	The oath I swore to you by YHWH, the God of Israel, that your son
	Solomon should succeed me as king and that he should sit upon my
	throne in my stead, I will fulfill this very day!”
}

\eJ{
	Bathsheba bowed low in homage to the king with her face to the
	ground, and she said, “May my lord King David live forever!”
}

\eJ{
	Then King David said, “Summon to me the priest Zadok, the prophet
	Nathan, and Benaiah son of Jehoiada.” When they came before the
	king,
}

\eJ{
	the king said to them, “Take my loyal soldiers, and have my son
	Solomon ride on my mule and bring him down to Gihon.
}

\eJ{
	Let the priest Zadok and the prophet Nathan anoint him there king
	over Israel, whereupon you shall sound the horn and shout, ‘Long
	live King Solomon!’
}

\eJ{
	Then march up after him, and let him come in and sit on my throne.
	For he shall succeed me as king; him I designate to be ruler of
	Israel and Judah.”
}

\eJ{
	Benaiah son of Jehoiada spoke up and said to the king, “Amen! And
	may YHWH, the God of my lord the king, so ordain.
}

\eJ{
	As YHWH was with my lord the king, so may He be with Solomon; and
	may He exalt his throne even higher than the throne of my lord
	King David.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֵּרֶד צָדוֹק הַכֹּהֵן וְנָתָן הַנָּבִיא וּבְנָיָהוּ בֶן־יְהוֹיָדָע וְהַכְּרֵתִי וְהַפְּלֵתִי וַיַּרְכִּבוּ אֶת־שְׁלֹמֹה עַל־פִּרְדַּת הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד וַיֹּלִכוּ אֹתוֹ
	עַל־גִּחוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Then the priest Zadok, and the prophet Nathan, and Benaiah son of
	Jehoiada went down with the Cherethites and the Pelethites. They
	had Solomon ride on King David’s mule and they led him to Gihon.
}

\eJ{
	The priest Zadok took the horn of oil from the Tent and anointed
	Solomon. They sounded the horn and all the people shouted, “Long
	live King Solomon!”
}

\eJ{
	All the people then marched up behind him, playing on flutes and
	making merry till the earth was split open by the uproar.
}

\eJ{
	Adonijah and all the guests who were with him, who had just
	finished eating, heard it. When Joab heard the sound of the horn,
	he said, “Why is the city in such an uproar?”
}

\eJ{
	He was still speaking when the priest Jonathan son of Abiathar
	arrived. “Come in,” said Adonijah. “You are a worthy man, and you
	surely bring good news.”
}

\eJ{
	But Jonathan replied to Adonijah, “Alas, our lord King David has
	made Solomon king!
}

\eJ{
	The king sent with him the priest Zadok and the prophet Nathan and
	Benaiah son of Jehoiada, and the Cherethites and Pelethites. They
	had him ride on the king’s mule,
}

\eJ{
	and the priest Zadok and the prophet Nathan anointed him king at
	Gihon. Then they came up from there making merry, and the city
	went into an uproar. That’s the noise you heard.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְגַם־בָּאוּ עַבְדֵי הַמֶּלֶךְ לְבָרֵךְ אֶת־אֲדֹנֵינוּ הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד לֵאמֹר יֵיטֵב (אלהיך) [אֱלֹהִים] אֶת־שֵׁם שְׁלֹמֹה מִשְּׁמֶךָ וִיגַדֵּל אֶת־כִּסְאוֹ מִכִּסְאֶךָ
	וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ הַמֶּלֶךְ עַל־הַמִּשְׁכָּב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	further, the king’s courtiers came to congratulate our lord King
	David, saying, ‘May God make the renown of Solomon even greater
	than yours, and may He exalt his throne even higher than yours!’
	And the king bowed low on his couch.
}

\eJ{
	And further, this is what the king said, ‘Praised be YHWH, the God
	of Israel who has this day provided a successor to my throne,
	while my own eyes can see it.’”
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon, all of Adonijah’s guests rose in alarm and each went
	his own way.
}

\eJ{
	Adonijah, in fear of Solomon, went at once [to the Tent] and
	grasped the horns of the altar.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיֻּגַּד לִשְׁלֹמֹה לֵאמֹר הִנֵּה אֲדֹנִיָּהוּ יָרֵא אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה וְהִנֵּה אָחַז בְּקַרְנוֹת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לֵאמֹר יִשָּׁבַע־לִי כַיּוֹם הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה אִם־יָמִית
	אֶת־עַבְדּוֹ בֶּחָרֶב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	It was reported to Solomon: “Adonijah is in fear of King Solomon
	and has grasped the horns of the altar, saying, ‘Let King Solomon
	first swear to me that he will not put his servant to the sword.’”
}

\eJ{
	Solomon said, “If he behaves worthily, not a hair of his head
	shall fall to the ground; but if he is caught in any offense, he
	shall die.”
}

\eJ{
	So King Solomon sent and had him taken down from the altar. He
	came and bowed before King Solomon, and Solomon said to him, “Go
	home.”
}

\eJ{
	When David’s life was drawing to a close, he instructed his son
	Solomon as follows:
}

\eJ{
	“I am going the way of all the earth; be strong and show yourself
	a man.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְשָׁמַרְתָּ אֶת־מִשְׁמֶרֶת יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ לָלֶכֶת בִּדְרָכָיו לִשְׁמֹר חֻקֹּתָיו מִצְוֺתָיו וּמִשְׁפָּטָיו וְעֵדְוֺתָיו כַּכָּתוּב בְּתוֹרַת מֹשֶׁה לְמַעַן תַּשְׂכִּיל אֵת
	כּל־אֲשֶׁר תַּעֲשֶׂה וְאֵת כּל־אֲשֶׁר תִּפְנֶה שָׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Keep the charge of YHWH your God, walking in His ways and
	following His laws, His commandments, His rules, and His
	admonitions as recorded in the Teaching of Moses, in order that
	you may succeed in whatever you undertake and wherever you turn.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	לְמַעַן יָקִים יְהֹוָה אֶת־דְּבָרוֹ אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר עָלַי לֵאמֹר אִם־יִשְׁמְרוּ בָנֶיךָ אֶת־דַּרְכָּם לָלֶכֶת לְפָנַי בֶּאֱמֶת בְּכל־לְבָבָם וּבְכל־נַפְשָׁם לֵאמֹר לֹא־יִכָּרֵת לְךָ
	אִישׁ מֵעַל כִּסֵּא יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Then YHWH will fulfill the promise that He made concerning me: ‘If
	your descendants are scrupulous in their conduct, and walk before
	Me faithfully, with all their heart and soul, your line on the
	throne of Israel shall never end!’%
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְגַם אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ אֵת אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה לִי יוֹאָב בֶּן־צְרוּיָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לִשְׁנֵי־שָׂרֵי צִבְאוֹת יִשְׂרָאֵל לְאַבְנֵר בֶּן־נֵר וְלַעֲמָשָׂא בֶן־יֶתֶר וַיַּהַרְגֵם וַיָּשֶׂם
	דְּמֵי־מִלְחָמָה בְּשָׁלֹם וַיִּתֵּן דְּמֵי מִלְחָמָה בַּחֲגֹרָתוֹ אֲשֶׁר בְּמתְנָיו וּבְנַעֲלוֹ אֲשֶׁר בְּרַגְלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So act in accordance with your wisdom, and see that his white hair
	does not go down to Sheol in peace.
}

\eJ{
	“But deal graciously with the sons of Barzillai the Gileadite, for
	they befriended me when I fled from your brother Absalom; let them
	be among those that eat at your table.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהִנֵּה עִמְּךָ שִׁמְעִי בֶן־גֵּרָא בֶן־הַיְמִינִי מִבַּחֻרִים וְהוּא קִלְלַנִי קְלָלָה נִמְרֶצֶת בְּיוֹם לֶכְתִּי מַחֲנָיִם וְהוּא־יָרַד לִקְרָאתִי הַיַּרְדֵּן וָאֶשָּׁבַע לוֹ
	בַיהֹוָה לֵאמֹר אִם־אֲמִיתְךָ בֶּחָרֶב׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	“You must also deal with Shimei son of Gera, the Benjaminite from
	Bahurim. He insulted me outrageously when I was on my way to
	Mahanaim; but he came down to meet me at the Jordan, and I swore
	to him by YHWH: ‘I will not put you to the sword.’
}

\eJ{
	So do not let him go unpunished; for you are a wise man and you
	will know how to deal with him and send his gray hair down to
	Sheol in blood.”
}

\eJ{
	So David slept with his fathers, and he was buried in the City of
	David.
}

\eJ{
	The length of David’s reign over Israel was forty years: he
	reigned seven years in Hebron, and he reigned thirty-three years
	in Jerusalem.
}

\eJ{
	And Solomon sat upon the throne of his father David, and his rule
	was firmly established.
}

\eJ{
	Adonijah son of Haggith came to see Bathsheba, Solomon’s mother.
	She said, “Do you come with friendly intent?” “Yes,” he replied;
}

\eJ{
	and he continued, “I would like to have a word with you.” “Speak
	up,” she said.
}

\eJ{
	Then he said, “You know that the kingship was rightly mine and
	that all Israel wanted me to reign. But the kingship passed on to
	my brother; it came to him by the will of YHWH.
}

\eJ{
	And now I have one request to make of you; do not refuse me.” She
	said, “Speak up.”
}

\eJ{
	He replied, “Please ask King Solomon—for he won’t refuse you—to
	give me Abishag the Shunammite as wife.”
}

\eJ{
	“Very well,” said Bathsheba, “I will speak to the king in your
	behalf.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַתָּבֹא בַת־שֶׁבַע אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה לְדַבֶּר־לוֹ עַל־אֲדֹנִיָּהוּ וַיָּקם הַמֶּלֶךְ לִקְרָאתָהּ וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ לָהּ וַיֵּשֶׁב עַל־כִּסְאוֹ וַיָּשֶׂם כִּסֵּא לְאֵם הַמֶּלֶךְ
	וַתֵּשֶׁב לִימִינוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So Bathsheba went to King Solomon to speak to him about Adonijah.
	The king rose to greet her and bowed down to her. He sat on his
	throne; and he had a throne placed for the queen mother, and she
	sat on his right.
}

\eJ{
	She said, “I have one small request to make of you, do not refuse
	me.” He responded, “Ask, Mother; I shall not refuse you.”
}

\eJ{
	Then she said, “Let Abishag the Shunammite be given to your
	brother Adonijah as wife.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיַּעַן הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה וַיֹּאמֶר לְאִמּוֹ וְלָמָה אַתְּ שֹׁאֶלֶת אֶת־אֲבִישַׁג הַשֻּׁנַמִּית לַאֲדֹנִיָּהוּ וְשַׁאֲלִי־לוֹ אֶת־הַמְּלוּכָה כִּי הוּא אָחִי הַגָּדוֹל מִמֶּנִּי
	וְלוֹ וּלְאֶבְיָתָר הַכֹּהֵן וּלְיוֹאָב בֶּן־צְרוּיָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	The king replied to his mother, “Why request Abishag the
	Shunammite for Adonijah? Request the kingship for him! For he is
	my older brother, and the priest Abiathar and Joab son of Zeruiah
	are on his side.”
}

\eJ{
	Thereupon, King Solomon swore by YHWH, saying, “So may God do to
	me and even more, if broaching this matter does not cost Adonijah
	his life!
}

\eJ{
	Now, as YHWH lives, who has established me and set me on the
	throne of my father David and who has provided him with a house,
	as he promised, Adonijah shall be put to death this very day!”
}

\eJ{
	And Solomon instructed Benaiah son of Jehoiada, who struck
	Adonijah down; and so he died.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וּלְאֶבְיָתָר הַכֹּהֵן אָמַר הַמֶּלֶךְ עֲנָתֹת לֵךְ עַל־שָׂדֶיךָ כִּי אִישׁ מָוֶת אָתָּה וּבַיּוֹם הַזֶּה לֹא אֲמִיתֶךָ כִּי־נָשָׂאתָ אֶת־אֲרוֹן אֲדֹנָי יֱהֹוִה לִפְנֵי דָּוִד
	אָבִי וְכִי הִתְעַנִּיתָ בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר־הִתְעַנָּה אָבִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	So Solomon dismissed Abiathar from his office of priest of
	YHWH—thus fulfilling what YHWH had spoken at Shiloh regarding the
	house of Eli.
}

\eJ{
	When the news reached Joab, he fled to the Tent of YHWH and
	grasped the horns of the altar—for Joab had sided with Adonijah,
	though he had not sided with Absalom.
}

\eJ{
	King Solomon was told that Joab had fled to the Tent of YHWH and
	that he was there by the altar; so Solomon sent Benaiah son of
	Jehoiada, saying, “Go and strike him down.”
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיָּבֹא בְנָיָהוּ אֶל־אֹהֶל יְהֹוָה וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו כֹּה־אָמַר הַמֶּלֶךְ צֵא וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא כִּי פֹה אָמוּת וַיָּשֶׁב בְּנָיָהוּ אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּבָר לֵאמֹר כֹּה־דִבֶּר יוֹאָב
	וְכֹה עָנָנִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	Benaiah went to the Tent of YHWH and said to him, “Thus said the
	king: Come out!” “No!” he replied; “I will die here.” Benaiah
	reported back to the king that Joab had answered thus and thus,
}

\eJ{
	and the king said, “Do just as he said; strike him down and bury
	him, and remove guilt from me and my father’s house for the blood
	of the innocent that Joab has shed.
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וְהֵשִׁיב יְהֹוָה אֶת־דָּמוֹ עַל־רֹאשׁוֹ אֲשֶׁר פָּגַע בִּשְׁנֵי־אֲנָשִׁים צַדִּקִים וְטֹבִים מִמֶּנּוּ וַיַּהַרְגֵם בַּחֶרֶב וְאָבִי דָוִד לֹא יָדָע אֶת־אַבְנֵר בֶּן־נֵר
	שַׂר־צְבָא יִשְׂרָאֵל וְאֶת־עֲמָשָׂא בֶן־יֶתֶר שַׂר־צְבָא יְהוּדָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	May the guilt for their blood come down upon the head of Joab and
	his descendants forever, and may good fortune from YHWH be granted
	forever to David and his descendants, his house and his throne.”
}

\eJ{
	So Benaiah son of Jehoiada went up and struck him down. And he was
	buried at his home in the wilderness.
}

\eJ{
	In his place, the king appointed Benaiah son of Jehoiada over the
	army, and in place of Abiathar, the king appointed the priest
	Zadok.
}

\eJ{
	Then the king summoned Shimei and said to him, “Build yourself a
	house in Jerusalem and stay there—do not ever go out from there
	anywhere else.
}

\eJ{
	On the very day that you go out and cross the Wadi Kidron, you can
	be sure that you will die; your blood shall be on your own head.”
}

\eJ{
	“That is fair,” said Shimei to the king, “your servant will do
	just as my lord the king has spoken.” And for a long time, Shimei
	remained in Jerusalem.
}

\eJ{
	Three years later, two slaves of Shimei ran away to King Achish
	son of Maacah of Gath. Shimei was told, “Your slaves are in Gath.”
}

\eJ{
	Shimei thereupon saddled his ass and went to Achish in Gath to
	claim his slaves; and Shimei returned from Gath with his slaves.
}

\eJ{
	Solomon was told that Shimei had gone from Jerusalem to Gath and
	back,
}

tex.original:
\hJ{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיִּקְרָא לְשִׁמְעִי וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו הֲלוֹא הִשְׁבַּעְתִּיךָ בַיהֹוָה וָאָעִד בְּךָ לֵאמֹר בְּיוֹם צֵאתְךָ וְהָלַכְתָּ אָנֶה וָאָנָה יָדֹעַ תֵּדַע כִּי מוֹת
	תָּמוּת וַתֹּאמֶר אֵלַי טוֹב הַדָּבָר שָׁמָעְתִּי׃
}

tex.english:
\eJ{
	and the king summoned Shimei and said to him, “Did I not adjure
	you by YHWH and warn you, ‘On the very day that you leave and go
	anywhere else, you can be sure that you will die,’ and did you not
	say to me, ‘It is fair; I accept’?
}

\eJ{
	Why did you not abide by the oath before YHWH and by the orders
	which I gave you?”
}

\eJ{
	The king said further to Shimei, “You know all the wrong, which
	you remember very well, that you did to my father David. Now YHWH
	brings down your wrongdoing upon your own head.
}

\eJ{
	But King Solomon shall be blessed, and the throne of David shall
	be established before YHWH forever.”
}

\eJ{
	The king gave orders to Benaiah son of Jehoiada and he went out
	and struck Shimei down; and so he died. Thus the kingdom was
	secured in Solomon’s hands.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיִּתְחַתֵּן שְׁלֹמֹה אֶת־פַּרְעֹה מֶלֶךְ מִצְרָיִם וַיִּקַּח אֶת־בַּת־פַּרְעֹה וַיְבִיאֶהָ אֶל־עִיר דָּוִד עַד כַּלֹּתוֹ לִבְנוֹת אֶת־בֵּיתוֹ וְאֶת־בֵּית יְהֹוָה וְאֶת־חוֹמַת
	יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם סָבִיב׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Solomon allied himself by marriage with Pharaoh king of Egypt. He
	married Pharaoh’s daughter and brought her to the City of David
	[to live there] until he had finished building his palace, and the
	House of YHWH, and the walls around Jerusalem.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The people, however, continued to offer sacrifices at the open
	shrines, because up to that time no house had been built for the
	name of YHWH.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And Solomon, though he loved YHWH and followed the practices of
	his father David, also sacrificed and offered at the shrines.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The king went to Gibeon to sacrifice there, for that was the
	largest shrine; on that altar Solomon presented a thousand burnt
	offerings.%
}

\eDtrH{
	At Gibeon YHWH appeared to Solomon in a dream by night; and God
	said, “Ask, what shall I grant you?”%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר שְׁלֹמֹה אַתָּה עָשִׂיתָ עִם־עַבְדְּךָ דָוִד אָבִי חֶסֶד גָּדוֹל כַּאֲשֶׁר הָלַךְ לְפָנֶיךָ בֶּאֱמֶת וּבִצְדָקָה וּבְיִשְׁרַת לֵבָב עִמָּךְ וַתִּשְׁמר־לוֹ אֶת־הַחֶסֶד
	הַגָּדוֹל הַזֶּה וַתִּתֶּן־לוֹ בֵן יֹשֵׁב עַל־כִּסְאוֹ כַּיּוֹם הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And now, O LORD my God, You have made Your servant king in place
	of my father David; but I am a young lad, with no experience in
	leadership.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Your servant finds himself in the midst of the people You have
	chosen, a people too numerous to be numbered or counted.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Grant, then, Your servant an understanding mind to judge Your
	people, to distinguish between good and bad; for who can judge
	this vast people of Yours?”%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 3:9. Solomon asks for a listening heart. In Hebrew, wisdom begins in the
%		ear, not the head.
%	}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים אֵלָיו יַעַן אֲשֶׁר שָׁאַלְתָּ אֶת־הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה וְלֹא־שָׁאַלְתָּ לְּךָ יָמִים רַבִּים וְלֹא־שָׁאַלְתָּ לְּךָ עֹשֶׁר וְלֹא שָׁאַלְתָּ נֶפֶשׁ אֹיְבֶיךָ
	וְשָׁאַלְתָּ לְּךָ הָבִין לִשְׁמֹעַ מִשְׁפָּט׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And God said to him, “Because you asked for this—you did not ask
	for long life, you did not ask for riches, you did not ask for the
	life of your enemies, but you asked for discernment in dispensing
	justice—%
}

\eDtrH{
	I now do as you have spoken. I grant you a wise and discerning
	mind; there has never been anyone like you before, nor will anyone
	like you arise again.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And I also grant you what you did not ask for—both riches and
	glory all your life—the like of which no king has ever had.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And I will further grant you long life, if you will walk in My
	ways and observe My laws and commandments, as did your father
	David.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then Solomon awoke: it was a dream! He went to Jerusalem, stood
	before the Ark of the Covenant of YHWH, and sacrificed burnt
	offerings and presented offerings of well-being; and he made a
	banquet for all his courtiers.%
}

\eX{
	Later two prostitutes came to the king and stood before him.
}

\eX{
	The first woman said, “Please, my lord! This woman and I live in
	the same house; and I gave birth to a child while she was in the
	house.
}

\eX{
	On the third day after I was delivered, this woman also gave birth
	to a child. We were alone; there was no one else with us in the
	house, just the two of us in the house.
}

\eX{
	During the night this woman’s child died, because she lay on it.
}

\eX{
	She arose in the night and took my son from my side while your
	maidservant was asleep, and laid him in her bosom; and she laid
	her dead son in my bosom.
}

\eX{
	When I arose in the morning to nurse my son, there he was, dead;
	but when I looked at him closely in the morning, it was not the
	son I had borne.”
}

\eX{
	The other woman spoke up, “No, the live one is my son, and the
	dead one is yours!” But the first insisted, “No, the dead boy is
	yours; mine is the live one!” And they went on arguing before the
	king.
}

\eX{
	The king said, “One says, ‘This is my son, the live one, and the
	dead one is yours’; and the other says, ‘No, the dead boy is
	yours, mine is the live one.’
}

\eX{
	So the king gave the order, “Fetch me a sword.” A sword was
	brought before the king,
}

\eX{
	and the king said, “Cut the live child in two, and give half to
	one and half to the other.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתֹּאמֶר הָאִשָּׁה אֲשֶׁר־בְּנָהּ הַחַי אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ כִּי־נִכְמְרוּ רַחֲמֶיהָ עַל־בְּנָהּ וַתֹּאמֶר בִּי אֲדֹנִי תְּנוּ־לָהּ אֶת־הַיָּלוּד הַחַי וְהָמֵת אַל־תְּמִיתֻהוּ וְזֹאת
	אֹמֶרֶת גַּם־לִי גַם־לָךְ לֹא יִהְיֶה גְּזֹרוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the king spoke up. “Give the live child to her,” he said,
	“and do not put it to death; she is its mother.”
}

\eX{
	When all Israel heard the decision that the king had rendered,
	they stood in awe of the king; for they saw that he possessed
	divine wisdom to execute justice.
}

\eX{
	Elihoreph and Ahijah sons of Shisha—scribes; Jehoshaphat son of
	Ahilud—recorder;
}

\eX{
	Benaiah son of Jehoiada—over the army; Zadok and Abiathar—priests;
}

\eX{
	Azariah son of Nathan—in charge of the prefects; Zabud son of
	Nathan the priest—companion of the king;
}

\eX{
	Ahishar—in charge of the palace; and Adoniram son of Abda—in
	charge of the forced labor.
}

\eX{
	Solomon had twelve prefects governing all Israel, who provided
	food for the king and his household; each had to provide food for
	one month in the year.
}

\eX{
	And these were their names: Ben-hur, in the hill country of
	Ephraim;
}

\eX{
	Ben-deker, in Makaz, Shaalbim, Beth-shemesh, and Elon-beth-hanan;
}

\eX{
	Ben-hesed in Arubboth—he governed Socho and all the Hepher area;
}

\eX{
	Ben-abinadab, [in] all of Naphath-dor (Solomon’s daughter Taphath
	was his wife);
}

\eX{
	Baana son of Ahilud [in] Taanach and Megiddo and all Beth-shean,
	which is beside Zarethan, below Jezreel—from Beth-shean to
	Abel-meholah as far as the other side of Jokmeam;
}

\eX{
	Ben-geber, in Ramoth-gilead—he governed the villages of Jair son
	of Manasseh which are in Gilead, and he also governed the district
	of Argob which is in Bashan, sixty large towns with walls and
	bronze bars;
}

\eX{
	Ahimaaz, in Naphtali (he too took a daughter of
	Solomon—Basemath—to wife);
}

\eX{
	Geber son of Uri, in the region of Gilead, the country of Sihon,
	king of the Amorites, and Og, king of Bashan; and one prefect who
	was in the land.
}

\eX{
	Judah and Israel were as numerous as the sands of the sea; they
	ate and drank and were content.
}

\eX{
	Solomon’s rule extended over all the kingdoms from the Euphrates
	to the land of the Philistines and the boundary of Egypt. They
	brought Solomon tribute and were subject to him all his life.
}

\eX{
	Solomon’s daily provisions consisted of 30 kors of semolina, and
	60 kors of [ordinary] flour,
}

\eX{
	10 fattened oxen, 20 pasture-fed oxen, and 100 sheep and goats,
	besides deer and gazelles, roebucks and fatted geese.
}

\eX{
	For he controlled the whole region west of the Euphrates—all the
	kings west of the Euphrates, from Tiphsah to Gaza—and he had peace
	on all his borders round about.
}

\eX{
	All the days of Solomon, Judah and Israel from Dan to Beer-sheba
	dwelt in safety, everyone under his own vine and under his own fig
	tree.
}

\eX{
	Solomon had 40,000 stalls of horses for his chariotry and 12,000
	horsemen.
}

\eX{
	All those prefects, each during his month, would furnish
	provisions for King Solomon and for all who were admitted to King
	Solomon’s table; they did not fall short in anything.
}

\eX{
	They would also, each in his turn, deliver barley and straw for
	the horses and the swift steeds to the places where they were
	stationed.
}

\eX{
	God endowed Solomon with wisdom and discernment in great measure,
	with understanding as vast as the sands on the seashore.
}

\eX{
	Solomon’s wisdom was greater than the wisdom of all the Kedemites
	and than all the wis dom of the Egyptians.
}

\eX{
	He was the wisest of all men: [wiser] than Ethan the Ezrahite, and
	Heman, Chalkol, and Darda the sons of Mahol. His fame spread among
	all the surrounding nations.
}

\eX{
	He composed three thousand proverbs, and his songs numbered one
	thousand and five.
}

\eX{
	He discoursed about trees, from the cedar in Lebanon to the hyssop
	that grows out of the wall; and he discoursed about beasts, birds,
	creeping things, and fishes.
}

\eX{
	Men of all peoples came to hear Solomon’s wisdom, [sent] by all
	the kings of the earth who had heard of his wisdom.
}

\eX{
	King Hiram of Tyre sent his officials to Solomon when he heard
	that he had been anointed king in place of his father; for Hiram
	had always been a friend of David.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ אֶת־דָּוִד אָבִי כִּי לֹא יָכֹל לִבְנוֹת בַּיִת לְשֵׁם יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהָיו מִפְּנֵי הַמִּלְחָמָה אֲשֶׁר סְבָבֻהוּ עַד תֵּת־יְהֹוָה אֹתָם תַּחַת כַּפּוֹת (רגלו)
	[רַגְלָי]׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“You know that my father David could not build a house for the
	name of YHWH his God because of the enemies that encompassed him,
	until YHWH had placed them under the soles of his feet.
}

\eX{
	But now YHWH my God has given me respite all around; there is no
	adversary and no mischance.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהִנְנִי אֹמֵר לִבְנוֹת בַּיִת לְשֵׁם יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהָי כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה אֶל־דָּוִד אָבִי לֵאמֹר בִּנְךָ אֲשֶׁר אֶתֵּן תַּחְתֶּיךָ עַל־כִּסְאֶךָ הוּא־יִבְנֶה הַבַּיִת
	לִשְׁמִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And so I propose to build a house for the name of YHWH my God, as
	YHWH promised my father David, saying, ‘Your son, whom I will set
	on your throne in your place, shall build the house for My name.’
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְעַתָּה צַוֵּה וְיִכְרְתוּ־לִי אֲרָזִים מִן־הַלְּבָנוֹן וַעֲבָדַי יִהְיוּ עִם־עֲבָדֶיךָ וּשְׂכַר עֲבָדֶיךָ אֶתֵּן לְךָ כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר תֹּאמֵר כִּי אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ כִּי אֵין בָּנוּ
	אִישׁ יֹדֵעַ לִכְרת־עֵצִים כַּצִּדֹנִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Please, then, give orders for cedars to be cut for me in the
	Lebanon. My servants will work with yours, and I will pay you any
	wages you may ask for your servants; for as you know, there is
	none among us who knows how to cut timber like the Sidonians.”
}

\eX{
	When Hiram heard Solomon’s message, he was overjoyed. “Praised be
	YHWH this day,” he said, “for granting David a wise son to govern
	this great people.”
}

\eX{
	So Hiram sent word to Solomon: “I have your message; I will supply
	all the cedar and cypress logs you require.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	עֲבָדַי יֹרִדוּ מִן־הַלְּבָנוֹן יָמָּה וַאֲנִי אֲשִׂימֵם דֹּבְרוֹת בַּיָּם עַד־הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר־תִּשְׁלַח אֵלַי וְנִפַּצְתִּים שָׁם וְאַתָּה תִשָּׂא וְאַתָּה תַּעֲשֶׂה אֶת־חֶפְצִי
	לָתֵת לֶחֶם בֵּיתִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So Hiram kept Solomon provided with all the cedar and cypress wood
	he required,
}

\eX{
	and Solomon delivered to Hiram 20,000 kors of wheat as provisions
	for his household and 20 kors of beaten oil. Such was Solomon’s
	annual payment to Hiram.
}

\eX{
	YHWH had given Solomon wisdom, as He had promised him. There was
	friendship between Hiram and Solomon, and the two of them made a
	treaty.
}

\eX{
	King Solomon imposed forced labor on all Israel; the levy came to
	30,000 men.
}

\eX{
	He sent them to the Lebanon in shifts of 10,000 a month: they
	would spend one month in the Lebanon and two months at home.
	Adoniram was in charge of the forced labor.
}

\eX{
	Solomon also had 70,000 porters and 80,000 quarriers in the hills,
}

\eX{
	apart from Solomon’s 3,300 officials who were in charge of the
	work and supervised the gangs doing the work.
}

\eX{
	The king ordered huge blocks of choice stone to be quarried, so
	that the foundations of the house might be laid with hewn stones.
}

\eX{
	Solomon’s masons, Hiram’s masons, and the men of Gebal shaped
	them. Thus the timber and the stones for building the house were
	made ready.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי בִשְׁמוֹנִים שָׁנָה וְאַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת שָׁנָה לְצֵאת בְּנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵאֶרֶץ־מִצְרַיִם בַּשָּׁנָה הָרְבִיעִית בְּחֹדֶשׁ זִו הוּא הַחֹדֶשׁ הַשֵּׁנִי לִמְלֹךְ שְׁלֹמֹה
	עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיִּבֶן הַבַּיִת לַיהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	In the four hundred and eightieth year after the Israelites left
	the land of Egypt, in the month of Ziv—that is, the second
	month—in the fourth year of his reign over Israel, Solomon began
	to build the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	The House which King Solomon built for YHWH was 60 cubits long, 20
	cubits wide, and 30 cubits high.
}

\eX{
	The portico in front of the Great Hall of the House was 20 cubits
	long—along the width of the House—and 10 cubits deep to the front
	of the House.
}

\eX{
	Against the outside wall of the House—the outside walls of the
	House enclosing the Great Hall and the Shrine—he built a storied
	structure; and he made side chambers all around.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	(היצוע) [הַיָּצִיעַ] הַתַּחְתֹּנָה חָמֵשׁ בָּאַמָּה רחְבָּהּ וְהַתִּיכֹנָה שֵׁשׁ בָּאַמָּה רחְבָּהּ וְהַשְּׁלִישִׁית שֶׁבַע בָּאַמָּה רחְבָּהּ כִּי מִגְרָעוֹת נָתַן לַבַּיִת סָבִיב
	חוּצָה לְבִלְתִּי אֲחֹז בְּקִירוֹת־הַבָּיִת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The lowest story was 5 cubits wide, the middle one 6 cubits wide,
	and the third 7 cubits wide; for he had provided recesses around
	the outside of the House so as not to penetrate the walls of the
	House.
}

\eX{
	When the House was built, only finished stones cut at the quarry
	were used, so that no hammer or ax or any iron tool was heard in
	the House while it was being built.
}

\eX{
	The entrance to the middle [story of] the side chambers was on the
	right side of the House; and winding stairs led up to the middle
	chambers, and from the middle chambers to the third story.
}

\eX{
	When he finished building the House, he paneled the House with
	beams and planks of cedar.
}

\eX{
	He built the storied structure against the entire House—each story
	5 cubits high, so that it encased the House with timbers of cedar.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר־אַתָּה בֹנֶה אִם־תֵּלֵךְ בְּחֻקֹּתַי וְאֶת־מִשְׁפָּטַי תַּעֲשֶׂה וְשָׁמַרְתָּ אֶת־כּל־מִצְוֺתַי לָלֶכֶת בָּהֶם וַהֲקִמֹתִי אֶת־דְּבָרִי אִתָּךְ אֲשֶׁר
	דִּבַּרְתִּי אֶל־דָּוִד אָבִיךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“With regard to this House you are building—if you follow My laws
	and observe My rules and faithfully keep My commandments, I will
	fulfill for you the promise that I gave to your father David:%
}

\eDtrH{
	I will abide among the children of Israel, and I will never
	forsake My people Israel.”%
}

\eX{
	he paneled the walls of the House on the inside with planks of
	cedar. He also overlaid the walls on the inside with wood, from
	the floor of the House to the ceiling. And he overlaid the floor
	of the House with planks of cypress.
}

\eX{
	Twenty cubits from the rear of the House, he built [a partition]
	of cedar planks from the floor to the walls; he furnished its
	interior to serve as a shrine, as the Holy of Holies.
}

\eX{
	The front part of the House, that is, the Great Hall, measured 40
	cubits.
}

\eX{
	The cedar of the interior of the House had carvings of gourds and
	calyxes; it was all cedar, no stone was exposed.
}

\eX{
	In the innermost part of the House, he fixed a Shrine in which to
	place the Ark of YHWH’s Covenant.
}

\eX{
	The interior of the Shrine was 20 cubits long, 20 cubits wide, and
	20 cubits high. He overlaid it with solid gold; he similarly
	overlaid [its] cedar altar.
}

\eX{
	Solomon overlaid the interior of the House with solid gold; and he
	inserted golden chains into the door of the Shrine. He overlaid
	[the Shrine] with gold,
}

\eX{
	so that the entire House was overlaid with gold; he even overlaid
	with gold the entire altar of the Shrine. And so the entire House
	was completed.
}

\eX{
	In the Shrine he made two cherubim of olive wood, each 10 cubits
	high.
}

\eX{
	[One] had a wing measuring 5 cubits and another wing measuring 5
	cubits, so that the spread from wingtip to wingtip was 10 cubits;
}

\eX{
	and the wingspread of the other cherub was also 10 cubits. The two
	cherubim had the same measurements and proportions:
}

\eX{
	the height of the one cherub was 10 cubits, and so was that of the
	other cherub.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּתֵּן אֶת־הַכְּרוּבִים בְּתוֹךְ הַבַּיִת הַפְּנִימִי וַיִּפְרְשׂוּ אֶת־כַּנְפֵי הַכְּרֻבִים וַתִּגַּע כְּנַף־הָאֶחָד בַּקִּיר וּכְנַף הַכְּרוּב הַשֵּׁנִי נֹגַעַת בַּקִּיר
	הַשֵּׁנִי וְכַנְפֵיהֶם אֶל־תּוֹךְ הַבַּיִת נֹגְעֹת כָּנָף אֶל־כָּנָף׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He placed the cherubim inside the inner chamber. Since the wings
	of the cherubim were extended, a wing of the one touched one wall
	and a wing of the other touched the other wall, while their wings
	in the center of the chamber touched each other.
}

\eX{
	All over the walls of the House, of both the inner area and the
	outer area, he carved reliefs of cherubim, palms, and calyxes,
}

\eX{
	and he overlaid the floor of the House with gold, both the inner
	and the outer areas.
}

\eX{
	For the entrance of the Shrine he made doors of olive wood, the
	pilasters and the doorposts having five sides.
}

\eX{
	The double doors were of olive wood, and on them he carved reliefs
	of cherubim, palms, and calyxes. He overlaid them with gold,
	hammering the gold onto the cherubim and the palms.
}

\eX{
	For the entrance of the Great Hall, too, he made doorposts of
	oleaster wood, having four sides,
}

\eX{
	and the double doors of cypress wood, each door consisting of two
	rounded planks.
}

\eX{
	On them he carved cherubim, palms, and calyxes, overlaying them
	with gold applied evenly over the carvings.
}

\eX{
	He built the inner enclosure of three courses of hewn stones and
	one course of cedar beams.
}

\eX{
	In the fourth year, in the month of Ziv, the foundations of the
	House of YHWH were laid;
}

\eX{
	and in the eleventh year, in the month of Bul—that is, the eighth
	month—the House was completed according to all its details and all
	its specifications. It took him seven years to build it.
}

\eX{
	And it took Solomon thirteen years to build his palace, until his
	whole palace was completed.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּבֶן אֶת־בֵּית יַעַר הַלְּבָנוֹן מֵאָה אַמָּה ארְכּוֹ וַחֲמִשִּׁים אַמָּה רחְבּוֹ וּשְׁלֹשִׁים אַמָּה קוֹמָתוֹ עַל אַרְבָּעָה טוּרֵי עַמּוּדֵי אֲרָזִים וּכְרֻתוֹת אֲרָזִים
	עַל־הָעַמּוּדִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He built the Lebanon Forest House with four rows of cedar columns,
	and with hewn cedar beams above the columns. Its length was 100
	cubits, its breadth 50 cubits, and its height 30 cubits.
}

\eX{
	It was paneled above with cedar, with the planks that were above
	on the 45 columns—15 in each row.
}

\eX{
	And there were three rows of window frames, with three tiers of
	windows facing each other.
}

\eX{
	All the doorways and doorposts had square frames—with three tiers
	of windows facing each other.
}

\eX{
	He made the portico of columns 50 cubits long and 30 cubits wide;
	the portico was in front of [the columns], and there were columns
	with a canopy in front of them.
}

\eX{
	He made the throne portico, where he was to pronounce judgment—the
	Hall of Judgment. It was paneled with cedar from floor to floor.
}

\eX{
	The house that he used as a residence, in the rear courtyard, back
	of the portico, was of the same construction. Solomon also
	constructed a palace like that portico for the daughter of
	Pharaoh, whom he had married.
}

\eX{
	All these buildings, from foundation to coping and all the way out
	to the great courtyard, were of choice stones, hewn according to
	measure, smooth on all sides.
}

\eX{
	The foundations were huge blocks of choice stone, stones of 10
	cubits and stones of 8 cubits;
}

\eX{
	and above were choice stones, hewn according to measure, and cedar
	wood.
}

\eX{
	The large surrounding courtyard had three tiers of hewn stone and
	a row of cedar beams, the same as for the inner court of the House
	of YHWH, and for the portico of the House.
}

\eX{
	King Solomon sent for Hiram and brought him down from Tyre.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	בֶּן־אִשָּׁה אַלְמָנָה הוּא מִמַּטֵּה נַפְתָּלִי וְאָבִיו אִישׁ־צֹרִי חֹרֵשׁ נְחֹשֶׁת וַיִּמָּלֵא אֶת־הַחכְמָה וְאֶת־הַתְּבוּנָה וְאֶת־הַדַּעַת לַעֲשׂוֹת כּל־מְלָאכָה בַּנְּחֹשֶׁת
	וַיָּבוֹא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה וַיַּעַשׂ אֶת־כּל־מְלַאכְתּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He was the son of a widow of the tribe of Naphtali, and his father
	had been a Tyrian, a coppersmith. He was endowed with skill,
	ability, and talent for executing all work in bronze. He came to
	King Solomon and executed all his work.
}

\eX{
	He cast two columns of bronze; one column was 18 cubits high and
	measured 12 cubits in circumference, [and similarly] the other
	column.
}

\eX{
	He made two capitals, cast in bronze, to be set upon the two
	columns, the height of each of the two capitals being 5 cubits;
}

\eX{
	also nets of meshwork with festoons of chainwork for the capitals
	that were on the top of the columns, seven for each of the two
	capitals.
}

\eX{
	He made the columns so that there were two rows [of pomegranates]
	encircling the top of the one network, to cover the capitals that
	were on the top of the pomegranates; and he did the same for [the
	network on] the second capital.
}

\eX{
	The capitals upon the columns of the portico were of lily design,
	4 cubits high;
}

\eX{
	so also the capitals upon the two columns extended above and next
	to the bulge that was beside the network. There were 200
	pomegranates in rows around the top of the second capital.
}

\eX{
	He set up the columns at the portico of the Great Hall; he set up
	one column on the right and named it Jachin, and he set up the
	other column on the left and named it Boaz.
}

\eX{
	Upon the top of the columns there was a lily design. Thus the work
	of the columns was completed.
}

\eX{
	Then he made the tank of cast metal, 10 cubits across from brim to
	brim, completely round; it was 5 cubits high, and it measured 30
	cubits in circumference.
}

\eX{
	There were gourds below the brim completely encircling it—ten to a
	cubit, encircling the tank; the gourds were in two rows, cast in
	one piece with it.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	עֹמֵד עַל־שְׁנֵי עָשָׂר בָּקָר שְׁלֹשָׁה פֹנִים צָפוֹנָה וּשְׁלֹשָׁה פֹנִים יָמָּה וּשְׁלֹשָׁה פֹּנִים נֶגְבָּה וּשְׁלֹשָׁה פֹּנִים מִזְרָחָה וְהַיָּם עֲלֵיהֶם מִלְמָעְלָה
	וְכל־אֲחֹרֵיהֶם בָּיְתָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	It stood upon twelve oxen: three facing north, three facing west,
	three facing south, and three facing east, with the tank resting
	upon them; their haunches were all turned inward.
}

\eX{
	It was a handbreadth thick, and its brim was made like that of a
	cup, like the petals of a lily. Its capacity was 2,000 baths.
}

\eX{
	He made the ten laver stands of bronze. The length of each laver
	stand was 4 cubits and the width 4 cubits, and the height was 3
	cubits.
}

\eX{
	The structure of the laver stands was as follows: They had insets,
	and there were insets within the frames;
}

\eX{
	and on the insets within the frames were lions, oxen, and
	cherubim. Above the frames was a stand; and both above and below
	the lions and the oxen were spirals of hammered metal.
}

\eX{
	Each laver stand had four bronze wheels and [two] bronze
	axletrees. Its four legs had brackets; the brackets were under the
	laver, cast with spirals beyond each.
}

\eX{
	Its funnel, within the crown, rose a cubit above it; this funnel
	was round, in the fashion of a stand, a cubit and a half in
	diameter. On the funnel too there were carvings. But the insets
	were square, not round.
}

\eX{
	And below the insets were the four wheels. The axletrees of the
	wheels were [fixed] in the laver stand, and the height of each
	wheel was a cubit and a half.
}

\eX{
	The structure of the wheels was like the structure of chariot
	wheels; and their axletrees, their rims, their spokes, and their
	hubs were all of cast metal.
}

\eX{
	Four brackets ran to the four corners of each laver stand; the
	brackets were of a piece with the laver stand.
}

\eX{
	At the top of the laver stand was a round band half a cubit high,
	and together with the top of the laver stand; its sides and its
	insets were of one piece with it.
}

\eX{
	On its surface—on its sides—and on its insets [Hiram] engraved
	cherubim, lions, and palms, as the clear space on each allowed,
	with spirals round about.
}

\eX{
	It was after this manner that he made the ten laver stands, all of
	them cast alike, of the same measure and the same form.
}

\eX{
	Then he made ten bronze lavers, one laver on each of the ten laver
	stands, each laver measuring 4 cubits and each laver containing
	forty baths.
}

\eX{
	He disposed the laver stands, five at the right side of the House
	and five at its left side; and the tank he placed on the right
	side of the House, at the southeast [corner].
}

\eX{
	Hiram also made the lavers, the scrapers, and the sprinkling
	bowls. So Hiram finished all the work that he had been doing for
	King Solomon on the House of YHWH:
}

\eX{
	the two columns, the two globes of the capitals upon the columns;
	and the two pieces of network to cover the two globes of the
	capitals upon the columns;
}

\eX{
	the four hundred pomegranates for the two pieces of network, two
	rows of pomegranates for each network, to cover the two globes of
	the capitals upon the columns;
}

\eX{
	the pails, the scrapers, and the sprinkling bowls. All those
	vessels in the House of YHWH that Hiram made for King Solomon were
	of burnished bronze.
}

\eX{
	The king had them cast in earthen molds, in the plain of the
	Jordan between Succoth and Zarethan.
}

\eX{
	Solomon left all the vessels [unweighed] because of their very
	great quantity; the weight of the bronze was not reckoned.
}

\eX{
	And Solomon made all the furnishings that were in the House of
	YHWH: the altar, of gold; the table for the bread of display, of
	gold;
}

\eX{
	the lampstands—five on the right side and five on the left—in
	front of the Shrine, of solid gold; and the petals, lamps, and
	tongs, of gold;
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהַסִּפּוֹת וְהַמְזַמְּרוֹת וְהַמִּזְרָקוֹת וְהַכַּפּוֹת וְהַמַּחְתּוֹת זָהָב סָגוּר וְהַפֹּתוֹת לְדַלְתוֹת הַבַּיִת הַפְּנִימִי לְקֹדֶשׁ הַקֳּדָשִׁים לְדַלְתֵי הַבַּיִת לַהֵיכָל
	זָהָב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	the basins, snuffers, sprinkling bowls, ladles, and fire pans, of
	solid gold; and the hinge sockets for the doors of the innermost
	part of the House, the Holy of Holies, and for the doors of the
	Great Hall of the House, of gold.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתִּשְׁלַם כּל־הַמְּלָאכָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה בֵּית יְהֹוָה וַיָּבֵא שְׁלֹמֹה אֶת־קדְשֵׁי דָּוִד אָבִיו אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף וְאֶת־הַזָּהָב וְאֶת־הַכֵּלִים נָתַן בְּאֹצְרוֹת
	בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When all the work that King Solomon had done in the House of YHWH
	was completed, Solomon brought in the sacred donations of his
	father David—the silver, the gold, and the vessels—and deposited
	them in the treasury of the House of YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	אָז יַקְהֵל שְׁלֹמֹה אֶת־זִקְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־כּל־רָאשֵׁי הַמַּטּוֹת נְשִׂיאֵי הָאָבוֹת לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ שְׁלֹמֹה יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם לְהַעֲלוֹת אֶת־אֲרוֹן
	בְּרִית־יְהֹוָה מֵעִיר דָּוִד הִיא צִיּוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Then Solomon convoked the elders of Israel—all the heads of the
	tribes and the ancestral chieftains of the Israelites—before King
	Solomon in Jerusalem, to bring up the Ark of the Covenant of YHWH
	from the City of David, that is, Zion.%
}

\eDtrH{
	All the men of Israel gathered before King Solomon at the Feast,
	in the month of Ethanim—that is, the seventh month.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When all the elders of Israel had come, the priests lifted the Ark%
}

\eDtrH{
	and carried up the Ark of YHWH. Then the priests and the Levites
	brought the Tent of Meeting and all the holy vessels that were in
	the Tent.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Meanwhile, King Solomon and the whole community of Israel, who
	were assembled with him before the Ark, were sacrificing sheep and
	oxen in such abundance that they could not be numbered or counted.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The priests brought the Ark of YHWH’s Covenant to its place
	underneath the wings of the cherubim, in the Shrine of the House,
	in the Holy of Holies;%
}

\eDtrH{
	for the cherubim had their wings spread out over the place of the
	Ark, so that the cherubim shielded the Ark and its poles from
	above.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The poles projected so that the ends of the poles were visible in
	the sanctuary in front of the Shrine, but they could not be seen
	outside; and there they remain to this day.%
}

\eDtrH{
	There was nothing inside the Ark but the two tablets of stone
	which Moses placed there at Horeb, when YHWH made [a covenant]
	with the Israelites after their departure from the land of Egypt.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When the priests came out of the sanctuary—for the cloud had
	filled the House of YHWH%
}

\eDtrH{
	and the priests were not able to remain and perform the service
	because of the cloud, for the Presence of YHWH filled the House of
	YHWH—%
}

\eDtrH{
	then Solomon declared: “YHWH has chosen To abide in a thick cloud:%
}

\eDtrH{
	I have now built for You A stately House, A place where You May
	dwell forever.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then, with the whole congregation of Israel standing, the king
	faced about and blessed the whole congregation of Israel.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He said: “Praised be YHWH, the God of Israel, who has fulfilled
	with deeds the promise He made to my father David. For He said,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	מִן־הַיּוֹם אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִי אֶת־עַמִּי אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל מִמִּצְרַיִם לֹא־בָחַרְתִּי בְעִיר מִכֹּל שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לִבְנוֹת בַּיִת לִהְיוֹת שְׁמִי שָׁם וָאֶבְחַר בְּדָוִד
	לִהְיוֹת עַל־עַמִּי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	‘Ever since I brought My people Israel out of Egypt, I have not
	chosen a city among all the tribes of Israel for building a House
	where My name might abide; but I have chosen David to rule My
	people Israel.’%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Now my father David had intended to build a House for the name of
	YHWH, the God of Israel.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But YHWH said to my father David, ‘As regards your intention to
	build a House for My name, you did right to have that intention.%
}

\eDtrH{
	However, you shall not build the House yourself; instead, your
	son, the issue of your loins, shall build the House for My name.’%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיָּקֶם יְהֹוָה אֶת־דְּבָרוֹ אֲשֶׁר דִּבֵּר וָאָקֻם תַּחַת דָּוִד אָבִי וָאֵשֵׁב עַל־כִּסֵּא יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה וָאֶבְנֶה הַבַּיִת לְשֵׁם יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי
	יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“And YHWH has fulfilled the promise that He made: I have succeeded
	my father David and have ascended the throne of Israel, as YHWH
	promised. I have built the House for the name of YHWH, the God of
	Israel;%
}

\eDtrH{
	and I have set a place there for the Ark, containing the covenant
	which YHWH made with our fathers when He brought them out from the
	land of Egypt.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then Solomon stood before the altar of YHWH in the presence of the
	whole community of Israel; he spread the palms of his hands toward
	heaven%
}

\eDtrH{
	and said, “O LORD God of Israel, in the heavens above and on the
	earth below there is no god like You, who keep Your gracious
	covenant with Your servants when they walk before You in
	wholehearted devotion;%
}

\eDtrH{
	You who have kept the promises You made to Your servant, my father
	David, fulfilling with deeds the promise You made—as is now the
	case.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְעַתָּה יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל שְׁמֹר לְעַבְדְּךָ דָוִד אָבִי אֵת אֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְתָּ לּוֹ לֵאמֹר לֹא־יִכָּרֵת לְךָ אִישׁ מִלְּפָנַי יֹשֵׁב עַל־כִּסֵּא יִשְׂרָאֵל רַק
	אִם־יִשְׁמְרוּ בָנֶיךָ אֶת־דַּרְכָּם לָלֶכֶת לְפָנַי כַּאֲשֶׁר הָלַכְתָּ לְפָנָי׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And now, O LORD God of Israel, keep the further promise that You
	made to Your servant, my father David: ‘Your line on the throne of
	Israel shall never end, if only your descendants will look to
	their way and walk before Me as you have walked before Me.’%
}

\eDtrH{
	Now, therefore, O God of Israel, let the promise that You made to
	Your servant my father David be fulfilled.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“But will God really dwell on earth? Even the heavens to their
	uttermost reaches cannot contain You, how much less this House
	that I have built!%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 8:27. The skies and the skies of the skies is not pious excess. It is
%		Solomon noticing that a temple is theologically absurd and building it anyway.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Yet turn, O LORD my God, to the prayer and supplication of Your
	servant, and hear the cry and prayer which Your servant offers
	before You this day.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	לִהְיוֹת עֵינֶךָ פְתֻחֹת אֶל־הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה לַיְלָה וָיוֹם אֶל־הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אָמַרְתָּ יִהְיֶה שְׁמִי שָׁם לִשְׁמֹעַ אֶל־הַתְּפִלָּה אֲשֶׁר יִתְפַּלֵּל עַבְדְּךָ אֶל־הַמָּקוֹם
	הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	May Your eyes be open day and night toward this House, toward the
	place of which You have said, ‘My name shall abide there’; may You
	heed the prayers which Your servant will offer toward this place.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְשָׁמַעְתָּ אֶל־תְּחִנַּת עַבְדְּךָ וְעַמְּךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר יִתְפַּלְלוּ אֶל־הַמָּקוֹם הַזֶּה וְאַתָּה תִּשְׁמַע אֶל־מְקוֹם שִׁבְתְּךָ אֶל־הַשָּׁמַיִם וְשָׁמַעְתָּ
	וְסָלָחְתָּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And when You hear the supplications which Your servant and Your
	people Israel offer toward this place, give heed in Your heavenly
	abode—give heed and pardon.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Whenever one man commits an offense against another, and the
	latter utters an imprecation to bring a curse upon him, and comes
	with his imprecation before Your altar in this House,%
}

\eDtrH{
	oh, hear in heaven and take action to judge Your servants,
	condemning him who is in the wrong and bringing down the
	punishment of his conduct on his head, vindicating him who is in
	the right by rewarding him according to his righteousness.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Should Your people Israel be routed by an enemy because they have
	sinned against You, and then turn back to You and acknowledge Your
	name, and they offer prayer and supplication to You in this House,%
}

\eDtrH{
	oh, hear in heaven and pardon the sin of Your people Israel, and
	restore them to the land that You gave to their fathers.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Should the heavens be shut up and there be no rain, because they
	have sinned against You, and then they pray toward this place and
	acknowledge Your name and repent of their sins, when You answer
	them,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאַתָּה תִּשְׁמַע הַשָּׁמַיִם וְסָלַחְתָּ לְחַטַּאת עֲבָדֶיךָ וְעַמְּךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי תוֹרֵם אֶת־הַדֶּרֶךְ הַטּוֹבָה אֲשֶׁר יֵלְכוּ־בָהּ וְנָתַתָּה מָטָר עַל־אַרְצְךָ
	אֲשֶׁר־נָתַתָּה לְעַמְּךָ לְנַחֲלָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	oh, hear in heaven and pardon the sin of Your servants, Your
	people Israel, after You have shown them the proper way in which
	they are to walk; and send down rain upon the land which You gave
	to Your people as their heritage.%
}

\eDtrH{
	So, too, if there is a famine in the land, if there is pestilence,
	blight, mildew, locusts or caterpillars, or if an enemy oppresses
	them in any of the settlements of the land. “In any plague and in
	any disease,%
}

\eDtrH{
	in any prayer or supplication offered by any person among all Your
	people Israel—each of whom knows his own affliction—when he
	spreads his palms toward this House,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאַתָּה תִּשְׁמַע הַשָּׁמַיִם מְכוֹן שִׁבְתֶּךָ וְסָלַחְתָּ וְעָשִׂיתָ וְנָתַתָּ לָאִישׁ כְּכל־דְּרָכָיו אֲשֶׁר תֵּדַע אֶת־לְבָבוֹ כִּי־אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ לְבַדְּךָ אֶת־לְבַב
	כּל־בְּנֵי הָאָדָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	oh, hear in Your heavenly abode, and pardon and take action!
	Render to each man according to his ways as You know his heart to
	be—for You alone know the hearts of all men—%
}

\eDtrH{
	so that they may revere You all the days that they live on the
	land that You gave to our fathers.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Or if a foreigner who is not of Your people Israel comes from a
	distant land for the sake of Your name—%
}

\eDtrH{
	for they shall hear about Your great name and Your mighty hand and
	Your outstretched arm—when he comes to pray toward this House,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	אַתָּה תִּשְׁמַע הַשָּׁמַיִם מְכוֹן שִׁבְתֶּךָ וְעָשִׂיתָ כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר־יִקְרָא אֵלֶיךָ הַנּכְרִי לְמַעַן יֵדְעוּן כּל־עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ אֶת־שְׁמֶךָ לְיִרְאָה אֹתְךָ כְּעַמְּךָ
	יִשְׂרָאֵל וְלָדַעַת כִּי־שִׁמְךָ נִקְרָא עַל־הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר בָּנִיתִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	“When Your people take the field against their enemy by whatever
	way You send them, and they pray to YHWH in the direction of the
	city which You have chosen, and of the House which I have built to
	Your name,%
}

\eDtrH{
	oh, hear in heaven their prayer and supplication and uphold their
	cause.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“When they sin against You—for there is no man who does not
	sin—and You are angry with them and deliver them to the enemy, and
	their captors carry them off to an enemy land, near or far;%
}

\eDtrH{
	and then they take it to heart in the land to which they have been
	carried off, and they repent and make supplication to You in the
	land of their captors, saying: ‘We have sinned, we have acted
	perversely, we have acted wickedly,’%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְשָׁבוּ אֵלֶיךָ בְּכל־לְבָבָם וּבְכל־נַפְשָׁם בְּאֶרֶץ אֹיְבֵיהֶם אֲשֶׁר־שָׁבוּ אֹתָם וְהִתְפַּלְלוּ אֵלֶיךָ דֶּרֶךְ אַרְצָם אֲשֶׁר נָתַתָּה לַאֲבוֹתָם הָעִיר אֲשֶׁר בָּחַרְתָּ
	וְהַבַּיִת אֲשֶׁר־[בָּנִיתִי] (בנית) לִשְׁמֶךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	oh, give heed in Your heavenly abode to their prayer and
	supplication, uphold their cause,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and pardon Your people who have sinned against You for all the
	transgressions that they have committed against You. Grant them
	mercy in the sight of their captors that they may be merciful to
	them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For they are Your very own people that You freed from Egypt, from
	the midst of the iron furnace.%
}

\eDtrH{
	May Your eyes be open to the supplication of Your servant and the
	supplication of Your people Israel, and may You heed them whenever
	they call upon You.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For You, O Lord GOD, have set them apart for Yourself from all the
	peoples of the earth as Your very own, as You promised through
	Moses Your servant when You freed our fathers from Egypt.”%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיְהִי כְּכַלּוֹת שְׁלֹמֹה לְהִתְפַּלֵּל אֶל־יְהֹוָה אֵת כּל־הַתְּפִלָּה וְהַתְּחִנָּה הַזֹּאת קָם מִלִּפְנֵי מִזְבַּח יְהֹוָה מִכְּרֹעַ עַל־בִּרְכָּיו וְכַפָּיו פְּרֻשׂוֹת
	הַשָּׁמָיִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	When Solomon finished offering to YHWH all this prayer and
	supplication, he rose from where he had been kneeling, in front of
	the altar of YHWH, his hands spread out toward heaven.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He stood, and in a loud voice blessed the whole congregation of
	Israel:%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Praised be YHWH who has granted a haven to His people Israel,
	just as He promised; not a single word has failed of all the
	gracious promises that He made through His servant Moses.%
}

\eDtrH{
	May YHWH our God be with us, as He was with our fathers. May He
	never abandon or forsake us.%
}

\eDtrH{
	May He incline our hearts to Him, that we may walk in all His ways
	and keep the commandments, the laws, and the rules, which He
	enjoined upon our fathers.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְיִהְיוּ דְבָרַי אֵלֶּה אֲשֶׁר הִתְחַנַּנְתִּי לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה קְרֹבִים אֶל־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ יוֹמָם וָלָיְלָה לַעֲשׂוֹת מִשְׁפַּט עַבְדּוֹ וּמִשְׁפַּט עַמּוֹ יִשְׂרָאֵל
	דְּבַר־יוֹם בְּיוֹמוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And may these words of mine, which I have offered in supplication
	before YHWH, be close to YHWH our God day and night, that He may
	provide for His servant and for His people Israel, according to
	each day’s needs—%
}

\eDtrH{
	to the end that all the peoples of the earth may know that YHWH
	alone is God, there is no other.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And may you be wholehearted with YHWH our God, to walk in His ways
	and keep His commandments, even as now.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	The king and all Israel with him offered sacrifices before YHWH.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיִּזְבַּח שְׁלֹמֹה אֵת זֶבַח הַשְּׁלָמִים אֲשֶׁר זָבַח לַיהֹוָה בָּקָר עֶשְׂרִים וּשְׁנַיִם אֶלֶף וְצֹאן מֵאָה וְעֶשְׂרִים אָלֶף וַיַּחְנְכוּ אֶת־בֵּית יְהֹוָה הַמֶּלֶךְ
	וְכל־בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Solomon offered 22,000 oxen and 120,000 sheep as sacrifices of
	well-being to YHWH. Thus the king and all the Israelites dedicated
	the House of YHWH.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא קִדַּשׁ הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־תּוֹךְ הֶחָצֵר אֲשֶׁר לִפְנֵי בֵית־יְהֹוָה כִּי־עָשָׂה שָׁם אֶת־הָעֹלָה וְאֶת־הַמִּנְחָה וְאֵת חֶלְבֵי הַשְּׁלָמִים כִּי־מִזְבַּח הַנְּחֹשֶׁת
	אֲשֶׁר לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה קָטֹן מֵהָכִיל אֶת־הָעֹלָה וְאֶת־הַמִּנְחָה וְאֵת חֶלְבֵי הַשְּׁלָמִים׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַשׂ שְׁלֹמֹה בָעֵת־הַהִיא אֶת־הֶחָג וְכל־יִשְׂרָאֵל עִמּוֹ קָהָל גָּדוֹל מִלְּבוֹא חֲמָת עַד־נַחַל מִצְרַיִם לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ שִׁבְעַת יָמִים וְשִׁבְעַת יָמִים
	אַרְבָּעָה עָשָׂר יוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	So Solomon and all Israel with him—a great assemblage, [coming]
	from Lebo-hamath to the Wadi of Egypt—observed the Feast at that
	time before YHWH our God, seven days and again seven days,
	fourteen days in all.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי שִׁלַּח אֶת־הָעָם וַיְבָרְכוּ אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֵּלְכוּ לְאהֳלֵיהֶם שְׂמֵחִים וְטוֹבֵי לֵב עַל כּל־הַטּוֹבָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה יְהֹוָה לְדָוִד עַבְדּוֹ
	וּלְיִשְׂרָאֵל עַמּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	On the eighth day he let the people go. They bade the king
	good-bye and went to their homes, joyful and glad of heart over
	all the goodness that YHWH had shown to His servant David and His
	people Israel.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When Solomon had finished building the House of YHWH and the royal
	palace and everything that Solomon had set his heart on
	constructing,%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH appeared to Solomon a second time, as He had appeared to him
	at Gibeon.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֵלָיו שָׁמַעְתִּי אֶת־תְּפִלָּתְךָ וְאֶת־תְּחִנָּתְךָ אֲשֶׁר הִתְחַנַּנְתָּה לְפָנַי הִקְדַּשְׁתִּי אֶת־הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר בָּנִתָה לָשׂוּם־שְׁמִי שָׁם
	עַד־עוֹלָם וְהָיוּ עֵינַי וְלִבִּי שָׁם כּל־הַיָּמִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	YHWH said to him, “I have heard the prayer and the supplication
	which you have offered to Me. I consecrate this House which you
	have built and I set My name there forever. My eyes and My heart
	shall ever be there.%
}

\eDtrH{
	As for you, if you walk before Me as your father David walked
	before Me, wholeheartedly and with uprightness, doing all that I
	have commanded you [and] keeping My laws and My rules,%
}

\eDtrH{
	then I will establish your throne of kingship over Israel forever,
	as I promised your father David, saying, ‘Your line on the throne
	of Israel shall never end.’%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	אִם־שׁוֹב תְּשֻׁבוּן אַתֶּם וּבְנֵיכֶם מֵאַחֲרַי וְלֹא תִשְׁמְרוּ מִצְוֺתַי חֻקֹּתַי אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לִפְנֵיכֶם וַהֲלַכְתֶּם וַעֲבַדְתֶּם אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים וְהִשְׁתַּחֲוִיתֶם
	לָהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	[But] if you and your descendants turn away from Me and do not
	keep the commandments [and] the laws which I have set before you,
	and go and serve other gods and worship them,%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהִכְרַתִּי אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵעַל פְּנֵי הָאֲדָמָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לָהֶם וְאֶת־הַבַּיִת אֲשֶׁר הִקְדַּשְׁתִּי לִשְׁמִי אֲשַׁלַּח מֵעַל פָּנָי וְהָיָה יִשְׂרָאֵל לְמָשָׁל
	וְלִשְׁנִינָה בְּכל־הָעַמִּים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	then I will sweep Israel off the land which I gave them; I will
	reject the House which I have consecrated to My name; and Israel
	shall become a proverb and a byword among all peoples.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And as for this House, once so exalted, everyone passing by it
	shall be appalled and shall hiss. And when they ask, ‘Why did YHWH
	do thus to the land and to this House?’%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאָמְרוּ עַל אֲשֶׁר עָזְבוּ אֶת־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיהֶם אֲשֶׁר הוֹצִיא אֶת־אֲבֹתָם מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם וַיַּחֲזִקוּ בֵּאלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים (וישתחו) [וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ] לָהֶם וַיַּעַבְדֻם
	עַל־כֵּן הֵבִיא יְהֹוָה עֲלֵיהֶם אֵת כּל־הָרָעָה הַזֹּאת׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	they shall be told, ‘It is because they forsook YHWH their God who
	freed them from the land of Egypt, and they embraced other gods
	and worshiped them and served them; therefore YHWH has brought all
	this calamity upon them.’”%
}

\eX{
	At the end of the twenty years during which Solomon constructed
	the two buildings, YHWH’s House and the royal palace—
}

\eX{
	since King Hiram of Tyre had supplied Solomon with all the cedar
	and cypress timber and gold that he required—King Solomon in turn
	gave Hiram twenty towns in the region of Galilee.
}

\eX{
	But when Hiram came from Tyre to inspect the towns that Solomon
	had given him, he was not pleased with them.
}

\eX{
	“My brother,” he said, “what sort of towns are these you have
	given me?” So they were named the land of Cabul, as is still the
	case.
}

\eX{
	However, Hiram sent the king one hundred and twenty talents of
	gold.
}

\eX{
	This was the purpose of the forced labor which Solomon imposed: It
	was to build the House of YHWH, his own palace, the Millo, and the
	wall of Jerusalem, and [to fortify] Hazor, Megiddo, and Gezer.
}

\eX{
	(Pharaoh king of Egypt had come up and captured Gezer; he
	destroyed it by fire, killed the Canaanites who dwelt in the town,
	and gave it as dowry to his daughter, Solomon’s wife.)
}

\eX{
	Baalith, and Tamar in the wilderness, in the land [of Judah],
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאֵת כּל־עָרֵי הַמִּסְכְּנוֹת אֲשֶׁר הָיוּ לִשְׁלֹמֹה וְאֵת עָרֵי הָרֶכֶב וְאֵת עָרֵי הַפָּרָשִׁים וְאֵת חֵשֶׁק שְׁלֹמֹה אֲשֶׁר חָשַׁק לִבְנוֹת בִּירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וּבַלְּבָנוֹן
	וּבְכֹל אֶרֶץ מֶמְשַׁלְתּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	and all of Solomon’s garrison towns, chariot towns, and cavalry
	towns—everything that Solomon set his heart on building in
	Jerusalem and in the Lebanon, and throughout the territory that he
	ruled.
}

\eX{
	All the people that were left of the Amorites, Hittites,
	Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites who were not of the Israelite
	stock—
}

\eX{
	those of their descendants who remained in the land and whom the
	Israelites were not able to annihilate—of these Solomon made a
	slave force, as is still the case.
}

\eX{
	But he did not reduce any Israelites to slavery; they served,
	rather, as warriors and as his attendants, officials, and
	officers, and as commanders of his chariotry and cavalry.
}

\eX{
	These were the prefects that were in charge of Solomon’s works and
	were foremen over the people engaged in the work, who numbered
	550.
}

\eX{
	As soon as Pharaoh’s daughter went up from the City of David to
	the palace that he had built for her, he built the Millo.
}

\eX{
	Solomon used to offer burnt offerings and sacrifices of well-being
	three times a year on the altar that he had built for YHWH, and he
	used to offer incense on the one that was before YHWH. And he kept
	the House in repair.
}

\eX{
	King Solomon also built a fleet of ships at Ezion-geber, which is
	near Eloth on the shore of the Sea of Reeds in the land of Edom.
}

\eX{
	Hiram sent servants of his with the fleet, mariners who were
	experienced on the sea, to serve with Solomon’s men.
}

\eX{
	They came to Ophir; there they obtained gold in the amount of four
	hundred and twenty talents, which they delivered to King Solomon.
}

\eX{
	The queen of Sheba heard of Solomon’s fame, through the name of
	YHWH, and she came to test him with hard questions.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתָּבֹא יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ְמָה בְּחַיִל כָּבֵד מְאֹד גְּמַלִּים נֹשְׂאִים בְּשָׂמִים וְזָהָב רַב־מְאֹד וְאֶבֶן יְקָרָה וַתָּבֹא אֶל־שְׁלֹמֹה וַתְּדַבֵּר אֵלָיו אֵת כּל־אֲשֶׁר הָיָה
	עִם־לְבָבָהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	She arrived in Jerusalem with a very large retinue, with camels
	bearing spices, a great quantity of gold, and precious stones.
	When she came to Solomon, she asked him all that she had in mind.
}

\eX{
	Solomon had answers for all her questions; there was nothing that
	the king did not know, [nothing] to which he could not give her an
	answer.
}

\eX{
	When the queen of Sheba observed all of Solomon’s wisdom, and the
	palace he had built,
}

\eX{
	the fare of his table, the seating of his courtiers, the service
	and attire of his attendants, and his wine service, and the burnt
	offerings that he offered at the House of YHWH, she was left
	breathless.
}

\eX{
	She said to the king, “The report I heard in my own land about you
	and your wisdom was true.
}

\eX{
	But I did not believe the reports until I came and saw with my own
	eyes that not even the half had been told me; your wisdom and
	wealth surpass the reports that I heard.
}

\eX{
	How fortunate are your men and how fortunate are these your
	courtiers, who are always in attendance on you and can hear your
	wisdom!
}

\eX{
	Praised be YHWH your God, who delighted in you and set you on the
	throne of Israel. It is because of YHWH’s everlasting love for
	Israel that He made you king to administer justice and
	righteousness.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתִּתֵּן לַמֶּלֶךְ מֵאָה וְעֶשְׂרִים כִּכַּר זָהָב וּבְשָׂמִים הַרְבֵּה מְאֹד וְאֶבֶן יְקָרָה לֹא בָא כַבֹּשֶׂם הַהוּא עוֹד לָרֹב אֲשֶׁר־נָתְנָה מַלְכַּת־שְׁבָא לַמֶּלֶךְ
	שְׁלֹמֹה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	She presented the king with one hundred and twenty talents of
	gold, and a large quantity of spices, and precious stones. Never
	again did such a vast quantity of spices arrive as that which the
	queen of Sheba gave to King Solomon.—
}

\eX{
	Moreover, Hiram’s fleet, which carried gold from Ophir, brought in
	from Ophir a huge quantity of almug wood and precious stones.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעַשׂ הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־עֲצֵי הָאַלְמֻגִּים מִסְעָד לְבֵית־יְהֹוָה וּלְבֵית הַמֶּלֶךְ וְכִנֹּרוֹת וּנְבָלִים לַשָּׁרִים לֹא בָא־כֵן עֲצֵי אַלְמֻגִּים וְלֹא נִרְאָה עַד הַיּוֹם
	הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The king used the almug wood for decorations in the House of YHWH
	and in the royal palace, and for harps and lyres for the
	musicians. Such a quantity of almug wood has never arrived or been
	seen to this day.—
}

\eX{
	King Solomon, in turn, gave the queen of Sheba everything she
	wanted and asked for, in addition to what King Solomon gave her
	out of his royal bounty. Then she and her attendants left and
	returned to her own land.
}

\eX{
	The weight of the gold which Solomon received every year was 666
	talents of gold,
}

\eX{
	besides what came from tradesmen, from the traffic of the
	merchants, and from all the kings of Arabia and the governors of
	the regions.
}

\eX{
	King Solomon made 200 shields of beaten gold—600 shekels of gold
	to each shield—
}

\eX{
	and 300 bucklers of beaten gold—three minas of gold to each
	buckler. The king placed them in the Lebanon Forest House.
}

\eX{
	The king also made a large throne of ivory, and he overlaid it
	with refined gold.
}

\eX{
	Six steps led up to the throne, and the throne had a back with a
	rounded top, and arms on either side of the seat. Two lions stood
	beside the arms,
}

\eX{
	and twelve lions stood on the six steps, six on either side. No
	such throne was ever made for any other kingdom.
}

\eX{
	All King Solomon’s drinking cups were of gold, and all the
	utensils of the Lebanon Forest House were of pure gold: silver did
	not count for anything in Solomon’s days.
}

\eX{
	For the king had a Tarshish fleet on the sea, along with Hiram’s
	fleet. Once every three years, the Tarshish fleet came in, bearing
	gold and silver, ivory, apes, and peacocks.
}

\eX{
	King Solomon excelled all the kings on earth in wealth and in
	wisdom.
}

\eX{
	All the world came to pay homage to Solomon and to listen to the
	wisdom with which God had endowed him;
}

\eX{
	and each one would bring his tribute—silver and gold objects,
	robes, weapons and spices, horses and mules—in the amount due each
	year.
}

\eX{
	Solomon assembled chariots and horses. He had 1,400 chariots and
	12,000 horses, which he stationed in the chariot towns and with
	the king in Jerusalem.
}

\eX{
	The king made silver as plentiful in Jerusalem as stones, and
	cedars as plentiful as sycamores in the Shephelah.
}

\eX{
	Solomon’s horses were procured from Mizraim and Kue. The king’s
	dealers would buy them from Kue at a fixed price.
}

\eX{
	A chariot imported from Mizraim cost 600 shekels of silver, and a
	horse 150; these in turn were exported by them to all the kings of
	the Hittites and the kings of the Arameans.
}

\eDtrH{
	King Solomon loved many foreign women in addition to Pharaoh’s
	daughter—Moabite, Ammonite, Edomite, Phoenician, and Hittite
	women,%
}

\eDtrH{
	from the nations of which YHWH had said to the Israelites, “None
	of you shall join them and none of them shall join you, lest they
	turn your heart away to follow their gods.” Such Solomon clung to
	and loved.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He had seven hundred royal wives and three hundred concubines; and
	his wives turned his heart away.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In his old age, his wives turned away Solomon’s heart after other
	gods, and he was not as wholeheartedly devoted to YHWH his God as
	his father David had been.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Solomon followed Ashtoreth the goddess of the Phoenicians, and
	Milcom the abomination of the Ammonites.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Solomon did what was displeasing to YHWH and did not remain loyal
	to YHWH like his father David.%
}

\eDtrH{
	At that time, Solomon built a shrine for Chemosh the abomination
	of Moab on the hill near Jerusalem, and one for Molech the
	abomination of the Ammonites.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And he did the same for all his foreign wives who offered and
	sacrificed to their gods.%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH was angry with Solomon, because his heart turned away from
	YHWH, the God of Israel, who had appeared to him twice%
}

\eDtrH{
	and had commanded him about this matter, not to follow other gods;
	he did not obey what YHWH had commanded.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה לִשְׁלֹמֹה יַעַן אֲשֶׁר הָיְתָה־זֹּאת עִמָּךְ וְלֹא שָׁמַרְתָּ בְּרִיתִי וְחֻקֹּתַי אֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִי עָלֶיךָ קָרֹעַ אֶקְרַע אֶת־הַמַּמְלָכָה מֵעָלֶיךָ
	וּנְתַתִּיהָ לְעַבְדֶּךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	And YHWH said to Solomon, “Because you are guilty of this —you
	have not kept My covenant and the laws which I enjoined upon you—I
	will tear the kingdom away from you and give it to one of your
	servants.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But, for the sake of your father David, I will not do it in your
	lifetime; I will tear it away from your son.%
}

\eDtrH{
	However, I will not tear away the whole kingdom; I will give your
	son one tribe, for the sake of My servant David and for the sake
	of Jerusalem which I have chosen.”%
}

\eX{
	So YHWH raised up an adversary against Solomon, the Edomite Hadad,
	who was of the royal family of Edom.
}

\eX{
	When David was in Edom, Joab the army commander went up to bury
	the slain, and he killed every male in Edom;
}

\eX{
	for Joab and all Israel stayed there for six months until he had
	killed off every male in Edom.
}

\eX{
	But Hadad, together with some Edomite men, servants of his father,
	escaped and headed for Egypt; Hadad was then a young boy.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּקֻמוּ מִמִּדְיָן וַיָּבֹאוּ פָּארָן וַיִּקְחוּ אֲנָשִׁים עִמָּם מִפָּארָן וַיָּבֹאוּ מִצְרַיִם אֶל־פַּרְעֹה מֶלֶךְ־מִצְרַיִם וַיִּתֶּן־לוֹ בַיִת וְלֶחֶם אָמַר לוֹ וְאֶרֶץ
	נָתַן לוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Setting out from Midian, they came to Paran and took along with
	them men from Paran. Thus they came to Egypt, to Pharaoh king of
	Egypt, who gave him a house, assigned a food allowance to him, and
	granted him an estate.
}

\eX{
	Pharaoh took a great liking to Hadad and gave him his
	sister-in-law, the sister of Queen Tahpenes, as wife.
}

\eX{
	The sister of Tahpenes bore him a son, Genubath. Tahpenes weaned
	him in Pharaoh’s palace, and Genubath remained in Pharaoh’s palace
	among the sons of Pharaoh.
}

\eX{
	When Hadad heard in Egypt that David had been laid to rest with
	his fathers and that Joab the army commander was dead, Hadad said
	to Pharaoh, “Give me leave to go to my own country.”
}

\eX{
	Pharaoh replied, “What do you lack with me, that you want to go to
	your own country?” But he said, “Nevertheless, give me leave to
	go.”
}

\eX{
	Another adversary that God raised up against Solomon was Rezon son
	of Eliada, who had fled from his lord, King Hadadezer of Zobah,
}

\eX{
	when David was slaughtering them. He gathered men about him and
	became captain over a troop; they went to Damascus and settled
	there, and they established a kingdom in Damascus.
}

\eX{
	He was an adversary of Israel all the days of Solomon, adding to
	the trouble [caused by] Hadad; he repudiated [the authority of]
	Israel and reigned over Aram.
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam son of Nebat, an Ephraimite of Zeredah, the son of a
	widow whose name was Zeruah, was in Solomon’s service; he raised
	his hand against the king.
}

\eX{
	The circumstances under which he raised his hand against the king
	were as follows: Solomon built the Millo and repaired the breach
	of the city of his father, David.
}

\eX{
	This Jeroboam was an able man, and when Solomon saw that the young
	man was a capable worker, he appointed him over all the forced
	labor of the House of Joseph.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיְהִי בָּעֵת הַהִיא וְיָרבְעָם יָצָא מִירוּשָׁלָ͏ִם וַיִּמְצָא אֹתוֹ אֲחִיָּה הַשִּׁילֹנִי הַנָּבִיא בַּדֶּרֶךְ וְהוּא מִתְכַּסֶּה בְּשַׂלְמָה חֲדָשָׁה וּשְׁנֵיהֶם לְבַדָּם
	בַּשָּׂדֶה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	During that time Jeroboam went out of Jerusalem and the prophet
	Ahijah of Shiloh met him on the way. He had put on a new robe; and
	when the two were alone in the open country,%
}

\eDtrH{
	Ahijah took hold of the new robe he was wearing and tore it into
	twelve pieces.%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Take ten pieces,” he said to Jeroboam. “For thus said YHWH, the
	God of Israel: I am about to tear the kingdom out of Solomon’s
	hands, and I will give you ten tribes.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But one tribe shall remain his—for the sake of My servant David
	and for the sake of Jerusalem, the city that I have chosen out of
	all the tribes of Israel.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	יַעַן אֲשֶׁר עֲזָבוּנִי וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ לְעַשְׁתֹּרֶת אֱלֹהֵי צִדֹנִין לִכְמוֹשׁ אֱלֹהֵי מוֹאָב וּלְמִלְכֹּם אֱלֹהֵי בְנֵי־עַמּוֹן וְלֹא־הָלְכוּ בִדְרָכַי לַעֲשׂוֹת הַיָּשָׁר
	בְּעֵינַי וְחֻקֹּתַי וּמִשְׁפָּטַי כְּדָוִד אָבִיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	However, I will not take the entire kingdom away from him, but
	will keep him as ruler as long as he lives for the sake of My
	servant David whom I chose, and who kept My commandments and My
	laws.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But I will take the kingship out of the hands of his son and give
	it to you—the ten tribes.%
}

\eDtrH{
	To his son I will give one tribe, so that there may be a lamp for
	My servant David forever before Me in Jerusalem—the city where I
	have chosen to establish My name.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But you have been chosen by Me; reign wherever you wish, and you
	shall be king over Israel.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהָיָה אִם־תִּשְׁמַע אֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר אֲצַוֶּךָ וְהָלַכְתָּ בִדְרָכַי וְעָשִׂיתָ הַיָּשָׁר בְּעֵינַי לִשְׁמוֹר חֻקּוֹתַי וּמִצְוֺתַי כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה דָּוִד עַבְדִּי וְהָיִיתִי
	עִמָּךְ וּבָנִיתִי לְךָ בַיִת־נֶאֱמָן כַּאֲשֶׁר בָּנִיתִי לְדָוִד וְנָתַתִּי לְךָ אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	and I will chastise David’s descendants for that [sin], though not
	forever.”%
}

\eX{
	Solomon sought to put Jeroboam to death, but Jeroboam promptly
	fled to King Shishak of Egypt; and he remained in Egypt till the
	death of Solomon.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Solomon’s reign, and all his actions and his
	wisdom, are recorded in the book of the Annals of Solomon.
}

\eX{
	The length of Solomon’s reign in Jerusalem, over all Israel, was
	forty years.
}

\eX{
	Solomon slept with his fathers and was buried in the city of his
	father David; and his son Rehoboam succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	Rehoboam went to Shechem, for all Israel had come to Shechem to
	acclaim him as king.
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam son of Nebat learned of it while he was still in Egypt;
	for Jeroboam had fled from King Solomon, and had settled in Egypt.
}

\eX{
	They sent for him; and Jeroboam and all the assembly of Israel
	came and spoke to Rehoboam as follows:
}

\eX{
	“Your father made our yoke heavy. Now lighten the harsh labor and
	the heavy yoke which your father laid on us, and we will serve
	you.”
}

\eX{
	He answered them, “Go away for three days and then come back to
	me.” So the people went away.
}

\eX{
	King Rehoboam took counsel with the elders who had served his
	father Solomon during his lifetime. He said, “What answer do you
	advise [me] to give to this people?”
}

\eX{
	They answered him, “If you will be a servant to those people today
	and serve them, and if you respond to them with kind words, they
	will be your servants always.”
}

\eX{
	But he ignored the advice that the elders gave him, and took
	counsel with the young men who had grown up with him and were
	serving him.
}

\eX{
	“What,” he asked, “do you advise that we reply to the people who
	said to me, ‘Lighten the yoke that your father placed upon us’?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְדַבְּרוּ אֵלָיו הַיְלָדִים אֲשֶׁר גָּדְלוּ אִתּוֹ לֵאמֹר כֹּה־תֹאמַר לָעָם הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר דִּבְּרוּ אֵלֶיךָ לֵאמֹר אָבִיךָ הִכְבִּיד אֶת־עֻלֵּנוּ וְאַתָּה הָקֵל מֵעָלֵינוּ כֹּה
	תְּדַבֵּר אֲלֵיהֶם קטנִּי עָבָה מִמּתְנֵי אָבִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And the young men who had grown up with him answered, “Speak thus
	to the people who said to you, ‘Your father made our yoke heavy,
	now you make it lighter for us.’ Say to them, ‘My little finger is
	thicker than my father’s loins.
}

\eX{
	My father imposed a heavy yoke on you, and I will add to your
	yoke; my father flogged you with whips, but I will flog you with
	scorpions.’”
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam and all the people came to Rehoboam on the third day,
	since the king had told them: “Come back on the third day.”
}

\eX{
	The king answered the people harshly, ignoring the advice that the
	elders had given him.
}

\eX{
	He spoke to them in accordance with the advice of the young men,
	and said, “My father made your yoke heavy, but I will add to your
	yoke; my father flogged you with whips, but I will flog you with
	scorpions.”
}

\eX{
	(The king did not listen to the people; for YHWH had brought it
	about in order to fulfill the promise that YHWH had made through
	Ahijah the Shilonite to Jeroboam son of Nebat.)
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּרְא כּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי לֹא־שָׁמַע הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲלֵהֶם וַיָּשִׁבוּ הָעָם אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּבָר לֵאמֹר מַה־לָּנוּ חֵלֶק בְּדָוִד וְלֹא־נַחֲלָה בְּבֶן־יִשַׁי לְאֹהָלֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל
	עַתָּה רְאֵה בֵיתְךָ דָּוִד וַיֵּלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל לְאֹהָלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When all Israel saw that the king had not listened to them, the
	people answered the king: “We have no portion in David, No share
	in Jesse’s son! To your tents, O Israel! Now look to your own
	House, O David.” So the Israelites returned to their homes.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 12:16. To your tents, Israel echoes the old tribal cry. The united
%		monarchy comes apart in a sentence that sounds older than the monarchy.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But Rehoboam continued to reign over the Israelites who lived in
	the towns of Judah.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח הַמֶּלֶךְ רְחַבְעָם אֶת־אֲדֹרָם אֲשֶׁר עַל־הַמַּס וַיִּרְגְּמוּ כל־יִשְׂרָאֵל בּוֹ אֶבֶן וַיָּמֹת וְהַמֶּלֶךְ רְחַבְעָם הִתְאַמֵּץ לַעֲלוֹת בַּמֶּרְכָּבָה לָנוּס
	יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	King Rehoboam sent Adoram, who was in charge of the forced labor,
	but all Israel pelted him to death with stones. Thereupon King
	Rehoboam hurriedly mounted his chariot and fled to Jerusalem.
}

\eX{
	Thus Israel revolted against the House of David, as is still the
	case.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי כִּשְׁמֹעַ כּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי־שָׁב יָרבְעָם וַיִּשְׁלְחוּ וַיִּקְרְאוּ אֹתוֹ אֶל־הָעֵדָה וַיַּמְלִיכוּ אֹתוֹ עַל־כּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא הָיָה אַחֲרֵי בֵית־דָּוִד זוּלָתִי
	שֵׁבֶט־יְהוּדָה לְבַדּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When all Israel heard that Jeroboam had returned, they sent
	messengers and summoned him to the assembly and made him king over
	all Israel. Only the tribe of Judah remained loyal to the House of
	David.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	(ויבאו) [וַיָּבֹא] רְחַבְעָם יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וַיַּקְהֵל אֶת־כּל־בֵּית יְהוּדָה וְאֶת־שֵׁבֶט בִּנְיָמִן מֵאָה וּשְׁמֹנִים אֶלֶף בָּחוּר עֹשֵׂה מִלְחָמָה לְהִלָּחֵם עִם־בֵּית
	יִשְׂרָאֵל לְהָשִׁיב אֶת־הַמְּלוּכָה לִרְחַבְעָם בֶּן־שְׁלֹמֹה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	On his return to Jerusalem, Rehoboam mustered all the House of
	Judah and the tribe of Benjamin, 180,000 picked warriors, to fight
	against the House of Israel, in order to restore the kingship to
	Rehoboam son of Solomon.
}

\eX{
	“Say to King Rehoboam son of Solomon of Judah, and to all the
	House of Judah and Benjamin and the rest of the people:
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	כֹּה אָמַר יְהֹוָה לֹא־תַעֲלוּ וְלֹא־תִלָּחֲמוּן עִם־אֲחֵיכֶם בְּנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל שׁוּבוּ אִישׁ לְבֵיתוֹ כִּי מֵאִתִּי נִהְיָה הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה וַיִּשְׁמְעוּ אֶת־דְּבַר יְהֹוָה
	וַיָּשֻׁבוּ לָלֶכֶת כִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Thus said YHWH: You shall not set out to make war on your kinsmen
	the Israelites. Let every man return to his home, for this thing
	has been brought about by Me.” They heeded the word of YHWH and
	turned back, in accordance with the word of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam fortified Shechem in the hill country of Ephraim and
	resided there; he moved out from there and fortified Penuel.
}

\eDtrH{
	Jeroboam said to himself, “Now the kingdom may well return to the
	House of David.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	אִם־יַעֲלֶה הָעָם הַזֶּה לַעֲשׂוֹת זְבָחִים בְּבֵית־יְהֹוָה בִּירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וְשָׁב לֵב הָעָם הַזֶּה אֶל־אֲדֹנֵיהֶם אֶל־רְחַבְעָם מֶלֶךְ יְהוּדָה וַהֲרָגֻנִי וְשָׁבוּ אֶל־רְחַבְעָם
	מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	If these people still go up to offer sacrifices at the House of
	YHWH in Jerusalem, the heart of these people will turn back to
	their master, King Rehoboam of Judah; they will kill me and go
	back to King Rehoboam of Judah.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	So the king took counsel and made two golden calves. He said to
	the people, “You have been going up to Jerusalem long enough. This
	is your god, O Israel, who brought you up from the land of Egypt!”%
}

\eDtrH{
	That proved to be a cause of guilt, for the people went to worship
	[the calf at Bethel and] the one at Dan.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He also made cult places and appointed priests from the ranks of
	the people who were not of Levite descent.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַשׂ יָרבְעָם חָג בַּחֹדֶשׁ הַשְּׁמִינִי בַחֲמִשָּׁה־עָשָׂר יוֹם לַחֹדֶשׁ כֶּחָג אֲשֶׁר בִּיהוּדָה וַיַּעַל עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ כֵּן עָשָׂה בְּבֵית־אֵל לְזַבֵּחַ לָעֲגָלִים
	אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה וְהֶעֱמִיד בְּבֵית אֵל אֶת־כֹּהֲנֵי הַבָּמוֹת אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַל עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה בְּבֵית־אֵל בַּחֲמִשָּׁה עָשָׂר יוֹם בַּחֹדֶשׁ הַשְּׁמִינִי בַּחֹדֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר־בָּדָא (מלבד) [מִלִּבּוֹ] וַיַּעַשׂ חָג לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל
	וַיַּעַל עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לְהַקְטִיר׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	On the fifteenth day of the eighth month—the month in which he had
	contrived of his own mind to establish a festival for the
	Israelites—Jeroboam ascended the altar that he had made in Bethel.
	As he ascended the altar to present an offering,%
}

\eX{
	a man of God arrived at Bethel from Judah at the command of YHWH.
	While Jeroboam was standing on the altar to present the offering,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְרָא עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ בִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה וַיֹּאמֶר מִזְבֵּחַ מִזְבֵּחַ כֹּה אָמַר יְהֹוָה הִנֵּה־בֵן נוֹלָד לְבֵית־דָּוִד יֹאשִׁיָּהוּ שְׁמוֹ וְזָבַח עָלֶיךָ אֶת־כֹּהֲנֵי
	הַבָּמוֹת הַמַּקְטִרִים עָלֶיךָ וְעַצְמוֹת אָדָם יִשְׂרְפוּ עָלֶיךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He gave a portent on that day, saying, “Here is the portent that
	YHWH has decreed: This altar shall break apart, and the ashes on
	it shall be spilled.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי כִשְׁמֹעַ הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־דְּבַר אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר קָרָא עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ בְּבֵית־אֵל וַיִּשְׁלַח יָרבְעָם אֶת־יָדוֹ מֵעַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לֵאמֹר תִּפְשֻׂהוּ וַתִּיבַשׁ
	יָדוֹ אֲשֶׁר שָׁלַח עָלָיו וְלֹא יָכֹל לַהֲשִׁיבָהּ אֵלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When the king heard what the man of God had proclaimed against the
	altar in Bethel, Jeroboam stretched out his arm above the altar
	and cried, “Seize him!” But the arm that he stretched out against
	him became rigid, and he could not draw it back.
}

\eX{
	The altar broke apart and its ashes were spilled—the very portent
	that the man of God had announced at YHWH’s command.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעַן הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים חַל־נָא אֶת־פְּנֵי יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ וְהִתְפַּלֵּל בַּעֲדִי וְתָשֹׁב יָדִי אֵלָי וַיְחַל אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים אֶת־פְּנֵי יְהֹוָה
	וַתָּשׁב יַד־הַמֶּלֶךְ אֵלָיו וַתְּהִי כְּבָרִאשֹׁנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the king spoke up and said to the man of God, “Please entreat
	YHWH your God and pray for me that I may be able to draw back my
	arm.” The man of God entreated YHWH and the king was able to draw
	his arm back; it became as it was before.
}

\eX{
	The king said to the man of God, “Come with me to my house and
	have some refreshment; and I shall give you a gift.”
}

\eX{
	But the man of God replied to the king, “Even if you give me half
	your wealth, I will not go in with you, nor will I eat bread or
	drink water in this place;
}

\eX{
	for so I was commanded by the word of YHWH: You shall eat no bread
	and drink no water, nor shall you go back by the road by which you
	came.”
}

\eX{
	So he left by another road and did not go back by the road on
	which he had come to Bethel.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְנָבִיא אֶחָד זָקֵן יֹשֵׁב בְּבֵית־אֵל וַיָּבוֹא בְנוֹ וַיְסַפֶּר־לוֹ אֶת־כּל־הַמַּעֲשֶׂה אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים הַיּוֹם בְּבֵית־אֵל אֶת־הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר
	אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיְסַפְּרוּם לַאֲבִיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	There was an old prophet living in Bethel; and his sons came and
	told him all the things that the man of God had done that day in
	Bethel [and] the words that he had spoken to the king. When they
	told it to their father,
}

\eX{
	their father said to them, “Which road did he leave by?” His sons
	had seen the road taken by the man of God who had come from Judah.
}

\eX{
	“Saddle the ass for me,” he said to his sons. They saddled the ass
	for him, and he mounted it
}

\eX{
	and rode after the man of God. He came upon him sitting under a
	terebinth and said to him, “Are you the man of God who came from
	Judah?” “Yes, I am,” he answered.
}

\eX{
	He replied, “I may not go back with you and enter your home; and I
	may not eat bread or drink water in this place;
}

\eX{
	the order I received by the word of YHWH was: You shall not eat
	bread or drink water there; nor shall you return by the road on
	which you came.”
}

\eX{
	“I am a prophet, too,” said the other, “and an angel said to me by
	command of YHWH: Bring him back with you to your house, that he
	may eat bread and drink water.” He was lying to him.
}

\eX{
	So he went back with him, and he ate bread and drank water in his
	house.
}

\eX{
	While they were sitting at the table, the word of YHWH came to the
	prophet who had brought him back.
}

\eX{
	He cried out to the man of God who had come from Judah: “Thus said
	YHWH: Because you have flouted the word of YHWH and have not
	observed what YHWH your God commanded you,
}

\eX{
	but have gone back and eaten bread and drunk water in the place of
	which He said to you, ‘Do not eat bread or drink water [there],’
	your corpse shall not come to the grave of your fathers.”
}

\eX{
	After he had eaten bread and had drunk, he saddled the ass for
	him—for the prophet whom he had brought back.
}

\eX{
	He set out, and a lion came upon him on the road and killed him.
	His corpse lay on the road, with the ass standing beside it, and
	the lion also standing beside the corpse.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהִנֵּה אֲנָשִׁים עֹבְרִים וַיִּרְאוּ אֶת־הַנְּבֵלָה מֻשְׁלֶכֶת בַּדֶּרֶךְ וְאֶת־הָאַרְיֵה עֹמֵד אֵצֶל הַנְּבֵלָה וַיָּבֹאוּ וַיְדַבְּרוּ בָעִיר אֲשֶׁר הַנָּבִיא הַזָּקֵן יֹשֵׁב
	בָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Some men who passed by saw the corpse lying on the road and the
	lion standing beside the corpse; they went and told it in the town
	where the old prophet lived.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁמַע הַנָּבִיא אֲשֶׁר הֱשִׁיבוֹ מִן־הַדֶּרֶךְ וַיֹּאמֶר אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים הוּא אֲשֶׁר מָרָה אֶת־פִּי יְהֹוָה וַיִּתְּנֵהוּ יְהֹוָה לָאַרְיֵה וַיִּשְׁבְּרֵהוּ וַיְמִתֵהוּ
	כִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר־לוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And when the prophet who had brought him back from the road heard
	it, he said, “That is the man of God who flouted YHWH’s command;
	YHWH gave him over to the lion, which mauled him and killed him in
	accordance with the word that YHWH had spoken to him.”
}

\eX{
	He said to his sons, “Saddle the ass for me,” and they did so.
}

\eX{
	He set out and found the corpse lying on the road, with the ass
	and the lion standing beside the corpse; the lion had not eaten
	the corpse nor had it mauled the ass.
}

\eX{
	The prophet lifted up the corpse of the man of God, laid it on the
	ass, and brought it back; it was brought to the town of the old
	prophet for lamentation and burial.
}

\eX{
	He laid the corpse in his own burial place; and they lamented over
	it, “Alas, my brother!”
}

\eX{
	After burying him, he said to his sons, “When I die, bury me in
	the grave where the man of God lies buried; lay my bones beside
	his.
}

\eX{
	For what he announced by the word of YHWH against the altar in
	Bethel, and against all the cult places in the towns of Samaria,
	shall surely come true.”
}

\eX{
	Even after this incident, Jeroboam did not turn back from his evil
	way, but kept on appointing priests for the shrines from the ranks
	of the people. He ordained as priests of the shrines any who so
	desired.
}

\eX{
	Thereby the House of Jeroboam incurred guilt—to their utter
	annihilation from the face of the earth.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יָרבְעָם לְאִשְׁתּוֹ קוּמִי נָא וְהִשְׁתַּנִּית וְלֹא יֵדְעוּ כִּי־[אַתְּ] (אתי) אֵשֶׁת יָרבְעָם וְהָלַכְתְּ שִׁלֹה הִנֵּה־שָׁם אֲחִיָּה הַנָּבִיא הוּא־דִבֶּר עָלַי
	לְמֶלֶךְ עַל־הָעָם הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Jeroboam said to his wife, “Go and disguise yourself, so that you
	will not be recognized as Jeroboam’s wife, and go to Shiloh. The
	prophet Ahijah lives there, the one who predicted that I would be
	king over this people.
}

\eX{
	Take with you ten loaves, some wafers, and a jug of honey, and go
	to him; he will tell you what will happen to the boy.”
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam’s wife did so; she left and went to Shiloh and came to
	the house of Ahijah. Now Ahijah could not see, for his eyes had
	become sightless with age;
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיהֹוָה אָמַר אֶל־אֲחִיָּהוּ הִנֵּה אֵשֶׁת יָרבְעָם בָּאָה לִדְרֹשׁ דָּבָר מֵעִמְּךָ אֶל־בְּנָהּ כִּי־חֹלֶה הוּא כָּזֹה וְכָזֶה תְּדַבֵּר אֵלֶיהָ וִיהִי כְבֹאָהּ וְהִיא
	מִתְנַכֵּרָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	but YHWH had said to Ahijah, “Jeroboam’s wife is coming to inquire
	of you concerning her son, who is sick. Speak to her thus and
	thus. When she arrives, she will be in disguise.”
}

\eX{
	Ahijah heard the sound of her feet as she came through the door,
	and he said, “Come in, wife of Jeroboam. Why are you disguised? I
	have a harsh message for you.
}

\eX{
	Go tell Jeroboam: Thus said YHWH, the God of Israel: I raised you
	up from among the people and made you a ruler over My people
	Israel;
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וָאֶקְרַע אֶת־הַמַּמְלָכָה מִבֵּית דָּוִד וָאֶתְּנֶהָ לָךְ וְלֹא־הָיִיתָ כְּעַבְדִּי דָוִד אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַר מִצְוֺתַי וַאֲשֶׁר־הָלַךְ אַחֲרַי בְּכל־לְבָבוֹ לַעֲשׂוֹת רַק הַיָּשָׁר
	בְּעֵינָי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	I tore away the kingdom from the House of David and gave it to
	you. But you have not been like My servant David, who kept My
	commandments and followed Me with all his heart, doing only what
	was right in My sight.
}

\eX{
	You have acted worse than all those who preceded you; you have
	gone and made for yourself other gods and molten images to vex Me;
	and Me you have cast behind your back.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	לָכֵן הִנְנִי מֵבִיא רָעָה אֶל־בֵּית יָרבְעָם וְהִכְרַתִּי לְיָרבְעָם מַשְׁתִּין בְּקִיר עָצוּר וְעָזוּב בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל וּבִעַרְתִּי אַחֲרֵי בֵית־יָרבְעָם כַּאֲשֶׁר יְבַעֵר הַגָּלָל
	עַד־תֻּמּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Therefore I will bring disaster upon the House of Jeroboam and
	will cut off from Jeroboam every male, bond and free, in Israel. I
	will sweep away the House of Jeroboam utterly, as dung is swept
	away.
}

\eX{
	Anyone belonging to Jeroboam who dies in the town shall be
	devoured by dogs; and anyone who dies in the open country shall be
	eaten by the birds of the air; for YHWH has spoken.
}

\eX{
	As for you, go back home; as soon as you set foot in the town, the
	child will die.
}

\eX{
	And all Israel shall lament over him and bury him; he alone of
	Jeroboam’s family shall be brought to burial, for in him alone of
	the House of Jeroboam has some devotion been found to YHWH, the
	God of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Moreover, YHWH will raise up a king over Israel who will destroy
	the House of Jeroboam, this day and even now.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהִכָּה יְהֹוָה אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר יָנוּד הַקָּנֶה בַּמַּיִם וְנָתַשׁ אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵעַל הָאֲדָמָה הַטּוֹבָה הַזֹּאת אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לַאֲבוֹתֵיהֶם וְזֵרָם מֵעֵבֶר לַנָּהָר
	יַעַן אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ אֶת־אֲשֵׁרֵיהֶם מַכְעִיסִים אֶת־יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He will forsake Israel because of the sins that Jeroboam committed
	and led Israel to commit.”
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam’s wife got up and left, and she went to Tirzah. As soon
	as she stepped over the threshold of her house, the child died.
}

\eX{
	They buried him and all Israel lamented over him, in accordance
	with the word that YHWH had spoken through His servant the prophet
	Ahijah.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Jeroboam’s reign, how he fought and how he
	ruled, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam reigned twenty-two years; then he slept with his fathers,
	and his son Nadab succeeded him as king.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּרְחַבְעָם בֶּן־שְׁלֹמֹה מָלַךְ בִּיהוּדָה בֶּן־אַרְבָּעִים וְאַחַת שָׁנָה רְחַבְעָם בְּמלְכוֹ וּשְׁבַע עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה מָלַךְ בִּירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם הָעִיר אֲשֶׁר־בָּחַר יְהֹוָה לָשׂוּם
	אֶת־שְׁמוֹ שָׁם מִכֹּל שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְשֵׁם אִמּוֹ נַעֲמָה הָעַמֹּנִית׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Judah did what was displeasing to YHWH, and angered Him more than
	their fathers had done by the sins that they committed.
}

\eX{
	They too built for themselves shrines, pillars, and sacred posts
	on every high hill and under every leafy tree;
}

\eX{
	there were also male prostitutes in the land. [Judah] imitated all
	the abhorrent practices of the nations that YHWH had dispossessed
	before the Israelites.
}

\eX{
	In the fifth year of King Rehoboam, King Shishak of Egypt marched
	against Jerusalem
}

\eX{
	and carried off the treasures of the House of YHWH and the
	treasures of the royal palace. He carried off everything; he even
	carried off all the golden shields that Solomon had made.
}

\eX{
	King Rehoboam had bronze shields made instead, and he entrusted
	them to the officers of the guard who guarded the entrance to the
	royal palace.
}

\eX{
	Whenever the king went into the House of YHWH, the guards would
	carry them and then bring them back to the armory of the guards.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Rehoboam’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	Rehoboam slept with his fathers and was buried with his fathers in
	the City of David; his mother’s name was Naamah the Ammonitess.
	His son Abijam succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the eighteenth year of King Jeroboam son of Nebat, Abijam
	became king over Judah.
}

\eX{
	He reigned three years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Maacah
	daughter of Abishalom.
}

\eX{
	He continued in all the sins that his father before him had
	committed; he was not wholehearted with YHWH his God, like his
	father David.
}

\eX{
	Yet, for the sake of David, YHWH his God gave him a lamp in
	Jerusalem, by raising up his descendant after him and by
	preserving Jerusalem.
}

\eX{
	For David had done what was pleasing to YHWH and never turned
	throughout his life from all that He had commanded him, except in
	the matter of Uriah the Hittite.
}

\eX{
	There was war between Abijam and Jeroboam all the days of his
	life.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Abijam’s reign and all his actions are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah; there was war
	between Abijam and Jeroboam.
}

\eX{
	Abijam slept with his fathers; he was buried in the City of David,
	and his son Asa succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the twentieth year of King Jeroboam of Israel, Asa became king
	over Judah.
}

\eX{
	He reigned forty-one years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was
	Maacah daughter of Abishalom.
}

\eX{
	Asa did what was pleasing to YHWH, as his father David had done.
}

\eX{
	He expelled the male prostitutes from the land, and he removed all
	the idols that his ancestors had made.
}

\eX{
	He also deposed his mother Maacah from the rank of queen mother,
	because she had made an abominable thing for [the goddess]
	Asherah. Asa cut down her abominable thing and burnt it in the
	Wadi Kidron.
}

\eX{
	The shrines, indeed, were not abolished; however, Asa was
	wholehearted with YHWH his God all his life.
}

\eX{
	He brought into the House of YHWH all the consecrated things of
	his father and his own consecrated things —silver, gold, and
	utensils.
}

\eX{
	There was war between Asa and King Baasha of Israel all their
	days.
}

\eX{
	King Baasha of Israel advanced against Judah, and he fortified
	Ramah to prevent anyone belonging to King Asa from going out or
	coming in.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח אָסָא אֶת־כּל־הַכֶּסֶף וְהַזָּהָב הַנּוֹתָרִים בְּאוֹצְרוֹת בֵּית־יְהֹוָה וְאֶת־אוֹצְרוֹת בֵּית (מלך) [הַמֶּלֶךְ] וַיִּתְּנֵם בְּיַד־עֲבָדָיו וַיִּשְׁלָחֵם הַמֶּלֶךְ
	אָסָא אֶל־בֶּן־הֲדַד בֶּן־טַבְרִמֹּן בֶּן־חֶזְיוֹן מֶלֶךְ אֲרָם הַיֹּשֵׁב בְּדַמֶּשֶׂק לֵאמֹר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“There is a pact between you and me, and between your father and
	my father. I herewith send you a gift of silver and gold: Go and
	break your pact with King Baasha of Israel, so that he may
	withdraw from me.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁמַע בֶּן־הֲדַד אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אָסָא וַיִּשְׁלַח אֶת־שָׂרֵי הַחֲיָלִים אֲשֶׁר־לוֹ עַל־עָרֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיַּךְ אֶת־עִיּוֹן וְאֶת־דָּן וְאֵת אָבֵל בֵּית־מַעֲכָה וְאֵת
	כּל־כִּנְרוֹת עַל כּל־אֶרֶץ נַפְתָּלִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ben-hadad responded to King Asa’s request; he sent his army
	commanders against the towns of Israel and captured Ijon, Dan,
	Abel-beth-maacah, and all Chinneroth, as well as all the land of
	Naphtali.
}

\eX{
	When Baasha heard about it, he stopped fortifying Ramah and
	remained in Tirzah.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהַמֶּלֶךְ אָסָא הִשְׁמִיעַ אֶת־כּל־יְהוּדָה אֵין נָקִי וַיִּשְׂאוּ אֶת־אַבְנֵי הָרָמָה וְאֶת־עֵצֶיהָ אֲשֶׁר בָּנָה בַּעְשָׁא וַיִּבֶן בָּם הַמֶּלֶךְ אָסָא אֶת־גֶּבַע בִּנְיָמִן
	וְאֶת־הַמִּצְפָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then King Asa mustered all Judah, with no exemptions; and they
	carried away the stones and timber with which Baasha had fortified
	Ramah. With these King Asa fortified Geba of Benjamin, and Mizpah.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְיֶתֶר כּל־דִּבְרֵי־אָסָא וְכל־גְּבוּרָתוֹ וְכל־אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה וְהֶעָרִים אֲשֶׁר בָּנָה הֲלֹא־הֵמָּה כְתוּבִים עַל־סֵפֶר דִּבְרֵי הַיָּמִים לְמַלְכֵי יְהוּדָה רַק לְעֵת זִקְנָתוֹ
	חָלָה אֶת־רַגְלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	All the other events of Asa’s reign, and all his exploits, and all
	his actions, and the towns that he fortified, are recorded in the
	Annals of the Kings of Judah. However, in his old age he suffered
	from a foot ailment.
}

\eX{
	Asa slept with his fathers and was buried with his fathers in the
	city of his father David. His son Jehoshaphat succeeded him as
	king.
}

\eX{
	Nadab son of Jeroboam had become king over Israel in the second
	year of King Asa of Judah, and he reigned over Israel for two
	years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; he continued in the ways of
	his father, in the sins which he caused Israel to commit.
}

\eX{
	Then Baasha son of Ahijah, of the House of Issachar, conspired
	against him; and Baasha struck him down at Gibbethon of the
	Philistines, while Nadab and all Israel were laying siege to
	Gibbethon.
}

\eX{
	Baasha killed him in the third year of King Asa of Judah and
	became king in his stead.
}

\eX{
	As soon as he became king, he struck down all the House of
	Jeroboam; he did not spare a single soul belonging to Jeroboam
	until he destroyed it—in accordance with the word that YHWH had
	spoken through His servant Ahijah the Shilonite—
}

\eX{
	because of the sins which Jeroboam committed and which he caused
	Israel to commit, thereby vexing YHWH, the God of Israel.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Nadab’s reign and all his actions are recorded
	in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	There was war between Asa and King Baasha of Israel all their
	days.
}

\eX{
	In the third year of King Asa of Judah, Baasha son of Ahijah
	became king in Tirzah over all Israel—for twenty-four years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; he followed the ways of
	Jeroboam and the sins which he caused Israel to commit.
}

\eX{
	The word of YHWH came to Jehu son of Hanani against Baasha:
}

\eX{
	“Because I lifted you up from the dust and made you a ruler over
	My people Israel, but you followed the way of Jeroboam and caused
	My people Israel to sin, vexing Me with their sins—
}

\eX{
	I am going to sweep away Baasha and his house. I will make your
	house like the House of Jeroboam son of Nebat.
}

\eX{
	Anyone belonging to Baasha who dies in the town shall be devoured
	by dogs, and anyone belonging to him who dies in the open country
	shall be devoured by the birds of the sky.”
}

\eX{
	The other events of Baasha’s reign and his actions and his
	exploits are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Baasha slept with his fathers and was buried in Tirzah. His son
	Elah succeeded him as king.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְגַם בְּיַד־יֵהוּא בֶן־חֲנָנִי הַנָּבִיא דְּבַר־יְהֹוָה הָיָה אֶל־בַּעְשָׁא וְאֶל־בֵּיתוֹ וְעַל כּל־הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה בְּעֵינֵי יְהֹוָה לְהַכְעִיסוֹ בְּמַעֲשֵׂה יָדָיו
	לִהְיוֹת כְּבֵית יָרבְעָם וְעַל אֲשֶׁר־הִכָּה אֹתוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	In the twenty-sixth year of King Asa of Judah, Elah son of Baasha
	became king over Israel, at Tirzah—for two years.
}

\eX{
	His officer Zimri, commander of half the chariotry, committed
	treason against him while he was at Tirzah drinking himself drunk
	in the house of Arza, who was in charge of the palace at Tirzah.
}

\eX{
	Zimri entered, struck him down, and killed him; he succeeded him
	as king in the twenty-seventh year of King Asa of Judah.
}

\eX{
	No sooner had he become king and ascended the throne than he
	struck down all the House of Baasha; he did not leave a single
	male of his, nor any kinsman or friend.
}

\eX{
	Thus Zimri destroyed all the House of Baasha, in accordance with
	the word that YHWH had spoken through the prophet Jehu—
}

\eX{
	because of the sinful acts which Baasha and his son Elah
	committed, and which they caused Israel to commit, vexing YHWH,
	the God of Israel, with their false gods.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Elah’s reign and all his actions are recorded
	in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	During the twenty-seventh year of King Asa of Judah, Zimri reigned
	in Tirzah for seven days. At the time, the troops were encamped at
	Gibbethon of the Philistines.
}

\eX{
	When the troops who were encamped there learned that Zimri had
	committed treason and had struck down the king, that very day, in
	the camp, all Israel acclaimed the army commander Omri king over
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	Omri and all Israel then withdrew from Gibbethon and laid siege to
	Tirzah.
}

\eX{
	When Zimri saw that the town was taken, he went into the citadel
	of the royal palace and burned down the royal palace over himself.
	And so he died—
}

\eX{
	because of the sins which he committed and caused Israel to
	commit, doing what was displeasing to YHWH and following the ways
	of Jeroboam.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Zimri’s reign, and the treason which he
	committed, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Then the people of Israel split into two factions: a part of the
	people followed Tibni son of Ginath to make him king, and the
	other part followed Omri.
}

\eX{
	Those who followed Omri proved stronger than those who followed
	Tibni son of Ginath; Tibni died and Omri became king.
}

\eX{
	In the thirty-first year of King Asa of Judah, Omri became king
	over Israel—for twelve years. He reigned in Tirzah six years.
}

\eX{
	Then he bought the hill of Samaria from Shemer for two talents of
	silver; he built [a town] on the hill and named the town which he
	built Samaria, after Shemer, the owner of the hill.
}

\eX{
	Omri did what was displeasing to YHWH; he was worse than all who
	preceded him.
}

\eX{
	He followed all the ways of Jeroboam son of Nebat and the sins
	which he committed and caused Israel to commit, vexing YHWH, the
	God of Israel, with their futilities.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Omri’s reign, [and] his actions, and the
	exploits he performed, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	Omri slept with his fathers and was buried in Samaria; and his son
	Ahab succeeded him as king.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאַחְאָב בֶּן־עמְרִי מָלַךְ עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵל בִּשְׁנַת שְׁלֹשִׁים וּשְׁמֹנֶה שָׁנָה לְאָסָא מֶלֶךְ יְהוּדָה וַיִּמְלֹךְ אַחְאָב בֶּן־עמְרִי עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּשֹׁמְרוֹן עֶשְׂרִים
	וּשְׁתַּיִם שָׁנָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ahab son of Omri became king over Israel in the thirty-eighth year
	of King Asa of Judah, and Ahab son of Omri reigned over Israel in
	Samaria for twenty-two years.
}

\eX{
	Ahab son of Omri did what was displeasing to YHWH, more than all
	who preceded him.
}

\eX{
	Not content to follow the sins of Jeroboam son of Nebat, he took
	as wife Jezebel daughter of King Ethbaal of the Phoenicians, and
	he went and served Baal and worshiped him.
}

\eX{
	He erected an altar to Baal in the temple of Baal which he built
	in Samaria.
}

\eX{
	Ahab also made a sacred post. Ahab did more to vex YHWH, the God
	of Israel, than all the kings of Israel who preceded him.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	בְּיָמָיו בָּנָה חִיאֵל בֵּית הָאֱלִי אֶת־יְרִיחֹה בַּאֲבִירָם בְּכֹרוֹ יִסְּדָהּ (ובשגיב) [וּבִשְׂגוּב] צְעִירוֹ הִצִּיב דְּלָתֶיהָ כִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר בְּיַד
	יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן־נוּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	During his reign, Hiel the Bethelite fortified Jericho. He laid
	its foundations at the cost of Abiram his first-born, and set its
	gates in place at the cost of Segub his youngest, in accordance
	with the words that YHWH had spoken through Joshua son of Nun.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלִיָּהוּ הַתִּשְׁבִּי מִתֹּשָׁבֵי גִלְעָד אֶל־אַחְאָב חַי־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר עָמַדְתִּי לְפָנָיו אִם־יִהְיֶה הַשָּׁנִים הָאֵלֶּה טַל וּמָטָר כִּי
	אִם־לְפִי דְבָרִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Elijah the Tishbite, an inhabitant of Gilead, said to Ahab, “As
	YHWH lives, the God of Israel whom I serve, there will be no dew
	or rain except at my bidding.”
}

\eX{
	“Leave this place; turn eastward and go into hiding by the Wadi
	Cherith, which is east of the Jordan.
}

\eX{
	You will drink from the wadi, and I have commanded the ravens to
	feed you there.”
}

\eX{
	He proceeded to do as YHWH had bidden: he went, and he stayed by
	the Wadi Cherith, which is east of the Jordan.
}

\eX{
	The ravens brought him bread and meat every morning and every
	evening, and he drank from the wadi.
}

\eX{
	After some time the wadi dried up, because there was no rain in
	the land.
}

\eX{
	“Go at once to Zarephath of Sidon, and stay there; I have
	designated a widow there to feed you.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּקם וַיֵּלֶךְ צָרְפַתָה וַיָּבֹא אֶל־פֶּתַח הָעִיר וְהִנֵּה־שָׁם אִשָּׁה אַלְמָנָה מְקֹשֶׁשֶׁת עֵצִים וַיִּקְרָא אֵלֶיהָ וַיֹּאמַר קְחִי־נָא לִי מְעַט־מַיִם בַּכְּלִי
	וְאֶשְׁתֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So he went at once to Zarephath. When he came to the entrance of
	the town, a widow was there gathering wood. He called out to her,
	“Please bring me a little water in your pitcher, and let me
	drink.”
}

\eX{
	As she went to fetch it, he called out to her, “Please bring along
	a piece of bread for me.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתֹּאמֶר חַי־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ אִם־יֶשׁ־לִי מָעוֹג כִּי אִם־מְלֹא כַף־קֶמַח בַּכַּד וּמְעַט־שֶׁמֶן בַּצַּפָּחַת וְהִנְנִי מְקֹשֶׁשֶׁת שְׁנַיִם עֵצִים וּבָאתִי וַעֲשִׂיתִיהוּ לִי
	וְלִבְנִי וַאֲכַלְנֻהוּ וָמָתְנוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“Don’t be afraid,” said Elijah to her. “Go and do as you have
	said; but first make me a small cake from what you have there, and
	bring it out to me; then make some for yourself and your son.
}

\eX{
	For thus said YHWH, the God of Israel: The jar of flour shall not
	give out and the jug of oil shall not fail until the day that YHWH
	sends rain upon the ground.”
}

\eX{
	She went and did as Elijah had spoken, and she and he and her
	household had food for a long time.
}

\eX{
	The jar of flour did not give out, nor did the jug of oil fail,
	just as YHWH had spoken through Elijah.
}

\eX{
	After a while, the son of the mistress of the house fell sick, and
	his illness grew worse, until he had no breath left in him.
}

\eX{
	She said to Elijah, “What harm have I done you, O man of God, that
	you should come here to recall my sin and cause the death of my
	son?”
}

\eX{
	“Give me the boy,” he said to her; and taking him from her arms,
	he carried him to the upper chamber where he was staying, and laid
	him down on his own bed.
}

\eX{
	He cried out to YHWH and said, “O LORD my God, will You bring
	calamity upon this widow whose guest I am, and let her son die?”
}

\eX{
	Then he stretched out over the child three times, and cried out to
	YHWH, saying, “O LORD my God, let this child’s life return to his
	body!”
}

\eX{
	YHWH heard Elijah’s plea; the child’s life returned to his body,
	and he revived.
}

\eX{
	Elijah picked up the child and brought him down from the upper
	room into the main room, and gave him to his mother. “See,” said
	Elijah, “your son is alive.”
}

\eX{
	And the woman answered Elijah, “Now I know that you are a man of
	God and that the word of YHWH is truly in your mouth.”
}

\eX{
	Much later, in the third year, the word of YHWH came to Elijah:
	“Go, appear before Ahab; then I will send rain upon the earth.”
}

\eX{
	Thereupon Elijah set out to appear before Ahab. The famine was
	severe in Samaria.
}

\eX{
	Ahab had summoned Obadiah, the steward of the palace. (Obadiah
	revered YHWH greatly.
}

\eX{
	When Jezebel was killing off the prophets of YHWH, Obadiah had
	taken a hundred prophets and hidden them, fifty to a cave, and
	provided them with food and drink.)
}

\eX{
	And Ahab had said to Obadiah, “Go through the land, to all the
	springs of water and to all the wadis. Perhaps we shall find some
	grass to keep horses and mules alive, so that we are not left
	without beasts.”
}

\eX{
	They divided the country between them to explore it, Ahab going
	alone in one direction and Obadiah going alone in another
	direction.
}

\eX{
	Obadiah was on the road, when Elijah suddenly confronted him.
	[Obadiah] recognized him and flung himself on his face, saying,
	“Is that you, my lord Elijah?”
}

\eX{
	“Yes, it is I,” he answered. “Go tell your lord: Elijah is here!”
}

\eX{
	But he said, “What wrong have I done, that you should hand your
	servant over to Ahab to be killed?
}

\eX{
	As YHWH your God lives, there is no nation or kingdom to which my
	lord has not sent to look for you; and when they said, ‘He is not
	here,’ he made that kingdom or nation swear that you could not be
	found.
}

\eX{
	When I leave you, the spirit of YHWH will carry you off I don’t
	know where; and when I come and tell Ahab and he does not find
	you, he will kill me. Yet your servant has revered YHWH from my
	youth.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	הֲלֹא־הֻגַּד לַאדֹנִי אֵת אֲשֶׁר־עָשִׂיתִי בַּהֲרֹג אִיזֶבֶל אֵת נְבִיאֵי יְהֹוָה וָאַחְבִּא מִנְּבִיאֵי יְהֹוָה מֵאָה אִישׁ חֲמִשִּׁים חֲמִשִּׁים אִישׁ בַּמְּעָרָה וָאֲכַלְכְּלֵם
	לֶחֶם וָמָיִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	My lord has surely been told what I did when Jezebel was killing
	the prophets of YHWH, how I hid a hundred of the prophets of YHWH,
	fifty men to a cave, and provided them with food and drink.
}

\eX{
	And now you say, ‘Go tell your lord: Elijah is here.’ Why, he will
	kill me!”
}

\eX{
	Elijah replied, “As YHWH of Hosts lives, whom I serve, I will
	appear before him this very day.”
}

\eX{
	Obadiah went to find Ahab, and informed him; and Ahab went to meet
	Elijah.
}

\eX{
	When Ahab caught sight of Elijah, Ahab said to him, “Is that you,
	you troubler of Israel?”
}

\eX{
	He retorted, “It is not I who have brought trouble on Israel, but
	you and your father’s House, by forsaking the commandments of YHWH
	and going after the Baalim.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְעַתָּה שְׁלַח קְבֹץ אֵלַי אֶת־כּל־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל־הַר הַכַּרְמֶל וְאֶת־נְבִיאֵי הַבַּעַל אַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת וַחֲמִשִּׁים וּנְבִיאֵי הָאֲשֵׁרָה אַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת אֹכְלֵי שֻׁלְחַן
	אִיזָבֶל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Now summon all Israel to join me at Mount Carmel, together with
	the four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal and the four hundred
	prophets of Asherah, who eat at Jezebel’s table.”
}

\eX{
	Ahab sent orders to all the Israelites and gathered the prophets
	at Mount Carmel.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּגַּשׁ אֵלִיָּהוּ אֶל־כּל־הָעָם וַיֹּאמֶר עַד־מָתַי אַתֶּם פֹּסְחִים עַל־שְׁתֵּי הַסְּעִפִּים אִם־יְהֹוָה הָאֱלֹהִים לְכוּ אַחֲרָיו וְאִם־הַבַּעַל לְכוּ אַחֲרָיו וְלֹא־עָנוּ
	הָעָם אֹתוֹ דָּבָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Elijah approached all the people and said, “How long will you keep
	hopping between two opinions? If YHWH is God, follow Him; and if
	Baal, follow him!” But the people answered him not a word.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 18:21. Limping on two branches is the image. Elijah turns indecision into
%		a body moving badly.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then Elijah said to the people, “I am the only prophet of YHWH
	left, while the prophets of Baal are four hundred and fifty men.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְיִתְּנוּ־לָנוּ שְׁנַיִם פָּרִים וְיִבְחֲרוּ לָהֶם הַפָּר הָאֶחָד וִינַתְּחֻהוּ וְיָשִׂימוּ עַל־הָעֵצִים וְאֵשׁ לֹא יָשִׂימוּ וַאֲנִי אֶעֱשֶׂה אֶת־הַפָּר הָאֶחָד וְנָתַתִּי
	עַל־הָעֵצִים וְאֵשׁ לֹא אָשִׂים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Let two young bulls be given to us. Let them choose one bull, cut
	it up, and lay it on the wood, but let them not apply fire; I will
	prepare the other bull, and lay it on the wood, and will not apply
	fire.
}

\eX{
	You will then invoke your god by name, and I will invoke YHWH by
	name; and let us agree: the god who responds with fire, that one
	is God.” And all the people answered, “Very good!”
}

\eX{
	Elijah said to the prophets of Baal, “Choose one bull and prepare
	it first, for you are the majority; invoke your god by name, but
	apply no fire.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְחוּ אֶת־הַפָּר אֲשֶׁר־נָתַן לָהֶם וַיַּעֲשׂוּ וַיִּקְרְאוּ בְשֵׁם־הַבַּעַל מֵהַבֹּקֶר וְעַד־הַצּהֳרַיִם לֵאמֹר הַבַּעַל עֲנֵנוּ וְאֵין קוֹל וְאֵין עֹנֶה וַיְפַסְּחוּ
	עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When noon came, Elijah mocked them, saying, “Shout louder! After
	all, he is a god. But he may be in conversation, he may be
	detained, or he may be on a journey, or perhaps he is asleep and
	will wake up.”
}

\eX{
	So they shouted louder, and gashed themselves with knives and
	spears, according to their practice, until the blood streamed over
	them.
}

\eX{
	When noon passed, they kept raving until the hour of presenting
	the meal offering. Still there was no sound, and none who
	responded or heeded.
}

\eX{
	Then Elijah said to all the people, “Come closer to me”; and all
	the people came closer to him. He repaired the damaged altar of
	YHWH.
}

\eX{
	Then Elijah took twelve stones, corresponding to the number of the
	tribes of the sons of Jacob—to whom the word of YHWH had come:
	“Israel shall be your name”—
}

\eX{
	and with the stones he built an altar in the name of YHWH. Around
	the altar he made a trench large enough for two seahs of seed.
}

\eX{
	He laid out the wood, and he cut up the bull and laid it on the
	wood.
}

\eX{
	And he said, “Fill four jars with water and pour it over the burnt
	offering and the wood.” Then he said, “Do it a second time”; and
	they did it a second time. “Do it a third time,” he said; and they
	did it a third time.
}

\eX{
	The water ran down around the altar, and even the trench was
	filled with water.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי בַּעֲלוֹת הַמִּנְחָה וַיִּגַּשׁ אֵלִיָּהוּ הַנָּבִיא וַיֹּאמַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי אַבְרָהָם יִצְחָק וְיִשְׂרָאֵל הַיּוֹם יִוָּדַע כִּי־אַתָּה אֱלֹהִים בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל וַאֲנִי
	עַבְדֶּךָ (ובדבריך) [וּבִדְבָרְךָ] עָשִׂיתִי אֵת כּל־הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Answer me, O LORD, answer me, that this people may know that You,
	O LORD, are God; for You have turned their hearts backward.”
}

\eX{
	Then fire from YHWH descended and consumed the burnt offering, the
	wood, the stones, and the earth; and it licked up the water that
	was in the trench.
}

\eX{
	When they saw this, all the people flung themselves on their faces
	and cried out: “YHWH alone is God, YHWH alone is God!”
}

\eX{
	Then Elijah said to them, “Seize the prophets of Baal, let not a
	single one of them get away.” They seized them, and Elijah took
	them down to the Wadi Kishon and slaughtered them there.
}

\eX{
	Elijah said to Ahab, “Go up, eat and drink, for there is a
	rumbling of [approaching] rain,”
}

\eX{
	and Ahab went up to eat and drink. Elijah meanwhile climbed to the
	top of Mount Carmel, crouched on the ground, and put his face
	between his knees.
}

\eX{
	And he said to his servant, “Go up and look toward the Sea.” He
	went up and looked and reported, “There is nothing.” Seven times
	[Elijah] said, “Go back,”
}

\eX{
	and the seventh time, [the servant] reported, “A cloud as small as
	a man’s hand is rising in the west.” Then [Elijah] said, “Go say
	to Ahab, ‘Hitch up [your chariot] and go down before the rain
	stops you.’”
}

\eX{
	Meanwhile the sky grew black with clouds; there was wind, and a
	heavy downpour fell; Ahab mounted his chariot and drove off to
	Jezreel.
}

\eX{
	The hand of YHWH had come upon Elijah. He tied up his skirts and
	ran in front of Ahab all the way to Jezreel.
}

\eX{
	When Ahab told Jezebel all that Elijah had done and how he had put
	all the prophets to the sword,
}

\eX{
	Jezebel sent a messenger to Elijah, saying, “Thus and more may the
	gods do if by this time tomorrow I have not made you like one of
	them.”
}

\eX{
	Frightened, he fled at once for his life. He came to Beer-sheba,
	which is in Judah, and left his servant there;
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהוּא־הָלַךְ בַּמִּדְבָּר דֶּרֶךְ יוֹם וַיָּבֹא וַיֵּשֶׁב תַּחַת רֹתֶם (אחת) [אֶחָד] וַיִּשְׁאַל אֶת־נַפְשׁוֹ לָמוּת וַיֹּאמֶר רַב עַתָּה יְהֹוָה קַח נַפְשִׁי כִּי־לֹא־טוֹב
	אָנֹכִי מֵאֲבֹתָי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	he himself went a day’s journey into the wilderness. He came to a
	broom bush and sat down under it, and prayed that he might die.
	“Enough!” he cried. “Now, O LORD, take my life, for I am no better
	than my fathers.”
}

\eX{
	He lay down and fell asleep under a broom bush. Suddenly an angel
	touched him and said to him, “Arise and eat.”
}

\eX{
	He looked about; and there, beside his head, was a cake baked on
	hot stones and a jar of water! He ate and drank, and lay down
	again.
}

\eX{
	The angel of YHWH came a second time and touched him and said,
	“Arise and eat, or the journey will be too much for you.”
}

\eX{
	He arose and ate and drank; and with the strength from that meal
	he walked forty days and forty nights as far as the mountain of
	God at Horeb.
}

\eX{
	There he went into a cave, and there he spent the night. Then the
	word of YHWH came to him. He said to him, “Why are you here,
	Elijah?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר קַנֹּא קִנֵּאתִי לַיהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי צְבָאוֹת כִּי־עָזְבוּ בְרִיתְךָ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־מִזְבְּחֹתֶיךָ הָרָסוּ וְאֶת־נְבִיאֶיךָ הָרְגוּ בֶחָרֶב וָאִוָּתֵר אֲנִי לְבַדִּי
	וַיְבַקְשׁוּ אֶת־נַפְשִׁי לְקַחְתָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He replied, “I am moved by zeal for YHWH, the God of Hosts, for
	the Israelites have forsaken Your covenant, torn down Your altars,
	and put Your prophets to the sword. I alone am left, and they are
	out to take my life.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר צֵא וְעָמַדְתָּ בָהָר לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה וְהִנֵּה יְהֹוָה עֹבֵר וְרוּחַ גְּדוֹלָה וְחָזָק מְפָרֵק הָרִים וּמְשַׁבֵּר סְלָעִים לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה לֹא בָרוּחַ יְהֹוָה וְאַחַר
	הָרוּחַ רַעַשׁ לֹא בָרַעַשׁ יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	After the earthquake—fire; but YHWH was not in the fire. And after
	the fire—a soft murmuring sound.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 19:12. A thin sound of silence is famously hard to carry over. The Hebrew
%		is not a thunderclap. It is the thing after all the thunderclaps have failed.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When Elijah heard it, he wrapped his mantle about his face and
	went out and stood at the entrance of the cave. Then a voice
	addressed him: “Why are you here, Elijah?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר קַנֹּא קִנֵּאתִי לַיהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי צְבָאוֹת כִּי־עָזְבוּ בְרִיתְךָ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־מִזְבְּחֹתֶיךָ הָרָסוּ וְאֶת־נְבִיאֶיךָ הָרְגוּ בֶחָרֶב וָאִוָּתֵר אֲנִי לְבַדִּי
	וַיְבַקְשׁוּ אֶת־נַפְשִׁי לְקַחְתָּהּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He answered, “I am moved by zeal for YHWH, the God of Hosts; for
	the Israelites have forsaken Your covenant, torn down Your altars,
	and have put Your prophets to the sword. I alone am left, and they
	are out to take my life.”
}

\eX{
	YHWH said to him, “Go back by the way you came, [and] on to the
	wilderness of Damascus. When you get there, anoint Hazael as king
	of Aram.
}

\eX{
	Also anoint Jehu son of Nimshi as king of Israel, and anoint
	Elisha son of Shaphat of Abel-meholah to succeed you as prophet.
}

\eX{
	Whoever escapes the sword of Hazael shall be slain by Jehu, and
	whoever escapes the sword of Jehu shall be slain by Elisha.
}

\eX{
	I will leave in Israel only seven thousand—every knee that has not
	knelt to Baal and every mouth that has not kissed him.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ מִשָּׁם וַיִּמְצָא אֶת־אֱלִישָׁע בֶּן־שָׁפָט וְהוּא חֹרֵשׁ שְׁנֵים־עָשָׂר צְמָדִים לְפָנָיו וְהוּא בִּשְׁנֵים הֶעָשָׂר וַיַּעֲבֹר אֵלִיָּהוּ אֵלָיו וַיַּשְׁלֵךְ
	אַדַּרְתּוֹ אֵלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He set out from there and came upon Elisha son of Shaphat as he
	was plowing. There were twelve yoke of oxen ahead of him, and he
	was with the twelfth. Elijah came over to him and threw his mantle
	over him.
}

\eX{
	He left the oxen and ran after Elijah, saying: “Let me kiss my
	father and mother good-by, and I will follow you.” And he answered
	him, “Go back. What have I done to you?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב מֵאַחֲרָיו וַיִּקַּח אֶת־צֶמֶד הַבָּקָר וַיִּזְבָּחֵהוּ וּבִכְלִי הַבָּקָר בִּשְּׁלָם הַבָּשָׂר וַיִּתֵּן לָעָם וַיֹּאכֵלוּ וַיָּקם וַיֵּלֶךְ אַחֲרֵי אֵלִיָּהוּ
	וַיְשָׁרְתֵהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He turned back from him and took the yoke of oxen and slaughtered
	them; he boiled their meat with the gear of the oxen and gave it
	to the people, and they ate. Then he arose and followed Elijah and
	became his attendant.
}

\eX{
	King Ben-hadad of Aram gathered his whole army; thirty-two kings
	accompanied him with horses and chariots. He advanced against
	Samaria, laid siege to it, and attacked it.
}

\eX{
	to say to him, “Thus said Ben-hadad: Your silver and gold are
	mine, and your beautiful wives and children are mine.”
}

\eX{
	The king of Israel replied, “As you say, my lord king: I and all I
	have are yours.”
}

\eX{
	Then the messengers came again and said, “Thus said Ben-hadad:
	When I sent you the order to give me your silver and gold, and
	your wives and children,
}

\eX{
	I meant that tomorrow at this time I will send my servants to you
	and they will search your house and the houses of your courtiers
	and seize everything you prize and take it away.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְרָא מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל לְכל־זִקְנֵי הָאָרֶץ וַיֹּאמֶר דְּעוּ־נָא וּרְאוּ כִּי רָעָה זֶה מְבַקֵּשׁ כִּי־שָׁלַח אֵלַי לְנָשַׁי וּלְבָנַי וּלְכַסְפִּי וְלִזְהָבִי וְלֹא
	מָנַעְתִּי מִמֶּנּוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the king of Israel summoned all the elders of the land, and
	he said, “See for yourselves how that man is bent on evil! For
	when he demanded my wives and my children, my silver and my gold,
	I did not refuse him.”
}

\eX{
	All the elders and all the people said, “Do not obey and do not
	submit!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לְמַלְאֲכֵי בֶן־הֲדַד אִמְרוּ לַאדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ כֹּל אֲשֶׁר־שָׁלַחְתָּ אֶל־עַבְדְּךָ בָרִאשֹׁנָה אֶעֱשֶׂה וְהַדָּבָר הַזֶּה לֹא אוּכַל לַעֲשׂוֹת וַיֵּלְכוּ הַמַּלְאָכִים
	וַיְשִׁבֻהוּ דָּבָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So he said to Ben-hadad’s messengers, “Tell my lord the king: All
	that you first demanded of your servant I shall do, but this thing
	I cannot do.” The messengers went and reported this to him.
}

\eX{
	Thereupon Ben-hadad sent him this message: “May the gods do thus
	to me and even more, if the dust of Samaria will provide even a
	handful for each of the men who follow me!”
}

\eX{
	The king of Israel replied, “Tell him: Let not him who girds on
	his sword boast like him who ungirds it!”
}

\eX{
	On hearing this reply—while he and the other kings were drinking
	together at Succoth—he commanded his followers, “Advance!” And
	they advanced against the city.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהִנֵּה נָבִיא אֶחָד נִגַּשׁ אֶל־אַחְאָב מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמֶר כֹּה אָמַר יְהֹוָה הֲרָאִיתָ אֵת כּל־הֶהָמוֹן הַגָּדוֹל הַזֶּה הִנְנִי נֹתְנוֹ בְיָדְךָ הַיּוֹם וְיָדַעְתָּ
	כִּי־אֲנִי יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then a certain prophet went up to King Ahab of Israel and said,
	“Thus said YHWH: Do you see that great host? I will deliver it
	into your hands today, and you shall know that I am YHWH.”
}

\eX{
	“Through whom?” asked Ahab. He answered, “Thus said YHWH: Through
	the aides of the provincial governors.” He asked, “Who shall begin
	the battle?” And he answered, “You.”
}

\eX{
	So he mustered the aides of the provincial governors, 232 strong,
	and then he mustered all the troops—all the Israelites—7,000
	strong.
}

\eX{
	They marched out at noon, while Ben-hadad was drinking himself
	drunk at Succoth together with the thirty-two kings allied with
	him.
}

\eX{
	The aides of the provincial governors rushed out first. Ben-hadad
	sent [scouts], who told him, “Some men have come out from
	Samaria.”
}

\eX{
	He said, “If they have come out to surrender, take them alive; and
	if they have come out for battle, take them alive anyhow.”
}

\eX{
	But the others—the aides of the provincial governors, with the
	army behind them—had already rushed out of the city,
}

\eX{
	and each of them struck down his opponent. The Arameans fled, and
	Israel pursued them; but King Ben-hadad of Aram escaped on a horse
	with other horsemen.
}

\eX{
	The king of Israel came out and attacked the horses and chariots,
	and inflicted a great defeat on the Arameans.
}

\eX{
	Then the prophet approached the king of Israel and said to him,
	“Go, keep up your efforts, and consider well what you must do; for
	the king of Aram will attack you at the turn of the year.”
}

\eX{
	Now the ministers of the king of Aram said to him, “Their God is a
	God of mountains; that is why they got the better of us. But if we
	fight them in the plain, we will surely get the better of them.
}

\eX{
	Do this: Remove all the kings from their posts and appoint
	governors in their place.
}

\eX{
	Then muster for yourself an army equal to the army you lost, horse
	for horse and chariot for chariot. And let us fight them in the
	plain, and we will surely get the better of them.” He took their
	advice and acted accordingly.
}

\eX{
	At the turn of the year, Ben-hadad mustered the Arameans and
	advanced on Aphek to fight Israel.
}

\eX{
	Now the Israelites had been mustered and provisioned, and they
	went out against them; but when the Israelites encamped against
	them, they looked like two flocks of goats, while the Arameans
	covered the land.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּגַּשׁ אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמֶר כֹּה־אָמַר יְהֹוָה יַעַן אֲשֶׁר אָמְרוּ אֲרָם אֱלֹהֵי הָרִים יְהֹוָה וְלֹא־אֱלֹהֵי עֲמָקִים הוּא וְנָתַתִּי
	אֶת־כּל־הֶהָמוֹן הַגָּדוֹל הַזֶּה בְּיָדֶךָ וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי־אֲנִי יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the man of God approached and spoke to the king of Israel,
	“Thus said YHWH: Because the Arameans have said, ‘YHWH is a God of
	mountains, but He is not a God of lowlands,’ I will deliver that
	great host into your hands; and you shall know that I am YHWH.”
}

\eX{
	For seven days they were encamped opposite each other. On the
	seventh day, the battle was joined and the Israelites struck down
	100,000 Aramean foot soldiers in one day.
}

\eX{
	The survivors fled to Aphek, inside the town, and the wall fell on
	the 27,000 survivors. Ben-hadad also fled and took refuge inside
	the town, in an inner chamber.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו עֲבָדָיו הִנֵּה־נָא שָׁמַעְנוּ כִּי מַלְכֵי בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל כִּי־מַלְכֵי חֶסֶד הֵם נָשִׂימָה נָּא שַׂקִּים בְּמתְנֵינוּ וַחֲבָלִים בְּרֹאשֵׁנוּ וְנֵצֵא
	אֶל־מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל אוּלַי יְחַיֶּה אֶת־נַפְשֶׁךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	His ministers said to him, “We have heard that the kings of the
	House of Israel are magnanimous kings. Let us put sackcloth on our
	loins and ropes on our heads, and surrender to the king of Israel;
	perhaps he will spare your life.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּחְגְּרוּ שַׂקִּים בְּמתְנֵיהֶם וַחֲבָלִים בְּרָאשֵׁיהֶם וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל־מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמְרוּ עַבְדְּךָ בֶן־הֲדַד אָמַר תְּחִי־נָא נַפְשִׁי וַיֹּאמֶר הַעוֹדֶנּוּ חַי
	אָחִי הוּא׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So they girded sackcloth on their loins and wound ropes around
	their heads, and came to the king of Israel and said, “Your
	servant Ben-hadad says, ‘I beg you, spare my life.’” He replied,
	“Is he still alive? He is my brother.”
}

\eX{
	The men divined his meaning and quickly caught the word from him,
	saying, “Yes, Ben-hadad is your brother.” “Go, bring him,” he
	said. Ben-hadad came out to him, and he invited him into his
	chariot.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו הֶעָרִים אֲשֶׁר־לָקַח־אָבִי מֵאֵת אָבִיךָ אָשִׁיב וְחֻצוֹת תָּשִׂים לְךָ בְדַמֶּשֶׂק כַּאֲשֶׁר־שָׂם אָבִי בְּשֹׁמְרוֹן וַאֲנִי בַּבְּרִית אֲשַׁלְּחֶךָּ
	וַיִּכְרת־לוֹ בְרִית וַיְשַׁלְּחֵהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	A certain man, a disciple of the prophets, said to another, at the
	word of YHWH, “Strike me”; but the man refused to strike him.
}

\eX{
	He said to him, “Because you have not obeyed YHWH, a lion will
	strike you dead as soon as you leave me.” And when he left, a lion
	came upon him and killed him.
}

\eX{
	Then he met another man and said, “Come, strike me.” So the man
	struck him and wounded him.
}

\eX{
	Then the prophet, disguised by a cloth over his eyes, went and
	waited for the king by the road.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי הַמֶּלֶךְ עֹבֵר וְהוּא צָעַק אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֹּאמֶר עַבְדְּךָ יָצָא בְקֶרֶב־הַמִּלְחָמָה וְהִנֵּה־אִישׁ סָר וַיָּבֵא אֵלַי אִישׁ וַיֹּאמֶר שְׁמֹר אֶת־הָאִישׁ הַזֶּה
	אִם־הִפָּקֵד יִפָּקֵד וְהָיְתָה נַפְשְׁךָ תַּחַת נַפְשׁוֹ אוֹ כִכַּר־כֶּסֶף תִּשְׁקוֹל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	While your servant was busy here and there, [the man] got away.”
	The king of Israel responded, “You have your verdict; you
	pronounced it yourself.”
}

\eX{
	Quickly he removed the cloth from his eyes, and the king
	recognized him as one of the prophets.
}

\eX{
	He said to him, “Thus said YHWH: Because you have set free the man
	whom I doomed, your life shall be forfeit for his life and your
	people for his people.”
}

\eX{
	Dispirited and sullen, the king of Israel left for home and came
	to Samaria.
}

\eX{
	[The following events] occurred sometime afterward: Naboth the
	Jezreelite owned a vineyard in Jezreel, adjoining the palace of
	King Ahab of Samaria.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְדַבֵּר אַחְאָב אֶל־נָבוֹת לֵאמֹר תְּנָה־לִּי אֶת־כַּרְמְךָ וִיהִי־לִי לְגַן־יָרָק כִּי הוּא קָרוֹב אֵצֶל בֵּיתִי וְאֶתְּנָה לְךָ תַּחְתָּיו כֶּרֶם טוֹב מִמֶּנּוּ אִם טוֹב
	בְּעֵינֶיךָ אֶתְּנָה־לְךָ כֶסֶף מְחִיר זֶה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ahab said to Naboth, “Give me your vineyard, so that I may have it
	as a vegetable garden, since it is right next to my palace. I will
	give you a better vineyard in exchange; or, if you prefer, I will
	pay you the price in money.”
}

\eX{
	But Naboth replied, “YHWH forbid that I should give up to you what
	I have inherited from my fathers!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹא אַחְאָב אֶל־בֵּיתוֹ סַר וְזָעֵף עַל־הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר־דִּבֶּר אֵלָיו נָבוֹת הַיִּזְרְעֵאלִי וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא־אֶתֵּן לְךָ אֶת־נַחֲלַת אֲבוֹתָי וַיִּשְׁכַּב עַל־מִטָּתוֹ
	וַיַּסֵּב אֶת־פָּנָיו וְלֹא־אָכַל לָחֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ahab went home dispirited and sullen because of the answer that
	Naboth the Jezreelite had given him: “I will not give up to you
	what I have inherited from my fathers!” He lay down on his bed and
	turned away his face, and he would not eat.
}

\eX{
	His wife Jezebel came to him and asked him, “Why are you so
	dispirited that you won’t eat?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְדַבֵּר אֵלֶיהָ כִּי־אֲדַבֵּר אֶל־נָבוֹת הַיִּזְרְעֵאלִי וָאֹמַר לוֹ תְּנָה־לִּי אֶת־כַּרְמְךָ בְּכֶסֶף אוֹ אִם־חָפֵץ אַתָּה אֶתְּנָה־לְךָ כֶרֶם תַּחְתָּיו וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא־אֶתֵּן
	לְךָ אֶת־כַּרְמִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So he told her, “I spoke to Naboth the Jezreelite and proposed to
	him, ‘Sell me your vineyard for money, or if you prefer, I’ll give
	you another vineyard in exchange’; but he answered, ‘I will not
	give my vineyard to you.’”
}

\eX{
	His wife Jezebel said to him, “Now is the time to show yourself
	king over Israel. Rise and eat something, and be cheerful; I will
	get the vineyard of Naboth the Jezreelite for you.”
}

\eX{
	So she wrote letters in Ahab’s name and sealed them with his seal,
	and sent the letters to the elders and the nobles who lived in the
	same town with Naboth.
}

\eX{
	In the letters she wrote as follows: “Proclaim a fast and seat
	Naboth at the front of the assembly.
}

\eX{
	And seat two scoundrels opposite him, and let them testify against
	him: ‘You have reviled God and king!’ Then take him out and stone
	him to death.”
}

\eX{
	His townsmen—the elders and nobles who lived in his town—did as
	Jezebel had instructed them, just as was written in the letters
	she had sent them:
}

\eX{
	They proclaimed a fast and seated Naboth at the front of the
	assembly.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ שְׁנֵי הָאֲנָשִׁים בְּנֵי־בְלִיַּעַל וַיֵּשְׁבוּ נֶגְדּוֹ וַיְעִדֻהוּ אַנְשֵׁי הַבְּלִיַּעַל אֶת־נָבוֹת נֶגֶד הָעָם לֵאמֹר בֵּרַךְ נָבוֹת אֱלֹהִים וָמֶלֶךְ וַיֹּצִאֻהוּ
	מִחוּץ לָעִיר וַיִּסְקְלֻהוּ בָאֲבָנִים וַיָּמֹת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the two scoundrels came and sat down opposite him; and the
	scoundrels testified against Naboth publicly as follows: “Naboth
	has reviled God and king.” Then they took him outside the town and
	stoned him to death.
}

\eX{
	Word was sent to Jezebel: “Naboth has been stoned to death.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי כִּשְׁמֹעַ אִיזֶבֶל כִּי־סֻקַּל נָבוֹת וַיָּמֹת וַתֹּאמֶר אִיזֶבֶל אֶל־אַחְאָב קוּם רֵשׁ אֶת־כֶּרֶם נָבוֹת הַיִּזְרְעֵאלִי אֲשֶׁר מֵאֵן לָתֶת־לְךָ בְכֶסֶף כִּי אֵין נָבוֹת
	חַי כִּי־מֵת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	As soon as Jezebel heard that Naboth had been stoned to death, she
	said to Ahab, “Go and take possession of the vineyard which Naboth
	the Jezreelite refused to sell you for money; for Naboth is no
	longer alive, he is dead.”
}

\eX{
	When Ahab heard that Naboth was dead, Ahab set out for the
	vineyard of Naboth the Jezreelite to take possession of it.
}

\eX{
	“Go down and confront King Ahab of Israel who [resides] in
	Samaria. He is now in Naboth’s vineyard; he has gone down there to
	take possession of it.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְדִבַּרְתָּ אֵלָיו לֵאמֹר כֹּה אָמַר יְהֹוָה הֲרָצַחְתָּ וְגַם־יָרָשְׁתָּ וְדִבַּרְתָּ אֵלָיו לֵאמֹר כֹּה אָמַר יְהֹוָה בִּמְקוֹם אֲשֶׁר לָקְקוּ הַכְּלָבִים אֶת־דַּם נָבוֹת
	יָלֹקּוּ הַכְּלָבִים אֶת־דָּמְךָ גַּם־אָתָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Say to him, ‘Thus said YHWH: Would you murder and take possession?
	Thus said YHWH: In the very place where the dogs lapped up
	Naboth’s blood, the dogs will lap up your blood too.’”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 21:19. Have you murdered and also taken possession? is compact courtroom
%		Hebrew. The verb sequence does not let Ahab separate violence from paperwork.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ahab said to Elijah, “So you have found me, my enemy?” “Yes, I
	have found you,” he replied. “Because you have committed yourself
	to doing what is evil in the sight of YHWH,
}

\eX{
	I will bring disaster upon you. I will make a clean sweep of you,
	I will cut off from Israel every male belonging to Ahab,bond and
	free.
}

\eX{
	And I will make your house like the House of Jeroboam son of Nebat
	and like the House of Baasha son of Ahijah, because of the
	provocation you have caused by leading Israel to sin.
}

\eX{
	And YHWH has also spoken concerning Jezebel: ‘The dogs shall
	devour Jezebel in the field of Jezreel.
}

\eX{
	All of Ahab’s line who die in the town shall be devoured by dogs,
	and all who die in the open country shall be devoured by the birds
	of the sky.’”
}

\eX{
	Indeed, there never was anyone like Ahab, who committed himself to
	doing what was displeasing to YHWH, at the instigation of his wife
	Jezebel.
}

\eX{
	He acted most abominably, straying after the fetishes just like
	the Amorites, whom YHWH had dispossessed before the Israelites.
}

\eX{
	When Ahab heard these words, he rent his clothes and put sackcloth
	on his body. He fasted and lay in sackcloth and walked about
	subdued.
}

\eX{
	“Have you seen how Ahab has humbled himself before Me? Because he
	has humbled himself before Me, I will not bring the disaster in
	his lifetime; I will bring the disaster upon his house in his
	son’s time.”
}

\eX{
	There was a lull of three years, with no war between Aram and
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	In the third year, King Jehoshaphat of Judah came to visit the
	king of Israel.
}

\eX{
	The king of Israel said to his courtiers, “You know that
	Ramoth-gilead belongs to us, and yet we do nothing to recover it
	from the hands of the king of Aram.”
}

\eX{
	And he said to Jehoshaphat, “Will you come with me to battle at
	Ramoth-gilead?” Jehoshaphat answered the king of Israel, “I will
	do what you do; my troops shall be your troops, my horses shall be
	your horses.”
}

\eX{
	But Jehoshaphat said further to the king of Israel, “Please, first
	inquire of YHWH.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְבֹּץ מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־הַנְּבִיאִים כְּאַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת אִישׁ וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵהֶם הַאֵלֵךְ עַל־רָמֹת גִּלְעָד לַמִּלְחָמָה אִם־אֶחְדָּל וַיֹּאמְרוּ עֲלֵה וְיִתֵּן אֲדֹנָי
	בְּיַד הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So the king of Israel gathered the prophets, about four hundred
	men, and asked them, “Shall I march upon Ramoth-gilead for battle,
	or shall I not?” “March,” they said, “and YHWH will deliver [it]
	into Your Majesty’s hands.”
}

\eX{
	Then Jehoshaphat asked, “Isn’t there another prophet of YHWH here
	through whom we can inquire?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל־יְהוֹשָׁפָט עוֹד אִישׁ־אֶחָד לִדְרֹשׁ אֶת־יְהֹוָה מֵאֹתוֹ וַאֲנִי שְׂנֵאתִיו כִּי לֹא־יִתְנַבֵּא עָלַי טוֹב כִּי אִם־רָע מִיכָיְהוּ בֶּן־יִמְלָה
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹשָׁפָט אַל־יֹאמַר הַמֶּלֶךְ כֵּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So the king of Israel summoned an officer and said, “Bring Micaiah
	son of Imlah at once.”
}

\eX{
	The king of Israel and King Jehoshaphat of Judah were seated on
	their thrones, arrayed in their robes, on the threshing floor at
	the entrance of the gate of Samaria; and all the prophets were
	prophesying before them.
}

\eX{
	Zedekiah son of Chenaanah had provided himself with iron horns;
	and he said, “Thus said YHWH: With these you shall gore the
	Arameans till you make an end of them.”
}

\eX{
	And all the other prophets were prophesying similarly, “March upon
	Ramoth-gilead and triumph! YHWH will deliver it into Your
	Majesty’s hands.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהַמַּלְאָךְ אֲשֶׁר־הָלַךְ לִקְרֹא מִיכָיְהוּ דִּבֶּר אֵלָיו לֵאמֹר הִנֵּה־נָא דִּבְרֵי הַנְּבִיאִים פֶּה־אֶחָד טוֹב אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ יְהִי־נָא (דבריך) [דְבָרְךָ] כִּדְבַר אַחַד
	מֵהֶם וְדִבַּרְתָּ טּוֹב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The messenger who had gone to summon Micaiah said to him: “Look,
	the words of the prophets are with one accord favorable to the
	king. Let your word be like that of the rest of them; speak a
	favorable word.”
}

\eX{
	“As YHWH lives,” Micaiah answered, “I will speak only what YHWH
	tells me.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבוֹא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיֹּאמֶר הַמֶּלֶךְ אֵלָיו מִיכָיְהוּ הֲנֵלֵךְ אֶל־רָמֹת גִּלְעָד לַמִּלְחָמָה אִם־נֶחְדָּל וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו עֲלֵה וְהַצְלַח וְנָתַן יְהֹוָה בְּיַד
	הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When he came before the king, the king said to him, “Micaiah,
	shall we march upon Ramoth-gilead for battle, or shall we not?” He
	answered him, “March and triumph! YHWH will deliver [it] into Your
	Majesty’s hands.”
}

\eX{
	The king said to him, “How many times must I adjure you to tell me
	nothing but the truth in the name of YHWH?”
}

\eX{
	Then he said, “I saw all Israel scattered over the hills like
	sheep without a shepherd; and YHWH said, ‘These have no master;
	let everyone return to his home in safety.’”
}

\eX{
	“Didn’t I tell you,” said the king of Israel to Jehoshaphat, “that
	he would not prophesy good fortune for me, but only misfortune?”
}

\eX{
	But [Micaiah] said, “I call upon you to hear the word of YHWH! I
	saw YHWH seated upon His throne, with all the host of heaven
	standing in attendance to the right and to the left of Him.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 22:19. The army of the skies stands around YHWH like a royal court. The
%		scene is not monotheism cleaned for company; it is Israelite theology with the
%		furniture still in the room.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	YHWH asked, ‘Who will entice Ahab so that he will march and fall
	at Ramoth-gilead?’ Then one said thus and another said thus,
}

\eX{
	until a certain spirit came forward and stood before YHWH and
	said, ‘I will entice him.’ ‘How?’ YHWH asked him.
}

\eX{
	And he replied, ‘I will go out and be a lying spirit in the mouth
	of all his prophets.’ Then He said, ‘You will entice and you will
	prevail. Go out and do it.’
}

\eX{
	So YHWH has put a lying spirit in the mouth of all these prophets
	of yours; for YHWH has decreed disaster upon you.”
}

\eX{
	Thereupon Zedekiah son of Chenaanah stepped up and struck Micaiah
	on the cheek, and demanded, “Which way did the spirit of YHWH pass
	from me to speak with you?”
}

\eX{
	And Micaiah replied, “You’ll find out on the day when you try to
	hide in the innermost room.”
}

\eX{
	Then the king of Israel said, “Take Micaiah and turn him over to
	Amon, the city’s governor, and to Prince Joash,
}

\eX{
	and say, ‘The king’s orders are: Put this fellow in prison, and
	let his fare be scant bread and scant water until I come home
	safe.’”
}

\eX{
	To which Micaiah retorted, “If you ever come home safe, YHWH has
	not spoken through me.” He said further, “Listen, all you
	peoples!”
}

\eX{
	So the king of Israel and King Jehoshaphat of Judah marched upon
	Ramoth-gilead.
}

\eX{
	The king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, “Disguise yourself and go
	into the battle; but you, wear your robes.” So the king of Israel
	went into the battle disguised.
}

\eX{
	Now the king of Aram had instructed his thirty-two chariot
	officers: “Don’t attack anyone, small or great, except the king of
	Israel.”
}

\eX{
	So when the chariot officers saw Jehoshaphat, whom they took for
	the king of Israel, they turned upon him to attack him, and
	Jehoshaphat cried out.
}

\eX{
	And when the chariot officers became aware that he was not the
	king of Israel, they turned back from pursuing him.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאִישׁ מָשַׁךְ בַּקֶּשֶׁת לְתֻמּוֹ וַיַּכֶּה אֶת־מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל בֵּין הַדְּבָקִים וּבֵין הַשִּׁרְיָן וַיֹּאמֶר לְרַכָּבוֹ הֲפֹךְ יָדְךָ וְהוֹצִיאֵנִי מִן־הַמַּחֲנֶה כִּי
	החֳלֵיתִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then a man drew his bow at random and he hit the king of Israel
	between the plates of the armor; and he said to his charioteer,
	“Turn the horses around and get me behind the lines; I’m wounded.”
}

\eX{
	The battle raged all day long, and the king remained propped up in
	the chariot facing Aram; the blood from the wound ran down into
	the hollow of the chariot, and at dusk he died.
}

\eX{
	As the sun was going down, a shout went through the army: “Every
	man to his own town! Every man to his own district.”
}

\eX{
	So the king died and was brought to Samaria. They buried the king
	in Samaria,
}

\eX{
	and they flushed out the chariot at the pool of Samaria. Thus the
	dogs lapped up his blood and the whores bathed [in it], in
	accordance with the word that YHWH had spoken.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Ahab’s reign, and all his actions—the ivory
	palace that he built and all the towns that he fortified—are all
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Ahab slept with his fathers, and his son Ahaziah succeeded him as
	king.
}

\eX{
	Jehoshaphat son of Asa had become king of Judah in the fourth year
	of King Ahab of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Jehoshaphat was thirty-five years old when he became king, and he
	reigned in Jerusalem for twenty-five years. His mother’s name was
	Azubah daughter of Shilhi.
}

\eX{
	He followed closely the course of his father Asa and did not
	deviate from it, doing what was pleasing to YHWH.
}

\eX{
	However, the shrines did not cease to function; the people still
	sacrificed and offered at the shrines.
}

\eX{
	As for the other events of Jehoshaphat’s reign and the valor he
	displayed in battle, they are recorded in the Annals of the Kings
	of Judah.
}

\eX{
	(He also stamped out the remaining male prostitutes who had
	survived in the land from the time of his father Asa.)
}

\eX{
	Jehoshaphat constructed Tarshish ships to sail to Ophir for gold.
	But he did not sail because the ships were wrecked at Ezion-geber.
}

\eX{
	Then Ahaziah son of Ahab proposed to Jehoshaphat, “Let my servants
	sail on the ships with your servants”; but Jehoshaphat would not
	agree.
}

\eX{
	Jehoshaphat slept with his fathers and was buried with his fathers
	in the city of his father David, and his son Jehoram succeeded him
	as king.
}

\eX{
	[Meanwhile,] Ahaziah son of Ahab had become king of Israel, in
	Samaria, in the seventeenth year of King Jehoshaphat of Judah; he
	reigned over Israel two years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, following in the footsteps of
	his father and his mother, and in those of Jeroboam son of Nebat
	who had caused Israel to sin.
}

\eX{
	He worshiped Baal and bowed down to him; he vexed YHWH, the God of
	Israel, just as his father had done.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּפֹּל אֲחַזְיָה בְּעַד הַשְּׂבָכָה בַּעֲלִיָּתוֹ אֲשֶׁר בְּשֹׁמְרוֹן וַיָּחַל וַיִּשְׁלַח מַלְאָכִים וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵהֶם לְכוּ דִרְשׁוּ בְּבַעַל זְבוּב אֱלֹהֵי עֶקְרוֹן
	אִם־אֶחְיֶה מֵחֳלִי זֶה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ahaziah fell through the lattice in his upper chamber at Samaria
	and was injured. So he sent messengers, whom he instructed: “Go
	inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron, whether I shall recover
	from this injury.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּמַלְאַךְ יְהֹוָה דִּבֶּר אֶל־אֵלִיָּה הַתִּשְׁבִּי קוּם עֲלֵה לִקְרַאת מַלְאֲכֵי מֶלֶךְ־שֹׁמְרוֹן וְדַבֵּר אֲלֵהֶם הֲמִבְּלִי אֵין־אֱלֹהִים בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל אַתֶּם הֹלְכִים לִדְרֹשׁ
	בְּבַעַל זְבוּב אֱלֹהֵי עֶקְרוֹן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But an angel of YHWH said to Elijah the Tishbite, “Go and confront
	the messengers of the king of Samaria and say to them, ‘Is there
	no God in Israel that you go to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of
	Ekron?
}

\eX{
	Assuredly, thus said YHWH: You shall not rise from the bed you are
	lying on, but you shall die.’” And Elijah went.
}

\eX{
	The messengers returned to Ahaziah; and he asked, “Why have you
	come back?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו אִישׁ עָלָה לִקְרָאתֵנוּ וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלֵינוּ לְכוּ שׁוּבוּ אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲשֶׁר־שָׁלַח אֶתְכֶם וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֵלָיו כֹּה אָמַר יְהֹוָה הֲמִבְּלִי אֵין־אֱלֹהִים
	בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל אַתָּה שֹׁלֵחַ לִדְרֹשׁ בְּבַעַל זְבוּב אֱלֹהֵי עֶקְרוֹן לָכֵן הַמִּטָּה אֲשֶׁר־עָלִיתָ שָּׁם לֹא־תֵרֵד מִמֶּנָּה כִּי־מוֹת תָּמוּת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“What sort of man was it,” he asked them, “who came toward you and
	said these things to you?”
}

\eX{
	“A hairy man,” they replied, “with a leather belt tied around his
	waist.” “That’s Elijah the Tishbite!” he said.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 1:8. A hairy man can also be read as “a possessor of hair.” The story
%		recognizes Elijah by costume, not by résumé.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then he sent to him a captain of fifty with his fifty men. He
	climbed up to him, and found him sitting at the top of a hill.
	“Man of God,” he said to him, “by order of the king, come down!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעֲנֶה אֵלִיָּהוּ וַיְדַבֵּר אֶל־שַׂר הַחֲמִשִּׁים וְאִם־אִישׁ אֱלֹהִים אָנִי תֵּרֶד אֵשׁ מִן־הַשָּׁמַיִם וְתֹאכַל אֹתְךָ וְאֶת־חֲמִשֶּׁיךָ וַתֵּרֶד אֵשׁ מִן־הַשָּׁמַיִם
	וַתֹּאכַל אֹתוֹ וְאֶת־חֲמִשָּׁיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Elijah replied to the captain of the fifty, “If I am a man of God,
	let fire come down from heaven and consume you with your fifty
	men!” And fire came down from heaven and consumed him and his
	fifty men.
}

\eX{
	The king then sent to him another captain with his fifty men; and
	he addressed him as follows: “Man of God, by order of the king,
	come down at once!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעַן אֵלִיָּה וַיְדַבֵּר אֲלֵיהֶם אִם־אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים אָנִי תֵּרֶד אֵשׁ מִן־הַשָּׁמַיִם וְתֹאכַל אֹתְךָ וְאֶת־חֲמִשֶּׁיךָ וַתֵּרֶד אֵשׁ־אֱלֹהִים מִן־הַשָּׁמַיִם וַתֹּאכַל
	אֹתוֹ וְאֶת־חֲמִשָּׁיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But Elijah answered him, “If I am a man of God, let fire come down
	from heaven and consume you with your fifty men!” And fire of God
	came down from heaven and consumed him and his fifty men.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב וַיִּשְׁלַח שַׂר־חֲמִשִּׁים שְׁלִשִׁים וַחֲמִשָּׁיו וַיַּעַל וַיָּבֹא שַׂר־הַחֲמִשִּׁים הַשְּׁלִישִׁי וַיִּכְרַע עַל־בִּרְכָּיו לְנֶגֶד אֵלִיָּהוּ וַיִּתְחַנֵּן אֵלָיו
	וַיְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים תִּיקַר־נָא נַפְשִׁי וְנֶפֶשׁ עֲבָדֶיךָ אֵלֶּה חֲמִשִּׁים בְּעֵינֶיךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then he sent a third captain of fifty with his fifty men. The
	third captain of fifty climbed to the top, knelt before Elijah,
	and implored him, saying, “Oh, man of God, please have regard for
	my life and the lives of these fifty servants of yours!
}

\eX{
	Already fire has come from heaven and consumed the first two
	captains of fifty and their men; I beg you, have regard for my
	life!”
}

\eX{
	Then the angel of YHWH said to Elijah, “Go down with him, do not
	be afraid of him.” So he rose and went down with him to the king.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו כֹּה־אָמַר יְהֹוָה יַעַן אֲשֶׁר־שָׁלַחְתָּ מַלְאָכִים לִדְרֹשׁ בְּבַעַל זְבוּב אֱלֹהֵי עֶקְרוֹן הֲמִבְּלִי אֵין־אֱלֹהִים בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל לִדְרֹשׁ בִּדְבָרוֹ לָכֵן
	הַמִּטָּה אֲשֶׁר־עָלִיתָ שָּׁם לֹא־תֵרֵד מִמֶּנָּה כִּי־מוֹת תָּמוּת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He said to him, “Thus said YHWH: Because you sent messengers to
	inquire of Baal-zebub the god of Ekron—as if there were no God in
	Israel whose word you could seek—assuredly, you shall not rise
	from the bed which you are lying on; but you shall die.”
}

\eX{
	And [Ahaziah] died, according to the word of YHWH that Elijah had
	spoken. Jehoram succeeded him as king, in the second year of King
	Jehoram son of Jehoshaphat of Judah, for he had no son.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Ahaziah’s reign [and] his actions are recorded
	in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	When YHWH was about to take Elijah up to heaven in a whirlwind,
	Elijah and Elisha had set out from Gilgal.
}

\eX{
	Elijah said to Elisha, “Stay here, for YHWH has sent me on to
	Bethel.” “As YHWH lives and as you live,” said Elisha, “I will not
	leave you.” So they went down to Bethel.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּצְאוּ בְנֵי־הַנְּבִיאִים אֲשֶׁר־בֵּית־אֵל אֶל־אֱלִישָׁע וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו הֲיָדַעְתָּ כִּי הַיּוֹם יְהֹוָה לֹקֵחַ אֶת־אֲדֹנֶיךָ מֵעַל רֹאשֶׁךָ וַיֹּאמֶר גַּם־אֲנִי יָדַעְתִּי
	הֶחֱשׁוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Disciples of the prophets at Bethel came out to Elisha and said to
	him, “Do you know that YHWH will take your master away from you
	today?” He replied, “I know it, too; be silent.”
}

\eX{
	Then Elijah said to him, “Elisha, stay here, for YHWH has sent me
	on to Jericho.” “As YHWH lives and as you live,” said Elisha, “I
	will not leave you.” So they went on to Jericho.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּגְּשׁוּ בְנֵי־הַנְּבִיאִים אֲשֶׁר־בִּירִיחוֹ אֶל־אֱלִישָׁע וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו הֲיָדַעְתָּ כִּי הַיּוֹם יְהֹוָה לֹקֵחַ אֶת־אֲדֹנֶיךָ מֵעַל רֹאשֶׁךָ וַיֹּאמֶר גַּם־אֲנִי
	יָדַעְתִּי הֶחֱשׁוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The disciples of the prophets who were at Jericho came over to
	Elisha and said to him, “Do you know that YHWH will take your
	master away from you today?” He replied, “I know it, too; be
	silent.”
}

\eX{
	Elijah said to him, “Stay here, for YHWH has sent me on to the
	Jordan.” “As YHWH lives and as you live, I will not leave you,” he
	said, and the two of them went on.
}

\eX{
	Fifty men of the disciples of the prophets followed and stood by
	at a distance from them as the two of them stopped at the Jordan.
}

\eX{
	Thereupon Elijah took his mantle and, rolling it up, he struck the
	water; it divided to the right and left, so that the two of them
	crossed over on dry land.
}

\eX{
	As they were crossing, Elijah said to Elisha, “Tell me, what can I
	do for you before I am taken from you?” Elisha answered, “Let a
	double portion of your spirit pass on to me.”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 2:9. A double mouthful is inheritance language. Elisha is not asking for
%		twice Elijah’s power so much as the firstborn share of his spirit.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“You have asked a difficult thing,” he said. “If you see me as I
	am being taken from you, this will be granted to you; if not, it
	will not.”
}

\eX{
	As they kept on walking and talking, a fiery chariot with fiery
	horses suddenly appeared and separated one from the other; and
	Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind.
}

\eX{
	Elisha saw it, and he cried out, “Oh, father, father! Israel’s
	chariots and horsemen!” When he could no longer see him, he
	grasped his garments and rent them in two.
}

\eX{
	He picked up Elijah’s mantle, which had dropped from him; and he
	went back and stood on the bank of the Jordan.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח אֶת־אַדֶּרֶת אֵלִיָּהוּ אֲשֶׁר־נָפְלָה מֵעָלָיו וַיַּכֶּה אֶת־הַמַּיִם וַיֹּאמַר אַיֵּה יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי אֵלִיָּהוּ אַף־הוּא וַיַּכֶּה אֶת־הַמַּיִם וַיֵּחָצוּ הֵנָּה
	וָהֵנָּה וַיַּעֲבֹר אֱלִישָׁע׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Taking the mantle which had dropped from Elijah, he struck the
	water and said, “Where is YHWH, the God of Elijah?” As he too
	struck the water, it parted to the right and to the left, and
	Elisha crossed over.
}

\eX{
	When the disciples of the prophets at Jericho saw him from a
	distance, they exclaimed, “The spirit of Elijah has settled on
	Elisha!” And they went to meet him and bowed low before him to the
	ground.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו הִנֵּה־נָא יֵשׁ־אֶת־עֲבָדֶיךָ חֲמִשִּׁים אֲנָשִׁים בְּנֵי־חַיִל יֵלְכוּ נָא וִיבַקְשׁוּ אֶת־אֲדֹנֶיךָ פֶּן־נְשָׂאוֹ רוּחַ יְהֹוָה וַיַּשְׁלִכֵהוּ בְּאַחַד
	הֶהָרִים אוֹ בְּאַחַת (הגיאות) [הַגֵּיאָיוֹת] וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא תִשְׁלָחוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They said to him, “Your servants have fifty able men with them.
	Let them go and look for your master; perhaps the spirit of YHWH
	has carried him off and cast him upon some mountain or into some
	valley.” “Do not send them,” he replied.
}

\eX{
	But they kept pressing him for a long time, until he said, “Send
	them.” So they sent out fifty men, who searched for three days but
	did not find him.
}

\eX{
	They came back to him while he was still in Jericho; and he said
	to them, “I told you not to go.”
}

\eX{
	The men of the town said to Elisha, “Look, the town is a pleasant
	place to live in, as my lord can see; but the water is bad and the
	land causes bereavement.”
}

\eX{
	He responded, “Bring me a new dish and put salt in it.” They
	brought it to him;
}

\eX{
	he went to the spring and threw salt into it. And he said, “Thus
	said YHWH: I heal this water; no longer shall death and
	bereavement come from it!”
}

\eX{
	The water has remained wholesome to this day, in accordance with
	the word spoken by Elisha.
}

\eX{
	From there he went up to Bethel. As he was going up the road, some
	little boys came out of the town and jeered at him, saying, “Go
	away, baldhead! Go away, baldhead!”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 2:23. “Go up, baldhead” is not playground teasing in a vacuum. The verb
%		“go up” has just carried Elijah out of the story; the boys are mocking prophetic
%		departure and Elisha’s succession. Still: bears. The text is not housebroken.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He turned around and looked at them and cursed them in the name of
	YHWH. Thereupon, two she-bears came out of the woods and mangled
	forty-two of the children.
}

\eX{
	He went on from there to Mount Carmel, and from there he returned
	to Samaria.
}

\eX{
	Jehoram son of Ahab became king of Israel in Samaria in the
	eighteenth year of King Jehoshaphat of Judah; and he reigned
	twelve years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, yet not like his father and
	mother, for he removed the pillars of Baal that his father had
	made.
}

\eX{
	However, he clung to the sins which Jeroboam son of Nebat caused
	Israel to commit; he did not depart from them.
}

\eX{
	Now King Mesha of Moab was a sheep breeder; and he used to pay as
	tribute to the king of Israel a hundred thousand lambs and the
	wool of a hundred thousand rams.
}

\eX{
	But when Ahab died, the king of Moab rebelled against the king of
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	So King Jehoram promptly set out from Samaria and mustered all
	Israel.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ וַיִּשְׁלַח אֶל־יְהוֹשָׁפָט מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה לֵאמֹר מֶלֶךְ מוֹאָב פָּשַׁע בִּי הֲתֵלֵךְ אִתִּי אֶל־מוֹאָב לַמִּלְחָמָה וַיֹּאמֶר אֶעֱלֶה כָּמוֹנִי כָמוֹךָ כְּעַמִּי
	כְעַמֶּךָ כְּסוּסַי כְּסוּסֶיךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And he asked, “Which route shall we take?” [Jehoram] replied, “The
	road through the wilderness of Edom.”
}

\eX{
	So the king of Israel, the king of Judah, and the king of Edom set
	out, and they marched for seven days until they rounded [the tip
	of the Dead Sea]; and there was no water left for the army or for
	the animals that were with them.
}

\eX{
	“Alas!” cried the king of Israel. “YHWH has brought these three
	kings together only to deliver them into the hands of Moab.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹשָׁפָט הַאֵין פֹּה נָבִיא לַיהֹוָה וְנִדְרְשָׁה אֶת־יְהֹוָה מֵאוֹתוֹ וַיַּעַן אֶחָד מֵעַבְדֵי מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיֹּאמֶר פֹּה אֱלִישָׁע בֶּן־שָׁפָט אֲשֶׁר־יָצַק
	מַיִם עַל־יְדֵי אֵלִיָּהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But Jehoshaphat said, “Isn’t there a prophet of YHWH here, through
	whom we may inquire of YHWH?” One of the courtiers of the king of
	Israel spoke up and said, “Elisha son of Shaphat, who poured water
	on the hands of Elijah, is here.”
}

\eX{
	“The word of YHWH is with him,” said Jehoshaphat. So the king of
	Israel and Jehoshaphat and the king of Edom went down to him.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלִישָׁע אֶל־מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל מַה־לִּי וָלָךְ לֵךְ אֶל־נְבִיאֵי אָבִיךָ וְאֶל־נְבִיאֵי אִמֶּךָ וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל אַל כִּי־קָרָא יְהֹוָה לִשְׁלֹשֶׁת
	הַמְּלָכִים הָאֵלֶּה לָתֵת אוֹתָם בְּיַד־מוֹאָב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Elisha said to the king of Israel, “What have you to do with me?
	Go to your father’s prophets or your mother’s prophets.” But the
	king of Israel said, “Don’t [say that], for YHWH has brought these
	three kings together only to deliver them into the hands of Moab.”
}

\eX{
	“As YHWH of Hosts lives, whom I serve,” Elisha answered, “were it
	not that I respect King Jehoshaphat of Judah, I wouldn’t look at
	you or notice you.
}

\eX{
	Now then, get me a musician.” As the musician played, the hand of
	YHWH came upon him,
}

\eX{
	and he said, “Thus said YHWH: This wadi shall be full of pools.
}

\eX{
	For thus said YHWH: You shall see no wind, you shall see no rain,
	and yet the wadi shall be filled with water; and you and your
	cattle and your pack animals shall drink.
}

\eX{
	And this is but a slight thing in the sight of YHWH, for He will
	also deliver Moab into your hands.
}

\eX{
	You shall conquer every fortified town and every splendid city;
	you shall fell every good tree and stop up all wells of water; and
	every fertile field you shall ruin with stones.”
}

\eX{
	And in the morning, when it was time to present the meal offering,
	water suddenly came from the direction of Edom and the land was
	covered by the water.
}

\eX{
	Meanwhile, all the Moabites had heard that the kings were
	advancing to make war on them; every man old enough to bear arms
	rallied, and they stationed themselves at the border.
}

\eX{
	Next morning, when they rose, the sun was shining over the water,
	and from the distance the water appeared to the Moabites as red as
	blood.
}

\eX{
	“That’s blood!” they said. “The kings must have fought among
	themselves and killed each other. Now to the spoil, Moab!”
}

\eX{
	They entered the Israelite camp, and the Israelites arose and
	attacked the Moabites, who fled before them. They advanced,
	constantly attacking the Moabites,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהֶעָרִים יַהֲרֹסוּ וְכל־חֶלְקָה טוֹבָה יַשְׁלִיכוּ אִישׁ־אַבְנוֹ וּמִלְאוּהָ וְכל־מַעְיַן־מַיִם יִסְתֹּמוּ וְכל־עֵץ־טוֹב יַפִּילוּ עַד־הִשְׁאִיר אֲבָנֶיהָ בַּקִּיר חֲרָשֶׂת
	וַיָּסֹבּוּ הַקַּלָּעִים וַיַּכּוּהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Seeing that the battle was going against him, the king of Moab led
	an attempt of seven hundred swordsmen to break a way through to
	the king of Edom; but they failed.
}

\eX{
	So he took his first-born son, who was to succeed him as king, and
	offered him up on the wall as a burnt offering. A great wrath came
	upon Israel, so they withdrew from him and went back to [their
	own] land.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאִשָּׁה אַחַת מִנְּשֵׁי בְנֵי־הַנְּבִיאִים צָעֲקָה אֶל־אֱלִישָׁע לֵאמֹר עַבְדְּךָ אִישִׁי מֵת וְאַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ כִּי עַבְדְּךָ הָיָה יָרֵא אֶת־יְהֹוָה וְהַנֹּשֶׁה בָּא לָקַחַת
	אֶת־שְׁנֵי יְלָדַי לוֹ לַעֲבָדִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	A certain woman, the wife of one of the disciples of the prophets,
	cried out to Elisha: “Your servant my husband is dead, and you
	know how your servant revered YHWH. And now a creditor is coming
	to seize my two children as slaves.”
}

\eX{
	Elisha said to her, “What can I do for you? Tell me, what have you
	in the house?” She replied, “Your maidservant has nothing at all
	in the house, except a jug of oil.”
}

\eX{
	“Go,” he said, “and borrow vessels outside, from all your
	neighbors, empty vessels, as many as you can.
}

\eX{
	Then go in and shut the door behind you and your children, and
	pour [oil] into all those vessels, removing each one as it is
	filled.”
}

\eX{
	She went away and shut the door behind her and her children. They
	kept bringing [vessels] to her and she kept pouring.
}

\eX{
	When the vessels were full, she said to her son, “Bring me another
	vessel.” He answered her, “There are no more vessels”; and the oil
	stopped.
}

\eX{
	She came and told the man of God, and he said, “Go sell the oil
	and pay your debt, and you and your children can live on the
	rest.”
}

tex.commentary:
Qere Ketiv.

%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 4:7. The oil stops only when there is no vessel left. The miracle is
%		measured by empty containers: grace with inventory control.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	One day Elisha visited Shunem. A wealthy woman lived there, and
	she urged him to have a meal; and whenever he passed by, he would
	stop there for a meal.
}

\eX{
	Once she said to her husband, “I am sure it is a holy man of God
	who comes this way regularly.
}

\eX{
	Let us make a small enclosed upper chamber and place a bed, a
	table, a chair, and a lampstand there for him, so that he can stop
	there whenever he comes to us.”
}

\eX{
	One day he came there; he retired to the upper chamber and lay
	down there.
}

\eX{
	He said to his servant Gehazi, “Call that Shunammite woman.” He
	called her, and she stood before him.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ אֱמר־נָא אֵלֶיהָ הִנֵּה חָרַדְתְּ אֵלֵינוּ אֶת־כּל־הַחֲרָדָה הַזֹּאת מֶה לַעֲשׂוֹת לָךְ הֲיֵשׁ לְדַבֶּר־לָךְ אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ אוֹ אֶל־שַׂר הַצָּבָא וַתֹּאמֶר בְּתוֹךְ
	עַמִּי אָנֹכִי יֹשָׁבֶת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He said to him, “Tell her, ‘You have gone to all this trouble for
	us. What can we do for you? Can we speak in your behalf to the
	king or to the army commander?’” She replied, “I live among my own
	people.”
}

\eX{
	“What then can be done for her?” he asked. “The fact is,” said
	Gehazi, “she has no son, and her husband is old.”
}

\eX{
	“Call her,” he said. He called her, and she stood in the doorway.
}

\eX{
	And Elisha said, “At this season next year, you will be embracing
	a son.” She replied, “Please, my lord, man of God, do not delude
	your maidservant.”
}

\eX{
	The woman conceived and bore a son at the same season the
	following year, as Elisha had assured her.
}

\eX{
	The child grew up. One day, he went out to his father among the
	reapers.
}

\eX{
	[Suddenly] he cried to his father, “Oh, my head, my head!” He said
	to a servant, “Carry him to his mother.”
}

\eX{
	He picked him up and brought him to his mother. And the child sat
	on her lap until noon; and he died.
}

\eX{
	She took him up and laid him on the bed of the man of God, and
	left him and closed the door.
}

\eX{
	Then she called to her husband: “Please, send me one of the
	servants and one of the she-asses, so I can hurry to the man of
	God and back.”
}

\eX{
	But he said, “Why are you going to him today? It is neither new
	moon nor sabbath.” She answered, “It’s all right.”
}

\eX{
	She had the ass saddled, and said to her servant, “Urge [the
	beast] on; see that I don’t slow down unless I tell you.”
}

\eX{
	She went on until she came to the man of God on Mount Carmel. When
	the man of God saw her from afar, he said to his servant Gehazi,
	“There is that Shunammite woman.
}

\eX{
	Go, hurry toward her and ask her, ‘How are you? How is your
	husband? How is the child?’” “We are well,” she replied.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתָּבֹא אֶל־אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים אֶל־הָהָר וַתַּחֲזֵק בְּרַגְלָיו וַיִּגַּשׁ גֵּיחֲזִי לְהדְפָהּ וַיֹּאמֶר אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים הַרְפֵּה־לָהּ כִּי־נַפְשָׁהּ מָרָה־לָהּ וַיהֹוָה הֶעְלִים
	מִמֶּנִּי וְלֹא הִגִּיד לִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But when she came up to the man of God on the mountain, she
	clasped his feet. Gehazi stepped forward to push her away; but the
	man of God said, “Let her alone, for she is in bitter distress;
	and YHWH has hidden it from me and has not told me.”
}

\eX{
	Then she said, “Did I ask my lord for a son? Didn’t I say: ‘Don’t
	mislead me’?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר לְגֵיחֲזִי חֲגֹר מתְנֶיךָ וְקַח מִשְׁעַנְתִּי בְיָדְךָ וָלֵךְ כִּי־תִמְצָא אִישׁ לֹא תְבָרְכֶנּוּ וְכִי־יְבָרֶכְךָ אִישׁ לֹא תַעֲנֶנּוּ וְשַׂמְתָּ מִשְׁעַנְתִּי
	עַל־פְּנֵי הַנָּעַר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He said to Gehazi, “Tie up your skirts, take my staff in your
	hand, and go. If you meet anyone, do not greet him; and if anyone
	greets you, do not answer him. And place my staff on the face of
	the boy.”
}

\eX{
	But the boy’s mother said, “As YHWH lives and as you live, I will
	not leave you!” So he arose and followed her.
}

\eX{
	Gehazi had gone on before them and had placed the staff on the
	boy’s face; but there was no sound or response. He turned back to
	meet him and told him, “The boy has not awakened.”
}

\eX{
	Elisha came into the house, and there was the boy, laid out dead
	on his couch.
}

\eX{
	He went in, shut the door behind the two of them, and prayed to
	YHWH.
}

\eX{
	Then he mounted [the bed] and placed himself over the child. He
	put his mouth on its mouth, his eyes on its eyes, and his hands on
	its hands, as he bent over it. And the body of the child became
	warm.
}

\eX{
	He stepped down, walked once up and down the room, then mounted
	and bent over him. Thereupon, the boy sneezed seven times, and the
	boy opened his eyes.
}

\eX{
	[Elisha] called Gehazi and said, “Call the Shunammite woman,” and
	he called her. When she came to him, he said, “Pick up your son.”
}

\eX{
	She came and fell at his feet and bowed low to the ground; then
	she picked up her son and left.
}

\eX{
	Elisha returned to Gilgal. There was a famine in the land, and the
	disciples of the prophets were sitting before him. He said to his
	servant, “Set the large pot [on the fire] and cook a stew for the
	disciples of the prophets.”
}

\eX{
	So one of them went out into the fields to gather sprouts. He came
	across a wild vine and picked from it wild gourds, as many as his
	garment would hold. Then he came back and sliced them into the pot
	of stew, for they did not know [what they were];
}

\eX{
	and they served it for the men to eat. While they were still
	eating of the stew, they began to cry out: “O man of God, there is
	death in the pot!” And they could not eat it.
}

\eX{
	“Fetch some flour,” [Elisha] said. He threw it into the pot and
	said, “Serve it to the people and let them eat.” And there was no
	longer anything harmful in the pot.
}

\eX{
	A man came from Baal-shalishah and he brought the man of God some
	bread of the first reaping—twenty loaves of barley bread, and some
	fresh grain in his sack. And [Elisha] said, “Give it to the people
	and let them eat.”
}

\eX{
	His attendant replied, “How can I set this before a hundred men?”
	But he said, “Give it to the people and let them eat. For thus
	said YHWH: They shall eat and have some left over.”
}

\eX{
	So he set it before them; and when they had eaten, they had some
	left over, as YHWH had said.
}

\eX{
	Naaman, commander of the army of the king of Aram, was important
	to his lord and high in his favor, for through him YHWH had
	granted victory to Aram. But the man, though a great warrior, was
	a leper.
}

\eX{
	Once, when the Arameans were out raiding, they carried off a young
	girl from the land of Israel, and she became an attendant to
	Naaman’s wife.
}

\eX{
	She said to her mistress, “I wish Master could come before the
	prophet in Samaria; he would cure him of his leprosy.”
}

\eX{
	[Naaman] went and told his lord just what the girl from the land
	of Israel had said.
}

\eX{
	And the king of Aram said, “Go to the king of Israel, and I will
	send along a letter.” He set out, taking with him ten talents of
	silver, six thousand shekels of gold, and ten changes of clothing.
}

\eX{
	He brought the letter to the king of Israel. It read: “Now, when
	this letter reaches you, know that I have sent my courtier Naaman
	to you, that you may cure him of his leprosy.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי כִּקְרֹא מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־הַסֵּפֶר וַיִּקְרַע בְּגָדָיו וַיֹּאמֶר הַאֱלֹהִים אָנִי לְהָמִית וּלְהַחֲיוֹת כִּי־זֶה שֹׁלֵחַ אֵלַי לֶאֱסֹף אִישׁ מִצָּרַעְתּוֹ כִּי
	אַךְ־דְּעוּ־נָא וּרְאוּ כִּי־מִתְאַנֶּה הוּא לִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When the king of Israel read the letter, he rent his clothes and
	cried, “Am I God, to deal death or give life, that this fellow
	writes to me to cure a man of leprosy? Just see for yourselves
	that he is seeking a pretext against me!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי כִּשְׁמֹעַ אֱלִישָׁע אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים כִּי־קָרַע מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־בְּגָדָיו וַיִּשְׁלַח אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ לֵאמֹר לָמָּה קָרַעְתָּ בְּגָדֶיךָ יָבֹא־נָא אֵלַי וְיֵדַע כִּי
	יֵשׁ נָבִיא בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When Elisha, the man of God, heard that the king of Israel had
	rent his clothes, he sent a message to the king: “Why have you
	rent your clothes? Let him come to me, and he will learn that
	there is a prophet in Israel.”
}

\eX{
	So Naaman came with his horses and chariots and halted at the door
	of Elisha’s house.
}

\eX{
	Elisha sent a messenger to say to him, “Go and bathe seven times
	in the Jordan, and your flesh shall be restored and you shall be
	clean.”
}

\eX{
	But Naaman was angered and walked away. “I thought,” he said, “he
	would surely come out to me, and would stand and invoke YHWH his
	God by name, and would wave his hand toward the spot, and cure the
	affected part.
}

\eX{
	Are not the Amanah and the Pharpar, the rivers of Damascus, better
	than all the waters of Israel? I could bathe in them and be
	clean!” And he stalked off in a rage.
}

\eX{
	But his servants came forward and spoke to him. “Sir,” they said,
	“if the prophet told you to do something difficult, would you not
	do it? How much more when he has only said to you, ‘Bathe and be
	clean.’”
}

\eX{
	So he went down and immersed himself in the Jordan seven times, as
	the man of God had bidden; and his flesh became like a little
	boy’s, and he was clean.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 5:14. Naaman goes down seven times. The great man has to become small
%		enough to fit through a river.
%	}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב אֶל־אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים הוּא וְכל־מַחֲנֵהוּ וַיָּבֹא וַיַּעֲמֹד לְפָנָיו וַיֹּאמֶר הִנֵּה־נָא יָדַעְתִּי כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהִים בְּכל־הָאָרֶץ כִּי אִם־בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל וְעַתָּה
	קַח־נָא בְרָכָה מֵאֵת עַבְדֶּךָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Returning with his entire retinue to the man of God, he stood
	before him and exclaimed, “Now I know that there is no God in the
	whole world except in Israel! So please accept a gift from your
	servant.”
}

\eX{
	But he replied, “As YHWH lives, whom I serve, I will not accept
	anything.” He pressed him to accept, but he refused.
}

\eX{
	And Naaman said, “Then at least let your servant be given two
	mule-loads of earth; for your servant will never again offer up
	burnt offering or sacrifice to any god, except YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	לַדָּבָר הַזֶּה יִסְלַח יְהֹוָה לְעַבְדֶּךָ בְּבוֹא אֲדֹנִי בֵית־רִמּוֹן לְהִשְׁתַּחֲוֺת שָׁמָּה וְהוּא נִשְׁעָן עַל־יָדִי וְהִשְׁתַּחֲוֵיתִי בֵּית רִמֹּן בְּהִשְׁתַּחֲוָיָתִי
	בֵּית רִמֹּן יִסְלַח־(נא)־יְהֹוָה לְעַבְדְּךָ בַּדָּבָר הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And he said to him, “Go in peace.” When he had gone some distance
	from him,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר גֵּיחֲזִי נַעַר אֱלִישָׁע אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים הִנֵּה חָשַׂךְ אֲדֹנִי אֶת־נַעֲמָן הָאֲרַמִּי הַזֶּה מִקַּחַת מִיָּדוֹ אֵת אֲשֶׁר־הֵבִיא חַי־יְהֹוָה כִּי־אִם־רַצְתִּי אַחֲרָיו
	וְלָקַחְתִּי מֵאִתּוֹ מְאוּמָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Gehazi, the attendant of Elisha the man of God, thought: “My
	master has let that Aramean Naaman off without accepting what he
	brought! As YHWH lives, I will run after him and get something
	from him.”
}

\eX{
	So Gehazi hurried after Naaman. When Naaman saw someone running
	after him, he alighted from his chariot to meet him and said, “Is
	all well?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר שָׁלוֹם אֲדֹנִי שְׁלָחַנִי לֵאמֹר הִנֵּה עַתָּה זֶה בָּאוּ אֵלַי שְׁנֵי־נְעָרִים מֵהַר אֶפְרַיִם מִבְּנֵי הַנְּבִיאִים תְּנָה־נָּא לָהֶם כִּכַּר־כֶּסֶף וּשְׁתֵּי חֲלִפוֹת
	בְּגָדִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“All is well,” he replied. “My master has sent me to say: Two
	youths, disciples of the prophets, have just come to me from the
	hill country of Ephraim. Please give them a talent of silver and
	two changes of clothing.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר נַעֲמָן הוֹאֵל קַח כִּכָּרָיִם וַיִּפְרץ־בּוֹ וַיָּצַר כִּכְּרַיִם כֶּסֶף בִּשְׁנֵי חֲרִטִים וּשְׁתֵּי חֲלִפוֹת בְּגָדִים וַיִּתֵּן אֶל־שְׁנֵי נְעָרָיו וַיִּשְׂאוּ
	לְפָנָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Naaman said, “Please take two talents.” He urged him, and he
	wrapped the two talents of silver in two bags and gave them, along
	with two changes of clothes, to two of his servants, who carried
	them ahead of him.
}

\eX{
	When [Gehazi] arrived at the citadel, he took [the things] from
	them and deposited them in the house. Then he dismissed the men
	and they went their way.
}

\eX{
	He entered and stood before his master; and Elisha said to him,
	“Where have you been, Gehazi?” He replied, “Your servant has not
	gone anywhere.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו לֹא־לִבִּי הָלַךְ כַּאֲשֶׁר הָפַךְ־אִישׁ מֵעַל מֶרְכַּבְתּוֹ לִקְרָאתֶךָ הַעֵת לָקַחַת אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף וְלָקַחַת בְּגָדִים וְזֵיתִים וּכְרָמִים וְצֹאן וּבָקָר
	וַעֲבָדִים וּשְׁפָחוֹת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then [Elisha] said to him, “Did not my spirit go along when a man
	got down from his chariot to meet you? Is this a time to take
	money in order to buy clothing and olive groves and vineyards,
	sheep and oxen, and male and female slaves?
}

\eX{
	Surely, the leprosy of Naaman shall cling to you and to your
	descendants forever.” And as [Gehazi] left his presence, he was
	snow-white with leprosy.
}

\eX{
	The disciples of the prophets said to Elisha, “See, the place
	where we live under your direction is too cramped for us.
}

\eX{
	Let us go to the Jordan, and let us each get a log there and build
	quarters there for ourselves to live in.” “Do so,” he replied.
}

\eX{
	Then one of them said, “Will you please come along with your
	servants?” “Yes, I will come,” he said;
}

\eX{
	and he accompanied them. So they went to the Jordan and cut
	timber.
}

\eX{
	As one of them was felling a trunk, the iron ax head fell into the
	water. And he cried aloud, “Alas, master, it was a borrowed one!”
}

\eX{
	“Where did it fall?” asked the man of God. He showed him the spot;
	and he cut off a stick and threw it in, and he made the ax head
	float.
}

\eX{
	While the king of Aram was waging war against Israel, he took
	counsel with his officers and said, “I will encamp in such and
	such a place.”
}

\eX{
	But the man of God sent word to the king of Israel, “Take care not
	to pass through that place, for the Arameans are encamped there.”
}

\eX{
	So the king of Israel sent word to the place of which the man of
	God had told him. Time and again he alerted such a place and took
	precautions there.
}

\eX{
	Greatly agitated about this matter, the king of Aram summoned his
	officers and said to them, “Tell me! Who of us is on the side of
	the king of Israel?”
}

\eX{
	“No one, my lord king,” said one of the officers. “Elisha, that
	prophet in Israel, tells the king of Israel the very words you
	speak in your bedroom.”
}

\eX{
	“Go find out where he is,” he said, “so that I can have him
	seized.” It was reported to him that [Elisha] was in Dothan;
}

\eX{
	so he sent horses and chariots there and a strong force. They
	arrived at night and encircled the town.
}

\eX{
	When the attendant of the man of God rose early and went outside,
	he saw a force, with horses and chariots, surrounding the town.
	“Alas, master, what shall we do?” his servant asked him.
}

\eX{
	“Have no fear,” he replied. “There are more on our side than on
	theirs.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּתְפַּלֵּל אֱלִישָׁע וַיֹּאמַר יְהֹוָה פְּקַח־נָא אֶת־עֵינָיו וְיִרְאֶה וַיִּפְקַח יְהֹוָה אֶת־עֵינֵי הַנַּעַר וַיַּרְא וְהִנֵּה הָהָר מָלֵא סוּסִים וְרֶכֶב אֵשׁ סְבִיבֹת
	אֱלִישָׁע׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then Elisha prayed: “LORD, open his eyes and let him see.” And
	YHWH opened the servant’s eyes and he saw the hills all around
	Elisha covered with horses and chariots of fire.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 6:17. Elisha does not ask for more horses and chariots. He asks that the
%		servant’s eyes be opened to what is already there.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	[The Arameans] came down against him, and Elisha prayed to YHWH:
	“Please strike this people with a blinding light.” And He struck
	them with a blinding light, as Elisha had asked.
}

\eX{
	Elisha said to them, “This is not the road, and that is not the
	town; follow me, and I will lead you to the man you want.” And he
	led them to Samaria.
}

\eX{
	When they entered Samaria, Elisha said, “O LORD, open the eyes of
	these men so that they may see.” YHWH opened their eyes and they
	saw that they were inside Samaria.
}

\eX{
	When the king of Israel saw them, he said to Elisha, “Father,
	shall I strike them down?”
}

\eX{
	“No, do not,” he replied. “Did you take them captive with your
	sword and bow that you would strike them down? Rather, set food
	and drink before them, and let them eat and drink and return to
	their master.”
}

\eX{
	So he prepared a lavish feast for them and, after they had eaten
	and drunk, he let them go, and they returned to their master. And
	the Aramean bands stopped invading the land of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Sometime later, King Ben-hadad of Aram mustered his entire army
	and marched upon Samaria and besieged it.
}

\eX{
	There was a great famine in Samaria, and the siege continued until
	a donkey’s head sold for eighty [shekels] of silver and a quarter
	of a kab of doves’ dung for five shekels.
}

\eX{
	Once, when the king of Israel was walking on the city wall, a
	woman cried out to him: “Help me, Your Majesty!”
}

\eX{
	“Don’t [ask me],” he replied. “Let YHWH help you! Where could I
	get help for you, from the threshing floor or from the winepress?
}

\eX{
	But what troubles you?” the king asked her. The woman answered,
	“That woman said to me, ‘Give up your son and we will eat him
	today; and tomorrow we’ll eat my son.’
}

\eX{
	So we cooked my son and we ate him. The next day I said to her,
	‘Give up your son and let’s eat him’; but she hid her son.”
}

\eX{
	When the king heard what the woman said, he rent his clothes; and
	as he walked along the wall, the people could see that he was
	wearing sackcloth underneath.
}

\eX{
	He said, “Thus and more may God do to me if the head of Elisha son
	of Shaphat remains on his shoulders today.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וֶאֱלִישָׁע יֹשֵׁב בְּבֵיתוֹ וְהַזְּקֵנִים יֹשְׁבִים אִתּוֹ וַיִּשְׁלַח אִישׁ מִלְּפָנָיו בְּטֶרֶם יָבֹא הַמַּלְאָךְ אֵלָיו וְהוּא אָמַר אֶל־הַזְּקֵנִים הַרְּאִיתֶם כִּי־שָׁלַח
	בֶּן־הַמְרַצֵּחַ הַזֶּה לְהָסִיר אֶת־רֹאשִׁי רְאוּ כְּבֹא הַמַּלְאָךְ סִגְרוּ הַדֶּלֶת וּלְחַצְתֶּם אֹתוֹ בַּדֶּלֶת הֲלוֹא קוֹל רַגְלֵי אֲדֹנָיו אַחֲרָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	While he was still talking to them, the messenger came to him and
	said, “This calamity is from YHWH. What more can I hope for from
	YHWH?”
}

\eX{
	And Elisha replied, “Hear the word of YHWH. Thus said YHWH: This
	time tomorrow, a seah of choice flour shall sell for a shekel at
	the gate of Samaria, and two seahs of barley for a shekel.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעַן הַשָּׁלִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־לַמֶּלֶךְ נִשְׁעָן עַל־יָדוֹ אֶת־אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים וַיֹּאמַר הִנֵּה יְהֹוָה עֹשֶׂה אֲרֻבּוֹת בַּשָּׁמַיִם הֲיִהְיֶה הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה וַיֹּאמֶר הִנְּכָה
	רֹאֶה בְּעֵינֶיךָ וּמִשָּׁם לֹא תֹאכֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The aide on whose arm the king was leaning spoke up and said to
	the man of God, “Even if YHWH were to make windows in the sky,
	could this come to pass?” And he retorted, “You shall see it with
	your own eyes, but you shall not eat of it.”
}

\eX{
	There were four men, lepers, outside the gate. They said to one
	another, “Why should we sit here waiting for death?
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	אִם־אָמַרְנוּ נָבוֹא הָעִיר וְהָרָעָב בָּעִיר וָמַתְנוּ שָׁם וְאִם־יָשַׁבְנוּ פֹה וָמָתְנוּ וְעַתָּה לְכוּ וְנִפְּלָה אֶל־מַחֲנֵה אֲרָם אִם־יְחַיֻּנוּ נִחְיֶה וְאִם־יְמִיתֻנוּ
	וָמָתְנוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	If we decide to go into the town, what with the famine in the
	town, we shall die there; and if we just sit here, still we die.
	Come, let us desert to the Aramean camp. If they let us live, we
	shall live; and if they put us to death, we shall but die.”
}

\eX{
	They set out at twilight for the Aramean camp; but when they came
	to the edge of the Aramean camp, there was no one there.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַאדֹנָי הִשְׁמִיעַ אֶת־מַחֲנֵה אֲרָם קוֹל רֶכֶב קוֹל סוּס קוֹל חַיִל גָּדוֹל וַיֹּאמְרוּ אִישׁ אֶל־אָחִיו הִנֵּה שָׂכַר־עָלֵינוּ מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת־מַלְכֵי הַחִתִּים
	וְאֶת־מַלְכֵי מִצְרַיִם לָבוֹא עָלֵינוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	For YHWH had caused the Aramean camp to hear a sound of chariots,
	a sound of horses—the din of a huge army. They said to one
	another, “The king of Israel must have hired the kings of the
	Hittites and the kings of Mizraim to attack us!”
}

\eX{
	And they fled headlong in the twilight, abandoning their tents and
	horses and asses—the [entire] camp just as it was—as they fled for
	their lives.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ הַמְצֹרָעִים הָאֵלֶּה עַד־קְצֵה הַמַּחֲנֶה וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל־אֹהֶל אֶחָד וַיֹּאכְלוּ וַיִּשְׁתּוּ וַיִּשְׂאוּ מִשָּׁם כֶּסֶף וְזָהָב וּבְגָדִים וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיַּטְמִנוּ
	וַיָּשֻׁבוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל־אֹהֶל אַחֵר וַיִּשְׂאוּ מִשָּׁם וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיַּטְמִנוּ׃
}

\hX{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ אִישׁ אֶל־רֵעֵהוּ לֹא־כֵן אֲנַחְנוּ עֹשִׂים הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה יוֹם־בְּשֹׂרָה הוּא וַאֲנַחְנוּ מַחְשִׁים וְחִכִּינוּ עַד־אוֹר הַבֹּקֶר וּמְצָאָנוּ עָווֹן וְעַתָּה לְכוּ
	וְנָבֹאָה וְנַגִּידָה בֵּית הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then they said to one another, “We are not doing right. This is a
	day of good news, and we are keeping silent! If we wait until the
	light of morning, we shall incur guilt. Come, let us go and inform
	the king’s palace.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ וַיִּקְרְאוּ אֶל־שֹׁעֵר הָעִיר וַיַּגִּידוּ לָהֶם לֵאמֹר בָּאנוּ אֶל־מַחֲנֵה אֲרָם וְהִנֵּה אֵין־שָׁם אִישׁ וְקוֹל אָדָם כִּי אִם־הַסּוּס אָסוּר וְהַחֲמוֹר אָסוּר
	וְאֹהָלִים כַּאֲשֶׁר־הֵמָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They went and called out to the gatekeepers of the city and told
	them, “We have been to the Aramean camp. There is not a soul
	there, nor any human sound; but the horses are tethered and the
	asses are tethered and the tents are undisturbed.”
}

\eX{
	The gatekeepers called out, and the news was passed on into the
	king’s palace.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּקם הַמֶּלֶךְ לַיְלָה וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־עֲבָדָיו אַגִּידָה־נָּא לָכֶם אֵת אֲשֶׁר־עָשׂוּ לָנוּ אֲרָם יָדְעוּ כִּי־רְעֵבִים אֲנַחְנוּ וַיֵּצְאוּ מִן־הַמַּחֲנֶה לְהֵחָבֵה (בהשדה)
	[בַשָּׂדֶה] לֵאמֹר כִּי־יֵצְאוּ מִן־הָעִיר וְנִתְפְּשֵׂם חַיִּים וְאֶל־הָעִיר נָבֹא׃
}

\hX{
	וַיַּעַן אֶחָד מֵעֲבָדָיו וַיֹּאמֶר וְיִקְחוּ־נָא חֲמִשָּׁה מִן־הַסּוּסִים הַנִּשְׁאָרִים אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁאֲרוּ־בָהּ הִנָּם כְּכל־[הֲמוֹן] (ההמון) יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁאֲרוּ־בָהּ
	הִנָּם כְּכל־הֲמוֹן יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר־תָּמּוּ וְנִשְׁלְחָה וְנִרְאֶה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They took two teams of horses and the king sent them after the
	Aramean army, saying, “Go and find out.”
}

\eX{
	They followed them as far as the Jordan, and found the entire road
	full of clothing and gear which the Arameans had thrown away in
	their haste; and the messengers returned and told the king.
}

\eX{
	The people then went out and plundered the Aramean camp. So a seah
	of choice flour sold for a shekel, and two seahs of barley for a
	shekel—as YHWH had spoken.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהַמֶּלֶךְ הִפְקִיד אֶת־הַשָּׁלִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־נִשְׁעָן עַל־יָדוֹ עַל־הַשַּׁעַר וַיִּרְמְסֻהוּ הָעָם בַּשַּׁעַר וַיָּמֹת כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר בְּרֶדֶת
	הַמֶּלֶךְ אֵלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Now the king had put the aide on whose arm he leaned in charge of
	the gate; and he was trampled to death in the gate by the
	people—just as the man of God had spoken, as he had spoken when
	the king came down to him.
}

\eX{
	For when the man of God said to the king, “This time tomorrow two
	seahs of barley shall sell at the gate of Samaria for a shekel,
	and a seah of choice flour for a shekel,”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעַן הַשָּׁלִישׁ אֶת־אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים וַיֹּאמַר וְהִנֵּה יְהֹוָה עֹשֶׂה אֲרֻבּוֹת בַּשָּׁמַיִם הֲיִהְיֶה כַּדָּבָר הַזֶּה וַיֹּאמֶר הִנְּךָ רֹאֶה בְּעֵינֶיךָ וּמִשָּׁם לֹא
	תֹאכֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	the aide answered the man of God and said, “Even if YHWH made
	windows in the sky, could this come to pass?” And he retorted,
	“You shall see it with your own eyes, but you shall not eat of
	it.”
}

\eX{
	That is exactly what happened to him: The people trampled him to
	death in the gate.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וֶאֱלִישָׁע דִּבֶּר אֶל־הָאִשָּׁה אֲשֶׁר־הֶחֱיָה אֶת־בְּנָהּ לֵאמֹר קוּמִי וּלְכִי (אתי) [אַתְּ] וּבֵיתֵךְ וְגוּרִי בַּאֲשֶׁר תָּגוּרִי כִּי־קָרָא יְהֹוָה לָרָעָב וְגַם־בָּא
	אֶל־הָאָרֶץ שֶׁבַע שָׁנִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Elisha had said to the woman whose son he revived, “Leave
	immediately with your family and go sojourn somewhere else; for
	YHWH has decreed a seven-year famine upon the land, and it has
	already begun.”
}

\eX{
	The woman had done as the man of God had spoken; she left with her
	family and sojourned in the land of the Philistines for seven
	years.
}

\eX{
	At the end of the seven years, the woman returned from the land of
	the Philistines and went to the king to complain about her house
	and farm.
}

\eX{
	Now the king was talking to Gehazi, the servant of the man of God,
	and he said, “Tell me all the wonderful things that Elisha has
	done.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי הוּא מְסַפֵּר לַמֶּלֶךְ אֵת אֲשֶׁר־הֶחֱיָה אֶת־הַמֵּת וְהִנֵּה הָאִשָּׁה אֲשֶׁר־הֶחֱיָה אֶת־בְּנָהּ צֹעֶקֶת אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ עַל־בֵּיתָהּ וְעַל־שָׂדָהּ וַיֹּאמֶר גֵּחֲזִי
	אֲדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ זֹאת הָאִשָּׁה וְזֶה־בְּנָהּ אֲשֶׁר־הֶחֱיָה אֱלִישָׁע׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	While he was telling the king how [Elisha] had revived a dead
	person, in came the woman whose son he had revived, complaining to
	the king about her house and farm. “My lord king,” said Gehazi,
	“this is the woman and this is her son whom Elisha revived.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁאַל הַמֶּלֶךְ לָאִשָּׁה וַתְּסַפֶּר־לוֹ וַיִּתֶּן־לָהּ הַמֶּלֶךְ סָרִיס אֶחָד לֵאמֹר הָשֵׁיב אֶת־כּל־אֲשֶׁר־לָהּ וְאֵת כּל־תְּבוּאֹת הַשָּׂדֶה מִיּוֹם עזְבָה אֶת־הָאָרֶץ
	וְעַד־עָתָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The king questioned the woman, and she told him [the story]; so
	the king assigned a eunuch to her and instructed him: “Restore all
	her property, and all the revenue from her farm from the time she
	left the country until now.”
}

\eX{
	Elisha arrived in Damascus at a time when King Ben-hadad of Aram
	was ill. The king was told, “The man of God is on his way here,”
}

\eX{
	and he said to Hazael, “Take a gift with you and go meet the man
	of God, and through him inquire of YHWH: Will I recover from this
	illness?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ חֲזָאֵל לִקְרָאתוֹ וַיִּקַּח מִנְחָה בְיָדוֹ וְכל־טוּב דַּמֶּשֶׂק מַשָּׂא אַרְבָּעִים גָּמָל וַיָּבֹא וַיַּעֲמֹד לְפָנָיו וַיֹּאמֶר בִּנְךָ בֶן־הֲדַד מֶלֶךְ־אֲרָם
	שְׁלָחַנִי אֵלֶיךָ לֵאמֹר הַאֶחְיֶה מֵחֳלִי זֶה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Hazael went to meet him, taking with him as a gift forty
	camel-loads of all the bounty of Damascus. He came and stood
	before him and said, “Your son, King Ben-hadad of Aram, has sent
	me to you to ask: Will I recover from this illness?”
}

\eX{
	Elisha said to him, “Go and say to him, ‘You will recover.’
	However, YHWH has revealed to me that he will die.”
}

\eX{
	The man of God kept his face expressionless for a long time; and
	then he wept.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר חֲזָאֵל מַדּוּעַ אֲדֹנִי בֹכֶה וַיֹּאמֶר כִּי־יָדַעְתִּי אֵת אֲשֶׁר־תַּעֲשֶׂה לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל רָעָה מִבְצְרֵיהֶם תְּשַׁלַּח בָּאֵשׁ וּבַחֻרֵיהֶם בַּחֶרֶב תַּהֲרֹג
	וְעֹלְלֵיהֶם תְּרַטֵּשׁ וְהָרֹתֵיהֶם תְּבַקֵּעַ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“But how,” asked Hazael, “can your servant, who is a mere dog,
	perform such a mighty deed?” Elisha replied, “YHWH has shown me a
	vision of you as king of Aram.”
}

\eX{
	He left Elisha and returned to his master, who asked him, “What
	did Elisha say to you?” He replied, “He told me that you would
	recover.”
}

\eX{
	The next day, [Hazael] took a piece of netting, dipped it in
	water, and spread it over his face. So [Ben-hadad] died, and
	Hazael succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the fifth year of King Joram son of Ahab of Israel—Jehoshaphat
	had been king of Judah—Joram son of King Jehoshaphat of Judah
	became king.
}

\eX{
	He was thirty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in
	Jerusalem eight years.
}

\eX{
	He followed the practices of the kings of Israel—whatever the
	House of Ahab did, for he had married a daughter of Ahab—and he
	did what was displeasing to YHWH.
}

\eX{
	However, YHWH refrained from destroying Judah, for the sake of His
	servant David, in accordance with His promise to maintain a lamp
	for his descendants for all time.
}

\eX{
	During his reign, the Edomites rebelled against Judah’s rule and
	set up a king of their own.
}

\eX{
	Joram crossed over to Zair with all his chariotry. He arose by
	night and attacked the Edomites, who were surrounding him and the
	chariot commanders; but his troops fled to their homes.
}

\eX{
	Thus Edom fell away from Judah, as is still the case. Libnah
	likewise fell away at that time.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Joram’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	Joram slept with his fathers and was buried with his fathers in
	the City of David; his son Ahaziah succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the twelfth year of King Joram son of Ahab of Israel, Ahaziah
	son of Joram became king of Judah.
}

\eX{
	Ahaziah was twenty-two years old when he became king, and he
	reigned in Jerusalem one year; his mother’s name was Athaliah
	daughter of King Omri of Israel.
}

\eX{
	He walked in the ways of the House of Ahab and did what was
	displeasing to YHWH, like the House of Ahab, for he was related by
	marriage to the House of Ahab.
}

\eX{
	He marched with Joram son of Ahab to battle against King Hazael of
	Aram at Ramoth-gilead, but the Arameans wounded Joram.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב יוֹרָם הַמֶּלֶךְ לְהִתְרַפֵּא בְיִזְרְעֶאל מִן־הַמַּכִּים אֲשֶׁר יַכֻּהוּ אֲרַמִּים בָּרָמָה בְּהִלָּחֲמוֹ אֶת־חֲזָהאֵל מֶלֶךְ אֲרָם וַאֲחַזְיָהוּ בֶן־יְהוֹרָם מֶלֶךְ
	יְהוּדָה יָרַד לִרְאוֹת אֶת־יוֹרָם בֶּן־אַחְאָב בְּיִזְרְעֶאל כִּי־חֹלֶה הוּא׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	King Joram retired to Jezreel to recover from the wounds which the
	Arameans had inflicted upon him at Ramah, when he fought against
	King Hazael of Aram. And King Ahaziah son of Joram of Judah went
	down to Jezreel to visit Joram son of Ahab while he was ill.
}

\eX{
	Then the prophet Elisha summoned one of the disciples of the
	prophets and said to him, “Tie up your skirts, and take along this
	flask of oil, and go to Ramoth-gilead.
}

\eX{
	When you arrive there, go and see Jehu son of Jehoshaphat son of
	Nimshi; get him to leave his comrades, and take him into an inner
	room.
}

\eX{
	Then take the flask of oil and pour some on his head, and say,
	‘Thus said YHWH: I anoint you king over Israel.’ Then open the
	door and flee without delay.”
}

\eX{
	The young man, the servant of the prophet, went to Ramoth-gilead.
}

\eX{
	When he arrived, the army commanders were sitting together. He
	said, “Commander, I have a message for you.” “For which one of
	us?” Jehu asked. He answered, “For you, commander.”
}

\eX{
	So [Jehu] arose and went inside; and [the disciple] poured the oil
	on his head, and said to him, “Thus said YHWH, the God of Israel:
	I anoint you king over the people of YHWH, over Israel.
}

\eX{
	You shall strike down the House of Ahab your master; thus will I
	avenge on Jezebel the blood of My servants the prophets, and the
	blood of the other servants of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	The whole House of Ahab shall perish, and I will cut off every
	male belonging to Ahab, bond and free in Israel.
}

\eX{
	I will make the House of Ahab like the House of Jeroboam son of
	Nebat, and like the House of Baasha son of Ahijah.
}

\eX{
	The dogs shall devour Jezebel in the field of Jezreel, with none
	to bury her.” Then he opened the door and fled.
}

\eX{
	Jehu went out to the other officers of his master, and they asked
	him, “Is all well? What did that madman come to you for?” He said
	to them, “You know the man and his ranting!”
}

\eX{
	“You’re lying,” they said. “Tell us [the truth].” Then he replied,
	“Thus and thus he said: Thus said YHWH: I anoint you king over
	Israel!”
}

\eX{
	Quickly each man took his cloak and placed it under him, on the
	top step. They sounded the horn and proclaimed, “Jehu is king!”
}

\eX{
	Thus Jehu son of Jehoshaphat son of Nimshi conspired against
	Joram. Joram and all Israel had been defending Ramoth-gilead
	against King Hazael of Aram,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב יְהוֹרָם הַמֶּלֶךְ לְהִתְרַפֵּא בְיִזְרְעֶאל מִן־הַמַּכִּים אֲשֶׁר יַכֻּהוּ אֲרַמִּים בְּהִלָּחֲמוֹ אֶת־חֲזָאֵל מֶלֶךְ אֲרָם וַיֹּאמֶר יֵהוּא אִם־יֵשׁ נַפְשְׁכֶם אַל־יֵצֵא
	פָלִיט מִן־הָעִיר לָלֶכֶת (לגיד) [לְהַגִּיד] בְּיִזְרְעֶאל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	but King Joram had gone back to Jezreel to recover from the wounds
	which the Arameans had inflicted on him in his battle with King
	Hazael of Aram. Jehu said, “If such is your wish, allow no one to
	slip out of the town to go and report this in Jezreel.”
}

\eX{
	Then Jehu mounted his chariot and drove to Jezreel; for Joram was
	lying ill there, and King Ahaziah of Judah had gone down to visit
	Joram.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְהַצֹּפֶה עֹמֵד עַל־הַמִּגְדָּל בְּיִזְרְעֶאל וַיַּרְא אֶת־שִׁפְעַת יֵהוּא בְּבֹאוֹ וַיֹּאמֶר שִׁפְעַת אֲנִי רֹאֶה וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹרָם קַח רַכָּב וּשְׁלַח לִקְרָאתָם וְיֹאמַר
	הֲשָׁלוֹם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The lookout was stationed on the tower in Jezreel, and he saw the
	troop of Jehu as he approached. He called out, “I see a troop!”
	Joram said, “Dispatch a horseman to meet them and let him ask: Is
	all well?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ רֹכֵב הַסּוּס לִקְרָאתוֹ וַיֹּאמֶר כֹּה־אָמַר הַמֶּלֶךְ הֲשָׁלוֹם וַיֹּאמֶר יֵהוּא מַה־לְּךָ וּלְשָׁלוֹם סֹב אֶל־אַחֲרָי וַיַּגֵּד הַצֹּפֶה לֵאמֹר בָּא־הַמַּלְאָךְ
	עַד־הֵם וְלֹא־שָׁב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The horseman went to meet him, and he said, “The king inquires: Is
	all well?” Jehu replied, “What concern of yours is it whether all
	is well? Fall in behind me.” The lookout reported: “The messenger
	has reached them, but has not turned back.”
}

\eX{
	So he sent out a second horseman. He came to them and said, “Thus
	says the king: Is all well?” Jehu answered, “What concern of yours
	is it whether all is well? Fall in behind me.”
}

\eX{
	And the lookout reported, “The messenger has reached them, but has
	not turned back. And it looks like the driving of Jehu son of
	Nimshi, who drives wildly.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹרָם אֱסֹר וַיֶּאְסֹר רִכְבּוֹ וַיֵּצֵא יְהוֹרָם מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל וַאֲחַזְיָהוּ מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה אִישׁ בְּרִכְבּוֹ וַיֵּצְאוּ לִקְרַאת יֵהוּא וַיִּמְצָאֻהוּ
	בְּחֶלְקַת נָבוֹת הַיִּזְרְעֵאלִי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Joram ordered, “Hitch up [the chariot]!” They hitched up his
	chariot; and King Joram of Israel and King Ahaziah of Judah went
	out, each in his own chariot, to meet Jehu. They met him at the
	field of Naboth the Jezreelite.
}

\eX{
	When Joram saw Jehu, he asked, “Is all well, Jehu?” But Jehu
	replied, “How can all be well as long as your mother Jezebel
	carries on her countless harlotries and sorceries?”
}

\eX{
	Thereupon Joram turned his horses around and fled, crying out to
	Ahaziah, “Treason, Ahaziah!”
}

\eX{
	But Jehu drew his bow and hit Joram between the shoulders, so that
	the arrow pierced his heart; and he collapsed in his chariot.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל־בִּדְקַר שָׁלִשֹׁה שָׂא הַשְׁלִכֵהוּ בְּחֶלְקַת שְׂדֵה נָבוֹת הַיִּזְרְעֵאלִי כִּי־זְכֹר אֲנִי וָאַתָּה אֵת רֹכְבִים צְמָדִים אַחֲרֵי אַחְאָב אָבִיו וַיהֹוָה נָשָׂא
	עָלָיו אֶת־הַמַּשָּׂא הַזֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Jehu thereupon ordered his officer Bidkar, “Pick him up and throw
	him into the field of Naboth the Jezreelite. Remember how you and
	I were riding side by side behind his father Ahab, when YHWH made
	this pronouncement about him:
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	אִם־לֹא אֶת־דְּמֵי נָבוֹת וְאֶת־דְּמֵי בָנָיו רָאִיתִי אֶמֶשׁ נְאֻם־יְהֹוָה וְשִׁלַּמְתִּי לְךָ בַּחֶלְקָה הַזֹּאת נְאֻם־יְהֹוָה וְעַתָּה שָׂא הַשְׁלִכֵהוּ בַּחֶלְקָה כִּדְבַר
	יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	‘I swear, I have taken note of the blood of Naboth and the blood
	of his sons yesterday—declares YHWH. And I will requite you in
	this plot—declares YHWH.’ So pick him up and throw him unto the
	plot in accordance with the word of YHWH.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַאֲחַזְיָה מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה רָאָה וַיָּנס דֶּרֶךְ בֵּית הַגָּן וַיִּרְדֹּף אַחֲרָיו יֵהוּא וַיֹּאמֶר גַּם־אֹתוֹ הַכֻּהוּ אֶל־הַמֶּרְכָּבָה בְּמַעֲלֵה־גוּר אֲשֶׁר אֶת־יִבְלְעָם
	וַיָּנס מְגִדּוֹ וַיָּמת שָׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	On seeing this, King Ahaziah of Judah fled along the road to
	Beth-haggan. Jehu pursued him and said, “Shoot him down too!” [And
	they shot him] in his chariot at the ascent of Gur, which is near
	Ibleam. He fled to Megiddo and died there.
}

\eX{
	His servants conveyed him in a chariot to Jerusalem, and they
	buried him in his grave with his fathers, in the City of David.
}

\eX{
	(Ahaziah had become king over Judah in the eleventh year of Joram
	son of Ahab.)
}

\eX{
	Jehu went on to Jezreel. When Jezebel heard of it, she painted her
	eyes with kohl and dressed her hair, and she looked out of the
	window.
}

\eX{
	As Jehu entered the gate, she called out, “Is all well, Zimri,
	murderer of your master?”
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 9:31. “Zimri” is a one-word history lesson. Jezebel meets Jehu by naming
%		the earlier regicide who lasted seven days.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He looked up toward the window and said, “Who is on my side, who?”
	And two or three eunuchs leaned out toward him.
}

\eX{
	“Throw her down,” he said. They threw her down; and her blood
	spattered on the wall and on the horses, and they trampled her.
}

\eX{
	Then he went inside and ate and drank. And he said, “Attend to
	that cursed woman and bury her, for she was a king’s daughter.”
}

\eX{
	So they went to bury her; but all they found of her were the
	skull, the feet, and the hands.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשֻׁבוּ וַיַּגִּידוּ לוֹ וַיֹּאמֶר דְּבַר־יְהֹוָה הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר בְּיַד־עַבְדּוֹ אֵלִיָּהוּ הַתִּשְׁבִּי לֵאמֹר בְּחֵלֶק יִזְרְעֶאל יֹאכְלוּ הַכְּלָבִים אֶת־בְּשַׂר
	אִיזָבֶל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They came back and reported to him; and he said, “It is just as
	YHWH spoke through His servant Elijah the Tishbite: The dogs shall
	devour the flesh of Jezebel in the field of Jezreel;
}

\eX{
	and the carcass of Jezebel shall be like dung on the ground, in
	the field of Jezreel, so that none will be able to say: ‘This was
	Jezebel.’”
}

\eX{
	Ahab had seventy descendants in Samaria. Jehu wrote letters and
	sent them to Samaria, to the elders and officials of Jezreel and
	to the guardians of [the children] of Ahab, as follows:
}

\eX{
	“Now, when this letter reaches you—since your master’s sons are
	with you and you also have chariots and horses, and a fortified
	city and weapons—
}

\eX{
	select the best and the most suitable of your master’s sons and
	set him on his father’s throne, and fight for your master’s
	house.”
}

\eX{
	But they were overcome by fear, for they thought, “If the two
	kings could not stand up to him, how can we?”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח אֲשֶׁר־עַל־הַבַּיִת וַאֲשֶׁר עַל־הָעִיר וְהַזְּקֵנִים וְהָאֹמְנִים אֶל־יֵהוּא לֵאמֹר עֲבָדֶיךָ אֲנַחְנוּ וְכֹל אֲשֶׁר־תֹּאמַר אֵלֵינוּ נַעֲשֶׂה לֹא־נַמְלִךְ אִישׁ
	הַטּוֹב בְּעֵינֶיךָ עֲשֵׂה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The steward of the palace and the governor of the city and the
	elders and the guardians sent this message to Jehu: “We are your
	subjects, and we shall do whatever you tell us to. We shall not
	proclaim anyone king; do whatever you like.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּכְתֹּב אֲלֵיהֶם סֵפֶר שֵׁנִית לֵאמֹר אִם־לִי אַתֶּם וּלְקֹלִי אַתֶּם שֹׁמְעִים קְחוּ אֶת־רָאשֵׁי אַנְשֵׁי בְנֵי־אֲדֹנֵיכֶם וּבֹאוּ אֵלַי כָּעֵת מָחָר יִזְרְעֶאלָה וּבְנֵי
	הַמֶּלֶךְ שִׁבְעִים אִישׁ אֶת־גְּדֹלֵי הָעִיר מְגַדְּלִים אוֹתָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But when the letter reached them, they took the princes and
	slaughtered all seventy of them; they put their heads in baskets
	and sent them to him in Jezreel.
}

\eX{
	A messenger came and reported to him: “They have brought the heads
	of the princes.” He said, “Pile them up in two heaps at the
	entrance of the gate before morning.”
}

\eX{
	In the morning he went out and stood there; and he said to all the
	people, “Are you blameless? True, I conspired against my master
	and killed him; but who struck down all of these?
}

\eX{
	Know, then, that nothing that YHWH has spoken concerning the House
	of Ahab shall remain unfulfilled, for YHWH has done what he
	announced through His servant Elijah.”
}

\eX{
	And Jehu struck down all that were left of the House of Ahab in
	Jezreel—and all his notables, intimates, and priests—till he left
	him no survivor.
}

\eX{
	He then set out for Samaria. On the way, when he was at Beth-eked
	of the shepherds,
}

\eX{
	Jehu came upon the kinsmen of King Ahaziah of Judah. “Who are
	you?” he asked. They replied, “We are the kinsmen of Ahaziah, and
	we have come to pay our respects to the sons of the king and the
	sons of the queen mother.”
}

\eX{
	“Take them alive!” he said. They took them alive and then
	slaughtered them at the pit of Beth-eked, forty-two of them; he
	did not spare a single one.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ מִשָּׁם וַיִּמְצָא אֶת־יְהוֹנָדָב בֶּן־רֵכָב לִקְרָאתוֹ וַיְבָרְכֵהוּ וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו הֲיֵשׁ אֶת־לְבָבְךָ יָשָׁר כַּאֲשֶׁר לְבָבִי עִם־לְבָבֶךָ וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹנָדָב יֵשׁ
	וָיֵשׁ תְּנָה אֶת־יָדֶךָ וַיִּתֵּן יָדוֹ וַיַּעֲלֵהוּ אֵלָיו אֶל־הַמֶּרְכָּבָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“Come with me,” he said, “and see my zeal for YHWH.” And he was
	taken along in the chariot.
}

\eX{
	Arriving in Samaria, [Jehu] struck down all the survivors of [the
	House of] Ahab in Samaria, until he wiped it out, fulfilling the
	word that YHWH had spoken to Elijah.
}

\eX{
	Jehu assembled all the people and said to them, “Ahab served Baal
	little; Jehu shall serve him much!
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְעַתָּה כל־נְבִיאֵי הַבַּעַל כּל־עֹבְדָיו וְכל־כֹּהֲנָיו קִרְאוּ אֵלַי אִישׁ אַל־יִפָּקֵד כִּי זֶבַח גָּדוֹל לִי לַבַּעַל כֹּל אֲשֶׁר־יִפָּקֵד לֹא יִחְיֶה וְיֵהוּא עָשָׂה
	בְעקְבָּה לְמַעַן הַאֲבִיד אֶת־עֹבְדֵי הַבָּעַל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Jehu gave orders to convoke a solemn assembly for Baal, and one
	was proclaimed.
}

\eX{
	Jehu sent word throughout Israel, and all the worshipers of Baal
	came, not a single one remained behind. They came into the temple
	of Baal, and the temple of Baal was filled from end to end.
}

\eX{
	He said to the man in charge of the wardrobe, “Bring out the
	vestments for all the worshipers of Baal”; and he brought
	vestments out for them.
}

\eX{
	Then Jehu and Jehonadab son of Rechab came into the temple of
	Baal, and they said to the worshipers of Baal, “Search and make
	sure that there are no worshipers of YHWH among you, but only
	worshipers of Baal.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ לַעֲשׂוֹת זְבָחִים וְעֹלוֹת וְיֵהוּא שָׂם־לוֹ בַחוּץ שְׁמֹנִים אִישׁ וַיֹּאמֶר הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר־יִמָּלֵט מִן־הָאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מֵבִיא עַל־יְדֵיכֶם נַפְשׁוֹ תַּחַת
	נַפְשׁוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So they went in to offer sacrifices and burnt offerings. But Jehu
	had stationed eighty of his men outside and had said, “Whoever
	permits the escape of a single one of the men I commit to your
	charge shall forfeit life for life.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי כְּכַלֹּתוֹ לַעֲשׂוֹת הָעֹלָה וַיֹּאמֶר יֵהוּא לָרָצִים וְלַשָּׁלִשִׁים בֹּאוּ הַכּוּם אִישׁ אַל־יֵצֵא וַיַּכּוּם לְפִי־חָרֶב וַיַּשְׁלִכוּ הָרָצִים וְהַשָּׁלִשִׁים
	וַיֵּלְכוּ עַד־עִיר בֵּית־הַבָּעַל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They brought out the pillars of the temple of Baal and burned
	them.
}

\eX{
	They destroyed the pillar of Baal, and they tore down the temple
	of Baal and turned it into latrines, as is still the case.
}

\eX{
	However, Jehu did not turn away from the sinful objects by which
	Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused Israel to sin, namely, the golden
	calves at Bethel and at Dan.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה אֶל־יֵהוּא יַעַן אֲשֶׁר־הֱטִיבֹתָ לַעֲשׂוֹת הַיָּשָׁר בְּעֵינַי כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר בִּלְבָבִי עָשִׂיתָ לְבֵית אַחְאָב בְּנֵי רְבִעִים יֵשְׁבוּ לְךָ עַל־כִּסֵּא
	יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	YHWH said to Jehu, “Because you have acted well and done what was
	pleasing to Me, having carried out all that I desired upon the
	House of Ahab, four generations of your descendants shall occupy
	the throne of Israel.”
}

\eX{
	But Jehu was not careful to follow the Teaching of YHWH, the God
	of Israel, with all his heart; he did not turn away from the sins
	that Jeroboam had caused Israel to commit.
}

\eX{
	In those days YHWH began to reduce Israel; and Hazael harassed
	them throughout the territory of Israel
}

\eX{
	east of the Jordan, all the land of Gilead—the Gadites, the
	Reubenites, and the Manassites—from Aroer, by the Wadi Arnon, up
	to Gilead and Bashan.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Jehu’s reign, and all his actions, and all his
	exploits, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Jehu slept with his fathers and he was buried in Samaria; he was
	succeeded as king by his son Jehoahaz.
}

\eX{
	Jehu reigned over Israel for twenty-eight years in Samaria.
}

\eX{
	When Athaliah, the mother of Ahaziah, learned that her son was
	dead, she promptly killed off all who were of royal stock.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתִּקַּח יְהוֹשֶׁבַע בַּת־הַמֶּלֶךְ־יוֹרָם אֲחוֹת אֲחַזְיָהוּ אֶת־יוֹאָשׁ בֶּן־אֲחַזְיָה וַתִּגְנֹב אֹתוֹ מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי־הַמֶּלֶךְ (הממותתים) [הַמּוּמָתִים] אֹתוֹ
	וְאֶת־מֵינִקְתּוֹ בַּחֲדַר הַמִּטּוֹת וַיַּסְתִּרוּ אֹתוֹ מִפְּנֵי עֲתַלְיָהוּ וְלֹא הוּמָת׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He stayed with her for six years, hidden in the House of YHWH,
	while Athaliah reigned over the land.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּבַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית שָׁלַח יְהוֹיָדָע וַיִּקַּח אֶת־שָׂרֵי (המאיות) [הַמֵּאוֹת] לַכָּרִי וְלָרָצִים וַיָּבֵא אֹתָם אֵלָיו בֵּית יְהֹוָה וַיִּכְרֹת לָהֶם בְּרִית וַיַּשְׁבַּע
	אֹתָם בְּבֵית יְהֹוָה וַיַּרְא אֹתָם אֶת־בֶּן־הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	In the seventh year, Jehoiada sent for the chiefs of the hundreds
	of the Carites and of the guards, and had them come to him in the
	House of YHWH. He made a pact with them, exacting an oath from
	them in the House of YHWH, and he showed them the king’s son.
}

\eX{
	He instructed them: “This is what you must do: One-third of those
	who are on duty for the week shall maintain guard over the royal
	palace;
}

\eX{
	another third shall be [stationed] at the Sur Gate; and the other
	third shall be at the gate behind the guards; you shall keep guard
	over the House on every side.
}

\eX{
	The two divisions of yours who are off duty this week shall keep
	guard over the House of YHWH for the protection of the king.
}

\eX{
	You shall surround the king on every side, every man with his
	weapons at the ready; and whoever breaks through the ranks shall
	be killed. Stay close to the king in his comings and goings.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיַּעֲשׂוּ שָׂרֵי (המאיות) [הַמֵּאוֹת] כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה יְהוֹיָדָע הַכֹּהֵן וַיִּקְחוּ אִישׁ אֶת־אֲנָשָׁיו בָּאֵי הַשַּׁבָּת עִם יֹצְאֵי הַשַּׁבָּת וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל־יְהוֹיָדָע
	הַכֹּהֵן׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The chiefs of hundreds did just as Jehoiada ordered: Each took his
	men—those who were on duty that week and those who were off duty
	that week—and they presented themselves to Jehoiada the priest.
}

\eX{
	The priest gave the chiefs of hundreds King David’s spears and
	quivers that were kept in the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	The guards, each with his weapons at the ready, stationed
	themselves—from the south end of the House to the north end of the
	House, at the altar and the House—to guard the king on every side.
}

\eX{
	[Jehoiada] then brought out the king’s son, and placed upon him
	the crown and the insignia. They anointed him and proclaimed him
	king; they clapped their hands and shouted, “Long live the king!”
}

\eX{
	When Athaliah heard the shouting of the guards [and] the people,
	she came out to the people in the House of YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַתֵּרֶא וְהִנֵּה הַמֶּלֶךְ עֹמֵד עַל־הָעַמּוּד כַּמִּשְׁפָּט וְהַשָּׂרִים וְהַחֲצֹצְרוֹת אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וְכל־עַם הָאָרֶץ שָׂמֵחַ וְתֹקֵעַ בַּחֲצֹצְרוֹת וַתִּקְרַע עֲתַלְיָה
	אֶת־בְּגָדֶיהָ וַתִּקְרָא קֶשֶׁר קָשֶׁר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	She looked about and saw the king standing by the pillar, as was
	the custom, the chiefs with their trumpets beside the king, and
	all the people of the land rejoicing and blowing trumpets.
	Athaliah rent her garments and cried out, “Treason, treason!”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְצַו יְהוֹיָדָע הַכֹּהֵן אֶת־שָׂרֵי (המאיות) [הַמֵּאוֹת ] פְּקֻדֵי הַחַיִל וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם הוֹצִיאוּ אֹתָהּ אֶל־מִבֵּית לַשְּׂדֵרֹת וְהַבָּא אַחֲרֶיהָ הָמֵת בֶּחָרֶב כִּי
	אָמַר הַכֹּהֵן אַל־תּוּמַת בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They cleared a passageway for her and she entered the royal palace
	through the horses’ entrance: there she was put to death.
}

\eX{
	And Jehoiada solemnized the covenant between YHWH, on the one
	hand, and the king and the people, on the other—as well as between
	the king and the people—that they should be the people of YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֹאוּ כל־עַם הָאָרֶץ בֵּית־הַבַּעַל וַיִּתְּצֻהוּ אֶת־מִזְבְּחֹתָו וְאֶת־צְלָמָיו שִׁבְּרוּ הֵיטֵב וְאֵת מַתָּן כֹּהֵן הַבַּעַל הָרְגוּ לִפְנֵי הַמִּזְבְּחוֹת וַיָּשֶׂם
	הַכֹּהֵן פְּקֻדֹּת עַל־בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Thereupon all the people of the land went to the temple of Baal.
	They tore it down and smashed its altars and images to bits, and
	they slew Mattan, the priest of Baal, in front of the altars.
	[Jehoiada] the priest then placed guards over the House of YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח אֶת־שָׂרֵי הַמֵּאוֹת וְאֶת־הַכָּרִי וְאֶת־הָרָצִים וְאֵת כּל־עַם הָאָרֶץ וַיֹּרִידוּ אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ מִבֵּית יְהֹוָה וַיָּבוֹאוּ דֶּרֶךְ־שַׁעַר הָרָצִים בֵּית הַמֶּלֶךְ
	וַיֵּשֶׁב עַל־כִּסֵּא הַמְּלָכִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He took the chiefs of hundreds, the Carites, the guards, and all
	the people of the land, and they escorted the king from the House
	of YHWH into the royal palace by the gate of the guards. And he
	ascended the royal throne.
}

\eX{
	All the people of the land rejoiced, and the city was quiet. As
	for Athaliah, she had been put to the sword in the royal palace.
}

\eX{
	Jehoash began his reign in the seventh year of Jehu, and he
	reigned in Jerusalem forty years. His mother’s name was Zibiah of
	Beer-sheba.
}

\eX{
	All his days Jehoash did what was pleasing to YHWH, as the priest
	Jehoiada instructed him.
}

\eX{
	The shrines, however, were not removed; the people continued to
	sacrifice and offer at the shrines.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוֹאָשׁ אֶל־הַכֹּהֲנִים כֹּל כֶּסֶף הַקֳּדָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר־יוּבָא בֵית־יְהֹוָה כֶּסֶף עוֹבֵר אִישׁ כֶּסֶף נַפְשׁוֹת עֶרְכּוֹ כּל־כֶּסֶף אֲשֶׁר יַעֲלֶה עַל לֶב־אִישׁ
	לְהָבִיא בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Jehoash said to the priests, “All the money, current money,
	brought into the House of YHWH as sacred donations—any money a man
	may pay as the money equivalent of persons, or any other money
	that a man may be minded to bring to the House of YHWH—
}

\eX{
	let the priests receive it, each from his benefactor; they, in
	turn, shall make repairs on the House, wherever damage may be
	found.”
}

\eX{
	But in the twenty-third year of King Jehoash, [it was found that]
	the priests had not made the repairs on the House.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְרָא הַמֶּלֶךְ יְהוֹאָשׁ לִיהוֹיָדָע הַכֹּהֵן וְלַכֹּהֲנִים וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵהֶם מַדּוּעַ אֵינְכֶם מְחַזְּקִים אֶת־בֶּדֶק הַבָּיִת וְעַתָּה אַל־תִּקְחוּ־כֶסֶף מֵאֵת מַכָּרֵיכֶם
	כִּי־לְבֶדֶק הַבַּיִת תִּתְּנֻהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So King Jehoash summoned the priest Jehoiada and the other priests
	and said to them, “Why have you not kept the House in repair? Now
	do not accept money from your benefactors any more, but have it
	donated for the repair of the House.”
}

\eX{
	The priests agreed that they would neither accept money from the
	people nor make repairs on the House.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח יְהוֹיָדָע הַכֹּהֵן אֲרוֹן אֶחָד וַיִּקֹּב חֹר בְּדַלְתּוֹ וַיִּתֵּן אֹתוֹ אֵצֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ (בימין) [מִיָּמִין] בְּבוֹא־אִישׁ בֵּית יְהֹוָה וְנָתְנוּ־שָׁמָּה
	הַכֹּהֲנִים שֹׁמְרֵי הַסַּף אֶת־כּל־הַכֶּסֶף הַמּוּבָא בֵית־יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And the priest Jehoiada took a chest and bored a hole in its lid.
	He placed it at the right side of the altar as one entered the
	House of YHWH, and the priestly guards of the threshold deposited
	there all the money that was brought into the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	Whenever they saw that there was much money in the chest, the
	royal scribe and the high priest would come up and put the money
	accumulated in the House of YHWH into bags, and they would count
	it.
}

\eX{
	Then they would deliver the money that was weighed out to the
	overseers of the work, who were in charge of the House of YHWH.
	These, in turn, used to pay the carpenters and the laborers who
	worked on the House of YHWH,
}

\eX{
	and the masons and the stonecutters. They also paid for wood and
	for quarried stone with which to make the repairs on the House of
	YHWH, and for every other expenditure that had to be made in
	repairing the House.
}

\eX{
	However, no silver bowls and no snuffers, basins, or trumpets—no
	vessels of gold or silver—were made at the House of YHWH from the
	money brought into the House of YHWH;
}

\eX{
	this was given only to the overseers of the work for the repair of
	the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	No check was kept on the men to whom the money was delivered to
	pay the workers; for they dealt honestly.
}

\eX{
	Money brought as a guilt offering or as a sin offering was not
	deposited in the House of YHWH; it went to the priests.
}

\eX{
	At that time, King Hazael of Aram came up and attacked Gath and
	captured it; and Hazael proceeded to march on Jerusalem.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקַּח יְהוֹאָשׁ מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה אֵת כּל־הַקֳּדָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר־הִקְדִּישׁוּ יְהוֹשָׁפָט וִיהוֹרָם וַאֲחַזְיָהוּ אֲבֹתָיו מַלְכֵי יְהוּדָה וְאֶת־קֳדָשָׁיו וְאֵת כּל־הַזָּהָב
	הַנִּמְצָא בְּאֹצְרוֹת בֵּית־יְהֹוָה וּבֵית הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיִּשְׁלַח לַחֲזָאֵל מֶלֶךְ אֲרָם וַיַּעַל מֵעַל יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The other events of Joash’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	His courtiers formed a conspiracy against Joash and assassinated
	him at Beth-millo that leads down to Silla.
}

\eX{
	The courtiers who assassinated him were Jozacar son of Shimeath
	and Jehozabad son of Shomer. He died and was buried with his
	fathers in the City of David; and his son Amaziah succeeded him as
	king.
}

\eX{
	In the twenty-third year of King Joash son of Ahaziah of Judah,
	Jehoahaz son of Jehu became king over Israel in Samaria—for
	seventeen years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH. He persisted in the sins
	which Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused Israel to commit; he did
	not depart from them.
}

\eX{
	YHWH was angry with Israel and He repeatedly delivered them into
	the hands of King Hazael of Aram and into the hands of Ben-hadad
	son of Hazael.
}

\eX{
	But Jehoahaz pleaded with YHWH; and YHWH listened to him, for He
	saw the suffering that the king of Aram inflicted upon Israel.
}

\eX{
	So YHWH granted Israel a deliverer, and they gained their freedom
	from Aram; and Israel dwelt in its homes as before.
}

\eX{
	However, they did not depart from the sins which the House of
	Jeroboam had caused Israel to commit; they persisted in them. Even
	the sacred post stood in Samaria.
}

\eX{
	In fact, Jehoahaz was left with a force of only fifty horsemen,
	ten chariots, and ten thousand foot soldiers; for the king of Aram
	had decimated them and trampled them like the dust under his feet.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Jehoahaz’s reign, and all his actions and his
	exploits, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Jehoahaz slept with his fathers and he was buried in Samaria; his
	son Joash succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the thirty-seventh year of King Joash of Judah, Jehoash son of
	Jehoahaz became king of Israel in Samaria—for sixteen years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; he did not depart from any of
	the sins which Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused Israel to commit;
	he persisted in them.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Joash’s reign, and all his actions, and his
	exploits in his war with King Amaziah of Judah, are recorded in
	the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Joash slept with his fathers and Jeroboam occupied his throne;
	Joash was buried in Samaria with the kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Elisha had been stricken with the illness of which he was to die,
	and King Joash of Israel went down to see him. He wept over him
	and cried, “Father, father! Israel’s chariots and horsemen!”
}

\eX{
	Elisha said to him, “Get a bow and arrows”; and he brought him a
	bow and arrows.
}

\eX{
	Then he said to the king of Israel, “Grasp the bow!” And when he
	had grasped it, Elisha put his hands over the king’s hands.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר פְּתַח הַחַלּוֹן קֵדְמָה וַיִּפְתָּח וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלִישָׁע יְרֵה וַיּוֹר וַיֹּאמֶר חֵץ־תְּשׁוּעָה לַיהֹוָה וְחֵץ תְּשׁוּעָה בַאֲרָם וְהִכִּיתָ אֶת־אֲרָם בַּאֲפֵק
	עַד־כַּלֵּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“Open the window toward the east,” he said; and he opened it.
	Elisha said, “Shoot!” and he shot. Then he said, “An arrow of
	victory for YHWH! An arrow of victory over Aram! You shall rout
	Aram completely at Aphek.”
}

\eX{
	He said, “Now pick up the arrows.” And he picked them up. “Strike
	the ground!” he said to the king of Israel; and he struck three
	times and stopped.
}

\eX{
	The man of God was angry with him and said to him, “If only you
	had struck five or six times! Then you would have annihilated
	Aram; as it is, you shall defeat Aram only three times.”
}

\eX{
	Elisha died and he was buried. Now bands of Moabites used to
	invade the land at the coming of every year.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי הֵם קֹבְרִים אִישׁ וְהִנֵּה רָאוּ אֶת־הַגְּדוּד וַיַּשְׁלִיכוּ אֶת־הָאִישׁ בְּקֶבֶר אֱלִישָׁע וַיֵּלֶךְ וַיִּגַּע הָאִישׁ בְּעַצְמוֹת אֱלִישָׁע וַיְחִי וַיָּקם
	עַל־רַגְלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Once a man was being buried, when the people caught sight of such
	a band; so they threw the corpse into Elisha’s grave and made off.
	When the [dead] man came in contact with Elisha’s bones, he came
	to life and stood up.
}

\eX{
	King Hazael of Aram had oppressed the Israelites throughout the
	reign of Jehoahaz.
}

\eX{
	But YHWH was gracious and merciful to them, and He turned back to
	them for the sake of His covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
	He refrained from destroying them, and He still did not cast them
	out from His presence.
}

\eX{
	When King Hazael of Aram died, his son Ben-hadad succeeded him as
	king;
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב יְהוֹאָשׁ בֶּן־יְהוֹאָחָז וַיִּקַּח אֶת־הֶעָרִים מִיַּד בֶּן־הֲדַד בֶּן־חֲזָאֵל אֲשֶׁר לָקַח מִיַּד יְהוֹאָחָז אָבִיו בַּמִּלְחָמָה שָׁלֹשׁ פְּעָמִים הִכָּהוּ יוֹאָשׁ
	וַיָּשֶׁב אֶת־עָרֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	and then Jehoash son of Jehoahaz recovered from Ben-hadad son of
	Hazael the towns which had been taken from his father Jehoahaz in
	war. Three times Joash defeated him, and he recovered the towns of
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	In the second year of King Joash son of Joahaz of Israel, Amaziah
	son of King Joash of Judah became king.
}

\eX{
	He was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned
	twenty-nine years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Jehoaddan of
	Jerusalem.
}

\eX{
	He did what was pleasing to YHWH, but not like his ancestor David;
	he did just as his father Joash had done.
}

\eX{
	However, the shrines were not removed; the people continued to
	sacrifice and make offerings at the shrines.
}

\eX{
	Once he had the kingdom firmly in his grasp, he put to death the
	courtiers who had assassinated his father the king.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאֶת־בְּנֵי הַמַּכִּים לֹא הֵמִית כַּכָּתוּב בְּסֵפֶר־תּוֹרַת־מֹשֶׁה אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה יְהֹוָה לֵאמֹר לֹא־יוּמְתוּ אָבוֹת עַל־בָּנִים וּבָנִים לֹא־יוּמְתוּ עַל־אָבוֹת כִּי אִם־אִישׁ
	בְּחֶטְאוֹ (ימות) [יוּמָת]׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He defeated ten thousand Edomites in the Valley of Salt, and he
	captured Sela in battle and renamed it Joktheel, as is still the
	case.
}

\eX{
	Then Amaziah sent envoys to King Jehoash son of Jehoahaz son of
	Jehu of Israel, with this message: “Come, let us confront each
	other.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח יְהוֹאָשׁ מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל־אֲמַצְיָהוּ מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה לֵאמֹר הַחוֹחַ אֲשֶׁר בַּלְּבָנוֹן שָׁלַח אֶל־הָאֶרֶז אֲשֶׁר בַּלְּבָנוֹן לֵאמֹר תְּנָה־אֶת־בִּתְּךָ לִבְנִי
	לְאִשָּׁה וַתַּעֲבֹר חַיַּת הַשָּׂדֶה אֲשֶׁר בַּלְּבָנוֹן וַתִּרְמֹס אֶת־הַחוֹחַ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	King Jehoash of Israel sent back this message to King Amaziah of
	Judah: “The thistle in Lebanon sent this message to the cedar in
	Lebanon, ‘Give your daughter to my son in marriage.’ But a wild
	beast in Lebanon went by and trampled down the thistle.
}

\eX{
	Because you have defeated Edom, you have become arrogant. Stay
	home and enjoy your glory, rather than provoke disaster and fall,
	dragging Judah down with you.”
}

\eX{
	But Amaziah paid no heed; so King Jehoash of Israel advanced, and
	he and King Amaziah of Judah confronted each other at Beth-shemesh
	in Judah.
}

\eX{
	The Judites were routed by Israel, and they all fled to their
	homes.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְאֵת אֲמַצְיָהוּ מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה בֶּן־יְהוֹאָשׁ בֶּן־אֲחַזְיָהוּ תָּפַשׂ יְהוֹאָשׁ מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּבֵית שָׁמֶשׁ (ויבאו) [וַיָּבֹא] יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וַיִּפְרֹץ בְּחוֹמַת
	יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם בְּשַׁעַר אֶפְרַיִם עַד־שַׁעַר הַפִּנָּה אַרְבַּע מֵאוֹת אַמָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	King Jehoash of Israel captured King Amaziah son of Jehoash son of
	Ahaziah of Judah at Beth-shemesh. He marched on Jerusalem, and he
	made a breach of four hundred cubits in the wall of Jerusalem,
	from the Ephraim Gate to the Corner Gate.
}

\eX{
	He carried off all the gold and silver and all the vessels that
	there were in the House of YHWH and in the treasuries of the royal
	palace, as well as hostages; and he returned to Samaria.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Jehoash’s reign, and all his actions and
	exploits, and his war with King Amaziah of Judah, are recorded in
	the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Jehoash slept with his fathers, and was buried in Samaria with the
	kings of Israel; his son Jeroboam succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	King Amaziah son of Joash of Judah lived fifteen years after the
	death of King Jehoash son of Jehoahaz of Israel.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Amaziah’s reign are recorded in the Annals of
	the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	A conspiracy was formed against him in Jerusalem and he fled to
	Lachish; but they sent men after him to Lachish, and they killed
	him there.
}

\eX{
	They brought back his body on horses, and he was buried with his
	fathers in Jerusalem, in the City of David.
}

\eX{
	Then all the people of Judah took Azariah, who was sixteen years
	old, and proclaimed him king to succeed his father Amaziah.
}

\eX{
	It was he who rebuilt Elath and restored it to Judah, after King
	[Amaziah] slept with his fathers.
}

\eX{
	In the fifteenth year of King Amaziah son of Joash of Judah, King
	Jeroboam son of Joash of Israel became king in Samaria—for
	forty-one years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; he did not depart from all
	the sins that Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused Israel to commit.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	הוּא הֵשִׁיב אֶת־גְּבוּל יִשְׂרָאֵל מִלְּבוֹא חֲמָת עַד־יָם הָעֲרָבָה כִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר בְּיַד־עַבְדּוֹ יוֹנָה בֶן־אֲמִתַּי הַנָּבִיא אֲשֶׁר מִגַּת
	הַחֵפֶר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	It was he who restored the territory of Israel from Lebo-hamath to
	the sea of the Arabah, in accordance with the promise that YHWH,
	the God of Israel, had made through His servant, the prophet Jonah
	son of Amittai from Gath-hepher.
}

\eX{
	For YHWH saw the very bitter plight of Israel, with neither bond
	nor free left, and with none to help Israel.
}

\eX{
	And YHWH resolved not to blot out the name of Israel from under
	heaven; and he delivered them through Jeroboam son of Joash.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְיֶתֶר דִּבְרֵי יָרבְעָם וְכל־אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה וּגְבוּרָתוֹ אֲשֶׁר־נִלְחָם וַאֲשֶׁר הֵשִׁיב אֶת־דַּמֶּשֶׂק וְאֶת־חֲמָת לִיהוּדָה בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל הֲלֹא־הֵם כְּתוּבִים עַל־סֵפֶר דִּבְרֵי
	הַיָּמִים לְמַלְכֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The other events of Jeroboam’s reign, and all his actions and
	exploits, how he fought and recovered Damascus and Hamath for
	Judah in Israel, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of
	Israel.
}

\eX{
	Jeroboam slept with his fathers, the kings of Israel, and his son
	Zechariah succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the twenty-seventh year of King Jeroboam of Israel, Azariah son
	of King Amaziah of Judah became king.
}

\eX{
	He was sixteen years old when he became king, and he reigned
	fifty-two years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Jecoliah of
	Jerusalem.
}

\eX{
	He did what was pleasing to YHWH, just as his father Amaziah had
	done.
}

\eX{
	However, the shrines were not removed; the people continued to
	sacrifice and make offerings at the shrines.
}

\eX{
	YHWH struck the king with a plague, and he was a leper until the
	day of his death; he lived in isolated quarters, while Jotham, the
	king’s son, was in charge of the palace and governed the people of
	the land.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Azariah’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	Azariah slept with his fathers, and he was buried with his fathers
	in the City of David; his son Jotham succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the thirty-eighth year of King Azariah of Judah, Zechariah son
	of Jeroboam became king over Israel in Samaria—for six months.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, as his fathers had done; he
	did not depart from the sins which Jeroboam son of Nebat had
	caused Israel to commit.
}

\eX{
	Shallum son of Jabesh conspired against him and struck him down
	before the people and killed him, and succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Zechariah’s reign are recorded in the Annals
	of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	This was in accord with the word that YHWH had spoken to Jehu:
	“Four generations of your descendants shall occupy the throne of
	Israel.” And so it came about.
}

\eX{
	Shallum son of Jabesh became king in the thirty-ninth year of King
	Uzziah of Judah, and he reigned in Samaria one month.
}

\eX{
	Then Menahem son of Gadi set out from Tirzah and came to Samaria;
	he attacked Shallum son of Jabesh in Samaria and killed him, and
	he succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Shallum’s reign, and the conspiracy that he
	formed, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	At that time, [marching] from Tirzah, Menahem subdued Tiphsah and
	all who were in it, and its territory; and because it did not
	surrender, he massacred [its people] and ripped open all its
	pregnant women.
}

\eX{
	In the thirty-ninth year of King Azariah of Judah, Menahem son of
	Gadi became king over Israel in Samaria—for ten years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; throughout his days he did
	not depart from the sins which Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused
	Israel to commit.
}

\eX{
	King Pul of Assyria invaded the land, and Menahem gave Pul a
	thousand talents of silver that he might support him and
	strengthen his hold on the kingdom.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּצֵא מְנַחֵם אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵל עַל כּל־גִּבּוֹרֵי הַחַיִל לָתֵת לְמֶלֶךְ אַשּׁוּר חֲמִשִּׁים שְׁקָלִים כֶּסֶף לְאִישׁ אֶחָד וַיָּשׁב מֶלֶךְ אַשּׁוּר וְלֹא־עָמַד שָׁם
	בָּאָרֶץ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Menahem exacted the money from Israel: every man of means had to
	pay fifty shekels of silver for the king of Assyria. The king of
	Assyria withdrew and did not remain in the land.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Menahem’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	Menahem slept with his fathers, and his son Pekahiah succeeded him
	as king.
}

\eX{
	In the fiftieth year of King Azariah of Judah, Pekahiah son of
	Menahem became king over Israel in Samaria—for two years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; he did not depart from the
	sins which Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused Israel to commit.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּקְשֹׁר עָלָיו פֶּקַח בֶּן־רְמַלְיָהוּ שָׁלִישׁוֹ וַיַּכֵּהוּ בְשֹׁמְרוֹן בְּאַרְמוֹן בֵּית־[הַמֶּלֶךְ] (מלך) אֶת־אַרְגֹּב וְאֶת־הָאַרְיֵה וְעִמּוֹ חֲמִשִּׁים אִישׁ מִבְּנֵי
	גִלְעָדִים וַיְמִתֵהוּ וַיִּמְלֹךְ תַּחְתָּיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	His aide, Pekah son of Remaliah, conspired against him and struck
	him down in the royal palace in Samaria; with him were fifty
	Gileadites, with men from Argob and Arieh; and he killed him and
	succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Pekahiah’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	In the fifty-second year of King Azariah of Judah, Pekah son of
	Remaliah became king over Israel and Samaria—for twenty years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH; he did not depart from the
	sins which Jeroboam son of Nebat had caused Israel to commit.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	בִּימֵי פֶּקַח מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל בָּא תִּגְלַת פִּלְאֶסֶר מֶלֶךְ אַשּׁוּר וַיִּקַּח אֶת־עִיּוֹן וְאֶת־אָבֵל בֵּית־מַעֲכָה וְאֶת־יָנוֹחַ וְאֶת־קֶדֶשׁ וְאֶת־חָצוֹר וְאֶת־הַגִּלְעָד
	וְאֶת־הַגָּלִילָה כֹּל אֶרֶץ נַפְתָּלִי וַיַּגְלֵם אַשּׁוּרָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	In the days of King Pekah of Israel, King Tiglath-pileser of
	Assyria came and captured Ijon, Abel- beth-maacah, Janoah, Kedesh,
	Hazor—Gilead, Galilee, the entire region of Naphtali; and he
	deported the inhabitants to Assyria.
}

\eX{
	Hoshea son of Elah conspired against Pekah son of Remaliah,
	attacked him, and killed him. He succeeded him as king in the
	twentieth year of Jotham son of Uzziah.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Pekah’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Israel.
}

\eX{
	In the second year of King Pekah son of Remaliah of Israel, Jotham
	son of King Uzziah of Judah became king.
}

\eX{
	He was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned
	sixteen years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Jerusha daughter
	of Zadok.
}

\eX{
	He did what was pleasing to YHWH, just as his father Uzziah had
	done.
}

\eX{
	However, the shrines were not removed; the people continued to
	sacrifice and make offerings at the shrines. It was he who built
	the Upper Gate of the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Jotham’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	In those days, YHWH began to incite King Rezin of Aram and Pekah
	son of Remaliah against Judah.
}

\eX{
	Jotham slept with his fathers, and he was buried with his fathers
	in the city of his ancestor David; his son Ahaz succeeded him as
	king.
}

\eX{
	In the seventeenth year of Pekah son of Remaliah, Ahaz son of King
	Jotham of Judah became king.
}

\eX{
	Ahaz was twenty years old when he became king, and he reigned
	sixteen years in Jerusalem. He did not do what was pleasing to
	YHWH his God, as his ancestor David had done,
}

\eX{
	but followed the ways of the kings of Israel. He even consigned
	his son to the fire, in the abhorrent fashion of the nations which
	YHWH had dispossessed before the Israelites.
}

\eX{
	He sacrificed and made offerings at the shrines, on the hills, and
	under every leafy tree.
}

\eX{
	Then King Rezin of Aram and King Pekah son of Remaliah of Israel
	advanced on Jerusalem for battle. They besieged Ahaz, but could
	not overcome [him].
}

\eX{
	At that time King Rezin of Aram recovered Elath for Aram; he drove
	out the Judites from Elath, and Edomites came to Elath and settled
	there, as is still the case.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח אָחָז מַלְאָכִים אֶל־תִּגְלַת פְּלֶסֶר מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר לֵאמֹר עַבְדְּךָ וּבִנְךָ אָנִי עֲלֵה וְהוֹשִׁעֵנִי מִכַּף מֶלֶךְ־אֲרָם וּמִכַּף מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל הַקּוֹמִים
	עָלָי׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Ahaz sent messengers to King Tiglath-pileser of Assyria to say, “I
	am your servant and your son; come and deliver me from the hands
	of the king of Aram and from the hands of the king of Israel, who
	are attacking me.”
}

\eX{
	Ahaz took the gold and silver that were on hand in the House of
	YHWH and in the treasuries of the royal palace and sent them as a
	gift to the king of Assyria.
}

\eX{
	The king of Assyria responded to his request; the king of Assyria
	marched against Damascus and captured it. He deported its
	inhabitants to Kir and put Rezin to death.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ הַמֶּלֶךְ אָחָז לִקְרַאת תִּגְלַת פִּלְאֶסֶר מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר דּוּמֶּשֶׂק וַיַּרְא אֶת־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ אֲשֶׁר בְּדַמָּשֶׂק וַיִּשְׁלַח הַמֶּלֶךְ אָחָז אֶל־אוּרִיָּה הַכֹּהֵן
	אֶת־דְּמוּת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ וְאֶת־תַּבְנִיתוֹ לְכל־מַעֲשֵׂהוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	When King Ahaz went to Damascus to greet King Tiglath-pileser of
	Assyria, he saw the altar in Damascus. King Ahaz sent the priest
	Uriah a sketch of the altar and a detailed plan of its
	construction.
}

\eX{
	The priest Uriah did just as King Ahaz had instructed him from
	Damascus; the priest Uriah built the altar before King Ahaz
	returned from Damascus.
}

\eX{
	When the king returned from Damascus, and when the king saw the
	altar, the king drew near the altar, ascended it,
}

\eX{
	and offered his burnt offering and meal offering; he poured his
	libation, and he dashed the blood of his offering of well-being
	against the altar.
}

\eX{
	As for the bronze altar which had been before YHWH, he moved it
	from its place in front of the Temple, between the [new] altar and
	the House of YHWH, and placed it on the north side of the [new]
	altar.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	(ויצוהו) [וַיְצַוֶּה] הַמֶּלֶךְ־אָחָז אֶת־אוּרִיָּה הַכֹּהֵן לֵאמֹר עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ הַגָּדוֹל הַקְטֵר אֶת־עֹלַת־הַבֹּקֶר וְאֶת־מִנְחַת הָעֶרֶב וְאֶת־עֹלַת הַמֶּלֶךְ
	וְאֶת־מִנְחָתוֹ וְאֵת עֹלַת כּל־עַם הָאָרֶץ וּמִנְחָתָם וְנִסְכֵּיהֶם וְכל־דַּם עֹלָה וְכל־דַּם־זֶבַח עָלָיו תִּזְרֹק וּמִזְבַּח הַנְּחֹשֶׁת יִהְיֶה־לִּי לְבַקֵּר׃
}

\hX{
	וַיְקַצֵּץ הַמֶּלֶךְ אָחָז אֶת־הַמִּסְגְּרוֹת הַמְּכוֹנֹת וַיָּסַר מֵעֲלֵיהֶם (ואת) [אֶת־]הַכִּיֹּר וְאֶת־הַיָּם הוֹרִד מֵעַל הַבָּקָר הַנְּחֹשֶׁת אֲשֶׁר תַּחְתֶּיהָ וַיִּתֵּן
	אֹתוֹ עַל מַרְצֶפֶת אֲבָנִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	King Ahaz cut off the insets—the laver stands—and removed the
	lavers from them. He also removed the tank from the bronze oxen
	that supported it and set it on a stone pavement—
}

\eX{
	on account of the king of Assyria. He also extended to the House
	of YHWH the sabbath passage that had been built in the palace and
	the king’s outer entrance.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Ahaz’s reign, and his actions, are recorded in
	the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	Ahaz slept with his fathers and was buried with his fathers in the
	City of David; his son Hezekiah succeeded him as king.
}

\eX{
	In the twelfth year of King Ahaz of Judah, Hoshea son of Elah
	became king over Israel in Samaria—for nine years.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, though not as much as the
	kings of Israel who preceded him.
}

\eX{
	King Shalmaneser marched against him, and Hoshea became his vassal
	and paid him tribute.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּמְצָא מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר בְּהוֹשֵׁעַ קֶשֶׁר אֲשֶׁר שָׁלַח מַלְאָכִים אֶל־סוֹא מֶלֶךְ־מִצְרַיִם וְלֹא־הֶעֱלָה מִנְחָה לְמֶלֶךְ אַשּׁוּר כְּשָׁנָה בְשָׁנָה וַיַּעְצְרֵהוּ מֶלֶךְ
	אַשּׁוּר וַיַּאַסְרֵהוּ בֵּית כֶּלֶא׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But the king of Assyria caught Hoshea in an act of treachery: he
	had sent envoys to King So of Egypt, and he had not paid the
	tribute to the king of Assyria, as in previous years. And the king
	of Assyria arrested him and put him in prison.
}

\eX{
	Then the king of Assyria marched against the whole land; he came
	to Samaria and besieged it for three years.
}

\eX{
	In the ninth year of Hoshea, the king of Assyria captured Samaria.
	He deported the Israelites to Assyria and settled them in Halah,
	at the [River] Habor, at the River Gozan, and in the towns of
	Media.
}

\eDtrH{
	This happened because the Israelites sinned against YHWH their
	God, who had freed them from the land of Egypt, from the hand of
	Pharaoh king of Egypt. They worshiped other gods%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 17:7. The fall of Israel is explained as exodus reversal. The God who
%		brought them up from Egypt now lets Assyria carry them away.
%	}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	and followed the customs of the nations which YHWH had
	dispossessed before the Israelites and the customs which the kings
	of Israel had practiced.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The Israelites committed against YHWH their God acts which were
	not right: They built for themselves shrines in all their
	settlements, from watchtowers to fortified cities;%
}

\eDtrH{
	they set up pillars and sacred posts for themselves on every lofty
	hill and under every leafy tree;%
}

\eDtrH{
	and they offered sacrifices there, at all the shrines, like the
	nations whom YHWH had driven into exile before them. They
	committed wicked acts to vex YHWH,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and they worshiped fetishes concerning which YHWH had said to
	them, “You must not do this thing.”%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיָּעַד יְהֹוָה בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל וּבִיהוּדָה בְּיַד כּל־[נְבִיאֵי] (נביאו) כל־חֹזֶה לֵאמֹר שֻׁבוּ מִדַּרְכֵיכֶם הָרָעִים וְשִׁמְרוּ מִצְוֺתַי חֻקּוֹתַי כְּכל־הַתּוֹרָה אֲשֶׁר
	צִוִּיתִי אֶת־אֲבֹתֵיכֶם וַאֲשֶׁר שָׁלַחְתִּי אֲלֵיכֶם בְּיַד עֲבָדַי הַנְּבִיאִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	But they did not obey; they stiffened their necks, like their
	fathers who did not have faith in YHWH their God;%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיִּמְאֲסוּ אֶת־חֻקָּיו וְאֶת־בְּרִיתוֹ אֲשֶׁר כָּרַת אֶת־אֲבוֹתָם וְאֵת עֵדְוֺתָיו אֲשֶׁר הֵעִיד בָּם וַיֵּלְכוּ אַחֲרֵי הַהֶבֶל וַיֶּהְבָּלוּ וְאַחֲרֵי הַגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר
	סְבִיבֹתָם אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה יְהֹוָה אֹתָם לְבִלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת כָּהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	they spurned His laws and the covenant that He had made with their
	fathers, and the warnings He had given them. They went after
	delusion and were deluded; [they imitated] the nations that were
	about them, which YHWH had forbidden them to emulate.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַזְבוּ אֶת־כּל־מִצְוֺת יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵיהֶם וַיַּעֲשׂוּ לָהֶם מַסֵּכָה (שנים) [שְׁנֵי] עֲגָלִים וַיַּעֲשׂוּ אֲשֵׁירָה וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ לְכל־צְבָא הַשָּׁמַיִם וַיַּעַבְדוּ
	אֶת־הַבָּעַל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	They rejected all the commandments of YHWH their God; they made
	molten idols for themselves—two calves—and they made a sacred post
	and they bowed down to all the host of heaven, and they worshiped
	Baal.%
}

\eDtrH{
	They consigned their sons and daughters to the fire; they
	practiced augury and divination, and gave themselves over to what
	was displeasing to YHWH and vexed Him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	YHWH was incensed at Israel and He banished them from His
	presence; none was left but the tribe of Judah alone.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Nor did Judah keep the commandments of YHWH their God; they
	followed the customs that Israel had practiced.%
}

\eDtrH{
	So YHWH spurned all the offspring of Israel, and He afflicted them
	and delivered them into the hands of plunderers, and finally He
	cast them out from His presence.%
}

\eDtrH{
	For Israel broke away from the House of David, and they made
	Jeroboam son of Nebat king. Jeroboam caused Israel to stray from
	YHWH and to commit great sin,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and the Israelites persisted in all the sins which Jeroboam had
	committed; they did not depart from them.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In the end, YHWH removed Israel from His presence, as He had
	warned them through all His servants the prophets. So the
	Israelites were deported from their land to Assyria, as is still
	the case.%
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּבֵא מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר מִבָּבֶל וּמִכּוּתָה וּמֵעַוָּא וּמֵחֲמָת וּסְפַרְוַיִם וַיֹּשֶׁב בְּעָרֵי שֹׁמְרוֹן תַּחַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיִּרְשׁוּ אֶת־שֹׁמְרוֹן וַיֵּשְׁבוּ
	בְּעָרֶיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The king of Assyria brought [people] from Babylon, Cuthah, Avva,
	Hamath, and Sepharvaim, and he settled them in the towns of
	Samaria in place of the Israelites; they took possession of
	Samaria and dwelt in its towns.
}

\eX{
	When they first settled there, they did not worship YHWH; so YHWH
	sent lions against them which killed some of them.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמְרוּ לְמֶלֶךְ אַשּׁוּר לֵאמֹר הַגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר הִגְלִיתָ וַתּוֹשֶׁב בְּעָרֵי שֹׁמְרוֹן לֹא יָדְעוּ אֶת־מִשְׁפַּט אֱלֹהֵי הָאָרֶץ וַיְשַׁלַּח־בָּם אֶת־הָאֲרָיוֹת וְהִנָּם
	מְמִיתִים אוֹתָם כַּאֲשֶׁר אֵינָם יֹדְעִים אֶת־מִשְׁפַּט אֱלֹהֵי הָאָרֶץ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The king of Assyria gave an order: “Send there one of the priests
	whom you have deported; let him go and dwell there, and let him
	teach them the practices of the God of the land.”
}

\eX{
	So one of the priests whom they had exiled from Samaria came and
	settled in Bethel; he taught them how to worship YHWH.
}

\eX{
	However, each nation continued to make its own gods and to set
	them up in the cult places which had been made by the people of
	Samaria; each nation [set them up] in the towns in which it lived.
}

\eX{
	The Babylonians made Succoth-benoth, and the men of Cuth made
	Nergal, and the men of Hamath made Ashima,
}

\eX{
	and the Avvites made Nibhaz and Tartak; and the Sepharvites burned
	their children [as offerings] to Adrammelech and Anamelech, the
	gods of Sepharvaim.
}

\eX{
	They worshiped YHWH, but they also appointed from their own ranks
	priests of the shrines, who officiated for them in the cult
	places.
}

\eX{
	They worshiped YHWH, while serving their own gods according to the
	practices of the nations from which they had been deported.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה הֵם עֹשִׂים כַּמִּשְׁפָּטִים הָרִאשֹׁנִים אֵינָם יְרֵאִים אֶת־יְהֹוָה וְאֵינָם עֹשִׂים כְּחֻקֹּתָם וּכְמִשְׁפָּטָם וְכַתּוֹרָה וְכַמִּצְוָה אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה יְהֹוָה
	אֶת־בְּנֵי יַעֲקֹב אֲשֶׁר־שָׂם שְׁמוֹ יִשְׂרָאֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	To this day, they follow their former practices. They do not
	worship YHWH [properly]. They do not follow the laws and
	practices, the Teaching and Instruction that YHWH enjoined upon
	the descendants of Jacob—who was given the name Israel—
}

\eX{
	with whom He made a covenant and whom He commanded: “You shall
	worship no other gods; you shall not bow down to them nor serve
	them nor sacrifice to them.
}

\eX{
	You must worship only YHWH your God, who brought you out of the
	land of Egypt with great might and with an outstretched arm: to
	Him alone shall you bow down and to Him alone shall you sacrifice.
}

\eX{
	You shall observe faithfully, all your days, the laws and the
	practices; the Teaching and Instruction that I wrote down for you;
	do not worship other gods.
}

\eX{
	Do not forget the covenant that I made with you; do not worship
	other gods.
}

\eX{
	Worship only YHWH your God, and He will save you from the hands of
	all your enemies.”
}

\eX{
	But they did not obey; they continued their former practices.
}

\eX{
	Those nations worshiped YHWH, but they also served their idols. To
	this day their children and their children’s children do as their
	ancestors did.
}

\eDtrH{
	In the third year of King Hoshea son of Elah of Israel, Hezekiah
	son of King Ahaz of Judah became king.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned
	in Jerusalem twenty-nine years; his mother’s name was Abi daughter
	of Zechariah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He did what was pleasing to YHWH, just as his father David had
	done.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	הוּא הֵסִיר אֶת־הַבָּמוֹת וְשִׁבַּר אֶת־הַמַּצֵּבֹת וְכָרַת אֶת־הָאֲשֵׁרָה וְכִתַּת נְחַשׁ הַנְּחֹשֶׁת אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה מֹשֶׁה כִּי עַד־הַיָּמִים הָהֵמָּה הָיוּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵל
	מְקַטְּרִים לוֹ וַיִּקְרָא־לוֹ נְחֻשְׁתָּן׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He abolished the shrines and smashed the pillars and cut down the
	sacred post. He also broke into pieces the bronze serpent that
	Moses had made, for until that time the Israelites had been
	offering sacrifices to it; it was called Nehushtan.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He trusted only in YHWH the God of Israel; there was none like him
	among all the kings of Judah after him, nor among those before
	him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He clung to YHWH; he did not turn away from following Him, but
	kept the commandments that YHWH had given to Moses.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And YHWH was always with him; he was successful wherever he
	turned. He rebelled against the king of Assyria and would not
	serve him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He overran Philistia as far as Gaza and its border areas, from
	watchtower to fortified town.%
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי בַּשָּׁנָה הָרְבִיעִית לַמֶּלֶךְ חִזְקִיָּהוּ הִיא הַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית לְהוֹשֵׁעַ בֶּן־אֵלָה מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל עָלָה שַׁלְמַנְאֶסֶר מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר עַל־שֹׁמְרוֹן
	וַיָּצַר עָלֶיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	In the fourth year of King Hezekiah, which was the seventh year of
	King Hoshea son of Elah of Israel, King Shalmaneser of Assyria
	marched against Samaria and besieged it,
}

\eX{
	and he captured it at the end of three years. In the sixth year of
	Hezekiah, which was the ninth year of King Hoshea of Israel,
	Samaria was captured;
}

\eX{
	and the king of Assyria deported the Israelites to Assyria. He
	settled them in Halah, along the Habor [and] the River Gozan, and
	in the towns of Media.
}

\eX{
	[This happened] because they did not obey YHWH their God; they
	transgressed His covenant—all that Moses the servant of YHWH had
	commanded. They did not obey and they did not fulfill it.
}

\eX{
	In the fourteenth year of King Hezekiah, King Sennacherib of
	Assyria marched against all the fortified towns of Judah and
	seized them.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח חִזְקִיָּה מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה אֶל־מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר לָכִישָׁה לֵאמֹר חָטָאתִי שׁוּב מֵעָלַי אֵת אֲשֶׁר־תִּתֵּן עָלַי אֶשָּׂא וַיָּשֶׂם מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר עַל־חִזְקִיָּה
	מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה שְׁלֹשׁ מֵאוֹת כִּכַּר־כֶּסֶף וּשְׁלֹשִׁים כִּכַּר זָהָב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Hezekiah gave him all the silver that was on hand in the House of
	YHWH and in the treasuries of the palace.
}

\eX{
	At that time Hezekiah cut down the doors and the doorposts of the
	Temple of YHWH, which King Hezekiah had overlaid [with gold], and
	gave them to the king of Assyria.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁלַח מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר אֶת־תַּרְתָּן וְאֶת־רַב־סָרִיס וְאֶת־רַבְשָׁקֵה מִן־לָכִישׁ אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ חִזְקִיָּהוּ בְּחֵיל כָּבֵד יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם וַיַּעֲלוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם
	וַיַּעֲלוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ וַיַּעַמְדוּ בִּתְעָלַת הַבְּרֵכָה הָעֶלְיוֹנָה אֲשֶׁר בִּמְסִלַּת שְׂדֵה כֹבֵס׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	They summoned the king; and Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was in
	charge of the palace, Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph the
	recorder went out to them.
}

\eX{
	The Rabshakeh said to them, “You tell Hezekiah: Thus said the
	Great King, the King of Assyria: What makes you so confident?
}

\eX{
	You must think that mere talk is counsel and valor for war! Look,
	on whom are you relying, that you have rebelled against me?
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	עַתָּה הִנֵּה בָטַחְתָּ לְּךָ עַל־מִשְׁעֶנֶת הַקָּנֶה הָרָצוּץ הַזֶּה עַל־מִצְרַיִם אֲשֶׁר יִסָּמֵךְ אִישׁ עָלָיו וּבָא בְכַפּוֹ וּנְקָבָהּ כֵּן פַּרְעֹה מֶלֶךְ־מִצְרַיִם
	לְכל־הַבֹּטְחִים עָלָיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	You rely, of all things, on Egypt, that splintered reed of a
	staff, which enters and punctures the palm of anyone who leans on
	it! That’s what Pharaoh king of Egypt is like to all who rely on
	him.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְכִי־תֹאמְרוּן אֵלַי אֶל־יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ בָּטָחְנוּ הֲלוֹא־הוּא אֲשֶׁר הֵסִיר חִזְקִיָּהוּ אֶת־בָּמֹתָיו וְאֶת־מִזְבְּחֹתָיו וַיֹּאמֶר לִיהוּדָה וְלִירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם לִפְנֵי
	הַמִּזְבֵּחַ הַזֶּה תִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ בִּירוּשָׁלָ͏ִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And if you tell me that you are relying on YHWH your God, He is
	the very one whose shrines and altars Hezekiah did away with,
	telling Judah and Jerusalem, ‘You must worship only at this altar
	in Jerusalem.’
}

\eX{
	Come now, make this wager with my master, the king of Assyria:
	I’ll give you two thousand horses if you can produce riders to
	mount them.
}

\eX{
	So how could you refuse anything even to the deputy of one of my
	master’s lesser servants, relying on Egypt for chariots and
	horsemen?
}

\eX{
	And do you think I have marched against this land to destroy it
	without YHWH? YHWH Himself told me: Go up against that land and
	destroy it.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֶלְיָקִים בֶּן־חִלְקִיָּהוּ וְשֶׁבְנָה וְיוֹאָח אֶל־רַבְשָׁקֵה דַּבֶּר־נָא אֶל־עֲבָדֶיךָ אֲרָמִית כִּי שֹׁמְעִים אֲנָחְנוּ וְאַל־תְּדַבֵּר עִמָּנוּ יְהוּדִית בְּאזְנֵי
	הָעָם אֲשֶׁר עַל־הַחֹמָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Eliakim son of Hilkiah, Shebna, and Joah replied to the Rabshakeh,
	“Please, speak to your servants in Aramaic, for we understand it;
	do not speak to us in Judean in the hearing of the people on the
	wall.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם רַבְשָׁקֵה הַעַל אֲדֹנֶיךָ וְאֵלֶיךָ שְׁלָחַנִי אֲדֹנִי לְדַבֵּר אֶת־הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה הֲלֹא עַל־הָאֲנָשִׁים הַיֹּשְׁבִים עַל־הַחֹמָה לֶאֱכֹל אֶת־[צוֹאָתָם]
	(חריהם) וְלִשְׁתּוֹת אֶת־[מֵימֵי רַגְלֵיהֶם] (שניהם) עִמָּכֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	But the Rabshakeh answered them, “Was it to your master and to you
	that my master sent me to speak those words? It was precisely to
	the men who are sitting on the wall—who will have to eat their
	dung and drink their urine with you.”
}

\eX{
	And the Rabshakeh stood and called out in a loud voice in Judean:
	“Hear the words of the Great King, the King of Assyria.
}

\eX{
	Thus said the king: Don’t let Hezekiah deceive you, for he will
	not be able to deliver you from my hands.
}

\eX{
	Don’t let Hezekiah make you rely on YHWH, saying: YHWH will surely
	save us: this city will not fall into the hands of the king of
	Assyria.
}

\eX{
	Don’t listen to Hezekiah. For thus said the king of Assyria: Make
	your peace with me and come out to me, so that you may all eat
	from your vines and your fig trees and drink water from your
	cisterns,
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	עַד־בֹּאִי וְלָקַחְתִּי אֶתְכֶם אֶל־אֶרֶץ כְּאַרְצְכֶם אֶרֶץ דָּגָן וְתִירוֹשׁ אֶרֶץ לֶחֶם וּכְרָמִים אֶרֶץ זֵית יִצְהָר וּדְבַשׁ וִחְיוּ וְלֹא תָמֻתוּ וְאַל־תִּשְׁמְעוּ
	אֶל־חִזְקִיָּהוּ כִּי־יַסִּית אֶתְכֶם לֵאמֹר יְהֹוָה יַצִּילֵנוּ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	until I come and take you away to a land like your own, a land of
	grain [fields] and vineyards, of bread and wine, of olive oil and
	honey, so that you may live and not die. Don’t listen to Hezekiah,
	who misleads you by saying, ‘YHWH will save us.’
}

\eX{
	Did any of the gods of other nations save his land from the king
	of Assyria?
}

\eX{
	Where were the gods of Hamath and Arpad? Where were the gods of
	Sepharvaim, Hena, and Ivvah? [And] did they save Samaria from me?
}

\eX{
	Which among all the gods of [those] countries saved their
	countries from me, that YHWH should save Jerusalem from me?”
}

\eX{
	But the people were silent and did not say a word in reply; for
	the king’s order was: “Do not answer him.”
}

\eX{
	And so Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was in charge of the palace,
	Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph the recorder came to
	Hezekiah with their clothes rent, and they reported to him what
	the Rabshakeh had said.
}

\eX{
	When King Hezekiah heard this, he rent his clothes, and covered
	himself with sackcloth, and went into the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	He also sent Eliakim, who was in charge of the palace, Shebna the
	scribe, and the senior priests, covered with sackcloth, to the
	prophet Isaiah son of Amoz.
}

\eX{
	They said to him, “Thus said Hezekiah: This day is a day of
	distress, of chastisement, and of disgrace. The babes have reached
	the birthstool, but the strength to give birth is lacking.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	אוּלַי יִשְׁמַע יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֵת כּל־דִּבְרֵי רַבְשָׁקֵה אֲשֶׁר שְׁלָחוֹ מֶלֶךְ־אַשּׁוּר אֲדֹנָיו לְחָרֵף אֱלֹהִים חַי וְהוֹכִיחַ בַּדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַע יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ
	וְנָשָׂאתָ תְפִלָּה בְּעַד הַשְּׁאֵרִית הַנִּמְצָאָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Isaiah said to them, “Tell your master as follows: Thus said YHWH:
	Do not be frightened by the words of blasphemy against Me that you
	have heard from the minions of the king of Assyria.
}

\eX{
	I will delude him; he will hear a rumor and return to his land,
	and I will make him fall by the sword in his land.”
}

\eX{
	The Rabshakeh, meanwhile, heard that [the king] had left Lachish;
	he turned back and found the king of Assyria attacking Libnah.
}

\eX{
	But [the king of Assyria] learned that King Tirhakah of Nubia had
	come out to fight him; so he again sent messengers to Hezekiah,
	saying,
}

\eX{
	“Tell this to King Hezekiah of Judah: Do not let your God, on whom
	you are relying, mislead you into thinking that Jerusalem will not
	be delivered into the hands of the king of Assyria.
}

\eX{
	You yourself have heard what the kings of Assyria have done to all
	the lands, how they have annihilated them; and can you escape?
}

\eX{
	Were the nations that my predecessors destroyed—Gozan, Haran,
	Rezeph, and the Beth-edenites in Telassar—saved by their gods?
}

\eX{
	Where is the king of Hamath? And the king of Arpad? And the kings
	of Lair, Sepharvaim, Hena, and Ivvah?”
}

\eX{
	Hezekiah took the letter from the messengers and read it. Hezekiah
	then went up to the House of YHWH and spread it out before YHWH.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּתְפַּלֵּל חִזְקִיָּהוּ לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה וַיֹּאמַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל יֹשֵׁב הַכְּרֻבִים אַתָּה־הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים לְבַדְּךָ לְכֹל מַמְלְכוֹת הָאָרֶץ אַתָּה עָשִׂיתָ
	אֶת־הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֶת־הָאָרֶץ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And Hezekiah prayed to YHWH and said, “O LORD of Hosts, Enthroned
	on the Cherubim! You alone are God of all the kingdoms of the
	earth. You made the heavens and the earth.
}

\eX{
	O LORD, incline Your ear and hear; open Your eyes and see. Hear
	the words that Sennacherib has sent to blaspheme the living God!
}

\eX{
	True, O LORD, the kings of Assyria have annihilated the nations
	and their lands,
}

\eX{
	and have committed their gods to the flames and have destroyed
	them; for they are not gods, but man’s handiwork of wood and
	stone.
}

\eX{
	But now, O LORD our God, deliver us from his hands, and let all
	the kingdoms of the earth know that You alone, O LORD, are God.”
}

\eX{
	Then Isaiah son of Amoz sent this message to Hezekiah: “Thus said
	YHWH, the God of Israel: I have heard the prayer you have offered
	to Me concerning King Sennacherib of Assyria.
}

\eX{
	This is the word that YHWH has spoken concerning him: “Fair Maiden
	Zion despises you, She mocks at you; Fair Jerusalem shakes Her
	head at you.
}

\eX{
	Whom have you blasphemed and reviled? Against whom made loud your
	voice And haughtily raised your eyes? Against the Holy One of
	Israel!
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	בְּיַד מַלְאָכֶיךָ חֵרַפְתָּ אֲדֹנָי וַתֹּאמֶר (ברכב) [בְּרֹב] רִכְבִּי אֲנִי עָלִיתִי מְרוֹם הָרִים יַרְכְּתֵי לְבָנוֹן וְאֶכְרֹת קוֹמַת אֲרָזָיו מִבְחוֹר בְּרֹשָׁיו וְאָבוֹאָה
	מְלוֹן קִצֹּה יַעַר כַּרְמִלּוֹ׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	It is I who have drawn and drunk the waters of strangers; I have
	dried up with the soles of my feet All the streams of Egypt.’
}

\eX{
	Have you not heard? Of old I planned that very thing, I designed
	it long ago, And now have fulfilled it. And it has come to pass,
	Laying waste fortified towns In desolate heaps.
}

\eX{
	Their inhabitants are helpless, Dismayed and shamed. They were but
	grass of the field And green herbage, Grass of the roofs that is
	blasted Before the standing grain.
}

\eX{
	I know your stayings And your goings and comings, And how you have
	raged against Me.
}

\eX{
	Because you have raged against Me, And your tumult has reached My
	ears, I will place My hook in your nose And My bit between your
	jaws; And I will make you go back by the road By which you came.
}

\eX{
	“And this is the sign for you: This year you eat what grows of
	itself, and the next year what springs from that; and in the third
	year, sow and reap, and plant vineyards and eat their fruit.
}

\eX{
	And the survivors of the House of Judah that have escaped shall
	regenerate its stock below and produce boughs above.
}

\eX{
	For a remnant shall come forth from Jerusalem, Survivors from
	Mount Zion. The zeal of YHWH of Hosts Shall bring this to pass.
}

\eX{
	Assuredly, thus said YHWH concerning the king of Assyria: He shall
	not enter this city: He shall not shoot an arrow at it, Or advance
	upon it with a shield, Or pile up a siege mound against it.
}

\eX{
	He shall go back By the way he came; He shall not enter this city
	—declares YHWH.
}

\eX{
	I will protect and save this city for My sake, And for the sake of
	My servant David.”
}

\eX{
	That night an angel of YHWH went out and struck down one hundred
	and eighty-five thousand in the Assyrian camp, and the following
	morning they were all dead corpses.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 19:35. One hundred eighty-five thousand is not narrated as a battle. The
%		army wakes up as corpses, which is efficient theology and terrible morning news.
%	}

tex.english:
\eX{
	So King Sennacherib of Assyria broke camp and retreated, and
	stayed in Nineveh.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי הוּא מִשְׁתַּחֲוֶה בֵּית נִסְרֹךְ אֱלֹהָיו וְאַדְרַמֶּלֶךְ וְשַׂרְאֶצֶר [בָּנָיו] הִכֻּהוּ בַחֶרֶב וְהֵמָּה נִמְלְטוּ אֶרֶץ אֲרָרָט וַיִּמְלֹךְ אֵסַר־חַדֹּן בְּנוֹ
	תַּחְתָּיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	While he was worshiping in the temple of his god Nisroch, his sons
	Adrammelech and Sarezer struck him down with the sword. They fled
	to the land of Ararat, and his son Esarhaddon succeeded him as
	king.
}

\eX{
	In those days Hezekiah fell dangerously ill. The prophet Isaiah
	son of Amoz came and said to him, “Thus said YHWH: Set your
	affairs in order, for you are going to die; you will not get
	well.”
}

\eX{
	Thereupon Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and prayed to YHWH.
	He said,
}

\eX{
	“Please, O LORD, remember how I have walked before You sincerely
	and wholeheartedly, and have done what is pleasing to You.” And
	Hezekiah wept profusely.
}

\eX{
	Before Isaiah had gone out of the middle court, the word of YHWH
	came to him:
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	שׁוּב וְאָמַרְתָּ אֶל־חִזְקִיָּהוּ נְגִיד־עַמִּי כֹּה־אָמַר יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֵי דָּוִד אָבִיךָ שָׁמַעְתִּי אֶת־תְּפִלָּתֶךָ רָאִיתִי אֶת־דִּמְעָתֶךָ הִנְנִי רֹפֶא לָךְ בַּיּוֹם
	הַשְּׁלִישִׁי תַּעֲלֶה בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	“Go back and say to Hezekiah, the ruler of My people: Thus said
	YHWH, the God of your father David: I have heard your prayer, I
	have seen your tears. I am going to heal you; on the third day you
	shall go up to the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	And I will add fifteen years to your life. I will also rescue you
	and this city from the hands of the king of Assyria. I will
	protect this city for My sake and for the sake of My servant
	David.”—
}

\eX{
	Then Isaiah said, “Get a cake of figs.” And they got one, and they
	applied it to the rash, and he recovered.—
}

\eX{
	Hezekiah asked Isaiah, “What is the sign that YHWH will heal me
	and that I shall go up to the House of YHWH on the third day?”
}

\eX{
	Isaiah replied, “This is the sign for you from YHWH that YHWH will
	do the thing that He has promised: Shall the shadow advance ten
	steps or recede ten steps?”
}

\eX{
	Hezekiah said, “It is easy for the shadow to lengthen ten steps,
	but not for the shadow to recede ten steps.”
}

\eX{
	So the prophet Isaiah called to YHWH, and He made the shadow which
	had descended on the dial of Ahaz recede ten steps.
}

\eX{
	At that time, King Berodach-baladan son of Baladan of Babylon sent
	[envoys with] a letter and a gift to Hezekiah, for he had heard
	about Hezekiah’s illness.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁמַע עֲלֵיהֶם חִזְקִיָּהוּ וַיַּרְאֵם אֶת־כּל־בֵּית נְכֹתֹה אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף וְאֶת־הַזָּהָב וְאֶת־הַבְּשָׂמִים וְאֵת שֶׁמֶן הַטּוֹב וְאֵת בֵּית כֵּלָיו וְאֵת כּל־אֲשֶׁר נִמְצָא
	בְּאוֹצְרֹתָיו לֹא־הָיָה דָבָר אֲשֶׁר לֹא־הֶרְאָם חִזְקִיָּהוּ בְּבֵיתוֹ וּבְכל־מֶמְשַׁלְתּוֹ׃
}

\hX{
	וַיָּבֹא יְשַׁעְיָהוּ הַנָּבִיא אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ חִזְקִיָּהוּ וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו מָה אָמְרוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים הָאֵלֶּה וּמֵאַיִן יָבֹאוּ אֵלֶיךָ וַיֹּאמֶר חִזְקִיָּהוּ מֵאֶרֶץ רְחוֹקָה
	בָּאוּ מִבָּבֶל׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Then the prophet Isaiah came to King Hezekiah. “What,” he demanded
	of him, “did those men say to you? Where have they come to you
	from?” “They have come,” Hezekiah replied, “from a far country,
	from Babylon.”
}

\eX{
	Next he asked, “What have they seen in your palace?” And Hezekiah
	replied, “They have seen everything that is in my palace. There
	was nothing in my storehouses that I did not show them.”
}

\eX{
	A time is coming when everything in your palace which your
	ancestors have stored up to this day will be carried off to
	Babylon; nothing will remain behind, said YHWH.
}

\eX{
	And some of your sons, your own issue, whom you will have
	fathered, will be taken to serve as eunuchs in the palace of the
	king of Babylon.”
}

\eX{
	Hezekiah declared to Isaiah, “The word of YHWH that you have
	spoken is good.” For he thought, “It means that safety is assured
	for my time.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְיֶתֶר דִּבְרֵי חִזְקִיָּהוּ וְכל־גְּבוּרָתוֹ וַאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה אֶת־הַבְּרֵכָה וְאֶת־הַתְּעָלָה וַיָּבֵא אֶת־הַמַּיִם הָעִירָה הֲלֹא־הֵם כְּתוּבִים עַל־סֵפֶר דִּבְרֵי הַיָּמִים
	לְמַלְכֵי יְהוּדָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The other events of Hezekiah’s reign, and all his exploits, and
	how he made the pool and the conduit and brought the water into
	the city, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	Hezekiah slept with his fathers, and his son Manasseh succeeded
	him as king.
}

\eX{
	Manasseh was twelve years old when he became king, and he reigned
	fifty-five years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Hephzibah.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, following the abhorrent
	practices of the nations that YHWH had dispossessed before the
	Israelites.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשׁב וַיִּבֶן אֶת־הַבָּמוֹת אֲשֶׁר אִבַּד חִזְקִיָּהוּ אָבִיו וַיָּקֶם מִזְבְּחֹת לַבַּעַל וַיַּעַשׂ אֲשֵׁרָה כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה אַחְאָב מֶלֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ
	לְכל־צְבָא הַשָּׁמַיִם וַיַּעֲבֹד אֹתָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	He rebuilt the shrines that his father Hezekiah had destroyed; he
	erected altars for Baal and made a sacred post, as King Ahab of
	Israel had done. He bowed down to all the host of heaven and
	worshiped them,
}

\eX{
	and he built altars for them in the House of YHWH, of which YHWH
	had said, “I will establish My name in Jerusalem.”
}

\eX{
	He built altars for all the hosts of heaven in the two courts of
	the House of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	He consigned his son to the fire; he practiced soothsaying and
	divination, and consulted ghosts and familiar spirits; he did much
	that was displeasing to YHWH, to vex Him.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיָּשֶׂם אֶת־פֶּסֶל הָאֲשֵׁרָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה בַּבַּיִת אֲשֶׁר אָמַר יְהֹוָה אֶל־דָּוִד וְאֶל־שְׁלֹמֹה בְנוֹ בַּבַּיִת הַזֶּה וּבִירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם אֲשֶׁר בָּחַרְתִּי מִכֹּל שִׁבְטֵי
	יִשְׂרָאֵל אָשִׂים אֶת־שְׁמִי לְעוֹלָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The sculptured image of Asherah that he made he placed in the
	House concerning which YHWH had said to David and to his son
	Solomon, “In this House and in Jerusalem, which I chose out of all
	the tribes of Israel, I will establish My name forever.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וְלֹא אֹסִיף לְהָנִיד רֶגֶל יִשְׂרָאֵל מִן־הָאֲדָמָה אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לַאֲבוֹתָם רַק אִם־יִשְׁמְרוּ לַעֲשׂוֹת כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִים וּלְכל־הַתּוֹרָה אֲשֶׁר־צִוָּה אֹתָם עַבְדִּי
	מֹשֶׁה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And I will not again cause the feet of Israel to wander from the
	land that I gave to their fathers, if they will but faithfully
	observe all that I have commanded them—all the Teachings with
	which My servant Moses charged them.”
}

\eX{
	But they did not obey, and Manasseh led them astray to do greater
	evil than the nations that YHWH had destroyed before the
	Israelites.
}

\eDtrH{
	“Because King Manasseh of Judah has done these abhorrent things—he
	has outdone in wickedness all that the Amorites did before his
	time—and because he led Judah to sin with his fetishes,%
}

\eDtrH{
	assuredly, thus said YHWH, the God of Israel: I am going to bring
	such a disaster on Jerusalem and Judah that both ears of everyone
	who hears about it will tingle.%
}

\eDtrH{
	I will apply to Jerusalem the measuring line of Samaria and the
	weights of the House of Ahab; I will wipe Jerusalem clean as one
	wipes a dish and turns it upside down.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And I will cast off the remnant of My own people and deliver them
	into the hands of their enemies. They shall be plunder and prey to
	all their enemies%
}

\eDtrH{
	because they have done what is displeasing to Me and have been
	vexing Me from the day that their fathers came out of Egypt to
	this day.”%
}

\eX{
	Moreover, Manasseh put so many innocent persons to death that he
	filled Jerusalem [with blood] from end to end—besides the sin he
	committed in causing Judah to do what was displeasing to YHWH.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Manasseh’s reign, and all his actions, and the
	sins he committed, are recorded in the Annals of the Kings of
	Judah.
}

\eX{
	Manasseh slept with his fathers and was buried in the garden of
	his palace, in the garden of Uzza; and his son Amon succeeded him
	as king.
}

\eX{
	Amon was twenty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned
	two years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Meshullemeth
	daughter of Haruz of Jotbah.
}

\eX{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, as his father Manasseh had
	done.
}

\eX{
	He walked in all the ways of his father, worshiping the fetishes
	which his father had worshiped and bowing down to them.
}

\eX{
	He forsook YHWH, the God of his fathers, and did not follow the
	way of YHWH.
}

\eX{
	Amon’s courtiers conspired against him; and they killed the king
	in his palace.
}

\eX{
	But the people of the land put to death all who had conspired
	against King Amon, and the people of the land made his son Josiah
	king in his stead.
}

\eX{
	The other events of Amon’s reign [and] his actions are recorded in
	the Annals of the Kings of Judah.
}

\eX{
	He was buried in his tomb in the garden of Uzza; and his son
	Josiah succeeded him as king.
}

\eDtrH{
	Josiah was eight years old when he became king, and he reigned
	thirty-one years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name was Jedidah
	daughter of Adaiah of Bozkath.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He did what was pleasing to YHWH and he followed all the ways of
	his ancestor David; he did not deviate to the right or to the
	left.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In the eighteenth year of King Josiah, the king sent the scribe
	Shaphan son of Azaliah son of Meshullam to the House of YHWH,
	saying,%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Go to the high priest Hilkiah and let him weigh the silver that
	has been deposited in the House of YHWH, which the guards of the
	threshold have collected from the people.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And let it be delivered to the overseers of the work who are in
	charge at the House of YHWH, that they in turn may pay it out to
	the workmen that are in the House of YHWH, for the repair of the
	House:%
}

\eDtrH{
	to the carpenters, the laborers, and the masons, and for the
	purchase of wood and quarried stones for repairing the House.%
}

\eDtrH{
	However, no check is to be kept on them for the silver that is
	delivered to them, for they deal honestly.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then the high priest Hilkiah said to the scribe Shaphan, “I have
	found a scroll of the Teaching in the House of YHWH.” And Hilkiah
	gave the scroll to Shaphan, who read it.%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 22:8. Finding the scroll is either reform, rediscovery, or a controlled
%		explosion in the archive. The text lets the book do what kings could not.
%	}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיָּבֹא שָׁפָן הַסֹּפֵר אֶל־הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיָּשֶׁב אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּבָר וַיֹּאמֶר הִתִּיכוּ עֲבָדֶיךָ אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף הַנִּמְצָא בַבַּיִת וַיִּתְּנֻהוּ עַל־יַד עֹשֵׂי הַמְּלָאכָה
	הַמֻּפְקָדִים בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	The scribe Shaphan then went to the king and reported to the king:
	“Your servants have melted down the silver that was deposited in
	the House, and they have delivered it to the overseers of the work
	who are in charge at the House of YHWH.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	The scribe Shaphan also told the king, “The high priest Hilkiah
	has given me a scroll”; and Shaphan read it to the king.%
}

\eDtrH{
	When the king heard the words of the scroll of the Teaching, he
	rent his clothes.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And the king gave orders to the priest Hilkiah, and to Ahikam son
	of Shaphan, Achbor son of Michaiah, the scribe Shaphan, and Asaiah
	the king’s minister:%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	לְכוּ דִרְשׁוּ אֶת־יְהֹוָה בַּעֲדִי וּבְעַד־הָעָם וּבְעַד כּל־יְהוּדָה עַל־דִּבְרֵי הַסֵּפֶר הַנִּמְצָא הַזֶּה כִּי־גְדוֹלָה חֲמַת יְהֹוָה אֲשֶׁר־הִיא נִצְּתָה בָנוּ עַל אֲשֶׁר
	לֹא־שָׁמְעוּ אֲבֹתֵינוּ עַל־דִּבְרֵי הַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה לַעֲשׂוֹת כְּכל־הַכָּתוּב עָלֵינוּ׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וַיֵּלֶךְ חִלְקִיָּהוּ הַכֹּהֵן וַאֲחִיקָם וְעַכְבּוֹר וְשָׁפָן וַעֲשָׂיָה אֶל־חֻלְדָּה הַנְּבִיאָה אֵשֶׁת שַׁלֻּם בֶּן־תִּקְוָה בֶּן־חַרְחַס שֹׁמֵר הַבְּגָדִים וְהִיא יֹשֶׁבֶת
	בִּירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם בַּמִּשְׁנֶה וַיְדַבְּרוּ אֵלֶיהָ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	So the priest Hilkiah, and Ahikam, Achbor, Shaphan, and Asaiah
	went to the prophetess Huldah—the wife of Shallum son of Tikvah
	son of Harhas, the keeper of the wardrobe—who was living in
	Jerusalem in the Mishneh, and they spoke to her.%
}

\eDtrH{
	She responded: “Thus said YHWH, the God of Israel: Say to the man
	who sent you to me:%
}

\eDtrH{
	Thus said YHWH: I am going to bring disaster upon this place and
	its inhabitants, in accordance with all the words of the scroll
	which the king of Judah has read.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Because they have forsaken Me and have made offerings to other
	gods and vexed Me with all their deeds, My wrath is kindled
	against this place and it shall not be quenched.%
}

\eDtrH{
	But say this to the king of Judah, who sent you to inquire of
	YHWH: Thus said YHWH, the God of Israel: As for the words which
	you have heard—%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	יַעַן רַךְ־לְבָבְךָ וַתִּכָּנַע מִפְּנֵי יְהֹוָה בְּשׁמְעֲךָ אֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְתִּי עַל־הַמָּקוֹם הַזֶּה וְעַל־יֹשְׁבָיו לִהְיוֹת לְשַׁמָּה וְלִקְלָלָה וַתִּקְרַע אֶת־בְּגָדֶיךָ
	וַתִּבְכֶּה לְפָנָי וְגַם אָנֹכִי שָׁמַעְתִּי נְאֻם־יְהֹוָה׃
}

\hDtrH{
	לָכֵן הִנְנִי אֹסִפְךָ עַל־אֲבֹתֶיךָ וְנֶאֱסַפְתָּ אֶל־קִבְרֹתֶיךָ בְּשָׁלוֹם וְלֹא־תִרְאֶינָה עֵינֶיךָ בְּכֹל הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר־אֲנִי מֵבִיא עַל־הַמָּקוֹם הַזֶּה וַיָּשִׁבוּ
	אֶת־הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּבָר׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Assuredly, I will gather you to your fathers and you will be laid
	in your tomb in peace. Your eyes shall not see all the disaster
	which I will bring upon this place.” So they brought back the
	reply to the king.%
}

\eDtrH{
	At the king’s summons, all the elders of Judah and Jerusalem
	assembled before him.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעַל הַמֶּלֶךְ בֵּית־יְהֹוָה וְכל־אִישׁ יְהוּדָה וְכל־יֹשְׁבֵי יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם אִתּוֹ וְהַכֹּהֲנִים וְהַנְּבִיאִים וְכל־הָעָם לְמִקָּטֹן וְעַד־גָּדוֹל וַיִּקְרָא בְאזְנֵיהֶם
	אֶת־כּל־דִּבְרֵי סֵפֶר הַבְּרִית הַנִּמְצָא בְּבֵית יְהֹוָה׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וַיַּעֲמֹד הַמֶּלֶךְ עַל־הָעַמּוּד וַיִּכְרֹת אֶת־הַבְּרִית לִפְנֵי יְהֹוָה לָלֶכֶת אַחַר יְהֹוָה וְלִשְׁמֹר מִצְוֺתָיו וְאֶת־עֵדְוֺתָיו וְאֶת־חֻקֹּתָיו בְּכל־לֵב וּבְכל־נֶפֶשׁ
	לְהָקִים אֶת־דִּבְרֵי הַבְּרִית הַזֹּאת הַכְּתֻבִים עַל־הַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה וַיַּעֲמֹד כּל־הָעָם בַּבְּרִית׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וַיְצַו הַמֶּלֶךְ אֶת־חִלְקִיָּהוּ הַכֹּהֵן הַגָּדוֹל וְאֶת־כֹּהֲנֵי הַמִּשְׁנֶה וְאֶת־שֹׁמְרֵי הַסַּף לְהוֹצִיא מֵהֵיכַל יְהֹוָה אֵת כּל־הַכֵּלִים הָעֲשׂוּיִם לַבַּעַל
	וְלָאֲשֵׁרָה וּלְכֹל צְבָא הַשָּׁמָיִם וַיִּשְׂרְפֵם מִחוּץ לִירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם בְּשַׁדְמוֹת קִדְרוֹן וְנָשָׂא אֶת־עֲפָרָם בֵּית־אֵל׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וְהִשְׁבִּית אֶת־הַכְּמָרִים אֲשֶׁר נָתְנוּ מַלְכֵי יְהוּדָה וַיְקַטֵּר בַּבָּמוֹת בְּעָרֵי יְהוּדָה וּמְסִבֵּי יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם וְאֶת־הַמְקַטְּרִים לַבַּעַל לַשֶּׁמֶשׁ וְלַיָּרֵחַ
	וְלַמַּזָּלוֹת וּלְכֹל צְבָא הַשָּׁמָיִם׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּצֵא אֶת־הָאֲשֵׁרָה מִבֵּית יְהֹוָה מִחוּץ לִירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם אֶל־נַחַל קִדְרוֹן וַיִּשְׂרֹף אֹתָהּ בְּנַחַל קִדְרוֹן וַיָּדֶק לְעָפָר וַיַּשְׁלֵךְ אֶת־עֲפָרָהּ עַל־קֶבֶר בְּנֵי
	הָעָם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He brought out the [image of] Asherah from the House of YHWH to
	the Kidron Valley outside Jerusalem, and burned it in the Kidron
	Valley; he beat it to dust and scattered its dust over the burial
	ground of the common people.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He tore down the cubicles of the male prostitutes in the House of
	YHWH, at the place where the women wove coverings for Asherah.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיָּבֵא אֶת־כּל־הַכֹּהֲנִים מֵעָרֵי יְהוּדָה וַיְטַמֵּא אֶת־הַבָּמוֹת אֲשֶׁר קִטְּרוּ־שָׁמָּה הַכֹּהֲנִים מִגֶּבַע עַד־בְּאֵר שָׁבַע וְנָתַץ אֶת־בָּמוֹת הַשְּׁעָרִים אֲשֶׁר־פֶּתַח
	שַׁעַר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ שַׂר־הָעִיר אֲשֶׁר־עַל־שְׂמֹאול אִישׁ בְּשַׁעַר הָעִיר׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	The priests of the shrines, however, did not ascend the altar of
	YHWH in Jerusalem, but they ate unleavened bread along with their
	kinsmen.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He also defiled Topheth, which is in the Valley of Ben-hinnom, so
	that no one might consign his son or daughter to the fire of
	Molech.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּשְׁבֵּת אֶת־הַסּוּסִים אֲשֶׁר נָתְנוּ מַלְכֵי יְהוּדָה לַשֶּׁמֶשׁ מִבֹּא בֵית־יְהֹוָה אֶל־לִשְׁכַּת נְתַן־מֶלֶךְ הַסָּרִיס אֲשֶׁר בַּפַּרְוָרִים וְאֶת־מַרְכְּבוֹת הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ
	שָׂרַף בָּאֵשׁ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He did away with the horses that the kings of Judah had dedicated
	to the sun, at the entrance of the House of YHWH, near the chamber
	of the eunuch Nathan-melech, which was in the precincts. He burned
	the chariots of the sun.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְאֶת־הַמִּזְבְּחוֹת אֲשֶׁר עַל־הַגָּג עֲלִיַּת אָחָז אֲשֶׁר־עָשׂוּ מַלְכֵי יְהוּדָה וְאֶת־הַמִּזְבְּחוֹת אֲשֶׁר־עָשָׂה מְנַשֶּׁה בִּשְׁתֵּי חַצְרוֹת בֵּית־יְהֹוָה נָתַץ הַמֶּלֶךְ
	וַיָּרץ מִשָּׁם וְהִשְׁלִיךְ אֶת־עֲפָרָם אֶל־נַחַל קִדְרוֹן׃
}

\hDtrH{
	וְאֶת־הַבָּמוֹת אֲשֶׁר עַל־פְּנֵי יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם אֲשֶׁר מִימִין לְהַר־הַמַּשְׁחִית אֲשֶׁר בָּנָה שְׁלֹמֹה מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל לְעַשְׁתֹּרֶת שִׁקֻּץ צִידֹנִים וְלִכְמוֹשׁ שִׁקֻּץ
	מוֹאָב וּלְמִלְכֹּם תּוֹעֲבַת בְּנֵי־עַמּוֹן טִמֵּא הַמֶּלֶךְ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He shattered their pillars and cut down their sacred posts and
	covered their sites with human bones.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְגַם אֶת־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ אֲשֶׁר בְּבֵית־אֵל הַבָּמָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה יָרבְעָם בֶּן־נְבָט אֲשֶׁר הֶחֱטִיא אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל גַּם אֶת־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ הַהוּא וְאֶת־הַבָּמָה נָתָץ וַיִּשְׂרֹף
	אֶת־הַבָּמָה הֵדַק לְעָפָר וְשָׂרַף אֲשֵׁרָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	As for the altar in Bethel [and] the shrine made by Jeroboam son
	of Nebat who caused Israel to sin—that altar, too, and the shrine
	as well, he tore down. He burned down the shrine and beat it to
	dust, and he burned the sacred post.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיִּפֶן יֹאשִׁיָּהוּ וַיַּרְא אֶת־הַקְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר־שָׁם בָּהָר וַיִּשְׁלַח וַיִּקַּח אֶת־הָעֲצָמוֹת מִן־הַקְּבָרִים וַיִּשְׂרֹף עַל־הַמִּזְבֵּחַ וַיְטַמְּאֵהוּ כִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה
	אֲשֶׁר קָרָא אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר קָרָא אֶת־הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Josiah turned and saw the graves that were there on the hill; and
	he had the bones taken out of the graves and burned on the altar.
	Thus he defiled it, in fulfillment of the word of YHWH foretold by
	the man of God who foretold these happenings.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר מָה הַצִּיּוּן הַלָּז אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי רֹאֶה וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו אַנְשֵׁי הָעִיר הַקֶּבֶר אִישׁ־הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר־בָּא מִיהוּדָה וַיִּקְרָא אֶת־הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה אֲשֶׁר
	עָשִׂיתָ עַל הַמִּזְבַּח בֵּית־אֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He asked, “What is the marker I see there?” And the men of the
	town replied, “That is the grave of the man of God who came from
	Judah and foretold these things that you have done to the altar of
	Bethel.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	“Let him be,” he said, “let no one disturb his bones.” So they
	left his bones undisturbed together with the bones of the prophet
	who came from Samaria.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְגַם אֶת־כּל־בָּתֵּי הַבָּמוֹת אֲשֶׁר בְּעָרֵי שֹׁמְרוֹן אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ מַלְכֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לְהַכְעִיס הֵסִיר יֹאשִׁיָּהוּ וַיַּעַשׂ לָהֶם כְּכל־הַמַּעֲשִׂים אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה
	בְּבֵית־אֵל׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Josiah also abolished all the cult places in the towns of Samaria,
	which the kings of Israel had built, vexing [YHWH]. He dealt with
	them just as he had done to Bethel:%
}

\eDtrH{
	He slew on the altars all the priests of the shrines who were
	there, and he burned human bones on them. Then he returned to
	Jerusalem.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The king commanded all the people, “Offer the passover sacrifice
	to YHWH your God as prescribed in this scroll of the covenant.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	Now the passover sacrifice had not been offered in that manner in
	the days of the chieftains who ruled Israel, or during the days of
	the kings of Israel and the kings of Judah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Only in the eighteenth year of King Josiah was such a passover
	sacrifice offered in that manner to YHWH in Jerusalem.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְגַם אֶת־הָאֹבוֹת וְאֶת־הַיִּדְּעֹנִים וְאֶת־הַתְּרָפִים וְאֶת־הַגִּלֻּלִים וְאֵת כּל־הַשִּׁקֻּצִים אֲשֶׁר נִרְאוּ בְּאֶרֶץ יְהוּדָה וּבִירוּשָׁלַ͏ִם בִּעֵר יֹאשִׁיָּהוּ לְמַעַן
	הָקִים אֶת־דִּבְרֵי הַתּוֹרָה הַכְּתֻבִים עַל־הַסֵּפֶר אֲשֶׁר מָצָא חִלְקִיָּהוּ הַכֹּהֵן בֵּית יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	There was no king like him before who turned back to YHWH with all
	his heart and soul and might, in full accord with the Teaching of
	Moses; nor did any like him arise after him.%
}

\eDtrH{
	However, YHWH did not turn away from His awesome wrath which had
	blazed up against Judah because of all the things Manasseh did to
	vex Him.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיֹּאמֶר יְהֹוָה גַּם אֶת־יְהוּדָה אָסִיר מֵעַל פָּנַי כַּאֲשֶׁר הֲסִרֹתִי אֶת־יִשְׂרָאֵל וּמָאַסְתִּי אֶת־הָעִיר הַזֹּאת אֲשֶׁר־בָּחַרְתִּי אֶת־יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וְאֶת־הַבַּיִת
	אֲשֶׁר אָמַרְתִּי יִהְיֶה שְׁמִי שָׁם׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	YHWH said, “I will also banish Judah from My presence as I
	banished Israel; and I will reject the city of Jerusalem which I
	chose and the House where I said My name would abide.”%
}

\eDtrH{
	The other events of Josiah’s reign, and all his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In his days, Pharaoh Neco, king of Egypt, marched against the king
	of Assyria to the River Euphrates; King Josiah marched toward him,
	but when he confronted him at Megiddo, [Pharaoh Neco] slew him.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיַּרְכִּבֻהוּ עֲבָדָיו מֵת מִמְּגִדּוֹ וַיְבִאֻהוּ יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וַיִּקְבְּרֻהוּ בִּקְבֻרָתוֹ וַיִּקַּח עַם־הָאָרֶץ אֶת־יְהוֹאָחָז בֶּן־יֹאשִׁיָּהוּ וַיִּמְשְׁחוּ אֹתוֹ
	וַיַּמְלִיכוּ אֹתוֹ תַּחַת אָבִיו׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	His servants conveyed his body in a chariot from Megiddo to
	Jerusalem, and they buried him in his tomb. Then the people of the
	land took Jehoahaz; they anointed him and made him king in place
	of his father.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Jehoahaz was twenty-three years old when he became king, and he
	reigned three months in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Hamutal
	daughter of Jeremiah of Libnah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, just as his fathers had done.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Pharaoh Neco imprisoned him in Riblah in the region of Hamath, to
	keep him from reigning in Jerusalem. And he imposed on the land an
	indemnity of one hundred talents of silver and a talent of gold.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Then Pharaoh Neco appointed Eliakim son of Josiah king in place of
	his father Josiah, changing his name to Jehoiakim. He took
	Jehoahaz and brought him to Egypt, where he died.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהַכֶּסֶף וְהַזָּהָב נָתַן יְהוֹיָקִים לְפַרְעֹה אַךְ הֶעֱרִיךְ אֶת־הָאָרֶץ לָתֵת אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף עַל־פִּי פַרְעֹה אִישׁ כְּעֶרְכּוֹ נָגַשׂ אֶת־הַכֶּסֶף וְאֶת־הַזָּהָב אֶת־עַם
	הָאָרֶץ לָתֵת לְפַרְעֹה נְכֹה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	Jehoiakim gave Pharaoh the silver and the gold, and he made an
	assessment on the land to pay the money demanded by Pharaoh. He
	exacted from the people of the land the silver and gold to be paid
	Pharaoh Neco, according to each man’s assessment.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Jehoiakim was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he
	reigned eleven years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Zebudah
	daughter of Pedaiah of Rumah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, just as his ancestors had
	done.%
}

\eDtrH{
	In his days, King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon came up, and Jehoiakim
	became his vassal for three years. Then he turned and rebelled
	against him.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיְשַׁלַּח יְהֹוָה בּוֹ אֶת־גְּדוּדֵי כַשְׂדִּים וְאֶת־גְּדוּדֵי אֲרָם וְאֵת גְּדוּדֵי מוֹאָב וְאֵת גְּדוּדֵי בְנֵי־עַמּוֹן וַיְשַׁלְּחֵם בִּיהוּדָה לְהַאֲבִידוֹ כִּדְבַר יְהֹוָה
	אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר בְּיַד עֲבָדָיו הַנְּבִיאִים׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	YHWH let loose against him the raiding bands of the Chaldeans,
	Arameans, Moabites, and Ammonites; He let them loose against Judah
	to destroy it, in accordance with the word that YHWH had spoken
	through His servants the prophets.%
}

\eDtrH{
	All this befell Judah at the command of YHWH, who banished [them]
	from His presence because of all the sins that Manasseh had
	committed,%
}

\eDtrH{
	and also because of the blood of the innocent that he shed. For he
	filled Jerusalem with the blood of the innocent, and YHWH would
	not forgive.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The other events of Jehoiakim’s reign, and all of his actions, are
	recorded in the Annals of the Kings of Judah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Jehoiakim slept with his fathers, and his son Jehoiachin succeeded
	him as king.%
}

\eDtrH{
	The king of Egypt did not venture out of his country again, for
	the king of Babylon had seized all the land that had belonged to
	the king of Egypt, from the Wadi of Egypt to the River Euphrates.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Jehoiachin was eighteen years old when he became king, and he
	reigned three months in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Nehushta
	daughter of Elnathan of Jerusalem.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, just as his father had done.%
}

\eDtrH{
	At that time, the troops of King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon marched
	against Jerusalem, and the city came under siege.%
}

\eDtrH{
	King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon advanced against the city while his
	troops were besieging it.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Thereupon King Jehoiachin of Judah, along with his mother, and his
	courtiers, commanders, and officers, surrendered to the king of
	Babylon. The king of Babylon took him captive in the eighth year
	of his reign.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיּוֹצֵא מִשָּׁם אֶת־כּל־אוֹצְרוֹת בֵּית יְהֹוָה וְאוֹצְרוֹת בֵּית הַמֶּלֶךְ וַיְקַצֵּץ אֶת־כּל־כְּלֵי הַזָּהָב אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה שְׁלֹמֹה מֶלֶךְ־יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּהֵיכַל יְהֹוָה
	כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יְהֹוָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He carried off from Jerusalem all the treasures of the House of
	YHWH and the treasures of the royal palace; he stripped off all
	the golden decorations in the Temple of YHWH—which King Solomon of
	Israel had made—as YHWH had warned.%
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וְהִגְלָה אֶת־כּל־יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וְאֶת־כּל־הַשָּׂרִים וְאֵת כּל־גִּבּוֹרֵי הַחַיִל (עשרה) [עֲשֶׂרֶת] אֲלָפִים גּוֹלֶה וְכל־הֶחָרָשׁ וְהַמַּסְגֵּר לֹא נִשְׁאַר זוּלַת דַּלַּת
	עַם־הָאָרֶץ׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	He exiled all of Jerusalem: all the commanders and all the
	warriors—ten thousand exiles—as well as all the craftsmen and
	smiths; only the poorest people in the land were left.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He deported Jehoiachin to Babylon; and the king’s mother and wives
	and officers and the notables of the land were brought as exiles
	from Jerusalem to Babylon.%
}

\eDtrH{
	All the able men, to the number of seven thousand—all of them
	warriors, trained for battle—and a thousand craftsmen and smiths
	were brought to Babylon as exiles by the king of Babylon.%
}

\eDtrH{
	And the king of Babylon appointed Mattaniah, Jehoiachin’s uncle,
	king in his place, changing his name to Zedekiah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Zedekiah was twenty-one years old when he became king, and he
	reigned eleven years in Jerusalem; his mother’s name was Hamutal
	daughter of Jeremiah of Libnah.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He did what was displeasing to YHWH, just as Jehoiakim had done.%
}

\eDtrH{
	Indeed, Jerusalem and Judah were a cause of anger for YHWH, so
	that He cast them out of His presence. Zedekiah rebelled against
	the king of Babylon.%
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי בִשְׁנַת הַתְּשִׁיעִית לְמלְכוֹ בַּחֹדֶשׁ הָעֲשִׂירִי בֶּעָשׂוֹר לַחֹדֶשׁ בָּא נְבֻכַדְנֶאצַּר מֶלֶךְ־בָּבֶל הוּא וְכל־חֵילוֹ עַל־יְרוּשָׁלַ͏ִם וַיִּחַן עָלֶיהָ וַיִּבְנוּ
	עָלֶיהָ דָּיֵק סָבִיב׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	And in the ninth year of his reign, on the tenth day of the tenth
	month, Nebuchadnezzar moved against Jerusalem with his whole army.
	He besieged it; and they built towers against it all around.
}

\eX{
	The city continued in a state of siege until the eleventh year of
	King Zedekiah.
}

\eX{
	By the ninth day [of the fourth month] the famine had become acute
	in the city; there was no food left for the common people.
}

\eX{
	Then [the wall of] the city was breached. All the soldiers [left
	the city] by night through the gate between the double walls,
	which is near the king’s garden—the Chaldeans were all around the
	city; and [the king] set out for the Arabah.
}

\eX{
	But the Chaldean troops pursued the king, and they overtook him in
	the steppes of Jericho as his entire force left him and scattered.
}

\eX{
	They captured the king and brought him before the king of Babylon
	at Riblah; and they put him on trial.
}

\eX{
	They slaughtered Zedekiah’s sons before his eyes; then Zedekiah’s
	eyes were put out. He was chained in bronze fetters and he was
	brought to Babylon.
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 25:7. Zedekiah’s last sight is the slaughter of his sons. Then his eyes
%		are put out. The verse makes memory the final punishment.
%	}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּבַחֹדֶשׁ הַחֲמִישִׁי בְּשִׁבְעָה לַחֹדֶשׁ הִיא שְׁנַת תְּשַׁע־עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה לַמֶּלֶךְ נְבֻכַדְנֶאצַּר מֶלֶךְ־בָּבֶל בָּא נְבוּזַרְאֲדָן רַב־טַבָּחִים עֶבֶד מֶלֶךְ־בָּבֶל
	יְרוּשָׁלָ͏ִם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	On the seventh day of the fifth month—that was the nineteenth year
	of King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon—Nebuzaradan, the chief of the
	guards, an officer of the king of Babylon, came to Jerusalem.
}

\eX{
	He burned the House of YHWH, the king’s palace, and all the houses
	of Jerusalem; he burned down the house of every notable person.
}

\eX{
	The entire Chaldean force that was with the chief of the guard
	tore down the walls of Jerusalem on every side.
}

\eX{
	The remnant of the people that was left in the city, the defectors
	who had gone over to the king of Babylon—and the remnant of the
	population—were taken into exile by Nebuzaradan, the chief of the
	guards.
}

\eX{
	But some of the poorest in the land were left by the chief of the
	guards, to be vinedressers and field hands.
}

\eX{
	The Chaldeans broke up the bronze columns of the House of YHWH,
	the stands, and the bronze tank that was in the House of YHWH; and
	they carried the bronze away to Babylon.
}

\eX{
	They also took all the pails, scrapers, snuffers, ladles, and all
	the other bronze vessels used in the service.
}

\eX{
	The chief of the guards took whatever was of gold and whatever was
	of silver: firepans and sprinkling bowls.
}

\eX{
	The two columns, the one tank, and the stands that Solomon
	provided for the House of YHWH—all these objects contained bronze
	beyond weighing.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	שְׁמֹנֶה עֶשְׂרֵה אַמָּה קוֹמַת הָעַמּוּד הָאֶחָד וְכֹתֶרֶת עָלָיו נְחֹשֶׁת וְקוֹמַת הַכֹּתֶרֶת שָׁלֹשׁ (אמה) [אַמּוֹת] וּשְׂבָכָה וְרִמֹּנִים עַל־הַכֹּתֶרֶת סָבִיב הַכֹּל נְחֹשֶׁת
	וְכָאֵלֶּה לַעַמּוּד הַשֵּׁנִי עַל־הַשְּׂבָכָה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	The chief of the guards also took Seraiah, the chief priest,
	Zephaniah, the deputy priest, and the three guardians of the
	threshold.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וּמִן־הָעִיר לָקַח סָרִיס אֶחָד אֲשֶׁר־הוּא פָקִיד עַל־אַנְשֵׁי הַמִּלְחָמָה וַחֲמִשָּׁה אֲנָשִׁים מֵרֹאֵי פְנֵי־הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲשֶׁר נִמְצְאוּ בָעִיר וְאֵת הַסֹּפֵר שַׂר הַצָּבָא
	הַמַּצְבִּא אֶת־עַם הָאָרֶץ וְשִׁשִּׁים אִישׁ מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ הַנִּמְצְאִים בָּעִיר׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Nebuzaradan, the chief of the guards, took them and brought them
	to the king of Babylon at Riblah.
}

\eX{
	The king of Babylon had them struck down and put to death at
	Riblah, in the region of Hamath. Thus Judah was exiled from its
	land.
}

\eX{
	King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon put Gedaliah son of Ahikam son of
	Shaphan in charge of the people whom he left in the land of Judah.
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיִּשְׁמְעוּ כל־שָׂרֵי הַחֲיָלִים הֵמָּה וְהָאֲנָשִׁים כִּי־הִפְקִיד מֶלֶךְ־בָּבֶל אֶת־גְּדַלְיָהוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל־גְּדַלְיָהוּ הַמִּצְפָּה וְיִשְׁמָעֵאל בֶּן־נְתַנְיָה וְיוֹחָנָן
	בֶּן־קָרֵחַ וּשְׂרָיָה בֶן־תַּנְחֻמֶת הַנְּטֹפָתִי וְיַאֲזַנְיָהוּ בֶּן־הַמַּעֲכָתִי הֵמָּה וְאַנְשֵׁיהֶם׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	Gedaliah reassured them and their men, saying, “Do not be afraid
	of the servants of the Chaldeans. Stay in the land and serve the
	king of Babylon, and it will go well with you.”
}

tex.original:
\hX{
	וַיְהִי בַּחֹדֶשׁ הַשְּׁבִיעִי בָּא יִשְׁמָעֵאל בֶּן־נְתַנְיָה בֶּן־אֱלִישָׁמָע מִזֶּרַע הַמְּלוּכָה וַעֲשָׂרָה אֲנָשִׁים אִתּוֹ וַיַּכּוּ אֶת־גְּדַלְיָהוּ וַיָּמֹת
	וְאֶת־הַיְּהוּדִים וְאֶת־הַכַּשְׂדִּים אֲשֶׁר־הָיוּ אִתּוֹ בַּמִּצְפָּה׃
}

tex.english:
\eX{
	In the seventh month, Ishmael son of Nethaniah son of Elishama,
	who was of royal descent, came with ten men, and they struck down
	Gedaliah and he died; [they also killed] the Judeans and the
	Chaldeans who were present with him at Mizpah.
}

\eX{
	And all the people, young and old, and the officers of the troops
	set out and went to Egypt because they were afraid of the
	Chaldeans.
}

tex.original:
\hDtrH{
	וַיְהִי בִשְׁלֹשִׁים וָשֶׁבַע שָׁנָה לְגָלוּת יְהוֹיָכִין מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה בִּשְׁנֵים עָשָׂר חֹדֶשׁ בְּעֶשְׂרִים וְשִׁבְעָה לַחֹדֶשׁ נָשָׂא אֱוִיל מְרֹדַךְ מֶלֶךְ בָּבֶל בִּשְׁנַת
	מלְכוֹ אֶת־רֹאשׁ יְהוֹיָכִין מֶלֶךְ־יְהוּדָה מִבֵּית כֶּלֶא׃
}

tex.english:
\eDtrH{
	In the thirty-seventh year of the exile of King Jehoiachin of
	Judah, on the twenty-seventh day of the twelfth month, King
	Evil-merodach of Babylon, in the year he became king, took note of
	King Jehoiachin of Judah and released him from prison.%
}

\eDtrH{
	He spoke kindly to him, and gave him a throne above those of other
	kings who were with him in Babylon.%
}

\eDtrH{
	His prison garments were removed, and [Jehoiachin] received
	regular rations by his favor for the rest of his life.%
}

\eDtrH{
	A regular allotment of food was given him at the instance of the
	king—an allotment for each day—all the days of his life.%
}

tex.commentary:
%	\footnote{
%		Footnote 25:30. The book ends with a ration, not a throne. It is a small daily
%		mercy after a very large ruin.
%	}
